/* CAPE Digital Media Unit 2 (DME2) · Module 1: Visual and Interactive Design. Developed by Kester Small. Built from her DME2 course outline and the CAPE Digital Media syllabus. */
(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M1","code":"Module 1","title":"Visual and Interactive Design","overview":"<p>Module 1 turns you from a consumer of digital media into a designer and producer. You will learn the pre-production process that every professional product starts with (problem definition, brainstorming, sketches, storyboards, Gantt charts and prototype testing), then master the elements of visual design, colour theory, harmony rules and the rules of composition, together with the five principles of interactive design. You will critique indigenous Caribbean digital products, choose the right platforms and file formats, produce audio, video and image work, animate in 2D and 3D, collaborate with digital tools, plan a promotional campaign, track design trends and write a project proposal with a budget and a pitch. This module follows the ten sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20% (1 hour 30 minutes). <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory questions, one per module, each worth 15 marks, together worth 20% (2 hours 15 minutes, no internet). Expect practical tasks such as planning, sketching, editing and exporting files, alongside structured and essay parts. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design a product or service, preferably for a local social or entrepreneurial need, and implement it with digital tools. It is marked out of 45 (weighted to 90): <b>Proposal 14</b>, <b>Progress and Process 12</b> and <b>Product 19</b>, which includes a <b>30-second audio-visual short ad</b> worth 5 marks. The report must not exceed 1,500 words. Module 1 gives you the planning, design, animation, campaign and proposal skills that your whole SBA depends on.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Read each learn screen, try the activities (use the easier version if you need a first step), complete the quick check-in honestly, and write the short task at the end of each unit. Keep a sketchbook or digital folder of your mood boards, colour schemes, storyboards and animation tests: this becomes evidence for your SBA progress and process marks. Finish with the Module 1 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to apply design principles to the creation of a digital media product through structured pre-production planning, from problem definition and brainstorming to storyboards, Gantt charts, prototype validation and implementation.","By the end you will be able to create design solutions grounded in the elements of visual design, colour theory and harmony rules, rules of composition and the principles of interactive design.","By the end you will be able to critique the impact and motivations for indigenous Caribbean digital products through case study analysis, screening and critical observation.","By the end you will be able to select and apply appropriate digital media platforms and file formats (MP4, MP3, PDF, PNG, JPEG, GIF) for a given purpose and audience, including importing, exporting, sharing and embedding.","By the end you will be able to integrate basic image manipulation, sound editing, video capture, lighting, editing and export principles into digital media designs.","By the end you will be able to apply the basics of 2D and 3D animation, including keyframing, timing, spacing, squash and stretch, interpolation and a basic walk cycle.","By the end you will be able to use interactive digital media tools and electronic communities effectively for collaboration and self-directed activities, observing netiquette.","By the end you will be able to apply current digital media industry practices to plan a promotional campaign and measure its effectiveness with analytics and KPIs.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence trends and developments in visual and interactive design and use research tools to track them.","By the end you will be able to outline the basic considerations for developing a project proposal, including writing, sources of funding, budgeting and pitching."],"glossary":[{"term":"Problem definition","def":"The first pre-production step: a clear statement of the challenge, purpose and goal the digital media product must address."},{"term":"Target audience","def":"The specific group of people a product is designed for, described by needs, demographics and behaviours."},{"term":"Mind map","def":"A brainstorming diagram that branches related ideas out from one central topic."},{"term":"SCAMPER","def":"A brainstorming technique that improves an existing idea by asking how to Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate or Reverse it."},{"term":"Mood board","def":"A collage of images, colours, textures and fonts that sets the look and feel of a project."},{"term":"Style guide","def":"A document of fixed rules (logo use, fonts, hex colour codes, spacing) that keeps every piece of a project consistent."},{"term":"Storyboard","def":"A sequence of sketched frames with notes that plans the shots of a video, animation or interactive product."},{"term":"Gantt chart","def":"A bar chart that shows project tasks against time, with durations, overlaps and milestones."},{"term":"Milestone","def":"A key checkpoint in a production schedule, such as 'storyboard approved' or 'final cut delivered'."},{"term":"Prototype","def":"An early, testable model of a product used to check an idea before full production."},{"term":"Idea validation","def":"Testing a concept or prototype with real target users to confirm it meets their needs."},{"term":"Elements of visual design","def":"The basic building blocks of any design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour."},{"term":"Value","def":"The lightness or darkness of a colour or tone."},{"term":"Primary colours","def":"Colours that cannot be made by mixing others; red, yellow and blue on the traditional colour wheel (red, green and blue for screens)."},{"term":"Tertiary colours","def":"Colours made by mixing a primary colour with a neighbouring secondary colour, such as red-orange."},{"term":"Complementary scheme","def":"A harmony rule that pairs two colours directly opposite each other on the colour wheel for maximum contrast."},{"term":"Analogous scheme","def":"A harmony rule using three to five colours that sit side by side on the colour wheel for a calm, unified look."},{"term":"Triadic scheme","def":"A harmony rule using three colours evenly spaced (120 degrees apart) around the colour wheel."},{"term":"Split-complementary scheme","def":"A harmony rule that pairs a base colour with the two colours either side of its complement."},{"term":"Tetradic scheme","def":"A harmony rule using four colours made of two complementary pairs."},{"term":"Tint","def":"A hue lightened by adding white."},{"term":"Shade","def":"A hue darkened by adding black."},{"term":"Tone","def":"A hue muted by adding grey."},{"term":"Rule of thirds","def":"A composition guide that divides the frame into a three-by-three grid and places key subjects on the lines or intersections."},{"term":"Golden ratio","def":"A proportion of about 1 to 1.618, used in the golden rectangle and golden section to create naturally pleasing layouts."},{"term":"Point of interest","def":"The part of a design or frame that the viewer's eye is drawn to first; the most prominent subject."},{"term":"Negative space","def":"The empty or white space around and between elements, which gives a design room to breathe."},{"term":"Proximity","def":"Placing related elements close together so viewers read them as a group."},{"term":"Perceivability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can easily see and recognise what can be clicked, tapped or used."},{"term":"Learnability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can quickly learn how to use an interface."},{"term":"Predictability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can correctly expect what will happen before they act."},{"term":"Feedback (interactive design)","def":"An interactive design principle: the system responds to every user action with a visible or audible signal."},{"term":"Indigenous digital product","def":"Digital content created by local or regional creators that reflects their own culture, identity and stories."},{"term":"Cultural authenticity","def":"Representing a culture accurately and respectfully, ideally with input from people who belong to it."},{"term":"Knowledge platform","def":"A platform built to collect and share information, such as Wikipedia or Quora."},{"term":"Crowd-sourcing platform","def":"A platform that gathers ideas, work or funds from a large online crowd, such as GoFundMe or Kickstarter."},{"term":"Infrastructure platform","def":"A platform that provides the underlying computing services other products are built on, such as cloud hosting."},{"term":"Lossy compression","def":"Compression that permanently removes some data to shrink files, as in JPEG and MP3."},{"term":"Lossless compression","def":"Compression that shrinks files without losing any data, as in PNG."},{"term":"Embedding","def":"Placing content from another source, such as a YouTube video or Google Map, so it displays inside your own page."},{"term":"Cross-platform compatibility","def":"The ability of a file or product to work correctly on different devices, browsers and operating systems."},{"term":"Three-point lighting","def":"A standard lighting set-up using a key light, a fill light and a back light."},{"term":"Colour grading","def":"Adjusting the colours and mood of video footage during editing."},{"term":"Keyframe","def":"A frame that marks the start or end of a movement or change in an animation."},{"term":"Interpolation (tweening)","def":"The automatic generation of in-between frames by software between two keyframes."},{"term":"Squash and stretch","def":"An animation principle that deforms an object to show weight, flexibility and impact."},{"term":"Spacing","def":"How far an object moves between frames; close spacing reads as slow and wide spacing as fast."},{"term":"Walk cycle","def":"A looping series of poses (contact, down, passing, up) that makes a character appear to walk."},{"term":"Vertex, edge and face","def":"The parts of a 3D mesh: a point, a line joining two points, and a flat surface enclosed by edges."},{"term":"Graph editor","def":"A tool in software such as Blender that shows animation curves so you can adjust easing and timing."},{"term":"Electronic community","def":"An online group, forum or wiki where people with shared interests communicate and collaborate."},{"term":"Netiquette","def":"The rules of polite, professional behaviour in online communication."},{"term":"Guerrilla marketing","def":"Unconventional, low-cost, high-impact promotion designed to surprise people in public spaces."},{"term":"Viral marketing","def":"Content designed to be shared rapidly from person to person for organic reach."},{"term":"Key performance indicator (KPI)","def":"A measurable value, such as click-through rate, used to judge whether a campaign meets its goals."},{"term":"Commoditisation","def":"The process by which technology becomes standardised, widely available and cheap."},{"term":"Glassmorphism","def":"A UI trend using frosted, semi-transparent panels over blurred backgrounds."},{"term":"Executive summary","def":"The short opening section of a proposal that summarises the whole project for busy decision makers."},{"term":"Crowdfunding","def":"Raising money for a project through small contributions from many people online."},{"term":"Pitch deck","def":"A short visual presentation used to persuade stakeholders or investors to support a project."}],"units":[{"id":"M1U1","title":"Pre-production principles: creating a digital media product","objectives":["Apply the stages of the creative design process (acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation, selection, implementation, evaluation) to a real local problem, linking back to Unit 1.","Define the creative process (Whyte, 2013), describe its seven stages with their plain-language glosses, and compare the linear, cyclic, stepped and branched network models.","Define a design problem, its purpose and goal, and analyse the target audience and market for a digital media product.","Use brainstorming processes such as mind mapping, SCAMPER and rapid ideation, and plan designs with rough sketches, mood boards and style guides.","Create storyboards and set timelines using production schedules, milestones, work plans and Gantt charts.","Explain the role of pre-production (concept development, logistical planning, risk reduction, creative alignment), its core elements (resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice, health and safety), the factors affecting creation, and the virtuous cycle.","Explain how prototypes are built and validated with target users, and how a validated prototype is implemented as a finished product."],"videos":[{"title":"The design process explained","q":"design thinking process explained for students empathise define ideate prototype test"},{"title":"SCAMPER brainstorming technique","q":"SCAMPER technique explained with examples creative thinking"},{"title":"How to make a storyboard","q":"how to make a storyboard for video beginners shot types"},{"title":"Gantt charts for beginners","q":"gantt chart explained for beginners project timeline milestones"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Recap: the creative design process","html":"<p>In Unit 1 you met the <b>creative design process</b>. The creative process is defined as:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">\"The sequence of activities that leads from the developing of ideas to producing a <b>desired outcome</b>.\" (Whyte, 2013)</p>\n<p>Unit 2 now asks you to <b>apply</b> it to real products. There are seven stages, and you are expected to document every one of them. The plain-language glosses are in brackets.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example: a hurricane-preparedness app for Sint Maarten</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Acceptance</b> (or understand the problem)</td><td>You commit to undertaking the task and identify the time, energy, resources and responsibility needed for the idea to come to life.</td><td>The Island Pulse Media agency agrees to build the app for a community group after Hurricane Irma and lists the staff, budget and weeks needed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Analysis</b> (the problem or situation)</td><td>You research the project idea and its market, and speak to the people the product will be useful to.</td><td>Keisha surveys residents in Cul de Sac and Dutch Quarter about how they got storm news in 2017.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Definition</b> (restate the problem clearly by defining the goal)</td><td>You focus on the problem to be solved and set out the scope: timelines, milestones and production schedule.</td><td>\"Residents need one trusted, low-data place for shelter and storm updates.\" Launch milestone: 1 June.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Ideation and brainstorming</b> (to think of the possibilities, come up with options)</td><td>You generate ideas and keep a log of all of them, even ones that seem irrelevant now.</td><td>Mind map: SMS alerts, WhatsApp bot, offline map, radio stream, checklist app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Selection</b> (decide on the best option by comparing feasibility, risks and so on)</td><td>You choose the idea that most closely meets the project's objectives.</td><td>The offline checklist and shelter map wins: it is feasible and works with no signal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Implementation</b> (by taking the plunge)</td><td>You work out the tasks and their sequence (often in a Gantt chart), then use resources to execute them.</td><td>Jerome builds it in Thunkable; Maya builds a matching web page.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. Evaluation</b> (assess whether the solution works and how it could be improved)</td><td>You gather feedback from the audience, stakeholders and clients, often with a rubric, and decide what to improve or eliminate.</td><td>Residents test it during a June drill; fixes are made before peak season.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The same journey is also described as <b>pre-production principles</b>: problem definition, target audience and market, brainstorming, planning designs, rough sketches, storyboards, timelines, prototype and idea validation, then implementation. Section 1.1 of the course outline follows this list, and this unit works through each part in order. The next screens give full notes on each stage and on the four creative process models.</p>\n<p>Pre-production is everything you do <b>before</b> full production begins. It is <b>the planning phase of any digital media project</b>, which lays the foundation for smooth execution and high-quality output. It is cheaper to change a sketch than a finished video, so good pre-production avoids costly mistakes in production and post-production.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your Unit 2 SBA proposal (14 marks) is really a pre-production document. Every technique in this unit, from the problem statement to the Gantt chart, can appear as evidence in your proposal and in your Progress and Process section.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The creative process is the set of steps that takes you from an idea to a finished product. It has seven stages: acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation and evaluation. Think of hosting a family cook-out in Grenada: you agree to host, ask what people like, decide the menu, list ideas, pick the best ones, cook, then ask everyone how it went.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Pre-production is not separate from the creative process: it covers the first five stages plus the planning part of implementation (the Gantt chart, the schedule and the prototype). A Grade One answer shows that the stages overlap and loop. Evaluation often sends a team back to analysis, for example when residents testing a hurricane app in Sint Maarten say the text is too small to read. A common mistake is to treat ideation and selection as one step. Examiners expect you to show that ideas are generated <b>without judgement</b> first, then compared on feasibility, cost and risk. When a question asks you to <b>apply</b> the process, name each stage and give one action from the scenario for it.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the creative design process in order","intro":"<p>The Gouyave Heritage Club in Grenada wants a short digital tour of its fishing town. Put the seven stages of the creative process in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Acceptance: the team commits to the task and identifies the time, resources and responsibility needed."},{"t":"Analysis: the team researches the idea and its market and asks residents and visitors for their opinion."},{"t":"Definition: the team restates the problem as a clear goal and sets the scope, timeline and milestones."},{"t":"Ideation and brainstorming: the team logs every idea, such as an audio walk, a map and a video series."},{"t":"Selection: ideas are compared on feasibility and risk, and the one that best meets the objectives is chosen."},{"t":"Implementation: the team takes the plunge, sequences the tasks in a Gantt chart and produces the tour."},{"t":"Evaluation: visitors and the club give feedback, and the team decides what to improve or eliminate."}],"win":"Correct. Acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation, evaluation: the sequence of activities that leads from developing ideas to producing a desired outcome (Whyte, 2013).","tutor":[{"h":"Understand before you create","p":"<p>Research and defining the problem must come before you generate ideas.</p>"},{"h":"Many, then one","p":"<p>Ideation produces many ideas; selection narrows them to one.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Design process order (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four key stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Analysis: research the users and the problem."},{"t":"Ideation and brainstorming: come up with options."},{"t":"Implementation: take the plunge and build the chosen idea."},{"t":"Evaluation: test it with users."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Put the creative design process in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>No stage names this time. Read what Island Pulse Media does for the organisers of Antigua Sailing Week, who want a race-results page for visitors. Put the team's actions in the order of the seven-stage creative process.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The studio signs the job and lists the staff, weeks and budget it will need."},{"t":"Team members interview skippers and visitors at English Harbour and study what other regatta apps offer."},{"t":"The team writes: \"Visitors need race times and results in one low-data place\", and sets a launch milestone of 1 April."},{"t":"Every suggestion goes on the ideas log: a live results page, a WhatsApp bot, a printed map, even a drone livestream."},{"t":"The ideas are scored for cost, risk and fit with the goal; the live results web page scores highest."},{"t":"The tasks are sequenced in a Gantt chart, and the page is built and tested on phones."},{"t":"After race week, survey results are compared with the goal and a list of fixes is drawn up for next year."}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised each stage from its action alone: acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation and evaluation. That is how examiners test the process inside a scenario."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The creative process","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>The creative process</b> is \"the sequence of activities that leads from the developing of ideas to producing a desired outcome.\" (Whyte, 2013)</p>\n<p>This module has four general objectives: <b>understand</b> the creative process from idea to implementation; <b>appreciate</b> its role and importance in solution design; <b>plan and design</b> creative solutions; and <b>implement</b> solutions using digital content creation tools.</p>\n<p><b>Key points for each stage</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1. Acceptance</b>: the first stage, when you have committed to undertaking the task. The time, energy, resources and responsibility needed for the idea to come to life are identified.</li>\n<li><b>2. Analysis</b>: comprehensive research into the project idea and the market it will seek. Collect and examine all relevant information, and speak to the people your product or service will be useful to.</li>\n<li><b>3. Definition</b>: clearly focus on the problem to be solved and set out the scope of the project, including timelines, milestones and the production schedule.</li>\n<li><b>4. Ideation and brainstorming</b>: ideation is short for <b>idea generation</b>. Keep a log of all ideas, without discarding any that seem irrelevant now; they may be useful later. Build on ideas with free writing, mind maps and word association.</li>\n<li><b>5. Selection</b>: choose the idea, or way of executing it, that most closely meets the objectives of the project.</li>\n<li><b>6. Implementation</b>: work out all the tasks and their sequence (a Gantt chart shows this graphically), then use resources to execute them.</li>\n<li><b>7. Evaluation</b>: gather feedback from the target audience, stakeholders, clients and colleagues; review the procedures used; decide what to improve and what to eliminate. A rubric or standardised form may be used to judge the outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Creative process models</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:440px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four creative process models: linear arrow chain, cyclic loop, stepped chain with loops back, and branched network that splits and converges\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<path d=\"M10 30 h55 M58 25 l7 5 l-7 5\"/>\n<circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"30\" r=\"20\"/><path d=\"M140 17 l5 0 l-1 6\"/>\n<path d=\"M190 20 h20 v20 h20 v20 M200 30 q-8 -14 4 -14 M220 50 q-8 -14 4 -14\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 30 l20 -15 M260 30 l20 0 M260 30 l20 15 M280 15 l25 15 M280 30 h25 M280 45 l25 -15 M305 30 h15\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"38\" y=\"80\">Linear</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"80\">Cyclic</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"80\">Stepped with</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"92\">feedback loops</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"80\">Branched</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"92\">network</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linear model</b>: one step after the other; each step must be clearly completed before you progress to the next.</li>\n<li><b>Cyclic model</b>: the stages form a circle, and you repeat steps until the creative process is complete.</li>\n<li><b>Stepped with immediate feedback loops</b>: each step can feed back to the previous step, so problems are fixed as you go until the process is fully completed.</li>\n<li><b>Branched network</b>: after analysis and definition the process splits into several possible ideas, which converge at selection; implementation then leads to evaluation and re-analysis. Paths can be explored and revisited.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, Malik at the Island Pulse Media uses a stepped model for a 30-second Carnival ad, checking each storyboard frame with Zara before moving on, while Maya uses a branched network to try three website layouts for a boutique on Old Street in Roseau at once.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Compare these models as a group and <b>justify</b> which best fits your project. Your SBA proposal earns marks for naming a model and showing evidence of every phase, so say which model you used and why.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The creative process has seven stages, from agreeing to do the job to checking whether it worked. Teams move through them in patterns called models. A linear model goes one step at a time. A cyclic model goes round in a circle. A stepped model lets you go back a step to fix things. A branched network tries several ideas at once, like a cook in Castries testing three sauces.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Exam questions often ask you to <b>justify</b> a model, so link the model to the project's risk and deadline. A <b>linear</b> model suits a small, well-understood job with fixed sign-offs, such as resizing a client's logo for new signs. A <b>branched network</b> suits a new product where the best idea is unknown, but it costs more time because several options are developed in parallel. A <b>stepped model with feedback loops</b> is common in animation, where each storyboard frame or animatic is checked before moving on. A common misconception is that a team must use only one model. Real teams often mix them, for example branching at ideation and then working step by step during implementation.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match each stage to its description","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is labelling the project wall for a breadfruit festival campaign in Kingstown. Match each stage of the creative process to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"Commit to the task; identify the time, energy, resources and responsibility needed"},{"l":"Analysis","r":"Research the idea and its market; speak to the people the product will be useful to"},{"l":"Definition","r":"Restate the problem as a clear goal; set the scope, timelines and milestones"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"Generate ideas and keep a log of all of them, even those that seem irrelevant now"},{"l":"Selection","r":"Compare options on feasibility and risk; choose the one that best meets the objectives"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"Take the plunge: sequence the tasks in a Gantt chart and use resources to execute them"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Gather feedback from the audience and clients, often with a rubric, to see what to improve"}],"win":"Excellent. You can describe all seven stages of the creative process, from acceptance to evaluation.","tutor":[{"h":"Research or scope?","p":"<p>One stage collects information and opinions; the next uses it to state the goal and plan the scope.</p>"},{"h":"Many, then one","p":"<p>One stage keeps every idea; the next compares them and picks the best.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the stages (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"Commit to the task"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"Come up with options"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"Take the plunge and build it"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Check whether it works and how to improve it"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match each stage to its description (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: the cards now show what the team actually did, not a definition. Keisha's team is running a campaign for Dominica's World Creole Music Festival. Match each stage to the action that belongs to it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"The agency signs on and estimates it needs three staff, US$2,000 and five weeks"},{"l":"Analysis","r":"The team surveys festival-goers in Roseau and reviews last year's social media comments"},{"l":"Definition","r":"The goal is written down: increase early-bird ticket sales by 15% before 1 October"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"A mind map logs twenty ideas, including a Kwéyòl phrase-of-the-day reel"},{"l":"Selection","r":"Three concepts are scored for cost, risk and reach, and the reel series scores highest"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"The reels are filmed, edited and scheduled, following the Gantt chart"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Ticket data and a feedback form show which reels worked and what to drop next year"}],"win":"Excellent. You can spot each stage of the creative process from what a team does, which is exactly how a scenario question tests it."}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Which creative process model?","q":"Maya at the Island Pulse Media is designing a booking website for the Placencia Kayak Tours. After analysis and definition she develops three different homepage ideas at the same time, brings them together to select one, and after implementation the evaluation sends her back to re-analyse. Which creative process model is she using?","options":[{"t":"Linear model","fb":"A linear model follows one line, completing each step before the next. Maya explored three paths at once."},{"t":"Cyclic model","fb":"A cyclic model repeats the stages in a circle, but it does not split into several parallel ideas."},{"t":"Branched network","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The process splits into several ideas after analysis and definition, converges at selection, and evaluation leads to re-analysis."},{"t":"Stepped with immediate feedback loops","fb":"This model feeds back to the previous step as you go. The key feature here is the split into several ideas that converge."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Look for the split","p":"<p>Which model divides into several possible ideas and then brings them back together?</p>"},{"h":"Converge","p":"<p>Options converge at selection in one particular model.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which model? (Foundation)","q":"In which model must each step be clearly completed before you move to the next?","options":[{"t":"Cyclic model","fb":"The cyclic model repeats steps in a circle."},{"t":"Linear model","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. The linear model goes one step after the other, in a line."},{"t":"Branched network","fb":"A branched network splits into several paths."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which creative process model? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Island Pulse Media is making a two-minute explainer animation for a solar-energy start-up in Georgetown, Guyana. The client must sign off each stage (script, storyboard, animatic, final cut) before the next begins, but the team also wants to fix small problems in the previous step as they go, without restarting the project. Which model BEST fits, and why?","options":[{"t":"Cyclic model, because animation always repeats the same steps.","fb":"A cyclic model repeats the whole circle of stages until the process is complete. Here the team moves forward, making small fixes at each step."},{"t":"Stepped with immediate feedback loops, because the stages are approved in sequence but each step can feed back to the previous one to fix problems as the team goes.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It keeps the ordered sign-offs and allows the small, immediate corrections the team wants."},{"t":"Branched network, because the animation has several scenes.","fb":"Several scenes are not several alternative ideas. A branched network splits into different possible solutions that converge at selection."},{"t":"Linear model, because the client signs off each stage in order.","fb":"Ordered sign-off fits a linear model, but a linear model does not feed back to fix the previous step. The team wants both."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"The role of pre-production","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Pre-production</b> is the planning phase of any digital media project. It lays the foundation for smooth execution and high-quality output, regardless of whether the content is a video, image or audio piece.</p>\n<p><b>(a) Key roles of pre-production</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Concept development</b>: refining ideas, storyboarding, scripting or outlining the creative vision.</li>\n<li><b>Logistical planning</b>: scheduling, budgeting, scouting locations and assembling the right team.</li>\n<li><b>Risk reduction</b>: identifying potential issues ahead of time (technical, legal or safety) and planning around them.</li>\n<li><b>Creative alignment</b>: ensuring all team members share a unified understanding of the project's goals and style.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pre-production helps <b>avoid costly mistakes</b> during production and post-production.</p>\n<p><b>(b) Core elements of pre-production</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Barbados example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Resources</b></td><td><b>Money (budget)</b>: determines the scale, equipment, crew and quality. <b>Time</b>: scheduling for shoots, editing and deadlines. <b>Personnel</b>: directors, producers, editors, photographers, sound engineers. <b>Facilities</b>: studios, post-production houses, sound booths. <b>Location</b>: where filming or recording takes place, including logistics such as travel and weather.</td><td>Malik books a sound booth for a voice-over and plans a beach shoot near Grantley Adams International Airport around the flight times and the forecast.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Clearances</b></td><td><b>Permits</b>: required for shooting in public or regulated spaces. <b>Releases</b>: consent forms from talent, interviewees or property owners.</td><td>Permission before flying a drone near the airport; release forms from vendors filmed on Swan Street in Bridgetown.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Copyright and intellectual property</b></td><td>Use of original content or proper licensing of third-party assets (music, images, scripts); avoiding infringement by making sure all material is legal and cleared.</td><td>Licensing a soca track instead of ripping it from YouTube.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Codes of practice and regulations</b></td><td>Industry standards (broadcast codes, advertising guidelines); legal and ethical compliance, such as truthful advertising and age-appropriate content.</td><td>A rum advert must not target under-18s; a hotel promo must not exaggerate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Health and safety</b></td><td>Risk assessments for filming conditions, such as working with equipment or stunts.</td><td>Checking cables, heat and sea conditions before a sunrise shoot at Bathsheba.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The Island Pulse Media keeps a one-page pre-production checklist with these five headings, and Zara will not approve a shoot until every row has a tick.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the four roles and five core elements by name. If a question asks you to \"explain the role of pre-production\", give the role, say what it involves, and finish with the benefit: avoiding costly mistakes in production and post-production.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Pre-production is the planning you do before you film, record or design anything. It has four jobs: develop the idea, plan the practical details, spot problems early and make sure the whole team agrees on the goal. It also covers five core elements: resources, clearances, copyright, codes of practice, and health and safety. Planning a school fete in Antigua works the same way: book the field, get permission and check safety first.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The strongest answers explain <b>why</b> pre-production saves money: the cost of a change rises at every stage. Moving a scene on a storyboard takes minutes; reshooting it after crew, talent and location have been paid can wipe out the budget. Notice how the core elements connect. A drone shot is a <b>resource</b> decision, but flying near Grantley Adams International Airport also needs a <b>clearance</b>, and a crowded beach adds a <b>health and safety</b> risk. A common mistake is to treat copyright as a post-production issue. Music must be licensed before editing starts, or the whole cut may need to be redone. In an \"explain\" question, give the role, what it involves and the benefit.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which core element of pre-production?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is filling in the pre-production checklist for a Sugar Mas promo video in Basseterre. Sort each task under its core element.</p>","main":"Which core element does this task belong to?","bins":["Resources","Clearances","Copyright and IP","Codes, regulations, health and safety"],"items":[{"t":"Check that every photo in the end titles is original or cleared.","bin":2,"fb":"All material used must be legal and cleared: copyright and IP."},{"t":"Book an editor and a sound engineer for two days.","bin":0,"fb":"Personnel are a resource."},{"t":"Do a risk assessment for filming on a moving float at night.","bin":3,"fb":"Risk assessments for filming conditions are health and safety."},{"t":"License the calypso track used as background music.","bin":2,"fb":"Properly licensing third-party music protects against copyright infringement."},{"t":"Make sure the sponsor's claims in the advert are truthful.","bin":3,"fb":"Truthful advertising is legal and ethical compliance under codes of practice."},{"t":"Collect signed consent forms from the troupe members filmed.","bin":1,"fb":"Releases (consent forms) from talent and interviewees are clearances."},{"t":"Get a permit to film in a public area during the parade.","bin":1,"fb":"Permits for public or regulated spaces are clearances."},{"t":"Set a budget of US$1,500 for crew, gear hire and editing.","bin":0,"fb":"Money (budget) is a resource: it decides scale, equipment, crew and quality."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can place pre-production tasks under resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice and health and safety.","tutor":[{"h":"Permission from whom?","p":"<p>Permission from authorities or people filmed is one element; permission to use someone else's creative work is another.</p>"},{"h":"Money, time, people, places","p":"<p>These all belong to the same core element.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Core elements (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Set the project budget.","bin":0,"fb":"Money is a resource."},{"t":"Get a filming permit.","bin":1,"fb":"Permits are clearances."},{"t":"License the background music.","bin":2,"fb":"Licensing protects copyright."},{"t":"Do a risk assessment for the shoot.","bin":3,"fb":"Risk assessment is health and safety."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which core element of pre-production? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Five bins now: codes of practice and health and safety are separate. Keisha is planning a music video shoot at Pigeon Island, St. Lucia, with two 16-year-old dancers. Sort each task under its core element.</p>","main":"Which core element does this task belong to?","bins":["Resources","Clearances","Copyright and IP","Codes of practice and regulations","Health and safety"],"items":[{"t":"Confirm that the sample used in the beat has been cleared by its original producer.","bin":2,"fb":"Using someone else's recording legally is a copyright and IP matter."},{"t":"Make sure no alcohol brand appears in a way that appeals to under-18 viewers.","bin":3,"fb":"Age-appropriate content is a code of practice and advertising rule."},{"t":"Hire a gimbal and two LED panels for the weekend.","bin":0,"fb":"Equipment hire is a resource, paid for from the budget."},{"t":"Book the boat to the location, with a back-up date in case the sea is rough.","bin":0,"fb":"Location logistics, travel and weather planning are part of resources."},{"t":"Get written permission from the site's managers to film there.","bin":1,"fb":"A permit to film in a regulated space is a clearance."},{"t":"Keep the licence for the stock drone clip used in the intro.","bin":2,"fb":"Third-party footage must be properly licensed, and the licence kept as proof."},{"t":"Assess the risk of filming dancers on wet rocks and plan first aid.","bin":4,"fb":"A risk assessment for filming conditions is health and safety."},{"t":"Tape down cables and keep the lights away from the water's edge.","bin":4,"fb":"Protecting crew from trips and electrical hazards is health and safety."},{"t":"Have the parents of the two 16-year-old dancers sign release forms.","bin":1,"fb":"Releases (consent forms) for people filmed are clearances. Minors need a parent or guardian to sign."},{"t":"Remove the claim that the beach is \"never crowded\" unless it is true.","bin":3,"fb":"Truthful advertising is a code of practice."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated legal permissions (clearances) from ownership of creative material (copyright and IP), and rules about content (codes of practice) from risks to people (health and safety)."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Problem definition: purpose and goal","html":"<p>Every strong digital media product starts with a clearly <b>defined problem</b>. If the problem is vague, the product will be vague. Problem definition answers four questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What is the challenge?</b> The real need or gap, described from the user's point of view.</li>\n<li><b>Who has the problem?</b> The people affected (this leads into audience analysis).</li>\n<li><b>What is the purpose?</b> What the product must do: inform, educate, persuade, entertain, sell or connect.</li>\n<li><b>What is the goal?</b> A measurable result that tells you whether the product worked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Problem statement:</b> one or two sentences that name the user, the need and the reason it matters, without naming the solution. A useful pattern is: \"[User] needs a way to [need] because [insight].\"</p>\n<p>Compare these two statements written by Zara, creative director at the Island Pulse Media, for a client, Bay Street Craft Market in Nassau:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Weak:</b> \"The market needs a cool app.\" This names a solution, gives no user and cannot be measured.</li>\n<li><b>Strong:</b> \"Cruise visitors who have only four hours in Nassau need a quick way to find local craft stalls because most leave the port without visiting the market. Goal: increase visitors to the market by 20% in the next cruise season.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice that the strong version does not say \"app\". Keeping the solution open allows ideation to find the best format, which might be a QR-code map at the Nassau cruise port, an Instagram Reel series or a printed flyer with a link.</p>\n<p><b>Setting a good goal.</b> Many designers use <b>SMART</b> goals: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. \"Make people like the market\" is not SMART; \"Get 500 map scans at the cruise pier between November and April\" is.</p>\n<p><b>The design brief.</b> The problem statement and goal are usually written into a <b>design brief</b>, the agreed document between client and designer. A brief normally includes the background, problem statement, purpose, target audience, deliverables (for example, a 30-second video and three social posts), budget, deadline and constraints such as brand colours or data limits.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, if a question says \"state the problem the product addresses\", write a user-centred problem statement, not a description of the product. Examiners reward the user, the need and the reason.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Before you design anything, say clearly what the problem is. A good problem statement names who has the problem, what they need and why. It does not name the solution yet. Then set a goal you can measure. For example: \"Visitors to Oistins need to find the Friday fish fry stalls quickly because many get lost. Goal: 300 map scans in one month.\"</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A Grade One candidate keeps the <b>purpose</b> (what the product must do, such as inform or persuade) separate from the <b>goal</b> (the measurable result). Many weak answers mix them up, or give a goal that cannot be checked, such as \"raise awareness\". Make awareness measurable: \"70% of surveyed beachgoers can name two turtle rules after watching\". Also watch for the hidden solution: \"people need an app to...\" has already chosen the format, which closes off ideation. In a design brief the problem statement links straight to the evaluation stage, because the goal you set now becomes the test you apply at the end.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choose the strongest problem statement","q":"Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad is worried that beachgoers disturb nesting turtles. Which is the STRONGEST problem statement for a digital media product?","options":[{"t":"Turtles are important to the Caribbean.","fb":"This is a true background fact, but it does not state who has the problem, what they need or what success looks like."},{"t":"The group wants a beautiful website with lots of turtle photos.","fb":"This describes a product and its style, not the user problem. A beautiful website might not change anyone's behaviour."},{"t":"We need to make a TikTok video about turtles.","fb":"This names a solution before the problem is understood, and it has no user or measurable goal."},{"t":"Beachgoers at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many disturb nests without realising it; goal: reduce reported disturbances by half during the next nesting season.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It names the users, the need and the insight, does not lock in a solution, and has a measurable, time-bound goal."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Four parts","p":"<p>Look for a user, a need, a reason and a measurable goal.</p>"},{"h":"Solution trap","p":"<p>A problem statement should not jump straight to the format (video, website, app).</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Strongest problem statement (Foundation)","q":"Which is a good problem statement?","options":[{"t":"We need a turtle video.","fb":"This names a solution, not the problem."},{"t":"Beachgoers need to learn how to avoid turtle nests because many disturb them by accident.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. It names the user, the need and the reason."},{"t":"Turtles are nice.","fb":"This is an opinion, not a problem statement."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choose the strongest problem statement (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A youth group in Basseterre, St. Kitts, wants a digital product to reduce litter on the J'ouvert route. All four statements below sound reasonable. Which one would earn FULL marks as a problem statement with a goal?","options":[{"t":"The youth group needs to reduce litter by 25% next year because the streets are dirty.","fb":"The goal is measurable, but the user is the group, not the revellers who drop the litter, and the reason gives no insight into why it happens."},{"t":"J'ouvert revellers in Basseterre need a quick way to find the nearest bin along the route because most litter is dropped where no bin is in sight; goal: cut next year's clean-up time by 25%.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It names the users, the need and an insight from research, leaves the format open and sets a measurable, time-bound goal."},{"t":"Revellers need an Instagram filter that shows bin locations because filters are fun; goal: get lots of downloads.","fb":"It names a solution (an Instagram filter) before ideation, and \"lots of downloads\" is neither measurable nor linked to litter."},{"t":"J'ouvert revellers in Basseterre need to know where bins are because litter is a problem.","fb":"Close, but the reason is vague and there is no measurable, time-bound goal."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Target audience and market analysis","html":"<p>The <b>target audience</b> is the specific group of people a product is designed for. The <b>target market</b> is the wider group of potential users or buyers, including their size and the competition. You are expected to analyse both before designing.</p>\n<p><b>Ways to describe an audience</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Demographics</b></td><td>Measurable facts about people</td><td>Age, gender, location, income, education, language, occupation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Psychographics</b></td><td>Values, interests, attitudes and lifestyle</td><td>Loves soca, eco-conscious, values family, follows fitness trends</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Behaviours</b></td><td>What people actually do</td><td>Uses WhatsApp daily, shops at night, buys data by the day, watches Reels with the sound off</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Needs and pain points</b></td><td>What frustrates or motivates them</td><td>\"I can't find bus times\", \"Data is expensive\", \"I want local news fast\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Technical context</b></td><td>Devices, connectivity and skills</td><td>Mid-range Android phone, patchy signal in the hills, low digital literacy</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Research methods.</b> <b>Primary research</b> is information you collect yourself: surveys (Google Forms), interviews, focus groups and observation. <b>Secondary research</b> uses existing data: reports from the ITU, GSMA or World Bank, statistics offices, competitor websites and app store reviews. In your SBA, primary research with real local users is strong evidence.</p>\n<p><b>Market analysis</b> asks: How big is the market? Who are the competitors and what do they do badly? What makes your product different (its <b>unique selling point</b>)? Keisha at the Island Pulse Media often runs a quick competitor check by searching the App Store, Google Play and Instagram for similar products in Jamaica, Curaçao and Barbados.</p>\n<p><b>User personas.</b> Designers turn research into a <b>persona</b>: an imagined but realistic profile of a typical user. For example, \"Andre, 17, Sixth Form student in May Pen; Android phone; buys data weekly; uses Instagram and WhatsApp; wants quick, visual information.\" Personas keep the whole team designing for real people instead of for themselves.</p>\n<p>Audience analysis drives every later decision: language and tone, colours, file sizes, video length, platform (TikTok or Facebook?) and accessibility features such as captions.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to \"describe the target audience\", give at least one demographic, one psychographic or behavioural detail and one technical detail, then <b>explain</b> how each affects the design.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The target audience is the group your product is made for. Describe them with facts (age, where they live), values (what they care about), behaviours (what they do) and the devices they use. You find this out by asking them directly, with surveys and interviews, or by reading reports. A bakery in Kingston might find its audience is office workers aged 25 to 40 who order lunch on WhatsApp.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Strong analysis turns each audience fact into a design decision. \"Uses budget Android phones with prepaid data\" should lead to small file sizes and a web page rather than a large app. \"Watches Reels with the sound off\" should lead to captions. Without that link, an audience description earns few marks. Know the limits of each research method: an online survey is quick but only reaches people who are already online, while observation shows what people actually do rather than what they say they do. A persona summarises research, so a persona built on guesswork is a common weakness. Base it on real survey or interview data from the territory you are designing for.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour?","intro":"<p>Keisha is building a persona for Fish Fry Hop food-tour app in Providenciales. Sort each research finding.</p>","main":"What kind of audience information is this?","bins":["Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviour"],"items":[{"t":"Users see themselves as adventurous foodies.","bin":1,"fb":"Self-image and lifestyle are psychographic."},{"t":"Users read reviews on TripAdvisor before choosing.","bin":2,"fb":"Reading reviews before deciding is a behaviour."},{"t":"Users book tours on their phones in the evening.","bin":2,"fb":"This describes what people actually do, so it is a behaviour."},{"t":"Most users have a household income above the regional average.","bin":0,"fb":"Income is a measurable demographic factor."},{"t":"Most users are aged 25 to 44.","bin":0,"fb":"Age is a measurable fact about people, so it is demographic."},{"t":"Users share food photos on Instagram during the meal.","bin":2,"fb":"Sharing photos is an action they take, so it is a behaviour."},{"t":"Many visitors come from the United States and Canada.","bin":0,"fb":"Location or country of origin is demographic."},{"t":"Users value authentic local food over chain restaurants.","bin":1,"fb":"Values and attitudes are psychographic."}],"win":"Well sorted. Demographics are measurable facts, psychographics are values and lifestyle, and behaviours are what people actually do. A good persona uses all three.","tutor":[{"h":"Facts, feelings, actions","p":"<p>Demographic = facts you could count. Psychographic = what people value or believe. Behaviour = what they do.</p>"},{"h":"Test it","p":"<p>Can you see it happening? If yes, it is probably a behaviour.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Audience information (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Users are aged 25 to 44.","bin":0,"fb":"Age is demographic."},{"t":"Users value authentic local food.","bin":1,"fb":"Values are psychographic."},{"t":"Users book on their phones at night.","bin":2,"fb":"An action is a behaviour."},{"t":"Users see themselves as adventurous.","bin":1,"fb":"Self-image is psychographic."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha is now building a full persona for a CSEC revision app for secondary students in Tobago. Sort each research finding into one of FIVE kinds of audience information. Some cards are close: read them carefully.</p>","main":"What kind of audience information is this?","bins":["Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviour","Technical context","Need or pain point"],"items":[{"t":"They believe passing CSEC Mathematics is the key to a good future.","bin":1,"fb":"A belief or attitude is psychographic."},{"t":"\"I can never find past-paper answers that explain the working.\"","bin":4,"fb":"An unmet need is a pain point, and it points straight to a feature."},{"t":"They revise in short bursts on the bus home.","bin":2,"fb":"This is what they actually do: a behaviour."},{"t":"Most speak English at home and some also speak Spanish.","bin":0,"fb":"Language is a measurable demographic fact."},{"t":"They skip any video that is longer than three minutes.","bin":2,"fb":"An observed action is a behaviour."},{"t":"Most use budget Android phones with 32 GB of storage.","bin":3,"fb":"Devices and their limits are technical context."},{"t":"The mobile signal is weak in some villages on the windward coast.","bin":3,"fb":"Connectivity is technical context."},{"t":"Most respondents are aged 15 to 17 and attend school in Scarborough.","bin":0,"fb":"Age and location are measurable facts: demographic."},{"t":"\"My data runs out before the end of the week.\"","bin":4,"fb":"A frustration the user states in their own words is a pain point."},{"t":"They admire local YouTubers who explain topics in a relaxed way.","bin":1,"fb":"Interests and tastes are psychographic."}],"win":"Excellent. A Grade One persona uses all five kinds of information, and each one can be turned into a design decision."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Brainstorming: mind mapping, SCAMPER and rapid ideation","html":"<p><b>Brainstorming</b> is the ideation stage: generating as many ideas as possible before judging them. The golden rules are: go for quantity, defer judgement, build on others' ideas and welcome wild ideas. Judging comes later, in the selection stage.</p>\n<p><b>1. Mind mapping</b> (popularised by Tony Buzan) places the central problem in the middle of the page and branches out into themes, then sub-ideas. It shows connections between ideas visually. Tools include paper and markers, Miro, Canva Whiteboards and Coggle.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Mind map with a central idea and four branches\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"75\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"22\"/>\n<line x1=\"110\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"30\"/>\n<line x1=\"110\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"120\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"79\">Carnival promo</text>\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"24\">Audience</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"24\">Platforms</text>\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"136\">Visual style</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"136\">Content ideas</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p><b>2. SCAMPER</b> (developed by Bob Eberle from Alex Osborn's idea checklist) takes an existing product and asks seven question prompts:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Letter</th><th>Prompt</th><th>Example: the Daily Bake bakery's printed menu</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>ubstitute</td><td>What can be swapped?</td><td>Replace the paper menu with a QR-code menu.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>ombine</td><td>What can be merged?</td><td>Combine the menu with WhatsApp ordering.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>dapt</td><td>What can be borrowed from elsewhere?</td><td>Adapt a food-delivery app's photo cards.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>M</b>odify</td><td>What can be enlarged, shrunk or changed?</td><td>Magnify the photos of johnny cakes and coconut tarts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>P</b>ut to another use</td><td>Who else could use it?</td><td>Reuse menu photos as Instagram posts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>liminate</td><td>What can be removed?</td><td>Remove items that rarely sell to simplify choice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>R</b>everse / Rearrange</td><td>What if the order changed?</td><td>Show today's specials first instead of last.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>3. Rapid ideation</b> means producing many ideas very quickly under a time limit. A popular method is <b>Crazy 8s</b>: fold a sheet into eight panels and sketch eight different ideas in eight minutes. Speed stops you from over-thinking and polishing too early.</p>\n<p>Other techniques include <b>brainwriting</b> (everyone writes ideas silently, then passes them on), <b>word association</b> and <b>reverse brainstorming</b> (\"How could we make this problem worse?\").</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to \"explain\" a brainstorming technique, describe how it works <b>and</b> how it helps the designer. For example: \"SCAMPER helps by giving a checklist of prompts, so the team improves an existing product systematically instead of waiting for inspiration.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Brainstorming means coming up with lots of ideas before you judge any of them. A mind map puts the problem in the middle and branches out. SCAMPER uses seven prompts, such as Substitute and Combine, to improve something that already exists. Crazy 8s means sketching eight ideas in eight minutes. A roti shop in San Fernando could use SCAMPER to turn its paper menu into a WhatsApp menu.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choose the technique to fit the problem. A <b>mind map</b> suits a brand-new brief because it explores themes and connections. <b>SCAMPER</b> works best when an existing product needs improving, since every prompt starts from something real. <b>Rapid ideation</b> beats perfectionism: a team that sketches twenty rough ideas usually finds a better one than a team that polishes its first idea. A common exam mistake is to name a technique without explaining <b>how</b> it generates ideas. Another is to judge ideas during brainstorming, when criticism belongs in the selection stage. Groupthink is a real risk, so methods such as brainwriting let quieter team members contribute before louder voices take over.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the SCAMPER prompt","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is using SCAMPER to improve a Castries community library's old storytime videos. Match each SCAMPER prompt to his idea.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Replace the live-action narrator with an animated pelican character"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Merge the storytime video with a short quiz at the end"},{"l":"Adapt","r":"Borrow the \"choose your path\" idea from interactive video games"},{"l":"Modify","r":"Shorten each 20-minute video into 3-minute episodes"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Use the recorded audio as a podcast for car journeys"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Remove the long title sequence at the start"},{"l":"Reverse / Rearrange","r":"Start with the story's exciting ending, then go back to the beginning"}],"win":"Excellent. You can now use SCAMPER as a structured checklist to improve an existing product, which is exactly how designers use it.","tutor":[{"h":"Swap or merge?","p":"<p>Substitute swaps one thing for another. Combine joins two things together.</p>"},{"h":"Change size or remove?","p":"<p>Modify changes something (shorter, bigger). Eliminate takes it away completely.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match SCAMPER (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Replace the narrator with a cartoon character"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Add a quiz to the end of the video"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Remove the long title sequence"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Turn the audio into a podcast"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the SCAMPER prompt (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder ideas, and some prompts look alike. Malik is improving a Belize City tour company's printed walking-tour leaflet. Match each SCAMPER prompt to the idea that fits it BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Swap the printed leaflet for a QR code at each stop that plays a 60-second audio clip"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Join the walking tour with a food-tasting stop and sell them as one ticket"},{"l":"Adapt","r":"Borrow the badge-collecting idea from fitness apps so walkers unlock a stamp at each site"},{"l":"Modify","r":"Enlarge the map and add bold colour-coded routes for visitors with poor eyesight"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Offer the tour videos to schools as a local history lesson"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Drop the three least-visited stops so the tour fits into one hour"},{"l":"Reverse / Rearrange","r":"Start the tour at the water taxi terminal, where visitors arrive, instead of ending there"}],"win":"Excellent. You told apart prompts that look alike: Substitute swaps one part for another, Adapt borrows an idea from somewhere else, and Modify changes size, shape or emphasis."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Planning designs: rough sketches, mood boards and style guides","html":"<p>Once an idea is selected, designers plan how it will <b>look and feel</b> before producing anything. Three planning documents are named in the course outline.</p>\n<p><b>1. Rough sketches</b></p>\n<p>Quick, loose drawings that explore layout, composition and characters. They are deliberately unfinished. <b>Thumbnails</b> are very small sketches used to try many layouts fast; <b>roughs</b> are larger and more developed. Sketches are cheap: Zara at the Island Pulse Media will sketch twenty poster layouts on paper in the time it takes to build one in Illustrator. Use pencil and paper, a black marker for final lines, or a tablet in Krita or Procreate. Annotate sketches with notes about colours, fonts and sizes.</p>\n<p><b>2. Mood boards</b></p>\n<p>A <b>mood board</b> is a collage of images, colours, textures, fonts and words that captures the intended <b>feeling</b> and visual direction of a project. It is an inspiration tool, not a final design. A mood board for a Vincy Mas promo might include photos of costumes, feathers and sequins, a bright tropical palette, bold display type and words such as \"energy\", \"heritage\" and \"jump-up\". Tools include Canva, Pinterest boards, Milanote and Figma. Mood boards help a client agree on the direction early, before money is spent.</p>\n<p><b>3. Style guides</b></p>\n<p>A <b>style guide</b> (or brand guide) is the rulebook that keeps every part of a product consistent. It usually specifies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Logo</b> usage: versions, minimum size and clear space.</li>\n<li><b>Colour palette</b> with exact values: hex for screens, CMYK for print (you will learn these in the next unit).</li>\n<li><b>Typography</b>: fonts, sizes and weights for headings and body text.</li>\n<li><b>Imagery</b>: photo style, icon style and illustration style.</li>\n<li><b>Tone of voice</b>: friendly, formal, playful.</li>\n<li><b>UI components</b> for websites and apps: buttons, spacing, grids.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Rough sketch</th><th>Mood board</th><th>Style guide</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Explore layout and ideas</td><td>Set the feeling and direction</td><td>Fix the rules for consistency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Finish</td><td>Loose, unfinished</td><td>Collage of found material</td><td>Precise and exact values</td></tr>\n<tr><td>When</td><td>Early ideation and planning</td><td>Early, before designs</td><td>Before and during production</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may ask you to <b>draw</b> a rough sketch or storyboard with pencil and black marker. Label it clearly: title, dimensions, colour notes and the position of text. Neat labels earn marks even if your drawing is simple.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Designers plan the look before they build. Rough sketches are quick drawings to try out layouts. A mood board is a collage of pictures, colours and words that shows the feeling you want. A style guide is the rulebook, with exact fonts, colours and logo rules. Think of a Carnival band: sketches of costumes, a mood board of feathers and colours, then exact rules for the people sewing.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The three documents answer different questions. Sketches ask \"what could it look like?\", mood boards ask \"how should it feel?\" and style guides state \"how must it always look?\". A mood board is not a design: copying its images straight into a final product can breach copyright, so it guides direction only. Style guides matter most when several people work on a product over time; a tourism authority in St. Kitts may use one logo and palette across its website, buses and Instagram for years. In Paper 02, label sketches with dimensions, colour notes and text positions. When you write a style guide entry, give exact values, such as a hex code and a font size, not just \"blue\" or \"big\".</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is planning a website for the Punda Market Collective in Willemstad. Sort each planning item.</p>","main":"Which planning document does this belong in?","bins":["Rough sketch","Mood board","Style guide"],"items":[{"t":"Primary colour: #E07A1F; never place the logo on a busy photo.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact colour values and logo rules are style guide content."},{"t":"Eight tiny thumbnail layouts drawn in eight minutes.","bin":0,"fb":"Thumbnails are small rough sketches for exploring many layouts."},{"t":"All buttons have rounded corners and 12 px padding.","bin":2,"fb":"Rules for UI components keep the site consistent, so they belong in the style guide."},{"t":"Heading font: Montserrat Bold, 32 px; body: Open Sans, 16 px.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact typography rules belong in the style guide."},{"t":"Words like \"fresh\", \"bilingual\" and \"market morning\" beside fabric textures.","bin":1,"fb":"Mood words and textures set the direction, so this is a mood board."},{"t":"A quick pencil drawing showing the menu bar at the top and three product boxes below.","bin":0,"fb":"A loose layout drawing that explores structure is a rough sketch."},{"t":"Photos of spice stalls, woven baskets and sunset colours pinned together.","bin":1,"fb":"A collage of inspiring images that captures a feeling is a mood board."},{"t":"A loose drawing of the homepage hero area with notes saying \"photo here\".","bin":0,"fb":"An annotated, unfinished layout is a rough sketch."}],"win":"Well done. Rough sketches explore, mood boards inspire and set the feeling, and style guides fix exact rules for consistency.","tutor":[{"h":"Exact numbers","p":"<p>If it gives precise values (hex codes, pixel sizes, font names), it is a rule.</p>"},{"h":"Feeling or layout?","p":"<p>Collected images and words that set a mood are different from your own drawing of a layout.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Planning documents (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A quick pencil drawing of a page layout.","bin":0,"fb":"Rough sketch."},{"t":"A collage of inspiring photos and colours.","bin":1,"fb":"Mood board."},{"t":"Heading font: Montserrat Bold, 32 px.","bin":2,"fb":"Style guide rule."},{"t":"Primary colour: #E07A1F.","bin":2,"fb":"Style guide rule."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Colours, fonts and layouts appear in all three documents, so ask whether each item <b>explores</b>, <b>inspires</b> or <b>fixes an exact rule</b>. Maya is planning a website for a reggae festival in Montego Bay.</p>","main":"Which planning document does this belong in?","bins":["Rough sketch","Mood board","Style guide"],"items":[{"t":"Tone of voice: warm and proud; no slang in headlines.","bin":2,"fb":"Tone of voice rules are part of a style guide."},{"t":"Three loose colour swatches beside sunset photos to suggest a warm evening mood.","bin":1,"fb":"Colours without exact values, used for feeling, belong on the mood board."},{"t":"Photos of old sound-system posters, red, gold and green fabric and the word \"roots\".","bin":1,"fb":"A collage of images and words that sets the feeling is a mood board."},{"t":"An annotated rough of the line-up page, with a note saying \"photos to be decided\".","bin":0,"fb":"Still open and unfinished, so it is a rough sketch."},{"t":"A pencil layout of the ticket page with arrows saying \"button goes here\".","bin":0,"fb":"An annotated, unfinished layout explores structure: a rough sketch."},{"t":"Six stamp-sized thumbnail layouts of the homepage.","bin":0,"fb":"Thumbnails explore many layouts quickly: rough sketches."},{"t":"The logo must always have clear space around it equal to the height of its first letter.","bin":2,"fb":"Logo clear-space rules belong in the style guide."},{"t":"A photo of a hand-painted dancehall sign, pinned to suggest a hand-made feel.","bin":1,"fb":"It inspires a direction without fixing a rule: mood board."},{"t":"Primary green is #1B7F3B on screen, with a matching CMYK value for printed posters.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact colour values are a style guide rule."}],"win":"Excellent. The same topic, such as colour, can appear in every document. What matters is whether it explores (sketch), inspires (mood board) or fixes an exact rule (style guide)."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Storyboarding: planning the visual sequence","html":"<p>A <b>storyboard</b> is a sequence of drawn frames (panels) that shows what the audience will see and hear, shot by shot, in a video, animation or interactive product. The SBA mark scheme rewards a storyboard, rough design, wireframe or mock-up.</p>\n<p><b>What each storyboard frame should include</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Frame or scene number</b> and the <b>duration</b> (for example, Scene 3, 0:08 to 0:12).</li>\n<li>A <b>sketch</b> of the visual: subject, background and composition.</li>\n<li><b>Shot type</b>: extreme long shot (ELS), long shot (LS), medium shot (MS), close-up (CU), extreme close-up (ECU).</li>\n<li><b>Camera angle and movement</b>: high angle, low angle, eye level; pan, tilt, zoom, tracking.</li>\n<li><b>Audio</b>: dialogue or narration, music and sound effects.</li>\n<li><b>On-screen text</b> or graphics, and the <b>transition</b> to the next frame (cut, fade, dissolve).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Example: a 30-second ad for Rum Point Bay Tours in the Cayman Islands (your SBA ad must also be 30 seconds)</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Frame</th><th>Time</th><th>Visual and shot</th><th>Audio</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>0:00 to 0:05</td><td>ELS, drone pan over the North Sound</td><td>Soft steel pan music fades in</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>0:05 to 0:12</td><td>MS, smiling guide on the boat, eye level</td><td>VO: \"Discover the island like a local.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>0:12 to 0:20</td><td>CU, guests tasting Cayman rum cake; quick cuts</td><td>Laughter, music builds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>0:20 to 0:30</td><td>Logo, website and WhatsApp number on brand colour; fade out</td><td>VO: \"Book today.\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Related planning tools</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Script</b>: the written words, dialogue and directions. A storyboard is usually drawn from a script.</li>\n<li><b>Shot list</b>: a checklist of every shot needed, used on the day of filming.</li>\n<li><b>Animatic</b>: the storyboard frames timed to a rough soundtrack, to test pacing before animation (common in studios that work with Blender or Adobe Animate).</li>\n<li><b>Wireframe and flowchart</b>: for websites and apps, the \"storyboard\" is a set of screen layouts plus a flowchart showing how users move between screens (Modules 2 and 3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Storyboards let the whole team, and the client, agree on the sequence before anyone films. Malik at the Island Pulse Media says one hour of storyboarding saves a day of reshoots.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, a storyboard with stick figures still scores well if every frame has a number, timing, shot type and audio note. Drawing skill is not the main thing being marked; planning is.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A storyboard is a set of drawn boxes that shows what the viewer will see and hear, one shot at a time. Each frame has a number, a time, a sketch, the shot type, the sound and any words on screen. It works like a comic strip plan. A team filming a cricket promo in Guyana would storyboard the bowler's run-up, the shot and the crowd before filming.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A storyboard is a communication tool, so clarity matters more than drawing skill. Show camera movement with arrows, mark the transition between frames and give timings that add up to the total length: a 30-second advert whose frames add up to 42 seconds shows poor planning. Grade One answers also show how the documents link. The script supplies the words, the storyboard shows the visuals, the shot list turns the frames into a checklist for the shoot and the animatic tests the timing. For interactive products a storyboard alone is not enough, because users choose their own path; a wireframe and a flowchart are needed to show the screens and how users move between them.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"What belongs in a storyboard frame?","intro":"<p>Malik is checking a student's storyboard for a 30-second Emancipation Day video for the Guyana Heritage Collective. Pick the details that a well-annotated storyboard frame should include.</p>","q":"Select ALL the details that belong in a storyboard frame annotation.","lines":["Scene 2, 0:06 to 0:12","Close-up of hands beating a drum, low angle","Narration: \"We remember, we rise.\"","Transition: slow dissolve to Scene 3","The total budget for the whole project","A list of every team member's phone number","On-screen text: \"1 July: Emancipation Day\"","The client's invoice and payment terms"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,6],"win":"Correct. A storyboard frame shows number and timing, the visual with shot type and angle, audio, on-screen text and the transition. Budgets, contacts and invoices belong in other planning documents.","miss":"Not quite. Ask: does this detail describe what the audience will see or hear at this moment? Budgets, phone lists and invoices are project management documents, not storyboard content.","tutor":[{"h":"See and hear","p":"<p>A storyboard frame records what appears on screen and what is heard, and when.</p>"},{"h":"Other documents","p":"<p>Money and contacts belong in the budget and the work plan.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Storyboard details (Foundation)","q":"Select the details that belong in a storyboard frame.","lines":["Scene 2, 0:06 to 0:12","Close-up of a drum, low angle","The total project budget","Narration: \"We remember, we rise.\""],"correct":[0,1,3],"win":"Yes. Timing, shot and audio belong in the frame.","miss":"The budget is not part of a storyboard frame. Try again."},
 "up": {"title":"What belongs in a storyboard frame? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Malik is checking a storyboard for a 30-second Emancipation Day video. This time some details belong in OTHER planning documents: the production schedule, the equipment list, the clearances file or the budget. Pick only what belongs in a storyboard frame.</p>","q":"Select ALL the details that belong in a storyboard frame annotation, and none that belong in other planning documents.","lines":["Frame 4, 0:15 to 0:20","MS, low angle, slow tilt up from the drum to the drummer's face","SFX: crowd cheering under the music","Cut to Frame 5","Caption: \"Emancipation Day, Georgetown, 1 August\"","Crew call time: 6:30 a.m. at the National Park","Equipment: two lavalier microphones and one gimbal","Release forms collected from all the drummers","Narration: \"Our story, our rhythm.\"","The editor's day rate and invoice date"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,8],"win":"Correct. Frame number and timing, the shot with its angle and movement, sound, captions, narration and the transition all describe what the audience sees and hears. Call times go in the production schedule, gear in the equipment list, releases in the clearances file and rates in the budget.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask: does it describe what the audience will see or hear at this moment? Call times, equipment, release forms and rates are real planning details, but they belong in other documents."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Timelines, Gantt charts and the production schedule","html":"<p>You must be able to set <b>timelines</b>, a <b>production schedule</b> and a <b>work plan</b>, and use <b>Gantt charts</b> and <b>milestones</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Timeline</b>: the overall sequence of phases and dates from start to launch.</li>\n<li><b>Milestone</b>: a key checkpoint with no duration, such as \"storyboard approved\" or \"proposal submitted\".</li>\n<li><b>Production schedule</b>: the detailed day-by-day plan of what is produced, where, when and by whom (for example, filming at Brimstone Hill Fortress on Saturday at 7 a.m. before the heat and crowds).</li>\n<li><b>Work plan</b>: lists every task, who is responsible, the resources needed and the deadline. In group SBAs it shows each member's contribution.</li>\n<li><b>Gantt chart</b>: a bar chart (named after Henry Gantt, early 1900s) with tasks listed down the side and time across the top. Each bar shows when a task starts and ends; overlapping bars show tasks running at the same time.</li>\n</ul>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:460px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Simple Gantt chart with five tasks across four weeks\">\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\">\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"14\">Wk 1</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"14\">Wk 2</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"14\">Wk 3</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"14\">Wk 4</text>\n<text x=\"4\" y=\"36\">Research</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"60\">Storyboard</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"84\">Filming</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"108\">Editing</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"132\">Launch</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"26\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\"/><rect x=\"140\" y=\"50\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\"/>\n<rect x=\"190\" y=\"74\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"98\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\"/>\n<path d=\"M300 118 l8 8 l-8 8 l-8 -8 z\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"140\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>The diamond on the last row is a common symbol for a <b>milestone</b>. Notice <b>dependencies</b>: editing cannot start until filming has begun, so its bar starts later. The longest chain of dependent tasks is the <b>critical path</b>; a delay on it delays the whole project. Build in <b>buffer time</b> for Caribbean realities such as rain on a filming day, power cuts or slow uploads.</p>\n<p><b>Planning goes further than the schedule.</b> In pre-production, teams also prepare the paperwork for the <b>core elements</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Resources</b>: a <b>budget</b> (equipment hire, software subscriptions, props, transport, data), an <b>equipment list</b> (camera or phone, tripod, microphone, lights, storage, charged batteries), personnel, facilities and locations;</li>\n<li><b>Clearances</b>: <b>permits</b> to film in public or on private property, and <b>releases</b> (model release forms) for people filmed;</li>\n<li><b>Copyright and IP</b>: music licences or royalty-free tracks, cleared images and scripts;</li>\n<li><b>Codes of practice and regulations</b>: truthful advertising, age-appropriate content and respect for culture;</li>\n<li><b>Health and safety</b>: a risk assessment when filming near roads, the sea or at night.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tools: Excel or Google Sheets (with coloured cells), Canva Gantt templates, Trello, Microsoft Planner and TeamGantt.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Progress and Process section (12 marks) rewards evidence of incremental progress. A Gantt chart updated each week, with actual versus planned dates, is excellent evidence.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A timeline shows the main phases and dates of a project. A Gantt chart is a bar chart: tasks go down the side, weeks go across the top, and each bar shows when a task starts and ends. A milestone is a key checkpoint, often drawn as a diamond. Add spare days, called buffer time, in case of rain or power cuts, as film crews in Dominica know.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key idea is <b>dependency</b>. If editing depends on filming and filming depends on the storyboard, those tasks form a chain. The longest chain of dependent tasks is the <b>critical path</b>: any delay on it moves the launch date, while a delay on a task off the critical path (such as designing a thumbnail) may not. That is why buffer time is placed before key milestones, not scattered at random. A common error is to draw every bar from Week 1 to the end, which shows no planning at all. Keep planned and actual dates side by side; the gap between them shows that the team monitored progress and adjusted the schedule.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Reading the production plan","intro":"<p>Jerome, Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are planning a promotional video for the Klein Bonaire Dive Centre. Answer each question about their planning.</p>","items":[{"text":"On their Gantt chart, \"Client approves storyboard\" is shown as a diamond with no length. This is a...","options":["dependency","milestone","work plan"],"answer":1,"fb":"A milestone is a key checkpoint with no duration, often drawn as a diamond."},{"text":"Editing cannot begin until underwater filming is complete. This relationship is called a...","options":["dependency","mood board","buffer"],"answer":0,"fb":"When one task must wait for another, the tasks are dependent."},{"text":"The document that lists each task, who is responsible and the deadline is the...","options":["storyboard","work plan","style guide"],"answer":1,"fb":"A work plan assigns tasks, people, resources and deadlines."},{"text":"Filming is booked for 7 a.m. Saturday at the reef, with a back-up date the next weekend. Where is this recorded?","options":["the mood board","the production schedule","the problem statement"],"answer":1,"fb":"The production schedule gives the detailed when, where and who of production."},{"text":"The divers who appear in the video must sign a form allowing their image to be used. This is a...","options":["safety briefing","shot list","model release form"],"answer":2,"fb":"A model release form gives legal permission to use a person's image, a key legal consideration in pre-production."},{"text":"Two extra days are added before the deadline in case of rough seas. This is...","options":["buffer time","a milestone","the critical path"],"answer":0,"fb":"Buffer time protects the deadline from delays such as bad weather."}],"win":"Excellent. You can read and explain a production plan: milestones, dependencies, work plans, schedules, buffers and legal paperwork.","tutor":[{"h":"Diamonds","p":"<p>On a Gantt chart, a diamond marks a point in time rather than a task.</p>"},{"h":"Who, what, when","p":"<p>The work plan assigns people to tasks; the production schedule fixes when and where production happens.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Production plan (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A key checkpoint on a Gantt chart with no duration is a...","options":["task","milestone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Milestones mark key points in time."},{"text":"Extra days added in case of delays are called...","options":["a dependency","buffer time"],"answer":1,"fb":"Buffer time protects the deadline."},{"text":"A form allowing a person's image to be used is a...","options":["model release form","mood board"],"answer":0,"fb":"It gives legal permission."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Reading the production plan (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder questions about dependencies, the critical path and protecting the deadline for Klein Bonaire Dive Centre's promotional video.</p>","items":[{"text":"Research (Week 1), storyboard (Week 2), filming (Weeks 3 to 4) and editing (Weeks 4 to 5) each depend on the task before. The storyboard is one week late and there is no buffer. What happens to the launch?","options":["It moves back one week because the storyboard is on the critical path","It stays the same because only one task is late","Only editing is affected, not the launch","Filming can simply start without the storyboard"],"answer":0,"fb":"Every task depends on the one before, so they all lie on the critical path. With no buffer, a one-week delay moves the launch back one week."},{"text":"The music licence and the voice-over can be done while filming is happening. On the Gantt chart these bars should...","options":["be drawn as diamonds","be placed on the critical path","come after the launch milestone","overlap with the filming weeks"],"answer":3,"fb":"Tasks that can run at the same time are shown as overlapping bars."},{"text":"Which of these is a milestone rather than a task?","options":["Recording the voice-over","Editing the rough cut","Client signs off the final cut","Filming at the reef"],"answer":2,"fb":"A sign-off is a checkpoint with no duration. The others take time, so they are tasks."},{"text":"Rain is forecast for most of the filming week in September. The BEST response in the plan is to...","options":["remove the evaluation stage to save time","start editing before filming","add buffer time and a back-up filming date to the production schedule","delete the sign-off milestone"],"answer":2,"fb":"Buffer time and a back-up date protect the deadline without cutting quality or approval."},{"text":"A group of four sixth-form students shares the work. Which document BEST shows the teacher who was responsible for each task?","options":["the mood board","the style guide","the work plan","the storyboard"],"answer":2,"fb":"A work plan names the person responsible, the resources and the deadline for every task."},{"text":"Which statement about the critical path is correct?","options":["It is the longest chain of dependent tasks, so a delay on it delays the whole project","It lists only the tasks that have buffer time","It is the shortest set of tasks in the project","It is another name for the production schedule"],"answer":0,"fb":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks; it sets the earliest possible finish date."}],"win":"Excellent. You can analyse a production plan: dependencies, overlapping tasks, milestones, buffer time, the critical path and who is responsible for what."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle","html":"<p>Even a well-planned project is shaped by things the team must manage. There are <b>seven factors affecting the creation of digital media content</b>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Technical factors</b>: equipment quality (cameras, microphones, editing software) and Internet bandwidth for online production or distribution.</li>\n<li><b>Creative direction</b>: style, tone, target audience and branding requirements; artistic vision (colour palette, sound design, visual effects).</li>\n<li><b>Budget constraints</b>: may limit location choices, talent, gear or post-production capabilities.</li>\n<li><b>Time constraints</b>: deadlines may rush planning, leading to reduced quality.</li>\n<li><b>Legal and ethical factors</b>: compliance with copyright, image rights and content regulations.</li>\n<li><b>Team collaboration</b>: communication and coordination affect the smooth flow of production.</li>\n<li><b>External conditions</b>: weather (for outdoor shoots), availability of key personnel, or current events.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, a hurricane watch in September (external condition) can cancel a beach shoot, and slow upload speeds (technical) can delay sending large video files to a client in Aruba.</p>\n<p><b>The virtuous cycle</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 220\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Virtuous cycle: media, technology, audience and impact in a loop, with product and optimise beside technology, markets and research beside impact, strategy and business development beside media\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"120\" y=\"10\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"180\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 30 Q260 40 268 92\"/><path d=\"M268 128 Q260 185 202 192\"/><path d=\"M118 192 Q60 185 52 128\"/><path d=\"M52 92 Q60 40 118 28\"/>\n<path d=\"M262 86 l6 8 l4 -9\"/><path d=\"M210 186 l-9 6 l9 4\"/><path d=\"M58 136 l-6 -8 l-4 9\"/><path d=\"M110 24 l9 4 l-8 5\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"29\">Media</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"114\">Technology</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"199\">Impact</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"114\">Audience</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"9\">Strategy, Biz Dev</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"9\">Product, Optimise</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"9\">Markets, Research</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"9\">Research, Strategy</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>In this diagram four core areas, <b>media, technology, audience and impact</b>, interact dynamically to drive growth and improvement. Around them are key business functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Product and Optimise</b> enhance technology.</li>\n<li><b>Markets and Research</b> influence impact.</li>\n<li><b>Strategy and Biz Dev</b> (business development) support media.</li>\n<li><b>Audience</b> is informed by research and strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each function feeds into and strengthens the others, creating <b>continuous feedback loops</b> that fuel innovation, efficiency and scalability. At Island Pulse Media, a new podcast (media) is streamed through an improved app (technology), grows a Caribbean listenership (audience), and listener data and sponsorship (impact) fund the next series.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss factors\" questions reward a factor, an example and its effect. Pair factors for depth: \"Budget constraints meant no drone, so the team used a phone gimbal; time constraints then left only one day for editing.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Many things can affect a media project: equipment and Internet speed, the creative style, money, time, the law, how well the team works together and outside events such as weather. The virtuous cycle shows how media, technology, audience and impact feed each other. A good podcast attracts listeners, listeners attract sponsors, and the sponsors' money pays for better equipment and the next series.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Factors rarely act alone, and examiners reward answers that show the chain reaction. A small budget can force cheaper equipment (technical), which needs more time in post-production (time), which may hurt quality (creative direction). Strong answers also say how each factor could be managed: a back-up date for weather, shared cloud folders for team collaboration, royalty-free music for legal risk. The virtuous cycle is a business idea that explains why media companies reinvest. If one link breaks, for example poor audience research leading to weak impact, the whole cycle slows. In a \"discuss\" question, give the factor, an example from the scenario, its effect and a way to reduce it.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing problems from a shoot for the Jacmel Food Festival in Haiti. Match each problem to its factor, then complete the virtuous cycle links.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Technical factors","r":"The old microphone picked up wind noise, and the slow connection delayed the upload"},{"l":"Creative direction","r":"The client wanted a bright, upbeat tone with the festival's brand colours"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"There was no money to hire a professional chef as presenter"},{"l":"Time constraints","r":"A two-day deadline rushed the planning and lowered the quality"},{"l":"Legal and ethical factors","r":"Diners' faces could not be shown without image rights cleared"},{"l":"External conditions","r":"A tropical storm warning moved the outdoor shoot"},{"l":"Product and Optimise","r":"Enhance technology in the virtuous cycle"},{"l":"Strategy and Biz Dev","r":"Support media in the virtuous cycle"}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify the factors affecting digital media creation and explain how business functions feed the virtuous cycle.","tutor":[{"h":"Rights, not money","p":"<p>When the problem is permission or regulation, it is not a budget problem.</p>"},{"h":"The cycle","p":"<p>Which functions improve the technology, and which ones support the media?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Factors (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"External conditions","r":"Bad weather cancels an outdoor shoot"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"No money to hire extra gear"},{"l":"Team collaboration","r":"Poor communication slows production"},{"l":"Markets and Research","r":"Influence impact in the virtuous cycle"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: all seven factors appear, the examples are subtler, and two virtuous-cycle links are included. Match each card to the factor or link it shows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Technical factors","r":"The 4K files were too large to upload over the hotel Wi-Fi, so the client in Aruba waited two days"},{"l":"Creative direction","r":"The brand guide demanded calm pastel colours, but the director wanted neon Carnival tones"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"The team filmed with a phone gimbal because hiring a drone cost too much"},{"l":"Time constraints","r":"The client moved the launch forward a week, so the colour grade was skipped"},{"l":"Legal and ethical factors","r":"A vendor asked not to be filmed, so the scene was reshot without her stall"},{"l":"Team collaboration","r":"The editor cut last week's script because the update was only sent to the director"},{"l":"External conditions","r":"The lead presenter fell ill on the shoot day, and a back-up host stepped in"},{"l":"Markets and Research","r":"Influence impact in the virtuous cycle"},{"l":"Audience","r":"Is informed by research and strategy in the virtuous cycle"}],"win":"Excellent. You identified all seven factors from realistic problems and placed two links of the virtuous cycle correctly."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Prototypes, idea validation and implementation","html":"<p>A <b>prototype</b> is an early, testable version of a product used to check an idea before full production. <b>Idea validation</b> is testing that prototype with real members of the target audience to confirm that the idea solves the problem and that people want and can use it.</p>\n<p><b>Levels of fidelity</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Best for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low-fidelity</b></td><td>Paper sketches, sticky notes, a rough storyboard or animatic</td><td>Testing the concept and flow quickly and cheaply</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Medium-fidelity</b></td><td>Clickable wireframes in Figma, a rough video cut</td><td>Testing navigation, timing and structure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>High-fidelity</b></td><td>Near-final colours, images and interactions; a polished draft video</td><td>Testing look, feel and detailed usability</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <b>minimum viable product (MVP)</b> is the simplest working version that delivers the core value, released to learn from real users.</p>\n<p><b>How to validate an idea</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decide what you want to learn (for example, \"Can visitors find the nearest craft stall in under one minute?\").</li>\n<li>Recruit five to eight real target users, such as classmates, customers at the client's shop or relatives in the right age group.</li>\n<li>Give them tasks and <b>observe</b> without helping. Use a think-aloud approach: they say what they are thinking.</li>\n<li>Collect feedback: short surveys, interviews, ratings, A/B tests of two versions, or analytics such as clicks and watch time.</li>\n<li><b>Iterate</b>: fix the problems and test again. This is the feedback loop you met in Unit 1.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Example: Jerome at the Island Pulse Media tested a paper prototype of a Paramaribo bus-route app with commuters at the Heiligenweg bus station. Users kept looking for the fare first, not the map, so he moved fares to the home screen before writing any code.</p>\n<p><b>Implementation</b> is moving from a validated prototype to the finished product. It covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Production</b>: filming, recording, illustrating, coding and building assets, following the style guide and storyboard.</li>\n<li><b>Post-production</b>: editing, colour correction, sound mixing, testing on different devices, exporting in the right formats.</li>\n<li><b>Launch or distribution</b>: publishing to YouTube, Instagram, a website or an app store, with a promotion plan.</li>\n<li><b>Evaluation</b>: measuring results against the goal set in the problem definition, then recommending improvements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Justify the use of a prototype\" questions want reasons: it reduces cost and risk, reveals usability problems early, gets client approval and gives evidence that users want the product. Always tie the reason to the scenario.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A prototype is an early, rough version of a product that you can test. It can be paper sketches or a clickable mock-up. Validation means testing it with real users to see whether the idea works and whether they want it. Then you fix the problems and test again. A ferry company in St. Lucia could test a paper booking screen with passengers before paying for an app.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Match the prototype's fidelity to the question you need answered. A paper prototype answers \"does the flow make sense?\" cheaply, but it cannot tell you whether a colour scheme looks professional; that needs a high-fidelity version. Testing too late is a common trap: once a product is fully built, people are reluctant to throw work away, so problems survive. Five to eight users usually reveal most usability problems, but they must be real target users, not the design team. An MVP is different from a prototype: it is a working product released to the public to learn from real use. In a \"justify\" answer, link the prototype to cost, risk and the scenario.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app","intro":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. Carnival City Committee wants a digital product to help people enjoy Antigua Carnival. Zara asks you to lead pre-production. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The committee chair says: \"Just build us an app with everything on it, fast.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Open Android Studio and start coding an app straight away.","to":"bad1","fb":"Jumping to production skips analysis and definition, so you may build the wrong product."},{"t":"Hold a short meeting to agree the brief: the problem, the users, the goal, the budget and the deadline.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Acceptance and definition come first; a clear brief prevents wasted work."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You survey 60 residents and visitors. Most want to know when and where bands and jump-ups pass, and many have limited data. Which problem statement do you write?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a quick, low-data way to know where and when events happen because they miss events and waste data searching social media.\"","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. It names users, need and insight, and keeps the solution open."},{"t":"\"The committee needs a beautiful app with a 3D Carnival City.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"This names a solution and ignores the users' data limits found in your research."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>In ideation your team suggests a full app, a WhatsApp alert channel, a printable QR map and a live TikTok feed. You select a low-data web map with WhatsApp alerts. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Skip planning: Carnival is only six weeks away.","to":"bad3","fb":"A tight deadline is exactly why you need a schedule. Without one, tasks clash and deadlines slip."},{"t":"Create rough sketches, a mood board and a style guide, a storyboard for the 30-second promo, and a Gantt chart with milestones and buffer time.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Planning designs and timelines before production keeps the team aligned and on time."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Maya has a clickable Figma prototype of the map. Before full development, what do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the team if they like it, then launch.","to":"bad4","fb":"The team are not the target audience. Only real users can validate the idea."},{"t":"Test it with eight real Carnival-goers on their own phones, observe them, collect feedback and fix problems.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Validating with target users catches problems while they are still cheap to fix."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The map launches on time and works well on low data. A Grade One answer would explain that you followed the full pre-production process: brief, research, problem definition, ideation and selection, planning designs, storyboard, schedule, then prototype validation before implementation.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong product.</b> After two weeks of coding, the committee realises they wanted event times, not a ticket shop. Try again and define the problem first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Too heavy.</b> The 3D Carnival City drains data and battery, and few people download it. Try again and let your audience research shape the problem statement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Missed deadline.</b> Filming and coding clash, the promo is late and nobody agrees on colours. Try again and plan before you produce.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> On launch day visitors cannot find the \"Now playing\" button. Try again and validate the prototype with real users.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Order matters","p":"<p>Brief and research, then problem, then ideas, then plans, then prototype.</p>"},{"h":"Who decides?","p":"<p>Real target users, not the team, validate a prototype.</p>"},{"h":"Constraints","p":"<p>Data limits and deadlines should shape your choices, not be ignored.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: the Carnival committee wants a digital product.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The chair says \"Build an app now.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree the brief and research the users.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Define before you design."},{"t":"Start coding immediately.","to":"bad","fb":"You might build the wrong thing."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You have a prototype. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Launch it without testing.","to":"bad","fb":"Untested products often confuse users."},{"t":"Test it with real Carnival-goers.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Real users validate the idea."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You defined the problem and validated the prototype.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Plan and test before you produce. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief. Carnival City Committee has <b>US$3,000</b> and <b>six weeks</b>. Every choice below sounds reasonable; pick the one a Grade One designer would defend. Some choices end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The committee chair says: \"Build us an app with everything on it, fast. Budget US$3,000, six weeks.\" Zara asks you to open the project. Which first step shows the best judgement?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Hold a briefing to agree the problem, the users, a measurable goal, the budget, the deadline and who signs off each stage.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Acceptance and definition come first, and agreeing sign-offs now prevents arguments later."},{"t":"Write a detailed technical specification for a native app so the developer can start on Monday.","to":"bad1","fb":"This chooses the solution before anyone has analysed the problem or the users."},{"t":"Ask the chair for a list of features and promise to deliver all of them.","to":"ok1","fb":"Listening to the client matters, but promising every feature with no analysis risks the budget and the deadline."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your survey of 60 people finds that most want live band locations, 70% use prepaid data, many visitors speak Spanish or French, and older residents dislike small text. Which problem statement and goal do you write?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a way to find events because they miss them. Goal: make Carnival better.\"","to":"ok2","fb":"The user and need are there, but your research findings and a measurable goal are missing."},{"t":"\"The committee needs a 3D Carnival City app with augmented reality floats.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"This names a heavy solution and ignores the data limits your research found."},{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a low-data, multilingual way to find where bands and jump-ups are, because they miss events and waste data searching social media. Goal: 3,000 users, and 80% of tested users finding the next event in under one minute, by the last day of Carnival.\"","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Users, need and insight come from your research, the format is left open and the goal is measurable."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Ideation produced a native app, a WhatsApp alert channel, a printable QR map, a live TikTok feed and a low-data web map. How do you select?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Choose the native app because it will look most professional at the launch.","to":"bad3","fb":"Looks are not a selection criterion on their own. A native app is the most expensive and slowest option."},{"t":"Score each idea against the goal for feasibility in six weeks, cost within US$3,000, data use and risk. The low-data web map with WhatsApp alerts scores highest.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Selection compares options against the objectives, not personal taste."},{"t":"Choose the TikTok feed because TikTok is the most popular platform with young people.","to":"ok3","fb":"Popular, but it ignores older residents, visitors without the app and the data problem."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The map needs the parade route from the police (due Week 2), and filming the 30-second promo needs Carnival City to be built (Week 3). What goes into your Gantt chart?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tasks linked by their dependencies (route data before the map build; Carnival City built before filming), sign-off milestones and a buffer week before launch.","to":"n5","fb":"Good. Dependencies and buffer time protect the critical path."},{"t":"One bar for each team member, all running from Week 1 to Week 6.","to":"bad4","fb":"Bars that cover the whole project show no sequence, no dependencies and no milestones."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Maya's clickable Figma prototype is ready. Who do you test it with, and how?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The five members of the design team, on the office Wi-Fi.","to":"bad5","fb":"The team knows the design too well, and office Wi-Fi hides the data problem."},{"t":"Eight Carnival-goers of different ages, including two Spanish or French speakers and two older residents, on their own prepaid phones, with set tasks and think-aloud.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. The test group matches the audience your research found, on their real devices."},{"t":"The committee chair only, because she is the client and must approve it.","to":"ok4","fb":"Client approval matters, but one person is not a typical user. You still need to validate with the audience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One project.</b> The map launches on time, works on prepaid data and in three languages, and 84% of tested users find the next event in under a minute. You can justify every pre-production decision with evidence from your research.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Over budget.</b> The feature list grows every week and the project runs out of money before testing. A partial success: next time, define the problem before agreeing the features.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Hard to evaluate.</b> The product launches, but with no measurable goal nobody can say whether it worked. Next time, turn your research into a precise statement and a measurable goal.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Narrow reach.</b> Young users enjoy the feed, but older residents and many visitors never see it. Next time, select against the whole audience and the goal.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Approved but untested.</b> The chair likes the map, but visitors struggle with the language menu on launch day. Next time, validate with real users as well as the client.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong product.</b> After two weeks of coding, the committee realises they wanted event times, not a ticket shop. Try again and define the problem first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Too heavy.</b> The 3D app drains data and battery and few people download it. Try again and let your research shape the problem statement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Out of time.</b> The native app is only half built when Carnival opens. Try again and select against feasibility, cost and the deadline.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Missed deadline.</b> The map build waits for route data nobody chased, and the promo shoot clashes with editing. Try again and plan the dependencies.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> On launch day, visitors on slow prepaid data cannot load the map. Try again and validate with real users on their own phones.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Why validate a prototype?","q":"Bridgetown Fit Club in Barbados has a paper prototype of a workout app. The owner wants to skip testing and go straight to full development. Which is the BEST justification for validating the prototype first?","options":[{"t":"Testing with real members reveals usability problems and confirms demand while changes are still cheap, reducing the risk of building an app nobody uses.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This links validation to cost, risk and user needs, which is what \"justify\" requires."},{"t":"Testing is required by app stores before any app is published.","fb":"App stores review apps for policy and security, but they do not require prototype testing with users."},{"t":"Testing makes the app look more professional.","fb":"Validation is about whether the idea works for users, not about appearance."},{"t":"Testing means the team will not need a Gantt chart.","fb":"Validation and scheduling are separate pre-production tasks. You still need a schedule."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Cost of change","p":"<p>Is it cheaper to change paper or finished code?</p>"},{"h":"Justify","p":"<p>A justification gives a reason and its benefit for this business.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Why validate? (Foundation)","q":"Why should a team test a prototype with real users?","options":[{"t":"To make it look nicer.","fb":"Testing is about usability and demand, not looks."},{"t":"Because app stores require it.","fb":"App stores do not require user testing."},{"t":"To find problems early, when they are cheap to fix.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Early fixes save time and money."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Why validate a prototype? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Bridgetown Fit Club in Barbados has a paper prototype of a class-booking app. The owner says: \"Testing is a waste of money. We will fix any problems after launch.\" Which response BEST justifies validating the prototype first?","options":[{"t":"Validation guarantees the app will succeed, so no evaluation will be needed after launch.","fb":"No test guarantees success. Evaluation after launch still measures the results against the goal."},{"t":"Testing the paper prototype with five to eight real members reveals usability problems and confirms demand while changes are still cheap, and gives the owner evidence before he pays for full development.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It links validation to cost, risk and real users, and answers the owner's concern about money."},{"t":"Testing with the owner's friends will show whether the app looks attractive, which is the main reason apps fail.","fb":"Friends are not typical users, and appearance is not the main thing a prototype test checks."},{"t":"A high-fidelity test is more accurate, so the club should finish the app first and test it afterwards.","fb":"Testing later is still useful, but finishing first throws away the main benefit of a prototype: cheap, early changes."}]}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product","genre":"Structured response: pre-production planning (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad wants a <b>30-second animated public-service video</b> to teach beachgoers how to behave near turtle nests. The video will be shared on Instagram, Facebook and on screens at Piarco International Airport. The group has a small budget and needs the video before nesting season begins in eight weeks.</p>","prompt":"<p>As the pre-production lead, write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>writes a <b>problem statement</b> with a measurable goal;</li><li><b>describes</b> the target audience, using at least two kinds of audience information;</li><li><b>explains</b> how ONE brainstorming technique (mind mapping or SCAMPER) could generate ideas for the video;</li><li><b>outlines</b> TWO planning documents the team should produce before animation begins (for example, a storyboard and a Gantt chart), and ONE core element of pre-production they must plan for (resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice, or health and safety);</li><li><b>explains</b> how the team could validate the idea before final production.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers ... need ... because ... Goal: ...<br><b>(b)</b> The target audience is ... (demographic). They ... (behaviour or psychographic). This means the video should ...<br><b>(c)</b> Using SCAMPER / a mind map, the team would ... This helps because ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, a storyboard showing ... Second, a Gantt chart showing ... The team must also plan for ... (core element) by ...<br><b>(e)</b> The team should build an animatic / rough version and test it with ... by ... then ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement names the user, the need and a reason, and includes a measurable goal.","I described the audience with at least two types of information and explained a design effect.","I explained HOW the brainstorming technique works and WHY it helps.","I outlined two planning documents with specific details for this video, and one core element of pre-production (for example copyright and IP).","I explained a validation method with real target users and iteration.","I linked my points to the turtle video scenario throughout."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem definition and audience analysis","marks":5,"look":["need","because","goal|reduce|increase|%|measur","demograph|age|tourist|visitor|resident","behavio|psychograph|value|phone|instagram|scroll"],"min":3,"strong":"Your problem statement and audience description are precise. Next step: explain how each audience detail changes the design.","weak":"Write the problem statement as \"[user] need [need] because [reason]\" and add a measurable goal. Then describe the audience with demographics (age, tourist or resident) and behaviours (watching Reels with the sound off)."},{"criterion":"Brainstorming and planning documents","marks":5,"look":["scamper|mind ?map|branch|substitut|combin","storyboard|frame|scene","shot|close-?up|narrat|caption|audio","gantt|timeline|milestone|schedule","week|deadline|buffer|depend","budget|resource|personnel|clearance|permit|release","copyright|licen|intellectual property|royalty","code of practice|regulation|risk assess|health and safety","ideation|selection|creative process|branched|cyclic|linear|stepped"],"min":3,"strong":"You explained the technique and planning documents clearly with specific details.","weak":"Explain how SCAMPER or a mind map actually works, then give specific contents for your storyboard (frames, timing, shot types, audio) and Gantt chart (tasks, weeks, milestones)."},{"criterion":"Validation and application to the scenario","marks":5,"look":["prototype|animatic|draft|rough","test|feedback|survey|observ","beach|turtle|nest|riviere|matura","improv|iterat|fix|revis|change"],"min":3,"strong":"Strong validation plan, well tied to the turtle scenario.","weak":"Describe a prototype (such as an animatic), who will test it (real beachgoers), how you will gather feedback and what you will do with it."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick, memorable way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many disturb nests without realising it. Goal: reduce disturbances reported by the Turtle Watch group by 50% in the coming nesting season.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The audience includes residents and tourists aged roughly 16 to 45 (demographic). Many scroll Instagram and Facebook with the sound off and watch only a few seconds of each video (behaviour), and many visitors care about protecting nature (psychographic). The video must therefore deliver its key message in the first five seconds, use large captions and simple icons so it works without sound, and use clear, friendly English with a Spanish or French tagline for airport viewers.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Using SCAMPER, the team would take an existing beach safety poster and ask prompts such as Substitute (replace the warning text with an animated baby turtle character), Combine (combine the safety rules with a countdown to hatching) and Eliminate (remove all but the three most important rules). SCAMPER helps because it gives a structured checklist, so the team improves a known idea systematically instead of waiting for inspiration.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, a <b>storyboard</b> of about six frames, each with timing (for example 0:00 to 0:05), shot type, the animated action, on-screen caption, music and the transition. This lets the client approve the sequence before animation begins. Second, a <b>Gantt chart</b> over eight weeks showing research, storyboard, animatic, animation, sound, review and export, with milestones such as \"storyboard approved\" in Week 2 and buffer time in Week 7 in case of delays. The team must also plan for <b>copyright and intellectual property</b>: the music and any turtle photos must be original or properly licensed (for example a royalty-free track with its licence saved), so the client can share the video on Instagram and at the airport without infringement.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> The team should build an <b>animatic</b>, the storyboard frames timed to a rough voice-over, and show it on a phone to about eight real beachgoers at Grande Riviere. They would observe whether viewers can recall the three rules afterwards and ask short survey questions. If viewers miss a rule, the team would revise the frames and test again before final animation, which saves time and money compared with fixing a finished video.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Structured questions in Paper 02 award marks part by part. Label each part (a), (b), (c) and use the command word: \"describe\" needs details, \"explain\" needs a reason (\"because\", \"this helps by ...\"). Keep every point tied to the scenario: turtles, beaches, Instagram, the airport screens.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: pre-production planning, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b> the problem statement: who needs what, and why? <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> TWO facts about the target audience: one demographic fact (such as age) and one behaviour (something they do). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE planning document the team should make before animation begins, and <b>state</b> ONE thing it will show. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE core element of pre-production and give ONE example of it for this video. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> who should watch a rough version of the video before it is finished, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere need ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> One demographic fact is ... One behaviour is ...<br><b>(c)</b> The team should make a ... It will show ...<br><b>(d)</b> One core element is ... For this video, the team must ...<br><b>(e)</b> The team should show a rough version to ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement says who, what they need and why.","I gave one demographic fact and one behaviour.","I named a planning document and said what it shows.","I named a core element and gave an example for the turtle video.","I said who should test the rough version and why."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem statement","marks":2,"look":["need","because"],"min":2,"strong":"Your statement names the users, their need and a reason.","weak":"Use the pattern: \"Beachgoers need ... because ...\"."},{"criterion":"Target audience","marks":2,"look":["age|tourist|visitor|resident|year|teen|adult","scroll|watch|instagram|facebook|phone|sound off|airport"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a demographic fact and a behaviour.","weak":"Give one fact such as age or \"tourist\", and one thing they do, such as scrolling Instagram with the sound off."},{"criterion":"Planning document","marks":2,"look":["storyboard|gantt|timeline|schedule|script|mood board","frame|scene|shot|week|task|deadline|order|sequence|show"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a planning document and what it shows.","weak":"Name a storyboard (it shows each scene in order) or a Gantt chart (it shows the tasks and the weeks)."},{"criterion":"Core element of pre-production","marks":2,"look":["resource|budget|clearance|permit|release|copyright|licen|code|health|safety","music|image|photo|turtle|beach|airport|money|staff|animator|form"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a core element and gave a fitting example.","weak":"Name a core element such as copyright and IP, then say what it means for this video, such as licensing the music."},{"criterion":"Testing the idea","marks":2,"look":["beachgoer|visitor|tourist|resident|user|people","because|so |find|problem|fix|check|understand|clear"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose real viewers and gave a reason.","weak":"Say that real beachgoers should watch a rough version, so the team can fix problems before the final video."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere need a quick way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many people disturb nests without knowing it.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One demographic fact is that many viewers are tourists aged about 18 to 45. One behaviour is that they scroll Instagram with the sound off.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The team should make a storyboard. It will show each scene of the video in order, with its timing and the words on screen.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One core element is copyright and IP. For this video, the team must use music they have a licence for, such as a royalty-free track.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The team should show a rough version to a few real beachgoers because they can say whether the message is clear before the final video is made.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the idea and 1 for a detail or reason linked to the turtle scenario. Encourage students to use every sentence starter and to underline the scenario word they used (beach, turtle, Instagram, airport). Move them on to the Core task once they can complete each part without the frame.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: pre-production planning, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad wants a <b>30-second animated public-service video</b> to teach beachgoers how to behave near turtle nests. It will be shared on Instagram and Facebook and shown on screens at Piarco International Airport, where the screens play <b>without sound</b>. The group has <b>US$1,200</b>, two volunteer animators who can only work in the evenings, and <b>eight weeks</b> before nesting season begins. Last year a printed poster campaign reached many residents but very few tourists.</p>","prompt":"<p>As the pre-production lead, write an extended response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>writes a <b>problem statement</b> with a SMART goal and <b>justifies</b> why it does not name the solution <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>analyses</b> the target audience using THREE kinds of audience information, explaining the design decision each one leads to <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>compares</b> mind mapping and SCAMPER for this brief and <b>recommends</b> one, with a reason <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>plans</b> the pre-production: the contents of the storyboard, a Gantt chart showing dependencies, the critical path and buffer time, and how the team will manage TWO core elements of pre-production <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> a low-fidelity and a high-fidelity prototype for validating this video and <b>recommends</b> a validation plan with a measurable test of success <i>(6 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":400,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers ... need ... because ... Goal: ... by ... The statement does not name a video because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Demographic: ... so the video will ... Behaviour: ... so ... Technical context / psychographic: ... so ...<br><b>(c)</b> A mind map would ... whereas SCAMPER would ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> Storyboard: ... frames, each with ... Gantt chart: ... depends on ... The critical path is ... Buffer: ... Core elements: ... managed by ... and ... managed by ...<br><b>(e)</b> A low-fidelity animatic would ... but ... A high-fidelity draft would ... but ... I recommend ... Success means ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement has a SMART goal, and I justified why it does not name the solution.","I used three different kinds of audience information, and each one leads to a design decision.","I compared both brainstorming techniques and made a recommendation tied to the brief.","My plan names storyboard contents, dependencies, the critical path, buffer time and two core elements with how to manage them.","I weighed low- against high-fidelity prototypes and gave a validation plan with a measurable success test.","Every part uses details from the stimulus: no sound at the airport, US$1,200, evening-only animators, eight weeks, tourists."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem statement and justification","marks":4,"look":["need","because","%|reduce|increase|measur|by (the )?(end|week|start)","solution|format|open|ideation|not (say|name|mention)"],"min":3,"strong":"SMART goal and a clear justification for keeping the solution open.","weak":"Add a measurable, time-bound goal and explain that naming the solution too early closes off ideation."},{"criterion":"Audience analysis","marks":5,"look":["demograph|age|tourist|visitor|resident","behavio|scroll|sound off|watch","psychograph|value|care|nature|eco","technical|phone|data|screen|airport","so |therefore|caption|icon|first (five|5|few) seconds|design"],"min":4,"strong":"Three kinds of information, each turned into a design decision.","weak":"Use three kinds of information (for example demographic, behaviour, technical context) and say what each one changes in the video."},{"criterion":"Brainstorming comparison","marks":4,"look":["mind ?map","scamper","whereas|however|while|but|compared","recommend|better|best|suit"],"min":3,"strong":"Both techniques compared, with a justified recommendation.","weak":"Explain how each technique works, compare them, then recommend one for this brief. SCAMPER suits improving last year's poster."},{"criterion":"Pre-production plan","marks":6,"look":["storyboard|frame","timing|0:0|second|shot|caption","gantt|week","depend|before|after","critical path","buffer","copyright|licen|resource|budget|clearance|release|code|health|safety"],"min":5,"strong":"A complete, realistic plan that fits the budget and the volunteers' hours.","weak":"Give storyboard contents, at least one dependency, the critical path, where the buffer goes, and two core elements with how each will be managed."},{"criterion":"Prototype evaluation and validation plan","marks":6,"look":["low.?fidelity|animatic|paper|rough","high.?fidelity|polished|near.?final","cheap|cost|quick|time","test|observ|survey|recall","beachgoer|tourist|visitor|airport","recommend|on balance|therefore","%|out of|measur|success"],"min":5,"strong":"Balanced evaluation and a measurable, realistic validation plan.","weak":"Give a strength and a weakness of each fidelity, recommend one (or both in sequence), and set a measurable test such as \"6 of 8 viewers recall the three rules\"."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Tourists and residents at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick, memorable way to learn how to behave near turtle nests, because most disturbances happen when visitors do not know the rules, and last year's posters reached few tourists. Goal: reduce disturbances reported by Turtle Watch volunteers by 50% during the coming nesting season, and have 70% of surveyed airport arrivals recall at least two rules. The statement deliberately does not say \"video\". Keeping the format open lets ideation consider other options (such as an airport loop, a sticker or a Reel series) and stops the team defending a solution before the problem is understood.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Demographic:</i> many viewers are tourists aged 18 to 45 arriving by air, plus local residents. The video therefore uses simple English with short Spanish and French captions. <i>Behaviour and technical context:</i> on Instagram, viewers scroll with the sound off, and the airport screens have no sound at all, so the key message must appear in the first five seconds as large captions and clear icons, with narration only as an extra. <i>Psychographic:</i> many visitors value nature and choose eco-friendly activities, so the tone is positive (\"help the hatchlings\") rather than a list of fines.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A mind map would place \"turtle-safe beaches\" in the centre and branch into audiences, messages and settings, which is useful for a brand-new idea. SCAMPER starts from an existing product, last year's poster, and asks prompts such as Substitute (an animated hatchling in place of warning text), Eliminate (keep only three rules) and Put to another use (reuse the frames as stickers). I recommend SCAMPER, because the team already has a product that worked with residents and needs to adapt it for tourists, and SCAMPER improves it systematically in a single evening session that suits the volunteers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Storyboard:</i> six frames from 0:00 to 0:30, each with timing, shot type, the animated action, the caption, music and the transition, ending on a logo frame. <i>Gantt chart:</i> research (Week 1); storyboard (Week 2, milestone \"storyboard approved\"); animatic and test (Week 3); animation (Weeks 4 to 6); sound and captions (Week 6); review and export (Week 7); buffer (Week 8). Animation depends on the approved storyboard, and sound depends on the picture lock, so research, storyboard, animatic, animation, review and export form the critical path. The buffer sits before the launch milestone because the animators work evenings only and any illness hits the critical path. <i>Core elements:</i> resources: the US$1,200 covers a royalty-free music licence and a small payment for a voice artist, using free software (Blender or OpenToonz) to save money; copyright and IP: the music licence and any reference photos are saved in a clearances folder so the airport and Instagram can show the video without infringement.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> A low-fidelity animatic (the storyboard frames timed to a rough voice-over) is quick and costs nothing but volunteer time, so it can be changed easily; however, it cannot show whether the final colours and icons read well on a bright airport screen. A high-fidelity draft shows the real look, but it takes weeks of evening work, so changes at that stage threaten the deadline. On balance I recommend testing the animatic first in Week 3, then checking one high-fidelity frame on an airport-sized screen. Eight viewers (four tourists, four residents) watch the animatic on a phone with the sound off; success means at least six of the eight can state the three rules afterwards. If they cannot, the team revises the frames and retests before animation begins.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 4 + 5 + 4 + 6 + 6). Reward evaluation over description: a Grade One response weighs options and reaches a justified recommendation in (c) and (e), and its Gantt chart in (d) is realistic for evening-only volunteers. Insist that every part uses at least one stimulus detail (no sound at the airport, US$1,200, eight weeks, tourists). Students who list without explaining should cap at about half marks.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U2","title":"Elements of visual design and colour theory","objectives":["Identify and apply the elements of visual design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour.","Explain the colour wheel, primary, secondary and tertiary colours, and the harmony rules: complementary, analogous, triadic, split-complementary and tetradic.","Create tints, shades, tones and gradients, and explain colour mixing (RGB additive and CMYK subtractive), hex codes and new colour creation.","Discuss colour psychology and the visual impact of specific colour combinations in a digital format, including contrast and accessibility.","Use colour theory to design a character that reads clearly as a hero or a villain."],"videos":[{"title":"The elements of art and design","q":"seven elements of art line shape form space texture value colour explained"},{"title":"Colour theory basics","q":"color theory basics color wheel harmonies complementary analogous triadic"},{"title":"RGB vs CMYK explained","q":"RGB vs CMYK explained additive subtractive color for designers"},{"title":"Colour in character design","q":"color theory in character design hero villain color palette"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"The elements of visual design","html":"<p>The <b>elements of visual design</b> are the basic building blocks of every image, logo, web page, animation and app screen. The <b>principles</b> (composition, balance, contrast and so on) are the rules for arranging these elements; you will study composition in the next unit. You should be able to name, explain and apply all seven elements.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Caribbean digital example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Line</b></td><td>A mark connecting two points. Lines can be straight, curved, thick, thin, implied or directional. They lead the eye and suggest mood: horizontal lines feel calm, diagonal lines feel energetic.</td><td>Curved wave lines across the header of a Bathsheba surf school website.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shape</b></td><td>A flat, two-dimensional enclosed area. <b>Geometric</b> shapes (circles, squares) feel ordered; <b>organic</b> shapes (leaves, waves) feel natural.</td><td>A round app icon with a flat hibiscus silhouette.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Form</b></td><td>A three-dimensional object, or the illusion of 3D using light and shadow (a sphere instead of a circle).</td><td>A 3D rotating conch shell modelled in Blender.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Space</b></td><td>The area around and between elements. <b>Positive space</b> is occupied by the subject; <b>negative (white) space</b> is empty. Space can also create depth.</td><td>A clean landing page for an Oranjestad jeweller with plenty of empty space around each ring.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Texture</b></td><td>How a surface feels or appears to feel. <b>Actual</b> texture can be touched; in digital media we use <b>visual (implied)</b> texture.</td><td>A sand or woven-straw texture behind the menu of a beach bar.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td>How light or dark a colour or tone is, from white to black. Value creates contrast, depth and focus.</td><td>A dark storm-grey background behind a bright yellow hurricane alert.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Colour</b></td><td>Light reflected or emitted, described by <b>hue</b> (the colour name), <b>saturation</b> (intensity) and <b>value</b> (lightness). Colour carries emotion and meaning.</td><td>Black, green and gold in a Jamaican Independence Day graphic.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Elements work together. A poster for the Island Pulse Media's Carnival campaign might use diagonal <b>lines</b> for energy, organic feather <b>shapes</b>, a glitter <b>texture</b>, strong light and dark <b>values</b> for contrast, and bold saturated <b>colour</b>, all arranged with enough <b>space</b> to read the date.</p>\n<p><b>Shape or form?</b> This is a common exam trap. Shape is 2D (length and width); form is 3D (length, width and depth) or looks 3D because of shading.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"analyse\" a design, go element by element and say what each one <b>does</b>: \"The diagonal lines create a sense of movement, suiting the energy of a Carnival event.\" Naming an element without its effect earns little credit.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The elements of visual design are the building blocks of every design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour. Line leads the eye. Shape is flat; form looks 3D. Space is the room around things. Texture is how a surface seems to feel. Value is how light or dark something is. Colour carries feeling. A hand-painted fruit stall sign in Bridgetown uses all seven.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Elements become powerful when they work with or against each other. Strong value contrast can make a small element dominant, and a single diagonal line in an otherwise horizontal layout pulls attention to itself. Grade One analysis explains <b>effect and purpose</b>: not \"the poster uses texture\", but \"a rough woven texture behind the menu suggests hand-made local food, which suits a beach bar's relaxed brand\". Watch two traps. Shape is 2D, while form is 3D or shaded to look 3D. And every colour has a value: a yellow and a grey can share the same value, so they blur together in greyscale or for viewers with low vision. Checking a design in greyscale tests its value structure.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the element of design","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing designs for the Grace Bay Beach Festival. Match each element to the design decision that uses it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"Diagonal stripes that lead the eye towards the ticket button"},{"l":"Shape","r":"A flat circle and triangle combined into a simple sun-and-sail logo"},{"l":"Form","r":"A shaded, rotating 3D steel pan in the opening animation"},{"l":"Space","r":"Wide empty margins around the headline so it can breathe"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A photo-realistic sand pattern behind the schedule"},{"l":"Value","r":"A very dark background so the white date stands out"},{"l":"Colour","r":"Hot pink and turquoise chosen to feel fun and tropical"}],"win":"Well matched. You linked each element of visual design to what it actually does in a design.","tutor":[{"h":"2D or 3D?","p":"<p>Flat outlines are one element; objects with depth and shading are another.</p>"},{"h":"Light and dark","p":"<p>Lightness and darkness is its own element, separate from hue.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the element (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"Diagonal stripes leading the eye"},{"l":"Form","r":"A shaded, 3D steel pan"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A sand pattern background"},{"l":"Space","r":"Empty margins around the headline"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the element of design (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each card describes an <b>effect</b>, not an object. Zara is reviewing designs for a resort in Negril, Jamaica. Match each element to the card that shows it at work.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"The model's gaze and pointing arm create an implied path that leads to the \"Book\" button"},{"l":"Shape","r":"Flat leaf and wave silhouettes with no shading give the eco-resort logo a natural look"},{"l":"Form","r":"Soft shadows and one highlight turn a flat circle into a glossy coconut"},{"l":"Space","r":"The empty area around the logo is deliberately larger than the logo itself"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A scanned linen weave behind the menu suggests a hand-made, rustic feel"},{"l":"Value","r":"The poster still reads clearly in greyscale because of its light and dark contrast"},{"l":"Colour","r":"A desaturated version of the palette marks the buttons that cannot be used yet"}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised each element from its effect, including implied line, the illusion of form and value working without any colour."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The colour wheel: primary, secondary and tertiary","html":"<p>The <b>colour wheel</b> arranges hues in a circle so designers can see how colours relate. Isaac Newton first bent the spectrum into a circle in the early 1700s. Artists traditionally use the <b>RYB</b> (red, yellow, blue) wheel, which is the one most exam questions on harmony rules use.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 200\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:240px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Twelve-part colour wheel labelled P for primary, S for secondary and T for tertiary\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"40\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"190\"/>\n<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"100\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"100\"/>\n<line x1=\"36\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"72\"/><line x1=\"164\" y1=\"164\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"128\"/>\n<line x1=\"164\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"72\"/><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"164\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"128\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"30\">Red (P)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"72\">Orange (S)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"140\">Yellow (P)</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"180\">Green (S)</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"140\">Blue (P)</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"72\">Violet (S)</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"98\">Tertiaries</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"110\">sit between</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary colours</b> cannot be made by mixing other colours. On the RYB wheel they are <b>red, yellow and blue</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Secondary colours</b> are made by mixing two primaries in equal amounts: red + yellow = <b>orange</b>; yellow + blue = <b>green</b>; blue + red = <b>violet (purple)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Tertiary colours</b> are made by mixing a primary with a neighbouring secondary. There are six, and each is named primary first: <b>red-orange, yellow-orange, yellow-green, blue-green, blue-violet and red-violet</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives the standard <b>12-part wheel</b>: 3 primaries, 3 secondaries and 6 tertiaries.</p>\n<p><b>Warm and cool colours.</b> Reds, oranges and yellows are <b>warm</b>: they feel energetic and appear to come forward. Blues, greens and violets are <b>cool</b>: they feel calm and appear to recede. A Caribbean sunset poster leans warm; a spa in Soufrière might choose cool blues and greens.</p>\n<p><b>Three properties of colour</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hue</b>: the pure colour family (red, blue-green).</li>\n<li><b>Saturation (chroma)</b>: how intense or pure it is, from vivid to dull grey.</li>\n<li><b>Value (brightness)</b>: how light or dark it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In GIMP, Inkscape and Photoshop you will see these as <b>HSV</b> or <b>HSL</b> sliders.</p>\n<p><b>Important:</b> screens do not mix colour like paint. Screens use the <b>RGB</b> model, where the primaries are red, green and blue. You will compare RGB and CMYK later in this unit, so always say which model you mean.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often asks \"Which colour is tertiary?\" Look for a hyphenated name that combines a primary and a secondary, such as blue-green. Orange, green and violet are secondary, never tertiary.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The colour wheel shows how colours relate. On the painter's (RYB) wheel the primaries are red, yellow and blue. Mix two primaries to get a secondary: orange, green or violet. Mix a primary with the secondary next to it to get a tertiary, such as blue-green. Warm colours (reds, oranges) feel lively; cool colours (blues, greens) feel calm, like the sea at Grand Anse in Grenada.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Three colour models give three different sets of primaries, and examiners test whether you know which applies. <b>RYB</b> is the artist's wheel used for harmony rules; <b>RGB</b> (red, green and blue light) is used by screens; <b>CMY(K)</b> (cyan, magenta and yellow ink, plus black) is used in print. \"Yellow is a primary\" is only right if you name the RYB model. Keep the three properties separate: changing hue moves around the wheel, changing saturation moves towards grey and changing value moves towards white or black. A dull turquoise is the same hue at lower saturation, not a different colour. Warm and cool are also relative: red-violet looks cool beside orange but warm beside blue.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Colour wheel quick-fire","intro":"<p>Students at the Sol Studio in Georgetown are mixing paint for a mural of Kaieteur Falls. Answer each question using the traditional RYB colour wheel.</p>","items":[{"text":"Mixing yellow and blue in equal amounts gives...","options":["violet","green","orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Yellow + blue = green, a secondary colour."},{"text":"Which of these is a tertiary colour?","options":["blue-green","orange","red"],"answer":0,"fb":"Blue-green is a primary (blue) mixed with a neighbouring secondary (green)."},{"text":"How many tertiary colours are on a standard 12-part wheel?","options":["twelve","three","six"],"answer":2,"fb":"There are six tertiaries, one between each primary and secondary."},{"text":"Which group contains only warm colours?","options":["red, blue, green","red, orange, yellow","blue, green, violet"],"answer":1,"fb":"Reds, oranges and yellows are warm; they feel energetic and seem to advance."},{"text":"A vivid turquoise is made duller and greyer. Which property has changed?","options":["saturation","hue","temperature"],"answer":0,"fb":"Saturation is intensity. The hue stays turquoise, but it becomes less pure."},{"text":"Red, yellow and blue are primary on the RYB wheel because...","options":["they cannot be made by mixing other colours","they are always used in logos","they are the brightest colours"],"answer":0,"fb":"Primary colours are the starting colours that cannot be mixed from others in that model."}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify primary, secondary and tertiary colours, warm and cool colours, and the properties hue, saturation and value.","tutor":[{"h":"Hyphen clue","p":"<p>Tertiary names combine a primary and a secondary with a hyphen.</p>"},{"h":"Colour name or intensity?","p":"<p>Hue is the colour family; saturation is how strong it is.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour wheel (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Red + yellow makes...","options":["green","orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Red + yellow = orange."},{"text":"Which is tertiary?","options":["violet","red-violet"],"answer":1,"fb":"Red-violet mixes a primary and a secondary."},{"text":"Which is a cool colour?","options":["orange","blue"],"answer":1,"fb":"Blue is cool and calming."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder quick-fire on the 12-part RYB wheel: tertiaries, complements and properties. Read carefully: the last question is about a <b>screen</b>, so it uses RGB, not RYB.</p>","items":[{"text":"Which tertiary sits between yellow and green?","options":["red-violet","yellow-green","blue-green","yellow-orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Yellow (primary) mixed with green (its neighbouring secondary) gives yellow-green."},{"text":"What is the complement of yellow-orange on the 12-part RYB wheel?","options":["red-violet","blue-green","blue-violet","violet"],"answer":2,"fb":"Count six steps round the wheel: directly opposite yellow-orange is blue-violet."},{"text":"Which pair contains one secondary colour AND one tertiary colour?","options":["green and red-orange","red and blue","orange and violet","blue-violet and yellow-green"],"answer":0,"fb":"Green is secondary; red-orange is tertiary. Red and blue are primaries, orange and violet are both secondaries, and the last pair are both tertiaries."},{"text":"A designer keeps a sea-green button the same hue but makes it lighter for the hover state. Which property changed?","options":["hue","temperature","saturation","value"],"answer":3,"fb":"Lighter or darker is value. The hue is still sea-green."},{"text":"Placed next to a bright orange, which colour looks COOLEST?","options":["red-orange","blue-green","yellow","red"],"answer":1,"fb":"Blue-green sits on the cool side of the wheel, almost opposite orange."},{"text":"On a phone screen, full red light plus full green light (with blue off) produces...","options":["yellow","black","orange","brown"],"answer":0,"fb":"Screens mix light additively (RGB): red + green = yellow. The RYB paint wheel would not apply here."}],"win":"Excellent. You can find complements and tertiaries by position on the wheel, separate hue, saturation and value, and switch to RGB when the medium is a screen."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour harmony rules","html":"<p><b>Colour harmony</b> is a pleasing arrangement of colours. <b>Harmony rules</b> (colour schemes) use the positions of hues on the colour wheel to choose colours that work together. There are five schemes you must know.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>How to find it on the wheel</th><th>Example (RYB)</th><th>Effect and use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Monochromatic</b> (bonus)</td><td>One hue with its tints, shades and tones</td><td>Navy, blue, sky blue</td><td>Calm, unified, elegant</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Complementary</b></td><td>Two colours <b>directly opposite</b> each other</td><td>Blue and orange; red and green; yellow and violet</td><td>Maximum contrast and energy; great for a call-to-action button</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Analogous</b></td><td>Two to four colours <b>next to each other</b></td><td>Yellow, yellow-orange, orange</td><td>Harmonious and natural, like a sunset over Negril</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Triadic</b></td><td>Three colours <b>evenly spaced</b> (120 degrees apart, a triangle)</td><td>Red, yellow, blue; or orange, green, violet</td><td>Vibrant but balanced; popular in children's media</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Split-complementary</b></td><td>A base colour plus the <b>two colours either side of its complement</b></td><td>Blue with yellow-orange and red-orange</td><td>Strong contrast with less tension than complementary; safer for beginners</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tetradic</b> (double complementary)</td><td><b>Two complementary pairs</b>, forming a rectangle (or a square if evenly spaced)</td><td>Red, green, orange and blue</td><td>Rich and varied, but hard to balance</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Using schemes well</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Let <b>one colour dominate</b>. A common guide is the <b>60-30-10 rule</b>: 60% main colour, 30% secondary colour, 10% accent.</li>\n<li>With complementary and tetradic schemes, reduce saturation or use tints and shades of some colours so they do not fight.</li>\n<li>Use the accent colour for the most important action, such as \"Book now\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean examples.</b> A Trinidad and Tobago Carnival band site might use a triadic palette for maximum vibrancy. A Dominica eco-lodge might use an analogous palette of greens and blue-greens drawn from the rainforest. Keisha at the Island Pulse Media designed a Jamaica-themed reggae event post in green with a gold-yellow accent and plenty of black, picking up colours associated with the Jamaican flag.</p>\n<p>Online tools such as Adobe Color and Coolors let you choose a base colour and generate each scheme, with the hex codes ready to copy into your style guide.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Split-complementary and tetradic are the two schemes students confuse. Split-complementary has <b>three</b> colours (base + two neighbours of the complement); tetradic has <b>four</b> (two full complementary pairs).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Harmony rules are recipes for colours that look good together, based on where they sit on the colour wheel. Complementary colours are opposite each other. Analogous colours sit side by side. Triadic colours form a triangle. Split-complementary uses one colour plus the two beside its opposite. Tetradic uses two opposite pairs. A rainforest lodge in Guyana might choose analogous greens and blue-greens.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Knowing the rule is the start; using it well earns the top marks. Complementary pairs give the most contrast, but at equal amounts and full saturation they fight, so let one colour dominate and use the other as an accent (the 60-30-10 guide). Triadic and tetradic schemes are vivid but hard to balance, so desaturate two of the colours or use their tints. Split-complementary gives strong contrast with less tension, which is why it is safer for beginners. A common error is counting: split-complementary has <b>three</b> colours and tetradic has <b>four</b>. Remember too that the rules are worked out on the RYB wheel; tools such as Adobe Color use an RGB wheel, so their complements differ slightly.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Name that harmony rule","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is choosing palettes for five client websites. Match each harmony rule to its palette description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Blue and orange, directly opposite on the wheel, for a dive shop's \"Book a dive\" button"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Yellow, yellow-orange and orange, side by side, for a sunset cruise site"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Red, yellow and blue, evenly spaced, for a children's reading app"},{"l":"Split-complementary","r":"Violet with yellow-orange and yellow-green for a jazz festival poster"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Two opposite pairs, red and green plus orange and blue, for a busy Carnival band site"},{"l":"Monochromatic","r":"Navy, blue and sky blue only, for a calm insurance company page"}],"win":"Very good. You can identify colour schemes by their positions on the colour wheel: opposite, adjacent, evenly spaced, split, double pairs or one hue.","tutor":[{"h":"Count the colours","p":"<p>Two opposite = one scheme. Three = triadic or split-complementary. Four = two pairs.</p>"},{"h":"Split","p":"<p>If a base colour is paired with the neighbours of its opposite, the complement has been split.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Harmony rules (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Two colours opposite each other"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Colours next to each other"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Three colours evenly spaced"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Two complementary pairs"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name that harmony rule (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each palette is given by colour names only. Work out where each colour sits on the 12-part RYB wheel, then match the palette to its harmony rule.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Red-orange and blue-green"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Blue, blue-green and green"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Yellow-orange, red-violet and blue-green"},{"l":"Split-complementary","r":"Yellow with red-violet and blue-violet"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Yellow, orange, violet and blue"},{"l":"Monochromatic","r":"A deep shade, the pure hue and a pale tint of green"}],"win":"Excellent. You worked out each scheme from wheel positions: opposite, adjacent, evenly spaced, split, two pairs or one hue."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Tints, shades, tones and gradients","html":"<p>You can create many new colours from a single hue by changing its <b>value</b> and <b>saturation</b>. Know all four: tints, shades, tones and gradients.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Term</th><th>Made by</th><th>Effect</th><th>Example from a teal hue</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tint</b></td><td>Adding <b>white</b> to a hue</td><td>Lighter, softer, fresher; often used for backgrounds</td><td>Pale aqua like shallow water at Grace Bay</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shade</b></td><td>Adding <b>black</b> to a hue</td><td>Darker, deeper, more serious or mysterious</td><td>Deep teal like the sea at night</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tone</b></td><td>Adding <b>grey</b> (white and black) to a hue</td><td>Muted, less saturated, sophisticated</td><td>Dusty sea-green for a boutique hotel</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A memory trick: a <b>t</b>int goes <b>t</b>owards white; a <b>sh</b>ade goes into the <b>sh</b>adows; a tone is \"toned down\" with grey.</p>\n<p><b>Gradients</b></p>\n<p>A <b>gradient</b> is a smooth, gradual transition from one colour (or value) to another. Each colour in a gradient is a <b>colour stop</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linear gradient</b>: colours change along a straight line (top to bottom, left to right or at an angle), such as a sky going from deep blue to pale blue at the horizon.</li>\n<li><b>Radial gradient</b>: colours spread outwards from a central point in a circle, such as a glowing sun or a spotlight effect.</li>\n<li><b>Angular (conic)</b> gradients sweep around a centre point; some apps also offer <b>diamond</b> or <b>reflected</b> gradients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gradients add depth, suggest light and make flat shapes look more three-dimensional (form). In GIMP, use the <b>Gradient tool (G)</b>: set the foreground and background colours, choose the shape (linear, radial and others) in Tool Options, then drag across the canvas. In Inkscape, open <b>Fill and Stroke</b> and choose linear or radial gradient. On the web, CSS creates gradients without any image file:</p>\n<pre><code>.hero {\n  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #0B3D91, #7FD1F5);\n}\n.sun {\n  background: radial-gradient(circle, #FFD23F, #F26B1D);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media uses a warm orange-to-pink linear gradient as the background of every Sunset Sessions promo, so the series is recognisable instantly.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 practical you may be asked to \"apply a gradient\" in GIMP or Inkscape. Name the tool, the gradient type and the two colour stops in your written answer, and export as PNG to keep the smooth transition sharp. Heavy JPEG compression can cause visible <b>banding</b> (stripes) in gradients.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>You can make many colours from one hue. Add white to get a tint, which is lighter. Add black to get a shade, which is darker. Add grey to get a tone, which is softer and more muted. A gradient is a smooth blend from one colour to another, like the sky over Barbados fading from deep blue overhead to pale blue at the horizon.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Tints, shades and tones let designers build a whole palette from one brand colour while keeping it unified. A typical app uses the pure hue for buttons, a pale tint for backgrounds, a deep shade for text and a tone for disabled states. Strictly, a tone mainly lowers saturation while tints and shades change value, but all three reduce saturation a little, which is why heavy tinting can make a brand colour look washed out. With gradients, check the export: JPEG compression can cause <b>banding</b>, visible stripes where the blend should be smooth, so export as PNG. A gradient between two complements often passes through a muddy grey in the middle; adding a middle colour stop keeps it clean.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Tint, shade or tone?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is building a colour palette for the Sorrel Express delivery app, starting from one rich sorrel red. Sort each variation.</p>","main":"How was this variation of the base red created?","bins":["Tint (added white)","Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)"],"items":[{"t":"A very pale blush for the splash-screen glow.","bin":0,"fb":"Very light versions of a hue are tints."},{"t":"A dusty, muted rose for disabled buttons.","bin":2,"fb":"Muted and greyish means grey was added, so it is a tone."},{"t":"Strawberry milk pink for a \"special offer\" sticker.","bin":0,"fb":"Red plus white makes a light pink tint."},{"t":"Near-black maroon for text that must be dark but still warm.","bin":1,"fb":"Lots of black added to red produces a deep shade."},{"t":"A greyish red that feels calm and sophisticated.","bin":2,"fb":"Grey reduces saturation without just lightening or darkening, which creates a tone."},{"t":"A dark burgundy shadow beneath the logo.","bin":1,"fb":"Burgundy is a dark shade of red."},{"t":"A deep wine colour for the header bar.","bin":1,"fb":"Darkening red with black gives a shade such as wine or maroon."},{"t":"A soft pink used for the app's background cards.","bin":0,"fb":"Pink is red lightened with white, so it is a tint."}],"win":"Well sorted. Tints add white, shades add black and tones add grey, which gives you a whole palette from one hue.","tutor":[{"h":"Lighter or darker?","p":"<p>Lighter versions point to one bin, darker versions to another.</p>"},{"h":"Muted","p":"<p>Words like dusty, greyish or muted suggest the colour has been dulled rather than lightened or darkened.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Tint, shade or tone? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Soft pink made from red.","bin":0,"fb":"Red + white = tint."},{"t":"Deep wine colour made from red.","bin":1,"fb":"Red + black = shade."},{"t":"Dusty, greyish rose.","bin":2,"fb":"Red + grey = tone."},{"t":"Dark burgundy.","bin":1,"fb":"Red + black = shade."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Tint, shade or tone? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's base colour is the sorrel red <b>#C0392B</b>. Some cards give hex codes: compare each pair with the base (higher towards FF = white added; lower towards 00 = black added; closer to 80 = grey added). A fourth bin catches colours that are not variations of this red at all.</p>","main":"How was this colour made from the base red, if it was?","bins":["Tint (added white)","Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)","Different hue"],"items":[{"t":"A deep teal, used as a complementary accent.","bin":3,"fb":"Teal is a different hue, opposite red on the wheel."},{"t":"A muted, greyish red with almost the same lightness as the base.","bin":2,"fb":"Less saturation without much change in value is a tone."},{"t":"#3A110D, a near-black wine for body text.","bin":1,"fb":"The channels are much lower but in the same proportion: a deep shade."},{"t":"#C0782B, an orange of similar lightness.","bin":3,"fb":"The green channel has risen on its own, so the hue has shifted to orange. That is a different hue, not a tint, shade or tone."},{"t":"#ECC4BF, a pale blush for background cards.","bin":0,"fb":"Every channel has moved up towards FF, so white was added: a tint."},{"t":"#A05C55, a dusty rose for disabled buttons.","bin":2,"fb":"Each channel has moved towards 80 (mid-grey), so grey was added: a tone."},{"t":"A soft pink made by raising value and lowering saturation in the HSV sliders.","bin":0,"fb":"Lighter and paler with the same hue is the effect of adding white: a tint."},{"t":"#601C15, a deep maroon for the header.","bin":1,"fb":"Every channel has been halved towards 00, so black was added: a shade."}],"win":"Excellent. You can read tints, shades and tones from hex values, and you can tell a variation of a hue from a change of hue."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour mixing: RGB, CMYK, hex codes and new colours","html":"<p><b>Colour mixing</b> works differently for light (screens) and for ink (print). You should be able to explain colour mixing and new colour creation, and know which model suits which output.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>RGB: additive</th><th>CMYK: subtractive</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Used for</td><td>Screens: phones, monitors, TVs, projectors, websites, apps, video</td><td>Print: flyers, posters, banners, magazines, packaging</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Primaries</td><td>Red, Green, Blue light</td><td>Cyan, Magenta, Yellow ink, plus Key (black)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Light is <b>added</b>; more light makes it brighter</td><td>Ink <b>absorbs (subtracts)</b> light reflected from white paper; more ink makes it darker</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mixing results</td><td>R + G = yellow; G + B = cyan; R + B = magenta; R + G + B = <b>white</b></td><td>C + M = blue; M + Y = red; C + Y = green; C + M + Y = a muddy dark brown, so black (K) ink is added</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No colour</td><td>All off = black</td><td>No ink = white paper</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Hex codes</b></p>\n<p>On the web, RGB colours are usually written as a <b>hexadecimal (hex) code</b>: a hash sign then six characters, <b>#RRGGBB</b>. Each pair is one channel from <b>00</b> (none) to <b>FF</b> (full, which is 255 in decimal). That is 256 levels per channel, so 256 x 256 x 256 gives about <b>16.7 million</b> possible colours.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FF0000;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FF0000</b> = pure red (red full, green and blue off), the same as rgb(255, 0, 0).</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FFFF00;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FFFF00</b> = yellow (red + green light).</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#000000;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#000000</b> = black; <span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FFFFFF</b> = white; equal pairs such as <b>#808080</b> are greys.</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#1FA3A3;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#1FA3A3</b> = a teal: little red, plenty of green and blue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Creating new colours.</b> Digitally, you create a new colour by adjusting RGB or HSV sliders, typing a hex code, sampling a colour from a photo with the <b>eyedropper (colour picker)</b>, or blending two colours. Zara at the Island Pulse Media sampled the exact orange of a flamboyant tree in bloom from a photo to create a signature colour for a client, then saved it in the style guide as a hex code for screens and a CMYK value for print.</p>\n<p><b>Screen to print.</b> Some bright RGB colours, such as vivid neon greens, are <b>out of gamut</b> for CMYK and print duller. Design in RGB for screens; convert to or check CMYK before sending a flyer to a print shop, and always ask the printer for their required profile.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: \"Which colours are the primaries for a computer monitor?\" The answer is red, green and blue, not red, yellow and blue. Match the model to the medium: light = RGB, ink = CMYK, paint = RYB.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Screens make colour with light, so they use RGB: red, green and blue light added together. Printers make colour with ink, so they use CMYK: cyan, magenta, yellow and black. A hex code such as #FF0000 is a screen colour written as code. If a restaurant in Ocho Rios wants its logo on Instagram and on printed menus, the designer needs an RGB version and a CMYK version.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Explain the models with the words <b>additive</b> and <b>subtractive</b>. RGB adds light, so all three channels at full strength make white. CMYK inks subtract light reflected from paper, so more ink makes it darker, and black (K) is added because C + M + Y gives only a muddy dark brown. Read a hex code as three pairs: #1FA3A3 has a low red pair (1F) and high, equal green and blue pairs (A3), so it is a teal. RGB has a wider <b>gamut</b> than CMYK, so vivid screen colours can print dull, which is why designers check a printed proof. A common error is \"convert to CMYK for the website\": CMYK is for print only, and web images use sRGB.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Screen or print?","q":"Regent Street Bookshop in Georgetown has a logo with the hex colour #00A86B. The owner wants the logo printed on 500 paper bags and also shown on the shop's Instagram page. Which statement is MOST accurate?","options":[{"t":"The logo should be designed in CMYK for Instagram because CMYK has more colours.","fb":"Instagram is viewed on screens, which use RGB. RGB generally has a wider gamut of bright colours than CMYK."},{"t":"The same hex code works perfectly on screens and paper because colours are universal.","fb":"Screens emit light (RGB) and paper reflects light through ink (CMYK), so colours can shift between them."},{"t":"The hex code is an RGB screen value, so it is right for Instagram, but the logo should be converted to a CMYK value and proof-checked before printing because ink mixes subtractively.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Hex codes describe additive RGB light for screens. Printing uses subtractive CMYK ink, so the colour must be converted and checked."},{"t":"The printer will use red, yellow and blue ink to match the hex code exactly.","fb":"Commercial printing uses cyan, magenta, yellow and black (CMYK), not RYB paint primaries."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Light or ink?","p":"<p>Instagram is seen on a lit screen. A paper bag reflects light through ink.</p>"},{"h":"Hex means...","p":"<p>Hex codes are a way of writing red, green and blue channel values.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Screen or print? (Foundation)","q":"Which colour model should be used for a flyer that will be printed?","options":[{"t":"RGB","fb":"RGB is for screens that emit light."},{"t":"Hex codes only","fb":"Hex codes are RGB values for screens."},{"t":"CMYK","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Printers mix cyan, magenta, yellow and black ink."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Screen or print? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"A Port of Spain events company's brand orange is #FF6A00. On the newly printed posters it looks noticeably duller than on Instagram, although the printer followed the file exactly. What is the MOST likely cause, and the best fix?","options":[{"t":"The printer used CMYK instead of RYB, so the posters should be printed with red, yellow and blue paint.","fb":"Commercial printing uses CMYK inks. RYB is the painter's model and does not apply to presses."},{"t":"The hex code was typed wrongly, because #FF6A00 is a brown.","fb":"#FF6A00 is full red, some green and no blue: a vivid orange. The code is not the problem."},{"t":"The vivid RGB orange lies outside the CMYK gamut, so the nearest printable colour was used; the designer should set a CMYK (or spot colour) value for print and approve a printed proof first.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Light can produce colours that ink cannot. Choosing a print value and checking a proof manages the gamut difference."},{"t":"Instagram adds extra saturation to every image, so the posters actually show the true colour.","fb":"The screen shows the RGB colour as designed. The difference comes from light versus ink, not from Instagram."}]}},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Create and save a new colour in GIMP","intro":"<p>In a Paper 02 practical, you must create a custom colour sampled from a photo of a Montserrat sunset and save it for reuse. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the sunset photo in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Select the Color Picker tool (O) and click the orange area of the sky to sample it."},{"t":"Click the foreground colour swatch to open the Change Foreground Color dialog."},{"t":"Fine-tune the colour with the HSV or RGB sliders and note the hex value in the HTML notation box."},{"t":"Click OK, then add the colour to a new palette in the Palettes dialog (Windows > Dockable Dialogs > Palettes)."},{"t":"Apply the colour to your design and export as PNG with a clear file name, such as Candidate123_Sunset_Palette.png."}],"win":"Correct. Sample, refine, record the hex value, save it to a palette, then apply and export: that is how designers create and reuse new colours.","tutor":[{"h":"Sample first","p":"<p>You need the image open and a colour sampled before you can refine it.</p>"},{"h":"Export last","p":"<p>Saving and exporting always comes at the end of a practical task.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"New colour in GIMP (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the photo."},{"t":"Sample a colour with the Color Picker."},{"t":"Adjust it and note the hex code."},{"t":"Apply it and export as PNG."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Create and save a new colour in GIMP (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: eight steps, including creating and naming a palette so the colour can be reused across a whole campaign. Put the GIMP workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the Montserrat sunset photo in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Choose the Color Picker tool (O) and click the orange area of the sky."},{"t":"Click the foreground colour swatch to open the Change Foreground Color dialog."},{"t":"Raise the saturation slightly with the HSV sliders, then copy the hex value from the HTML notation box."},{"t":"Open the Palettes dialog (Windows > Dockable Dialogs > Palettes) and create a new palette named Sunset_Brand."},{"t":"Add the foreground colour to the new palette and name the swatch with its hex code."},{"t":"Fill the poster's headline shape with the saved colour."},{"t":"Export the design as PNG (File > Export As) with a clear file name."}],"win":"Correct. Sample, refine, record the hex value, create and name a palette, save the swatch, apply it and export: the colour is now reusable and documented."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour psychology and meaning","html":"<p><b>Colour psychology</b> is the study of how colours influence emotions, perceptions and behaviour. Build colour psychology into pre-production planning, and be ready to explain the visual impact of colour. Meanings are <b>tendencies, not fixed rules</b>, and they change with culture, context and personal experience.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Colour</th><th>Common associations</th><th>Typical digital use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Red</b></td><td>Energy, passion, urgency, danger, love</td><td>Sale banners, alerts, food brands, Carnival</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Orange</b></td><td>Friendliness, warmth, fun, affordability</td><td>Call-to-action buttons, youth brands</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Yellow</b></td><td>Optimism, sunshine, attention, caution</td><td>Highlights, warnings, tourism</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Green</b></td><td>Nature, health, growth, money, \"go\"</td><td>Eco-tourism, health apps, banking</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blue</b></td><td>Trust, calm, security, the sea and sky</td><td>Banks, telecoms, airlines, social media</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purple / violet</b></td><td>Royalty, luxury, creativity, mystery</td><td>Beauty products, premium services, fantasy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Black</b></td><td>Power, elegance, sophistication, mourning, evil</td><td>Luxury brands, dramatic film posters</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>White</b></td><td>Purity, simplicity, cleanliness, space</td><td>Minimalist sites, health care</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Culture and context matter.</b> In many Western traditions black is worn for mourning, while in parts of East Asia white is linked with funerals. Colours also gain local meaning: national flag colours carry pride, and supporters often wear their team or party colours. In Trinidad and Tobago, the red, white and black of the flag feature strongly in Independence Day and other national celebrations, while colours associated with the Pan-African movement (red, gold and green) often appear in Emancipation Day designs across the region. Always check your audience before assuming what a colour means.</p>\n<p><b>Colour and brand.</b> Consistent colour helps people recognise a brand quickly. Many banks and telecoms around the world, and in the Caribbean, use blues to suggest trust and reliability. When you choose a brand colour, explain it with a reason linked to the audience and purpose, not simply \"I like it\".</p>\n<p><b>Colour and behaviour.</b> Colours guide action: a high-contrast accent button stands out; red badges on app icons create urgency; calm blues and greens suit a mental health app. Keisha at Island Pulse Media A/B-tests two button colours on a hotel booking page and keeps the one that gets more taps, rather than relying on assumptions.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" questions on colour psychology reward balance. State the common association, apply it to the product, then add a limitation: \"However, colour meanings vary between cultures, so the designer should test the palette with the target audience.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Colours make people feel things. Red feels urgent and exciting, blue feels calm and trustworthy, green feels natural and healthy, and purple feels luxurious. But meanings change between cultures and places. National colours carry pride: think of the black, gold and green of Jamaica's flag. Always check what a colour means to your audience before you use it.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Colour psychology describes a <b>tendency</b>, not a law, and a Grade One answer says so. The red that signals urgency on a sale banner can signal danger on a health app, so context decides the meaning. Brand colour also depends on competitors: if every bank in a territory uses blue, a new credit union may choose green to stand out while still suggesting growth and money. Avoid stereotyped claims such as \"pink is for girls\"; many audiences now reject them. The strongest evidence is testing with the real audience, for example an A/B test of two button colours or a short survey. In a \"discuss\" question, give the association, apply it to the product, then add a limitation.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Colour psychology for clients","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five new clients across the Caribbean. Choose the most suitable main colour for each brief.</p>","items":[{"text":"Calm Waters counselling app for teenagers in Barbados wants users to feel safe and relaxed.","options":["bright red","soft blue and green","black and neon yellow"],"answer":1,"fb":"Soft cool colours are associated with calm and trust; red and neon would feel urgent."},{"text":"Flash Sale Friday campaign for a Spanish Town electronics shop needs urgency.","options":["bold red with yellow accents","pastel lavender","beige"],"answer":0,"fb":"Red signals urgency and energy, and yellow grabs attention."},{"text":"Nature Isle Treks company in Dominica promotes rainforest hikes.","options":["silver and black","hot pink","greens and earthy browns"],"answer":2,"fb":"Greens and earth tones connect directly with nature and the rainforest."},{"text":"Velvet Nights luxury spa in Rodney Bay, St. Lucia, wants a premium, elegant feel.","options":["deep purple, black and gold","primary red, yellow and blue","lime green and orange"],"answer":0,"fb":"Purple, black and gold suggest luxury and sophistication; primary triads feel playful."},{"text":"A designer assumes white will mean \"pure\" to every visitor of a global wedding site. What is the risk?","options":["white is linked to mourning in some cultures","white cannot be shown on screens","none: colours mean the same everywhere"],"answer":0,"fb":"Colour meanings vary by culture, so a global audience needs testing."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched colours to emotions and purposes, and remembered that culture changes colour meaning.","tutor":[{"h":"Feel first","p":"<p>Decide what feeling the client wants, then pick the colours most associated with it.</p>"},{"h":"Audience","p":"<p>Who is viewing? Meanings shift between cultures.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour psychology (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A calm, relaxing app should mainly use...","options":["bright red","soft blue"],"answer":1,"fb":"Soft blue suggests calm."},{"text":"A flash sale needs urgency. Use...","options":["red","pale grey"],"answer":0,"fb":"Red suggests urgency."},{"text":"A rainforest tour company suits...","options":["silver","greens"],"answer":1,"fb":"Green links to nature."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Colour psychology for clients (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder briefs: each one has a trade-off. Choose the BEST colour decision for each Island Pulse Media client.</p>","items":[{"text":"A credit union in St. Vincent wants to look trustworthy but stand out from three rival banks that all use blue.","options":["neon purple","the same navy blue as the banks","bright red","a deep green with gold accents"],"answer":3,"fb":"Green suggests growth and money and gold adds value; both look serious but differ from the blue rivals."},{"text":"A hurricane-alert app must show its most urgent warning level. Which approach is BEST?","options":["soft blue to keep people calm","yellow text on white","red plus a warning icon and the words \"Evacuate now\"","red alone"],"answer":2,"fb":"Red signals urgency, and the icon and words make sure everyone, including colour-blind users, understands."},{"text":"A children's reading app in Belize for ages 5 to 8 should feel playful but not overwhelming.","options":["a single brown hue","a triadic palette with one colour dominant and the other two as tints","pure red, yellow and blue in equal amounts on every screen","black and grey only"],"answer":1,"fb":"A triad is playful; letting one colour dominate and softening the others with tints keeps it calm enough for reading."},{"text":"A villa company in Anguilla wants an elegant site with charcoal-grey text on a black background. Older guests will read it on tablets outdoors. What is the main risk?","options":["low value contrast makes the text hard to read and fails accessibility checks","charcoal is unlucky in every culture","charcoal cannot be shown in RGB","there is no risk because luxury brands use dark colours"],"answer":0,"fb":"Dark grey on black has very little value contrast, which gets worse in sunlight and for older eyes."},{"text":"A wedding-planning site in Curaçao serves couples from many countries and uses white for \"purity\". What should the designer do?","options":["test the palette with the target cultures, because white is linked to mourning in some","switch every colour to black","nothing, because white means purity everywhere","use only red"],"answer":0,"fb":"Colour meaning depends on culture, so a global audience needs testing."},{"text":"Keisha's team cannot agree between orange and green for a \"Book now\" button on a blue hotel page. What settles it BEST?","options":["blue, to match the page","the director's favourite colour","an A/B test that measures which button gets more taps","alternating the colours daily without measuring"],"answer":2,"fb":"Evidence from real users beats opinion. An A/B test measures the effect directly."}],"win":"Excellent. You balanced colour meaning against competitors, accessibility, culture and evidence, which is what a \"discuss\" or \"evaluate\" question rewards."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Visual impact of colour combinations in digital formats","html":"<p>You must be able to explain the <b>visual impact of specific colour combinations</b> in a <b>digital format</b>. On screens, colours glow, are viewed on many different devices and must be readable by everyone. Five issues matter most.</p>\n<p><b>1. Contrast and legibility.</b> Text must stand out from its background. Strong value contrast (dark on light, or light on dark) is more important than hue. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) recommend a contrast ratio of at least <b>4.5:1</b> for normal text and <b>3:1</b> for large text. Free tools such as the WebAIM Contrast Checker test this using hex codes.</p>\n<p><b>2. Vibrating colours.</b> Saturated complementary colours of similar value, such as pure red text on pure green, seem to <b>vibrate</b> or flicker at their edges on screen and cause eye strain. Use complements for accents and shapes, not for body text on each other.</p>\n<p><b>3. Colour vision deficiency.</b> Roughly 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women have some form of colour vision deficiency, most often red-green. Never use colour alone to carry meaning: add icons, labels or patterns. A hurricane-warning map from the Island Pulse Media uses colour <b>and</b> labels such as \"Zone A\" so every viewer can read it. Figma, Photoshop and browser developer tools can simulate colour-blind views.</p>\n<p><b>4. Device and context.</b> The same colours look different on a cheap phone in bright Caribbean sunlight, on a calibrated monitor and on a projector. Pale tints may disappear outdoors; very dark designs may hide detail on low-brightness screens. Many apps now offer <b>dark mode</b>, so designers prepare a palette that works on both light and dark backgrounds. Use the <b>sRGB</b> colour profile for web images so colours display consistently.</p>\n<p><b>5. Hierarchy and focus.</b> Colour tells the viewer what to look at first. A neutral layout with one bright accent draws the eye straight to the call to action. Too many saturated colours fight for attention and make a page feel chaotic.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Combination</th><th>Impact on screen</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Black or navy text on white or a pale tint</td><td>Excellent legibility for body text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>White text on a dark teal or deep blue banner</td><td>Strong, calm and readable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow text on white</td><td>Very poor contrast; nearly invisible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Red text on green (similar values)</td><td>Vibrates; unreadable for many colour-blind users</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light grey text on white</td><td>Looks stylish but fails contrast checks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orange button on a blue page</td><td>Complementary accent; the button pops</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Next unit (M1U2B) builds on this with the rules of composition, such as the rule of thirds and the golden ratio, and the five principles of interactive design.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you justify a colour combination in Paper 02 or your SBA, mention <b>contrast</b> and <b>accessibility</b>, not just mood. \"White text on deep blue gives high contrast for readability on phones in bright sunlight\" is a Grade One reason.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>On screens, colour must be easy to read for everyone. Text needs strong contrast with its background, such as dark text on a light page. Bright opposite colours, like red text on green, seem to shake. Some people cannot tell red from green, so never use colour alone to show meaning. Phones used in bright Caribbean sunshine need even stronger contrast.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Contrast is measured, not guessed. WCAG asks for at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text, and a checker such as WebAIM calculates the ratio from two hex codes. Contrast depends on <b>value</b>, not hue: pure yellow (#FFFF00) on white scores barely above 1:1, while navy on white scores over 14:1. A common misconception is that colour-blind users see only black and white. Most see colour but confuse particular pairs, so the fix is a second cue, such as an icon, a label or a pattern. Dark mode is not simply inverted colours: saturated hues glow on dark backgrounds, so designers often use slightly desaturated tints in dark themes.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the colour problems","intro":"<p>Maya is auditing the Port Side Eats food-ordering website before launch. Several colour decisions could cause problems on screen.</p>","q":"Select ALL the colour decisions that will cause legibility or accessibility problems.","lines":["Pale yellow menu text on a white background.","Navy body text on a very pale cream background.","Order status shown only as a red or green dot, with no words.","Bright red headings on a bright green banner of similar lightness.","White \"Order now\" text on a dark teal button.","Light grey prices on a white background to look minimal.","An orange accent button on an otherwise calm blue page.","Error messages in red with a warning icon and the word \"Error\"."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Correct. Low contrast (yellow or light grey on white), colour-only meaning and vibrating complements all hurt legibility and accessibility. Dark-on-light text, a contrasting accent and colour plus icon plus words all work well.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask: is the value contrast strong, and would someone with red-green colour blindness still understand it? Colour plus words or icons is safe; colour alone is not.","tutor":[{"h":"Squint test","p":"<p>Squint at the combination. If the text and background blur together, the value contrast is too low.</p>"},{"h":"Colour alone","p":"<p>Meaning that depends only on red versus green fails many users.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the colour problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the combinations that are hard to read.","lines":["Yellow text on a white background.","Black text on a white background.","Light grey text on a white background.","White text on a dark blue button."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Yellow and light grey on white have poor contrast.","miss":"Look for light text on a light background. Try again."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the colour problems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Maya is auditing a food-delivery app for a client in Castries before launch. This audit includes contrast ratios, dark mode and charts. Some decisions look stylish but fail; others look plain but pass.</p>","q":"Select ALL the colour decisions that will cause legibility or accessibility problems.","lines":["Delivery status shown by a green, amber or red dot with no label.","Error fields outlined in red AND marked with an icon and the message \"Phone number missing\".","Light grey (#AAAAAA) prices on white, measuring about 2.3:1.","Navy (#1B2A4A) body text on white, measuring well above 4.5:1.","A bright red \"Sale\" label on a bright green banner of the same lightness.","In dark mode, pure blue (#0000FF) links on a near-black background.","A sales chart that uses a red line and a green line with no labels or patterns.","White text on a dark teal (#0F5E5E) \"Order now\" button.","Opening hours shown only in white text on a pale tint banner.","One orange accent button on an otherwise calm blue and white page."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,6,8],"win":"Correct. Colour-only meaning (status dots, unlabelled chart lines), low contrast (light grey or white on pale, pure blue on near-black) and vibrating complements all fail. Navy on white, white on dark teal, colour plus icon plus words, and a single contrasting accent all pass.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask two questions: is the value contrast strong enough (4.5:1 for normal text), and would someone with red-green colour deficiency still understand it? Pure blue looks bright but is very dark in value, so it fails on near-black."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Designing a hero and a villain with colour","html":"<p>Try a classic activity: <b>design a character for a game, book, comic or animation using colour theory to show whether it is a hero or a villain</b>. Audiences read colour, value and shape in a fraction of a second, so character designers use them to signal personality before a single word is spoken.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Design choice</th><th>Typical hero</th><th>Typical villain</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hue</b></td><td>Blues (trust), warm golds and yellows (hope), clean whites, friendly greens</td><td>Blacks, deep purples, blood reds, sickly yellow-greens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td>Lighter values and tints; bright, open faces</td><td>Darker values and shades; faces in shadow</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Saturation</b></td><td>Clear, bright, healthy colours</td><td>Either very desaturated (cold, lifeless) or harsh, glowing accents</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Harmony</b></td><td>Often analogous or a calm, balanced scheme</td><td>Clashing or high-tension complementary accents (a green glow on purple)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shape and line</b></td><td>Rounded shapes (friendly) and squares (strong, dependable)</td><td>Sharp triangles, spikes and jagged diagonal lines (danger)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Texture and lighting</b></td><td>Smooth surfaces, warm front lighting</td><td>Rough or cracked textures, low under-lighting, dark rim light</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Contrast between them.</b> Heroes and villains are often given <b>opposing palettes</b>, sometimes near-complementary, so that every fight scene is easy to read: a gold-and-blue hero against a purple-and-green villain. Many well-known animated villains combine purple, black and green.</p>\n<p><b>Break the rules on purpose.</b> Rules can be broken for surprise: a villain in innocent pastel pink feels unsettling. Just be ready to explain the intention.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean inspiration.</b> Regional folklore is full of characters to reinterpret: Anansi the trickster spider, Papa Bois the forest guardian and the fiery Soucouyant from Trinidad and Tobago, the Ol' Higue of Guyana and the jumbies told of across the islands. Malik at the Island Pulse Media is designing an animated short where a young Barbudan fisher girl, drawn with sea blues, a sunny yellow headwrap and rounded shapes, faces a storm spirit drawn with storm-grey shades, a sickly green glow and jagged lines inspired by Hurricane Irma's clouds.</p>\n<p><b>Digital technique.</b> Sketch the character in pencil, scan or photograph it, then colour it in GIMP, Krita or Inkscape on separate layers. Build a small palette (three to five colours) with hex codes, test the character against the game or comic background for contrast, and do a <b>greyscale check</b>: convert the image to greyscale to confirm the value contrast still separates hero from villain.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a written justification, link each colour choice to a <b>meaning</b> and a <b>theory term</b>: \"The villain's cloak uses a deep shade of violet, associated with mystery and power, with a complementary yellow-green glow to create unsettling tension.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Character designers use colour to show who is good and who is bad before anyone speaks. Heroes often get light, bright, warm or trustworthy colours and rounded shapes. Villains often get dark shades, harsh glowing accents and sharp, spiky shapes. Think of Papa Bois, the forest guardian, drawn in calm greens, against a fiery Soucouyant in burning reds and black.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The most important tool is <b>value</b>, not hue. If the hero and villain look the same when converted to greyscale, players will confuse them in fast action, whatever their colours. Strong designs give the two characters opposing palettes, often near-complementary, so every confrontation reads clearly. Colour must also work against the <b>background</b>: a blue hero in a blue sky disappears. Breaking the conventions can be powerful, for example a villain in sweet pastels, but a Grade One justification states the intention and its effect. Finally, check accessibility. If danger is shown only by turning red, players with red-green colour deficiency miss it, so pair colour with shape, movement or an icon.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Colour a hero and a villain","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is making a mobile game, <i>Guardians of the Salt Pond</i>, set in Bonaire. Zara asks you to choose the colours for the hero, a young pelican named Pip, and the villain, a pollution monster called Smog. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>First, Pip the hero. Players are aged 9 to 14 and should trust and cheer for Pip instantly. Which palette do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Dark grey and black with red eyes to look cool.","to":"bad1","fb":"Dark values and red eyes are villain signals. Players would distrust Pip."},{"t":"Warm golden-yellow and soft sea blue with white highlights; light values and rounded shapes.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Light values, hopeful yellows and trustworthy blues read clearly as a hero."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Now Smog the villain. Smog must look threatening and must stand out clearly from Pip in every scene. Which palette?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Deep purple and charcoal shades with a sickly yellow-green glow, sharp jagged shapes.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Dark shades, tense accents and spiky shapes read as danger and contrast strongly with Pip."},{"t":"The same golden-yellow and sea blue as Pip, but slightly darker.","to":"bad2","fb":"If hero and villain share a palette, players cannot tell them apart quickly in action."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The game background is a bright blue sky and turquoise salt pond. Malik worries Pip's blue may blend in. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a greyscale and contrast check, then give Pip a darker outline and more yellow so Pip stands out from the background.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. Checking value contrast against the background keeps the hero readable."},{"t":"Ignore it; players will find Pip eventually.","to":"bad3","fb":"Poor contrast with the background makes the hero hard to follow, especially on small phone screens."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Keisha notes that some players may have red-green colour vision deficiency. Smog's attacks are shown only by turning red. What do you change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Make the red brighter.","to":"bad4","fb":"Brighter red still relies on colour alone, which some players cannot distinguish."},{"t":"Add a shape change and an icon to each attack, so it does not rely on colour alone, and record all hex codes in the style guide.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Colour plus shape and icons makes the game accessible, and the style guide keeps it consistent."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Testers instantly cheer for Pip and boo Smog. A Grade One justification would explain hue meanings, value and saturation, contrasting palettes, background contrast and accessibility, all supported with colour theory terms and hex codes.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed signals.</b> Testers think Pip is the bad guy. Try again and use lighter, hopeful colours for the hero.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Who is who?</b> In battle scenes players keep confusing Pip and Smog. Try again and give the villain a contrasting palette.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Lost hero.</b> On small screens in sunlight, Pip disappears into the sky. Try again and check contrast against the background.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded players.</b> Colour-blind testers still cannot see when an attack is coming. Try again and add a non-colour cue.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Light vs dark","p":"<p>Heroes usually get lighter values and hopeful hues; villains darker shades and tense accents.</p>"},{"h":"Read at a glance","p":"<p>Hero, villain and background must all be clearly separated by colour and value.</p>"},{"h":"Not colour alone","p":"<p>Add shape, icons or words so every player gets the message.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour a hero and a villain (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: choose colours for Pip the hero and Smog the villain.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which palette suits Pip the hero?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Black and grey with red eyes.","to":"bad","fb":"Those are villain signals."},{"t":"Golden-yellow and sea blue, light and rounded.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Bright, hopeful colours suit a hero."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Which palette suits Smog the villain?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The same colours as Pip.","to":"bad","fb":"Players could not tell them apart."},{"t":"Deep purple and charcoal with a sickly green glow.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Dark shades and tense accents signal danger."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your hero and villain are clearly different through colour.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about what each colour signals. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Colour a hero and a villain (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief for <i>Guardians of the Salt Pond</i>. Players are aged 9 to 14 and play on budget phones, often outdoors. The game background is a bright blue sky over a turquoise pond. Every option sounds reasonable; some end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>First, Pip the pelican hero. Players must trust Pip instantly AND see Pip clearly against the blue background. Which palette do you propose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A warm golden-yellow body, a soft sea-blue wing tip, a dark navy outline and white highlights, with rounded shapes.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Yellow reads as hopeful and stands out from the blue background, and the outline adds value contrast."},{"t":"Charcoal and red with sharp beak lines, so Pip looks tough.","to":"bad1","fb":"Dark values, red and sharp lines are villain signals."},{"t":"Sky blue and turquoise to match the Salt Pond, so Pip feels part of the island.","to":"ok1","fb":"Friendly colours, but Pip will blend into the background."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Now Smog, the pollution monster. Smog must look threatening and must contrast strongly with Pip. Which palette and harmony do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A darker golden-yellow and blue, close to Pip's palette.","to":"bad2","fb":"A shared palette makes hero and villain hard to tell apart."},{"t":"Deep violet and charcoal shades with a sickly yellow-green glow and jagged shapes; violet sits opposite Pip's yellow on the wheel.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Dark shades, a tense accent and spikes read as danger, and the complementary relationship separates the two characters."},{"t":"Bright red and orange flames in an analogous scheme, with rounded, bubbly shapes.","to":"ok2","fb":"Red-orange is menacing, but it is warm like Pip's yellow, and rounded shapes soften the threat."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>On the studio laptop, Pip and Smog look clearly different. Before launch, which check matters MOST for this audience?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the art director whether the colours look nice.","to":"ok3","fb":"Expert opinion helps, but it does not test the players' real screens or conditions."},{"t":"Increase the saturation of everything so it pops.","to":"bad3","fb":"More saturation does not add value contrast and can make colours vibrate."},{"t":"A greyscale value check plus a test on a budget phone outdoors, to make sure both characters stay readable on small, sunlit screens.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. Value contrast and real devices in real light are what this audience will experience."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Smog's attacks are shown only by Smog turning red. About 1 in 12 boys has some colour vision deficiency. What do you change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a shape change (spikes grow) and a warning icon to every attack, and record all hex codes and rules in the style guide.","to":"n5","fb":"Good. A non-colour cue makes the warning accessible, and the style guide keeps it consistent."},{"t":"Make the red brighter and make it flash.","to":"bad4","fb":"It still relies on colour, and fast flashing can be a risk for some players."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Zara asks you to justify the palettes in the design document. Which justification is strongest?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Pip uses warm yellow (#F2B705) for hope and a navy outline for contrast against the sky. Smog uses deep violet (#3D1A5B), Pip's complement, with a yellow-green glow for tension. Value contrast keeps them distinct in greyscale, and attacks use shape and icons as well as colour.\"","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Each choice has a colour, a theory term and an effect on the player."},{"t":"\"Pip is yellow because yellow is a happy colour, and Smog is purple because purple looks cool.\"","to":"ok4","fb":"There is a meaning for Pip, but no theory terms, no hex codes and no reason for Smog."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One design.</b> Testers cheer for Pip and boo Smog, colour-blind testers spot every attack, and your justification links hue, value, saturation, harmony, background contrast and accessibility with exact hex codes.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost in the sky.</b> Players like Pip but keep losing Pip against the background. A partial success: check contrast against the scene, not just the character's meaning.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Warm against warm.</b> Smog is scary, but in busy scenes the two warm characters blur. A partial success: give the villain a palette that contrasts with the hero.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Fine on the laptop only.</b> The director approves, but players outdoors struggle to see Smog's dark shapes. A partial success: test on the real devices and in the real light.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Weak justification.</b> The game works, but the design document would score low. A partial success: give each colour a hex code, a theory term and an effect.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed signals.</b> Testers think Pip is the bad guy. Try again and give the hero lighter, hopeful colours and rounded shapes.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Who is who?</b> In battle scenes players keep confusing Pip and Smog. Try again and give the villain a contrasting palette.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Eye strain.</b> The over-saturated colours vibrate on small screens and the characters still blend. Try again and check value contrast.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded players.</b> Colour-blind testers still cannot see when an attack is coming. Try again and add a non-colour cue.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme","genre":"Structured response: applying colour theory to character design (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Antigua-based studio Wadadli Comics is producing a digital comic, published on its website and on Instagram, based on Caribbean folklore. The hero is a young Anansi-inspired spider girl who protects her village; the villain is a fiery Soucouyant-inspired spirit. The studio wants the characters to be recognised instantly on phone screens.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a design justification for the studio that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>describes</b> a colour palette for the hero and a palette for the villain, naming at least two colours each with a hex code;</li><li><b>identifies</b> the harmony rule (for example, analogous, complementary, triadic, split-complementary or tetradic) used in each palette, or between the two characters;</li><li><b>explains</b> how hue, value (tints and shades) and saturation communicate hero or villain;</li><li><b>explains</b> TWO considerations for displaying these colours in a digital format (for example, contrast, colour-blind users, RGB vs CMYK, device screens).</li></ol>","min":200,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's palette is ... (#......) and ... (#......). The villain's palette is ... (#......) and ... (#......).<br><b>(b)</b> The hero uses a(n) ... scheme because ... Between the two characters, ... creates contrast.<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's ... hue suggests ... and lighter tints make her ... In contrast, the villain's darker shades of ... and ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ...</p>","checklist":["I named at least two colours for each character with valid hex codes (#RRGGBB).","I named a harmony rule correctly and explained how it is found on the colour wheel.","I used the terms hue, value, saturation, tint or shade correctly.","I linked each colour to a meaning and to hero or villain.","I explained two digital-format considerations such as contrast or accessibility.","I referred to the comic, the folklore characters or phone screens throughout."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Colour palette and harmony rule","marks":5,"look":["#[0-9a-f]{6}","complement|analog|triad|split|tetrad|monochrom","wheel|opposite|adjacent|next to|evenly","blue|gold|yellow|green|teal|white","red|orange|purple|violet|black|crimson|ember"],"min":3,"strong":"Your palettes are specific and your harmony rule is correctly identified. Next step: explain why the scheme suits each character.","weak":"Name real colours with hex codes (for example #1E5FA8 for a sea blue) and identify a harmony rule by its position on the colour wheel: opposite, adjacent or evenly spaced."},{"criterion":"Use of colour theory to communicate character","marks":5,"look":["hue","value|light|dark","tint|shade|tone","saturat|vivid|intens|muted","trust|hope|danger|evil|menace|warm|calm|threat"],"min":3,"strong":"You used colour theory terms precisely to explain how the characters read as hero and villain.","weak":"Use the terms hue, value, saturation, tint and shade, and link each to a feeling, for example \"dark shades of crimson suggest danger\"."},{"criterion":"Digital format considerations","marks":5,"look":["contrast","colou?r.?blind|deficien|accessib|wcag","rgb|screen|phone|device|srgb","cmyk|print|background|instagram|website"],"min":2,"strong":"Strong understanding of how colour behaves on screens and for different users.","weak":"Explain at least two digital issues: contrast against the background, colour-blind users (do not rely on colour alone), RGB for screens versus CMYK for print, or different phone screens."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's palette is a sea blue (#1E5FA8) for her body, a warm gold (#F2B705) for her headwrap and web patterns, and a pale aqua tint (#BFE9F2) for highlights. The villain's palette is a deep crimson (#8B0A1A), an ember orange (#F2541B) for her flames and a charcoal shade (#2B2B2B) for shadows.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The villain uses an <b>analogous</b> scheme of crimson, red-orange and orange, which sit next to each other on the colour wheel and work together like a single blaze of fire. The hero's blue and gold come from opposite sides of the wheel, so they form a near-<b>complementary</b> pair, which makes her pop. Between the two characters, the hero's cool blue and the villain's warm red-orange are also close to complementary, so every confrontation panel has strong, readable contrast.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The hero's blue <b>hue</b> is associated with trust and calm, and the gold suggests hope and warmth. Lighter <b>tints</b> and high, clean <b>saturation</b> make her face open and friendly. In contrast, the villain's crimson is a darker <b>shade</b> of red, associated with danger and anger, while the glowing, highly saturated orange flames feel aggressive. Her face sits in the charcoal shade, so low <b>value</b> makes her menacing. A greyscale check confirms that the hero is light and the villain is dark even without colour.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, <b>contrast</b>: the comic is read on phones, often outdoors in bright sunlight, so the speech bubbles will use near-black text (#1A1A1A) on white, which passes the WCAG 4.5:1 guideline, and the hero's dark outline keeps her visible against the blue sky. Second, <b>colour vision deficiency</b>: readers with red-green colour blindness may find the villain's reds hard to tell apart, so her flames will also have a jagged shape and flickering texture, so the meaning never depends on colour alone. Because the comic is digital, all colours are defined in RGB with the sRGB profile; if the studio later prints a book, they must convert to CMYK and check that the bright ember orange has not dulled.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Colour justifications earn marks when every choice has three parts: the colour (with a hex code), the theory term (hue, shade, complementary) and the effect on the audience. Avoid \"I chose blue because it looks nice\". Add a digital consideration, such as contrast or accessibility, to reach the top band.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: colour theory for character design, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO colours for the hero. Give a hex code for at least one of them. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO colours for the villain. Give a hex code for at least one of them. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>For ONE hero colour and ONE villain colour, <b>state</b> the feeling each colour gives. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> whether the villain's colours should be lighter or darker than the hero's. Use the word <b>tint</b> or <b>shade</b>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE thing the studio should check so the comic is easy to read on a phone. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's colours are ... (#......) and ...<br><b>(b)</b> The villain's colours are ... (#......) and ...<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's ... makes readers feel ... The villain's ... makes readers feel ...<br><b>(d)</b> The villain's colours should be ... because a ... is ...<br><b>(e)</b> The studio should check ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I named two hero colours with at least one hex code.","I named two villain colours with at least one hex code.","I gave a feeling for one colour of each character.","I used the word tint or shade correctly.","I gave one phone-screen check and a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Hero colours","marks":2,"look":["blue|gold|yellow|green|teal|white|aqua","#[0-9a-f]{6}"],"min":2,"strong":"Two hero colours with a hex code.","weak":"Name two colours for the hero and write one as a hex code, such as #1E5FA8 for sea blue."},{"criterion":"Villain colours","marks":2,"look":["red|crimson|orange|purple|violet|black|charcoal|ember","#[0-9a-f]{6}"],"min":2,"strong":"Two villain colours with a hex code.","weak":"Name two colours for the villain and write one as a hex code, such as #8B0A1A for deep crimson."},{"criterion":"Colour meaning","marks":2,"look":["trust|hope|calm|safe|friend|kind|happy|warm","danger|anger|evil|scary|threat|fear|menac"],"min":2,"strong":"You linked a colour of each character to a feeling.","weak":"Say what each colour makes readers feel: blue can feel trustworthy; dark red can feel dangerous."},{"criterion":"Tint or shade","marks":2,"look":["tint|shade","dark|light|black|white"],"min":2,"strong":"You used tint or shade correctly.","weak":"A shade is a colour with black added, so it is darker. Villains usually use darker shades."},{"criterion":"Phone-screen check","marks":2,"look":["contrast|read|colou?r.?blind|background|bright|sun","because|so |easy|clear|see"],"min":2,"strong":"A sensible check with a reason.","weak":"Check that the text has strong contrast with its background so readers can see it on a small phone screen."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's colours are sea blue (#1E5FA8) and warm gold.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The villain's colours are deep crimson (#8B0A1A) and charcoal black.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The hero's sea blue makes readers feel she can be trusted. The villain's deep crimson makes readers feel danger and anger.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The villain's colours should be darker, because a shade is a colour with black added, and dark shades look more threatening than the hero's light tints.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The studio should check that the speech-bubble text has strong contrast with its background, because readers use small phone screens, often in bright sunlight.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part. Accept any sensible colours, but at least one valid #RRGGBB code is needed in (a) and (b). In (d), insist on the correct meaning of tint (white added) or shade (black added). Students ready for more can add a harmony rule to their answer, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: colour theory for character design, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Antigua-based studio Wadadli Comics is producing a digital comic based on Caribbean folklore, published on its website (which has a dark mode) and on Instagram. The hero is a young Anansi-inspired spider girl who protects her village; the villain is a Soucouyant-inspired spirit who, in the folklore, appears as an old woman by day and becomes a ball of fire at night. The studio wants the characters recognised instantly on phone screens, and plans a printed collector's edition next year.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a design justification for the studio that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>specifies</b> a palette of three colours for each character, with hex codes, and states how the 60-30-10 guide applies to each <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>analyses</b> the harmony relationships within each palette AND between the two characters, using positions on the colour wheel <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> how hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together communicate hero or villain, including the result of a greyscale check <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE digital-format considerations (for example contrast with a WCAG ratio, colour vision deficiency, dark mode, RGB to CMYK for the print edition) and proposes a solution for each <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>justifies</b> ONE deliberate choice that breaks a colour convention, explaining its effect on readers <i>(4 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":380,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Hero: ... (#......, 60%), ... (#......, 30%), ... (#......, 10%). Villain: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Within the hero's palette ... sit ... on the wheel, forming ... The villain's ... are ... Between the characters ...<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's hue ... her light values ... In contrast ... In greyscale ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, contrast: ... Second, ... Third, ...<br><b>(e)</b> One convention I break is ... because ... Readers will ...</p>","checklist":["Each character has three colours with valid hex codes and a 60-30-10 split.","I analysed harmony within each palette and between the characters, using wheel positions.","I evaluated hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together, with a greyscale result.","I evaluated three digital considerations, each with a practical solution.","I justified one convention-breaking choice and its effect on readers.","I referred to the folklore, the phone screens, dark mode and the print edition."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Palettes with 60-30-10","marks":5,"look":["#[0-9a-f]{6}","60","30","10","hero|spider|anansi","villain|soucouyant|spirit"],"min":4,"strong":"Precise palettes with hex codes and clear proportions.","weak":"Give three hex codes per character and say which is the 60% main colour, the 30% secondary and the 10% accent."},{"criterion":"Harmony analysis","marks":5,"look":["complement|split|analog|triad|tetrad","opposite|adjacent|next to|either side|evenly","wheel","between|across|both characters|contrast"],"min":3,"strong":"Harmony analysed within and between palettes using wheel positions.","weak":"Name the scheme in each palette, show where the colours sit on the wheel, then explain the relationship between the hero and the villain."},{"criterion":"Colour theory evaluation","marks":5,"look":["hue","value","saturat","shape|line|round|jagged|spik","light|rim|shadow","greyscale|grayscale"],"min":4,"strong":"Several properties evaluated together, confirmed by a greyscale check.","weak":"Explain hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together, and say what a greyscale test shows."},{"criterion":"Digital considerations","marks":6,"look":["contrast|wcag|4\\.5","colou?r.?blind|deficien|accessib","dark mode|dark theme","cmyk|print|proof|gamut","srgb|rgb|screen|phone"],"min":4,"strong":"Three considerations evaluated, each with a workable solution.","weak":"Choose three: contrast (with a ratio), colour vision deficiency, dark mode or print conversion. Give a problem and a solution for each."},{"criterion":"Justified convention-breaking choice","marks":4,"look":["convention|rule|expect|usual|surprise","because|so that|therefore","reader|audience|viewer","day|old woman|pastel|soft|disguise|foreshadow"],"min":3,"strong":"A deliberate choice, clearly justified by its effect on readers.","weak":"Describe one choice that breaks a convention, why you made it and how readers will react."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Hero:</i> sea blue #1E5FA8 (60%, body and costume), warm gold #F2B705 (30%, headwrap and web patterns) and pale aqua tint #BFE9F2 (10%, highlights in her eyes and web). <i>Villain (night form):</i> charcoal #2B2B2B (60%, body and shadows), deep crimson #8B0A1A (30%, robes) and ember orange #F2541B (10%, the glowing flames). In each palette the 10% accent sits where I want the reader's eye: the hero's eyes and the villain's fire.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The villain's crimson (red) and ember orange (red-orange) sit next to each other on the wheel, so they are <b>analogous</b> and read as one blaze of fire; the charcoal is neutral and simply deepens the value. The hero's blue and gold (close to yellow-orange) form one half of blue's <b>split-complementary</b> scheme, giving her strong but not harsh contrast. Across both characters the relationship is even neater: blue's split-complement is yellow-orange and red-orange, so the hero's gold and the villain's ember complete one split-complementary scheme. Every confrontation panel therefore sets cool blue against warm fire.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The hero's blue <b>hue</b> suggests trust and calm, and her gold suggests hope. Her light <b>values</b> and clean, high <b>saturation</b> keep her face open, and rounded shapes with soft front lighting feel friendly. The villain's crimson is a dark <b>shade</b> linked to danger; her face sits in charcoal with low under-lighting and an ember rim light, and her flames are jagged, highly saturated shapes that feel aggressive. In a greyscale check the hero reads as mid-to-light grey and the villain as near-black with bright flame edges, so the two remain distinct even without colour.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Contrast:</i> speech bubbles use near-black #1A1A1A on white, about 17:1, far above the WCAG 4.5:1 guideline for readers on sunlit phones. <i>Colour vision deficiency:</i> red-green colour-blind readers may not separate crimson from charcoal easily, so the villain's danger is also carried by flickering, jagged flame shapes and a crackling sound-effect lettering style, never by colour alone. <i>Dark mode:</i> on the website's near-black theme, the charcoal villain could vanish, so each panel has its own light border and the villain has an ember rim light. <i>Print edition:</i> the comic is drawn in sRGB; the vivid ember orange may fall outside the CMYK gamut and print duller, so the studio should choose a CMYK value and approve a printed proof.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I break the \"villains are dark\" convention in the daytime panels. Following the folklore, the Soucouyant appears as a kindly old woman in soft, muted tones of lavender and cream, with lighter values than the hero. Readers will trust her at first, which builds suspense, and the reveal at nightfall, when she bursts into crimson and ember, has far more impact. To keep it fair, one small crimson accent, a scarf, appears in every daytime panel as foreshadowing, so attentive readers can spot the clue.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 5 + 5 + 5 + 6 + 4). Grade One responses go beyond naming schemes: they analyse the relationship between the two characters, confirm value contrast with a greyscale test and give a practical solution for each digital issue. Check every hex code is a valid #RRGGBB value and every harmony claim is correct on the 12-part RYB wheel. In (e), reward a choice that is justified by its effect on readers, not just described.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U2B","title":"Composition and principles of interactive design","objectives":["Apply the rules of composition: the rule of thirds, the golden ratio, the golden rectangle and the golden section.","Explain how point of interest and the prominence of the subject are created in a frame or layout.","Use composition elements: perspective, camera angles, lighting, repetition, proximity, white or negative space, balance, contrast, alignment and hierarchy.","Design a two-dimensional environment for a storyboard frame using rules of composition.","Explain and apply the principles of interactive design: consistency, perceivability, learnability, predictability and feedback, using real app and website examples."],"videos":[{"title":"Rule of thirds and the golden ratio","q":"rule of thirds vs golden ratio composition explained photography design"},{"title":"Camera angles and shot types","q":"camera angles and shot types explained high angle low angle dutch angle"},{"title":"Visual hierarchy and white space in design","q":"visual hierarchy white space balance contrast alignment graphic design basics"},{"title":"Principles of interaction design","q":"interaction design principles consistency feedback learnability predictability"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Composition decides what the eye sees first","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Composition</b> is the deliberate arrangement of elements in a frame (a photo, a video shot, a poster, a web page or a storyboard panel). It decides what the eye sees first.</p>\n<p>Composition has three jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>It directs attention</b>: the viewer's eye goes where the designer wants it to go.</li>\n<li><b>It creates order</b>: the viewer knows what to look at first, second and third.</li>\n<li><b>It sets mood</b>: a centred, still frame feels calm; a tilted, crowded frame feels tense.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good composition makes a message clear; poor composition makes it confusing, even when the photo is sharp and the colours are attractive. Remember: <b>rules are guidelines. Know them before you break them.</b> Breaking a rule on purpose (a centred subject for calm symmetry) is a design choice; breaking it by accident is a mistake.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>The rule of thirds puts subjects on power points.</b> Divide the frame into <b>nine equal parts</b> using two equally spaced horizontal lines and two equally spaced vertical lines. The <b>four crossings are power points</b> (also called sweet spots). Place the subject on one of the lines or, better still, on a power point.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" width=\"300\" height=\"180\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rule of thirds grid with subject on the upper right intersection\">\n<rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"296\" height=\"176\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"2\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n<line x1=\"200\" y1=\"2\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n<line x1=\"2\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"298\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n<line x1=\"2\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"298\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n<circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"60\" r=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<path d=\"M2 130 Q 80 118 150 128 T 298 124\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">subject</text>\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">horizon on lower third</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Place the horizon on the upper or lower third, never the middle.</b> At Grand Anse Beach, a horizon on the lower third stresses a dramatic sky; on the upper third it stresses the beach and boats.</li>\n<li><b>Leave lead room in front of moving or looking subjects.</b> A sailboat crossing Grand Anse Bay needs more space in front of it than behind it. In a newspaper interview photo, leave space on the side the speaker faces.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An off-centre subject creates movement and lets the background tell part of the story. Most phone cameras can show a thirds grid; in GIMP the Crop tool offers a \"Rule of thirds\" guide, and Canva and Figma let you add guides.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, do not just write \"I used the rule of thirds\". Say <b>where</b> the subject sits and <b>why</b>: \"The dancer is placed on the left vertical third, facing right, leaving lead room that suggests movement along the Carnival route.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Composition is how you arrange things in a frame so the viewer looks where you want. The rule of thirds splits the frame into nine equal boxes. Put your subject on a line or where two lines cross, and put the horizon on the top or bottom third, not the middle. Leave space in front of a moving subject, like a sailboat crossing Carlisle Bay in Barbados.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The rule of thirds works because an off-centre subject creates tension and movement and gives the background a role in the story. The strongest answers also know when to break it. A centred, symmetrical frame suits calm, formal or powerful subjects, such as a speaker at a podium or a church perfectly reflected in still water. Breaking the rule is a choice only if you can say why. Watch lead room carefully: a subject facing the edge of the frame feels cramped, which can be used on purpose to suggest someone trapped. In Paper 02, always name the position (left third, lower-right power point), the technique and the effect on the viewer.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"The golden ratio gives harmonious proportion","html":"<p>The <b>golden ratio</b> gives harmonious proportion. <b>Phi (φ) is about 1 : 1.618.</b> It appears in shell spirals, sunflower seed heads and many buildings, and designers use it because the proportion tends to feel balanced and natural. <b>The golden rectangle and spiral lead the eye inward.</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>How designers use it</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Golden ratio</b></td><td>Two quantities where the larger divided by the smaller equals the whole divided by the larger: about 1.618.</td><td>Setting proportions, e.g. a main column about 1.6 times the width of a sidebar.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Golden section</b></td><td>The point that divides a line in the golden ratio: about 61.8% of the way along (or 38.2% from the other end).</td><td>Placing a horizon or subject on the golden section lines rather than the thirds lines (this creates the <b>phi grid</b>).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Golden rectangle</b></td><td>A rectangle whose sides are in the golden ratio. Cut a square from it and the leftover piece is another golden rectangle.</td><td>Choosing frame or card proportions; repeated division produces the <b>golden spiral</b>.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 324 200\" width=\"324\" height=\"200\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Golden rectangle divided into a square and a smaller golden rectangle with a spiral\">\n<rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"320\" height=\"196\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"198\" y1=\"2\" x2=\"198\" y2=\"198\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"198\" y1=\"123\" x2=\"322\" y2=\"123\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"275\" y1=\"123\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"198\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<path d=\"M2 198 A196 196 0 0 1 198 2 A124 124 0 0 1 322 123 A75 75 0 0 1 247 198\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">square</text>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">golden rectangle</text>\n</svg>\n<p><b>Rule of thirds or golden section?</b> The phi grid lines sit a little closer to the centre than the thirds lines (about 38% and 62% instead of 33% and 67%). The rule of thirds is a quick, simplified version of the same idea. The golden spiral is used to lead the eye in a curve towards the subject, for example the curl of a wave at Bathsheba ending on a surfer.</p>\n<p><b>Poster example:</b> a poster is <b>24 in tall</b>. 24 divided by 1.618 is about 14.8, so the golden section sits about 14.8 in from the bottom, or about <b>9.2 in</b> from the top. The headline goes at <b>9.2 in</b>. The type sizes follow phi too: a <b>72 pt heading</b> and a <b>44 pt subheading</b> (72 divided by 44 is about 1.6).</p>\n<p>Questions may name the rule of thirds <b>or</b> the golden section/rectangle, so you should be able to explain both and say which you chose.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap gives you \"1.618\", \"61.8%\" and \"a square plus a smaller golden rectangle\" as options. All three describe the golden family; read carefully whether the question asks for the <b>ratio</b>, the <b>section</b> (a point on a line) or the <b>rectangle</b> (a shape).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The golden ratio is about 1 to 1.618. Shapes with this proportion tend to look balanced and natural, which is why it appears in shells, flowers and buildings. The golden section is a point about 62% of the way along a line. A designer laying out a web page for a Grenada spice shop might make the main column about 1.6 times wider than the sidebar.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Keep the three terms apart: the <b>ratio</b> is a number (about 1.618), the <b>section</b> is a point on a line (about 61.8% along) and the <b>rectangle</b> is a shape whose sides are in that ratio. Be ready to calculate. On a 1,080 px wide Instagram post, 1,080 ÷ 1.618 ≈ 667 px, so the golden section lines sit at about 413 px and 667 px from the left, closer to the centre than the thirds lines at 360 px and 720 px. A common misconception is that the golden ratio is a law of beauty; the evidence is mixed, and many attractive designs ignore it. Treat it as a proportion tool and say why you chose it over the simpler thirds grid.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is framing shots for a tourism reel about English Harbour in Antigua. Answer each question.</p>","items":[{"text":"Malik places the yacht exactly where the top-right grid lines cross in a 3 by 3 grid. He is using...","options":["a golden spiral","radial balance","the rule of thirds"],"answer":2,"fb":"The intersections of a 3 by 3 grid are the power points of the rule of thirds."},{"text":"The golden ratio is approximately...","options":["1 : 1.618","1 : 1.333","1 : 1.777"],"answer":0,"fb":"Phi is about 1.618. 1.333 is the 4:3 aspect ratio and 1.777 is 16:9, which are traps."},{"text":"A point about 61.8% along a line, dividing it in the golden ratio, is called the...","options":["vanishing point","golden section","golden rectangle"],"answer":1,"fb":"The golden section is a point (or line) that divides a length in the golden ratio."},{"text":"He wants the sea horizon to emphasise a dramatic sunset sky. Where should the horizon go?","options":["exactly in the centre","on the upper third line","on the lower third line"],"answer":2,"fb":"A low horizon gives about two thirds of the frame to the sky, which is the part he wants to emphasise."},{"text":"A rectangle that leaves another rectangle of the same proportions when a square is removed is a...","options":["safe frame","thirds grid","golden rectangle"],"answer":2,"fb":"That self-repeating property defines the golden rectangle and produces the golden spiral."},{"text":"A jet-ski rider is moving left to right. Where should Malik leave the most space?","options":["above the rider","behind the rider, on the left","in front of the rider, on the right"],"answer":2,"fb":"Lead room in front of a moving or looking subject gives it somewhere to go and feels natural."},{"text":"On a 24 in tall poster, where does the golden section put the headline, measured from the top?","options":["about 12 in","about 8 in","about 9.2 in"],"answer":2,"fb":"24 divided by 1.618 is about 14.8 in; 24 minus 14.8 leaves about 9.2 in from the top. 8 in is the thirds line and 12 in is the dead centre."}],"win":"Excellent. You can tell the rule of thirds from the golden ratio, section and rectangle, and you used lead room and horizon placement with purpose.","tutor":[{"h":"Grid or ratio?","p":"<p>Rule of thirds = a 3 by 3 grid. Golden family = the number 1.618.</p>"},{"h":"Point, shape or number?","p":"<p>Section is a point on a line; rectangle is a shape; ratio is a number.</p>"},{"h":"Space to move","p":"<p>Subjects need space in the direction they face or travel.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Thirds or golden? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Placing a subject where the lines of a 3 by 3 grid cross is the...","options":["golden spiral","rule of thirds"],"answer":1,"fb":"Yes: grid intersections are the rule of thirds power points."},{"text":"The golden ratio is about...","options":["1.333","1.618"],"answer":1,"fb":"Phi is about 1.618."},{"text":"A moving subject needs more space...","options":["in front of it","behind it"],"answer":0,"fb":"Lead room goes in front."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Thirds or golden? Quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: calculations on a 1,920 px wide video frame, phi type sizes and two judgement calls. Malik is framing shots for a tourism reel.</p>","items":[{"text":"On a 1,920 px wide frame, where are the two vertical rule-of-thirds lines?","options":["at 480 px and 1,440 px","at 640 px and 1,280 px","at 960 px only","at 733 px and 1,187 px"],"answer":1,"fb":"1,920 ÷ 3 = 640, so the lines are at 640 px and 1,280 px."},{"text":"On the same frame, the golden section (phi grid) lines sit at about...","options":["960 px","1,000 px and 1,500 px","640 px and 1,280 px","733 px and 1,187 px"],"answer":3,"fb":"1,920 ÷ 1.618 ≈ 1,187 px, and 1,920 − 1,187 ≈ 733 px."},{"text":"Compared with the thirds lines, the phi grid lines are...","options":["horizontal only","in exactly the same place","closer to the centre","closer to the edges"],"answer":2,"fb":"About 38% and 62% instead of 33% and 67%: a little closer to the centre."},{"text":"The heading is 72 pt. Using phi, which subheading size fits best?","options":["about 60 pt","about 54 pt","about 36 pt","about 44 pt"],"answer":3,"fb":"72 ÷ 1.618 ≈ 44.5, so about 44 pt."},{"text":"A news presenter is filmed dead centre, perfectly symmetrical, speaking straight to camera. Is ignoring the rule of thirds a mistake here?","options":["No, because the rule of thirds applies only to photos","No, a centred, symmetrical frame suits a formal, direct address","Yes, because centring always looks amateur","Yes, every subject must sit on a third"],"answer":1,"fb":"Breaking the rule on purpose is a design choice. Centring suits calm, formal, direct-to-camera shots."},{"text":"A frigate bird flies from RIGHT to LEFT across the frame. Which composition is BEST?","options":["bird dead centre","bird on the left third with the space behind it","bird on the right third, with open space to its left","bird on the left third, facing the left edge"],"answer":2,"fb":"Lead room goes in front of the movement. Flying left, the bird needs space on the left, so it sits on the right third."},{"text":"A golden spiral is BEST used to...","options":["set the aspect ratio of a video","measure colour contrast","lead the eye in a curve towards the subject","divide the frame into nine equal parts"],"answer":2,"fb":"The spiral is a path for the eye that ends on the point of interest."}],"win":"Excellent. You can calculate thirds and phi lines, use phi for type sizes, place lead room by direction of movement and justify breaking the rule."}},{"type":"learn","title":"One clear point of interest earns prominence","html":"<p>The <b>point of interest</b> (or <b>focal point</b>) is the place the eye goes to first. The <b>prominence of the subject</b> is how strongly it stands out from everything else. Every frame, poster or screen needs <b>one clear point of interest</b>; if everything shouts, nothing is heard.</p>\n<p>There are seven tools in two groups.</p>\n<p><b>Size, placement, contrast, isolation:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Size</b>: the biggest element usually wins attention.</li>\n<li><b>Placement</b>: on a power point or golden section line.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast</b>: light on dark, a bright colour among neutrals, sharp against soft.</li>\n<li><b>Isolation</b>: the subject alone, with plenty of negative space around it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Leading lines, depth of field, framing:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Leading lines</b>: roads, railings, piers or shorelines pointing at the subject. The Richard Haynes Boardwalk in Barbados is a natural leading line.</li>\n<li><b>Depth of field</b>: a shallow depth of field (\"portrait mode\") blurs the background so the subject pops.</li>\n<li><b>Framing</b>: a doorway, window, arch or palm leaves make a frame within the frame.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>The two-second test:</b> can a viewer say what it is about in two seconds? If not, the point of interest is not clear enough.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> the sorrel-drink brand \"Sweet Hill\" wants a product shot. A Grade One composition places the bottle on the right third, lit from the side, with a blurred view of the hills behind, a leading line from the edge of a wooden counter, and nothing else on the table. A weak shot has the bottle in the centre among glasses, fruit, a phone and a menu, all equally sharp and bright.</p>\n<p>In interface design the same idea applies. On an electricity bill payment screen the main action, the \"Pay now\" button, should be the most prominent element: larger, higher contrast and surrounded by space.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"explain how the subject is made prominent\", give at least <b>three different techniques</b> and say what each one does to the viewer's eye. One technique, however well described, rarely earns full marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Every design needs one main thing the eye sees first, called the point of interest. Make it stand out by making it bigger, placing it on a power point, giving it strong contrast, leaving space around it, pointing lines at it, blurring the background or framing it. Then ask: can someone tell what this is about in two seconds? A sorrel drink advert in Kingston should pass that test.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Prominence is relative: an element stands out only because of what surrounds it. One bright red button is prominent on a neutral page; on a page full of red banners it disappears. That is why removing elements is often the fastest way to create a focal point. Strong answers combine techniques, because each works on a different part of seeing: size and contrast catch the eye, leading lines direct it and isolation lets it rest. A common error is to give a secondary element the same weight as the subject, such as a sponsor logo as big as the product. In an interface, the most prominent element should match the user's main task, not the client's favourite feature.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the prominence techniques","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media wrote these notes about a Carnival poster for Trinidad and Tobago. Some describe techniques that make the main dancer more prominent; others weaken her prominence.</p>","q":"Select every note that INCREASES the prominence of the dancer.","lines":["The dancer is placed on the left power point of the thirds grid.","Her costume is bright gold against a deep blue night background.","The background crowd is blurred with a shallow depth of field.","Six sponsor logos of the same size as the dancer are placed around her.","A line of street lights along the parade route leads towards her.","Every element on the poster is lit equally brightly.","She fills about half of the frame height, much larger than anything else.","The event title, the dancer and a large map compete in the centre."],"correct":[0,1,2,4,6],"win":"Well spotted. Placement, colour contrast, focus, leading lines and scale all push the eye to one clear point of interest.","miss":"Look again. Ask of each note: does it pull the eye towards the dancer, or does it compete with her for attention?","tutor":[{"h":"Competition","p":"<p>Anything the same size or brightness as the subject competes with it.</p>"},{"h":"Five tools","p":"<p>Placement, contrast, focus, leading lines and scale are all on your list from the last screen.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the prominence techniques (Foundation)","q":"Select the notes that make the dancer stand out.","lines":["She is gold against a dark blue background.","The crowd behind her is blurred.","Six logos the same size as her surround her.","She is much larger than anything else."],"correct":[0,1,3],"win":"Yes. Contrast, focus and scale create prominence.","miss":"Which note gives other elements equal weight? That one weakens her."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the prominence techniques (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha wrote these notes about a website hero image for a chocolate maker in Grenada. The main subject is the chocolate bar. Some notes use subtle techniques, such as framing and implied lines; others quietly weaken the bar's prominence.</p>","q":"Select every note that INCREASES the prominence of the chocolate bar.","lines":["The bar sits on the lower-right power point.","A wooden counter edge runs diagonally from the left foreground towards the bar.","The cocoa pods in the background are softly blurred.","A cocoa leaf in the foreground frames the bar from the top-left corner.","The bar is lit from the side so its edge glows against a dark background.","The brand logo, the bar and a large \"Free shipping\" badge are all the same size.","Three other products of equal brightness sit beside the bar.","The background shelves are sharp and full of brightly labelled jars.","A model in the image is looking directly at the bar.","The \"Shop now\" button is the same colour as the background."],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,8],"win":"Well spotted. Placement, a leading line, shallow depth of field, framing, contrast and an implied line from the model's gaze all pull the eye to the bar. Equal-sized elements, equal brightness and a busy, sharp background compete with it.","miss":"Look again. Ask of each note: does it pull the eye towards the bar, or does it give something else equal weight? A person's gaze works like a leading line."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Perspective, camera angles and lighting","html":"<p><b>Perspective</b> is how three-dimensional depth is shown on a flat surface. In <b>one-point perspective</b>, parallel lines (a road, a corridor, a pier) meet at a single <b>vanishing point</b> on the horizon line: perfect for looking straight down Frederick Street in Port of Spain. <b>Two-point perspective</b> uses two vanishing points, for viewing a building from its corner. <b>Atmospheric perspective</b> makes distant hills paler, bluer and less detailed, which is how the Northern Range behind Port of Spain looks on a hazy day. <b>Overlap</b> and <b>size diminution</b> (far objects drawn smaller) add depth too.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Camera angle</th><th>What it is</th><th>Typical effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Eye level</b></td><td>Camera at the subject's eye height</td><td>Neutral, honest, relatable: interviews, vlogs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>High angle</b></td><td>Camera above, looking down</td><td>Subject looks smaller, weaker or vulnerable</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low angle</b></td><td>Camera below, looking up</td><td>Subject looks powerful, heroic, dominant</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bird's-eye / overhead</b></td><td>Directly above</td><td>Shows layout and pattern: a drone shot of a Carnival band, a flat-lay of johnny cakes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Worm's-eye</b></td><td>From ground level, looking straight up</td><td>Exaggerated height and drama</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dutch angle (canted)</b></td><td>Camera tilted so the horizon slopes</td><td>Unease, tension, confusion, often in horror or action</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Over-the-shoulder</b></td><td>Behind one character, framing another</td><td>Conversation and connection</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Lighting</b> shapes mood and directs attention.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Three-point lighting</b>: a <b>key light</b> (main, brightest), a <b>fill light</b> (softens shadows from the other side) and a <b>back light</b> or rim light (separates the subject from the background).</li>\n<li><b>High-key</b> lighting is bright with few shadows: cheerful adverts, beauty, tourism. <b>Low-key</b> lighting has deep shadows and strong contrast: drama, mystery.</li>\n<li><b>Hard light</b> (midday Caribbean sun) gives sharp shadows; <b>soft light</b> (cloudy sky, a diffuser) is flattering. The <b>golden hour</b> just after sunrise and before sunset gives warm, soft, low light, ideal for shooting at Maracas Bay or Las Cuevas.</li>\n<li><b>Backlighting</b> a subject against the sun creates a silhouette unless you add fill light or a reflector.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Always pair the technique with its <b>effect</b>: \"A low angle makes the cricketer look heroic\" earns more than \"a low angle was used\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Perspective makes a flat picture look deep, for example a road shrinking to one point in the distance. Camera angles change how we feel about a subject: from below, people look powerful; from above, they look small. Lighting sets the mood: bright light feels cheerful and deep shadows feel mysterious. Filming at golden hour on a Tobago beach gives warm, soft light.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Angles and lighting carry meaning, so choose them to fit the story, not just to look interesting. A low angle on a politician can suggest power or arrogance, depending on context; a high angle on a child can show vulnerability or simply the size of the market around her. Midday Caribbean sun is a practical problem: it casts hard shadows under eyes and hat brims, so crews shoot early or late, use a diffuser or add fill light with a reflector. Beginners often skip the <b>back light</b> in three-point lighting, yet it is what separates a dark-haired presenter from a dark background. When you analyse a shot, name the technique and explain its effect in that specific scene.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the technique to its effect","intro":"<p>Jerome and Malik are planning shots for a short film set in Castries and Soufrière. Match each technique to the effect it creates.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Low angle","r":"The headmistress looks powerful and in charge"},{"l":"High angle","r":"A lost child in the market looks small and vulnerable"},{"l":"Dutch angle","r":"The hurricane scene feels unstable and tense"},{"l":"Bird's-eye view","r":"A drone shows the pattern of a whole Carnival band"},{"l":"Low-key lighting","r":"A night scene full of mystery and deep shadow"},{"l":"Golden hour","r":"Warm, soft light for a romantic beach scene"},{"l":"One-point perspective","r":"Jeremie Street stretches towards a single vanishing point"},{"l":"Back light (rim light)","r":"The actor is separated from a dark background by a bright edge"}],"win":"Very good. You linked each angle, light and perspective technique to the meaning it creates, which is how Paper 02 analysis questions are marked.","tutor":[{"h":"Up or down?","p":"<p>Looking up at someone makes them bigger; looking down makes them smaller.</p>"},{"h":"Light and mood","p":"<p>Deep shadows suggest mystery; warm low sun suggests calm or romance.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the technique (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Low angle","r":"Subject looks powerful"},{"l":"High angle","r":"Subject looks small and weak"},{"l":"Dutch angle","r":"Scene feels tense and unstable"},{"l":"Golden hour","r":"Warm, soft evening light"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the technique to its effect (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: these techniques are easier to confuse. Match each one to the shot that shows it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Worm's-eye view","r":"From the sand looking straight up, the lighthouse seems to tower endlessly"},{"l":"Over-the-shoulder","r":"We see the fisherman's face past the edge of his daughter's head as they talk"},{"l":"Two-point perspective","r":"Seen from its corner, a Brazil Street building recedes towards two vanishing points"},{"l":"Atmospheric perspective","r":"The distant Pitons look paler, bluer and less detailed than the boat in front"},{"l":"High-key lighting","r":"A bright, almost shadowless shot for a sunscreen advert"},{"l":"Hard light","r":"Midday sun casts sharp, dark shadows under the vendor's hat brim"},{"l":"Fill light","r":"A reflector softens the shadows on the side of the face away from the key light"},{"l":"Backlighting without fill","r":"The surfer becomes a dark silhouette against the sunset"}],"win":"Excellent. You separated similar angles, the two kinds of perspective and four lighting choices by the effect each one creates."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Seven layout principles (1 to 4)","html":"<p><b>Seven layout principles organise any design.</b> Learn them in this order: <b>1 Repetition, 2 Proximity, 3 White space, 4 Balance, 5 Contrast, 6 Alignment, 7 Hierarchy.</b> Alignment, hierarchy, spacing and visual flow are also design principles for prototypes in Module 2, so you will use them again in your web pages and SBA product. Each principle comes with a <b>Quick test</b>: a question you can ask of any design.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Principle</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example</th><th>Quick test</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1 Repetition</b></td><td>Repeating the same colours, fonts, shapes and buttons ties a design together, so people recognise it at a glance.</td><td>The same button style on every app screen tells users what is tappable. A row of palm trees creates rhythm.</td><td>Do the same elements look the same wherever they appear?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2 Proximity</b></td><td>Items that belong together should sit close together. Unrelated items should sit further apart.</td><td>On an event poster, the date, time and venue sit in one block, while sponsor logos are grouped separately at the bottom.</td><td>Can you see which items form groups before reading the labels?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3 White space</b></td><td>White space, also called negative space, is the empty area around content. It gives the eye room to rest and focus.</td><td>Luxury resort websites use generous empty space, while cluttered bargain flyers fill every corner. Negative space can even form a second shape inside a logo.</td><td>Does each element have room to breathe?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4 Balance</b></td><td>Balance spreads visual weight evenly. <b>Symmetrical</b> balance mirrors both sides, <b>asymmetrical</b> balance uses different elements of equal weight, and <b>radial</b> balance arranges elements around a centre.</td><td>Formal certificates and wedding invitations use symmetry. Magazine layouts use asymmetry. A hurricane on a satellite map is radial.</td><td>Does one side of the page feel heavier than the other?</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Visual weight:</b> larger, darker, brighter, more detailed or more colourful elements feel heavier. White space does not have to be white; it is any empty area. Some texts also mention <b>mosaic balance</b>, where many equal elements fill the frame with no single focal point, like a grid of Instagram thumbnails.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Students often confuse proximity with alignment. Proximity is about <b>distance</b> (grouping); alignment is about <b>lining up edges</b>. Also, \"asymmetrical\" does not mean \"unbalanced\": it is balanced by different elements of equal weight.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Four layout principles help you organise a page. Repetition means using the same colours, fonts and buttons again and again. Proximity means putting things that belong together close together. White space is empty space that gives the eye a rest. Balance means spreading visual weight so no side feels too heavy. A tidy church bulletin in Montserrat uses all four.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>These principles change how fast people understand a page, not just how it looks. Proximity is processed before reading: viewers group nearby items automatically, so a price placed closer to the wrong photo will be read as belonging to it. White space is not wasted space. It signals quality, which is why luxury brands use so much of it, and it makes text easier to scan. Asymmetrical balance is harder than symmetry because you must judge visual weight: a small, bright, detailed element can balance a large, plain one. Repetition builds recognition across a whole product, but too much becomes dull, so designers repeat the system (fonts, colours, spacing) while varying the content.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match each principle to its quick test","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media pins the seven quick tests above her desk. Match each layout principle to the question that checks it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Repetition","r":"Do the same elements look the same wherever they appear?"},{"l":"Proximity","r":"Can you see which items form groups before reading the labels?"},{"l":"White space","r":"Does each element have room to breathe?"},{"l":"Balance","r":"Does one side of the page feel heavier than the other?"},{"l":"Contrast","r":"Can you read the body text easily, with at least 4.5 : 1 contrast?"},{"l":"Alignment","r":"Do the elements share a common edge or centre line?"},{"l":"Hierarchy","r":"Can you name the most important element within three seconds?"}],"win":"Excellent. You can now check any layout with all seven quick tests: repetition, proximity, white space, balance, contrast, alignment and hierarchy.","tutor":[{"h":"Groups and edges","p":"<p>Seeing groups is about distance. Sharing an edge is about lining up.</p>"},{"h":"Heavy or readable?","p":"<p>Visual weight on each side is one principle; how easily you can read text is another.</p>"},{"h":"Most important","p":"<p>Which principle ranks elements first, second and third?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the quick test (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Repetition","r":"Do the same elements look the same everywhere?"},{"l":"Proximity","r":"Can you see the groups before reading?"},{"l":"White space","r":"Does each element have room to breathe?"},{"l":"Hierarchy","r":"Can you name the most important element quickly?"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match each principle to its quick test (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: instead of the quick test, each card describes a <b>problem</b> found in a real layout. Match each problem to the principle that has failed.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Repetition","r":"The \"Donate\" button is blue and rounded on one page but red and square on another"},{"l":"Proximity","r":"A price sits closer to the wrong photo, so customers think it belongs to that tour"},{"l":"White space","r":"Every corner of the flyer is filled, and the eye has nowhere to rest"},{"l":"Balance","r":"A huge photo on the left and nothing on the right makes the page feel lopsided"},{"l":"Contrast","r":"Light grey captions on a white card are hard to read on a phone in sunlight"},{"l":"Alignment","r":"The headings start at slightly different distances from the left margin"},{"l":"Hierarchy","r":"The sponsor's logo is larger than the name of the event"}],"win":"Excellent. You can diagnose which principle has failed from the problem itself, which is the first step in any redesign answer."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Layout principles 5 to 7","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Principle</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example</th><th>Quick test</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5 Contrast</b></td><td>Contrast makes elements stand out through differences in colour, lightness and size. For body text, aim for at least a <b>4.5 : 1</b> contrast ratio (WCAG).</td><td>Gold on navy reads well, while yellow on white is hard to read. Large headings against small body text create emphasis.</td><td>Can you read the body text easily, with at least 4.5 : 1 contrast?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6 Alignment</b></td><td>Alignment lines elements up along shared edges or centres, which creates order. A grid helps you place every element consistently.</td><td>Web designers often use a <b>12-column grid</b>. Left-align long English text for easy reading, and centre short headings.</td><td>Do the elements share a common edge or centre line?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7 Hierarchy</b></td><td>Hierarchy shows the reader what to see first, second and third. The most important element should look the most important.</td><td>On a Heritage Night phone screen, the event name is largest, then the date and time, then the Get tickets button.</td><td>Can you name the most important element within three seconds?</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>WCAG is the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines. Free online contrast checkers will test your colour pairs. Contrast also comes from shape, texture and type (a bold sans-serif heading with light body text). Hierarchy creates <b>visual flow</b>, the path the eye follows (often a Z-pattern on a poster or an F-pattern on a text-heavy web page).</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Fixing last year's poster used six principles:</b><br>1 <b>Hierarchy</b>: event name largest, date second.<br>2 <b>Proximity</b>: date, time, venue in one block.<br>3 <b>White space</b>: clutter removed so the eye can rest.<br>4 <b>Alignment</b>: text lined up on one edge.<br>5 <b>Contrast</b>: gold on navy.<br>6 <b>Repetition</b>: a wave motif.</p>\n<p>Notice which principle was not needed: balance. Not every fix uses all seven; name the ones you actually used.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 \"redesign\" question, do what the poster fix does: number each change, name the principle and say what it improves. \"Hierarchy: I made the event name the largest element so it is read first\" earns the mark; \"I made it look better\" does not.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Contrast makes things stand out through differences in colour, lightness and size. Alignment lines things up along the same edge, so the page looks ordered. Hierarchy shows what to read first, second and third, usually by size and position. On a sports day poster in Antigua, the event name is biggest, the date comes next and the smaller details come last.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Hierarchy ties the other principles together: contrast, size, position and white space are the tools that build it. A quick test is to squint at the design; the shapes that stay visible should be the most important ones. Visual flow follows hierarchy, so the eye usually moves in a Z-pattern on a poster and an F-pattern on a text-heavy web page, and the call to action belongs where that path ends. Alignment mistakes are small but costly: text that is almost, but not quite, aligned looks careless. In a redesign question, number each change, name the principle and give the improvement, and never claim a principle you did not actually use.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which layout principle?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is redesigning the website of a West Bay dive shop in Grand Cayman. Sort each change by the principle it mainly applies.</p>","main":"Which principle does this change mainly apply?","bins":["Repetition","Proximity","Alignment","Hierarchy"],"items":[{"t":"The headline \"Dive the Wrecks of Grand Cayman\" is now three times larger than the body text.","bin":3,"fb":"Using size to show what matters most is hierarchy."},{"t":"The contact phone, email and address are moved together into one box.","bin":1,"fb":"Grouping related contact details is proximity."},{"t":"The price, duration and depth of each dive are grouped tightly under its photo.","bin":1,"fb":"Placing related information close together is proximity."},{"t":"All text blocks and images now line up on the same left margin.","bin":2,"fb":"Lining elements up on a common edge is alignment."},{"t":"Each section uses the same wave-shaped divider.","bin":0,"fb":"A repeated visual motif is repetition."},{"t":"Captions now start at the exact left edge of their photos.","bin":2,"fb":"Matching edges is alignment."},{"t":"Every page uses the same coral-orange buttons and the same heading font.","bin":0,"fb":"Reusing colours and fonts across pages is repetition, which builds unity and brand identity."},{"t":"The \"Book a dive\" button is bold, bright and placed above the details.","bin":3,"fb":"Weight, colour and position signal importance, so this is hierarchy."}],"win":"Well sorted. Repetition unifies, proximity groups, alignment orders and hierarchy ranks importance.","tutor":[{"h":"Same again?","p":"<p>If something is reused across the design, think repetition.</p>"},{"h":"Distance or edge?","p":"<p>Close together = proximity. Lined up = alignment.</p>"},{"h":"Most important first","p":"<p>Bigger, bolder or higher on the page usually signals hierarchy.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which layout principle? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Same button colour on every page.","bin":0,"fb":"Reused style: repetition."},{"t":"Price grouped right under the dive photo.","bin":1,"fb":"Grouping: proximity."},{"t":"All text on one left margin.","bin":2,"fb":"Common edge: alignment."},{"t":"Headline much larger than body text.","bin":3,"fb":"Importance by size: hierarchy."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which layout principle? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Six bins this time. Maya is redesigning the dive shop website again. Sort each change by the principle it MAINLY applies.</p>","main":"Which principle does this change mainly apply?","bins":["Proximity","White space","Balance","Contrast","Alignment","Hierarchy"],"items":[{"t":"\"Book a dive\" becomes the largest, boldest item directly under the headline.","bin":5,"fb":"Size, weight and position are used to rank importance: hierarchy."},{"t":"Two cluttered banners are removed, leaving an empty band between sections.","bin":1,"fb":"Removing clutter to create rest areas uses white space."},{"t":"Each price now sits directly under its own dive photo, with a clear gap before the next photo.","bin":0,"fb":"Closeness shows which price belongs to which photo: proximity."},{"t":"Grey caption text is darkened to navy so it passes the 4.5 : 1 check.","bin":3,"fb":"Increasing the difference in lightness between text and background is contrast."},{"t":"A large photo on the left is offset by a smaller, brightly coloured booking panel on the right.","bin":2,"fb":"Different elements of equal visual weight on each side: asymmetrical balance."},{"t":"Captions, headings and buttons now start on the same grid column.","bin":4,"fb":"Sharing a common edge on a grid is alignment."},{"t":"The contact page has a central crest with matching columns of text on either side.","bin":2,"fb":"Mirroring around a central axis is symmetrical balance."},{"t":"The page margins are doubled so the headline and photo have room to breathe.","bin":1,"fb":"More empty space around content is white space."},{"t":"The opening hours, phone and address are pulled into one block, away from the social media links.","bin":0,"fb":"Grouping related details and separating unrelated ones is proximity."},{"t":"The text in each card is centred on the same middle line as the photo above it.","bin":4,"fb":"Sharing a centre line is also alignment."}],"win":"Excellent. You told six principles apart, including both kinds of balance and both kinds of alignment."}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing a type of balance","q":"The Montserrat Heritage Circle wants a formal invitation for an Emancipation Day ceremony. The design must feel dignified, calm and orderly, with the foundation's crest at the top centre and the text mirrored around a central axis. Which type of balance BEST meets this brief?","options":[{"t":"Mosaic balance","fb":"Mosaic balance spreads many equal elements with no focal point. The brief has a clear focal point: the crest."},{"t":"Symmetrical balance","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Mirroring elements around a central axis gives formal, calm, dignified balance, which suits a solemn ceremony."},{"t":"Radial balance","fb":"Radial balance radiates from a centre point. The brief describes elements mirrored around a vertical axis, not radiating outward."},{"t":"Asymmetrical balance","fb":"Asymmetrical balance uses different weights on each side and feels dynamic and informal, not the dignified order the brief asks for."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Read the mood words","p":"<p>\"Formal\", \"dignified\", \"calm\" and \"mirrored\" are the clues.</p>"},{"h":"Axis or centre?","p":"<p>Mirrored around a line is different from radiating from a point.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a type of balance (Foundation)","q":"A design where the left half mirrors the right half uses...","options":[{"t":"symmetrical balance","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. A mirror image around a central axis is symmetrical."},{"t":"radial balance","fb":"Radial radiates from a centre point."},{"t":"asymmetrical balance","fb":"Asymmetrical uses different elements on each side."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing a type of balance (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A youth film festival in Kingston wants a poster that feels energetic and modern. A large, dark photo of a young director fills the left two-thirds. The designer must stop the poster feeling lopsided WITHOUT mirroring the photo. Which solution BEST applies balance?","options":[{"t":"Symmetrical balance: add a second photo of the same size on the right.","fb":"Mirroring would balance it, but the brief rules out mirroring, and symmetry feels formal rather than energetic."},{"t":"Radial balance: arrange all the text in a circle around the director's head.","fb":"Radial balance radiates from a centre point. The photo is off to the left, and circular text would be hard to read."},{"t":"Asymmetrical balance: place a smaller but brighter, high-contrast block with the festival title and dates on the right, so its visual weight matches the large photo.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Brightness, contrast and detail add visual weight, so a smaller element can balance a large, dark one while keeping the energy."},{"t":"Leave the right third empty, because white space always creates balance.","fb":"White space is useful, but a large empty area opposite a heavy, dark photo makes the poster feel more lopsided, not less."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"A 2D storyboard frame plans the shot","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>A 2D storyboard frame plans the shot.</b> Every frame records: <b>frame number, shot size and angle, camera move, duration, sound, transition.</b> Then build the picture in order: <b>horizon, vanishing point, thirds grid; then foreground, middle, background.</b></p>\n<p>Be ready to do this activity in class, in Paper 02 or as part of your SBA: <b>design a two-dimensional environment for a storyboard frame, applying rules of composition</b>. An environment frame (also called a background, establishing frame or layout) shows the setting where the action happens, before or behind the characters.</p>\n<p><b>Step by step (pencil and paper, then GIMP, Krita or Inkscape):</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Read the brief.</b> Who is the audience, what is the scene, what mood? Example: \"Opening frame of an animated short: sunrise over Roseau, calm and hopeful, after Hurricane Maria.\"</li>\n<li><b>Choose the aspect ratio.</b> Draw the frame box first: 16:9 for film or YouTube, 9:16 for a vertical reel. Mark a <b>safe area</b> margin so nothing important is cut off.</li>\n<li><b>Horizon, vanishing point, thirds grid.</b> Draw the horizon on the upper or lower third, never the middle. Mark the vanishing point on the horizon (one-point perspective for a street view, two-point for a corner building). Then lightly mark the thirds grid or a phi grid to place the subject. In GIMP use Image &gt; Guides &gt; New Guide (by Percent) at 33.3% and 66.7%; in Inkscape or Figma add guides or a layout grid.</li>\n<li><b>Plan depth with layers:</b> <b>foreground</b> (a palm frond or railing that frames the shot), <b>middle ground</b> (where the action and point of interest sit, e.g. the rebuilt bayfront on a power point) and <b>background</b> (paler hills and sky using atmospheric perspective). Keep each on a separate layer so a character can be added later.</li>\n<li><b>Lead the eye.</b> Use leading lines (the shoreline, the road) towards the point of interest. Leave negative space where a character will enter.</li>\n<li><b>Set light and value.</b> Decide where the sun is, then make the point of interest the area of highest contrast. Test in greyscale to check the value structure.</li>\n<li><b>Add shot notes.</b> Under the frame write the frame number, shot size and angle, camera move, duration, sound and transition.</li>\n<li><b>Export.</b> Save the working file (XCF, KRA or SVG) and export a copy as PNG. Use clear file names, e.g. <code>Candidate1234_Q1_EnvFrame.png</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Example: Roseau bayfront.</b> Golden hour. Subject on the lower-right power point. The bayfront railing runs as a leading line from the left foreground to the subject; the cruise pier and hills sit in the background.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Examiners reward <b>visible evidence</b> of composition. Leave your faint grid lines on a copy, or annotate the frame with arrows labelled \"horizon on lower third\", \"vanishing point\", \"leading line\" and \"point of interest on power point\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An environment frame shows the setting where the action happens. Draw the frame at the right shape (16:9 for YouTube, 9:16 for a phone reel), put the horizon on a third line, then plan the foreground, middle ground and background. Use leading lines to point at the subject. A frame of a river valley in Dominica might use the river itself as the leading line.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The environment frame does a job for the story: it tells the audience where and when they are, and what mood to expect, before any character appears. Plan for the action as well as the scenery. Leave negative space where the character will enter, and keep the point of interest inside the safe area so a vertical crop for Reels does not cut it off. Depth comes from several cues working together: overlap, size diminution, atmospheric perspective and value. A common weakness is a flat background where the foreground, middle ground and background all have the same detail and contrast. Exam answers earn credit for <b>visible</b> evidence, so annotate the horizon, vanishing point, leading line and power point.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build the environment frame in order","intro":"<p>Malik is drawing the opening environment frame for an animated short set at Prince George Wharf in Nassau. Put his steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Read the brief and note audience, scene and mood"},{"t":"Draw the frame at the correct aspect ratio with a safe area"},{"t":"Draw the horizon on a third line and set the vanishing point"},{"t":"Add the thirds grid or golden section guides"},{"t":"Sketch foreground, middle ground and background on separate layers"},{"t":"Add leading lines and light the point of interest"},{"t":"Write frame notes (number, shot, move, duration, sound, transition) and export a PNG"}],"win":"Great sequencing. Brief, frame, horizon and vanishing point, thirds grid, depth layers, leading lines and light, then notes and export: a logical composition workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Structure first","p":"<p>Work in this order: horizon, vanishing point, thirds grid; then foreground, middle, background.</p>"},{"h":"Detail later","p":"<p>Lighting and leading lines refine a layout that is already blocked in.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build the frame (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Read the brief"},{"t":"Draw the frame, horizon and thirds grid"},{"t":"Sketch foreground, middle ground and background"},{"t":"Add notes and export as PNG"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build the environment frame in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: the full ten-step workflow from the notes. Malik is drawing the opening environment frame for an animated short set at Prince George Wharf in Nassau. Put every step in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Read the brief and note the audience, scene and mood"},{"t":"Choose the aspect ratio, draw the frame box and mark a safe area"},{"t":"Draw the horizon on the upper or lower third"},{"t":"Mark the vanishing point on the horizon"},{"t":"Add thirds or phi guides to place the subject"},{"t":"Sketch the foreground, middle ground and background on separate layers"},{"t":"Draw leading lines towards the point of interest and leave negative space for the character"},{"t":"Set the light direction, then test the value structure in greyscale"},{"t":"Write the frame notes: number, shot, move, duration, sound and transition"},{"t":"Save the working file (XCF) and export a PNG copy with a clear file name"}],"win":"Excellent. The vanishing point needs the horizon first, the depth layers need the guides, and the greyscale test checks the finished value structure before you annotate and export."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Interactive design answers what a user does next","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Composition</b> controls what users <b>see</b>. <b>Interaction</b> controls what they <b>do</b>.</p>\n<p>There are five <b>principles of interactive design</b>: <b>Consistency, Perceivability, Learnability, Predictability, Feedback.</b> Each one answers a <b>silent question</b> that every user asks without saying it aloud.</p>\n<p><b>1 Consistency.</b> Consistency means the same thing looks and works the same way everywhere in a product.</p>\n<p><i>The silent question: will this work the way it did last time?</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the Back arrow in the same corner on every screen.</li>\n<li>Use the same colours and icons for the same action.</li>\n<li>Follow conventions: magnifying glass for search, cart icon, hamburger menu.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Good:</b> WhatsApp uses the same message bubbles and green send button throughout. <b>Poor:</b> a school portal shows Submit as a green button on one page and as a grey link on another.</p>\n<p><b>2 Perceivability.</b> Users must be able to see what can be tapped and what state something is in.</p>\n<p><i>The silent question: can I see it, and can I tap it?</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Touch targets at least <b>44 x 44 pt</b> (Apple) or <b>48 x 48 dp</b> (Android).</li>\n<li>Never rely on colour alone: pair red with a plus icon and a message.</li>\n<li>Make buttons look like buttons, not plain text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Good:</b> Google Maps shows a large blue Start button. <b>Poor:</b> directions appear as grey text that looks like an ordinary link.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Consistent and perceivable products feel familiar.</b> Consistency: same look and behaviour; follow platform conventions. Perceivability: clear signifiers; 44 pt or 48 dp touch targets; never colour alone.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A \"signifier\" is any visual clue that shows what an element does: a shadow under a button, an underline on a link. If an exam answer says \"users could not tell it was a button\", the principle is perceivability.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Interactive design is about what users do. Consistency means the same thing looks and works the same way everywhere, like a Back arrow that is always in the top-left corner. Perceivability means users can see what can be tapped: buttons look like buttons, touch targets are big enough and colour is never the only clue. A banking app in Belize should follow both.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Consistency works at two levels. <b>Internal</b> consistency is within your product: one button style, one meaning per icon. <b>External</b> consistency follows conventions users already know from other apps, such as a magnifying glass for search; breaking a convention forces users to relearn, so it needs a strong reason. Perceivability depends on <b>signifiers</b>: shadows, borders, underlines and labels that show what an element does. Flat, minimal design often removes them, which looks clean but leaves users guessing. Touch target size matters most for older users and for anyone using a phone one-handed on a moving bus. In an evaluation, name the principle, describe the evidence and explain its effect on users.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"What users see or what users do?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing a Carnival events app. Sort each note: is it about composition (what users see) or interaction (what users do)?</p>","main":"Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?","bins":["Composition (what users see)","Interaction (what users do)"],"items":[{"t":"The date, time and venue sit together in one block.","bin":0,"fb":"Grouping by proximity organises what users see."},{"t":"The J'ouvert headline is the largest element on the home screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Size and hierarchy decide what the eye sees first: composition."},{"t":"The band photo is placed on a power point with a blurred crowd behind.","bin":0,"fb":"Placement and depth of field are composition tools."},{"t":"Gold text on a navy background passes the 4.5 : 1 contrast check.","bin":0,"fb":"Contrast for readability is a layout (composition) principle."},{"t":"First-time users get three short onboarding tips before they start.","bin":1,"fb":"Onboarding helps users learn what to do: learnability, an interaction principle."},{"t":"Tapping \"Pay XCD 45.00\" does exactly what the label says.","bin":1,"fb":"An outcome label lets users predict what an action will do: interaction."},{"t":"The Back arrow is in the same top-left corner on every screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Controls that work the same way everywhere are about consistency of interaction."},{"t":"The Buy button turns into a spinner, then a green tick, after it is tapped.","bin":1,"fb":"A response to the user's action is feedback: interaction."}],"win":"Well sorted. Composition controls what users see; interaction controls what they do. A good app needs both.","tutor":[{"h":"Looking or acting?","p":"<p>If the note is about arrangement, size, colour or placement, think \"see\". If it is about tapping, responses or what happens next, think \"do\".</p>"},{"h":"Contrast is tricky","p":"<p>Readable text is a layout choice, even on a screen.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"See or do? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"The headline is the largest element.","bin":0,"fb":"Size and hierarchy: what users see."},{"t":"Date, time and venue are grouped together.","bin":0,"fb":"Proximity: what users see."},{"t":"A tick appears after the user taps Buy.","bin":1,"fb":"Feedback: what users do."},{"t":"Back always goes to the previous screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Consistent behaviour: what users do."}]},
 "up": {"title":"What users see or what users do? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder notes on the Carnival events app: some sound technical, and some mention colour or size but are really about what users do. Sort each note.</p>","main":"Is this note mainly about composition or interaction?","bins":["Composition (what users see)","Interaction (what users do)"],"items":[{"t":"A pop-up warns \"This cannot be undone\" before a ticket is cancelled.","bin":1,"fb":"Warning before an irreversible action supports predictability: interaction."},{"t":"The phone vibrates briefly when a ticket is added to the cart.","bin":1,"fb":"A haptic response to an action is feedback: interaction."},{"t":"On first launch, three swipeable tips explain the app in plain language.","bin":1,"fb":"Onboarding supports learnability: interaction."},{"t":"Sponsor logos are grouped in one strip at the bottom of the screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Grouping by proximity organises what users see: composition."},{"t":"The Pay button has a shadow and border so users can tell it is tappable.","bin":1,"fb":"A signifier that shows an element can be acted on is perceivability, an interactive principle."},{"t":"The headline and the Buy button share the same left edge.","bin":0,"fb":"Alignment is a layout principle: composition."},{"t":"Swiping left deletes a ticket, just as it does in the user's email app.","bin":1,"fb":"Following a gesture convention from other apps is external consistency: interaction."},{"t":"The screen follows a Z-pattern from the logo to the headline to the button.","bin":0,"fb":"Visual flow is created by layout and hierarchy: composition."},{"t":"A shallow depth of field blurs the crowd behind the band photo.","bin":0,"fb":"Depth of field directs the eye: composition."},{"t":"The band photo uses a golden spiral to lead the eye to the lead singer.","bin":0,"fb":"The golden spiral guides the eye: composition."}],"win":"Excellent. A colour, size or shape note belongs to interaction when its job is to show users what they can do or what just happened."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Learnability, predictability and feedback","html":"<p><b>3 Learnability.</b> A new user should be able to learn and use the product quickly, without a manual.</p>\n<p><i>The silent question: how do I do this the first time?</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use plain language: \"Pay bill\", not \"Initiate settlement\".</li>\n<li>Reveal advanced options progressively, and offer onboarding.</li>\n<li>Favour recognition over recall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Good:</b> Duolingo teaches through short guided lessons with clear labels. <b>Poor:</b> a screen of 40 unlabelled icons that users must memorise.</p>\n<p><b>4 Predictability.</b> Users should be able to anticipate what an action will do before they take it, and reverse it if needed.</p>\n<p><i>The silent question: what happens if I press this?</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Label buttons with their outcome, such as \"Pay XCD 45.00\" (Eastern Caribbean dollars).</li>\n<li>Offer undo for actions that can be reversed.</li>\n<li>Warn before irreversible actions and show previews.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Good:</b> Gmail offers Undo right after you send an email. <b>Poor:</b> a bookmark icon that quietly shares your list publicly.</p>\n<p><b>5 Feedback.</b> Every action should give a clear response, so users know the system received their input and what happened.</p>\n<p><i>The silent question: did that work?</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use visual, audio or haptic signals.</li>\n<li>Show a spinner while loading and a success tick when done.</li>\n<li>Make errors specific, such as \"Card number must be 16 digits\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Good:</b> WhatsApp ticks show whether a message was sent, delivered and read. <b>Poor:</b> the Heritage Collective ticket site charged customers three times with no confirmation.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Learnable and predictable products build trust.</b> Learnability: plain language, progressive disclosure, onboarding. Predictability: outcome labels, previews, undo (think of Gmail's Undo send).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The principles overlap. Consistency supports predictability and learnability. When you explain one, say how it helps another: \"Because Back always works the same way (consistency), users can predict it.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Learnability means a new user can work out the product quickly, without a manual. Predictability means users can guess what a button will do before they tap it, and can undo mistakes. Feedback means the product shows it heard you: a spinner, a tick or a clear error message. When you top up a phone in Trinidad, a \"Top-up successful\" message is feedback.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Feedback must be <b>timely</b>, <b>clear</b> and <b>specific</b>. Usability research suggests people notice a delay of more than about one second, and after about ten seconds they lose focus or tap again, which is exactly how double payments happen. An error message such as \"Something went wrong\" fails because it does not say what to fix. Predictability and learnability support each other: outcome labels like \"Pay XCD 45.00\" let new users predict the result, and an undo option makes it safe to explore, which speeds up learning. Progressive disclosure helps learnability by hiding advanced options until they are needed, but hiding too much makes features hard to find, so test with real first-time users.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match each principle to its silent question","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media tapes the five silent questions beside his screen while he tests apps. Match each interactive design principle to the question users silently ask, then to one of its rules.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Consistency","r":"Will this work the way it did last time?"},{"l":"Perceivability","r":"Can I see it, and can I tap it?"},{"l":"Learnability","r":"How do I do this the first time?"},{"l":"Predictability","r":"What happens if I press this?"},{"l":"Feedback","r":"Did that work?"},{"l":"Touch targets of 44 x 44 pt or 48 x 48 dp","r":"A perceivability rule: big enough to see and tap"},{"l":"A button labelled \"Pay XCD 45.00\"","r":"A predictability rule: label buttons with their outcome"}],"win":"Well matched. Each principle answers one silent question: last time, see and tap, first time, what happens, did it work.","tutor":[{"h":"Before or after?","p":"<p>\"What happens if...\" is asked before acting. \"Did that work?\" is asked after.</p>"},{"h":"First time or every time?","p":"<p>The first time is about learning; \"last time\" is about things staying the same.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the silent question (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Consistency","r":"Will this work the way it did last time?"},{"l":"Learnability","r":"How do I do this the first time?"},{"l":"Predictability","r":"What happens if I press this?"},{"l":"Feedback","r":"Did that work?"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match each principle to its silent question (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: instead of the silent question, each card is a <b>fix</b> Jerome made to a real app. Match each fix or technique to the principle it applies.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Consistency","r":"Put the Back arrow in the top-left corner of every screen"},{"l":"Perceivability","r":"Add a border, a shadow and a label so the \"Top up\" button looks tappable"},{"l":"Learnability","r":"Replace \"Initiate settlement\" with \"Pay bill\" and add three onboarding tips"},{"l":"Predictability","r":"Show a preview of a post before it is shared publicly"},{"l":"Feedback","r":"Show \"Card number must be 16 digits\" beside the field that is wrong"},{"l":"Progressive disclosure","r":"A learnability technique: hide advanced settings until they are needed"},{"l":"Undo send","r":"A predictability technique: let users reverse an action straight after it"}],"win":"Excellent. You can turn each principle into a concrete fix, which is what \"suggest an improvement\" questions need."}},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Good or poor example?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media has collected examples from apps and websites for a client workshop. Some are good interactive design; some are poor.</p>","q":"Select every GOOD example of interactive design.","lines":["WhatsApp uses the same message bubbles and green send button throughout.","A school portal shows Submit as a green button on one page and as a grey link on another.","Google Maps shows a large blue Start button.","Directions appear as grey text that looks like an ordinary link.","Duolingo teaches through short guided lessons with clear labels.","A screen of 40 unlabelled icons that users must memorise.","Gmail offers Undo right after you send an email.","A bookmark icon quietly shares your list publicly.","Heritage Collective ticket site charged customers three times with no confirmation."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Spot on. WhatsApp (consistency), Google Maps (perceivability), Duolingo (learnability) and Gmail Undo (predictability) are the good examples; the Heritage Collective site is a lesson in missing feedback.","miss":"Look again. Ask the silent question for each: will it work like last time, can I see it, can I learn it, can I predict it, did it work?","tutor":[{"h":"Pairs","p":"<p>The examples come in good and poor pairs for each principle. Find the partner of each one.</p>"},{"h":"Hidden surprises","p":"<p>Anything that happens without warning or without confirmation is poor.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Good or poor? (Foundation)","q":"Select the GOOD examples.","lines":["Gmail offers Undo right after you send an email.","A screen of 40 unlabelled icons to memorise.","WhatsApp ticks show sent, delivered and read.","Heritage Collective ticket site charges three times with no confirmation."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Undo and clear ticks help users.","miss":"Which examples leave users confused or surprised? Those are poor."},
 "up": {"title":"Good or poor example? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>New examples Maya has not discussed with the client yet. Decide which are good interactive design, using the five silent questions.</p>","q":"Select every GOOD example of interactive design.","lines":["A bus app shows \"Bus 4 arriving in 3 min\" and updates every 30 seconds.","A government form shows \"Error 0x45\" when a date is typed wrongly.","An airline app labels its main button \"Pay US$210 and confirm seat 14C\".","A shopping app moves the cart icon to a different corner on the checkout screen.","A banking app hides \"Transfer\" three menus deep behind an unlabelled icon.","A school app shows first-time users three short tips that they can skip.","A photo app deletes pictures instantly, with no warning and no recycle bin.","A food app's \"Order\" buttons are 48 dp tall and have clear text labels.","A ticket site shows a spinner, then \"Payment received: check your email for your tickets\".","A loyalty app uses a heart icon to mean \"share\" instead of \"like\"."],"correct":[0,2,5,7,8],"win":"Spot on. Live bus updates and the payment confirmation give feedback, the outcome label gives predictability, skippable tips support learnability and large labelled buttons give perceivability. The poor examples break feedback, consistency, learnability, predictability and convention.","miss":"Look again. For each example ask the silent questions: will it work like last time, can I see and tap it, can I learn it, can I predict it, did that work? A vague error code and a heart that means \"share\" both fail."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Evaluate any app with two sets of questions","html":"<p>Here is a simple method you can use on any app, website or kiosk, in class, in your SBA testing or in a Paper 02 \"evaluate\" question. Ask <b>two sets of questions</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Is the screen well composed?</th><th>Does it respond as users expect?</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Focal point</b>: is there one clear point of interest? Does it pass the two-second test?</td><td><b>Consistency</b>: will this work the way it did last time?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hierarchy</b>: can you name the most important element within three seconds?</td><td><b>Learnability</b>: how do I do this the first time?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Contrast</b>: can you read the body text easily, with at least 4.5 : 1 contrast?</td><td><b>Predictability</b>: what happens if I press this?</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td><b>Feedback</b>: did that work?</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> the Heritage Collective ticket site.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Composition:</b> the event photo is strong, but the \"Get tickets\" button is grey and the same size as the menu links, so hierarchy and contrast fail.</li>\n<li><b>Interaction:</b> after \"Pay\" there is no spinner and no confirmation, so feedback fails; customers tapped again and were charged three times. A button labelled \"Pay XCD 45.00\" and a success tick with an email receipt would fix predictability and feedback.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\"Evaluate\" is a high-level command word: give strengths <b>and</b> weaknesses, support each with evidence from the screen, and end with a judgement and an improvement.</p>\n<p><b>Future trends:</b> AI layout tools, accessibility by default, and 9:16 vertical video. Tools may suggest layouts, but you still need the principles to judge whether a suggestion works. Accessibility by default means contrast checks, large touch targets and captions are built in from the start, not added at the end. 9:16 means composing for a phone held upright, as in Instagram Reels and TikTok.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Structure an evaluation answer in two halves with clear labels (\"Composition:\" then \"Interaction:\"). Examiners can see at a glance that you covered both what users see and what users do.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To evaluate an app, ask two sets of questions. First, is the screen well composed? Check the focal point, the hierarchy and the contrast. Second, does it respond as users expect? Check consistency, learnability, predictability and feedback. Give a strength, a weakness and a fix for each, then a final judgement. Try it on any app you use, such as a bus app in Barbados.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Evaluate\" means you must reach a judgement, so prioritise. Not every problem is equally serious: a poorly chosen font is a weakness, but missing payment feedback that charges customers twice is critical. Rank problems by their impact on users and on the client, and fix the most damaging first. Support every point with evidence you can point to on the screen, not general opinion. Strong evaluations also recognise strengths, which shows balance and tells the client what to keep. Consider who the users are, too: an app for tourists needs stronger learnability than a staff tool used every day. A Grade One answer ends with a clear, justified recommendation, such as \"not ready to launch until feedback is fixed\".</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Name the interactive design principle","intro":"<p>Jerome is testing a beta of the \"Doubles Locator\" app, which shows doubles vendors in Port of Spain and San Fernando. Sort each observation by the principle it mainly shows.</p>","main":"Which principle does this observation mainly show?","bins":["Consistency","Perceivability","Learnability","Predictability"],"items":[{"t":"A first-time visitor finds and orders a plate of ribs in under a minute with no help.","bin":2,"fb":"Being easy to pick up quickly is learnability."},{"t":"Three short tips on first launch explain the main screens, and users still remember them a week later.","bin":2,"fb":"Quick to learn and easy to remember is learnability."},{"t":"Icons are labelled with words as well as pictures, and the text passes a contrast check.","bin":1,"fb":"Making information easy to see and understand is perceivability."},{"t":"The orange \"Order\" button looks and works the same on every stall page.","bin":0,"fb":"Same look and behaviour across screens is consistency."},{"t":"Tappable items have a shadow and a clear border, so users can tell they are buttons.","bin":1,"fb":"Making interactive elements visibly interactive is perceivability."},{"t":"\"Cancel\" always closes a pop-up without saving, exactly as users expect.","bin":3,"fb":"Behaving as users expect is predictability."},{"t":"The swipe gestures match those in other common apps users already know.","bin":0,"fb":"Following platform conventions is external consistency."},{"t":"Users correctly guess that the map pin icon will open directions.","bin":3,"fb":"Users knowing in advance what an action will do is predictability."}],"win":"Well done. You told consistency, perceivability, learnability and predictability apart. Feedback takes centre stage in the next challenge.","tutor":[{"h":"Across or before?","p":"<p>Same everywhere = consistency. Guessing correctly before acting = predictability.</p>"},{"h":"Seeing or learning?","p":"<p>Can users notice it? Perceivability. Can they pick it up fast and remember it? Learnability.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name the principle (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"The Order button is the same on every page.","bin":0,"fb":"Same everywhere: consistency."},{"t":"Buttons clearly look tappable.","bin":1,"fb":"Easy to notice: perceivability."},{"t":"A new user orders in a minute with no help.","bin":2,"fb":"Quick to learn: learnability."},{"t":"Users guess correctly that the pin opens directions.","bin":3,"fb":"Expected result: predictability."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name the interactive design principle (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>All five principles now, including feedback. Jerome is testing the Doubles Locator app again. Sort each observation by the principle it MAINLY shows.</p>","main":"Which principle does this observation mainly show?","bins":["Consistency","Perceivability","Learnability","Predictability","Feedback"],"items":[{"t":"Every stall page uses the same layout, so the menu is always in the same place.","bin":0,"fb":"The same layout across screens is internal consistency."},{"t":"Removing a favourite shows an Undo option for five seconds.","bin":3,"fb":"Undo makes an action reversible, so users can act without fear: predictability."},{"t":"Tourists who have never used the app find a stall in under a minute.","bin":2,"fb":"Quick success for first-time users shows learnability."},{"t":"Closed stalls show a grey pin AND the word \"Closed\".","bin":1,"fb":"Pairing colour with text means nobody relies on colour alone: perceivability."},{"t":"The button reads \"Call this stall\" with a phone icon, so users know it will start a call.","bin":3,"fb":"An outcome label lets users know what will happen: predictability."},{"t":"The cart icon is the same trolley shape that most shopping apps use.","bin":0,"fb":"Following a convention from other apps is external consistency."},{"t":"Buttons are at least 48 dp tall and have text labels, not just icons.","bin":1,"fb":"Large, labelled targets are easy to see and tap: perceivability."},{"t":"Advanced filters stay hidden until the user taps \"More filters\".","bin":2,"fb":"Progressive disclosure keeps the first experience simple: learnability."},{"t":"When a payment fails, the app says \"Card declined: try another card\".","bin":4,"fb":"A specific error message tells users what happened and what to do: feedback."},{"t":"After ordering, the screen shows \"Order sent: ready in about 15 minutes\".","bin":4,"fb":"A clear response confirming the action worked is feedback."}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify all five interactive design principles, including the subtle difference between an undo option (predictability) and a confirmation message (feedback)."}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Fixing the ferry booking app","intro":"<p>A ferry company runs trips between Kingstown, Bequia and Union Island. Its booking app has poor reviews. Zara asks you, the Island Pulse Media designer, to fix it using the principles of interactive design.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Reviews say: \"I tapped Pay and nothing happened, so I tapped again and was charged twice.\" What is your first fix?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Make the Pay button bigger and redder.","to":"bad1","fb":"Size helps perceivability, but users still would not know whether the payment worked."},{"t":"Disable the Pay button after one tap, show a spinner, then a clear confirmation screen and email receipt.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. The core problem is missing feedback; immediate, clear feedback stops repeat taps."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Next complaint: \"On the booking page Back means previous step, but on the payment page Back logs you out.\" What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a warning pop-up on the payment page explaining that Back logs you out.","to":"bad2","fb":"A warning patches the symptom. The control still behaves inconsistently and unpredictably."},{"t":"Make Back always return to the previous step on every screen, and place it in the same position each time.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. This fixes consistency, which also restores predictability."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Older passengers say the seat map is confusing: available and taken seats are both pale grey, and the icons have no labels. Your fix?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep the minimalist look; younger users like it, and older users will learn eventually.","to":"bad3","fb":"Style over usability excludes users. Perceivability and learnability matter for everyone."},{"t":"Change taken seats to red and available to green, with no other change.","to":"bad4","fb":"Better, but colour alone fails users with red-green colour blindness, and unlabelled icons still hurt learnability."},{"t":"Use strongly contrasting colours plus a symbol for taken seats, add text labels and a short legend, and test with older users.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You improved perceivability (contrast, not colour alone) and learnability (labels, legend), then validated with real users."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Double bookings stop, reviews improve and phone calls to the ferry office fall. A Grade One answer would name each fix as feedback, consistency, predictability, perceivability and learnability, with a reason.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Still charged twice.</b> A bigger button does not tell users their payment went through. The missing principle is feedback. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Users still get lost.</b> Pop-up warnings are ignored. The Back button must behave the same everywhere (consistency) so users can predict it. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Complaints continue.</b> Low-contrast, unlabelled seats are hard to perceive and slow to learn. Design for all users. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Partly fixed.</b> Users with colour vision deficiency still cannot tell the seats apart, and icons are still unlabelled. Use contrast, shape and text, not colour alone. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Did it work?","p":"<p>When users repeat an action, they usually lacked feedback.</p>"},{"h":"Same control, same result","p":"<p>A control that changes behaviour breaks consistency and predictability.</p>"},{"h":"Not colour alone","p":"<p>Combine colour with contrast, shape and labels.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Fixing the ferry app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: users are charged twice because they tap Pay again.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What is the best fix?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Change the button font.","to":"bad","fb":"The font does not tell users anything happened."},{"t":"Show a spinner, disable the button, then show a confirmation screen.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Clear feedback tells users the payment worked."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You applied the feedback principle.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Users need to know their action worked. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Fixing the ferry booking app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief: the ferry company (Kingstown, Bequia and Union Island) can only pay for <b>three fixes</b> before high season, and the CEO wants proof they worked. Prioritise, fix and measure. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Complaints include double charges after tapping Pay twice, an old-fashioned header colour and a seat map older passengers find confusing. Which do you fix FIRST?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The header colour, because first impressions matter.","to":"bad1","fb":"A dated colour is cosmetic. It does not stop anyone booking or cost them money."},{"t":"The double charges, because missing feedback is costing customers money right now.","to":"n2","fb":"Right. Rank problems by their impact: real financial harm comes first."},{"t":"The seat map, because older passengers are an important group.","to":"ok1","fb":"Important, but the double charges are costing money and trust every day."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How exactly do you fix the double charge?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a line of text on the payment page: \"Please tap only once.\"","to":"ok2","fb":"A warning puts the burden on users. The system should prevent the error and confirm success."},{"t":"Make the Pay button bigger and brighter.","to":"bad2","fb":"Size helps perceivability, but users still cannot tell whether the payment worked."},{"t":"Disable Pay after one tap, show a spinner within a second, then a confirmation screen with the booking reference and an email receipt.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Timely, clear, specific feedback, and the system itself prevents the repeat tap."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Second fix: Back means \"previous step\" on the booking pages but logs users out on the payment page. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a pop-up on the payment page explaining that Back logs you out.","to":"bad3","fb":"The pop-up patches the symptom; the control still behaves inconsistently."},{"t":"Make Back always return to the previous step, in the same corner on every screen, and move Log out into the account menu.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Consistent behaviour restores predictability too."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Third fix: the seat map shows available and taken seats both in pale grey, with unlabelled icons. What is your fix?</p>","choices":[{"t":"High-contrast colours plus a cross symbol on taken seats, text labels, a legend and 48 dp seat targets, then a test with five older passengers.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. Perceivability and learnability improved, and validated with the users who struggled."},{"t":"Red for taken seats and green for available ones, with nothing else changed.","to":"ok3","fb":"Better contrast, but colour alone fails users with red-green colour deficiency, and the icons are still unlabelled."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The CEO asks: \"How will you prove these fixes worked?\" What do you propose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Compare a month before and after: refunds for double charges, support calls and app ratings, plus a usability test where passengers book a seat while thinking aloud.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Measurable data plus observed use gives strong evidence."},{"t":"Ask the office staff whether they like the new version.","to":"ok4","fb":"Staff opinion is not evidence of what passengers experience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One fix.</b> Double-charge refunds fall to zero, support calls drop and ratings rise. You prioritised by impact, named each principle (feedback, consistency, predictability, perceivability, learnability) and proved the result with data.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong priority.</b> Older passengers are happier, but double charges continue all season and refunds pile up. A partial success: fix the most damaging problem first.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Fewer double charges, not none.</b> Some users miss the warning. A partial success: design the system so the mistake cannot happen.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Partly fixed.</b> Most passengers manage, but colour-blind users still cannot tell the seats apart. A partial success: use shape and text as well as colour.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>No proof.</b> The staff like it, but the CEO cannot see whether passengers do. A partial success: measure real outcomes.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Money still lost.</b> The new header looks fresh, but passengers are still charged twice. Try again and rank problems by impact.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Still charged twice.</b> A bigger button does not tell users their payment went through. Try again: the missing principle is feedback.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Users still lost.</b> Pop-ups are ignored, and Back still behaves differently. Try again: make the control consistent.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Evaluate an app with two sets of questions","genre":"Structured response: evaluating composition and interaction in a mobile app","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Basseterre Food Fest, held in St. Kitts each year, has hired Island Pulse Media to review its ticket app before launch. Zara describes the booking screen:<br>A photo of a vendor serving johnny cakes sits dead centre, with the horizon cutting the photo in half. The event name, date, venue and six sponsor logos are all the same size. Grey body text sits on a pale yellow background. The Book button is a small grey text link. On the next screen the Book button is a green box in a different corner. Icons have no labels. After a visitor taps \"Pay\", nothing happens for ten seconds and no confirmation appears; some visitors tapped again and were charged XCD 45.00 twice.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write an evaluation for Zara using the two sets of questions.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Composition: is the screen well composed?</b> Evaluate the screen against <b>focal point</b>, <b>hierarchy</b> and <b>contrast</b>. Give one improvement for each, using the rule of thirds or another composition technique where relevant. (6 marks)</li><li><b>Interaction: does it respond as users expect?</b> Evaluate the app against THREE of <b>consistency</b>, <b>learnability</b>, <b>predictability</b> and <b>feedback</b>. Use each principle's silent question and give one specific fix for each. (6 marks)</li></ol>","min":200,"max":400,"frame":"<p><b>(a) Composition.</b> Focal point: the two-second test ... because ... I would ...<br>Hierarchy: ... I would make ... largest, then ..., then ...<br>Contrast: ... I would change ... to ... so it passes 4.5 : 1.<br><b>(b) Interaction.</b> Consistency (will this work the way it did last time?): ... Fix: ...<br>Feedback (did that work?): ... Fix: ...<br>Predictability or learnability: ... Fix: ...<br><b>Judgement:</b> Overall the app ...</p>","checklist":["I answered in two labelled halves: composition (what users see) and interaction (what users do).","I evaluated focal point, hierarchy and contrast with evidence from the screen.","I gave a composition improvement for each, such as moving the vendor to a power point.","I evaluated three interactive design principles and used their silent questions.","I gave a specific fix for each interaction problem, such as a spinner and a confirmation screen.","I ended with an overall judgement about the app."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Composition: focal point, hierarchy and contrast","marks":6,"look":["focal|point of interest|two.?second|prominen","hierarch|largest|most important","contrast|4\\.5|readab|legib","third|power point|golden|horizon|off.?cent","white space|proximity|group|align|depth of field|blur"],"min":3,"strong":"You evaluated focal point, hierarchy and contrast with clear evidence and practical improvements. Next step: link each improvement to the festival visitors.","weak":"Cover all three composition questions: is there one clear focal point (two-second test), is the most important element largest (hierarchy), and can the text be read at 4.5 : 1 (contrast)? Give a fix for each, such as moving the vendor onto a power point and the horizon onto a third line."},{"criterion":"Interaction: principles, silent questions and fixes","marks":6,"look":["consisten|same way|last time","feedback|confirm|spinner|tick|did that work","predict|what happens|outcome|label|undo","learn|first time|onboard|plain language","fix|improve|change|replace|add"],"min":3,"strong":"You evaluated three interaction principles accurately, used the silent questions and proposed specific fixes.","weak":"Name three principles and give a fix for each: consistency (the Book button in the same style and corner on every screen), feedback (a spinner, then a confirmation and receipt), predictability (a button labelled \"Pay XCD 45.00\")."},{"criterion":"Application and judgement","marks":3,"look":["johnny cake|vendor|food|festival|basseterre","visitor|user|customer|tourist","overall|judgement|in conclusion|therefore|most urgent"],"min":2,"strong":"Your evaluation is tailored to the festival and ends with a clear, justified judgement.","weak":"Refer to the festival, the vendor photo and the visitors booking tickets, and finish with an overall judgement: which problem is most urgent and why."}],"model":"<p><b>(a) Composition: is the screen well composed?</b></p><p><b>Focal point:</b> the screen fails the two-second test. The vendor is dead centre and the horizon cuts the photo in half, so the image feels static, and six sponsor logos the same size as the event name compete for attention. I would move the vendor onto the right vertical third with the johnny cakes on the lower-right power point, put the horizon on the upper third and blur the crowd with a shallow depth of field so she becomes the one clear point of interest. <b>Hierarchy:</b> because every text element is the same size, a visitor cannot name the most important element within three seconds. I would make the event name largest, then the date, time and venue grouped in one block, then a large Book button; sponsor logos would move to a separate group at the bottom. <b>Contrast:</b> grey text on pale yellow is hard to read and is unlikely to reach the 4.5 : 1 WCAG ratio. I would use navy text on white, or gold on navy for the heading.</p><p><b>(b) Interaction: does it respond as users expect?</b></p><p><b>Consistency</b> (will this work the way it did last time?): no. The Book button is a grey link on one screen and a green box in another corner on the next. Fix: one Book button style in the same position on every screen. <b>Feedback</b> (did that work?): this is the worst failure. Ten seconds of silence after \"Pay\" made visitors tap again and pay XCD 45.00 twice. Fix: disable the button after one tap, show a spinner, then a success tick, a confirmation screen with the ticket QR code and an email receipt. <b>Predictability</b> (what happens if I press this?): unlabelled icons leave visitors guessing. Fix: label the payment button with its outcome, \"Pay XCD 45.00\", and add text labels to every icon, which also helps learnability for first-time tourists.</p><p><b>Judgement:</b> overall the app is not ready to launch. The composition problems make it look unprofessional, but the missing feedback is most urgent because it costs visitors real money and will damage the festival's reputation. Fixing feedback and consistency first, then hierarchy and contrast, would give visitors a screen they can read in two seconds and trust.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Use the two sets of questions as your headings: \"Composition\" (focal point, hierarchy, contrast) and \"Interaction\" (consistency, learnability, predictability, feedback). For each, give evidence from the screen, a fix and the effect on users. \"Evaluate\" needs a judgement at the end: say which problem matters most and why.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Evaluate an app with two sets of questions (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: composition and interaction, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Composition:</b> the vendor photo is dead centre and the horizon cuts it in half. <b>Give</b> ONE fix using the rule of thirds. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Composition:</b> <b>name</b> ONE hierarchy or contrast problem on the screen and <b>give</b> ONE fix. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Interaction:</b> the Book button looks different and sits in a different place on the next screen. <b>Name</b> the principle that is broken and <b>give</b> ONE fix. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Interaction:</b> after tapping Pay, nothing happens for ten seconds. <b>Name</b> the principle that is broken and <b>give</b> ONE fix. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> which problem should be fixed first, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would move the vendor to ... and put the horizon on ...<br><b>(b)</b> One problem is ... I would ...<br><b>(c)</b> The broken principle is ... I would ...<br><b>(d)</b> The broken principle is ... I would ...<br><b>(e)</b> The problem to fix first is ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I used the rule of thirds to fix the photo.","I named a hierarchy or contrast problem and a fix.","I named consistency for the Book button and gave a fix.","I named feedback for the Pay problem and gave a fix.","I chose the most urgent problem and gave a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Rule of thirds fix","marks":2,"look":["third|power point|off.?cent","horizon|vendor|move|place"],"min":2,"strong":"A correct thirds fix for the photo.","weak":"Move the vendor onto a third line or power point, and put the horizon on the upper or lower third."},{"criterion":"Hierarchy or contrast","marks":2,"look":["hierarch|largest|same size|contrast|grey|yellow|read","make|change|bigger|darker|navy|white|fix"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear problem with a sensible fix.","weak":"For example: everything is the same size, so make the event name largest; or grey on pale yellow is hard to read, so use navy on white."},{"criterion":"Consistency","marks":2,"look":["consisten","same|every screen|one style|position|corner"],"min":2,"strong":"You named consistency and gave a fix.","weak":"The principle is consistency: make the Book button the same style and in the same place on every screen."},{"criterion":"Feedback","marks":2,"look":["feedback","spinner|confirm|tick|receipt|message|disable"],"min":2,"strong":"You named feedback and gave a fix.","weak":"The principle is feedback: show a spinner, then a confirmation message, and stop the button being tapped twice."},{"criterion":"Priority","marks":2,"look":["pay|feedback|charg|twice|money","because|so |as "],"min":2,"strong":"You chose the most urgent problem and explained why.","weak":"Fix the payment problem first, because visitors are losing money when they are charged twice."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would move the vendor to the right third line, with the johnny cakes on a power point, and put the horizon on the upper third.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One problem is that the event name, date and six sponsor logos are all the same size, so nothing stands out. I would make the event name the largest element and move the logos to the bottom.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The broken principle is consistency. I would use the same Book button style in the same corner on every screen.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The broken principle is feedback. I would show a spinner straight away, then a confirmation screen and an email receipt, and disable the button after one tap.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The problem to fix first is the payment problem, because visitors are being charged XCD 45.00 twice and losing money.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for naming the problem or principle and 1 for a fix that fits the festival app. Accept either hierarchy or contrast in (b). Check that students use the exact principle names (consistency, feedback). Once secure, ask them to add a \"because\" to every fix, which moves them towards the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Evaluate an app with two sets of questions (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended evaluation: composition and interaction, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Basseterre Food Fest, held in St. Kitts each year, has hired Island Pulse Media to review its ticket app before launch. Zara describes the booking screen:<br>A photo of a vendor serving johnny cakes sits dead centre, with the horizon cutting the photo in half. The event name, date, venue and six sponsor logos are all the same size and scattered across the screen. Grey body text sits on a pale yellow background. The Book button is a small grey text link. On the next screen the Book button is a green box in a different corner. Icons have no labels, and the venue map uses only red and green dots to show open and sold-out zones. After a visitor taps \"Pay\", nothing happens for ten seconds and no confirmation appears; some visitors tapped again and were charged XCD 45.00 twice.<br>Analytics from a test week show that 40% of ticket buyers are cruise visitors using the app for the first time, and 30% of all visitors abandon the booking at the payment screen.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a full evaluation for Zara.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Composition:</b> evaluate the screen against focal point, hierarchy, contrast and ONE layout principle (proximity, white space or alignment). Justify each improvement with design theory. <i>(8 marks)</i></li><li><b>Interaction:</b> evaluate the app against ALL FIVE interactive design principles (consistency, perceivability, learnability, predictability and feedback), using each silent question, evidence from the stimulus and a specific fix. <i>(10 marks)</i></li><li><b>Prioritise:</b> rank the three most urgent fixes and justify the order using their impact on visitors and on the festival, including the analytics. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Validate:</b> propose how the redesign should be tested with real users, and how success will be measured. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":400,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a) Composition.</b> Focal point: ... Hierarchy: ... Contrast: ... Proximity / white space / alignment: ...<br><b>(b) Interaction.</b> Consistency (will this work the way it did last time?): ... Fix: ... Perceivability (can I see it, and can I tap it?): ... Learnability (how do I do this the first time?): ... Predictability (what happens if I press this?): ... Feedback (did that work?): ...<br><b>(c) Priorities.</b> First ... because ... Second ... Third ...<br><b>(d) Validation.</b> I would test ... with ... Success means ...</p>","checklist":["My composition section covers focal point, hierarchy, contrast and one more layout principle, each with a theory-based fix.","My interaction section covers all five principles with their silent questions, evidence and fixes.","I used the analytics (40% first-time cruise visitors, 30% abandoning at payment) in my reasoning.","I ranked three fixes and justified the order by impact.","My validation plan names real users, tasks and measurable success criteria.","I ended with a clear overall judgement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Composition evaluation","marks":8,"look":["focal|point of interest|two.?second","third|power point|horizon|golden","hierarch|largest|most important","contrast|4\\.5|wcag","proximity|white space|align|group","because|so that|therefore"],"min":5,"strong":"Four composition criteria evaluated, each with a fix justified by design theory.","weak":"Cover focal point, hierarchy, contrast and one layout principle. For each, give the evidence, a fix and the theory behind it."},{"criterion":"Interaction evaluation","marks":10,"look":["consisten","perceiv|signifier|see it|tap it|48|44","learn|first time|onboard|label","predict|what happens|outcome|undo","feedback|confirm|spinner|did that work","colou?r.?blind|colour alone|red and green|deficien","fix|change|add|replace"],"min":6,"strong":"All five principles evaluated with silent questions, evidence and precise fixes.","weak":"Work through all five principles. For each, ask its silent question, point to the problem in the stimulus and give a specific fix."},{"criterion":"Prioritisation","marks":4,"look":["first|most urgent|priority","second|then|next","third|finally|last","30%|40%|abandon|cruise|first.?time|money|refund"],"min":3,"strong":"A clear ranking justified by impact and the analytics.","weak":"Rank three fixes and use the analytics: 30% abandon at payment and 40% are first-time cruise visitors."},{"criterion":"Validation plan and judgement","marks":3,"look":["test|usability|think.?aloud|observ","cruise|visitor|tourist|user","measur|%|rate|fewer|success|compare"],"min":2,"strong":"A realistic test with measurable success criteria and a clear judgement.","weak":"Test the redesign with real visitors, including first-time cruise passengers, and state what numbers would show success."}],"model":"<p><b>(a) Composition: is the screen well composed?</b></p><p><b>Focal point:</b> the screen fails the two-second test. The vendor is dead centre and the horizon splits the photo in half, so the image feels static, and six equal-sized logos compete with it. Moving the vendor onto the right third, with the johnny cakes on the lower-right power point, placing the horizon on the upper third and blurring the crowd with a shallow depth of field would create one clear point of interest, because an off-centre, isolated subject draws the eye and lets the background tell the story. <b>Hierarchy:</b> with every element the same size, a cruise visitor cannot name the most important element within three seconds. The event name should be largest, then the date and venue, then a large Book button, so the eye follows a Z-pattern that ends on the action. <b>Contrast:</b> grey on pale yellow is unlikely to reach the WCAG 4.5 : 1 ratio, and it will be worse on phones in bright sun. Navy text on white, or white on a dark teal banner, would fix legibility because contrast depends on value, not hue. <b>Proximity:</b> the date, time and venue are scattered, so visitors cannot see which details belong together. Grouping them in one block, with sponsor logos in a separate strip at the bottom, would make the information readable before it is even read.</p><p><b>(b) Interaction: does it respond as users expect?</b></p><p><b>Consistency</b> (will this work the way it did last time?): no. The Book button is a grey link on one screen and a green box elsewhere. Fix: one button style in one position on every screen. <b>Perceivability</b> (can I see it, and can I tap it?): a small grey text link does not look tappable, and the map uses red and green alone. Fix: a 48 dp button with a clear label, and sold-out zones marked with a cross and the word \"Sold out\". <b>Learnability</b> (how do I do this the first time?): 40% of buyers are first-time cruise visitors, and unlabelled icons force them to guess. Fix: text labels on every icon and two short, skippable tips on first launch. <b>Predictability</b> (what happens if I press this?): \"Pay\" does not say how much or what will happen. Fix: label it \"Pay XCD 45.00 for 1 ticket\", with a summary screen before payment. <b>Feedback</b> (did that work?): the worst failure. Ten seconds of silence led to double charges. Fix: disable the button after one tap, show a spinner within a second, then a success screen with a QR ticket and an email receipt.</p><p><b>(c) Priorities.</b> First, feedback at payment, because it is costing visitors real money and is the most likely reason 30% abandon at that screen; every lost sale and refund hurts the festival. Second, perceivability and predictability of the Book and Pay buttons, because visitors cannot buy what they cannot find or trust. Third, learnability (labels and tips), because 40% of buyers are first-time users with no time to learn. Composition fixes follow, since they improve trust and clarity but do not block purchases.</p><p><b>(d) Validation.</b> Test a clickable prototype with eight users on their own phones, including four cruise visitors near the pier, asking them to buy one ticket while thinking aloud. Success means at least seven of eight complete the purchase in under two minutes without help, no double payments in a test week, and abandonment at payment falling well below 30%.</p><p><b>Judgement:</b> the app is not ready to launch. Its photo has potential, but until feedback, consistency and perceivability are fixed it will lose sales and damage the festival's reputation.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 8 + 10 + 4 + 3). Top responses use every stimulus detail, including the analytics, and do more than list problems: they rank them by impact and propose a measurable test. Cap responses at about half marks if they skip a principle or give fixes without evidence. Encourage the two labelled halves plus \"Priorities\" and \"Validation\" headings.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U3","title":"Impact and motivations for indigenous digital products","objectives":["Explain the meaning of culture in evolving technology and how cultural identity shapes digital creation.","Use a structured screening, critique and observation method, with clear criteria, to analyse Caribbean digital products.","Use case studies of Caribbean films, animation, games, apps, music videos and digital art to determine their impact and the motivations behind them.","Compare indigenous and imported digital content, and evaluate authenticity, cultural representation and stereotyping.","Discuss how local creators are supported through policy, funding, community and festivals such as the Trinidad and Tobago Film Festival, Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales.","Define indigenous and culture, identify where culture shows up in digital products, and screen them using six techniques (details and background, dialogue/speech, sound effects, camera angles, target audience/market, purpose)."],"videos":[{"title":"Cabbie Chronicles (syllabus resource)","q":"Cabbie Chronicles Jamaican animation"},{"title":"Santana: Pastor Stewart (screening list)","q":"Santana Pastor Stewart Roger Alexis"},{"title":"Caribbean animation industry","q":"Animae Caribe festival Caribbean animation industry"},{"title":"Caribbean film festivals and creators","q":"Trinidad and Tobago Film Festival Caribbean filmmakers"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Indigenous content shapes Caribbean creativity","html":"<p>Start with a simple idea: the Caribbean is home to much <b>\"indigenous content\"</b> that affects our creativity in design. <b>Indigenous</b> refers to an idea of the region or a particular location.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Indigenous: native to a place.</b> Produced, growing, living or occurring natively or naturally in a particular region or environment, as in indigenous plants or indigenous culture.</p>\n<p><b>Culture is the knowledge and character of a group.</b> In one sense, culture is the arts and other manifestations of human intellectual achievement, regarded collectively. In another, it is the characteristics and knowledge of a group: its <b>language and religion</b>, its <b>cuisine and social habits</b>, and its <b>music and arts</b>. Culture is passed on through everyday life, so a group can be recognised by its language, its food (roti, johnny cakes, a plate from the fish fry) and the music and art it makes.</p>\n<p><b>Culture grows a group identity.</b> CARLA (the Center for Advanced Research on Language Acquisition at the University of Minnesota) describes culture as shared patterns of behaviours and interactions, and cognitive constructs, learned through socialisation. Put simply, a group identity grows from social patterns unique to that group. Anthropologist Cristina De Rossi sums it up: culture encompasses \"religion, food, what we wear, how we wear it, our language, marriage, music, what we believe is right or wrong, how we greet visitors... and a million other things.\"</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Indigenous digital product:</b> a digital product (film, animation, game, app, music video, podcast, digital artwork, website) that is conceived, created and owned by people from a place, and that draws on that place's own culture, perspectives and stories. In this unit \"indigenous\" means <b>home-grown Caribbean</b>; it also includes the work of First Peoples of the region such as Kalinago and Lokono communities.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the two definitions word for word: <b>indigenous</b> (native to a place) and <b>culture</b> (the knowledge and character of a group). A Paper 02 answer that opens with an accurate definition, then applies it to a named Caribbean product, starts on firm ground.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Indigenous means native to a place. Culture is the knowledge and character of a group: its language, food, music, beliefs and customs. An indigenous digital product is a film, game, app or other digital work made by people from a place, using their own culture and stories. A Kalinago animator in Dominica telling a story from her own community is making indigenous content.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Two tests decide whether a product is indigenous, and a Grade One answer applies both: <b>who</b> conceived, created and owns it, and <b>whose</b> culture, perspectives and stories it draws on. A Caribbean setting alone is not enough; a foreign studio's island cartoon with no Caribbean writers fails the first test. Culture is not only festivals and food. It includes values, humour, ways of greeting and beliefs about right and wrong, so a product can feel authentic through small details of behaviour. Culture is also learned and always changing, which is why a modern soca music video can be just as indigenous as a recorded folk tale. Open your answer with a precise definition, then apply it.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Where culture shows up in evolving technology","html":"<p>Ask a practical question: <b>where</b> is culture visible in a digital product? There are six places to look.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Where culture shows up</th><th>What to look for</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Language</b></td><td>Dialect, accent, expressions and code-switching in speech, captions and on-screen text</td><td>Dialogue in Sint Maarten English, Jamaican Patois or Papiamentu</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Environment (screen)</b></td><td>The setting we see on screen: streets, buildings, landscape, weather</td><td>Bay Street in Nassau, the Castries market, a Kingston taxi stand</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Appearance</b></td><td>How characters look: skin tones, hair, dress, costume</td><td>Carnival costumes, school uniforms, locs and headwraps</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Content in the story</b></td><td>The plot, characters and themes</td><td>Anansi tales, migration, rebuilding after Hurricane Irma</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Details of the country</b></td><td>Facts, symbols and the <b>achievements of the country</b></td><td>Flags, landmarks, national heroes, sporting success</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>The way events are celebrated</b></td><td>How events are celebrated <b>in the country</b></td><td>Carnival, J'ouvert, Emancipation Day, Junkanoo</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>How technology changes culture</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Preservation</b>: old calypsos, oral histories and photographs can be digitised and archived. Recording elders telling Anansi stories in Antigua keeps them alive.</li>\n<li><b>Transformation</b>: traditions take new forms. Carnival is now livestreamed, J'ouvert is filmed by drones, and soca and dancehall spread through TikTok dance challenges.</li>\n<li><b>Global reach</b>: a creator in Paramaribo can reach the diaspora in New York, Toronto, London and Amsterdam in seconds.</li>\n<li><b>Threat of homogenisation</b>: the same global platforms push the same imported content to everyone. Young people may know more American slang than local expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>How culture shapes digital creation</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Content</b>: stories, folklore (duppies, Papa Bois, Anansi), history (emancipation, migration, hurricanes) and everyday life (minibuses, roadside food stalls, Carnival).</li>\n<li><b>Language and voice</b>: Sint Maarten English, Jamaican Patois, Trinidadian Creole, Papiamentu, Kwéyòl, Dutch and French.</li>\n<li><b>Aesthetics</b>: colours of chattel houses and Carnival costumes, rhythms of steel pan and soca used in editing pace.</li>\n<li><b>Values</b>: community, family and humour shape how characters behave and how stories end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an \"explain the meaning of culture in evolving technology\" question, give a definition of culture, then explain <b>both directions</b>: how technology changes culture and how culture shapes the technology and content people create. One direction alone usually caps your marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Culture shows up in digital products in six places: language, the setting on screen, how characters look, the story, facts and symbols of the country, and how events are celebrated. Technology also changes culture: it can preserve old stories, give traditions new forms and spread them worldwide. A Carnival livestream from Port of Spain reaches family in Toronto in seconds.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The relationship runs both ways, and examiners reward answers that show the tension. The same platform that lets a Vincentian creator reach the diaspora also pushes imported content to local teenagers, so technology can <b>preserve</b> and <b>threaten</b> a culture at the same time. A TikTok dance challenge can revive an old soca song but strip away its history. Recommendation algorithms favour content that is already popular, which tends to help large foreign producers. A strong answer therefore avoids \"technology is good for culture\" or \"technology destroys culture\" and explains under what conditions each happens. Use the six places where culture shows up as evidence headings when you analyse a specific product.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Where does culture show up?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is checking a draft Sint Maarten web video. For each detail, choose where culture shows up.</p>","items":[{"text":"The narrator speaks in a natural Sint Maarten English accent, using local expressions.","options":["Details of the country","Appearance","Language"],"answer":2,"fb":"Accent and local expressions show culture through language."},{"text":"The video is filmed along the Philipsburg boardwalk with Great Bay behind.","options":["Environment (screen)","The way events are celebrated","Content in the story"],"answer":0,"fb":"The setting we see on screen is the environment."},{"text":"Dancers wear sequinned Carnival costumes and headpieces.","options":["Details of the country","Language","Appearance"],"answer":2,"fb":"How people look and dress shows culture through appearance."},{"text":"The plot follows a family rebuilding their home after Hurricane Irma.","options":["Content in the story","Environment (screen)","Appearance"],"answer":0,"fb":"The plot and its themes are the content in the story."},{"text":"A segment celebrates a Sint Maarten athlete's national achievement.","options":["Details of the country","Appearance","Language"],"answer":0,"fb":"The achievement of the country belongs under details of the country."},{"text":"The final scene shows a J'ouvert jump-up at dawn during Carnival.","options":["The way events are celebrated","Environment (screen)","Content in the story"],"answer":0,"fb":"How an event is celebrated in the country is its own place where culture shows up."}],"win":"Excellent. You can spot culture in language, environment, appearance, story content, details of the country and the way events are celebrated.","tutor":[{"h":"Hear, see, follow","p":"<p>What you hear is language; what surrounds the action is environment; how people look is appearance; what happens is the story.</p>"},{"h":"Country and celebration","p":"<p>Achievements and symbols are details of the country; Carnival and holidays show how events are celebrated.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Where does culture show up? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Characters speak in Sint Maarten English.","options":["Appearance","Language"],"answer":1,"fb":"Speech shows culture through language."},{"text":"Dancers wear Carnival costumes.","options":["Environment (screen)","Appearance"],"answer":1,"fb":"Dress shows culture through appearance."},{"text":"The video shows a J'ouvert jump-up.","options":["Language","The way events are celebrated"],"answer":1,"fb":"Carnival shows how events are celebrated."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Where does culture show up? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: every question now has four options, and the products come from across the region. Choose where culture MAINLY shows up in each detail.</p>","items":[{"text":"Captions switch between Dutch and English as the characters code-switch mid-sentence.","options":["Appearance","Language","Details of the country","Environment (screen)"],"answer":1,"fb":"Code-switching between languages is culture shown through language."},{"text":"The scene is set inside a crowded minibus winding through the hills of Grenada.","options":["Details of the country","Environment (screen)","Content in the story","The way events are celebrated"],"answer":1,"fb":"The setting we see on screen is the environment."},{"text":"The national flag and a portrait of a national hero hang on the classroom wall.","options":["Appearance","Details of the country","Environment (screen)","Language"],"answer":1,"fb":"National symbols and heroes are details of the country."},{"text":"The main character has locs and a madras headwrap and wears her school uniform.","options":["Details of the country","Appearance","Content in the story","Language"],"answer":1,"fb":"Hair, dress and uniform show culture through appearance."},{"text":"The plot follows a young man deciding whether to migrate to New York or stay to run the family fishing boat.","options":["Appearance","Environment (screen)","The way events are celebrated","Content in the story"],"answer":3,"fb":"Migration is a theme of the plot: content in the story."},{"text":"A Spicemas scene in Grenada shows Jab Jab revellers covered in oil.","options":["Appearance","The way events are celebrated","Language","Details of the country"],"answer":1,"fb":"Costume is involved, but the detail is mainly about how a festival is celebrated."}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify where culture shows up even when a detail could seem to fit more than one heading."}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media collected these examples for a talk at a youth forum. Sort each one.</p>","main":"Which direction does this example mainly show?","bins":["Technology changing culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"items":[{"t":"A TikTok challenge makes an older soca song popular with a new generation.","bin":0,"fb":"A platform has transformed how and by whom a cultural product is enjoyed."},{"t":"A web designer picks a colour palette inspired by painted chattel houses.","bin":1,"fb":"Local aesthetics are driving design choices."},{"t":"An animator writes all dialogue in Bajan with local expressions.","bin":1,"fb":"Local language and voice are shaping how the product is made."},{"t":"An editor cuts a music video to the rhythm of steel pan.","bin":1,"fb":"A cultural rhythm shapes the editing pace of the product."},{"t":"Carnival parades in Port of Spain are now livestreamed to the diaspora.","bin":0,"fb":"A technology (livestreaming) has changed how a cultural event is experienced."},{"t":"Teenagers pick up American slang from streaming services more than from local radio.","bin":0,"fb":"Imported content delivered by technology is changing language: cultural homogenisation."},{"t":"A game designer bases her villain on the Caribbean folklore figure of the soucouyant.","bin":1,"fb":"Folklore (culture) is shaping the content of a digital product."},{"t":"Elders' stories are recorded and stored in an online heritage archive.","bin":0,"fb":"Digitisation is preserving culture: technology acting on culture."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can explain both directions: technology preserves, transforms and threatens culture, while culture supplies content, language, aesthetics and values for digital creation.","tutor":[{"h":"What acts on what?","p":"<p>Is a platform or device changing how people live, or is a tradition changing what gets made?</p>"},{"h":"Content clues","p":"<p>Folklore, dialect and local colours going INTO a product = culture shaping creation.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which direction? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Carnival is livestreamed to the diaspora.","bin":0,"fb":"Technology changes the experience."},{"t":"A game villain is based on a folklore figure.","bin":1,"fb":"Culture shapes the content."},{"t":"Streaming spreads American slang.","bin":0,"fb":"Technology changes language."},{"t":"Dialogue is written in local dialect.","bin":1,"fb":"Culture shapes the product."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Technology shaping culture or culture shaping technology? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: technology's effect on culture is now split into three. Sort each example by what it MAINLY shows.</p>","main":"What does this example mainly show?","bins":["Technology preserving culture","Technology transforming culture","Technology threatening culture","Culture shaping digital creation"],"items":[{"t":"A national archive digitises calypso recordings from the 1950s.","bin":0,"fb":"Digitising old recordings keeps them safe: preservation."},{"t":"Streaming recommendations push the same US shows to every teenager on the island.","bin":2,"fb":"The same imported content for everyone risks homogenisation: a threat."},{"t":"A game's villain is based on the Ol' Higue of Guyanese folklore.","bin":3,"fb":"Folklore supplies the content: culture shaping creation."},{"t":"An editor cuts a promo to the rhythm of tassa drumming.","bin":3,"fb":"A cultural rhythm sets the editing pace: culture shaping creation."},{"t":"A TikTok dance challenge makes an older soca song popular with a new generation.","bin":1,"fb":"A platform changes how and by whom the music is enjoyed: transformation."},{"t":"A web designer draws her palette from painted chattel houses.","bin":3,"fb":"Local aesthetics drive the design: culture shaping creation."},{"t":"J'ouvert is now filmed by drones and streamed live to the diaspora.","bin":1,"fb":"The tradition continues in a new form: transformation."},{"t":"Children use more American slang from YouTube than local expressions.","bin":2,"fb":"Imported media is replacing local language: a threat."},{"t":"Elders' oral histories are recorded and posted on a heritage website.","bin":0,"fb":"Recording and archiving stories preserves them for future generations."}],"win":"Excellent. You can show that technology preserves, transforms and threatens culture, and that culture in turn shapes what creators make."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Screening, critique and observation","html":"<p>You are expected to <b>screen</b> (watch, play or use) a product, <b>observe</b> it carefully and <b>critique</b> it.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>A critique is a careful judgement.</b> The word entered English in the early 18th century, when it meant a piece of writing that criticised a literary or artistic work. Criticism, critique and review overlap, but critique is the more formal: a careful judgement giving an opinion.</p>\n<p>In practice, a critique goes beyond saying whether you like a work. It explains <b>what the work does</b>, <b>how well it does it</b> and <b>who it is meant for</b>, using evidence and clear criteria.</p>\n<p><b>The method: before, during and after</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Before</b>: note the title, creator, country, year, format and platform. Research the intended audience and purpose.</li>\n<li><b>During (observation)</b>: watch or play at least twice. The first time, react as an audience member. The second time, pause and take timed notes (e.g. \"02:15 low angle on the cabbie, comic effect\"). Screenshot key frames for evidence.</li>\n<li><b>After (critique)</b>: apply your criteria, weigh strengths and weaknesses, and reach a justified judgement.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Questions to ask</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose and audience</b></td><td>Who is it for: locals, diaspora, tourists, children? Does it achieve its aim (entertain, inform, sell, preserve)?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cultural content and authenticity</b></td><td>Whose story is it? Are language, setting, music and characters true to the culture? Is anyone stereotyped?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Technical quality</b></td><td>Composition, camera work, lighting, sound, animation, editing, interface and performance on a phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Design and storytelling</b></td><td>Structure, pacing, character, use of visual design principles, interactivity in games and apps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Impact</b></td><td>Views, shares, awards, sales, media coverage, social and economic effects. Use real evidence or say \"check the latest figures\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Motivation</b></td><td>Why did the creator make it: identity, profit, education, social change, preservation, exposure?</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Language of critique:</b> use balanced, evaluative phrases: \"is effective because\", \"is limited by\", \"however\", \"on balance\", \"this suggests\". Support every point with an observation from the product.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Critique\" and \"evaluate\" questions need <b>strengths, weaknesses and a judgement</b>. A long description of the plot earns very little. Use the point, evidence, explain pattern for each criterion.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A critique is a careful, fair judgement of a work. Before watching, note who made it, when and for whom. Watch it twice: once to enjoy it and once to pause and take timed notes. Then judge it against clear criteria such as purpose, authenticity, technical quality and impact. A film review in a Jamaican newspaper should do the same, not just retell the plot.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A critique is only as strong as its evidence. Timed notes such as \"02:15, low angle on the cabbie\" let you point to a moment instead of giving a vague impression. Judge a product by its <b>own</b> purpose and audience: a low-budget comedy for local viewers should not lose marks for lacking Hollywood effects if its writing and timing work for that audience. Balance matters too. Support both strengths and weaknesses, and make sure the final judgement follows from them. A common mistake is to confuse personal taste with criteria. \"I did not like it\" is an opinion; \"the slow pacing in the middle section loses the comic energy\" is a critique.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Run a screening and critique","intro":"<p>Your class is critiquing a Caribbean animated short. Put the steps of the screening and critique method in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Record title, creator, country, year, format and platform"},{"t":"Research the intended audience and purpose"},{"t":"Watch once as an audience member and note first reactions"},{"t":"Watch again, pausing to take timed notes and screenshots"},{"t":"Apply each criterion: audience, authenticity, technical quality, storytelling, impact, motivation"},{"t":"Weigh strengths against weaknesses"},{"t":"Write a justified overall judgement supported by evidence"}],"win":"Correct. Context first, two viewings with careful observation, criteria, then a balanced and justified judgement: that is a structured critique.","tutor":[{"h":"Context first","p":"<p>You need to know who made it and for whom before you can judge it.</p>"},{"h":"Judge last","p":"<p>The judgement comes only after the evidence and the weighing up.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Run a critique (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Note the creator, year and audience"},{"t":"Watch and take timed notes"},{"t":"Apply the criteria"},{"t":"Write a justified judgement"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Run a screening and critique (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: the full method, including improvements and a final fact check. Put all nine steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Record the title, creator, country, year, format and platform"},{"t":"Research the intended audience and purpose"},{"t":"Watch once as an audience member and note first reactions"},{"t":"Watch again, taking timed notes under each screening technique and capturing screenshots"},{"t":"Apply each criterion: audience, authenticity, technical quality, storytelling, impact, motivation"},{"t":"Weigh the strengths against the weaknesses"},{"t":"Write a justified overall judgement supported by evidence"},{"t":"Suggest specific improvements the creator could make"},{"t":"Check every fact and figure in the critique against a reliable source before submitting"}],"win":"Correct. Context, two viewings, criteria, balance, judgement, improvements and a final fact check: a complete, honest critique."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Screening techniques","html":"<p>There are six <b>screening techniques</b>. Before you screen, watch the four videos of digital content indigenous to the Caribbean (including the <i>Santana</i> \"Pastor Stewart\" episode) <b>with an open mind</b>, then note what each element contributes before forming a judgement.</p>\n<p><b>Part 1: look closely</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Details and background</b>: look at the details and background. What does the setting tell you about the place, the time and the people? A painted chattel house or a Swan Street shopfront signals the Caribbean without a word being spoken.</li>\n<li><b>Dialogue/speech</b>: look at the dialogue and speech. Is it in a local dialect? Does the accent sound natural or forced? In <i>Santana</i>, Trinidadian speech carries much of the humour.</li>\n<li><b>Sound effects</b>: look at sound effects and their <b>appropriateness</b>. Do the sounds suit the scene (traffic on a Kingston street, steel pan at a fete), or do they distract?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Part 2: judge purpose and audience</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Camera angles</b>: look at camera angles and the importance of the captured images. A low angle can make a character look powerful; a wide shot can show off a landscape; a close-up shows emotion.</li>\n<li><b>Target audience/market</b>: identify the target audience or market. Is it for local viewers, the diaspora, tourists, children or adults?</li>\n<li><b>Purpose</b>: suggest or state clearly the purpose of the digital media product: to entertain, inform, persuade, sell or preserve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask <b>who</b> the product is made for and <b>why</b>. A video aimed at local viewers may use references that an outside audience would miss: a joke about a Bridgetown landmark or a line of Bajan slang lands at home but may puzzle a viewer in Toronto. That is not a weakness if the target audience is local.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use the six techniques as headings for your screening notes, and write at least one timed observation under each (for example \"01:40, close-up on Pastor Stewart's face, comic effect\"). In Paper 02, a critique organised by technique, with evidence, is far stronger than a plot summary.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are six screening techniques. Look at the details and background, listen to the dialogue and speech, and judge the sound effects. Then think about the camera angles, the target audience and the purpose. Write a timed note for each. Watching a Barbadian comedy sketch, you might note: \"00:40, close-up on the grandmother's face shows her shock.\"</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The techniques work best when you connect them. A low camera angle may only make sense once you know the purpose is comedy, because it makes a pompous character's fall funnier. Dialogue that an outsider finds hard to follow is a strength if the target audience is local. Grade One critiques use the techniques as <b>evidence for a judgement</b>, not as a checklist to tick: each observation should support a claim about how well the product achieves its purpose. Sound is often forgotten, yet unsuitable music or a sound effect that is too loud can undercut an otherwise authentic scene. Note what is <b>missing</b> as well as what is there.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the screening technique","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media screened a Caribbean short and wrote these notes. Match each screening technique to the note that uses it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Details and background","r":"\"Behind the actors you can see a painted chattel house and a snow-cone stall.\""},{"l":"Dialogue/speech","r":"\"The characters speak natural Trinidadian Creole, and the jokes depend on it.\""},{"l":"Sound effects","r":"\"The car horn and minibus music suit the street scene; nothing feels out of place.\""},{"l":"Camera angles","r":"\"A low-angle shot makes the pastor look powerful, which makes his fall funnier.\""},{"l":"Target audience/market","r":"\"It is aimed at adults in Trinidad and the diaspora who know the humour.\""},{"l":"Purpose","r":"\"The main aim is to entertain, while gently mocking hypocrisy.\""}],"win":"Well matched. You can use all six screening techniques: details and background, dialogue/speech, sound effects, camera angles, target audience/market and purpose.","tutor":[{"h":"Look or judge?","p":"<p>The first three techniques are about what you see and hear. The last three ask how it was filmed, who it is for and why it was made.</p>"},{"h":"Appropriateness","p":"<p>Which note judges whether the sounds suit the scene?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the technique (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Details and background","r":"\"A chattel house is behind the actors.\""},{"l":"Dialogue/speech","r":"\"They speak in Trinidadian Creole.\""},{"l":"Camera angles","r":"\"A low-angle shot makes him look powerful.\""},{"l":"Purpose","r":"\"The aim is to entertain.\""}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the screening technique (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: these notes are evaluative, and some mention more than one thing. Match each technique to the note that MAINLY uses it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Details and background","r":"\"The school timetable on the wall and the hurricane shutters quietly tell us it is September.\""},{"l":"Dialogue/speech","r":"\"The jokes land because the characters code-switch between Standard English and Creole.\""},{"l":"Sound effects","r":"\"A rooster crowing at midday is out of place and breaks the scene.\""},{"l":"Camera angles","r":"\"A tight close-up of the grandmother's hands makes us feel her grief.\""},{"l":"Target audience/market","r":"\"Jokes about a Kingston radio host would puzzle viewers in London, but the series is made for Jamaica.\""},{"l":"Purpose","r":"\"Behind the jokes, the short is trying to persuade young viewers to register to vote.\""}],"win":"Excellent. You matched evaluative notes to the right technique, which is how a critique turns observation into evidence."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Case studies: Caribbean animation","html":"<p>The syllabus names two resources for this objective: <b>Cabbie Chronicles</b> and <b>Santana</b>. Use them as your core case studies.</p>\n<p><b>Case study 1: Cabbie Chronicles (Jamaica)</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What</b>: a short 2D animated comedy, <i>Cabbie Chronicles: Drive Thru Drama</i>, by Jamaican animators Allison and Anieph Latchman. It won the Best Caribbean Animation Award at the Animae Caribe Animation and New Media Festival in 2010.</li>\n<li><b>Culture</b>: Kingston street life, a Jamaican taxi driver, Patois dialogue and the humour of everyday encounters.</li>\n<li><b>Motivations</b>: to tell Jamaican stories in a Jamaican voice; to show that world-standard animation can be made in the Caribbean; to build a portfolio and attract funding for a longer series.</li>\n<li><b>Impact</b>: it circulated widely online, was recognised at a regional festival, and is often cited as an example of what Caribbean animation can be. It helped prove that local audiences want to see themselves on screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Case study 2: Santana (Trinidad and Tobago)</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What</b>: a series of comic puppet shorts created and voiced by Trinidadian filmmaker Roger Alexis, who made his first short as a University of the West Indies film assignment. Characters include Santana, his girlfriend Janice, the neighbour Narine and <b>Pastor Stewart</b>, a charismatic but womanising preacher (the \"Santana Pastor Stewart\" episode is one of the videos to screen). The shorts use Trinidadian dialogue and soap-opera-style melodrama, are aimed at adults and use bawdy humour.</li>\n<li><b>Culture</b>: Trini accents, humour, family and neighbourhood drama, and the Indo- and Afro-Trinidadian mix of its characters.</li>\n<li><b>Motivations</b>: entertainment, local comedy and testing what a low-budget creator could achieve by distributing directly online.</li>\n<li><b>Impact</b>: the shorts went viral across the Caribbean and its diaspora, and led to a feature film, <i>I'm Santana!</i>, which premiered in Trinidad cinemas in January 2012. Alexis shared the shorts free on YouTube and on his own site, Lexo TV. It shows how <b>online distribution</b> lets a local creator build an audience without a TV network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Animation as an industry:</b> Trinidad and Tobago's Animae Caribe festival and its animation hub support productions such as <i>Spin &amp; Scratch</i>, an animated series by soca artist Anslem Douglas being developed at the Animae Caribe HOUSE animation hub. Projects like this are motivated by cultural celebration, youth employment and export earnings.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Note that Santana is adult comedy. For a critique, focus on its <b>techniques, language, distribution and impact</b>, and note that its humour relies partly on exaggerated character types, which links to the stereotypes discussion later in this unit.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Two well-known Caribbean animation case studies are Cabbie Chronicles from Jamaica, an animated comedy about a Kingston taxi driver, and Santana from Trinidad and Tobago, a comic puppet series by Roger Alexis that spread online and led to a feature film. Both use local voices, places and humour, and both show that Caribbean creators can reach large audiences.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Compare the two routes to impact. Cabbie Chronicles gained credibility through a festival award, which suits creators seeking funding and studio contacts. Santana grew through direct online distribution, building an audience without a TV network, and that audience made a feature film possible. Neither route is automatically better; a strong answer links the route to the creator's goal. Be careful with claims: use facts you can check (the creators, the country, the 2010 Animae Caribe award, the 2012 film) and describe reach cautiously. Santana also raises a critique point. Its exaggerated character types drive the comedy, so discuss whether this is affectionate self-mockery or whether it reinforces stereotypes.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the motivation","intro":"<p>Creators make indigenous digital products for different reasons. Match each Caribbean creator's statement to the main motivation behind it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Cultural identity","r":"\"I want Guyanese children to hear their own accent in a cartoon.\""},{"l":"Preservation","r":"\"Our grandparents' Anansi stories will be lost unless we record them now.\""},{"l":"Economic gain","r":"\"If the game sells well on Steam, I can hire three local artists.\""},{"l":"Education","r":"\"The app teaches students about emancipation in an interactive way.\""},{"l":"Social change","r":"\"Our music video challenges violence against women.\""},{"l":"Tourism and nation branding","r":"\"The digital campaign shows visitors the real Grenada beyond the beach.\""},{"l":"Exposure and career building","r":"\"Winning at a festival will get my work noticed by studios abroad.\""}],"win":"Very good. Identity, preservation, money, education, social change, nation branding and career building are the main motivations you can cite in a case study.","tutor":[{"h":"Who benefits?","p":"<p>Ask whether the main benefit goes to the culture, the learner, society, the economy or the creator's career.</p>"},{"h":"Keep and pass on","p":"<p>Saving something before it is lost is preservation.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the motivation (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Cultural identity","r":"\"Children should hear their own accent on screen.\""},{"l":"Preservation","r":"\"We must record the old stories before they are lost.\""},{"l":"Economic gain","r":"\"Sales will let me hire local artists.\""},{"l":"Education","r":"\"The app teaches history interactively.\""}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the motivation (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: these statements are less direct. Match each creator's words to the main motivation behind them.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Cultural identity","r":"\"Our hero speaks Kwéyòl because that is how my grandmother spoke to me.\""},{"l":"Preservation","r":"\"We filmed the last boat builders in Carriacou explaining every step of their craft.\""},{"l":"Economic gain","r":"\"Merchandise and streaming licences are what pay the studio's rent.\""},{"l":"Education","r":"\"Each episode ends with a short quiz on the history it has just shown.\""},{"l":"Social change","r":"\"The series shows a young father talking openly about depression, to break the silence.\""},{"l":"Tourism and nation branding","r":"\"Visitors should see Belize as more than cayes and resorts.\""},{"l":"Exposure and career building","r":"\"This short is my calling card for a job at an animation studio abroad.\""}],"win":"Excellent. You can infer a creator's motivation from indirect evidence, which is what a case study answer needs."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Case studies: film, games, apps, music videos and digital art","html":"<p><b>Film</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><i>The Harder They Come</i> (Jamaica, 1972), starring Jimmy Cliff, is a landmark of Caribbean cinema. Its soundtrack helped take reggae to the world, showing how one film can export a whole culture.</li>\n<li>Jamaican director Storm Saulter's <i>Better Mus' Come</i> (2010) and <i>Sprinter</i> (2018) tell Jamaican stories of politics, family and athletics, and screened at international festivals.</li>\n<li><b>Motivations</b>: telling stories from the inside, correcting outsiders' images, and building a regional film industry. <b>Impact</b>: international attention, tourism interest, jobs for crews and actors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Games</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><i>Duppy Detective Tashia</i> is a point-and-click mystery game by Glen Henry of Spritewrench Studios in Kingston, Jamaica, released on Steam. It is set in a hidden market under a cotton tree and draws on Caribbean folklore, including Anansi. It shows a solo indie developer reaching a global market through a digital store.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Apps</b> (keep claims general unless you have checked the facts): Caribbean developers have built apps for local news, ride hailing, mobile payments, Carnival schedules, tourism guides and Creole language learning. Their motivations are usually to solve local problems that imported apps ignore (local addresses, local currencies, local events) and to keep revenue in the region.</p>\n<p><b>Music videos</b>: soca, dancehall and reggae videos are some of the most-watched Caribbean digital products. They showcase landscapes, dance, fashion and language, and often support tourism, Carnival and artists' careers. Critique them for representation as well as production quality.</p>\n<p><b>Digital art</b>: Caribbean illustrators and designers share digital paintings, comics and NFT-style or print-on-demand work on Instagram and Behance. Typical motivations are personal identity, income from commissions and prints, and visibility for Caribbean aesthetics.</p>\n<p><b>Local connection</b>: in every territory, local creators produce Carnival livestreams, music videos for local soca and dancehall artists, short documentaries on hurricane recovery and tourism content. When you choose a local case study, interview the creator if you can: it gives you first-hand evidence of motivation.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For each case study, learn a <b>four-line profile</b>: what it is, who made it and where, why they made it (motivation) and what happened as a result (impact). Never invent viewing figures: write \"it was widely shared\" or \"check the current view count\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Caribbean digital products include films, games, apps, music videos and digital art. The Harder They Come (Jamaica, 1972) helped take reggae to the world. Duppy Detective Tashia is a Jamaican game built on folklore and sold on Steam. Local apps solve problems imported apps ignore, such as local addresses. For each case study, learn what it is, who made it, why and what happened.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each format reaches audiences differently, and that shapes its impact. A film can export a whole culture, as The Harder They Come did with reggae, but films are costly and slow to make. A game on a global store such as Steam lets one indie developer reach players worldwide, though standing out among thousands of releases is hard. Apps usually succeed by solving a local problem better than imported products, so their impact is practical rather than artistic. Music videos often support tourism and artists' careers, which makes them strong nation-branding tools. Choose case studies you can support with checked facts, and never guess viewing or sales figures.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Case study quick-fire","intro":"<p>Test your recall of the case studies in this unit.</p>","items":[{"text":"Cabbie Chronicles is an example of...","options":["a Barbadian mobile app","a Trinidadian puppet series","Jamaican 2D animation"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cabbie Chronicles is a Jamaican animated short by Allison and Anieph Latchman."},{"text":"Santana spread mainly through...","options":["national TV networks","a mobile game store","online sharing across the Caribbean and diaspora"],"answer":2,"fb":"The puppet shorts went viral online, showing the power of direct online distribution."},{"text":"Which film helped take reggae music to a world audience in the 1970s?","options":["The Harder They Come","Sprinter","I'm Santana!"],"answer":0,"fb":"The Harder They Come (1972) and its soundtrack exported reggae globally."},{"text":"Duppy Detective Tashia draws its setting and characters mainly from...","options":["American superhero comics","European fairy tales","Caribbean folklore"],"answer":2,"fb":"It is rooted in Caribbean folklore, including Anansi, which makes it an indigenous product."},{"text":"A developer builds a Carnival app because imported apps do not list local events. The main motivation is...","options":["avoiding the local market","copying a foreign trend","solving a local problem"],"answer":2,"fb":"Many indigenous apps exist to fill gaps that imported products ignore."},{"text":"Which is the BEST way to describe impact when you do not have reliable figures?","options":["Invent a realistic number","Leave impact out","Say it was widely shared and suggest checking current figures"],"answer":2,"fb":"Never invent statistics. Describe impact honestly and point to where figures can be checked."}],"win":"Excellent. You can profile Caribbean case studies by creator, culture, motivation and impact, honestly and accurately.","tutor":[{"h":"Country and format","p":"<p>Cabbie Chronicles: Jamaica, animation. Santana: Trinidad, puppets.</p>"},{"h":"Honesty with figures","p":"<p>Examiners reward accurate, cautious claims over made-up numbers.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Case study quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Cabbie Chronicles comes from...","options":["Barbados","Jamaica"],"answer":1,"fb":"It is a Jamaican animated short."},{"text":"Santana is a series of...","options":["puppet shorts","video games"],"answer":0,"fb":"It is a Trinidadian puppet series."},{"text":"If you have no reliable figure, you should...","options":["describe impact generally","invent a number"],"answer":0,"fb":"Never invent statistics."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Case study quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: these questions ask you to compare, judge and handle evidence, not just recall.</p>","items":[{"text":"Cabbie Chronicles gained recognition through a festival award, while Santana built its audience mainly online. What does this comparison BEST show?","options":["There is more than one route to impact: credibility from festivals and reach from direct online distribution","Neither product had any real impact","Online videos cannot win awards","Festivals are always better than online sharing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Both routes worked, for different goals. A strong answer links the route to what the creator wanted."},{"text":"Santana's online success led to which outcome?","options":["a mobile game on Steam","a reggae soundtrack album","a feature film, I'm Santana!, shown in Trinidad cinemas","a TV network buying all rights in 1972"],"answer":2,"fb":"The feature film premiered in Trinidad cinemas in January 2012."},{"text":"Why is The Harder They Come often used as an example of cultural export?","options":["Its soundtrack helped take reggae to audiences worldwide","It was the first Caribbean app","It was a puppet series","It was made by a Hollywood studio"],"answer":0,"fb":"One Jamaican film helped carry a whole musical culture abroad."},{"text":"Duppy Detective Tashia was released on Steam. What does this show about distribution?","options":["Folklore games cannot be sold abroad","Games must be sold in shops","Steam sells only Caribbean games","A solo indie developer can reach a global market through a digital store"],"answer":3,"fb":"Digital stores lower the barrier to a global audience, though standing out is still hard."},{"text":"A student writes \"Santana had 50 million views\" but has no source. What should she do?","options":["Keep it, because it sounds convincing","Double it to be safe","Replace it with a cautious claim, such as \"it was widely shared across the Caribbean and its diaspora\", or find a reliable source","Remove all mention of impact"],"answer":2,"fb":"Accurate, cautious claims score better than impressive guesses. Impact still needs discussing."},{"text":"Which point about Santana is EVALUATION rather than description?","options":["Its exaggerated character types drive the comedy, but they risk reinforcing stereotypes for viewers outside Trinidad","Santana is a puppet show","It was made in Trinidad","The characters include Pastor Stewart"],"answer":0,"fb":"Evaluation weighs a strength against a limitation. The others are facts."}],"win":"Excellent. You compared routes to impact, handled evidence honestly and recognised the difference between describing and evaluating."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Indigenous versus imported content","html":"<p><b>Imported content</b> is produced outside the region and brought in, mostly from the United States, Europe and increasingly Asia (for example Hollywood films, Netflix series, K-pop, Japanese anime and global games). Across the Caribbean, most TV, streaming, games and apps that young people use are imported.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Indigenous content</th><th>Imported content</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cultural relevance</td><td>Reflects local language, places, values and humour</td><td>Reflects the producing country's culture</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Budget and production values</td><td>Often small budgets, smaller crews, fewer resources</td><td>Often very large budgets, big studios, high polish</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Distribution and marketing</td><td>Often relies on social media, festivals and word of mouth</td><td>Global platforms, algorithms and large marketing campaigns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Audience size</td><td>Smaller home market, plus the diaspora</td><td>Global audiences</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Economic effect</td><td>Money, jobs and skills stay in the region</td><td>Money flows out through subscriptions and licences</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Identity effect</td><td>Builds pride and a sense of \"seeing ourselves\"</td><td>Can lead to cultural homogenisation and loss of local expression</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>A balanced view</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Imported content is not \"bad\". It entertains, teaches global skills and inspires local creators. Many Caribbean animators learned by studying Japanese anime and Pixar films.</li>\n<li><b>Cultural imperialism</b> is the concern that powerful countries' media dominate and gradually replace local cultures.</li>\n<li><b>Hybrid content</b> mixes both: a Caribbean anime-style comic, a soca track with K-pop choreography, or a local story told through a global platform such as YouTube or Steam. Hybrid work is often how indigenous content reaches global audiences.</li>\n<li>A product <i>about</i> the Caribbean is not automatically indigenous. A foreign studio's cartoon set on a Caribbean island, written without Caribbean writers, is imported content with a Caribbean theme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Compare\" questions need <b>similarities as well as differences</b>. Both types can be high quality, both use the same platforms and both compete for the same audience's attention. Show the examiner you see both sides, then judge.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Indigenous content is made in the region by local people. Imported content is made elsewhere, such as Hollywood films, K-pop and anime. Imported content often has bigger budgets and global marketing; indigenous content usually has smaller budgets but local relevance, and keeps money and jobs in the region. Hybrid content mixes both, like a Belizean comic drawn in a manga style.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A Grade One comparison avoids two easy positions: that imported content is \"bad\", or that indigenous content is automatically better. Both can be excellent, both compete on the same platforms and both shape young people's tastes. The real issue is <b>balance and power</b>. When most of what audiences see comes from a few large foreign producers, local expression can fade, which is the concern behind the term cultural imperialism. Hybrid work complicates the picture in a useful way: it can be the route by which indigenous stories reach global audiences, but if the imported style takes over completely, the local voice may disappear. Always judge by who creates the product and whose culture it carries.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid?","intro":"<p>Zara is building a playlist for a Dominica youth media workshop. Sort each product.</p>","main":"Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?","bins":["Indigenous","Imported","Hybrid"],"items":[{"t":"Cabbie Chronicles, made in Jamaica by Jamaican animators.","bin":0,"fb":"Conceived and made locally from local culture: indigenous."},{"t":"A Japanese anime series dubbed into English.","bin":1,"fb":"Produced in Japan; dubbing does not change who created it."},{"t":"A Trinidadian comic about Papa Bois drawn in a manga style.","bin":2,"fb":"Local folklore and creator using an imported art style: hybrid."},{"t":"A Hollywood superhero film streamed on a global platform.","bin":1,"fb":"Produced abroad for a global audience, with no local creative input: imported."},{"t":"A Roseau artist's bouyon track with K-pop-style choreography in the video.","bin":2,"fb":"Local creator and genre combined with an imported style: hybrid."},{"t":"A St. Lucian app that teaches Kwéyòl, built by a local developer.","bin":0,"fb":"Local language, local creator, local need: indigenous."},{"t":"A European studio's cartoon set on a Caribbean island, with no Caribbean writers.","bin":1,"fb":"A Caribbean setting does not make it indigenous. It was created from outside: imported."},{"t":"A Dominica documentary on Hurricane Maria recovery by local filmmakers.","bin":0,"fb":"Local creators telling a local story: indigenous."}],"win":"Well sorted. Who creates the product and whose culture it comes from decide whether it is indigenous; mixing local content with imported styles creates hybrid work.","tutor":[{"h":"Who made it?","p":"<p>A Caribbean setting alone is not enough. Check who wrote and created it.</p>"},{"h":"Mix of both","p":"<p>Local creator plus an imported style or format suggests hybrid.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A Hollywood film.","bin":1,"fb":"Made abroad: imported."},{"t":"A Jamaican animation by Jamaican creators.","bin":0,"fb":"Local creators and culture: indigenous."},{"t":"A local soca song with K-pop dance moves.","bin":2,"fb":"Local plus imported style: hybrid."},{"t":"A local documentary on Hurricane Maria.","bin":0,"fb":"Local story by local people: indigenous."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Indigenous, imported or hybrid? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: decide by <b>who created it</b> and <b>whose culture it carries</b>, not by where it was filmed, who paid for it or where it is shown.</p>","main":"Is this product indigenous, imported or hybrid?","bins":["Indigenous","Imported","Hybrid"],"items":[{"t":"A US studio's comedy about a Caribbean sports team, written and directed by Americans.","bin":1,"fb":"A Caribbean theme does not make it indigenous; it was created from outside."},{"t":"A K-pop group's music video filmed on a beach in Antigua.","bin":1,"fb":"The location is Caribbean, but the creators and culture are Korean: imported."},{"t":"An Antiguan dancehall artist's video with choreography borrowed from K-pop.","bin":2,"fb":"Local artist and genre mixed with an imported style: hybrid."},{"t":"A Hollywood film dubbed into Papiamentu for viewers in Curaçao.","bin":1,"fb":"Dubbing changes the language track, not who created the work: imported."},{"t":"A foreign studio's island cartoon co-written with Dominican writers and voiced by local actors as equal partners.","bin":2,"fb":"Created jointly by foreign and local creators: a hybrid co-production."},{"t":"A St. Lucian Kwéyòl storytelling podcast made by local teachers.","bin":0,"fb":"Local language, creators and purpose: indigenous."},{"t":"A Barbadian studio's animated series about a Bajan family, licensed to a US streaming service.","bin":0,"fb":"Local creators and local culture: indigenous. Where it streams does not change who made it."},{"t":"A Dominica documentary on the history of the Roseau market by local filmmakers, funded by a European cultural programme.","bin":0,"fb":"The funding is foreign, but the creators and story are local: indigenous."},{"t":"A Guyanese animator's short about the Ol' Higue, drawn in an anime style.","bin":2,"fb":"Local creator and folklore with an imported art style: hybrid."}],"win":"Excellent. Filming location, funding and distribution do not decide the category. Creators and cultural source do, and mixing them creates hybrid work."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Authenticity, representation and stereotypes","html":"<p><b>Authenticity</b> is the quality of being genuine and true to the culture represented: accurate language, real places, believable characters, respectful use of traditions, and creators who have the right knowledge or permission. <b>Cultural representation</b> is how a group of people is shown in media, and who gets to do the showing.</p>\n<p>A <b>stereotype</b> is an oversimplified, fixed and often negative image of a group: for example, Caribbean people shown only as lazy, always partying, always speaking in a comic accent, or always involved in crime. Imported media has often shown the Caribbean as just a beach backdrop for foreign visitors, with local people as waiters, villains or comic relief.</p>\n<p><b>Why authenticity and representation matter</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Identity and self-esteem</b>: young people need to see heroes who look, sound and live like them.</li>\n<li><b>Accuracy</b>: misrepresentation spreads false ideas to global audiences, tourists and investors.</li>\n<li><b>Respect</b>: sacred or historic traditions (such as Kalinago heritage, or emancipation history) should not be used carelessly.</li>\n<li><b>Audience trust</b>: Caribbean audiences quickly notice a fake accent or a wrong detail, and reject the product.</li>\n<li><b>Market value</b>: authentic stories stand out in a global market full of similar content.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Grey areas to discuss</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Comedy and exaggeration</b>: local comedy (such as Santana's puppets) often exaggerates character types for laughs. Is it affectionate self-mockery or does it reinforce stereotypes? Critique both sides.</li>\n<li><b>\"Tourist gaze\"</b>: tourism videos that show only beaches and luxury hide the real island of schools, workers and neighbourhoods.</li>\n<li><b>Who tells the story</b>: a foreign creator can make respectful work by consulting and employing local writers, actors and advisers. A local creator can still stereotype.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>How to make work authentic</b>: research with the community, consult elders and cultural experts, use local voice actors, film in real locations, credit and pay contributors, seek permission for traditional designs, and test with local audiences before release.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you identify a stereotype in a critique, explain <b>why</b> it is harmful and suggest <b>how</b> it could be fixed. Identification alone is description; explanation plus improvement is evaluation.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Authentic means genuine and true to the culture shown: real places, natural language and believable characters. A stereotype is a fixed, oversimplified image of a group, such as showing Caribbean people only as waiters or party-goers. Good creators research with the community, use local voice actors and ask permission before using traditional designs, as a Grenadian studio might when consulting elders about a folk tale.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Authenticity is about the <b>process</b> as well as the final product. Who was consulted, who was paid and credited, and who gave permission all matter, especially for sacred or historic traditions such as Kalinago heritage. Representation also has a quantity side: one stereotyped character may be harmless, but if local people only ever appear as waiters, villains or comic relief, that pattern shapes how outsiders and young viewers see the region. Comedy is the hardest grey area. Exaggeration can be affectionate self-mockery when it comes from inside the culture, yet the same joke can reinforce prejudice when a global audience sees nothing else. In a critique, identify the issue, explain the harm and suggest a fix.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the authenticity problems","intro":"<p>Malik is reviewing the script notes for a foreign-made tourism cartoon about St. Lucia.</p>","q":"Select every note that shows a problem with authenticity or representation.","lines":["Every local character is a waiter, a bartender or a comic taxi driver.","The voice actors are from St. Lucia and speak in natural local English and Kwéyòl.","The island is shown only as beaches and hotels; no schools, homes or workplaces appear.","Carnival is shown as random people dancing, with no reference to its history or its meaning.","The writers consulted local historians about Emancipation Day.","All the heroes are tourists; locals only help them or get in their way.","Real locations such as Castries Market and the Pitons are drawn accurately.","A Kalinago sacred symbol is used as decoration on a hotel sign without permission."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"win":"Well spotted. Limited roles, the tourist gaze, shallow use of tradition, sidelined local heroes and misuse of sacred symbols all damage authenticity.","miss":"Look again. Which notes show local people or traditions in a limited, inaccurate or disrespectful way?","tutor":[{"h":"Roles","p":"<p>Are local people only ever serving others or providing comedy?</p>"},{"h":"Good practice","p":"<p>Local voices, consultation and accurate locations are strengths, not problems.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the notes that show a representation problem.","lines":["Every local character is a waiter or comic taxi driver.","The voice actors are local.","The island is shown only as beaches and hotels.","All the heroes are tourists."],"correct":[0,2,3],"win":"Yes. Those notes stereotype or sideline local people.","miss":"Which note is actually a good practice? Leave that one out."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the authenticity problems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Malik is reviewing an agency's Emancipation Day video for a regional bank. The problems here are subtler than a cast of waiters: look at framing, voice, sacred material and tone.</p>","q":"Select every note that shows a problem with authenticity or representation.","lines":["Enslaved ancestors are shown only as suffering victims, with no scenes of resistance or achievement.","The script was checked by Caribbean historians before filming.","Music from another community's sacred ceremony is used as background without permission.","The voice-over uses a generic \"island\" accent that no territory actually speaks.","Descendants from the community are interviewed and credited by name.","The video cuts straight from emancipation to the bank's logo and a loan offer.","Former plantation sites are filmed on location, with permission from the owners.","Local performers are paid the same rate as the overseas crew.","Every Caribbean character speaks broken, comic English while the narrator speaks formally."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,8],"win":"Well spotted. One-sided victim framing, unpermitted sacred material, a fake accent, trivialising history with a sales pitch and comic speech for local characters all damage authenticity. Expert checks, credited community voices, permission and fair pay build it.","miss":"Look again. Ask of each note: does it show people or traditions fully, accurately and with respect, and did the right people give permission and get credit?"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Supporting local creators: policy, funding, community and festivals","html":"<p>Indigenous digital products only survive if creators can earn a living. Support comes from four main areas.</p>\n<p><b>1. Policy</b> (governments and regional bodies)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Film commissions</b> attract and support productions: for example the Trinidad and Tobago Film Company (FilmTT) and Film Jamaica (part of JAMPRO).</li>\n<li>Possible policies: tax incentives for local productions, local content quotas on broadcasters, creative industry strategies, copyright laws that protect creators, and including digital media in school curricula (as CAPE does).</li>\n<li>In each territory a government ministry looks after culture and youth (in Sint Maarten, for example, the Ministry of Education, Culture, Youth and Sport, ECYS); check your own ministry's current programmes for grants and cultural projects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Funding</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Government grants, regional funds (the Caribbean Development Bank has run a Cultural and Creative Industries Innovation Fund), international cultural programmes, private sponsorship by banks and telecom companies, crowdfunding (Kickstarter, GoFundMe, Patreon) and revenue from platforms (YouTube ads, Steam sales, streaming licences).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Community</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Audiences who watch, share, pay for and talk about local work. Creator collectives, co-working hubs, mentorship, school and university programmes (such as courses at a national college or university), local media coverage (newspapers, radio and TV), and businesses such as telecom or electricity companies sponsoring local content.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>4. Festivals and showcases</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Festival</th><th>Key facts</th><th>How it supports creators</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Trinidad and Tobago Film Festival (TTFF)</b></td><td>Held annually since 2006, usually in September; Caribbean and diaspora films</td><td>Screenings, jury and audience prizes, workshops, industry and producers' programmes, free community screenings</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Animae Caribe</b></td><td>Trinidad and Tobago's animation and digital media festival, running for over two decades</td><td>Showcases Caribbean animation, awards, training, and an animation hub that develops productions and employs young artists</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CaribbeanTales</b></td><td>Founded in Toronto, Canada, by filmmaker Frances-Anne Solomon; its international film festival began in 2006</td><td>Screens Caribbean and diaspora films, runs an incubator that develops emerging filmmakers' projects, and a streaming and distribution arm</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Try visiting animation studios, film festivals and workshops, virtually or face to face. Many festivals stream panels and screenings online.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In \"discuss how local creators can be supported\" questions, organise your answer under <b>policy, funding, community and festivals</b>, and give one specific example under each. Then evaluate: which support matters most for a small island like Grenada or St. Kitts, and why?</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Local creators need support to keep making work. Governments can offer grants, tax breaks and film commissions. Funding can come from sponsors, crowdfunding and online sales. Communities help by watching, sharing and paying for local work. Festivals such as the Trinidad and Tobago Film Festival and Animae Caribe give creators screenings, prizes and contacts. A student filmmaker in St. Kitts might use all four.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>For a small island such as Grenada, the four kinds of support are not equally practical. Large tax incentives need a big production sector to work, so smaller, targeted grants and school or university programmes may achieve more. Community support costs little but matters a great deal: audiences who share and pay for local work give creators income and evidence of demand, which strengthens funding applications. Festivals offer credibility and networks that a single creator cannot build alone, and many now run online, which cuts travel costs. A Grade One answer evaluates: it chooses the support that fits the territory's size and budget, and explains why it would matter most.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"The best support for a new creator","q":"A 17-year-old animator from Holetown, Barbados, has finished a five-minute 2D short about a Barbadian folk tale. She has no budget, no distributor and no industry contacts. Which step would BEST give her exposure, feedback and industry connections in the region?","options":[{"t":"Re-dub it with American accents so it appeals to imported-content audiences.","fb":"This removes the authenticity that makes the work distinctive, and does not give her exposure or contacts."},{"t":"Keep the film private until it is as polished as a Hollywood feature.","fb":"Waiting for big-studio polish delays her career. Festivals value authentic stories and emerging talent."},{"t":"Submit the short to a regional festival such as Animae Caribe and share it online with a festival screening announcement.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Festivals offer screenings, awards, workshops and contact with professionals, and festival selection gives credibility when she shares the film online."},{"t":"Wait until she can afford to buy television airtime.","fb":"Buying airtime is expensive and gives no industry feedback or networking. It ignores low-cost routes that exist now."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Three needs","p":"<p>Exposure, feedback and connections: which option gives all three?</p>"},{"h":"Keep it real","p":"<p>Any option that removes authenticity weakens an indigenous product.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The best support (Foundation)","q":"A young local animator wants exposure and industry contacts. The best first step is to...","options":[{"t":"submit her film to a regional festival","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Festivals give screenings, feedback and networking."},{"t":"change the accents to American ones","fb":"This removes the authenticity."},{"t":"hide the film until it is perfect","fb":"This delays exposure and feedback."}]},
 "up": {"title":"The best support for a new creator (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A small island's culture ministry has a small budget and can fund only ONE new measure to grow local animation. Which measure is MOST likely to help the most creators in the long term, and why?","options":[{"t":"A large tax incentive for international studios, because big productions bring prestige.","fb":"Tax incentives work best where there is a large production sector. On a small island they may mainly benefit foreign studios."},{"t":"A small annual youth content grant linked to mentoring and a showcase screening, because it gives many emerging creators money, skills and an audience at low cost.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It reaches many creators, builds skills as well as work, and creates an audience, all within a small budget."},{"t":"A one-off prize for the best film ever made on the island.","fb":"A single prize rewards one creator once. It does not build skills or a pipeline of new work."},{"t":"A rule forcing broadcasters to show 50% local content immediately.","fb":"Quotas can help later, but with little local content yet, broadcasters could not fill the slots. Capacity must be built first."}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Pitching an indigenous product","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media wants to create an animated web series based on Grenadian folklore and history. Zara asks you to lead the planning.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A foreign investor offers money on condition that the series is set on a generic \"tropical island\" with American-accented characters, to reach a bigger market. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Explain that authenticity is the selling point, and look for funding that lets the team keep Grenadian voices and settings.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Authentic, specific stories stand out in a crowded global market and serve local audiences."},{"t":"Accept the money and the conditions; a bigger audience matters more.","to":"bad1","fb":"This turns an indigenous product into imported-style content and loses its purpose."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You need to write the scripts. How do you research the folk tales and history?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy a few stories from a foreign website and let the team guess the accents.","to":"bad2","fb":"Second-hand sources and guessed accents lead to errors and stereotypes."},{"t":"Interview elders and local historians, consult cultural organisations, and hire local writers and voice actors.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Community consultation and local talent build authenticity and trust."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The pilot episode is ready. How do you fund the full series and build an audience?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Apply for regional creative-industry funding, approach local sponsors, enter the pilot in Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales, and release it on YouTube with a community screening in St. George's.","to":"good","fb":"Strong plan. You combined policy and funding routes, festivals and community support."},{"t":"Sell all rights to the first buyer for a small fee.","to":"bad4","fb":"Giving away all rights means the team loses control and future income from its own culture."},{"t":"Post it once on social media and hope it goes viral.","to":"bad3","fb":"Going viral is not a strategy. Sustained support needs funding, festivals and community."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The series screens at regional festivals, local schools use it in class and a sponsor funds a second season. A Grade One answer would explain how authenticity, community consultation and a mix of funding, festivals and community support made the product sustainable.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost identity.</b> The series looks like every other generic cartoon, local audiences ignore it and it fails to stand out abroad. Try again and protect authenticity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Backlash.</b> Viewers spot wrong details and fake accents and accuse the series of stereotyping. Try again and consult the community.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Quiet launch.</b> A few hundred views and no funding for episode two. Try again with a support strategy.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>No control.</b> The buyer changes the characters and keeps the profits. Try again and protect the creators' rights.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Authenticity sells","p":"<p>Specific local stories are a strength, not a weakness.</p>"},{"h":"Go to the source","p":"<p>Who knows the folk tales best?</p>"},{"h":"Many supports","p":"<p>Combine funding, festivals and community rather than relying on luck.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Pitching an indigenous product (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you are planning a folklore web series for Grenada.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How should you research the folk tales?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Interview elders and hire local writers and voice actors.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. This builds authenticity."},{"t":"Copy stories from a foreign website.","to":"bad","fb":"This risks errors and stereotypes."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Community consultation keeps the series authentic.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Go to the people who know the culture. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Pitching an indigenous product (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher version of the folklore web series pitch. Every decision now involves a trade-off between money, reach, authenticity and rights. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A foreign streaming service offers full funding if the series is set on a generic \"tropical island\" with American accents. A second offer gives half the money but lets you keep Grenadian settings and voices. A regional fund could cover the rest, but it takes six months to decide. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Take the full funding with the generic setting.","to":"bad1","fb":"The money is bigger, but the series stops being indigenous."},{"t":"Wait six months for the regional fund before doing anything.","to":"ok1","fb":"Cautious, but the team loses momentum and both offers may disappear."},{"t":"Take the smaller deal that protects authenticity, and apply to the regional fund for the gap.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. You protect the product's selling point and combine funding sources."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A respected elder storyteller offers her versions of the folk tales, but asks how she will be credited and paid. What do you offer?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A written agreement: credit as cultural adviser on every episode, a fee per story and a say in how her stories are adapted.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Credit, payment and consent make the process authentic."},{"t":"A thank-you in the credits of the first episode only.","to":"ok2","fb":"Some credit, but it undervalues her knowledge and gives her no say."},{"t":"Record her stories without an agreement, because folk tales belong to everyone.","to":"bad2","fb":"Her versions and her performance are her contribution. Using them without consent is disrespectful and risky."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The writers want natural Grenadian English in the dialogue, but the streaming partner worries that viewers abroad will not understand it. What is the best compromise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep the local dialogue and add accurate subtitles in several languages, plus a short glossary on the website.","to":"n4","fb":"Good. Authenticity is kept and reach is widened."},{"t":"Rewrite all dialogue in neutral American English.","to":"bad3","fb":"This removes the voice that makes the series distinctive."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The pilot is ready. How do you build an audience and keep the series funded?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post it once on social media and hope it goes viral.","to":"ok3","fb":"Going viral is not a strategy. Sustained support needs festivals, community and funders."},{"t":"Enter Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales, hold a community screening in St. George's, release it on YouTube with lesson packs for schools, and use the response as evidence for sponsors and the regional fund.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. Festivals, community and evidence of demand reinforce each other."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>After the festivals, a buyer offers a quick payment for ALL rights to the series. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Sell all rights, because the money is needed now.","to":"bad4","fb":"Short-term cash, but the team loses control of the characters and the stories."},{"t":"Decline an all-rights sale and offer a time-limited licence, so the team keeps ownership and future income.","to":"good","fb":"Smart. The team keeps control of its own culture and its future earnings."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One plan.</b> The series keeps its voice, the storyteller is credited and paid, festivals and schools build an audience, sponsors fund season two and the team still owns its work. You balanced authenticity, reach, funding and rights at every step.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Stalled.</b> By the time the regional fund says yes, the team has scattered to other jobs. A partial success: act on the best available option while applying for more.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Strained trust.</b> The series is good, but the storyteller feels used and other elders decline to help with season two. A partial success: credit, pay and consult contributors properly.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Quiet launch.</b> A few hundred views and no evidence to show funders. A partial success: build an audience on purpose.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost identity.</b> The series looks like every other generic cartoon and does not stand out at home or abroad. Try again and protect authenticity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Backlash.</b> The storyteller's family objects publicly and the community turns against the series. Try again and work with contributors on fair terms.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Flat voice.</b> Local viewers say the characters sound fake, and the series loses its appeal. Try again and keep the local voice.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>No control.</b> The buyer changes the characters and keeps the profits. Try again and protect the creators' rights.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Critique an indigenous digital product","genre":"Structured response: critiquing the impact and motivations of an indigenous digital product","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Ministry responsible for culture in St. Vincent and the Grenadines is considering whether to fund more local animation. A committee member asks: \"Why should we invest in Caribbean-made digital content when young people already watch so much from abroad?\"</p>","prompt":"<p>Using ONE Caribbean case study you have screened (for example Cabbie Chronicles, Santana or another named product):</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explain</b> TWO motivations of its creators. (4 marks)</li><li><b>Discuss</b> its cultural and economic impact, including how it compares with imported content. (5 marks)</li><li><b>Recommend</b>, with justification, TWO ways the government and community of St. Vincent and the Grenadines could support local creators. (4 marks)</li></ol>","min":200,"max":400,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> [Product] was created by ... in ... One motivation was ... This is shown by ... A second motivation was ...<br><b>(b)</b> Culturally, the product ... For example, ... Compared with imported content, it ... However, ... Economically, ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, ... because ... Second, ... because ... On balance, ...</p>","checklist":["I named a real case study with its creator and country.","I explained two different motivations with evidence from the product.","I used at least one screening technique (e.g. dialogue/speech, camera angles, target audience) as evidence.","I discussed cultural and economic impact, and compared with imported content.","I avoided inventing statistics.","I recommended two support methods (policy, funding, community or festivals) and justified each.","I referred to St. Vincent and the Grenadines in my recommendations."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Motivations explained with evidence","marks":4,"look":["motiv|reason|aim|purpose","identit|culture|voice|own stor","preserv|educat|profit|income|career|expos|entertain","cabbie|santana|jamaica|trinidad|creator|animator","camera|angle|dialogue|speech|sound effect|background|target audience|indigenous"],"min":2,"strong":"Your motivations are clearly explained and linked to evidence from the case study.","weak":"Name the case study and creator, then explain two different motivations (e.g. cultural identity and career exposure), each supported by something you observed using a screening technique (dialogue/speech, details and background, camera angles, sound effects)."},{"criterion":"Discussion of impact and comparison","marks":5,"look":["impact|effect|result","pride|represent|authentic|see themselves|identity","job|employ|econom|income|industr","import|foreign|hollywood|american|abroad","however|whereas|on the other hand|although"],"min":3,"strong":"You discussed both cultural and economic impact and compared fairly with imported content.","weak":"Cover cultural impact (identity, representation) and economic impact (jobs, money staying local), then compare with imported content using \"whereas\" or \"however\"."},{"criterion":"Justified recommendations for support","marks":4,"look":["fund|grant|sponsor|crowdfund","polic|tax|quota|law|ministry|government","festival|animae|caribbeantales|ttff|showcase|screening","communit|school|college|audience|share|workshop","because|so that|this would"],"min":3,"strong":"Your recommendations are specific to St. Vincent and the Grenadines and well justified.","weak":"Give two specific supports (e.g. a youth content grant and a local screening festival) and explain, with \"because\", how each helps creators in St. Vincent and the Grenadines."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Cabbie Chronicles is a short 2D animated comedy made by Jamaican animators Allison and Anieph Latchman. One motivation was cultural identity: the creators wanted Jamaicans to see their own streets, humour and Patois on screen, which is clear from the Kingston taxi setting and the natural local dialogue. A second motivation was career exposure: by making a polished short and entering it at the Animae Caribe festival, where it won an award in 2010, they showed studios and funders that world-standard animation could be made in the Caribbean, building a case for a longer series.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Culturally, the short gave Caribbean audiences a sense of pride and of \"seeing themselves\", whereas most cartoons they watch are imported from the United States or Japan and reflect those cultures. Its satire of everyday life is specific and authentic, which helps it stand out online. However, compared with imported content, its budget, length and marketing are limited, so its audience is smaller and largely regional and diaspora. Economically, products like this keep creative skills and income in the region and inspire young animators, although a single short cannot build an industry on its own; it needs ongoing funding.</p><p><b>(c)</b> First, the government, through the Ministry responsible for culture, should create a small youth digital content grant, because young creators in St. Vincent and the Grenadines often have the skills but not the money for software, equipment and voice actors. Second, the community should support an annual local screening night in Kingstown linked to regional festivals such as Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales, because it would give creators an audience, feedback and contacts with industry professionals. On balance, investing in local content is worthwhile because it protects Vincentian identity in a market dominated by imported media.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Label each part (a), (b) and (c) and match the marks: two clear motivations, a balanced discussion of impact with a comparison, and two recommendations each with \"because\". Never invent viewing figures or prize names; accurate, cautious claims score higher than impressive-sounding guesses.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Critique an indigenous digital product (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: an indigenous digital product, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Use ONE Caribbean case study you have screened (for example Cabbie Chronicles or Santana). Answer each part in one or two sentences.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> the product, its creator and its country. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE motivation of the creator and ONE thing in the product that shows it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE way the product shows Caribbean culture (for example its language or setting). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE difference between this product and imported content. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Suggest</b> ONE way St. Vincent and the Grenadines could support local creators, and say why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The product is ... It was made by ... in ...<br><b>(b)</b> One motivation was ... This is shown by ...<br><b>(c)</b> The product shows Caribbean culture through its ... For example, ...<br><b>(d)</b> Unlike imported content, this product ...<br><b>(e)</b> St. Vincent and the Grenadines could ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I named a real product, its creator and its country.","I gave one motivation and evidence for it.","I gave one way the product shows Caribbean culture.","I gave one difference from imported content.","I suggested one way to support creators and said why."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Product, creator and country","marks":2,"look":["cabbie|santana|harder they come|duppy|tashia|[A-Z][a-z]+ (chronicles|detective)","jamaica|trinidad|tobago|barbados|caribbean|latchman|alexis|henry"],"min":2,"strong":"You identified the product and where it comes from.","weak":"Name the product and say who made it and in which country, for example Cabbie Chronicles by Allison and Anieph Latchman, Jamaica."},{"criterion":"Motivation with evidence","marks":2,"look":["motiv|wanted|aim|reason|to (show|tell|entertain|earn|teach)","shown by|because|for example|such as"],"min":2,"strong":"A motivation supported by evidence from the product.","weak":"Say why the creators made it (for example to tell Jamaican stories in a Jamaican voice) and point to something in the product that shows it."},{"criterion":"Caribbean culture","marks":2,"look":["language|dialogue|patois|creole|accent|setting|street|place|humour|music|character","for example|such as|shows|uses"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear cultural feature with an example.","weak":"Choose one of the six places culture shows up, such as language, and give an example from the product."},{"criterion":"Comparison with imported content","marks":2,"look":["import|foreign|hollywood|american|abroad|us ","unlike|whereas|but|while|budget|local|own"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear difference from imported content.","weak":"Use \"unlike\" or \"whereas\": for example, it reflects local language and places, whereas most imported cartoons reflect American culture."},{"criterion":"Support suggestion","marks":2,"look":["grant|fund|festival|screening|sponsor|school|workshop|share|policy","because|so that|so "],"min":2,"strong":"A sensible suggestion with a reason.","weak":"Suggest one support, such as a youth content grant or a local screening night, and give a reason with \"because\"."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The product is Cabbie Chronicles. It was made by Allison and Anieph Latchman in Jamaica.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One motivation was to tell Jamaican stories in a Jamaican voice. This is shown by the Kingston street setting and the Patois dialogue.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The product shows Caribbean culture through its language. For example, the characters speak Jamaican Patois, and the jokes come from everyday life in Kingston.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Unlike most imported cartoons, which show American life, this product shows Jamaican people, places and humour.</p><p><b>(e)</b> St. Vincent and the Grenadines could hold a yearly screening night for local animators, because it would give them an audience and feedback.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the point and 1 for supporting detail. Accept any real Caribbean product the student has actually screened, but do not accept guessed facts or figures. Students who complete this confidently should move to the Core task, which asks for two motivations and a balanced discussion of impact.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Critique an indigenous digital product (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended critique: impact and motivations of an indigenous digital product, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Ministry responsible for culture in St. Vincent and the Grenadines is considering whether to fund more local animation, but it has money for only ONE programme this year. A committee member asks: \"Why should we invest in Caribbean-made digital content when young people already watch so much from abroad? And if we do invest, what is the single best thing to fund?\"</p>","prompt":"<p>Using ONE Caribbean case study you have screened, write a critique for the committee that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>critiques</b> the product using at least THREE screening techniques, with evidence from your screening notes, and judges how well it achieves its own purpose for its own audience <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>analyses</b> TWO motivations of its creators and <b>evaluates</b> how far each was achieved <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> its cultural and economic impact, compares it with imported content and discusses ONE authenticity or representation issue <i>(7 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>recommends</b> ONE support programme as the priority for St. Vincent and the Grenadines, weighing it against ONE alternative <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li>ends with a justified answer to the committee member's first question <i>(2 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":400,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> [Product] by ... (country, year) aims to ... for ... Details and background: ... Dialogue/speech: ... Target audience/market: ... Purpose: ... Overall, it succeeds / is limited because ...<br><b>(b)</b> The first motivation was ... This was achieved to a large extent because ... The second motivation was ... However, ...<br><b>(c)</b> Culturally, ... Economically, ... Compared with imported content, ... One representation issue is ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend ... rather than ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> The ministry should invest because ...</p>","checklist":["I used at least three screening techniques, each with evidence, and judged the product against its own purpose and audience.","I analysed two motivations and judged how far each was achieved.","I evaluated cultural and economic impact, compared with imported content and discussed an authenticity or representation issue.","I recommended one support programme and weighed it against an alternative.","I answered the committee member's question with a justified conclusion.","Every fact I used can be checked; I described impact cautiously where I had no figures."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Critique using screening techniques","marks":6,"look":["background|setting|detail","dialogue|speech|patois|creole|accent","sound|camera|angle|close.?up","audience|market","purpose|aim","succeed|effective|limited|works|achieve"],"min":4,"strong":"At least three techniques used as evidence for a clear judgement against the product's purpose.","weak":"Use three or more screening techniques as headings, give evidence under each and judge whether the product achieves its purpose for its audience."},{"criterion":"Motivations analysed and evaluated","marks":5,"look":["motiv","identit|voice|own stor|culture","career|expos|portfolio|fund|profit|income|entertain|preserv|educat","achiev|succe|extent|however|partly|limited"],"min":3,"strong":"Two motivations analysed, with a judgement of how far each was achieved.","weak":"Explain two different motivations, then judge each: was it fully, partly or not achieved, and what is the evidence?"},{"criterion":"Impact, comparison and representation","marks":7,"look":["cultur|pride|identit|see themselves","econom|job|income|industr|skill","import|foreign|hollywood|american|abroad","whereas|however|on the other hand|although","stereotyp|represent|authentic|exaggerat","diaspora|regional|audience"],"min":4,"strong":"Cultural and economic impact evaluated, a fair comparison made and a representation issue discussed from both sides.","weak":"Cover cultural and economic impact, compare with imported content using \"whereas\" or \"however\", and discuss one representation issue from both sides."},{"criterion":"Prioritised recommendation","marks":5,"look":["recommend|priority|best","grant|fund|festival|screening|mentor|school|college|workshop|sponsor","rather than|instead of|compared with|alternative","because|so that|this would","vincent|svg|kingstown|small island|budget"],"min":4,"strong":"One programme chosen, weighed against an alternative and justified for St. Vincent and the Grenadines.","weak":"Pick one programme, compare it with one alternative, and explain why yours suits the size and budget of St. Vincent and the Grenadines better."},{"criterion":"Justified conclusion","marks":2,"look":["invest|fund|support","because|therefore|on balance|overall"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear, justified answer to the committee.","weak":"End with one or two sentences that answer the committee member directly and give the main reason."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Cabbie Chronicles: Drive Thru Drama is a short 2D animated comedy by Jamaican animators Allison and Anieph Latchman, which won the Best Caribbean Animation Award at the Animae Caribe festival in 2010. Its purpose is to entertain while showing that Caribbean stories can be told in world-standard animation. <i>Details and background:</i> the Kingston street setting and the taxi at its centre place the story firmly in Jamaica, so local viewers recognise their own city. <i>Dialogue/speech:</i> the natural Patois dialogue carries the humour; for its target audience this is a strength, although viewers outside the region may miss some jokes. <i>Target audience/market:</i> it is aimed mainly at Jamaicans, the wider Caribbean and the diaspora, who share the references. <i>Purpose:</i> it entertains through the familiar comedy of everyday encounters. Judged against its own purpose and audience, it succeeds: its short length suits online viewing and its humour is specific rather than generic, although its brevity limits how far the characters can develop.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The first motivation was <b>cultural identity</b>: to tell Jamaican stories in a Jamaican voice. This was achieved to a large extent, because the setting, language and humour are local throughout and nothing has been softened for a foreign market. The second motivation was <b>exposure and career building</b>: to show that the Caribbean can produce professional animation and to attract support for longer work. The festival award shows this was partly achieved, since it brought recognition and credibility; however, one award and one short cannot on their own guarantee the funding a longer series needs, so this motivation depended on support beyond the creators' control.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Culturally, the short gives Caribbean audiences the experience of \"seeing themselves\" on screen, which builds pride, whereas most cartoons young people watch are imported from the United States or Japan and carry those cultures. It also proves to young animators that local work can be recognised. Economically, products like this keep creative skills and some income in the region, but compared with imported content their budgets, marketing and reach are small, so their economic effect is modest unless many such products are made. One representation issue is that the comedy relies on a familiar character type, the Kingston cabbie. For local viewers this is affectionate recognition from inside the culture; for a global audience that sees few other Jamaican characters, the same type could harden into a stereotype. The best answer is not to remove the humour but to have many more Caribbean stories, so no single type stands for everyone.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I recommend a <b>youth animation grant with mentoring and an annual showcase screening in Kingstown</b>, rather than a tax incentive for visiting studios. A tax incentive suits territories with a large production sector and may mainly benefit foreign companies, whereas a grant reaches many local creators at low cost, builds skills through mentoring and gives them an audience and evidence of demand to take to Animae Caribe or CaribbeanTales. On a small island with a limited budget, this is the measure most likely to create a steady pipeline of new work.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> The ministry should invest, because Caribbean-made content does something imported media cannot: it lets young people see and hear their own culture, and it builds local skills and jobs. Imported content will always be available; local content will only exist if it is supported.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 5 + 7 + 5 + 2). The model uses only checkable facts from the course notes; students should add their own timed screening notes (for example \"01:20, close-up...\") as evidence in (a). Reward evaluation over description: judgements of how far motivations were achieved, two-sided discussion of representation and a recommendation weighed against an alternative. Penalise guessed statistics.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U4","title":"Digital media platforms and formats","objectives":["Explain what a digital platform is, distinguish a platform from a format, and classify platforms by how we access content (apps, websites, dynamic platforms, eLearning environments, curated content sources and social media).","Differentiate among search engine, social, knowledge, application store, marketplace, media, crowdsourcing and infrastructure platforms, giving Caribbean examples.","Select appropriate formats for a given purpose and audience, and distinguish between MP4, MP3, PDF, PNG, JPEG and GIF.","Explain how formats work in terms of compression, resolution, colour depth and file size trade-offs.","Import, export, share and embed data for different uses.","Discuss cross-platform compatibility and apply it when publishing to different devices.","Carry out format conversions accurately under Paper 02 conditions."],"videos":[{"title":"Types of digital platforms","q":"types of digital platforms explained social marketplace app store infrastructure"},{"title":"JPEG vs PNG vs GIF","q":"jpeg vs png vs gif explained when to use each format"},{"title":"Colour depth and resolution","q":"image resolution and colour depth bit depth explained for beginners"},{"title":"How to embed a YouTube video","q":"how to embed a youtube video on a website iframe beginners"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"A platform is the medium that carries digital content","html":"<p>Start with one clear definition. Learn it word for word.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">A <b>platform</b> is the medium that carries digital content. It is the place through which digitally created media reaches its intended audience.</p>\n<p>The idea breaks down into three parts.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Where it lives.</b> A platform can be an <b>app</b>, a <b>website</b>, an <b>eLearning environment</b>, or a <b>curated content source</b>. A national newspaper's news app, the website of a guesthouse in Bequia, a school's Google Classroom and a Pinterest board of Carnival costume ideas are all platforms.</li>\n<li><b>Where it arrives.</b> One piece of content can reach <b>phones, tablets and computers</b>, with its presentation adapted for each screen. A Crop Over promo is watched on a phone on Broad Street in Bridgetown, on a tablet in a Holetown hotel and on a laptop in Kingston, and the platform lays it out to fit each one.</li>\n<li><b>Platform ≠ format.</b> The <b>platform</b> carries the message. The <b>format</b> is the form the message takes. A video is a format; YouTube is a platform that carries it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Content</th><th>Format (the form it takes)</th><th>Platform (what carries it)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Island Pulse Media's Carnival teaser</td><td>MP4 video</td><td>YouTube, Instagram</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A jingle for a Georgetown juice shop</td><td>MP3 audio</td><td>Spotify, a radio station's website, WhatsApp</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A hurricane preparedness checklist from a Dominica school</td><td>PDF document</td><td>The school's website, Google Classroom</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A Frederick Street boutique's logo</td><td>PNG image</td><td>Facebook page, online shop</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The same format can travel on many platforms, and one platform can carry many formats. Choosing well means choosing <b>both</b>: the right platform to reach the audience and the right format for that platform. The rest of this unit covers the platform types first, then the formats.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A common Paper 01 trap names \"MP4\" or \"JPEG\" as a platform. Ask yourself: is this the <b>container of the message</b> (format) or the <b>place that delivers it</b> (platform)? If you can upload something to it, it is a platform.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A platform is the medium that carries digital content to its audience, such as YouTube, Instagram or a website. A format is the form the content takes, such as an MP4 video or a PNG image. YouTube is the platform; the MP4 is the format. A Jamaican podcast is an MP3 file (the format) that reaches listeners through Spotify (the platform).</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The two decisions are linked but separate. Choosing the platform answers \"where is my audience?\"; choosing the format answers \"what will that platform and those devices accept?\". One format can travel on many platforms and one platform carries many formats, so a strong answer always gives both and explains the fit. Platforms also <b>adapt</b> content: YouTube re-encodes an uploaded MP4 into several resolutions so it plays smoothly on a phone in Roseau or a laptop in London. That is why the file you upload is not always the file people see. A quick exam test: if you can upload something to it, it is a platform; if you can save something as it, it is a format.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Platform or format?","intro":"<p>The platform carries the message. The format is the form the message takes. Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is listing everything in a client's campaign. Sort each item.</p>","main":"Is this a platform or a format?","bins":["Platform","Format"],"items":[{"t":"Google Classroom","bin":0,"fb":"Google Classroom is an eLearning environment: a platform that carries lessons, files and assignments to students."},{"t":"An MP4 promo video of Eagle Beach in Aruba","bin":1,"fb":"MP4 is the form the video takes. It needs a platform such as YouTube or Instagram to reach viewers."},{"t":"Instagram","bin":0,"fb":"Instagram is a social platform that carries photos and videos to followers."},{"t":"A PDF menu for a fish fry stall in Oistins","bin":1,"fb":"PDF is a document format. It could be shared on a website, WhatsApp or Facebook."},{"t":"YouTube","bin":0,"fb":"YouTube carries video to its audience. It is a platform; the video file uploaded to it has a format."},{"t":"A PNG logo with a transparent background","bin":1,"fb":"PNG is an image format. The logo could appear on many platforms."},{"t":"An MP3 radio jingle","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 is an audio format. Spotify or a station's website would be the platform."},{"t":"The Apple App Store","bin":0,"fb":"An app store is a platform that carries software to users."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can now separate the platform (what carries the message) from the format (the form the message takes).","tutor":[{"h":"Carry or form?","p":"<p>Does it deliver content to people, or is it the shape the content is saved in?</p>"},{"h":"File extension clue","p":"<p>If it sounds like a file ending (.mp4, .png, .pdf), it is probably a format.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Platform or format? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"YouTube","bin":0,"fb":"YouTube carries videos to viewers, so it is a platform."},{"t":"An MP4 video","bin":1,"fb":"MP4 is the form the video takes, so it is a format."},{"t":"Instagram","bin":0,"fb":"Instagram carries posts to followers, so it is a platform."},{"t":"A PDF menu","bin":1,"fb":"PDF is a document format."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Platform or format? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin. A <b>codec</b> is the compression method used <i>inside</i> a file, such as H.264 inside an MP4 container. Sort each item.</p>","main":"Is this a platform, a format or a codec?","bins":["Platform","Format","Codec (compression method inside a file)"],"items":[{"t":"An SVG logo","bin":1,"fb":"SVG is a vector image format."},{"t":"Spotify","bin":0,"fb":"Spotify carries audio to listeners: a platform."},{"t":"Airbnb","bin":0,"fb":"A marketplace that carries listings to guests: a platform."},{"t":"H.264","bin":2,"fb":"H.264 is the video compression method usually found inside an MP4 container."},{"t":"Google Play","bin":0,"fb":"An app store that carries software to users: a platform."},{"t":"A WAV recording of a jingle","bin":1,"fb":"WAV is an uncompressed audio format."},{"t":"Amazon Web Services","bin":0,"fb":"An infrastructure platform that supplies servers and storage."},{"t":"A HEIC photo taken on an iPhone","bin":1,"fb":"HEIC is an image file format."},{"t":"AAC audio inside an MP4","bin":2,"fb":"AAC is the audio codec inside the MP4 container."},{"t":"A CSV export of customer orders","bin":1,"fb":"CSV is a data format for moving tables between systems."},{"t":"H.265 (HEVC)","bin":2,"fb":"H.265 is a newer, more efficient video codec, but older devices may not play it."}],"win":"Excellent. A platform carries content, a format is the file's form, and a codec is the compression method inside the file. Knowing all three explains why one MP4 plays everywhere and another does not."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Where content lives: eight platform types","html":"<p>There are eight platform types you must be able to differentiate. Each one has a heading that sums up its purpose. Learn the headings: they are a quick way to remember the definitions.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Platform type and heading</th><th>Definition from the notes</th><th>Examples</th><th>Caribbean use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Search engine</b><br><i>Search engines help us find information</i></td><td>Uses software to search text or databases for specified information</td><td>Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Startpage, Yandex</td><td>A cruise visitor searches \"fish fry Oistins\" before coming ashore in Bridgetown</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social platform</b><br><i>Social platforms run on what users share</i></td><td>Third-party sites or apps built for social interaction and content sharing; most content comes from the community</td><td>Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, X (formerly Twitter)</td><td>A national newspaper shares breaking news on its Facebook page and readers comment</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Knowledge platform</b><br><i>Knowledge platforms keep information current and findable</i></td><td>A knowledge base brings information together; search and participation help people use and improve it</td><td>Wikipedia, Khan Academy, Stack Overflow</td><td>A CAPE student in Grenada checks Khan Academy for a maths topic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Application store</b><br><i>App stores put software one download away</i></td><td>Online portals for procuring and downloading software</td><td>Google Play, Apple App Store, Microsoft Store</td><td>A developer in Trinidad publishes a soca radio app</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Marketplace</b><br><i>Marketplaces connect sellers with buyers</i></td><td>Brings offers from sellers into one place so buyers can browse, compare and respond</td><td>Amazon, eBay, Craigslist, Airbnb, Fiverr</td><td>A Grace Bay villa listed on Airbnb; a Jamaican illustrator selling on Fiverr</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Media platform</b><br><i>Media platforms are built for sharing images and video</i></td><td>Puts visual content at the centre; grows through user-generated content</td><td>YouTube, Vimeo, Pinterest</td><td>Carnival road march videos shared on YouTube</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Crowdsourcing platform</b><br><i>Crowdsourcing and infrastructure platforms work behind the scenes</i></td><td>Communities respond to challenges with ideas and business propositions; the platform gathers contributions from many people</td><td>Waze, Wikipedia's volunteer editors; crowdfunding sites such as GoFundMe and Kickstarter gather money</td><td>Drivers reporting traffic on the Churchill-Roosevelt Highway; rebuilding fundraisers after Hurricane Beryl</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Infrastructure platform</b><br><i>(same heading)</i></td><td>Servers, storage and networking are allocated programmatically to support digital services</td><td>Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud</td><td>A Paramaribo online shop hosted in the cloud</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Some services sit across categories. Wikipedia is a <b>knowledge</b> platform built by <b>crowdsourcing</b>. YouTube is a <b>media</b> platform with a strong <b>social</b> layer. In the exam, classify by the <b>main purpose</b> described in the question, then mention the overlap if it helps your answer.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Differentiate\" means you must show how two types differ on the same feature. \"A marketplace connects sellers with buyers, whereas a media platform puts images and video at the centre for audiences to watch and share.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are eight platform types. Search engines help us find information. Social platforms run on what users share. Knowledge platforms keep information current. App stores offer software to download. Marketplaces connect sellers and buyers. Media platforms share images and video. Crowdsourcing platforms gather ideas or money from many people, and infrastructure platforms supply servers and storage. A Barbados villa listed on Airbnb is using a marketplace.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Many real services sit across several types, so classify by the <b>main purpose</b> in the scenario, then mention the overlap. YouTube is a media platform with a strong social layer; Wikipedia is a knowledge platform built by crowdsourcing; Amazon is a marketplace that also sells infrastructure through Amazon Web Services. One business might use four types at once: a Grenadian chocolate maker could appear in search results, post on Instagram, sell through a marketplace and host its shop on cloud infrastructure. To \"differentiate\", compare two types on the same feature, such as who supplies the content or how money changes hands. Never name a format (MP4) or a device (a phone) as a platform.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the platform type","intro":"<p>Keisha is mapping every platform the Island Pulse Media agency uses for its clients. Match each platform type to the example that best shows its main purpose.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Search engine","r":"A visitor types \"best beach bar Negril\" and gets a ranked list of links"},{"l":"Social platform","r":"A Kingston boutique posts outfit photos and replies to comments from followers"},{"l":"Knowledge platform","r":"Volunteers write and correct an encyclopedia article about Port Royal"},{"l":"Application store","r":"Jerome uploads a new version of a ferry timetable app for users to download"},{"l":"Marketplace","r":"A guesthouse in Treasure Beach lists rooms and pays a fee on each booking"},{"l":"Media platform","r":"A Barbadian band streams its new album to listeners around the world"},{"l":"Crowdsourcing platform","r":"Drivers across Jamaica report a blocked road so others can avoid it"},{"l":"Infrastructure platform","r":"Maya has cloud servers and storage allocated automatically to host a client's website"}],"win":"Well matched. You classified each platform by its main purpose, which is exactly how Paper 01 tests platform types.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask: what is it mainly for?","p":"<p>Finding pages, socialising, sharing knowledge, getting apps, buying and selling, streaming, gathering from the crowd, or running other services?</p>"},{"h":"Behind the scenes","p":"<p>If users never see it directly but other products run on it (servers, payments), it is infrastructure.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the platform type (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Search engine","r":"Typing a question and getting ranked links"},{"l":"Application store","r":"Downloading a ferry timetable app"},{"l":"Marketplace","r":"Booking a guesthouse room and paying a fee"},{"l":"Crowdsourcing platform","r":"Many people contributing ideas to solve a challenge"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the platform type (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: these examples could tempt you towards the wrong type. Match each platform type to the example whose MAIN purpose fits it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Search engine","r":"A diver in Bonaire types a question and sees a ranked list of pages from many different sites"},{"l":"Social platform","r":"A St. Kitts youth group's post spreads because members comment on it and repost it"},{"l":"Knowledge platform","r":"A coding forum where answers are voted up so the best fix appears first"},{"l":"Application store","r":"A browser catalogue where a teacher adds a screen-reader extension"},{"l":"Marketplace","r":"A Jamaican illustrator is hired for a logo through a site that takes a fee from each job"},{"l":"Media platform","r":"Viewers watch a Spicemas highlights reel and subscribe to the uploader's channel"},{"l":"Crowdsourcing platform","r":"After a storm in Dominica, thousands of donors each give a small amount to rebuild a school"},{"l":"Infrastructure platform","r":"A ticket site automatically gets extra servers when Carnival tickets go on sale"}],"win":"Excellent. You classified each service by its main purpose, even where it overlaps with other types."}},{"type":"learn","title":"How each platform type works","html":"<p>Here is more detail on each platform type. These are the points that turn a one-mark definition into a full explanation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Search engines.</b> Type a question or keywords, then use the results to find a useful source. Remember: <b>a search result points you toward information; check the source before you rely on it.</b> A result about Hurricane Irma recovery from a government site is more reliable than an anonymous blog.</li>\n<li><b>Social platforms: post, respond, share.</b> A photo, update or short video becomes part of a conversation when other users comment, react or pass it along. <b>The platform provides the space; its users keep the conversation moving.</b> When a Belize City bakery posts its September Celebrations johnny cake special, followers' comments and shares spread it further than the bakery could alone.</li>\n<li><b>Knowledge platforms.</b> A knowledge base brings information together. <b>Search</b> helps people find what they need, and <b>participation</b> (editing, answering, rating) helps keep it current.</li>\n<li><b>App stores: platform-run and third-party.</b> Some stores are run by the company behind the device or operating system (Apple's App Store, Google Play, the Microsoft Store). Others are <b>third-party</b> stores run by another company. App stores also go <b>beyond mobile devices</b>: browsers such as Chrome and Firefox offer stores or catalogues for extensions and add-ons.</li>\n<li><b>Marketplaces: transaction-based or classified listings.</b> In a <b>transaction-based</b> marketplace (Amazon, eBay) the owner manages the transaction through the site. <b>Classified-listing</b> platforms such as Craigslist often leave payment arrangements to buyers and sellers. Ask: what is being offered, who is selling it, and how does the exchange take place?</li>\n<li><b>Media platforms: a social layer surrounds the media.</b> Users upload their own work, so the platform grows through <b>user-generated content</b>. Profiles and comments connect each image or video to its creator and audience: a GIMP tutorial on YouTube is both something to watch and a place to ask questions.</li>\n<li><b>Crowdsourcing and infrastructure: behind the scenes.</b> One <b>coordinates contributions</b> from many people; the other <b>supplies computing resources</b> (servers, storage, networking) on demand. Both help digital projects operate beyond a single person or device.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Keeping examples up to date.</b> The app store examples span different periods of app-store history. Some have since closed or changed. BlackBerry's app store (BlackBerry World) closed at the end of 2019, and Cydia, a third-party store for modified iPhones, stopped selling apps in 2018. Amazon announced in 2025 that its Appstore would leave Android devices, while staying on its own Fire tablets. Twitter is now called <b>X</b>, and Creative Commons' CC Search became <b>Openverse</b>. In the exam, current examples such as Google Play and the Apple App Store are the safest choices.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To \"explain\" a platform type, give the definition <b>plus</b> how it works <b>plus</b> an example. \"A classified-listing marketplace such as Craigslist brings sellers' offers together, but leaves payment to the buyer and seller.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Each platform type works in its own way. Search engines point you to sources you should still check. On social platforms, users keep the conversation going by posting, replying and sharing. Some marketplaces handle payment; classified sites leave it to the buyer and seller. Media platforms grow from what users upload. A seller in Trinidad using a classified site arranges payment directly with the buyer.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Explaining <b>how</b> a platform works earns more than naming it, and the mechanism usually involves who controls what. Search engines rank results with algorithms, so a page can rank highly without being reliable; check the publisher, and notice results marked \"Sponsored\". Social and media platforms depend on user-generated content, which makes them cheap to grow but hard to moderate. A transaction-based marketplace protects buyers by managing payment, for a fee; a classified-listing site is free but riskier. Platforms also change: stores close, services are renamed and policies shift. That is why examiners prefer current, widely used examples, and why a strong answer names one the scenario's users would really use.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"How platforms work: quick-fire","intro":"<p>Use the details from the notes to answer each question about platforms used across the Caribbean.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Bahamian student finds a website claiming the island has no hurricane season. What is the best advice about search engines?","options":["Trust it if it is the first result","Check the source before you rely on it","Search engines only return verified facts"],"answer":1,"fb":"A search result only points you toward information. The top result is not automatically reliable, so check who published it."},{"text":"On a social platform, who mainly keeps the conversation moving?","options":["The users who comment, react and share","The platform's own staff","The search engine"],"answer":0,"fb":"The platform provides the space; its users keep the conversation moving through post, respond, share."},{"text":"A seller in Antigua lists a used laptop on a classified-listing site. How is payment usually arranged?","options":["The site always processes the payment","Payment is made through an app store","Buyer and seller usually arrange payment between themselves"],"answer":2,"fb":"Classified-listing platforms such as Craigslist often leave payment to buyers and sellers. Transaction-based marketplaces manage it through the site."},{"text":"Which is an example of an app store beyond mobile devices?","options":["A search engine results page","A browser's catalogue of extensions and add-ons","A cloud storage server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Browsers such as Chrome and Firefox offer stores or catalogues for extensions and add-ons."},{"text":"Malik posts a Blender tutorial on YouTube and viewers ask questions underneath. Which idea from the notes does this show?","options":["A social layer surrounds the media","Infrastructure allocation","Classified listings"],"answer":0,"fb":"Profiles and comments connect the video to its creator and audience, so a tutorial is both something to watch and a place to ask questions."},{"text":"Which pair work \"behind the scenes\"?","options":["Crowdsourcing and infrastructure platforms","Social and media platforms","Search engines and marketplaces"],"answer":0,"fb":"Crowdsourcing coordinates contributions from many people; infrastructure supplies servers, storage and networking."}],"win":"Well done. You can explain how each platform type works, not just name it.","tutor":[{"h":"Who does the work?","p":"<p>Is it the platform, the users, or computing resources in the background?</p>"},{"h":"Follow the money","p":"<p>For marketplaces, ask who handles the exchange.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"How platforms work (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Before relying on a search result you should...","options":["check the source","share it at once"],"answer":0,"fb":"A search result only points you toward information."},{"text":"On social platforms, most content comes from...","options":["the platform owner","the community of users"],"answer":1,"fb":"Social platforms run on what users share."},{"text":"Infrastructure platforms supply...","options":["servers, storage and networking","product reviews"],"answer":0,"fb":"They supply computing resources to support digital services."}]},
 "up": {"title":"How platforms work: quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each question asks you to explain a mechanism or choose a platform for a need.</p>","items":[{"text":"A cruise visitor searching \"best roti in Port of Spain\" sees a top result labelled \"Sponsored\". What should she understand?","options":["It is the most reliable result","The search engine's staff wrote it","It is a paid advert, so she should compare it with other results and reviews","Search engines only show verified businesses"],"answer":2,"fb":"Sponsored results are paid placements. Position does not prove reliability."},{"text":"A bakery's post reaches thousands of people although the bakery has only 300 followers. What MOST likely caused this?","options":["An infrastructure platform added servers","A search engine indexed it","Followers commented on and shared it, spreading it into their own networks","The platform's staff shared it"],"answer":2,"fb":"On social platforms, users' responses and shares carry content far beyond the original audience."},{"text":"Which marketplace gives a buyer in Antigua the MOST protection if a seller never delivers?","options":["A classified-listing site where payment is arranged privately","A social media post with a phone number","A search engine result","A transaction-based marketplace that manages payment through the site"],"answer":3,"fb":"When the site manages the transaction, it can hold or refund the payment."},{"text":"Amazon announced in 2025 that its Appstore would leave Android devices. What does this show for exam answers?","options":["Platforms change, so use current, widely used examples such as Google Play and the Apple App Store","Never mention app stores","Amazon no longer sells anything","All third-party stores have closed"],"answer":0,"fb":"Current, widely used examples are the safest choice."},{"text":"Why can a media platform grow quickly at low cost to its owner?","options":["Users upload their own content","It rents out servers","It is edited by volunteers like an encyclopedia","Its staff film all the videos"],"answer":0,"fb":"User-generated content means the audience also supplies the content."},{"text":"A Carnival ticket site needs extra computing power only during two weeks of ticket sales. Which platform type BEST meets this need?","options":["A search engine","A curated content source","A classified-listing site","An infrastructure platform that allocates resources on demand"],"answer":3,"fb":"Infrastructure platforms allocate servers and storage programmatically, so capacity can grow and shrink with demand."}],"win":"Excellent. You explained how platforms work: paid ranking, sharing networks, payment protection, user-generated content and on-demand infrastructure."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Six types of digital platform by access","html":"<p>The eight types you have learnt group platforms by their <b>purpose</b>. A second way to group them is by <b>how we access and use content</b>. There are <b>six types of digital platform</b>, and <b>a platform can fit more than one</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example from the notes</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Apps</b></td><td>Software installed on a phone, tablet or computer that delivers content</td><td>Google Earth</td><td>A ferry timetable app for crossings from St. Kitts to Nevis</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Websites</b></td><td>Pages viewed in a browser at a web address</td><td>CAPE Digital Media Resource Kit</td><td>The website of a guesthouse in Speightstown</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dynamic platforms</b></td><td>Platforms whose content changes constantly as users upload and interact</td><td>YouTube</td><td>A Trinidad and Tobago soca artiste's channel with new uploads each Carnival season</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>eLearning environments</b></td><td>Platforms built for teaching and learning, with lessons, resources and assessment</td><td>Moodle, Canvas</td><td>Google Classroom or Microsoft Teams used by a class at Coralstone College</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Curated content sources</b></td><td>Collections where items are selected and organised around a topic</td><td>Slideshare, Pinterest</td><td>A Pinterest board of Carnival costume designs collected by a Port of Spain designer</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social media</b></td><td>Platforms built for social interaction and sharing</td><td>Twitter (now called X)</td><td>A Barbados news outlet posting election updates on X and Facebook</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Overlaps.</b> YouTube is a dynamic platform, a media platform and, with its comments, a social one. Pinterest is a curated content source and a media platform. Name the type that matches the use described in the question.</p>\n<p><b>Which platform fits your message?</b> Match the platform to your <b>audience</b> and <b>goal</b>. Suppose you want to share a short tutorial with classmates. Ask three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Who needs to see it?</b> Your CAPE class.</li>\n<li><b>What should they learn?</b> For example, how to export a PNG with transparency in GIMP.</li>\n<li><b>Where will they find and watch it?</b> In the class's eLearning environment (Google Classroom), as an MP4 video.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then <b>choose a platform and a format, and explain why they suit your message</b>. Try naming one example of each of the six types that you use every day.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to \"recommend a platform\", always give the platform <b>and</b> a format, then link both to the audience: \"An unlisted YouTube video (MP4) embedded in Google Classroom, because classmates already check Classroom daily and it plays on their phones.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Platforms can also be grouped by how we use them: apps, websites, dynamic platforms (where content changes all the time), eLearning environments, curated content sources and social media. One platform can fit more than one type. A school in St. Vincent might use Microsoft Teams as its eLearning environment and a Pinterest board as a curated source of poster ideas.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The six access types matter when you <b>recommend</b> a platform, because they describe how the audience will meet the content. An app gives a richer, sometimes offline experience but must be downloaded and updated, a barrier for a cruise visitor in port for one day; a website needs no install. Dynamic platforms keep audiences returning, but content gets buried quickly. eLearning environments add structure, tracking and assessment that a plain YouTube link lacks. Curated sources save time but depend on someone's selection. A strong recommendation names the platform and the format, links both to the audience's devices and habits, and admits one limitation, such as \"students without data at home cannot stream the video\".</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Six types of digital platform","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is planning where a Bahamas heritage project will appear. Match each of the six platform types to the best example.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Apps","r":"Google Earth installed on a tablet to explore Fort Fincastle from above"},{"l":"Websites","r":"A guesthouse on Harbour Island with its own pages at a web address"},{"l":"Dynamic platforms","r":"A YouTube channel where new Carnival videos and comments appear every day"},{"l":"eLearning environments","r":"A Google Classroom where a CAPE class gets lessons and submits assignments"},{"l":"Curated content sources","r":"A Pinterest board collecting the best costume designs for one theme"},{"l":"Social media","r":"Posting election updates on X for followers to reply to and repost"}],"win":"Well matched. You can classify platforms by how we access and use content, the second way of grouping platforms.","tutor":[{"h":"Installed, browsed or learnt in?","p":"<p>Apps are installed, websites are visited in a browser, eLearning environments are built for lessons.</p>"},{"h":"Selected or constantly changing?","p":"<p>Curated sources are hand-picked collections; dynamic platforms change constantly with uploads.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Six platform types (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Apps","r":"Google Earth installed on a tablet"},{"l":"eLearning environments","r":"A class Google Classroom"},{"l":"Curated content sources","r":"A Pinterest board of costume ideas"},{"l":"Social media","r":"Posting updates on X"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Six types of digital platform (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: the examples are described by how people USE them, without naming the service. Match each type to the use that fits it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Apps","r":"A ferry timetable that still works offline once it is installed on a phone"},{"l":"Websites","r":"A volcano observatory's information pages, opened in any browser at a web address"},{"l":"Dynamic platforms","r":"A channel where new uploads and comments change the page every hour"},{"l":"eLearning environments","r":"A course space where students take quizzes and the teacher tracks their scores"},{"l":"Curated content sources","r":"A slide library where someone has selected the best decks on climate change for one topic"},{"l":"Social media","r":"Posts that followers like, reply to and repost to their own networks"}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised each access type from how people use it, which is what a recommendation question needs."}},{"type":"learn","title":"How formats work: compression, resolution and colour depth","html":"<p>Every file format is a set of rules for storing data. To choose formats well, you need four ideas.</p>\n<p><b>1. Resolution.</b> For images, resolution is the number of pixels: a 1080 by 1080 Instagram post has 1,166,400 pixels. For print, it is also described as pixel density (300 ppi is a common print standard; screens need far less). For video, resolution is the frame size (1280 by 720 is 720p HD; 1920 by 1080 is 1080p Full HD; 3840 by 2160 is 4K). For audio, the matching ideas are <b>sample rate</b> (44.1 kHz or 48 kHz) and <b>bit depth</b> (16 or 24 bit).</p>\n<p><b>2. Colour depth (bit depth).</b> The number of bits used for each pixel's colour. More bits mean more possible colours.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Colour depth</th><th>Possible colours</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1-bit</td><td>2 (black and white)</td><td>Simple line art, fax</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8-bit (indexed)</td><td>256</td><td>GIF, PNG-8, simple graphics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>24-bit (8 bits each for red, green, blue)</td><td>about 16.7 million</td><td>JPEG and PNG photos</td></tr>\n<tr><td>32-bit (24-bit plus 8-bit alpha)</td><td>16.7 million plus 256 levels of transparency</td><td>PNG logos with soft transparent edges</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>3. Compression.</b> <b>Lossless</b> compression (PNG, GIF's LZW method, ZIP) shrinks the file by storing patterns more efficiently, and the original can be rebuilt exactly. <b>Lossy</b> compression (JPEG, MP3, most MP4 video) permanently discards data that people are less likely to notice. Video adds <b>inter-frame</b> compression: it stores full key frames and then only the changes between frames, which is why a static interview compresses better than fast Carnival dancing.</p>\n<p><b>4. File size trade-offs.</b> Uncompressed size is roughly <b>width x height x bytes per pixel</b>. A 1080 by 1080 photo at 24-bit (3 bytes per pixel) is about 3.5 MB raw; as a quality-80 JPEG it may be a few hundred kilobytes. Higher resolution, greater colour depth and less compression all mean better quality but bigger files, slower uploads and higher data costs.</p>\n<p>That last point matters in the Caribbean, where many users pay for prepaid mobile data. A 40 MB menu PDF that a visitor must download on roaming data in Castries is a poor choice, however beautiful it looks.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question says \"explain the trade-off\", name both sides: \"Increasing the resolution improves detail, but it increases file size, so the page loads more slowly on mobile data.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Four ideas explain file formats. Resolution is how many pixels an image or video has. Colour depth is how many colours each pixel can show. Compression makes files smaller: lossless keeps every detail, while lossy throws some away. Bigger, sharper, less compressed files cost more data to download, which matters to someone in Belize on prepaid mobile data.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Be ready to calculate and to explain the trade-off. Raw size is width × height × bytes per pixel: a 1920 × 1080 image at 24-bit is 1920 × 1080 × 3 ≈ 6.2 MB before compression. Doubling both dimensions quadruples the pixels, so it roughly quadruples the raw size. Lossy compression removes detail people are less likely to notice, which is why it suits photographs but damages sharp text and logos. Video adds inter-frame compression, storing key frames and then only the changes, so fast movement needs more data than a still interview. A common misconception is that a higher ppi number makes an image sharper on screen; screens display pixels, so only the pixel dimensions matter there.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is preparing files for a Spicemas promotion in Grenada. Answer each question.</p>","items":[{"text":"Malik saves a poster as JPEG, opens it, edits it and saves again five times. The text edges look blotchy. Why?","options":["JPEG reduces the resolution each time","JPEG converts the image to 256 colours","JPEG is lossy, so each re-save discards more data"],"answer":2,"fb":"This is generation loss. JPEG re-compresses and discards data on every save. Keep a lossless master (XCF, PSD or PNG) and export a JPEG once at the end."},{"text":"An image uses 8 bits per pixel. How many colours can each pixel show?","options":["256","8","about 16.7 million"],"answer":0,"fb":"2 to the power of 8 is 256. That is why GIF and PNG-8 images are limited to 256 colours."},{"text":"Which change would REDUCE file size the most for a web banner photo?","options":["Saving it as an uncompressed TIFF","Changing it from 24-bit to 32-bit","Scaling it from 4000 px wide to 1600 px wide and exporting as JPEG"],"answer":2,"fb":"Fewer pixels plus lossy compression gives a much smaller file. Adding an alpha channel or saving uncompressed makes files bigger."},{"text":"A talking-head interview and a fast Carnival parade clip, both 1080p and one minute long, are exported at the same quality. Which is likely to be larger?","options":["The interview","The parade clip","They will be exactly the same size"],"answer":1,"fb":"Inter-frame compression stores only the changes between frames. Fast movement means many changes, so more data is needed to keep the same quality."},{"text":"Roughly how big is a raw 1920 by 1080 image at 24-bit colour?","options":["About 6 MB","About 600 MB","About 60 KB"],"answer":0,"fb":"1920 x 1080 = 2,073,600 pixels; x 3 bytes = about 6.2 MB before compression."},{"text":"Which pair are BOTH lossless?","options":["MP4 and GIF","JPEG and MP3","PNG and ZIP"],"answer":2,"fb":"PNG and ZIP rebuild the original data exactly. JPEG, MP3 and most MP4 video are lossy."}],"win":"Excellent. You can now explain how resolution, colour depth and compression drive file size, the core of \"how formats work\".","tutor":[{"h":"Powers of two","p":"<p>Colours = 2 to the power of the number of bits.</p>"},{"h":"Pixels x bytes","p":"<p>Raw size is width x height x bytes per pixel. 24-bit is 3 bytes.</p>"},{"h":"Lossy vs lossless","p":"<p>Does the original come back exactly? If yes, it is lossless.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Resolution and compression (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"An 8-bit image can show how many colours?","options":["8","256"],"answer":1,"fb":"2 to the power of 8 is 256."},{"text":"Which compression permanently discards data?","options":["lossy","lossless"],"answer":0,"fb":"Lossy compression, such as JPEG and MP3, throws some data away."},{"text":"More pixels usually means...","options":["a smaller file","a bigger file"],"answer":1,"fb":"Each pixel needs data, so more pixels means more data."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Resolution, depth and compression quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: calculations and explanations. Show your working on paper if it helps.</p>","items":[{"text":"Roughly how big is a raw 3840 × 2160 (4K) frame at 24-bit colour?","options":["about 25 MB","about 2.5 MB","about 6 MB","about 250 MB"],"answer":0,"fb":"3840 × 2160 = 8,294,400 pixels; × 3 bytes ≈ 24.9 MB."},{"text":"An image is resized from 2000 × 2000 px to 1000 × 1000 px. Its raw size becomes...","options":["the same","half","a quarter","double"],"answer":2,"fb":"Halving both dimensions leaves one quarter of the pixels, so one quarter of the data."},{"text":"How many colours can a 16-bit-per-pixel image show?","options":["65,536","16","about 16.7 million","256"],"answer":0,"fb":"2 to the power of 16 is 65,536."},{"text":"A PNG logo has an 8-bit alpha channel. How many levels of transparency can each pixel have?","options":["16","256","about 16.7 million","2"],"answer":1,"fb":"8 bits give 256 levels, from fully transparent to fully opaque."},{"text":"About how big is a 3-minute MP3 at 128 kbps?","options":["about 300 KB","about 30 MB","about 3 MB","about 30 KB"],"answer":2,"fb":"128 kbps ÷ 8 = 16 KB per second; × 180 seconds ≈ 2.9 MB."},{"text":"A designer says: \"Set the web banner to 300 ppi so it looks sharper on screens.\" Why is this wrong?","options":["Screens cannot show any image above 72 ppi","ppi changes the colour depth","ppi matters for print; on screen only the pixel dimensions affect detail","300 ppi is too low for screens"],"answer":2,"fb":"A 1600 px banner shows the same detail on screen whether it is tagged 72 or 300 ppi."},{"text":"Why does a JPEG of a sunset look fine while a JPEG of small menu text looks blotchy?","options":["Text increases the resolution","Lossy compression discards fine detail, which shows most around sharp edges such as text","JPEG adds transparency around text","JPEG can show only 256 colours"],"answer":1,"fb":"Smooth tones hide JPEG artefacts; sharp, high-contrast edges reveal them. Use PNG for text."}],"win":"Excellent. You can calculate file sizes, colour counts and bitrates, and explain why compression suits some images and not others."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Six formats compared: MP4, MP3, PDF, PNG, JPEG, GIF","html":"<p>There are six formats you must be able to <b>distinguish</b>, explain <b>how they work</b> and say <b>when to use</b> each.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Format</th><th>How it works</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limits</th><th>Best for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>JPEG</b> (.jpg)</td><td>Lossy raster image. Splits the picture into small blocks and discards fine detail and colour data; you choose a quality level (for example 80 to 90)</td><td>Small files, 24-bit colour, opens everywhere</td><td>No transparency; artefacts around sharp text; quality drops with re-saving</td><td>Photographs for web, social media and email</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>PNG</b> (.png)</td><td>Lossless raster image. Compresses patterns without losing data; supports 24-bit colour and an 8-bit alpha channel</td><td>Crisp edges and text; smooth transparency</td><td>Larger than JPEG for photos; no animation in standard use</td><td>Logos, icons, screenshots, graphics with text, images that need a transparent background</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GIF</b> (.gif)</td><td>Lossless (LZW) compression, but limited to a palette of 256 colours per frame; can hold many frames</td><td>Simple animation that plays everywhere; on/off transparency</td><td>Banding and grain in photos; large files for long animations; no sound</td><td>Short looping animations, reaction loops, simple icons</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>PDF</b> (.pdf)</td><td>A fixed-layout document format (an open ISO standard) that embeds fonts, images, vector graphics and links</td><td>Looks the same on any device; printable; can be searchable and accessible</td><td>Hard to edit; large if images are not compressed; awkward to read on small phones</td><td>Menus for print, forms, reports, portfolios, SBA documents, Paper 02 submissions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MP3</b> (.mp3)</td><td>Lossy audio. Perceptual coding removes sounds the human ear is less likely to notice; bitrate (128 to 320 kbps) sets quality</td><td>Small files (about 1 MB per minute at 128 kbps); plays on almost every device</td><td>Quality lost permanently; not ideal as an editing master</td><td>Podcasts, music sharing, radio ads, WhatsApp voice notes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MP4</b> (.mp4)</td><td>A <b>container</b> (MPEG-4 Part 14) that holds video (usually H.264) and audio (usually AAC) plus subtitles; uses lossy inter-frame compression</td><td>Very wide compatibility; good quality at modest sizes; accepted by YouTube, Instagram, TikTok and browsers</td><td>Lossy; large at high bitrates; newer codecs inside it (H.265) may not play on older devices</td><td>Promotional videos, Reels, web video, the SBA 30-second ad</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Notice two patterns. First, three formats are <b>lossy</b> (JPEG, MP3, MP4) and two are <b>lossless</b> (PNG, GIF), while PDF is a document wrapper that can contain both. Second, a <b>container</b> is not the same as a <b>codec</b>: MP4 is the box, H.264 is the method used to compress the pictures inside it.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often gives a scenario with one key clue: \"transparent background\" points to PNG, \"animation that plays in an email\" to GIF, \"must print exactly as designed\" to PDF, \"photograph for a website\" to JPEG.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Learn six formats. JPEG suits photos and makes small files. PNG keeps sharp edges and allows a transparent background, which is ideal for logos. GIF makes simple looping animations. PDF keeps a document's layout fixed for printing. MP3 is compressed audio for music and podcasts. MP4 holds video and sound for YouTube and Instagram. A Guyanese bakery's logo should be a PNG.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Look for the one clue in a scenario that decides the format, then give the technical reason. \"Transparent background\" rules out JPEG, which has no alpha channel. \"Animation inside an email\" points to GIF, because most email programs block video. \"Photograph on a fast-loading page\" points to JPEG, whose lossy compression suits continuous tones. Know the limits too: GIF's 256-colour palette causes banding in photos, and a photo saved as PNG is often several times larger than the JPEG. MP4 is a <b>container</b>, not a codec, so two MP4 files can behave differently if one uses H.264 and the other H.265. PDF is a wrapper that can hold both lossy and lossless images, so its size depends on the images inside.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which image or document format?","intro":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, needs files for several Caribbean clients. Sort each job into the best format.</p>","main":"Which format suits this job best?","bins":["JPEG","PNG","GIF","PDF"],"items":[{"t":"A photo of sunset at Seven Mile Beach in Negril for a hotel's website gallery","bin":0,"fb":"A continuous-tone photograph compresses well as JPEG with little visible loss, keeping the page fast."},{"t":"A product photo of a bottle of local pepper sauce for an Instagram post","bin":0,"fb":"Social platforms re-compress uploads anyway; a high-quality JPEG is the efficient choice for a photo."},{"t":"A screenshot of an app interface with small text, for a tutorial","bin":1,"fb":"Screenshots have sharp text and flat colour. PNG keeps them crisp; JPEG adds blur and artefacts around text."},{"t":"A three-frame waving flag loop to embed in an Emancipation Day email newsletter","bin":2,"fb":"Most email programs do not play video, but they do play GIF animation. Three simple frames suit the 256-colour palette."},{"t":"A 10-page SBA proposal with images and headings, to be submitted to the teacher","bin":3,"fb":"A PDF keeps headings, fonts and images in place whatever device the teacher uses to open it."},{"t":"A simple animated \"Book now\" button for a ferry company website banner","bin":2,"fb":"A short, flat-colour loop is a classic GIF use. It plays without a video player."},{"t":"Bayside Water Sports logo to place over a coloured website header","bin":1,"fb":"The logo needs a transparent background and crisp edges. PNG supports an alpha channel; JPEG does not."},{"t":"A restaurant menu in Oranjestad that must print on A4 exactly as designed","bin":3,"fb":"PDF fixes the layout and embeds fonts, so it prints the same on any computer."}],"win":"Well sorted. JPEG for photos, PNG for crisp graphics and transparency, GIF for simple loops, PDF for fixed-layout documents.","tutor":[{"h":"Look for the clue word","p":"<p>Photo? Transparent? Animated? Print exactly?</p>"},{"h":"Edges and text","p":"<p>Sharp edges and small text suffer under lossy compression.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which format? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A beach photo for a website","bin":0,"fb":"Photos suit JPEG."},{"t":"A logo with a transparent background","bin":1,"fb":"PNG supports transparency."},{"t":"A short looping animation in an email","bin":2,"fb":"GIF animates in email."},{"t":"A menu that must print exactly","bin":3,"fb":"PDF keeps the layout fixed."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which image or document format? (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: six formats, including audio and video. Zara needs files for more Caribbean clients. Sort each job into its best format.</p>","main":"Which format suits this job best?","bins":["JPEG","PNG","GIF","PDF","MP3","MP4"],"items":[{"t":"A vertical Reel showing a costume being made, with music","bin":5,"fb":"Video plus audio for Instagram means an MP4 (H.264 and AAC)."},{"t":"A signed volunteer form that must look identical when printed in Dominica and in Belize","bin":3,"fb":"PDF fixes the layout and embeds the fonts."},{"t":"A logo that must sit on both a dark and a light website header","bin":1,"fb":"PNG's alpha channel gives a transparent background that works on any colour."},{"t":"A flat, three-colour animated loading icon for a website","bin":2,"fb":"A simple, flat-colour loop fits GIF's palette and plays everywhere."},{"t":"A two-second animated \"Sold out\" sticker inside a newsletter email","bin":2,"fb":"Email programs play GIF animation but usually block video."},{"t":"A product photo for an online shop whose pages must load fast on mobile data","bin":0,"fb":"Lossy JPEG keeps photos small with little visible loss."},{"t":"A 30-second radio advert for a Jamaican bakery to stream on its website","bin":4,"fb":"MP3 is small and plays in every browser."},{"t":"A screenshot of a spreadsheet with small text, for a training guide","bin":1,"fb":"Sharp text and flat colour stay crisp in lossless PNG."},{"t":"A podcast episode for listeners with limited data","bin":4,"fb":"Lossy MP3 keeps speech clear at a small file size."},{"t":"A 20-page photo portfolio that a client will print at a shop","bin":3,"fb":"A multi-page, fixed-layout document for printing is a PDF job."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched six formats to their jobs: photos, crisp graphics, simple loops, fixed documents, audio and video."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Selecting formats for purpose and audience","html":"<p>Choosing a format is a design decision, not a habit. Use this four-question check, which matches the language examiners reward.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Purpose:</b> Will it be viewed, edited, printed, streamed, downloaded or archived? An editing master needs a lossless or project format (XCF, PSD, WAV, .aup3); a final delivery file can be lossy.</li>\n<li><b>Audience and device:</b> Who will open it, and on what? Older relatives on basic Android phones, cruise visitors on roaming data, a printer at a Kingstown print shop and a teacher on a school laptop all need different things.</li>\n<li><b>Platform requirements:</b> What does the platform accept? Instagram Reels expect vertical MP4; an app store wants PNG icons at fixed sizes; most email programs will not play video.</li>\n<li><b>Quality versus file size:</b> What is the smallest file that still looks or sounds right for this use?</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Purpose and audience</th><th>Good choice</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A St. Lucia tour operator's photo gallery for visitors on phones</td><td>JPEG, about 1600 px wide, quality 80</td><td>Sharp on phones, small enough to load on mobile data</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A Barbados school's sports day results for parents to print</td><td>PDF</td><td>Layout and fonts stay fixed on every device and printer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A radio-style ad for a Jamaican bakery to play on Spotify or a website</td><td>MP3 at 128 to 192 kbps (keep a WAV master)</td><td>Small, plays everywhere; the WAV keeps full quality for re-edits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A Curaçao festival's sponsor logos for a website footer</td><td>PNG with transparency (or SVG if the site supports it)</td><td>Crisp edges over any background</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A hurricane preparedness explainer for social media in Dominica</td><td>MP4, H.264, captions burned in or attached</td><td>Plays on every platform; captions help viewers who watch with sound off and those with hearing loss</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Accessibility is part of audience. A PDF should be text-based (not a scanned picture of text) so screen readers can read it; a video should have captions; a JPEG on a website needs alt text. Thinking about who might be excluded is a strong evaluation point in Paper 02.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"justify\" question, always give the format <b>plus</b> a reason linked to the audience: \"MP4, because every platform the hotel uses accepts it and guests can play it on their phones without extra apps.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To choose a format, ask four questions. What is the purpose: viewing, editing, printing or streaming? Who is the audience, and what devices do they use? What does the platform accept? What is the smallest file that still looks right? Then give your choice and a reason. A hotel in Nevis sending guests a printable price list should choose PDF.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The best format depends on the whole chain from master to audience, so professionals keep two versions: a lossless or project master for editing (XCF, PSD, WAV) and a delivery file chosen for the platform. Editing a delivery file and exporting it again, such as re-saving an MP3, causes generation loss. Accessibility is part of the audience question: a scanned PDF is a picture that screen readers cannot read, and a video without captions excludes deaf viewers and anyone watching with the sound off. In a \"justify\" answer, link the format to a specific feature of the audience or platform: data costs, older phones, printing or the platform's required aspect ratio.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"The right format for the audience","q":"The Lagoon Kayak Tours company in Antigua wants to send past guests a short animated \"We miss you!\" greeting inside a marketing email. Many guests read email on older phones. Which format is MOST appropriate for the animation?","options":[{"t":"PNG","fb":"PNG gives crisp, lossless images, but a standard PNG is a still image, so the greeting would not animate."},{"t":"GIF","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Most email programs display animated GIFs inline, while video is usually blocked or shown only as a link. A short, flat-colour loop suits GIF's 256-colour palette."},{"t":"MP4","fb":"MP4 is the best video format for social platforms, but most email programs will not play embedded video, so many guests would see nothing."},{"t":"PDF","fb":"A PDF is a fixed-layout document. It cannot play as an animation inside the body of an email."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Two clues","p":"<p>It must <b>animate</b>, and it must work <b>inside an email</b>.</p>"},{"h":"Think about the platform","p":"<p>What do email programs reliably display without a video player?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The right format (Foundation)","q":"Which format shows a short animation inside most emails?","options":[{"t":"PDF","fb":"PDF is a document format and does not animate in an email."},{"t":"JPEG","fb":"JPEG is a still photo format."},{"t":"GIF","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Email programs display animated GIFs."}]},
 "up": {"title":"The right format for the audience (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A dive shop in Grenada wants a 15-second clip of a turtle on its website home page AND a looping preview of it in its email newsletter. Many customers use phones on mobile data. Which plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"One long GIF of the full 15 seconds, used everywhere, because GIF plays on every platform.","fb":"A 15-second GIF of real footage would be very large and show banding. MP4 is much more efficient on the website."},{"t":"The MP4 in both places, because email programs play embedded video.","fb":"Most email programs block or ignore embedded video, so many customers would see nothing."},{"t":"An H.264 MP4 (muted, with captions) on the website, and a short, small GIF of a few key frames with a \"Watch the video\" link in the email.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. MP4 is far more efficient for real footage on the web, while a short GIF is what email programs will actually play."},{"t":"The original 4K .mov master in both places, for the best quality.","fb":"A 4K master is far too large for mobile data, and .mov with a newer codec may not play on many devices."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Importing, exporting, sharing and embedding","html":"<p>You must be able to <b>import, export, share and embed data for different uses</b>. These four actions are often confused, so learn them as a set.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Importing</b> brings outside data into an application: placing a photo into GIMP (File &gt; Open as Layers), loading a CSV of customer names into Google Sheets, adding clips to the OpenShot project files panel. The original file is not changed.</li>\n<li><b>Exporting</b> converts your project into a format another program or platform can use: GIMP's File &gt; Export As to make a JPEG from an .xcf project; Audacity's File &gt; Export Audio to make an MP3; Canva's Share &gt; Download as PNG, PDF or MP4. Saving keeps the editable project; exporting makes the delivery file.</li>\n<li><b>Sharing</b> gives others access to a file or link: a Google Drive link set to \"Anyone with the link can view\", a WeTransfer link for a large video, an AirDrop or WhatsApp attachment. Always check permissions: viewer, commenter or editor.</li>\n<li><b>Embedding</b> places live content from one platform inside another, so it plays or updates without being copied: a YouTube video in a WordPress page, a Google Map of Main Street in Oranjestad on a shop's contact page, an Instagram post in a news article, a Google Form in a school site.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most platforms give you embed code from a <b>Share &gt; Embed</b> button. A YouTube embed looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\"\n  src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID\"\n  title=\"Aruba Carnival highlights\"\n  allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Embedding saves storage and bandwidth on your own site (the video streams from YouTube's servers), keeps view counts on one platform and updates automatically. The trade-off: if the original is deleted or made private, the embed breaks, and the host platform may show its own branding or suggested videos.</p>\n<p>Exporting <b>data</b> matters too. A small business might export its Shopify orders as CSV to analyse in a spreadsheet, or export contacts from a sign-up form into Mailchimp. Formats like <b>CSV</b>, <b>JSON</b> and <b>XML</b> let data move between systems.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Distinguish clearly: \"Sharing a link sends people <b>to</b> the content; embedding brings the content <b>into</b> your page.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Importing brings a file into a program, like opening a photo in GIMP. Exporting turns your project into a file others can use, like a JPEG or an MP3. Sharing gives people access to a file or link, like a Google Drive link. Embedding places live content inside another page, like a YouTube video playing on a shop's website in Antigua.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The four actions answer different questions: import is \"how do I get material in?\", export is \"how do I make a usable file?\", share is \"how do others get access?\" and embed is \"how does content appear inside my page?\". Embedding has real advantages: the video streams from the media platform's servers, so a small website is not slowed down, and changes to the original appear automatically. Its weakness is <b>dependency</b>: if the owner deletes the video or makes it private, the embed breaks. Sharing has its own risk, permissions: an \"anyone can edit\" link to a price list invites accidental changes. Data export matters too, because CSV and JSON let a business move customer data between systems without retyping it.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Keisha's Carnival campaign files","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is preparing digital assets for the Jouvert Junction band's Vincy Mas launch. Help her make the best format and platform decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Malik has sent a 2 GB master video of the costume launch. Keisha needs to post a 30-second teaser on Instagram Reels and TikTok tonight. What should she export?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Upload the 2 GB 16:9 master file as it is; the apps will sort it out.","to":"bad1","fb":"The platforms will crop or letterbox a landscape video and may reject or heavily compress a huge file."},{"t":"A vertical 1080 by 1920 MP4 (H.264 video, AAC audio) with captions.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Vertical MP4 matches both platforms' specifications and captions help viewers who scroll with sound off."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The band leader wants the costume price list available to members. It includes photos and a table. Members will read it on phones and some will print it. Which format?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An editable Word file, shared with \"anyone can edit\" access.","to":"bad3","fb":"Anyone could change the prices, and layout may break on phones without Word."},{"t":"A text-based PDF with compressed images, shared through a view-only Google Drive link.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong choice. PDF keeps the table layout for printing, compressed images keep it small, and view-only sharing protects the original."},{"t":"A photo of the printed price list, posted as a JPEG.","to":"bad2","fb":"A photographed list is hard to read, cannot be searched and is inaccessible to screen readers."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Maya is building the band's registration page. Keisha wants the teaser video on it too. What should Maya do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Upload the teaser to YouTube and embed it with the Share > Embed code.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. YouTube streams the video efficiently to every device and the band's small web host is not overloaded."},{"t":"Put the 2 GB master file directly on the web server and link to it.","to":"bad4","fb":"Visitors would have to download a huge file and the band's hosting bandwidth would be used up quickly."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign ready.</b> The teaser plays well on Reels and TikTok, the price list prints cleanly and the registration page loads fast. A Grade One answer would explain why: each format matched its platform's specifications, the audience's devices and the purpose (view, print or embed).</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Cropped and blurry.</b> The landscape video is letterboxed into a tiny strip and heavily re-compressed. Try again and export to the platform's aspect ratio and format.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unreadable list.</b> Members zoom in and still cannot read the prices, and nothing can be searched. Try again and choose a fixed-layout document format.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Edited prices.</b> Someone accidentally deletes a row and the layout falls apart on phones. Try again and protect the file with the right format and permissions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Site crash.</b> The page takes minutes to load and the host suspends the site for using too much bandwidth. Try again and use embedding from a media platform.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Match the platform","p":"<p>Each platform has file specifications. Vertical apps want vertical video.</p>"},{"h":"View, print or edit?","p":"<p>Decide what the audience needs to do with the file, then pick the format and the permission level.</p>"},{"h":"Let platforms do the heavy lifting","p":"<p>Media platforms are built to stream video; small web hosts are not.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Carnival campaign files (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Keisha needs a teaser for Instagram Reels.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What should she export for Reels?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A vertical 1080 by 1920 MP4 with captions.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It matches the platform's specifications."},{"t":"The 2 GB landscape master file.","to":"bad","fb":"It would be cropped or heavily compressed."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the format to the platform.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The video looks wrong on phones. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Keisha's Carnival campaign files (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief for the Jouvert Junction band. More files, more platforms and an accessibility requirement. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Malik's master is a 2 GB, 16:9, 4K HEVC .mov. Keisha needs the teaser on Instagram Reels, TikTok and the band's YouTube channel tonight. What is the best export plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Upload the HEVC .mov master everywhere; the platforms will sort it out.","to":"bad1","fb":"The platforms will crop and heavily re-compress it, and the huge upload may fail."},{"t":"Export one 1080 × 1920 MP4 and upload it everywhere, including the YouTube channel.","to":"ok1","fb":"It works on phones, but viewers watching the channel on laptops and TVs see a narrow strip."},{"t":"Export a 1080 × 1920 H.264 MP4 with captions for Reels and TikTok, and a 1920 × 1080 H.264 MP4 for YouTube, keeping the .mov as the master.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Each platform gets its preferred shape and a widely supported codec, and the master is kept."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Band members send costume photos from their iPhones in HEIC format for the website gallery. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Convert them to JPEG, resize them to about 1600 px wide at quality 80, and rename them in lowercase with hyphens.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Compatible format, sensible size and web-safe file names."},{"t":"Upload the HEIC files directly; modern browsers can open anything.","to":"bad2","fb":"Many browsers and devices still cannot display HEIC."},{"t":"Convert them to PNG at full resolution for the best quality.","to":"ok2","fb":"PNG is compatible, but full-size PNG photos are very large and slow to load on mobile data."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The price list has photos and a table. Members read it on phones, some print it, and one member uses a screen reader. Which format and sharing setting?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An editable Word file, shared with \"anyone can edit\" access.","to":"bad3","fb":"Anyone could change the prices, and the layout may break on phones."},{"t":"A scanned PDF of the printed list, shared view-only.","to":"ok3","fb":"The layout is fixed, but a scan is a picture of text, so the screen reader cannot read it."},{"t":"A text-based PDF with compressed images and tagged headings, shared through a view-only link.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Fixed layout for printing, a small file, readable by screen readers and protected from edits."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Maya is building the band's registration page and wants the teaser on it. What should she do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Embed the YouTube version with Share > Embed, and add a text link to the video underneath in case the embed does not load.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. YouTube streams the video, and the fallback link covers a broken or blocked embed."},{"t":"Put the 2 GB master on the band's web server and link to it.","to":"bad4","fb":"Visitors would face a huge download, and the small host would run out of bandwidth."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One campaign.</b> Every platform gets the right shape and codec, the gallery loads quickly, the price list prints, reads aloud and cannot be edited, and the page streams the teaser with a fallback. You can justify each choice by platform, device, audience and purpose.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Half right.</b> Reels and TikTok look great, but the YouTube channel looks amateur on big screens. A partial success: export a version for each platform's shape.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Slow gallery.</b> The photos look sharp, but the page takes ages to load on phones. A partial success: match the format and size to photos on the web.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Not for everyone.</b> Most members are happy, but the screen-reader user cannot read the prices. A partial success: use a text-based PDF.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Cropped and blurry.</b> The landscape master is letterboxed and re-compressed. Try again and export to each platform's shape and codec.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Broken gallery.</b> Half the photos show as blank boxes. Try again and convert to a widely supported format.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Edited prices.</b> A row is deleted by accident and the layout falls apart. Try again with the right format and permissions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Site crash.</b> The page takes minutes to load and the host suspends the site. Try again and embed from a media platform.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cross-platform compatibility","html":"<p><b>Cross-platform compatibility</b> means a file, app or web page works correctly across different operating systems (Windows, macOS, Android, iOS, Linux), browsers, devices and screen sizes. When you export work, choose appropriate formats and ensure compatibility across different devices and platforms. Common problems and fixes:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Problem</th><th>Example</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Proprietary or newer formats</b></td><td>iPhones can save photos as HEIC and video as HEVC (H.265) .mov; some Windows PCs and older Android phones cannot open them</td><td>Export or convert to JPEG and H.264 MP4, or change the camera setting to \"Most Compatible\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Missing fonts</b></td><td>A flyer made with a special font opens on another computer with a plain substitute font</td><td>Export to PDF (fonts are embedded) or convert text to outlines for print</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Software-specific project files</b></td><td>Sending a .psd, .xcf or .aup3 file to a client who has no editing software</td><td>Send an exported PNG, JPEG, PDF, MP3 or MP4, and keep the project file as your master</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Screen size and orientation</b></td><td>A wide desktop banner is unreadable on a phone</td><td>Responsive design and separate crops (16:9, 1:1, 9:16)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>File names and paths</b></td><td>\"Final Logo (2).PNG\" breaks a web link because of spaces and capital letters on a case-sensitive server</td><td>Use lowercase, no spaces, hyphens or underscores: <code>final-logo.png</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Codec and browser support</b></td><td>OGG audio or a newer video codec plays in one browser but not another</td><td>Use widely supported defaults (MP3, H.264 MP4) or provide a fallback</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Compatibility also links to <b>access</b> in the Caribbean. Many users in rural Guyana, Belize or Dominica still use older, lower-cost phones and slower connections. A product that works only on the newest iPhone excludes part of the audience. Testing on several devices (a cheap Android phone, a school laptop, a tablet) before launch is part of professional practice.</p>\n<p>Standards help. Open, widely adopted formats such as PDF, PNG, JPEG, MP3 and MP4, and web standards such as HTML and CSS, exist so that content works anywhere.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an evaluation question, pair each compatibility problem with a practical fix. One problem plus one fix, explained, usually earns two marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Cross-platform compatibility means a file or app works on different devices, systems and browsers. Problems include iPhone HEIC photos that some PCs cannot open, missing fonts, project files nobody else can open and file names with spaces. Fix them by using common formats such as JPEG, PDF and MP4, and test on cheap phones too, as many users in rural Guyana still have.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Compatibility is really about the <b>weakest device in the audience</b>. Testing only on the designer's new laptop hides problems that a budget Android phone, an older browser or a slow connection will reveal. Open, widely supported standards (PDF, PNG, JPEG, MP3, H.264 MP4, HTML and CSS) exist to reduce this risk, which is why they are safer than the newest codecs, even when the newer ones are more efficient. Some problems are invisible to the creator: a case-sensitive web server treats \"Logo.PNG\" and \"logo.png\" as different files, so a link that works on Windows breaks online. In an evaluation, pair each problem with a practical fix and, where possible, a way to test it.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the compatibility problems","intro":"<p>Jerome sent files to the Jacmel Market Collective, a group of craft vendors in Haiti. Several vendors say the files will not open or look wrong.</p>","q":"Select every item that is likely to cause a cross-platform compatibility problem.","lines":["The vendor photos were sent straight from an iPhone in HEIC format.","The price flyer was sent as a PDF with fonts embedded.","The promo video was exported as H.265 inside a .mov file.","The logo was sent only as a GIMP .xcf project file.","The website images were named \"Stall Photo 1.JPG\" with spaces and capitals.","The jingle was exported as a 192 kbps MP3.","A 1080 by 1920 MP4 was prepared for Instagram Reels.","The website banner was designed only at 1920 px wide with small text."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,7],"win":"Well spotted. HEIC, H.265 .mov, project-only files, messy file names and desktop-only layouts are classic compatibility traps.","miss":"Check again. Ask of each item: will it open, play and display correctly on a cheap Android phone, a Windows laptop and a web server?","tutor":[{"h":"Widely supported defaults","p":"<p>PDF, PNG, JPEG, MP3 and H.264 MP4 open almost everywhere.</p>"},{"h":"Project vs export","p":"<p>Editing project files need the editing software to open them.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the compatibility problems (Foundation)","q":"Which TWO items could cause problems on other devices?","lines":["Photos sent in iPhone HEIC format.","A flyer sent as a PDF.","A logo sent only as a GIMP .xcf file.","A jingle sent as an MP3."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. HEIC and .xcf need special support; PDF and MP3 open almost anywhere.","miss":"Look for the formats that need special software or newer devices."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the compatibility problems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jerome is checking a second batch of files for the Jacmel Market Collective. These problems are harder to spot: some only appear on certain servers, browsers or older phones.</p>","q":"Select every item that is likely to cause a cross-platform compatibility problem.","lines":["The menu PDF uses a custom font that was not embedded.","The image is saved as \"Stall-Photo-1.JPG\" on a case-sensitive server, but the page links to \"stall-photo-1.jpg\".","The promo is an H.264 MP4 with AAC audio.","The audio was exported only as OGG, with no MP3 fallback, for a site visited on many different browsers.","The logo is supplied as both PNG and SVG.","The video was exported only with the newest AV1 codec, for viewers who mostly use older phones.","The gallery photos are JPEGs resized to 1600 px wide.","The website was tested on a budget Android phone, a school laptop and a tablet.","The client was sent only the Audacity .aup3 project of the jingle.","A desktop-only banner with 12 px text was used on the mobile site."],"correct":[0,1,3,5,8,9],"win":"Well spotted. Unembedded fonts, case mismatches on the server, missing fallbacks, codecs too new for the audience, project-only files and desktop-only layouts all break on some platforms.","miss":"Check again. Ask of each item: will it open, play and display correctly on an older Android phone, a different browser and a case-sensitive web server?"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Paper 02 format conversion tips","html":"<p>Paper 02 includes practical activities such as <b>scanning, photographing, digitizing, editing and format conversion</b>, done offline with tools such as GIMP, Paint.NET or Photoshop, Inkscape or Illustrator, and a way to export to PDF. Practise these conversions until they are automatic.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Conversion</th><th>Steps (GIMP / Inkscape / other)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Scanned or photographed sketch to JPEG for web</td><td>GIMP: File &gt; Open; Image &gt; Crop to Content or use the Crop tool; Colours &gt; Levels to whiten the paper; Image &gt; Scale Image to the stated width; File &gt; Export As, type <code>.jpg</code>, set Quality about 85, Export</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Logo to PNG with transparency</td><td>GIMP: Layer &gt; Transparency &gt; Add Alpha Channel; Fuzzy Select (U) the white background; Delete; File &gt; Export As <code>.png</code>. Inkscape: File &gt; Export (Export PNG Image), choose Page or Selection, set the pixel width</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Image to GIF</td><td>GIMP: Image &gt; Mode &gt; Indexed (up to 255 colours plus transparency); File &gt; Export As <code>.gif</code>. For animation, put each frame on its own layer and tick \"As animation\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vector logo to PDF</td><td>Inkscape: File &gt; Save a Copy, choose PDF; keep text as text or convert to paths</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Document or image to PDF</td><td>LibreOffice or Word: File &gt; Export as PDF; GIMP: File &gt; Export As <code>.pdf</code>; any program: Print to PDF</td></tr>\n<tr><td>WAV to MP3</td><td>Audacity: File &gt; Open the WAV; File &gt; Export Audio (older versions: File &gt; Export &gt; Export as MP3); choose MP3 and a bitrate such as 192 kbps</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Exam habits that protect marks:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read the question for the exact <b>format, size, resolution and file name</b> it asks for, and follow it to the letter.</li>\n<li>Save your editable master first (.xcf, .svg, .aup3), then export. If a conversion goes wrong, you can try again.</li>\n<li>Use the naming pattern you are given, for example <code>candidatenumber_q1_logo.png</code>: lowercase, no spaces.</li>\n<li>Check the result: open the exported file, confirm the dimensions (GIMP: Image &gt; Canvas Size shows them), and check the transparency or page count.</li>\n<li>There is <b>no Internet</b> in the exam, so practise with offline tools, not only Canva.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Save\" and \"Export\" are different in GIMP. Saving makes an .xcf project. Only <b>File &gt; Export As</b> creates a JPEG, PNG or GIF the examiner can open.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Paper 02 may ask you to scan, edit and convert files without the Internet. Practise in GIMP, Inkscape and Audacity: JPEGs for web photos, PNGs with transparency for logos, GIFs for simple animations, PDFs for documents and MP3s for audio. Always save your editable master first, use the exact file name asked for, and open the exported file to check it.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Most lost marks in practical conversion tasks come from not following instructions exactly, not from weak skills. If the question asks for a \"1200 px wide JPEG named candidatenumber_q1_logo.jpg\", a perfect PNG at 1000 px still loses marks. Build a checklist habit: format, dimensions, resolution, file name, then open and verify. Understand what each step does to the data. Scaling an image up cannot add real detail, so scan with enough resolution in the first place. Converting to indexed mode for a GIF reduces the colours permanently, so do it on a copy. Re-exporting a JPEG causes generation loss, which is why the master stays as .xcf and lets you try again within the time limit.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Convert a scanned sketch for Paper 02","intro":"<p>A Paper 02 task asks you to scan a pencil sketch of a Willemstad storefront, clean it up and submit it as a 1200 px wide JPEG. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Scan or photograph the sketch and open the file in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Crop away the edges of the paper and the scanner bed."},{"t":"Use Colours > Levels to make the paper white and the lines darker."},{"t":"Use Image > Scale Image to set the width to 1200 px with the chain linked."},{"t":"Save the editable master as an .xcf file."},{"t":"Use File > Export As, type the required file name ending in .jpg and set quality about 85."},{"t":"Open the exported JPEG and check its dimensions and appearance."}],"win":"Correct order. Capture, crop, adjust, resize, save the master, export to the required format, then verify: a reliable Paper 02 workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Clean before you shrink","p":"<p>Adjust and crop at full resolution, then resize.</p>"},{"h":"Master before export","p":"<p>Keep an editable copy before you make the lossy JPEG.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Convert a sketch (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the scanned sketch in GIMP."},{"t":"Crop and adjust the levels."},{"t":"Scale the image to 1200 px wide."},{"t":"Export As a JPEG with the required file name."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Convert a scanned sketch for Paper 02 (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: nine steps, including scanning resolution and straightening. Put the full workflow for the 1200 px wide JPEG of a Willemstad storefront in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Scan the sketch at 300 ppi, or photograph it flat in even light."},{"t":"Open the file in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Rotate the image slightly so the storefront's vertical lines are straight (Image > Transform > Arbitrary Rotation)."},{"t":"Crop away the paper edges and the scanner bed."},{"t":"Use Colours > Levels to make the paper white and the lines darker."},{"t":"Use Image > Scale Image to set the width to 1200 px with the chain linked."},{"t":"Save the editable master as an .xcf file."},{"t":"Use File > Export As, type the required lowercase file name ending in .jpg and set quality about 85."},{"t":"Open the exported JPEG and confirm it is 1200 px wide and looks clean."}],"win":"Correct. Capture with enough resolution, straighten before cropping, adjust levels, resize, save the master, export exactly as instructed, then verify."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Platforms and formats for a Caribbean business","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: digital media platforms and formats","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Fort Street Fudge and Coconut Co.</b> in Basseterre, St. Kitts, sells handmade fudge and coconut treats to cruise visitors and local residents. The owner, Ms. Liburd, has: a 4000 by 3000 px photo of her products; her logo on a white background; a 2-minute WAV recording of a radio jingle; a 3-minute landscape video of the shop; and a price list typed in Word. She wants to sell online, appear when visitors search for sweets near the cruise port, and reach people on their phones.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Recommend <b>THREE</b> different types of digital platform for the business, naming an example of each and explaining how it helps. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>For <b>FOUR</b> of her files, state the format you would convert it to for online use and justify your choice with reference to compression, quality or file size. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between <b>embedding</b> and <b>sharing</b> her shop video, using her website as an example. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> cross-platform compatibility issues she should consider, with a solution for each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A search engine such as ... would help because ... A marketplace or social platform such as ... A ... platform such as ...<br><b>(b)</b> The product photo should be exported as ... because ... The logo should be ... because ... The jingle ... The price list ...<br><b>(c)</b> Sharing the video means ... whereas embedding means ...<br><b>(d)</b> One compatibility issue is ... She could solve this by ... A second issue is ...</p>","checklist":["I named three different platform types with a real example of each, and showed that the platform carries the content to the audience.","I gave four file conversions, each with a format and a reason about compression, quality or size.","I used \"whereas\" to distinguish embedding from sharing.","I explained two compatibility issues and gave a fix for each.","I linked my answers to the business, its cruise visitors and local customers.","I labelled my answers (a) to (d)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Platform recommendations","marks":3,"look":["search engine|google|bing","marketplace|etsy|amazon|shop|social|instagram|facebook|tiktok","media platform|youtube|infrastructure|payment|wipay|paypal|cloud|crowd","carr(y|ies)|medium|reach(es)? (its|the|her)|intended audience|app\\b|website|dynamic|curated"],"min":2,"strong":"You recommended a range of platform types, used the idea of a platform as the medium that carries content to its audience, and explained how each helps the business.","weak":"Name three different types, e.g. a search engine (Google Business Profile so visitors find the shop near the port), a social platform (Instagram for product reels) and an infrastructure platform (a payment gateway for online orders)."},{"criterion":"Format choices and justification","marks":6,"look":["jpe?g","png|transparen","mp3","mp4|h\\.?264","pdf","lossy|lossless|compress|file size|smaller|kb|mb|resolution|scale|resiz"],"min":4,"strong":"Your format choices are accurate and justified with technical reasons.","weak":"Pair each file with a format and a reason: photo to a resized JPEG (lossy, much smaller); logo to PNG (lossless, transparent background); WAV jingle to MP3 (about a tenth of the size); Word price list to PDF (fixed layout, opens on any device)."},{"criterion":"Embedding, sharing and compatibility","marks":6,"look":["embed","share|link","whereas|while|however|instead","compatib|device|android|iphone|ios|windows|browser","font|heic|codec|project file|file name|phone|screen size|responsive"],"min":3,"strong":"You distinguished embedding from sharing clearly and gave practical compatibility fixes.","weak":"Explain that sharing sends people a link to the video on another platform, whereas embedding plays it inside her own page. Then give two issues, such as fonts changing in the Word file (fix: PDF) and an iPhone video in HEVC .mov (fix: export as H.264 MP4)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A platform is the medium that carries digital content to its intended audience, so she needs platforms her customers already use. First, a <b>search engine</b> listing such as a Google Business Profile would help cruise visitors who search \"sweets near Port Zante\" find the shop, its hours and directions. Second, a <b>social platform</b> such as Instagram would let her post short reels of fudge being made, reaching visitors planning their trip and local followers. Third, an <b>infrastructure platform</b> such as a payment gateway (for example PayPal or WiPay) or a hosted online shop would let her take secure payments so visitors can reorder after they return home.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The <b>product photo</b> should be scaled to about 1600 px wide and exported as a <b>JPEG</b> at quality 80. JPEG's lossy compression keeps photos looking good while cutting the file from several megabytes to a few hundred kilobytes, so it loads quickly on phones. The <b>logo</b> should be a <b>PNG</b>, because it is lossless, keeping the edges and text crisp, and it supports a transparent background so the logo sits cleanly on any colour. The <b>WAV jingle</b> should be exported as an <b>MP3</b> at 192 kbps: a 2-minute WAV is about 20 MB, while the MP3 is about 3 MB and plays on almost every device; she should keep the WAV as her master. The <b>price list</b> should be exported as a <b>PDF</b>, because it fixes the layout and embeds the fonts, so it looks the same and prints correctly whether a customer opens it on a phone or a laptop.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Sharing</b> the video means sending people a link (for example on WhatsApp) that takes them away to YouTube to watch it, <b>whereas embedding</b> means pasting YouTube's embed code into her website so the video plays inside her own page. Embedding keeps visitors on her site while YouTube's servers handle the streaming.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> One issue is <b>fonts</b>: if she sends the Word price list, a customer without her font will see a different font and a broken layout. She can solve this by sending a PDF with embedded fonts. A second issue is <b>video format and orientation</b>: if her phone records HEVC .mov files, some Android phones and Windows PCs may not play them, and a landscape video looks small on vertical phone screens. She should export an H.264 MP4 and create a vertical 9:16 version for social media, then test it on a cheap Android phone and a laptop before posting.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Format questions are easy marks if you always write <b>format + reason</b>. Name the compression type (lossy or lossless) and the practical effect (smaller file, crisp edges, transparency, fixed layout). For \"distinguish\", use one sentence with \"whereas\" and cover both terms.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Platforms and formats for a Caribbean business (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: platforms and formats, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO platforms Ms. Liburd could use and <b>state</b> what each would do for her business. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the format for her product photo and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the format for her logo and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the format for her price list and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE compatibility problem she might have and ONE way to fix it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> She could use ... to ... She could also use ... to ...<br><b>(b)</b> The product photo should be a ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> The logo should be a ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> The price list should be a ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> One problem is ... She could fix it by ...</p>","checklist":["I named two platforms and what each does for the business.","I chose JPEG (or another sensible format) for the photo, with a reason.","I chose PNG for the logo, with a reason.","I chose PDF for the price list, with a reason.","I gave one compatibility problem and a fix."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two platforms","marks":2,"look":["google|search|instagram|facebook|tiktok|youtube|whatsapp|shop|etsy|amazon|website","find|sell|post|reach|show|order|book"],"min":2,"strong":"Two platforms with what each does.","weak":"Name two platforms, such as Google (so visitors find the shop) and Instagram (to post product photos)."},{"criterion":"Photo format","marks":2,"look":["jpe?g","small|size|fast|load|photo|compress"],"min":2,"strong":"JPEG with a sound reason.","weak":"Choose JPEG, because it makes photos small so they load quickly on phones."},{"criterion":"Logo format","marks":2,"look":["png","transparen|background|sharp|crisp|edge"],"min":2,"strong":"PNG with a sound reason.","weak":"Choose PNG, because it can have a transparent background and keeps edges sharp."},{"criterion":"Price list format","marks":2,"look":["pdf","layout|print|same|any device|font|fixed"],"min":2,"strong":"PDF with a sound reason.","weak":"Choose PDF, because it looks the same on every device and prints correctly."},{"criterion":"Compatibility problem and fix","marks":2,"look":["font|heic|word|file name|phone|android|iphone|open|play|codec|mov","fix|export|convert|send|use|test|rename"],"min":2,"strong":"A real compatibility problem with a practical fix.","weak":"For example: a customer without Word cannot open the price list, so export it as a PDF."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> She could use Google, so cruise visitors searching for sweets near the port can find her shop. She could also use Instagram to post photos and short videos of her fudge.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The product photo should be a JPEG, because it makes the photo much smaller so it loads quickly on visitors' phones.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The logo should be a PNG, because it can have a transparent background, so the white box disappears, and it keeps the edges sharp.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The price list should be a PDF, because it looks the same on every phone and computer and prints correctly.</p><p><b>(e)</b> One problem is that customers without Word cannot open the price list properly. She could fix it by exporting it as a PDF.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the platform or format and 1 for a reason that fits the business. Accept other sensible platforms in (a). Push students to use the word \"because\" every time; a format with no reason earns only 1 mark. Move them on to the Core task when they can add a technical word (lossy, lossless, transparent, compressed) to each reason.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Platforms and formats for a Caribbean business (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: platforms and formats, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Fort Street Fudge and Coconut Co.</b> in Basseterre, St. Kitts, sells handmade fudge and coconut treats to cruise visitors and local residents. The owner, Ms. Liburd, has: a 4000 × 3000 px photo of her products; her logo on a white background; a 2-minute WAV recording of a radio jingle (CD quality); a 3-minute landscape 4K video of the shop recorded on an iPhone (HEVC .mov); and a price list typed in Word with a decorative font. She wants to sell online, appear when visitors search for sweets near the cruise port, reach people on their phones (many cruise visitors are on expensive roaming data) and print a small catalogue at a local print shop. Two regular customers use screen readers.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Recommend</b> a platform strategy using FOUR different platform types, with an example of each, and <b>evaluate</b> ONE limitation of your strategy. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>For ALL FIVE files, <b>specify</b> the delivery format and key settings (size, quality or bitrate) and <b>justify</b> each with reference to compression, colour depth or file size. Include at least ONE file-size calculation. <i>(8 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> embedding versus sharing the shop video, using her website and WhatsApp as examples. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> THREE cross-platform compatibility or accessibility issues, giving a fix for each and a plan for testing before launch. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Justify</b> which single change would bring her the most benefit in the first month. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":800,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Search engine: ... Social or media platform: ... Marketplace or infrastructure: ... Fourth type: ... One limitation is ...<br><b>(b)</b> Photo: ... because ... (calculation: ...) Logo: ... Jingle: ... Video: ... Price list: ...<br><b>(c)</b> Embedding ... whereas sharing ... On balance ...<br><b>(d)</b> Issue 1: ... Fix: ... Issue 2: ... Issue 3: ... Testing plan: ...<br><b>(e)</b> The most valuable first change is ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I recommended four different platform types with examples and evaluated a limitation.","I specified format and settings for all five files, each with a technical justification.","I included at least one correct file-size calculation.","I evaluated embedding against sharing for both the website and WhatsApp.","I covered three compatibility or accessibility issues, each with a fix, plus a testing plan.","I justified one priority change for the first month.","Every part refers to the business, its cruise visitors, local customers or the print shop."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Platform strategy and limitation","marks":5,"look":["search engine|google","social|instagram|facebook|tiktok|media platform|youtube","marketplace|shop|etsy|amazon|infrastructure|payment|paypal|wipay|cloud","app|knowledge|crowd|whatsapp|website|curated","limitation|however|risk|weakness|depend"],"min":4,"strong":"Four distinct platform types, well matched to the audience, with a real limitation evaluated.","weak":"Use four different types (for example search engine, social, media and infrastructure), give an example of each and evaluate one weakness, such as dependence on a platform's rules or fees."},{"criterion":"Format specifications and justification","marks":8,"look":["jpe?g","png|transparen","mp3|kbps","mp4|h\\.?264","pdf","lossy|lossless|compress","mb|kb|×|x \\d|calculat","px|quality|bitrate|1080|1600|resolution"],"min":6,"strong":"All five files specified with accurate settings, technical reasons and a correct calculation.","weak":"Give format and settings for all five files, explain each with compression, colour depth or size, and show one calculation (for example 4000 × 3000 × 3 bytes ≈ 36 MB raw)."},{"criterion":"Embedding versus sharing","marks":3,"look":["embed","shar|link|whatsapp","whereas|however|on balance|but"],"min":3,"strong":"A balanced evaluation of both methods in both contexts.","weak":"Explain what each method does, give an advantage and a drawback of each, and say which suits the website and which suits WhatsApp."},{"criterion":"Compatibility, accessibility and testing","marks":6,"look":["heic|hevc|mov|codec","font|embed","screen reader|alt text|caption|accessib|text.?based","android|iphone|browser|device|laptop","test|check|try","fix|convert|export|add"],"min":4,"strong":"Three issues evaluated with practical fixes and a realistic test plan.","weak":"Choose three issues (for example HEVC video, the decorative font, screen-reader access), give a fix for each and say which devices you will test on before launch."},{"criterion":"Justified priority","marks":3,"look":["first|priority|most|single","because|so that|therefore","visitor|cruise|search|find|sales|customer"],"min":2,"strong":"One clear priority, justified by its effect on sales or reach.","weak":"Pick the one change with the biggest early effect and explain why, using the cruise visitors or sales."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A platform is the medium that carries content to its audience, so the strategy follows where her customers already are. <b>Search engine:</b> a Google Business Profile with photos, hours and a map pin, so cruise visitors searching \"sweets near Port Zante\" find her before they walk ashore. <b>Social platform:</b> Instagram for short Reels of fudge being made, reaching visitors planning their trip and local followers. <b>Media platform:</b> a YouTube channel to host the shop video, which she can embed on her site. <b>Infrastructure platform:</b> a hosted online shop with a payment gateway such as WiPay or PayPal, so visitors can reorder from home. <i>Limitation:</i> every platform is controlled by someone else. Fees, algorithm changes or account suspensions could cut her reach overnight, so she should keep her own website and an email list as a base she controls.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Product photo:</i> raw, it is 4000 × 3000 × 3 bytes ≈ 36 MB. Scaled to 1600 × 1200 px it becomes about 5.8 MB raw, and as a quality-80 JPEG a few hundred kilobytes. Lossy JPEG compression suits a continuous-tone photo and protects visitors' roaming data. For the print catalogue, she should send the full-resolution image in the PDF instead. <i>Logo:</i> PNG-32 (24-bit colour plus an 8-bit alpha channel), with the white background removed, so the logo sits cleanly on any colour with crisp, lossless edges; SVG as well if her site supports it. <i>Jingle:</i> a CD-quality WAV is about 1,411 kbps, so 2 minutes ≈ 21 MB; an MP3 at 192 kbps is about 2.9 MB and plays everywhere. Lossy perceptual coding removes sounds listeners are unlikely to notice. She keeps the WAV as the master. <i>Video:</i> H.264 MP4 at 1080p with AAC audio, plus a 1080 × 1920 vertical version for Reels; this is far smaller than 4K, and H.264 plays on older Android phones that cannot decode HEVC. <i>Price list:</i> a text-based PDF with embedded fonts and compressed images, so the decorative font survives, it prints the same at the print shop and screen readers can read it.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Embedding</b> the YouTube video on her website keeps visitors on her page while YouTube's servers handle the streaming, but if the video is removed the embed breaks, and YouTube may show other channels' suggestions afterwards. <b>Sharing</b> a link on WhatsApp is ideal for customers who message her, but it sends them away from her shop to YouTube. On balance, she should embed on the website, where she wants sales, and share the link on WhatsApp, where people expect links.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Issue 1, video codec:</i> HEVC .mov files will not play on some Windows PCs and older Android phones; fix by exporting H.264 MP4. <i>Issue 2, fonts:</i> the Word file will substitute a plain font on other computers and break the layout; fix by exporting a PDF with embedded fonts. <i>Issue 3, accessibility:</i> her screen-reader customers cannot read images of text; fix with a text-based PDF, alt text on every product photo and captions on the video. <i>Testing plan:</i> before launch, open every page and file on a budget Android phone, an iPhone, a Windows laptop and with a screen reader turned on, and send the catalogue PDF to the print shop for a proof.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> The single most valuable change in the first month is the Google Business Profile with good photos and accurate hours, because cruise visitors have only a few hours ashore and decide where to go by searching on their phones. Being found is the step every sale depends on, and it costs almost nothing.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 5 + 8 + 3 + 6 + 3). Check calculations: 4000 × 3000 × 3 ≈ 36 MB raw; a CD-quality WAV is about 10 MB per minute; 192 kbps MP3 is about 1.4 MB per minute. Grade One responses specify settings (pixel width, quality, bitrate, codec), not just format names, and every justification links the technical property to a real customer: roaming data, older phones, the print shop or screen-reader users.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U5","title":"Audio and video production principles","objectives":["Apply basic image manipulation, editing and compositing techniques (layers, selections, masks, levels, clone and heal, export) in GIMP, Photoshop or Canva.","Record, manipulate and mix sound on multiple tracks in Audacity, and export in MP3, WAV or OGG, explaining when to use each.","Capture video with phones, tablets and cameras, using tripods or gimbals, and record clean sound indoors and outdoors with suitable microphones.","Edit video with cuts, transitions, basic effects, colour grading and text overlays, and light scenes with natural light and three-point lighting.","Export MP4 video with settings suited to YouTube, Instagram and the web.","Evaluate cloud-based authoring tools such as Figma, WeVideo, Canva, Adobe Express, Google Workspace and CapCut.","Plan, capture, edit and publish video using the key terms: the language of media, pre-production documents, the exposure triangle, audio levels and the Clean, Balance, Layer, Master mix, cutting principles and transitions, export settings, file management, licensing and publishing."],"videos":[{"title":"GIMP layer masks for beginners","q":"GIMP layer mask tutorial beginners remove background"},{"title":"Audacity noise reduction and normalise","q":"Audacity noise reduction normalize tutorial beginners voice recording"},{"title":"Three-point lighting explained","q":"three point lighting explained key fill back light beginners"},{"title":"OpenShot editing basics","q":"OpenShot video editor tutorial for beginners cut transitions titles export"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Image manipulation: layers, selections and masks","html":"<p>Image manipulation is the base of every poster, thumbnail and video title you will make. Three ideas make professional work possible, and they work the same way in <b>GIMP</b> (free, offline, allowed in Paper 02), <b>Photoshop</b> and, in a simpler form, <b>Canva</b>.</p>\n<p><b>1. Layers.</b> A layer is a transparent sheet stacked over others. Keep the background photo, text, logo and effects on separate layers so each can be moved or changed without damaging the rest. In GIMP open the Layers dialog with <b>Windows &gt; Dockable Dialogs &gt; Layers</b> (Ctrl+L); add a layer with <b>Layer &gt; New Layer</b>. Higher layers cover lower ones.</p>\n<p><b>2. Selections.</b> A selection limits an edit to one area. Choose the tool for the shape:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>GIMP tool (shortcut)</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rectangle (R) / Ellipse (E) Select</td><td>Boxes, frames, circular crops</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free Select / Lasso (F)</td><td>Hand-drawn or straight-edged shapes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fuzzy Select / Magic Wand (U)</td><td>Areas of similar colour, such as a plain sky over Belmopan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Select by Colour (Shift+O)</td><td>Every pixel of one colour across the image</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreground Select / Paths (B)</td><td>Complex edges such as hair or a person cut out from a background</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Photoshop adds <b>Select Subject</b> and the <b>Object Selection</b> tool, which use AI to find the main subject. <b>Feathering</b> (GIMP: Select &gt; Feather) softens a selection edge so a cut-out does not look pasted on.</p>\n<p><b>3. Layer masks.</b> A mask hides parts of a layer without deleting them: <b>white reveals, black hides, grey is partly see-through</b>. In GIMP: right-click the layer &gt; <b>Add Layer Mask</b> &gt; White (full opacity), then paint black on the mask with a soft brush. Because nothing is erased, you can repaint white to bring detail back. This is <b>non-destructive editing</b>, and examiners like the term.</p>\n<p><b>Correcting the image.</b> <b>Colours &gt; Levels</b> sets the black point, white point and midtones (fix a dull, hazy photo); <b>Colours &gt; Curves</b> gives finer control of contrast; <b>Hue-Saturation</b> adjusts colour strength. The <b>Clone</b> tool (C) copies pixels from a source you Ctrl-click; the <b>Heal</b> tool (H) does the same but blends texture and colour, ideal for removing a stray wire or a blemish. Photoshop's <b>Spot Healing Brush</b> and <b>Content-Aware Fill</b> do this automatically.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, say what you did <b>and why</b>: \"I used a layer mask rather than the eraser so the edit is non-destructive and can be corrected.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Three ideas make photo editing professional. Layers are see-through sheets stacked on top of each other, so you can change one part without spoiling the rest. Selections limit an edit to one area. Layer masks hide parts of a layer without deleting them: white shows, black hides. A designer retouching a photo of Pigeon Island for a St. Lucia brochure uses all three.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key professional idea is <b>non-destructive editing</b>: every change can be adjusted or undone later. A layer mask beats the eraser because hidden pixels can be brought back, and separate layers beat flattening because text or a logo can still be moved after the client's feedback. Choose the selection tool by the edge you need: Fuzzy Select works on flat, even colour but struggles with a cloudy sky or a gradient, where Select by Colour or Paths gives cleaner results. Feathering matters because real edges are slightly soft; a hard cut-out looks pasted on. Know Clone from Heal: Clone copies pixels exactly, which suits repeating texture, while Heal blends colour and texture, which suits small blemishes.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the image tool to the job","intro":"<p>Zara is retouching photos for the Blue Cove Marina brochure in Tortola. Match each GIMP or Photoshop tool to the job it does best.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Fuzzy Select (Magic Wand)","r":"Select a plain blue sky in one click so it can be replaced"},{"l":"Layer mask","r":"Hide the old background behind a sailor without deleting any pixels"},{"l":"Levels","r":"Brighten a hazy, grey-looking harbour photo by setting black and white points"},{"l":"Heal tool","r":"Remove a small stain on a boat's sail and blend it into the cloth texture"},{"l":"Clone tool","r":"Copy a section of water exactly to cover an unwanted floating bottle"},{"l":"Feather","r":"Soften the edge of a cut-out so it blends into the new scene"},{"l":"Hue-Saturation","r":"Make the turquoise water a little richer without changing brightness"},{"l":"Export As","r":"Create the final JPEG for the brochure website from the .xcf project"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose the right tool for selection, masking, correction, retouching and export, the core of image manipulation.","tutor":[{"h":"Select, hide or fix?","p":"<p>Selection tools choose an area; masks hide; Levels and Hue-Saturation correct; Clone and Heal retouch.</p>"},{"h":"Clone vs heal","p":"<p>One copies pixels exactly; the other blends them with the surroundings.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the image tool (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Fuzzy Select","r":"Select a plain sky in one click"},{"l":"Layer mask","r":"Hide part of a layer without deleting it"},{"l":"Levels","r":"Brighten a dull photo"},{"l":"Heal tool","r":"Remove a small blemish smoothly"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the image tool to the job (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: these jobs need you to choose between tools that seem similar. Match each tool to the job it does BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Select by Colour","r":"Pick every pixel of the same red across the whole flag in one step"},{"l":"Free Select (Lasso)","r":"Draw around a straight-edged shop sign by clicking its corner points"},{"l":"Foreground Select","r":"Separate a dancer with flying hair from a busy Carnival background"},{"l":"Layer mask","r":"Hide the crowd behind the singer so it can be brought back if the client asks"},{"l":"Curves","r":"Fine-tune contrast in the shadows without changing the highlights"},{"l":"Clone tool","r":"Copy a patch of brick exactly to repeat the wall pattern over a graffiti tag"},{"l":"Heal tool","r":"Remove a scratch from an old photo while blending its colour and grain"},{"l":"Feather","r":"Soften a cut-out's edge by 2 px so it does not look pasted on"}],"win":"Excellent. You told similar tools apart: global colour selection from contiguous selection, exact cloning from blended healing, and Curves from simpler adjustments."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Compositing step by step","html":"<p><b>Compositing</b> means combining elements from several images into one believable picture: a product placed on a beach, a student \"standing\" in front of Fort Charlotte, a band photo over a Carnival backdrop. A composite works when <b>light direction, colour, scale and perspective</b> match.</p>\n<p><b>GIMP compositing workflow (Paper 02 ready)</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Open the background</b>, for example a photo of Main Street in Road Town: File &gt; Open.</li>\n<li><b>Bring in the subject</b> as a new layer: File &gt; <b>Open as Layers</b> (Ctrl+Alt+O). The original file is not changed.</li>\n<li><b>Add an alpha channel</b> to the subject layer: Layer &gt; Transparency &gt; Add Alpha Channel.</li>\n<li><b>Select the subject</b> with Foreground Select or Paths, then Select &gt; Feather by 1 to 3 px.</li>\n<li><b>Add a layer mask</b> from the selection: Add Layer Mask &gt; Selection. Refine edges by painting black or white on the mask.</li>\n<li><b>Scale and place</b> the subject with the Scale tool (Shift+S) and Move tool (M), keeping proportions linked.</li>\n<li><b>Match the look</b>: use Levels or Curves on the subject layer so its brightness and colour match the background. If the sun comes from the left in the background, the subject's light must too.</li>\n<li><b>Add a shadow</b> on a new layer under the subject: paint soft black, lower the opacity to about 30 to 50 percent.</li>\n<li><b>Add text</b> with the Text tool (T) on its own layer, and save the master: File &gt; Save as <code>.xcf</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Export</b>: File &gt; Export As, <code>.jpg</code> for a photo composite or <code>.png</code> if it needs transparency.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Photoshop</b> follows the same logic, with Select Subject, Select and Mask for hair, and adjustment layers clipped to one layer. <b>Canva</b> is quicker but more limited: upload images, use <b>Edit image &gt; Background Remover</b> (a paid feature at the time of writing; check your plan), arrange items with <b>Position</b> (forward or backward), adjust with <b>Adjust</b>, then <b>Share &gt; Download</b> as PNG, JPEG or PDF. Canva needs an Internet connection, so it cannot be relied on in Paper 02.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Examiners spot bad composites by hard edges, missing shadows and mismatched light. Mention all three when you evaluate your own work.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Compositing means combining parts of several photos into one believable picture, like placing a product on a beach. Bring the subject in as a new layer, cut it out with a selection and a mask, then scale it and match its light and colour to the background. Add a soft shadow so it looks real. A Grenada spice brand could composite its jar onto a market stall.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A composite is judged by whether the viewer believes it, and four things give it away: mismatched <b>light direction</b>, <b>colour</b>, <b>scale</b> and <b>perspective</b>. If the background's sun is low on the left, the subject must be lit from the left and cast a long shadow to the right. Match colour by comparing the darkest and brightest points of both layers with Levels or Curves, not just by eye. Perspective is the hardest: a bottle photographed from above will never sit naturally on a table shot at eye level, so choose source images with matching camera angles before you start. Ethically, a composite in an advert or news story must not mislead; label it as an illustration where viewers could mistake it for a real event.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build a composite in GIMP","intro":"<p>Malik needs a poster that places a sorrel bottle on a Pigeon Point beach photo. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the beach photo as the background (File > Open)."},{"t":"Bring in the bottle photo with File > Open as Layers."},{"t":"Select the bottle and feather the selection by 2 px."},{"t":"Add a layer mask from the selection to hide the bottle's old background."},{"t":"Scale and move the bottle so its size and perspective fit the beach."},{"t":"Use Curves on the bottle layer to match the beach lighting, then add a soft shadow layer."},{"t":"Save the .xcf master, then Export As a JPEG for the poster."}],"win":"Correct. Background, import, select, mask, place, match the light, then save and export: a clean compositing workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Bring it in first","p":"<p>You cannot select the bottle until it is in the document as a layer.</p>"},{"h":"Match last, export at the very end","p":"<p>Colour matching and shadows come after the subject is placed.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build a composite (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the background photo."},{"t":"Open the subject as a new layer."},{"t":"Mask out the subject's old background."},{"t":"Export the final JPEG."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build a composite in GIMP (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: the full eleven-step workflow from the notes. Put every step for Malik's sorrel bottle composite in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the Pigeon Point beach photo as the background (File > Open)."},{"t":"Bring in the bottle photo with File > Open as Layers."},{"t":"Add an alpha channel to the bottle layer (Layer > Transparency > Add Alpha Channel)."},{"t":"Select the bottle with Foreground Select or Paths, then feather the selection by 2 px."},{"t":"Add a layer mask from the selection and refine its edges by painting black or white."},{"t":"Scale and move the bottle with proportions linked so its size and perspective fit the beach."},{"t":"Use Levels or Curves on the bottle layer so its brightness and colour match the beach."},{"t":"Paint a soft shadow on a new layer under the bottle and lower its opacity to about 40%."},{"t":"Add the poster text with the Text tool on its own layer."},{"t":"Save the master as an .xcf file."},{"t":"Export As a JPEG for the poster."}],"win":"Correct. Prepare the layer, select, mask, place, match the light, ground it with a shadow, add text, then save and export: a believable, editable composite."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Sound editing and mixing in Audacity","html":"<p><b>Audacity</b> is a free, open-source audio editor and the tool named in the exam equipment list. You will use it for voice-overs, podcasts, jingles and the soundtrack of your SBA 30-second ad.</p>\n<p><b>Recording technique</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Set the project rate (bottom left) to <b>44,100 Hz</b> for audio-only work or <b>48,000 Hz</b> for video (48 kHz is the broadcast standard).</li>\n<li>Choose the microphone in the Device Toolbar and click <b>Record</b> (R). Aim for the <b>target zone</b>: peaks between <b>-12 dB and -6 dB</b>. This headroom prevents distortion while keeping the signal clear and strong. If the waveform touches the top and the meter turns red, it is <b>clipping</b>, which is unrecoverable in post, so record again with lower input.</li>\n<li>Keep the mic about a hand-span (15 to 20 cm) from the mouth and slightly to the side to avoid \"popping\" Ps and Bs.</li>\n<li>Record <b>5 seconds of silence</b> (room tone) at the start; you will need it for noise reduction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The audio mixing workflow</b>: every mix, from a podcast episode to a Hollywood film, follows a version of four stages. The rule: <b>clean before you balance; balance before you layer; master at the very end.</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Definition</th><th>Audacity 3 steps</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Clean</b></td><td>Remove noise, hum and unwanted sounds</td><td>Select the room tone, <b>Effect &gt; Noise Removal and Repair &gt; Noise Reduction &gt; Get Noise Profile</b>; select the whole track (Ctrl+A) and apply with gentle settings (about 6 to 12 dB). Delete coughs and mistakes. Switch the track to <b>Spectrogram</b> view (track menu) to see clicks and hum</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Balance</b></td><td>Set track levels so dialogue is clear</td><td>Use each track's <b>gain</b> slider and <b>Mute/Solo</b>; <b>Effect &gt; Volume and Compression &gt; Normalize</b> the voice to about -1 dB</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Layer</b></td><td>Add music and effects that support the story</td><td><b>File &gt; Import &gt; Audio</b> (Ctrl+Shift+I) puts each file on its own track; drag clips by their top bar; dip the music under the voice with the <b>Envelope tool</b> or <b>Auto Duck</b>; add <b>Fade In</b> and <b>Fade Out</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Master</b></td><td>Apply limiting to keep output below 0 dB</td><td><b>Effect &gt; Volume and Compression &gt; Limiter</b> (limit to about -1 dB); <b>Loudness Normalization</b> if several clips must sound equally loud; then <b>File &gt; Export Audio</b></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The same jobs can be done in <b>Adobe Audition</b>: the <b>Spectral Frequency Display</b> (see and surgically remove noise), the <b>Noise Reduction effect</b>, the <b>Essential Sound panel</b> (presets for dialogue, music and ambience) and <b>Match Loudness</b> (normalise several clips to the same perceived volume). Audacity's Spectrogram view, Noise Reduction and Loudness Normalization are the free equivalents you can use in Paper 02.</p>\n<p>Save the project as <code>.aup3</code> (your master) before exporting a mixed file.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Normalise is not the same as amplify. Normalise sets the peak to a target level; it will not fix clipping, and it raises any noise along with the voice. That is why Clean comes before Balance.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Audacity is free software for recording and editing sound. Record with peaks between -12 dB and -6 dB so the sound is strong but never distorted. Then mix in four stages: clean the noise, balance the levels, layer music and effects, and master the final loudness. A podcaster in St. Kitts recording in her bedroom would follow the same steps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The order of the four stages matters because each one changes what the next works on. Cleaning first means noise is not boosted when you normalise. Balancing before layering sets the voice at a clear level before music competes with it. Mastering last controls the peak and loudness of the whole mix. A common misconception is that a quiet recording can simply be turned up later; amplifying it raises the noise floor too. Another is that Noise Reduction fixes everything: strong settings make voices sound watery or robotic, so use gentle reduction and prevent noise at the source. Clipping is the one fault that cannot be repaired, which is why headroom while recording is essential.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Audacity quick-fire","intro":"<p>Keisha is recording a 30-second radio-style ad for Johnny Cake Corner in Dangriga. Help her solve each problem.</p>","items":[{"text":"Her waveform is flattened at the top and the meter flashes red. What has happened?","options":["The noise profile is missing","Clipping: the input was too loud","The sample rate is wrong"],"answer":1,"fb":"Clipping happens when the signal is louder than the maximum. The flattened peaks are distortion that cannot be repaired, so she must lower the input level and record again."},{"text":"There is a constant air-conditioner hum behind her voice. What should she do first?","options":["Add a Fade In","Normalise the track","Select a few seconds of hum only, Get Noise Profile, then apply Noise Reduction"],"answer":2,"fb":"Noise Reduction learns the noise from a profile of room tone and removes it from the whole track. Normalising first would make the hum louder."},{"text":"She wants the soca music to dip automatically whenever she speaks.","options":["Change Tempo","Auto Duck","Clone tool"],"answer":1,"fb":"Auto Duck lowers one track (the music) when another track (the voice) has sound. The Envelope tool does the same thing by hand."},{"text":"Her voice is clean but quiet. Which effect brings the loudest peak up to -1 dB?","options":["Fade Out","Normalize","Noise Reduction"],"answer":1,"fb":"Normalize raises (or lowers) the whole track so the loudest peak reaches the target, without clipping."},{"text":"How should the music end so it does not stop abruptly?","options":["Delete the last 2 seconds","Export it as OGG","Select the last 2 seconds and apply Fade Out"],"answer":2,"fb":"A fade out reduces the volume smoothly to silence, which sounds professional."},{"text":"She needs to edit again next week. What should she save?","options":["Only the MP3","Nothing; Audacity remembers automatically","The .aup3 project file as the master, plus the exported MP3"],"answer":2,"fb":"The .aup3 project keeps the separate tracks and edits. An MP3 is a mixed, lossy file that is hard to re-edit."}],"win":"Excellent. You can record cleanly, remove noise, balance tracks and protect your master, the sound editing skills Paper 02 tests.","tutor":[{"h":"Order of operations","p":"<p>Record well, remove noise, then normalise, then mix and fade.</p>"},{"h":"Two tracks, one volume","p":"<p>Look for the effect that lowers one track because of another.</p>"},{"h":"Masters","p":"<p>Keep the editable project; export copies for delivery.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Audacity quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A red meter and flattened waveform mean...","options":["clipping","silence"],"answer":0,"fb":"The input was too loud and the sound is distorted."},{"text":"To remove a steady hum, use...","options":["Noise Reduction","Fade In"],"answer":0,"fb":"Noise Reduction uses a profile of the hum to remove it."},{"text":"To end music smoothly, use...","options":["Fade Out","Delete"],"answer":0,"fb":"A fade out lowers the volume gradually."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Audacity quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder problems from Keisha's next recording sessions. Think about why each fix works.</p>","items":[{"text":"Keisha's peaks sit at about -30 dB. She plans to Normalize later. What is the risk?","options":["Normalize will cause clipping","The file will turn into an MP3","Raising the level also raises the background noise, so she should record closer or with more gain","None, because Normalize fixes everything"],"answer":2,"fb":"Normalize lifts everything, including the noise floor. Get a strong, clean signal at the source."},{"text":"Which sequence follows the mixing rule?","options":["Master, Layer, Balance, Clean","Layer, Clean, Master, Balance","Clean, Balance, Layer, Master","Balance, Clean, Master, Layer"],"answer":2,"fb":"Clean before you balance, balance before you layer, master at the very end."},{"text":"After Noise Reduction at 30 dB, her voice sounds watery and robotic. What should she do?","options":["Normalize to 0 dB","Export as OGG","Apply it a second time","Undo and use a gentler setting (about 6 to 12 dB), then reduce the noise at the source next time"],"answer":3,"fb":"Heavy noise reduction damages the voice. Gentle settings and a quieter room work better."},{"text":"A car horn sounds once in the middle of a key sentence. What is the BEST fix?","options":["Re-record the sentence, or replace it with a clean take","Add a Fade In","Noise Reduction on the whole track","Apply a Limiter at -1 dB"],"answer":0,"fb":"Noise Reduction handles steady noise, not a sudden horn overlapping speech."},{"text":"Which view shows clicks and hum as visible marks, so they can be found precisely?","options":["Waveform view zoomed out","Auto Duck","Change Tempo","Spectrogram view"],"answer":3,"fb":"The spectrogram shows frequencies over time, so a hum appears as a line and a click as a spike."},{"text":"Two adverts both peak at -1 dB, but one sounds much louder. What should she use?","options":["Fade Out","Normalize again","Noise Reduction","Loudness Normalization, which matches perceived loudness"],"answer":3,"fb":"Peak level is not the same as perceived loudness. Loudness Normalization matches how loud they sound."},{"text":"Why is a Limiter applied at the very END of the mix?","options":["To remove hum","To add echo","To stop the combined voice, music and effects peaking above 0 dB on export","To change the sample rate"],"answer":2,"fb":"Only after all the tracks are layered can you control the final, combined peak."}],"win":"Excellent. You can explain why the mixing order matters, when noise reduction helps or harms, and the difference between peak and perceived loudness."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Audio export formats: MP3, WAV and OGG","html":"<p>There are three audio export formats to know. In Audacity, choose them in <b>File &gt; Export Audio</b> under Format. You must know how they differ and <b>when to use each</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>WAV (.wav)</th><th>MP3 (.mp3)</th><th>OGG (.ogg, Ogg Vorbis)</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Compression</b></td><td>Usually uncompressed PCM: every sample is kept</td><td>Lossy (perceptual coding)</td><td>Lossy (perceptual coding)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Approximate size</b></td><td>About 10 MB per minute (stereo, 44.1 kHz, 16-bit)</td><td>About 1 MB per minute at 128 kbps; about 2.4 MB at 320 kbps</td><td>Similar to MP3 or slightly smaller for the same quality</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Quality</b></td><td>Full quality; best for editing and archiving</td><td>Good to excellent at 192 to 320 kbps; detail lost permanently</td><td>Often slightly better than MP3 at low bitrates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Licensing</b></td><td>Widely supported standard</td><td>Patents have expired; universal support</td><td>Open and royalty-free</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Compatibility</b></td><td>Plays almost everywhere; large for the web</td><td>Plays on almost every device, browser, car stereo and platform</td><td>Strong in browsers, Android and games; weaker support on some Apple devices and older players</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Best for</b></td><td>Masters, studio work, importing into video editors, radio station delivery, the final exam file if quality is specified</td><td>Podcasts, sharing music, WhatsApp, websites, audio for most clients</td><td>Game sound effects and music (engines such as Godot and Unity support it), open-source projects, web audio with a fallback</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Rule of thumb:</b> record and edit in <b>WAV</b> (or the .aup3 project), deliver in <b>MP3</b>, and choose <b>OGG</b> when an open, royalty-free format is required or for games. Never edit and re-export an MP3 repeatedly: each lossy export loses more quality.</p>\n<p>Bitrate matters too. Speech for a podcast sounds fine at 96 to 128 kbps mono; music for a Jamaican dancehall producer's demo needs 256 to 320 kbps stereo. A radio station such as a local FM station in Antigua may ask for WAV so it can process the audio itself.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"When to use each\" questions need the format, the situation and the reason: \"WAV for the master, because it is uncompressed and keeps full quality for later editing.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Know three audio formats. WAV keeps every detail, so it is used for masters and editing, but the files are big. MP3 is compressed and small and plays almost everywhere, so it suits podcasts and sharing. OGG is also compressed, but open and free to use, which suits games. A soca producer in Trinidad would edit in WAV and send fans an MP3.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each lossy export removes information permanently, so the professional chain is always <b>WAV (or the project file) for editing, lossy for delivery</b>. If a client sends an MP3 and asks for changes, re-exporting it as MP3 compounds the loss, so ask for the original. Bitrate depends on the content: speech survives low bitrates well, while music with cymbals and steel pan harmonics reveals artefacts quickly. Compatibility can outweigh quality. OGG is often slightly better than MP3 at low bitrates, but weaker support on some Apple devices makes MP3 the safer choice for a public website. Always match the receiver's stated requirement: a radio station's specification beats your preference.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"MP3, WAV or OGG?","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has several audio jobs this week. Sort each into the most suitable export format.</p>","main":"Which audio format suits this job best?","bins":["WAV","MP3","OGG"],"items":[{"t":"The master voice-over for the SBA ad, to be imported into the video editor","bin":0,"fb":"Uncompressed WAV keeps full quality, so nothing is lost before the video editor compresses the final export."},{"t":"A jingle a client will send to friends on WhatsApp","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 is compact and opens on almost any phone."},{"t":"Background music for an open-source web project that must avoid any patented format","bin":2,"fb":"Ogg Vorbis is an open, royalty-free format designed for exactly this kind of project."},{"t":"Sound effects for Jerome's Android game built in Godot","bin":2,"fb":"OGG is open, royalty-free and well supported by game engines and Android."},{"t":"A studio recording of a steel pan band to be archived at full quality","bin":0,"fb":"Archiving needs every sample kept. WAV is uncompressed."},{"t":"A music track for a Barbados restaurant's website that must play on iPhones and every browser","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 has the widest compatibility, including Apple devices, where OGG support is weaker."},{"t":"A radio ad delivered to a local FM station that asked for uncompressed audio","bin":0,"fb":"If the client specifies uncompressed audio, deliver WAV."},{"t":"A weekly podcast episode about Guyanese history for listeners on mobile data","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 is small and plays on every podcast app and phone, which keeps data costs down for listeners."}],"win":"Well sorted. WAV for masters and full quality, MP3 for wide compatibility and small files, OGG for open, royalty-free and game audio.","tutor":[{"h":"Quality or size?","p":"<p>If full quality matters most, think uncompressed.</p>"},{"h":"Open and games","p":"<p>One format is open, royalty-free and loved by game engines.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A master recording at full quality","bin":0,"fb":"WAV is uncompressed."},{"t":"A podcast for phones","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 is small and plays everywhere."},{"t":"Game sound effects in an open-source engine","bin":2,"fb":"OGG is open and game-friendly."},{"t":"A jingle shared on WhatsApp","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 is compact and compatible."}]},
 "up": {"title":"MP3, WAV or OGG? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: each job has a constraint (a specification, a device, a licence or further editing). Sort each into the BEST format.</p>","main":"Which audio format suits this job best?","bins":["WAV","MP3","OGG"],"items":[{"t":"Looping ambient sounds for a Godot game that must avoid patented formats","bin":2,"fb":"OGG is open, royalty-free and well supported by game engines."},{"t":"A 40-minute speech podcast for listeners on prepaid data","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 keeps speech clear at small sizes and plays in every podcast app."},{"t":"Background music on a public website visited mostly on iPhones","bin":1,"fb":"MP3 has the widest support, including Apple devices where OGG support is weaker."},{"t":"A jingle for a radio station whose spec sheet says \"uncompressed, 48 kHz\"","bin":0,"fb":"The client's specification decides: deliver uncompressed WAV at 48 kHz."},{"t":"Sound effects for an Android game made in Unity","bin":2,"fb":"OGG is a strong, efficient choice for game audio on Android."},{"t":"A voice-over master that the video editor will import and compress later","bin":0,"fb":"Uncompressed WAV avoids losing quality before the final video compression."},{"t":"A demo track emailed to a client who will play it on her car stereo","bin":1,"fb":"Car stereos almost always play MP3."},{"t":"Audio for an open-source learning web app, with an MP3 fallback for Apple devices","bin":2,"fb":"The open format is the main file; the MP3 fallback covers weaker support."},{"t":"A client's original recording that may be re-edited many times","bin":0,"fb":"Repeated editing needs a lossless master, so nothing is lost each time."}],"win":"Excellent. You let the constraint decide: masters and specifications point to WAV, reach and compatibility to MP3, and open, royalty-free or game audio to OGG."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The language of media","html":"<p>The full media production pipeline has four verbs: <b>Plan</b> (concepts, scripts and production outlines before a single frame is captured), <b>Capture</b> (operate cameras and microphones to record high-quality video and audio), <b>Edit</b> (assemble footage, mix audio and shape the story) and <b>Publish</b> (export and distribute the finished work). These match <b>pre-production, production and post-production</b>, plus distribution.</p>\n<p>Before you pick up a camera, you need to understand how media communicates: shot composition, sequencing, narrative structure and the relationship between image and sound.</p>\n<p><b>Storytelling fundamentals: why story comes first.</b> Before any technical decision, know what you want your audience to <b>feel, think or do</b>. Story gives every shot a purpose.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Beginning, middle and end</b>: every piece needs structure, even a 30-second ad.</li>\n<li><b>Conflict</b> drives engagement: a vendor in Castries whose stall is wrecked by rain, then rebuilt.</li>\n<li><b>Point of view</b> shapes how audiences connect: whose eyes are we seeing through?</li>\n<li><b>Pacing</b> controls energy and emotional rhythm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Visual composition basics</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 135\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:300px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rule of thirds grid: a frame divided into nine equal parts with the subject placed on a line intersection\">\n<rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"236\" height=\"131\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"2\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"133\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"2\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"133\"/><line x1=\"2\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"238\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"2\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"238\" y2=\"90\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"45\" r=\"9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"172\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">subject on an intersection</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rule of thirds</b>: divide the frame into a 3 by 3 grid and place subjects along the lines or intersections for natural, pleasing composition. Put the horizon of Grand Anse on the lower third, not in the middle.</li>\n<li><b>Leading lines</b>: roads, fences or architectural elements guide the eye to the subject: the waterfront railing leading to a presenter, or the runway at V. C. Bird International Airport leading to a landing plane.</li>\n<li><b>Depth and layering</b>: foreground, midground and background add dimension and context that flat compositions lack (sea grapes in the foreground, a fisherman in the middle, Nevis on the horizon).</li>\n<li><b>Negative space</b>: empty space around the subject draws attention to what matters most. Less can absolutely be more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Standard shot types</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Shot</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wide / establishing shot</b></td><td>Sets the scene, orients the viewer, shows the environment and scale of the story world</td><td>St. George's and the Carenage from Fort George</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Medium shot</b></td><td>The workhorse of video production: shows body language and facial expression at the same time</td><td>A chef at Spice Lane Grill, waist up, talking while she cooks</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Close-up</b></td><td>Draws attention to emotion, detail or significance; creates intimacy and intensity</td><td>Her hands brushing tamarind glaze on ribs</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you evaluate a shot in Paper 02, name the composition technique <b>and</b> its effect: \"The leading line of the waterfront railing guides the viewer's eye to the presenter, so attention lands on her immediately.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Video tells stories with pictures and sound. Every piece, even a 30-second ad, needs a beginning, middle and end. Composition guides the eye: the rule of thirds, leading lines, layers of depth and empty space. Shots set the distance: wide shots show the place, medium shots show body language and close-ups show emotion. A Barbados tourism video might open wide on the Bathsheba coast.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Shots are a vocabulary, and sequencing them is the grammar. A classic pattern moves from wide to medium to close-up, guiding viewers from \"where are we?\" to \"who is this?\" to \"what do they feel?\". Breaking the pattern, for example opening on an extreme close-up, creates mystery because viewers must wait for the context. Story structure applies to promotional work too: a 30-second ad with a small conflict, such as rain threatening a food festival, holds attention better than a list of features. Sound and image work together, and music can even contradict a picture to create irony. When you analyse, explain how a shot choice serves the story's purpose, not just what the shot is.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Composition and shot types","intro":"<p>Malik is planning shots for a St. Kitts tourism Reel using the correct terms. Match each term to the shot or decision it describes.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Rule of thirds","r":"Placing the sunset horizon on the lower grid line and the boat on an intersection"},{"l":"Leading lines","r":"Using the runway markings at Robert L. Bradshaw International Airport to guide the eye towards a landing jet"},{"l":"Depth and layering","r":"Sea grapes in the foreground, a fisherman in the middle and Nevis on the horizon"},{"l":"Negative space","r":"A lone kayaker with wide, empty turquoise water around her"},{"l":"Wide / establishing shot","r":"Opening on all of Basseterre and its bay so viewers know where they are"},{"l":"Medium shot","r":"The tour guide from the waist up, so we see her gestures and her face"},{"l":"Close-up","r":"Only the guide's smiling eyes as she spots a turtle"}],"win":"Well matched. You can name the composition techniques and shot types, the creative vocabulary of video production.","tutor":[{"h":"Grid, line, layers or space?","p":"<p>Ask what is guiding the eye: a grid, a line, several layers, or emptiness.</p>"},{"h":"How much of the person?","p":"<p>All of the place, the body from the waist up, or one detail?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Composition and shot types (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Rule of thirds","r":"Placing the subject on a 3 by 3 grid line"},{"l":"Leading lines","r":"A road that guides the eye to the subject"},{"l":"Wide shot","r":"Shows the whole location and sets the scene"},{"l":"Close-up","r":"Shows emotion or a small detail"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Composition and shot types (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: nine terms, including two storytelling ideas, described by their effect. Match each term to the decision it describes.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Wide / establishing shot","r":"Opens the video so viewers know they are in St. George's harbour before any person appears"},{"l":"Medium shot","r":"Shows the vendor's gestures and face together as she explains her recipe"},{"l":"Close-up","r":"Holds on a single tear as the grandmother remembers the hurricane"},{"l":"Leading lines","r":"The pier's planks draw the eye straight to the fisherman at the end"},{"l":"Negative space","r":"The empty sky around a lone kite makes it feel small and free"},{"l":"Depth and layering","r":"Sea grapes in front, a cricketer in the middle and hills behind make the frame feel deep"},{"l":"Rule of thirds","r":"The surfer sits on the right vertical line, with the wave filling the rest of the frame"},{"l":"Conflict","r":"A rainstorm threatens the food festival, giving the 30-second ad a problem to solve"},{"l":"Point of view","r":"We see the market through the eyes of a child holding her mother's hand"}],"win":"Excellent. You linked composition, shot types and storytelling ideas to the effect each one has on the audience."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Pre-production documents and script formats","html":"<p>Put simply: <b>planning saves everything</b>. The best productions are planned, not improvised. Pre-production is where you make every creative decision on paper, so that on shoot day your team can focus on execution rather than problem-solving. Time spent in pre-production is returned many times over on set. (Module 1 Unit 1 covers the wider pre-production process.)</p>\n<p><b>Key pre-production documents</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Document</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Script</b></td><td>The complete written blueprint of the video: dialogue, action lines, scene descriptions and transitions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Storyboard</b></td><td>Visual panels that represent each planned shot; maps the visual flow before you set foot on location</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shot list</b></td><td>A detailed, numbered list of every shot you need, with notes on angle, lens and movement</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Production schedule</b></td><td>A time-blocked plan of the shooting day: locations, crew roles and estimated time per scene or setup</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Writing for video: two script formats</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Two-column (A/V) script</th><th>Screenplay format</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Layout</td><td><b>Video</b> descriptions in the left column; <b>audio</b> (narration, interviews, sound effects) in the right</td><td><b>Scene headings</b> (INT. or EXT., place, time), <b>action lines</b>, and <b>centred character dialogue</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Informational video, documentary, news-style and corporate content, ads</td><td>Short films, narrative projects, drama, scripted content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Caribbean example</td><td>An electricity company's hurricane-preparedness explainer: shots of shutters on the left, narrator on the right</td><td>A short film set at a Gouyave fish fry where two friends argue about leaving the island</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A two-column line for your SBA 30-second ad might read: <b>VIDEO</b> \"CU: hands drop plastic bottle in the school recycling bin\" | <b>AUDIO</b> \"VO: Every bottle counts. SFX: clunk.\" A screenplay line reads: <code>EXT. FREDERICK STREET, DAY.</code> then an action line, then the character's name centred above the dialogue.</p>\n<p><b>Location scouting checklist</b> (visit before shoot day)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ambient sound</b>: listen for traffic, air-conditioning, construction or echo. What sounds acceptable to the human ear can be very disruptive on a microphone. Near an airport, note the landing times.</li>\n<li><b>Lighting conditions</b>: visit at the same time of day you plan to shoot; note sun direction, window positions and power outlets.</li>\n<li><b>Space and access</b>: confirm you have <b>permission</b> to film there, and enough room for camera, crew and lights to move freely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA proposal and progress marks reward evidence of planning. Include a script (two-column suits an ad), a storyboard, a shot list and a schedule, and say which format you chose and why.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Good videos are planned on paper first. The script is the written plan of words and actions. The storyboard shows each shot as a drawing. The shot list numbers every shot needed. The schedule says who films what, where and when. Use a two-column script for ads and documentaries, and screenplay format for drama, such as a short film set in a Tobago village.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The documents form a chain, and each one solves a different problem. The script fixes the content, the storyboard fixes the look and order, the shot list makes shoot day efficient (shots are often filmed out of sequence to avoid moving the lights) and the schedule allocates time, crew and locations. Choosing a script format is a justification point: an A/V script lets a client see exactly which picture matches each line of narration, which is why advertisers prefer it, while screenplay format gives actors and directors the scene structure that drama needs. Location scouting is often skipped, yet sounds and light that we normally ignore, such as air-conditioning or a west-facing window at 4 p.m., can ruin a shoot.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay?","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has several productions in pre-production. Sort each feature or project into the script format it belongs to.</p>","main":"Which script format is this?","bins":["Two-column (A/V) script","Screenplay format"],"items":[{"t":"The SBA 30-second recycling ad with a voice-over and on-screen text","bin":0,"fb":"An ad with narration matched to shots is best planned in two columns."},{"t":"Character names and dialogue centred on the page","bin":1,"fb":"Centred dialogue under the character's name is how screenplays present speech."},{"t":"Video descriptions on the left, narration and sound effects on the right","bin":0,"fb":"Splitting picture (A for audio, V for video) into two columns is the defining feature of the A/V script."},{"t":"Scene headings such as INT. KITCHEN, NIGHT","bin":1,"fb":"INT. and EXT. scene headings are standard screenplay format."},{"t":"A documentary on the history of Anguilla's salt ponds with narration and interviews","bin":0,"fb":"Documentary and informational content usually uses the two-column format."},{"t":"Action lines describing what a character does","bin":1,"fb":"Action lines are a screenplay element that describe movement and setting."},{"t":"A corporate video for a Jolly Harbour marina showing its services","bin":0,"fb":"Corporate and informational videos pair shots with voice-over, which the A/V layout shows clearly."},{"t":"A 10-minute drama about a family preparing for a hurricane","bin":1,"fb":"Narrative and dramatic content with characters and scenes uses screenplay format."}],"win":"Well sorted. Two-column (A/V) scripts suit informational, documentary and ad content; screenplay format suits narrative drama.","tutor":[{"h":"Columns or scenes?","p":"<p>One format splits the page into picture and sound; the other is organised by scene headings and dialogue.</p>"},{"h":"Story or information?","p":"<p>Is it drama with characters, or information with a narrator?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"A/V script or screenplay? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Video on the left, audio on the right","bin":0,"fb":"That is the two-column A/V layout."},{"t":"INT. and EXT. scene headings","bin":1,"fb":"Scene headings belong to screenplays."},{"t":"A news-style documentary","bin":0,"fb":"Documentaries usually use A/V scripts."},{"t":"A short drama film","bin":1,"fb":"Narrative drama uses screenplay format."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Two-column (A/V) script or screenplay? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin. Some lines belong to OTHER pre-production documents (the shot list, the storyboard or the production schedule), not to either script format.</p>","main":"Where does this belong?","bins":["Two-column (A/V) script","Screenplay format","Neither: another pre-production document"],"items":[{"t":"EXT. SHOAL BAY, DAWN.","bin":1,"fb":"An EXT. scene heading belongs to screenplay format."},{"t":"MARIE (whispering), centred, above the line \"We can't stay here.\"","bin":1,"fb":"A centred character name with dialogue beneath is screenplay format."},{"t":"A news-style explainer on solar panels for a utility company in Belize","bin":0,"fb":"Informational content with narration suits the A/V format."},{"t":"A 15-minute drama about two sisters in Castries","bin":1,"fb":"Narrative drama uses screenplay format."},{"t":"A 30-second recycling advert with voice-over and on-screen text","bin":0,"fb":"Ads match narration to shots, so the A/V layout suits them."},{"t":"Saturday: 6:30 a.m. crew call at the kitchen; 8:00 a.m. move to the beach","bin":2,"fb":"Times, places and crew calls belong in the production schedule."},{"t":"Panel 3: sketch of the chef plating food, 0:08 to 0:12","bin":2,"fb":"A drawn panel with timing is part of the storyboard."},{"t":"She drops the phone and runs to the door.","bin":1,"fb":"An action line describing what a character does is a screenplay element."},{"t":"Shot 14: MS, low angle, 50 mm lens, slow push in","bin":2,"fb":"A numbered shot with lens and movement notes belongs in the shot list."},{"t":"VIDEO: CU hands pouring sorrel | AUDIO: VO \"A Christmas taste of home.\"","bin":0,"fb":"Picture in one column and sound in the other: an A/V script."}],"win":"Excellent. You can tell the two script formats apart and keep shot lists, storyboards and schedules in their own documents."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Capturing video: devices, camera settings and stabilisation","html":"<p>You must be able to capture video with a <b>camera, cell phone or tablet</b>, and with <b>mounted devices</b> such as a tripod or stabiliser. This is <b>Camera Operation: Seeing Like a Filmmaker</b>: moving from automatic settings into manual control, making intentional decisions about exposure, focus, white balance and frame rate. Good camera operation is an extension of storytelling.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Device</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limits</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Smartphone</b></td><td>Always with you; good 1080p or 4K; vertical video for Reels and TikTok</td><td>Small sensor, weaker in low light; weak built-in mic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tablet</b></td><td>Large screen to frame and review shots</td><td>Bulky to hold steady; often a weaker camera</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>DSLR or mirrorless camera</b></td><td>Bigger sensor, interchangeable lenses, full manual control, mic input</td><td>Cost, weight, more settings to learn</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Action camera</b></td><td>Waterproof, wide angle; snorkelling at Buccoo Reef in Tobago</td><td>Distorted wide image; limited zoom</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>The exposure triangle</b>: the single most important technical skill in camera operation. Changing one element always affects the others.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it controls</th><th>In practice</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Aperture</b> (f-stop)</td><td>How much light enters the lens, and the <b>depth of field</b></td><td>Wide (f/1.8): blurry background, perfect for interviews. Narrow (f/11): everything sharp, ideal for a landscape of Maracas Bay</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shutter speed</b></td><td>How long the sensor is exposed per frame</td><td><b>180-degree rule</b>: about double the frame rate, so 1/50 for 24 fps and 1/60 for 30 fps, for natural motion blur</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ISO</b></td><td>The sensor's sensitivity to light</td><td>Higher ISO adds noise (grain). Use the lowest ISO that gives correct exposure; add light rather than cranking ISO</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>White balance</b> makes colours accurate under daylight, tungsten or fluorescent light. Setting it manually prevents unwanted colour casts. Phones choose all of this automatically; a \"Pro\" mode or a free manual camera app lets you lock the settings.</p>\n<p><b>Frame rates and resolution</b>: <b>24 fps</b> cinematic look; <b>30 fps</b> standard for TV and web; <b>60 fps</b> smooth motion for sport or a Carnival parade; <b>120 fps</b> slow motion when played back at 24 or 30 fps. <b>1080p (Full HD)</b> is standard for most web and broadcast delivery; <b>4K (UHD)</b> gives high detail and lets you reframe in post-production; <b>720p</b> means smaller files for web-only content. The course default is 1080p at 24 fps unless the project calls for otherwise.</p>\n<p><b>Support gear</b>: a <b>tripod</b> for interviews, product shots and time-lapses of cruise ships arriving; a <b>slider</b> for slow, controlled moves; a <b>gimbal</b> (motorised stabiliser) for smooth walking shots along the Brian Lara Promenade; a <b>monopod</b> for events. Digital stabilisation crops the image slightly and cannot rescue very shaky footage.</p>\n<p><b>Good capture habits</b>: decide the orientation (16:9 or 9:16) first; lock focus and exposure on the subject; switch on airplane mode; record a few extra seconds before and after each action (<b>handles</b>); shoot wide, medium and close-up shots plus <b>B-roll</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To \"justify\" a setting, link it to the effect: \"f/1.8 blurs the busy Frederick Street background so the viewer focuses on the speaker.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Phones, tablets and cameras can all film well if you use them carefully. The exposure triangle controls brightness: aperture (which also sets background blur), shutter speed (motion blur) and ISO (sensitivity, which adds grain if it is too high). Set white balance so colours look natural. Use a tripod for still shots and a gimbal for walking shots, as on the Bridgetown boardwalk.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The exposure triangle is about trade-offs. Opening the aperture lets in more light but shrinks the depth of field; a faster shutter freezes motion but darkens the image; raising ISO brightens it but adds noise. In bright Caribbean sun, shooting at f/1.8 for a blurred background with a 1/50 shutter will overexpose, so videographers fit an <b>ND (neutral density) filter</b>, which works like sunglasses for the lens. The 180-degree rule keeps motion looking natural; a very fast shutter gives a choppy, staccato look that can suit action but feels odd in an interview. Shoot handles before and after each action, because editors need a few spare seconds to cut smoothly.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire","intro":"<p>Keisha is learning manual camera settings before a shoot for the Bay Breeze Boutique on Bay Street in Nassau. Choose the best setting for each situation.</p>","items":[{"text":"She wants the shop owner sharp and the busy street behind her softly blurred.","options":["A narrow aperture such as f/11","A wide aperture such as f/1.8","A very high ISO"],"answer":1,"fb":"A wide aperture gives a shallow depth of field, so the background blurs. This is perfect for interviews."},{"text":"She is filming at 24 fps. Which shutter speed follows the 180-degree rule?","options":["1/500","1/50","1/12"],"answer":1,"fb":"The 180-degree rule doubles the frame rate: 1/48 is the ideal, so 1/50 is the nearest standard setting. It gives natural-looking motion blur."},{"text":"Her footage inside a dim Cable Beach restaurant is grainy. What is the best fix?","options":["Add light, such as an LED panel, and lower the ISO","Raise the ISO further","Use a faster shutter speed"],"answer":0,"fb":"Grain comes from high ISO. Use the lowest ISO that works and add light rather than cranking ISO."},{"text":"Under warm tungsten bulbs, faces look orange on camera.","options":["Switch to 60 fps","Use a narrower aperture","Set the white balance manually to tungsten"],"answer":2,"fb":"White balance makes colours accurate for the light source. Matching it to tungsten removes the orange cast."},{"text":"She wants slow-motion shots of a jet landing at Lynden Pindling International Airport.","options":["Record at 120 fps and play back at 24 or 30 fps","Record at 24 fps","Record at 720p"],"answer":0,"fb":"High frame rates played back at a normal rate stretch time, giving smooth slow motion."},{"text":"For a wide shot of Nassau harbour from Fort Fincastle, everything from the rocks to the cruise ships must be sharp.","options":["f/11","f/1.8","ISO 6400"],"answer":0,"fb":"A narrow aperture gives a deep depth of field, which is ideal for landscapes."},{"text":"The client may want a vertical crop of a landscape shot later in the edit.","options":["Shoot in 720p to save space","Use a slower shutter speed","Shoot in 4K so there is room to reframe"],"answer":2,"fb":"4K allows reframing in post-production without losing too much detail."}],"win":"Excellent. You can control aperture, shutter speed, ISO, white balance and frame rate, the key manual settings.","tutor":[{"h":"Blur or sharp?","p":"<p>Low f-numbers mean a wide opening and a blurry background; high f-numbers keep more in focus.</p>"},{"h":"Double it","p":"<p>The shutter rule uses the frame rate times two.</p>"},{"h":"Grain","p":"<p>Which setting adds noise when it is raised?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A blurry background for an interview needs...","options":["a narrow aperture (f/11)","a wide aperture (f/1.8)"],"answer":1,"fb":"Wide aperture, shallow depth of field."},{"text":"At 24 fps the 180-degree rule gives a shutter speed of about...","options":["1/1000","1/50"],"answer":1,"fb":"Double the frame rate."},{"text":"High ISO causes...","options":["noise (grain)","slow motion"],"answer":0,"fb":"Use the lowest ISO you can."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Exposure triangle quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: every question involves a trade-off between settings. Keisha is shooting for Bay Breeze Boutique again.</p>","items":[{"text":"In midday sun she wants f/1.8 for a blurred background and 1/50 at 24 fps. Even at the lowest ISO the image is far too bright. What is the professional fix?","options":["Close the aperture to f/16","Raise the ISO","Fit an ND (neutral density) filter","Raise the shutter to 1/2000"],"answer":2,"fb":"An ND filter cuts the light without changing the aperture or shutter, so she keeps both the blur and natural motion."},{"text":"She raises the shutter speed from 1/50 to 1/500 at 24 fps. What happens to movement on screen?","options":["Nothing changes","It looks choppy and staccato, with very little motion blur","The colours change","It becomes smoother"],"answer":1,"fb":"Breaking the 180-degree rule removes natural motion blur. The image also gets darker."},{"text":"At f/1.8 the owner's nose is sharp but her eyes are soft. Why?","options":["The frame rate is too high","The depth of field is very shallow, so focus must be placed on the eyes","ISO is too low","White balance is wrong"],"answer":1,"fb":"A wide aperture gives a very thin zone of focus. Always focus on the eyes."},{"text":"Parade footage shot at 60 fps is placed on a 24 fps timeline. What does this make possible?","options":["Doubling the resolution","Removing noise","Nothing new","Slowing it to 40% speed for smooth slow motion"],"answer":3,"fb":"24 ÷ 60 = 0.4, so the clip can play at 40% speed with every frame used."},{"text":"Faces look orange on one side and blue on the other, because warm bulbs mix with daylight from a window. What is the best fix?","options":["Raise the ISO","Leave white balance on auto","Shoot at 120 fps","Block or gel one source so the lights match, then set white balance to the main source"],"answer":3,"fb":"One white balance setting cannot fix two colour temperatures. Make the sources match first."},{"text":"Which change makes a dim shot brighter WITHOUT adding noise or changing the depth of field?","options":["Raise the ISO","Add light with an LED panel or a reflector","Close the aperture","Open the aperture wider"],"answer":1,"fb":"Adding light raises exposure while leaving ISO and aperture untouched."},{"text":"The client wants a 16:9 YouTube video AND a 9:16 Reel from one shoot. Which capture choice helps MOST?","options":["Use a very high ISO","Shoot 4K 16:9 with the subject framed so a vertical crop is possible","Shoot 9:16 only","Shoot at 720p"],"answer":1,"fb":"4K leaves enough pixels to crop a sharp 1080 × 1920 vertical version."}],"win":"Excellent. You managed the trade-offs between aperture, shutter, ISO, white balance and frame rate, and planned capture for more than one platform."}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing a capture setup","q":"Malik must film a smooth walking tour along the Hip Strip in Montego Bay for a tourism Reel, using his smartphone. The footage he shot handheld yesterday was shaky. Which setup is MOST appropriate?","options":[{"t":"Handheld again, but with 4K resolution to make it sharper","fb":"Higher resolution does not remove shake. The footage would still be unsteady, and the files would be much larger."},{"t":"A gimbal stabiliser, with the phone set to vertical 9:16 at 1080p, focus and exposure locked","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A gimbal keeps a moving camera level and smooth, the vertical framing matches Reels, and locked exposure stops brightness jumping between sun and shade."},{"t":"A fixed tripod set up at one end of the Hip Strip","fb":"A tripod is excellent for still shots, but it cannot move with Malik for a walking tour."},{"t":"A tablet held at arm's length to see the frame better","fb":"A larger screen helps framing, but a tablet is heavier and harder to hold steady while walking."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Moving or still?","p":"<p>Is the camera staying in one place or travelling with the presenter?</p>"},{"h":"Shake vs sharpness","p":"<p>Resolution and stability are different problems.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a capture setup (Foundation)","q":"Which device keeps a moving phone smooth while walking?","options":[{"t":"A gimbal","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. A gimbal uses motors to cancel out shake."},{"t":"A fixed tripod","fb":"A tripod keeps the camera still in one place."},{"t":"A higher resolution setting","fb":"Resolution does not fix shake."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing a capture setup (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Keisha must film a 3-minute interview with a fisherman on a gently rocking boat off Port Antonio, plus walking B-roll along the dock. She has a smartphone, a gimbal, a tripod and a lavalier microphone with a windscreen. Which plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"The tripod on the boat deck for the interview, because it is the steadiest support.","fb":"A tripod is steady on land, but on a deck it moves with the boat and passes on every vibration."},{"t":"The phone on the tripod at the dock, with its built-in microphone, so the fisherman does not have to go out to sea.","fb":"It avoids the boat, but the built-in mic far from the speaker records mostly wind and water."},{"t":"Handheld with digital stabilisation set to maximum, to save setting up.","fb":"Digital stabilisation crops the image and cannot rescue heavy boat motion. Sound would still need the lavalier."},{"t":"Gimbal for the boat interview and the dock B-roll, the lavalier with its windscreen on the fisherman, focus and exposure locked, and the tripod kept for a still wide shot on land.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The gimbal smooths the boat's motion and the walking shots, the lavalier gives clean speech in the wind, and each tool is used where it works best."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Recording sound for video: sound is half the picture","html":"<p>Put bluntly: <b>poor audio will ruin a beautifully shot video</b>. Audiences will forgive imperfect visuals long before they forgive bad sound. Plan audio as carefully as the shots.</p>\n<p><b>Microphone types and when to use them</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Mic</th><th>How it picks up sound</th><th>Best use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shotgun</b></td><td>Highly directional; rejects sound from the sides</td><td>Mounted on a camera or boom pole; dialogue on location or run-and-gun shooting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lavalier (lav)</b>, wired or wireless</td><td>Small clip-on mic on the subject's clothing, 15 to 20 cm below the chin</td><td>Consistent, close-proximity audio for interviews and presentations</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Condenser</b> (often USB)</td><td>Sensitive, wide-frequency capture; picks up room echo</td><td>Controlled spaces such as voice-over booths or podcast studios with minimal ambient noise</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Handheld dynamic</b></td><td>Robust, less sensitive to background noise</td><td>Street interviews and reporters at Carnival in Port of Spain</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Built-in</b> (phone or camera)</td><td>Picks up everything around it</td><td>Backup sound and syncing only</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Setting audio levels: the target zone.</b> Aim for peaks between <b>-12 dB and -6 dB</b>. There are three <b>common level mistakes</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Peaking above 0 dB</b> causes clipping distortion, which is unrecoverable in post.</li>\n<li><b>Recording too quietly</b>: boosting a low signal later also amplifies the noise floor.</li>\n<li><b>Not monitoring with headphones</b>: you cannot fix what you cannot hear in real time. Never trust camera speakers on set.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Controlling your recording environment</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Eliminate background noise</b>: switch off air-conditioning, fridges and fans; close windows and doors. The quieter the space, the cleaner the recording.</li>\n<li><b>Mic placement</b>: as close to the source as possible without entering the frame. Closer placement gives a better <b>signal-to-noise ratio</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Room acoustics</b>: hard surfaces (tiled floors, concrete walls) cause echo and reverb; curtains, sofas and blankets absorb reflections. Scout the location before shoot day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Outdoors in the Caribbean</b>, <b>wind</b> is the biggest enemy: use a foam windscreen or furry \"dead cat\" and shelter the speaker; the east coasts of Barbados and Tobago are especially windy. Expect <b>uncontrolled noise</b> such as planes landing at a nearby airport, traffic on a busy main street, waves and roosters: wait for it to pass. Record 10 seconds of <b>room tone</b>, record a backup on a second device, and clap once on camera so you can <b>sync</b> separate audio in the editor.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Use a lavalier with a windscreen, keep peaks between -12 and -6 dB, and monitor on headphones\" earns more than \"make sure the sound is good\". Be specific.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Audiences forgive weak pictures before bad sound. Choose the right microphone: a clip-on lavalier for interviews, a shotgun for dialogue on location, a condenser for a quiet studio. Keep peaks between -12 and -6 dB, wear headphones and cut background noise. Outdoors, use a windscreen and wait for planes and traffic to pass, as any crew filming near the airport in Antigua knows.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Good location sound is mostly about <b>distance and control</b>. Moving the microphone much closer to the mouth greatly improves the voice compared with the background noise, which is why a lavalier on a shirt beats an expensive mic on a camera three metres away. Room acoustics matter as much as the mic: a sensitive condenser in a tiled kitchen records echo, while a dynamic mic rejects more of the room. Some noise can be fixed in post, but only steady noise such as hum; a jet, a rooster or overlapping chatter cannot be removed without damaging the voice. Professional crews therefore record room tone, a backup track and a sync clap, so the editor always has clean material.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The Maho Beach interview","intro":"<p>You are helping Malik at Island Pulse Media film a 2-minute interview with the owner of the Sunset Snorkel Tours on Maho Beach. Make the best production decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>It is windy, planes land every few minutes and the owner wants the beach behind her. Which microphone setup do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The phone's built-in mic from 3 metres away; it is the simplest option.","to":"bad1","fb":"An omnidirectional mic far away records mostly wind, waves and planes."},{"t":"A lavalier mic clipped to her shirt with a furry windscreen, plus the phone's built-in mic as backup.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. The lavalier sits close to her voice, the windscreen blocks wind and the backup protects you if something fails."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A plane is approaching during her best answer. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep recording and plan to remove the plane in Audacity later.","to":"bad2","fb":"Noise Reduction works on steady hum, not on loud, changing sounds that overlap the voice."},{"t":"Pause, let the plane pass, then ask her to repeat the answer.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Uncontrolled noise is best handled by waiting. Noise reduction cannot remove a jet engine from speech."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>It is 12 noon and the sun is directly overhead, causing dark shadows under her eyes. What lighting approach do you use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Shoot her facing straight into the midday sun so her face is bright.","to":"bad3","fb":"Facing the sun makes people squint and creates harsh shadows and burnt-out highlights."},{"t":"Move her into open shade under a palm with the bright beach behind, and bounce light onto her face with a reflector.","to":"n4","fb":"Strong choice. Open shade softens harsh light and the reflector acts as a fill light."},{"t":"Turn on the phone's built-in flash.","to":"bad3","fb":"A tiny flash cannot balance midday sun and flattens the image."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Back at the studio, how should Malik prepare the audio and video for the edit?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use only the built-in mic audio because syncing takes too long.","to":"bad4","fb":"The backup track is far noisier. Throwing away the best audio wastes the planning you did."},{"t":"Import the video and the lavalier WAV into the editor, sync them using the clap at the start, and reduce the wind noise gently.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Syncing a separate, high-quality track is standard professional practice."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A professional interview.</b> The voice is clear, the face is evenly lit and the beach looks bright behind her. A Grade One answer would explain why: a close lavalier with a windscreen, waiting out uncontrolled noise, using shade and a reflector as fill, and syncing the best audio in the edit.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable sound.</b> The wind roars and the owner is barely audible. Try again and get a directional or clip-on mic close to the speaker.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Ruined answer.</b> The jet noise covers her words and Noise Reduction only makes the voice sound robotic. Try again and wait for uncontrolled noise to pass.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Harsh pictures.</b> She squints, with dark eye shadows and white, clipped highlights. Try again and soften the light.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Wasted effort.</b> The final video has noisy audio even though a clean recording existed. Try again and sync the lavalier track.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Close and covered","p":"<p>Get the mic near the mouth and protect it from wind.</p>"},{"h":"Steady noise vs sudden noise","p":"<p>Software removes steady hum; it cannot remove a plane.</p>"},{"h":"Soften the sun","p":"<p>Shade plus a reflector beats direct midday sun.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The Maho Beach interview (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Malik is filming an interview on a windy beach.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which microphone setup should he use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The phone's built-in mic from 3 metres away.","to":"bad","fb":"It would mostly record wind and waves."},{"t":"A lavalier mic with a windscreen, clipped near her chin.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Close and protected from wind."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Her voice is clear above the wind.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The wind drowns her voice. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"The Maho Beach interview (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher shoot with Malik for Sunset Snorkel Tours. You have only ONE furry windscreen, the owner gets louder when excited, and the beach must stay visible behind her. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>It is windy and planes land every few minutes. You have a lavalier, a shotgun mic on the camera, the phone's built-in mic and ONE furry windscreen. Which setup do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The lavalier on her shirt under the furry windscreen, with the phone's mic recording as a backup.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. The mic is closest to her voice, the windscreen goes where wind matters most, and the backup protects you."},{"t":"The phone's built-in mic, three metres away.","to":"bad1","fb":"It will record mostly wind, waves and planes."},{"t":"The shotgun on the camera with the furry windscreen, two metres away.","to":"ok1","fb":"A shotgun is directional, but at two metres it still picks up waves and wind around her."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>In a test, her normal voice peaks at -3 dB, and she gets louder when she is excited. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Leave it, because -3 dB is still below 0.","to":"bad2","fb":"When she gets excited, her peaks will pass 0 dB and clip."},{"t":"Lower the input so normal speech peaks around -12 to -6 dB, leaving headroom for her excited answers.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Headroom prevents clipping, which cannot be repaired later."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>A plane approaches during her best answer. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Pause, let it pass, ask her to repeat the answer and note the take number.","to":"n4","fb":"Good. Waiting is the only reliable fix, and the note helps the editor find the clean take."},{"t":"Keep rolling; Noise Reduction will remove the plane later.","to":"bad3","fb":"Noise Reduction handles steady hum, not a jet engine over speech."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>It is noon and the sun is directly overhead, but she insists the beach must be visible behind her. What is your lighting approach?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Place her with the bright sea directly behind and expose for the sea.","to":"ok2","fb":"The beach looks great, but her face falls into shadow."},{"t":"Keep her in direct sun, facing it, so her face is bright.","to":"bad4","fb":"Overhead sun and squinting give harsh shadows and burnt-out highlights."},{"t":"Move her into open shade facing the camera, expose for her face, fill with the reflector, and accept a slightly brighter background.","to":"n5","fb":"Strong. Her face is soft and even, and the beach is still recognisable behind her."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Back at the studio, how do you prepare the audio for the edit?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use the phone's backup audio because it is already in sync.","to":"ok3","fb":"It saves a few minutes but throws away the clean lavalier recording."},{"t":"Sync the lavalier WAV to the video with the clap, clean it gently, balance it, add a light music bed with ducking and limit the mix below 0 dB.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. The best recording is used and the mix follows Clean, Balance, Layer, Master."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One interview.</b> Her voice is clear and never distorts, the plane never interrupts, her face is evenly lit with the beach behind, and the mix is clean. You can justify each choice: mic distance and wind protection, headroom, waiting out uncontrolled noise, soft fill light and syncing the best track.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Usable, not clean.</b> Her voice is there, but waves and gusts sit under every word. A partial success: get the mic close to the speaker.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Dark face.</b> The beach looks stunning, but viewers cannot see her expressions. A partial success: expose for the person and fill her face with light.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Noisy edit.</b> The finished video sounds windy, although a clean recording existed. A partial success: always sync the best audio.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable sound.</b> The wind roars and she is barely audible. Try again and get a clip-on mic close to her.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Distorted highlights.</b> Her most excited answers clip and crackle. Try again and leave headroom.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Ruined answer.</b> The jet covers her words and Noise Reduction makes her sound robotic. Try again and wait for the plane to pass.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Harsh pictures.</b> She squints, with dark eye shadows and white highlights. Try again and soften the light.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Lighting for video","html":"<p>Lighting is one of the most powerful tools a filmmaker has: it <b>defines mood, shapes depth, directs attention and ensures technical quality</b>. You need to know <b>lighting and light sources</b>, including natural light and three-point lighting.</p>\n<p><b>Three-point lighting setup</b>: the standard starting point for any interview or narrative scene. Master this foundation, then learn how to break the rules creatively once you understand why they exist.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 180\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three-point lighting plan: key light at 45 degrees to one side, fill light on the other side, back light behind the subject, camera in front\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"70\" r=\"16\"/>\n<rect x=\"145\" y=\"150\" width=\"30\" height=\"20\"/>\n<rect x=\"50\" y=\"110\" width=\"34\" height=\"22\"/>\n<rect x=\"236\" y=\"110\" width=\"34\" height=\"22\"/>\n<rect x=\"143\" y=\"8\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\"/>\n<line x1=\"84\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"146\" y2=\"78\"/>\n<line x1=\"236\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"174\" y2=\"78\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n<line x1=\"160\" y1=\"26\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"54\"/>\n<line x1=\"160\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"88\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"74\">Subject</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"164\">Camera</text>\n<text x=\"67\" y=\"146\">Key light</text>\n<text x=\"253\" y=\"146\">Fill light</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"40\">Back light</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Key light</b>: the primary light, at about <b>45 degrees</b> to the subject and slightly above eye level; it defines the face.</li>\n<li><b>Fill light</b>: a <b>softer light opposite the key</b> that reduces shadows (often half the key's strength or less). A <b>reflector</b> can be the fill.</li>\n<li><b>Back light</b> (rim or hair light): placed behind the subject to <b>separate it from the background</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Natural vs artificial light</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Natural light</b>: the sun gives beautiful, dynamic light, but it is unpredictable. Learn the <b>golden hour</b> (shortly after sunrise and before sunset) for warm, cinematic tones at Negril or Speyside, and use <b>reflectors</b> to fill shadows outdoors. Caribbean midday sun is hard and overhead, so move into open shade or use a diffuser.</li>\n<li><b>Artificial light</b>: LED panels, softboxes and practical lights give full control over direction, intensity and colour temperature. It is <b>consistent and repeatable</b>, essential for multi-day productions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Common lighting mistakes to avoid</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What goes wrong</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shooting into a window</b></td><td>Backlit subjects become silhouettes</td><td>Put the light source in front of the subject, or use exposure compensation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Overhead fluorescent lighting</b></td><td>Unflattering downward shadows under the eyes and nose</td><td>Add a fill light low down, or bounce light up with a reflector</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mixed colour temperatures</b></td><td>Daylight plus tungsten without correction gives unnatural, muddy colour</td><td>Match the light sources, or use coloured gels to unify them</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Colour temperature</b> is measured in kelvin (K): tungsten bulbs are warm (about 3200 K), daylight is cooler (about 5600 K). Set the camera's <b>white balance</b> to match the main light. Ring lights give even front light for vloggers; softboxes and umbrellas soften shadows; a gold reflector adds warmth.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you describe three-point lighting, give the <b>position and purpose</b> of each light. Position alone earns half the marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Three-point lighting is the standard setup. A key light at about 45 degrees gives the main light, a softer fill light on the other side reduces shadows, and a back light behind the subject separates them from the background. Natural light is beautiful but changes; golden hour gives warm, soft light. Avoid midday sun overhead, like the harsh noon light on a Jamaican beach.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Lighting decisions are about <b>ratio and direction</b>. The ratio between key and fill sets the mood: a strong key with little fill gives dramatic, low-key shadows, while a fill close to the key's strength gives the flat, bright look of high-key adverts. Colour temperature is a separate problem from brightness. Mixing window daylight (about 5600 K) with warm tungsten bulbs (about 3200 K) leaves skin orange on one side and blue on the other, and no single white balance setting can fix both, so match the sources or gel them. Practical lights in the frame, such as a lamp or fairy lights at a beach bar, add depth and realism when they are balanced with the main lighting.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the lighting mistakes","intro":"<p>Jerome set up lights for a vlog about his new app in a small Chaguanas office. Zara reviews his notes.</p>","q":"Select every statement that describes a lighting MISTAKE.","lines":["The key light is placed at about 45 degrees to the side of the camera.","The fill light is brighter than the key light, so there are no shadows at all and the face looks flat.","There is no back light, and his dark shirt merges into the dark wall.","A softbox is used on the key light to soften shadows.","He sits with a bright window directly behind him, so his face is in silhouette.","The ceiling tubes are cool and the desk lamp is warm, and white balance is left on auto, giving his face mixed colour casts.","A white reflector bounces some of the key light into the shadow side of his face.","White balance is set to match the LED panels at 5600 K."],"correct":[1,2,4,5],"win":"Well spotted. A fill brighter than the key, no back light, backlighting from a window and mixed colour temperatures are classic lighting faults.","miss":"Look again. Check the strength of each light, whether the subject separates from the background, what is behind him and whether the colours match.","tutor":[{"h":"Key is king","p":"<p>The key light should be the strongest. The fill only lifts shadows.</p>"},{"h":"What is behind?","p":"<p>A bright window behind the subject makes the camera expose for the window.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the lighting mistakes (Foundation)","q":"Which TWO are lighting mistakes?","lines":["A bright window directly behind the presenter.","A key light at 45 degrees to the camera.","A fill light brighter than the key light.","A reflector lifting the shadows."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Backlighting from a window causes silhouette, and the fill should be weaker than the key.","miss":"Look for the setups that cause silhouettes or flat faces."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the lighting mistakes (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Zara reviews Jerome's lighting notes for three different shoots. Some mistakes only matter for the mood or the camera, so read each one carefully.</p>","q":"Select every statement that describes a lighting MISTAKE.","lines":["For a dark, dramatic thriller scene, the key and fill are the same strength from opposite sides.","The key light is about 45 degrees from the camera and slightly above eye level.","A 3200 K tungsten key is used beside a window full of daylight, with white balance set to 3200 K.","A back light rims the presenter's hair and shoulders, separating him from a dark wall.","The only light is a ceiling panel directly above the presenter's head.","A lamp in the background is so bright that it is the brightest thing in the frame.","The fill is a white reflector at about half the key's brightness.","Golden-hour sun is used as a back light, with a reflector filling the face.","The presenter's glasses show a large reflection of the softbox.","White balance is locked at 5600 K to match the LED panels."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,8],"win":"Well spotted. Flat lighting for a dramatic scene, mixed colour temperatures, overhead-only light, a background lamp stealing attention and a reflection in glasses are all faults. The others are sound practice.","miss":"Look again. Check whether the lighting suits the mood, whether all sources share one colour temperature, where the shadows fall and what the brightest thing in the frame is."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Video editing: the edit workflow in OpenShot and WeVideo","html":"<p>Welcome to <b>the edit suite</b>: post-production is where raw footage becomes a story. Editing is both a technical craft and a creative art; your decisions about pacing, rhythm, structure and emotion shape how the audience experiences the work.</p>\n<p><b>The edit workflow</b>: <b>Ingest</b> (copy, organise and import the footage) &gt; <b>Rough cut</b> (clips in story order) &gt; <b>Fine cut</b> (trim every edit, tighten timing) &gt; <b>Sound mix</b> &gt; <b>Export</b>. Every professional editor, whatever the software, follows a version of this sequence; it prevents costly mistakes and keeps the project organised.</p>\n<p><b>Software.</b> The examples here use <b>Adobe Premiere Pro</b>, the industry standard for broadcasters and filmmakers. Its four main panels have equivalents in <b>OpenShot</b> (free and offline, good for Paper 02) and <b>WeVideo</b> (in the browser):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Premiere Pro panel</th><th>Job</th><th>OpenShot equivalent</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Project panel</td><td>Media library: import and organise footage, audio and graphics</td><td>Project Files (File &gt; Import Files, Ctrl+F)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Source and Program monitors</td><td>Preview raw clips and the edited sequence</td><td>Preview window</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Timeline panel</td><td>Arrange, trim and layer clips across tracks</td><td>Timeline, Razor tool, dragging clip edges to trim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effects and Lumetri Color panels</td><td>Transitions, effects and colour</td><td>Transitions and Effects tabs, Properties</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Cutting principles: how editors think</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cut on action</b>: cut mid-motion (as a person sits down) for seamless, invisible edits.</li>\n<li><b>Cut on motivation</b>: only cut when there is a reason: a sound, a look, a question or a revelation. Unmotivated cuts feel random.</li>\n<li><b>The L-cut and J-cut</b>: overlap sound and picture so the audio follows (L) or comes before (J) the image cut. Edits feel organic and professional.</li>\n<li><b>Rhythm and pacing</b>: short cuts create urgency and tension (a Carnival road march); long cuts feel calm and deliberate (a sunrise at Bathsheba).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Working with transitions</b>: the <b>hard cut</b> is the default, clean and invisible; a <b>dissolve / cross-fade</b> signals time passing or a gentle shift (use sparingly); a <b>fade to black</b> marks a major change, often at the start and end; <b>avoid wipes and spins</b>, which draw attention to themselves rather than serving the story.</p>\n<p><b>Colour correction vs colour grading.</b> <b>Correction</b> is the technical step and comes first: fix exposure, white balance and contrast so every clip looks neutral and consistent. <b>Grading</b> is the creative step and comes second: a stylised look that sets emotion. Warm tones feel nostalgic; cool, desaturated tones feel tense or clinical. In Premiere both happen in Lumetri Color (Basic Correction, then Creative and Curves); in OpenShot use effects such as Brightness and Contrast, Hue and Saturation in Properties.</p>\n<p><b>Titles and lower thirds</b>: Title &gt; Title (Ctrl+T) in OpenShot. Lower thirds identify speakers or places; keep them simple, readable and on screen long enough to read twice.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Name the principle and its effect: \"I used a J-cut so the steel pan music is heard before we see the band, which pulls the viewer into the next scene.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Editing turns raw footage into a story. Follow the workflow: ingest (import and organise), rough cut (clips in order), fine cut (trim every edit), sound mix and export. Cut on action or for a reason, and use simple hard cuts most of the time. Correct the colour first, then grade it for mood. An editor cutting a Crop Over video in Barbados follows these steps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Good editing is mostly invisible: viewers notice the story, not the cuts. Cutting on action hides an edit because the eye follows the movement across it. L-cuts and J-cuts make conversations flow, because in real life we often hear someone before we turn to look at them. Pacing should follow the content's emotion, not the editor's habit; a fast-cut sequence loses its power if the whole video is fast. Transitions carry meaning, so a dissolve between every clip tells viewers time has passed when it has not. Correct before you grade: a creative grade on clips with different white balances only exaggerates the mismatch. Finally, watch the export on a phone, where most of the audience will see it.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Edit the SBA 30-second ad","intro":"<p>Your SBA group is editing the 30-second audio-visual ad for a Seagrape Bay Secondary School recycling drive in OpenShot. Put the steps of the edit workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Ingest: copy the footage into the project folder, choose the 1080p 30 fps profile and import the clips, voice-over WAV and music."},{"t":"Rough cut: drag the clips onto the timeline in storyboard order."},{"t":"Fine cut: trim each edit with the Razor tool, cut on action, and bring the ad to exactly 30 seconds."},{"t":"Polish the fine cut: hard cuts or a few dissolves, colour correction then a consistent grade, and text with the call to action in the title-safe area."},{"t":"Sound mix: clean the voice-over, balance it above the music, layer the effects and master the output below 0 dB."},{"t":"Export as an H.264 MP4 with AAC audio at 48 kHz."},{"t":"Watch the whole exported file on a phone and check its length and settings."}],"win":"Correct order: ingest, rough cut, fine cut, polish, sound mix, export, review. This is the edit workflow, and the pipeline examiners expect you to describe.","tutor":[{"h":"Big to small","p":"<p>Get the structure right before polishing colour, text and sound.</p>"},{"h":"Correct, then grade","p":"<p>The technical colour step comes before the creative one.</p>"},{"h":"Last step","p":"<p>Nothing is finished until you have watched the exported file.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Edit the ad (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main stages of the edit workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Ingest: import the clips and audio."},{"t":"Rough cut, then fine cut on the timeline."},{"t":"Sound mix: balance voice and music."},{"t":"Export as MP4 and review it."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Edit the SBA 30-second ad (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: ten steps, including backing up, syncing separate sound and checking the export. Your group is editing the 30-second recycling ad for Seagrape Bay Secondary School in OpenShot. Put the full workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Copy the footage into the project folder structure and back it up before any editing."},{"t":"Create the project with a 1080p 30 fps profile and import the clips, the voice-over WAV and the music."},{"t":"Sync any separately recorded audio to its clip, using the clap at the start."},{"t":"Rough cut: place the clips on the timeline in storyboard order."},{"t":"Fine cut: trim with the Razor tool, cut on action and bring the ad to exactly 30 seconds."},{"t":"Correct the colour of every clip, then apply one consistent grade."},{"t":"Add titles, captions and the call to action inside the title-safe area."},{"t":"Sound mix: clean, balance, layer the music and effects, and master below 0 dB."},{"t":"Export an H.264 MP4 with AAC audio at 48 kHz."},{"t":"Check the export on a phone: length, sound, captions and file properties."}],"win":"Correct. Protect the footage, set up, sync, rough cut, fine cut, colour, graphics, sound mix, export and review: a complete, professional edit workflow."}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Cutting principles and transitions","intro":"<p>Zara is reviewing Malik's edit of a documentary about a heritage museum in Willemstad, Curaçao. Match each term to what she sees on the timeline.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Cut on action","r":"The edit happens as the curator lifts an old map, so the cut is invisible"},{"l":"Cut on motivation","r":"He cuts to the doorway only when a visitor's footsteps are heard"},{"l":"J-cut","r":"The interviewee's voice starts before we see her face"},{"l":"L-cut","r":"Her voice continues over a shot of the harbour after her face has gone"},{"l":"Fast pacing","r":"Short, quick shots of Carnival costumes to build excitement"},{"l":"Dissolve","r":"A gentle blend from a 1950s photo of the Handelskade to the waterfront today, showing time passing"},{"l":"Fade to black","r":"The screen darkens between the two chapters of the film"},{"l":"Colour correction","r":"Fixing white balance so every interview clip looks neutral and consistent"}],"win":"Very good. You can name the cutting principles, transitions and colour steps and explain their effect.","tutor":[{"h":"Sound first or picture first?","p":"<p>In a J-cut the sound leads; in an L-cut the sound lingers. Picture the letter shapes on a timeline.</p>"},{"h":"Why cut?","p":"<p>A movement, or a reason such as a sound or a look?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Cutting principles (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Cut on action","r":"Cutting in the middle of a movement"},{"l":"J-cut","r":"The next clip's sound starts before its picture"},{"l":"Hard cut","r":"One clip ends and the next begins instantly"},{"l":"Fade to black","r":"Marks a major scene change or chapter break"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Cutting principles and transitions (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: ten terms, including slow pacing and the difference between correction and grading. Match each term to what Zara sees on the timeline.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Cut on action","r":"The cut comes mid-swing as the cricketer hits the ball, so it feels seamless"},{"l":"Cut on motivation","r":"We cut to the door only when the knock is heard"},{"l":"J-cut","r":"We hear the steel band before we see it"},{"l":"L-cut","r":"The narrator keeps talking as the picture moves to the empty beach"},{"l":"Fast pacing","r":"Two-second shots of J'ouvert paint and dancers build energy"},{"l":"Slow pacing","r":"A twelve-second shot of sunrise at Westpunt lets the viewer breathe"},{"l":"Dissolve","r":"The young fisherman's face slowly blends into the same man, now old"},{"l":"Fade to black","r":"The screen goes dark before the credits roll"},{"l":"Colour correction","r":"Matching the white balance of two cameras so the interview cuts smoothly"},{"l":"Colour grading","r":"A warm, golden look added after correction to make the past feel nostalgic"}],"win":"Excellent. You separated similar ideas: J-cut from L-cut, fast from slow pacing, and technical correction from creative grading."}},{"type":"learn","title":"MP4 export settings for different platforms","html":"<p>Export settings directly affect the <b>quality, file size and compatibility</b> of your final video. In OpenShot: <b>File &gt; Export Project &gt; Export Video</b> (Ctrl+E); choose a <b>Profile</b>, <b>Target</b> = <b>MP4 (h.264)</b> and a <b>Quality</b>; Advanced lets you set bitrates. In WeVideo choose Export and the resolution.</p>\n<p><b>Export settings: getting it right</b> (four numbered settings)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Format: H.264.</b> The most universally compatible video codec; balances quality and file size; required by most web and social platforms.</li>\n<li><b>Preset: Match Source.</b> In Premiere Pro, \"Match Source, High Bitrate\" copies the sequence's resolution, frame rate and aspect ratio. In OpenShot, choose the <b>same profile</b> as the project (for example HD 1080p 30 fps).</li>\n<li><b>Bitrate: CBR vs VBR.</b> <b>Constant bitrate (CBR)</b> for broadcast or streaming requirements; <b>variable bitrate (VBR, 2-pass)</b> for the best quality-to-file-size ratio on the web.</li>\n<li><b>Audio: AAC, 48 kHz, stereo.</b> 48 kHz is the broadcast standard; stereo plays on all systems.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Platform-specific requirements</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Platform</th><th>Shape and frame size</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>YouTube</b></td><td>16:9, 1920 by 1080 or 3840 by 2160 (4K)</td><td>H.264 MP4; files up to 256 GB or 12 hours. 4K is often given as the maximum; YouTube now also accepts 8K, but 1080p or 4K is plenty for school work</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Instagram Reels</b></td><td>9:16, 1080 by 1920; feed posts 1:1 or 4:5</td><td>H.264 MP4; 1080p is the practical maximum; keep text away from the bottom edge; check the app's current file size limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Vimeo</b></td><td>16:9, up to 4K</td><td>H.264 or ProRes; storage depends on the plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Class platform</b> (Google Classroom or Teams)</td><td>1080p preferred</td><td>Some course sites set a 500 MB limit; your teacher will tell you the limit here, so keep SBA uploads small</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Website</b></td><td>16:9, 1280 by 720 or 1920 by 1080</td><td>Lower bitrate (2 to 5 Mbps) and \"fast start\" so it loads on mobile data</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Key terms</b>: <b>bitrate</b> (Mbps) is data per second of video, so higher means better quality and bigger files; keep the <b>frame rate</b> the same as the footage; the <b>codec</b> compresses the video (H.264 is the safe choice; H.265 makes smaller files but may not play everywhere).</p>\n<p>Plan <b>one master and several versions</b>: export a high-quality 16:9 master, then reframe the shots for a 9:16 version rather than adding black bars. For the SBA ad, name files clearly, for example <code>Group3_RecyclingAd_Reels_9x16_2026-11-02.mp4</code>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may give exact export settings. Read them twice, then check the exported file's properties (right-click &gt; Properties or Get Info) to confirm resolution, length and audio settings before you submit.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Export settings decide how good your video looks and how big the file is. Use the H.264 codec in an MP4, keep the same frame rate as your footage, and use AAC audio at 48 kHz. Match the shape to the platform: 16:9 landscape for YouTube, 9:16 vertical for Reels and a smaller file for websites. A tourism promo for Dominica needs a different version for each.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Bitrate is the setting that most affects quality and size. A 30-second video at 8 Mbps is about 30 MB (8 ÷ 8 = 1 MB per second), while the same clip at 2 Mbps is about 7.5 MB, which suits a website on mobile data. VBR 2-pass gives better quality for the same size, because it spends more data on complex scenes, such as fast Carnival dancing, and less on still shots. Changing the frame rate on export can cause stutter, so match the source. Reframing for 9:16 beats adding black bars, because the platform shows the video full screen. Platforms also re-compress uploads, so a clean, high-quality upload survives their compression best.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which platform are these export settings for?","intro":"<p>Malik has exported several versions of a promo for the Mango Festival in Roseau. Sort each export into the platform it suits best.</p>","main":"Which platform suits this export best?","bins":["YouTube","Instagram","Website"],"items":[{"t":"1080 by 1080 square post for the festival's feed","bin":1,"fb":"The 1:1 square is a classic Instagram feed format."},{"t":"A short H.264 MP4 with \"fast start\" enabled so it begins playing before fully downloading on the festival site","bin":2,"fb":"Fast start (web optimisation) moves the file information to the start so browsers can begin playing immediately."},{"t":"1080 by 1920, 9:16 vertical, 30 fps, text kept away from the bottom edge","bin":1,"fb":"Vertical 9:16 is the Reels format, and the bottom of the screen is covered by app buttons and captions."},{"t":"1080 by 1350, 4:5 portrait, to fill more of the phone screen in the feed","bin":1,"fb":"4:5 portrait is an Instagram feed format that takes up more screen than square."},{"t":"1280 by 720, muted, 10-second loop at about 2 Mbps for a homepage background","bin":2,"fb":"A short, muted, low-bitrate loop loads quickly on a web page without overwhelming mobile data."},{"t":"3840 by 2160 4K landscape documentary of the festival history","bin":0,"fb":"Long-form 4K landscape video is best hosted on YouTube, which streams it at a quality matched to each viewer's connection."},{"t":"1920 by 1080, 16:9, H.264, about 8 Mbps, 48 kHz AAC audio","bin":0,"fb":"Full HD landscape at a high bitrate matches YouTube's upload guidance for standard videos."}],"win":"Well sorted. YouTube: 16:9 at high quality; Instagram: 9:16, 1:1 or 4:5; website: smaller, web-optimised MP4. Matching export settings to the platform is a key Paper 02 skill.","tutor":[{"h":"Shape first","p":"<p>Landscape, vertical, square or portrait? That points to the platform.</p>"},{"h":"Who streams it?","p":"<p>Big, long files belong on a media platform; small, light files suit your own website.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which platform? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"1920 by 1080, 16:9 landscape","bin":0,"fb":"Landscape HD suits YouTube."},{"t":"1080 by 1920, 9:16 vertical","bin":1,"fb":"Vertical suits Reels."},{"t":"A small muted loop for a homepage","bin":2,"fb":"Small, light files suit websites."},{"t":"1080 by 1080 square","bin":1,"fb":"Square suits the Instagram feed."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which platform are these export settings for? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a fourth destination, the class platform, has its own file size limit. Sort each export Malik made into the destination it suits best.</p>","main":"Which destination suits this export best?","bins":["YouTube","Instagram","Website","Class platform (Classroom or Teams)"],"items":[{"t":"1080 × 1920, 9:16, with text kept clear of the bottom edge","bin":1,"fb":"Vertical 9:16 is the Reels format; app buttons cover the bottom."},{"t":"1920 × 1080, 16:9, about 8 Mbps, with chapters for a 12-minute tutorial","bin":0,"fb":"Full HD landscape with chapters is a YouTube format."},{"t":"3840 × 2160, 16:9 high-bitrate master of a 20-minute documentary","bin":0,"fb":"Long-form 4K landscape video belongs on YouTube, which streams it at the right quality for each viewer."},{"t":"A 2-minute screen recording compressed to fit a Teams assignment upload","bin":3,"fb":"An assignment upload is a class platform job with a size limit."},{"t":"1080 × 1350, 4:5 portrait feed post","bin":1,"fb":"4:5 fills more of the phone screen in the Instagram feed."},{"t":"1280 × 720, muted 10-second loop at 2 Mbps with fast start","bin":2,"fb":"A small, muted, web-optimised loop suits a homepage background."},{"t":"An H.264 MP4 at about 3 Mbps for a product page that mobile visitors open","bin":2,"fb":"A modest bitrate keeps the page fast on mobile data."},{"t":"A 1080p MP4 kept under the 500 MB course limit for the teacher to mark","bin":3,"fb":"Class platforms may set upload limits, so size matters more than maximum quality."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched shape, resolution, bitrate and file size to each destination, including the limits of a class platform."}},{"type":"learn","title":"File management, licensing and publishing","html":"<p>Getting your work in front of an audience is the final and essential step. Three habits protect your work and your reputation.</p>\n<p><b>1. File organisation and naming conventions</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Project folder structure</b>: clearly labelled subfolders: <code>/Footage</code>, <code>/Audio</code>, <code>/Graphics</code>, <code>/Exports</code>, <code>/Project Files</code>. Never leave files on the desktop or in Downloads.</li>\n<li><b>File naming</b>: ProjectName_SceneNumber_TakeNumber_Date, for example <code>SMAad_Scene02_Take03_2026-10-15.mp4</code>. Avoid spaces and special characters.</li>\n<li><b>Backup strategy: the 3-2-1 rule</b>: <b>3</b> copies of your data, on <b>2</b> different storage types, with <b>1</b> copy off-site or in the cloud. Hurricane Irma in 2017 showed why: lost footage cannot be recovered, so back up on shoot day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Music, sound effects and licensing</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Licensed music</b>: sources include Artlist, Epidemic Sound and Free Music Archive. Note that \"royalty-free\" does not always mean free: Artlist and Epidemic Sound are paid subscriptions, while Free Music Archive and the YouTube Audio Library offer free tracks with licence conditions (often a credit). Never use commercial tracks, such as a hit soca song, without clearance.</li>\n<li><b>Sound effects</b>: ambient sound, Foley effects and room tone add realism. Layer subtle SFX under the edit for a full, immersive soundscape.</li>\n<li><b>Copyright</b>: using copyrighted material without permission, even briefly, can get your video taken down, or the rights holder may claim it and earn the advertising money from it. Always verify the licence.</li>\n<li><b>Academic integrity</b>: using someone else's footage, audio or graphics without credit or permission is plagiarism, the same as copying a written paper. Credit all sources, collaborators and licensed assets. Collaboration is encouraged; deception is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Publishing and your digital portfolio</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Select your best work</b>: quality over quantity, 3 to 5 pieces that show range.</li>\n<li><b>Upload to Vimeo or YouTube</b>: Vimeo is preferred for its clean interface and password-protected sharing; YouTube's \"unlisted\" option also limits who sees it.</li>\n<li><b>Write a brief description</b>: your role, the tools used and the goal of each project.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Titles and descriptions</b>: for portfolios, describe the project and type, include your name and year, list software and your role (director, editor, camera), and keep it under 150 words. For public platforms, lead with the most important keyword, put search terms in the first two lines, use tags and categories, and add timestamp <b>chapters</b> for videos over 3 minutes.</p>\n<p><b>Professional conduct on set</b>: be on time, communicate clearly, respect the space and handle gear with care. These habits also earn marks for teamwork in the SBA's progress and process section.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In your SBA report, add a short credits list for every music track, image and sound effect, with its source and licence. It shows integrity and protects your group.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Protect your work with good habits. Keep clear folders for footage, audio, graphics, exports and project files, and name files consistently without spaces. Follow the 3-2-1 backup rule: three copies, on two kinds of storage, with one off-site. Use only music you have a licence for, and credit your sources. A Jamaican filmmaker who loses a hard drive in a storm will be glad of a cloud copy.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>File management protects time, money and reputation. Consistent names such as Scene02_Take03 let an editor find the right clip among hundreds, and dates written as YYYY-MM-DD sort correctly. The 3-2-1 rule guards against different risks: an external drive protects against laptop failure, but only an off-site or cloud copy survives a fire, flood or hurricane. Licensing is often misunderstood. \"Royalty-free\" means you do not pay each time you use the track, not that it costs nothing, and free tracks may still require a credit. Using unlicensed music can get a video muted or blocked, or its income claimed by the rights holder. Crediting sources and collaborators is also a matter of academic integrity.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the good publishing habits","intro":"<p>Jerome is finishing a short documentary about the Hillside Farm in Belize's Cayo District for his portfolio. Zara checks his notes against good practice.</p>","q":"Select every statement that shows GOOD practice.","lines":["His project has folders called Footage, Audio, Graphics, Exports and Project Files.","He named a clip \"final final NEW (2).mp4\".","He named a clip SentryHill_Scene04_Take02_2026-10-12.mp4.","He keeps the footage on his laptop, on an external drive and in cloud storage.","He used a popular commercial soca song because it was only 10 seconds long.","He used a Free Music Archive track and credited the artist as the licence requires.","His portfolio has every video he has ever made, including tests.","His YouTube title leads with the main keyword and the description lists his role and the tools used.","He used a friend's drone shots without asking or crediting her."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"win":"Well spotted. Clear folders, consistent names, the 3-2-1 backup rule, licensed and credited music, and keyword-led titles are the good habits.","miss":"Look again. Check each line for naming without spaces, three copies of the footage, licensed and credited media, and quality over quantity in the portfolio.","tutor":[{"h":"Names and copies","p":"<p>Good names follow a pattern without spaces. Good backups follow the 3-2-1 rule.</p>"},{"h":"Short is not safe","p":"<p>Using copyrighted material \"even briefly\" is still a problem.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the good publishing habits (Foundation)","q":"Which TWO show good practice?","lines":["Naming a file Project_Scene02_Take03_2026-10-12.mp4.","Naming a file \"final final (2).mp4\".","Keeping 3 copies of footage on 2 kinds of storage, 1 off-site.","Using a commercial song without permission because it is short."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Clear naming and the 3-2-1 backup rule protect your work.","miss":"Look for the clear file name and the safe backup plan."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the good publishing habits (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Zara checks Jerome's next documentary project. Some habits look careful but still leave a gap; others rest on a common myth.</p>","q":"Select every statement that shows GOOD practice.","lines":["Dates in file names use YYYY-MM-DD so they sort in order.","The footage is on the laptop and on an external drive kept in the same bag.","A paid \"royalty-free\" track is used, with the licence certificate saved in the project folder.","A YouTube Audio Library track that requires a credit is used without one.","The portfolio shows four pieces, each with his role and the tools listed.","A clip from a TV news broadcast is used because anything under 10 seconds is automatically allowed.","Raw footage is uploaded to cloud storage on shoot day, before editing begins.","The YouTube title is \"My video final\" with no description.","Timestamp chapters are added to the 6-minute documentary.","A collaborator's photos are credited by name in the description, with her permission."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,8,9],"win":"Well spotted. Sortable dates, a saved licence, a focused portfolio, same-day cloud backup, chapters and credited, permitted photos are all good practice. Two copies in one bag is not off-site, a required credit must be given, and there is no \"10-second rule\".","miss":"Look again. A backup in the same bag can be lost with the laptop, licence conditions such as credits must be met, and short clips of copyrighted material still need permission."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cloud-based authoring tools: Figma and WeVideo","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Cloud-based authoring tools</b> let you create, edit and publish digital content online. No downloads are needed: just a web browser and an Internet connection. These platforms offer <b>real-time collaboration</b>, <b>anywhere access</b>, <b>automatic updates</b> and <b>scalability</b> for many different creative needs.</p>\n<p>Key examples include <b>Figma</b> (design), <b>WeVideo</b> (video), <b>Canva, Adobe Express, Google Workspace and CapCut</b>, and similar platforms. Learn the main families below.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Family</th><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Popular cloud platforms</td><td><b>Google Docs</b></td><td>Real-time document collaboration with commenting, revision history and links to other Google services (Drive, Slides, Forms)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Microsoft 365</b> web apps</td><td>Browser versions of Word, Excel and PowerPoint with desktop-like features</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Creative Cloud</b></td><td>Professional tools such as Photoshop and Illustrator, with files synced across devices (and Photoshop on the web)</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Design and presentation</td><td><b>Canva</b></td><td>Graphic design with ready-made templates for social media posts, presentations and infographics, using drag-and-drop tools</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Prezi</b></td><td>Non-linear, zoomable presentations that break away from slide-after-slide</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Syllabus examples</td><td><b>Figma</b></td><td>Vector-based interface and graphic design with live collaboration and prototyping</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WeVideo</b></td><td>Browser-based video editing with a timeline, media library, text, transitions and shared projects</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> The older name is \"Microsoft Office 365\". Since 2020 Microsoft has branded most of these plans <b>Microsoft 365</b>. Use the new name in your answers; the web apps are the same idea.</p>\n<p><b>Figma in practice</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Frames</b> set the canvas size (presets such as iPhone or Instagram post); <b>auto layout</b> makes items resize neatly.</li>\n<li><b>Components</b> are reusable elements (a button, a logo) that update everywhere when the main component changes.</li>\n<li><b>Prototype mode</b> links frames with clicks and animations, so you can test an app idea before coding (Module 3).</li>\n<li><b>Real-time collaboration</b>: Zara can pin a comment on Maya's layout from home while Maya keeps working at the Island Pulse Media office.</li>\n<li><b>Export</b>: select a frame, then in the Export panel choose PNG, JPG, SVG or PDF and a scale (1x, 2x).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>WeVideo in practice</b>: upload clips to the media library, drag them onto the timeline, trim, add titles and music, then export MP4 at 1080p. On a shared class project, Malik can cut the Carnival J'ouvert footage while Keisha writes the captions.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 likes \"Which of the following is a cloud-based design tool?\" Figma and Canva are design tools; WeVideo is video; Google Docs and the Microsoft 365 web apps are documents. For \"describe\" questions, name the tool AND one feature, for example \"Canva provides templates for Instagram posts, which saves time for a small business in Grenville.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Cloud-based authoring tools run in a web browser, so there is nothing to install and teams can work together in real time. Figma is for designing interfaces and graphics; WeVideo is for editing video. Canva, Google Docs and the Microsoft 365 web apps are other examples. A student in Guyana and a classmate in Trinidad could edit the same Figma design at once.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The strengths and weaknesses of cloud tools come from the same feature: everything lives online. Real-time collaboration, automatic updates and access from any device are powerful, but they depend on a reliable connection, and free plans may limit storage or export quality, or add watermarks. Files sit on another company's servers, so the terms of service and privacy matter for client work. Figma's components show why cloud design scales: change one main button and every copy across an app updates. A strong evaluation weighs these points for a specific user, such as a small agency in an area with frequent power cuts, and suggests a safeguard, such as regular local exports. Remember that Paper 02 has no Internet.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Cloud tool features","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media team uses cloud tools every day. Match each tool or term to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Figma components","r":"Reusable elements that update everywhere when the main version changes"},{"l":"Figma prototype mode","r":"Linking frames with clicks so a client can test an app idea before coding"},{"l":"Google Docs","r":"Zara and Keisha edit the same script at once, with comments and revision history"},{"l":"Prezi","r":"A zoomable, non-linear presentation for a tourism pitch to a cruise port team"},{"l":"Canva","r":"Drag-and-drop templates for a quick Emancipation Day Instagram post"},{"l":"WeVideo","r":"A browser-based timeline editor for cutting clips, adding text and exporting video"},{"l":"Version history","r":"Restoring yesterday's copy after a teammate deletes a scene by mistake"},{"l":"Local backup","r":"Keeping copies of raw footage on an external drive in case the Internet goes down"}],"win":"Very good. You can match cloud tools to their jobs and explain the features and precautions that make cloud authoring powerful and safe.","tutor":[{"h":"Design, slides, documents or video?","p":"<p>Sort each tool into its family first: design, presentation, document or video.</p>"},{"h":"Safety nets","p":"<p>Two features protect your work: one is in the cloud, one is on your own drive.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Cloud tool features (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Figma","r":"A cloud tool for designing interfaces and graphics"},{"l":"WeVideo","r":"A cloud tool for editing video in the browser"},{"l":"Google Docs","r":"Writing a document together in real time"},{"l":"Canva","r":"Templates for social media graphics"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Cloud tool features (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: specific features and limits rather than whole tools. Match each feature to what it does for the Island Pulse Media team.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Figma auto layout","r":"Buttons resize neatly when the length of their text changes"},{"l":"Figma components","r":"Change one main button and every copy across the app updates"},{"l":"Figma prototype mode","r":"Link frames with clicks so a client can test the flow before any code is written"},{"l":"Figma export at 2x","r":"Produce a sharper PNG for high-density phone screens"},{"l":"WeVideo shared project","r":"Two students edit the same video timeline from different homes"},{"l":"Google Docs revision history","r":"Restore yesterday's script after a teammate deletes a scene"},{"l":"Prezi","r":"A zoomable, non-linear pitch that moves around one large map"},{"l":"Free plan limits","r":"A watermark and lower export quality on a no-cost account"},{"l":"Local export","r":"Download an MP4 or PNG copy in case the connection fails before a deadline"}],"win":"Excellent. You can name specific cloud features, their limits and the safeguard that protects a deadline."}},{"type":"learn","title":"E-learning authoring suites","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>E-learning authoring tools</b> help you create online courses. They turn static content into dynamic learning experiences through four key features: <b>interactive content</b> (engaging multimedia), <b>assessment tools</b> (quizzes and knowledge checks), <b>simulations</b> (real-world practice scenarios) and <b>responsive design</b> (works on all devices).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Group</th><th>Tool</th><th>Strength</th></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Articulate and Adobe</td><td><b>Articulate 360</b></td><td>Complete suite featuring <b>Storyline</b> and <b>Rise</b>: interactive courses with branching scenarios and assessments</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Captivate</b></td><td>Responsive e-learning; specialises in interactive simulations, software demos and virtual reality experiences</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Animate</b></td><td>Animation-focused (formerly <b>Flash</b>) for interactive multimedia and simulations</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">PowerPoint-based and video</td><td><b>iSpring Suite</b></td><td>Turns PowerPoint slides into interactive courses with quizzes and simulations</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Camtasia</b></td><td>Screen recording and video editing for video tutorials with interactive elements</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lectora</b></td><td>Responsive content; excels at branching scenarios and complex assessments</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"4\">Cloud-native and open</td><td><b>Elucidat</b></td><td>Collaborative, responsive course building; no programming skills required</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Easygenerator</b></td><td>Simplicity first: interactive courses with quizzes and branching</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adapt</b></td><td><b>Open-source</b>, based on <b>HTML5</b>; responsive content for different devices and screens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>H5P</b></td><td><b>Open-source</b> framework for interactive videos, presentations, quizzes and games</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Be precise in exams: Storyline, Captivate, Animate, iSpring and Camtasia are mainly <b>installed desktop programs</b> that publish to the web or to a learning platform. Rise, Elucidat and Easygenerator run fully in the <b>browser</b>. H5P is free and plugs into Moodle or WordPress, so a school could build Hurricane-preparedness quizzes at no cost.</p>\n<p><b>Benefits of cloud authoring</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Collaboration</b>: real-time teamwork from anywhere. Malik in Kingston and a client in Barbados review the same draft.</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility</b>: work from any device with Internet, including a phone or a school Chromebook.</li>\n<li><b>Scalability</b>: grow resources as needs change, from one lesson to a full staff-training course for a Grace Bay hotel.</li>\n<li><b>Auto-updates</b>: you always have the latest features, with nothing to install.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The downside is just as real. Cloud tools need a reliable connection, free plans may add watermarks or limit storage, and files sit on someone else's servers (check the terms of service). Paper 02 allows <b>no Internet</b>, so you cannot rely on them in the exam.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Remember Hurricane Irma in 2017. Work stored in the cloud survived when laptops were destroyed, but power and connections were down for weeks, so no one could open it. In an \"evaluate\" answer, give both sides and judge: \"On balance, Rise suits the Bonaire dive school because ..., provided staff also export an offline copy (SCORM or HTML5 package).\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>E-learning authoring tools build online courses with interactive content, quizzes, simulations and layouts that work on any device. Examples include Articulate 360, Adobe Captivate, iSpring Suite, Camtasia and the free H5P. Some are installed programs; others run in the browser. A hotel in Antigua could use one to train new staff on its booking system.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing a suite is a fit-for-purpose decision. Captivate excels at software simulations, so it suits training staff on a new billing system; Storyline and Lectora handle complex branching scenarios; iSpring suits trainers who already work in PowerPoint; H5P is free and plugs into Moodle or WordPress, which matters on a school budget. Consider the output too: SCORM or HTML5 packages let a course run inside a learning platform and report scores back. The cloud's benefits, collaboration and automatic updates, come with risks: connectivity, subscription costs and data on someone else's servers. A Grade One evaluation names the users, their devices and budget, weighs two tools and recommends one with a safeguard, such as an offline export.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite?","intro":"<p>Keisha is building a toolkit list for the Island Pulse Media. Sort each tool into its family.</p>","main":"Which family does this tool belong to?","bins":["Cloud platform (documents and files)","Design and presentation tool","E-learning authoring suite"],"items":[{"t":"iSpring Suite: turning PowerPoint slides into a quiz-based course.","bin":2,"fb":"iSpring converts PowerPoint into interactive e-learning."},{"t":"Microsoft 365 web apps: editing a budget in Excel in the browser.","bin":0,"fb":"The Microsoft 365 web apps are browser versions of Word, Excel and PowerPoint."},{"t":"Canva: a template for a breadfruit festival flyer.","bin":1,"fb":"Canva is a drag-and-drop graphic design tool with templates."},{"t":"Adobe Creative Cloud: syncing Photoshop files between the office and home.","bin":0,"fb":"Creative Cloud is a cloud platform for professional tools with cross-device syncing."},{"t":"Google Docs: co-writing a Carnival press release with comments.","bin":0,"fb":"Google Docs is a cloud document platform built for real-time collaboration and revision history."},{"t":"Articulate 360: a branching customer-service course with Storyline.","bin":2,"fb":"Articulate 360 (Storyline and Rise) builds interactive courses with branching and assessments."},{"t":"H5P: free interactive video questions for a Moodle class.","bin":2,"fb":"H5P is an open-source framework for interactive learning content."},{"t":"Adobe Captivate: a software demo and simulation for new electricity company billing staff.","bin":2,"fb":"Captivate specialises in simulations and software demos for e-learning."},{"t":"Prezi: a zoomable pitch for a national tourism board.","bin":1,"fb":"Prezi makes non-linear, zoomable presentations."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can tell cloud platforms, design and presentation tools, and e-learning authoring suites apart.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is the audience?","p":"<p>If the end product is a course with quizzes for learners, think e-learning suite.</p>"},{"h":"Look at the output","p":"<p>Documents and synced files point one way; flyers and pitches point another.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Google Docs","bin":0,"fb":"A cloud document platform."},{"t":"Canva","bin":1,"fb":"A design tool with templates."},{"t":"Prezi","bin":1,"fb":"A zoomable presentation tool."},{"t":"Articulate 360","bin":2,"fb":"An e-learning authoring suite."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Cloud platform, design tool or e-learning suite? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a fourth family, video editing tools, and more tools to sort. Sort each tool into its MAIN family.</p>","main":"Which family does this tool mainly belong to?","bins":["Cloud platform (documents and files)","Design and presentation tool","Video editing tool","E-learning authoring suite"],"items":[{"t":"Figma","bin":1,"fb":"Interface and graphic design with live collaboration."},{"t":"Canva","bin":1,"fb":"Template-based graphic design, mainly for social posts and presentations."},{"t":"Lectora","bin":3,"fb":"E-learning authoring with strong branching and assessments."},{"t":"Adobe Creative Cloud","bin":0,"fb":"A cloud platform that syncs professional tools and files across devices."},{"t":"Prezi","bin":1,"fb":"Zoomable, non-linear presentations."},{"t":"Google Docs","bin":0,"fb":"Real-time document collaboration: a cloud platform."},{"t":"WeVideo","bin":2,"fb":"A browser-based video timeline editor."},{"t":"Easygenerator","bin":3,"fb":"Simple, browser-based interactive course building."},{"t":"Microsoft 365 web apps","bin":0,"fb":"Browser versions of Word, Excel and PowerPoint: a cloud document platform."},{"t":"Articulate Rise","bin":3,"fb":"Browser-based course building, part of Articulate 360."},{"t":"Adapt","bin":3,"fb":"Open-source, HTML5-based responsive e-learning."},{"t":"CapCut","bin":2,"fb":"A popular video editing tool for phones and desktops."}],"win":"Excellent. You sorted twelve tools into four families by their main purpose."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan the production of a 30-second promo","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: audio and video production","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Spice Lane Grill</b> in Gouyave, Grenada, wants a 30-second promotional video for its weekend live music nights. The video will be posted on Instagram Reels and YouTube, and played on a muted screen in the restaurant. Filming will take place outdoors at sunset on a windy evening, plus one interview with the chef inside the noisy kitchen. The owner has a smartphone, a tablet, a tripod, a gimbal, a lavalier microphone, one LED panel and a reflector. The edit will be done in OpenShot and Audacity.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe how you would <b>capture video</b> for the outdoor shots and the chef interview, naming the device and stabilisation you would use and justifying each. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> steps you would take to record clean sound <b>outdoors</b> and <b>TWO</b> steps for <b>indoors</b>. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how you would light the chef interview using <b>three-point lighting</b> with the equipment available. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Outline the main editing and export steps, including the audio format for the voice-over master and the MP4 settings for <b>TWO</b> platforms. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":480,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> For the outdoor shots I would use ... on a gimbal because ... For the interview I would ... on a tripod because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Outdoors, I would ... and ... Indoors, I would ... and ...<br><b>(c)</b> The key light would be ... The fill would be ... The back light ...<br><b>(d)</b> In Audacity I would ... and save the master as ... In OpenShot I would ... For Instagram Reels I would export ... For YouTube ...</p>","checklist":["I named devices and stabilisation (tripod, gimbal) and justified each.","I gave two outdoor and two indoor sound steps, including a microphone choice.","I gave the position and purpose of the key, fill and back lights.","I named WAV for the master, used the Clean, Balance, Layer, Master mix and explained why.","I used camera terms (aperture, shutter speed, ISO, white balance, frame rate).","I gave MP4 settings (aspect ratio, resolution, codec) for two platforms.","I linked my answer to the restaurant, sunset and kitchen scenario."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Capture and sound","marks":7,"look":["gimbal","tripod","lavalier|lapel|clip","wind|windscreen|dead cat|foam","fan|extractor|echo|room tone|switch off|turn off|quiet","headphone|monitor|level|-12|-6|test|backup|clap|sync","aperture|f/|shutter|iso|white balance|180|exposure|frame rate|fps"],"min":4,"strong":"You chose capture equipment and sound techniques that fit each location and justified them.","weak":"Use the gimbal for smooth moving sunset shots and the tripod for a steady interview. Outdoors: a lavalier with a windscreen, shelter from the wind. Indoors: switch off the extractor fan and record room tone; monitor with headphones."},{"criterion":"Lighting","marks":3,"look":["key","fill|reflector","back ?light|rim|hair light|separat","45|side|behind|opposite"],"min":3,"strong":"Your three-point setup gives clear positions and purposes using the available equipment.","weak":"Place the LED panel as the key light at about 45 degrees to the camera, use the reflector on the opposite side as fill, and use a kitchen light behind the chef as a back light to separate him from the background."},{"criterion":"Editing and export","marks":5,"look":["wav","noise reduction|normali[sz]e|fade","razor|cut|trim|transition|text|title|caption|colou?r","9:16|1080 ?(by|x) ?1920|vertical","16:9|1920 ?(by|x) ?1080|landscape","mp4|h\\.?264","clean|balance|layer|master|limit","aac|48 ?khz|j-cut|l-cut|cut on action|correct"],"min":4,"strong":"You described a clear edit pipeline and correct export settings for each platform.","weak":"Name the steps: clean the voice-over in Audacity (noise reduction, normalise) and save a WAV master; cut to 30 seconds in OpenShot, add captions for the muted screen; export H.264 MP4 at 1080 by 1920 (9:16) for Reels and 1920 by 1080 (16:9) for YouTube."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> For the outdoor sunset shots I would use the <b>smartphone on the gimbal</b>, because the gimbal keeps a moving camera smooth as I walk past the band and the tables, and the phone records good 1080p footage. I would lock focus and exposure on the musicians so brightness does not jump as the sun sets, record at 1080p 30 fps with a shutter speed of 1/60 (the 180-degree rule) and set the white balance manually for the golden-hour light. For the chef interview I would put the smartphone on the <b>tripod</b>, because the shot must stay perfectly still for a minute or more and handheld shake would distract viewers. I would film a 16:9 master and leave space in the frame for a 9:16 crop.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Outdoors</b>, I would clip the <b>lavalier microphone</b> to the presenter and cover it with a foam or furry <b>windscreen</b>, because wind is the main noise source near the sea. I would also place the speaker with their back to the wind behind a wall, and wait for loud passing traffic before recording. <b>Indoors</b>, I would ask the staff to <b>switch off the extractor fan</b> and stop banging pans during takes, and record 10 seconds of <b>room tone</b> for noise reduction. I would <b>monitor with headphones</b> and keep peaks in the target zone between -12 dB and -6 dB, and clap once at the start so I can sync the lavalier audio in the edit.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I would use the <b>LED panel as the key light</b>, about 45 degrees to one side of the camera and slightly above the chef's eye level, as the main light on his face. The <b>reflector</b> would be the <b>fill</b> on the opposite side, bouncing some of the key light into the shadows so they are softer. For the <b>back light</b>, I would position the chef so a kitchen light is behind him, lighting his shoulders and separating him from the dark background. I would set the white balance to match the LED panel.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> In <b>Audacity</b>, I would follow the Clean, Balance, Layer, Master workflow: apply Noise Reduction to the interview and voice-over, Normalize the voice to -1 dB, layer the music under it with Auto Duck and a fade out, then apply a Limiter so the output stays below 0 dB. I would save a <b>WAV master</b>, because it is uncompressed and keeps full quality for the video editor. In <b>OpenShot</b>, I would import the clips, build a rough cut in storyboard order, use the Razor tool to trim it to exactly 30 seconds, cutting on action, use hard cuts with one dissolve, correct the white balance and add a warm grade to suit the sunset mood, and add <b>text overlays</b> with the date and a call to action, plus captions, because the restaurant screen is muted. I would export an <b>H.264 MP4</b> at <b>1080 by 1920 (9:16), 30 fps</b> for Instagram Reels, keeping text away from the bottom edge, and a <b>1920 by 1080 (16:9)</b> H.264 MP4 with AAC audio at 48 kHz stereo for <b>YouTube</b>. Finally, I would watch each exported file on a phone to check it.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Production questions reward <b>specific, justified technique</b>. Name the equipment, the setting and the reason: \"a lavalier with a windscreen, because ...\" Always give both the position and purpose of each light, and full export settings (codec, aspect ratio, resolution) for each platform.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Plan the production of a 30-second promo (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: audio and video production, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> what you would use to keep the camera steady for (i) the walking sunset shots and (ii) the chef interview, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE way to reduce wind noise outdoors and ONE way to reduce noise in the kitchen. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> where you would place the LED panel (key light) and the reflector (fill) for the chef interview. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the audio format you would save the voice-over master in, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the shape and size of MP4 you would export for Instagram Reels, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) For the sunset shots I would use a ... because ... (ii) For the interview I would use a ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Outdoors I would ... In the kitchen I would ...<br><b>(c)</b> The LED panel would go ... The reflector would go ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would save the master as a ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> For Reels I would export a ... MP4 because ...</p>","checklist":["I chose a gimbal for moving shots and a tripod for the interview.","I gave one outdoor and one indoor way to cut noise.","I placed the key light and the reflector.","I chose WAV for the master and said why.","I chose a vertical 9:16 MP4 for Reels and said why."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Stabilisation","marks":2,"look":["gimbal","tripod"],"min":2,"strong":"The right support for each kind of shot.","weak":"Use a gimbal for smooth walking shots and a tripod for a steady interview."},{"criterion":"Reducing noise","marks":2,"look":["wind|windscreen|dead cat|foam|shelter|lavalier","fan|extractor|switch off|turn off|quiet|close"],"min":2,"strong":"One outdoor and one indoor step.","weak":"Outdoors use a windscreen on the mic; indoors switch off the extractor fan."},{"criterion":"Key and fill","marks":2,"look":["45|side|front|angle|above","reflector|opposite|other side|fill"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear positions for the key and fill.","weak":"Put the LED panel at about 45 degrees to one side as the key, and the reflector on the other side as the fill."},{"criterion":"Master format","marks":2,"look":["wav","uncompress|full quality|quality|edit|lossless"],"min":2,"strong":"WAV with a sound reason.","weak":"Save the master as WAV, because it is uncompressed and keeps full quality for editing."},{"criterion":"Reels export","marks":2,"look":["9:16|1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1920|vertical","phone|full screen|reels|upright|instagram"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct shape and size with a reason.","weak":"Export a vertical 9:16 MP4 at 1080 × 1920, because Reels fill an upright phone screen."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) For the sunset shots I would use a gimbal, because it keeps the phone smooth while I walk. (ii) For the interview I would use a tripod, because the shot must stay still.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Outdoors I would put a furry windscreen on the lavalier mic. In the kitchen I would ask the staff to switch off the extractor fan while we record.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The LED panel would go about 45 degrees to one side of the camera, a little above the chef's eyes. The reflector would go on the other side to soften the shadows.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I would save the master as a WAV, because it is uncompressed and keeps full quality for editing.</p><p><b>(e)</b> For Reels I would export a vertical 9:16 MP4 at 1080 × 1920, because it fills the screen of a phone held upright.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the correct choice and 1 for the reason or position. Insist on the exact terms (gimbal, tripod, windscreen, key, fill, WAV, 9:16). Students ready for more should add one camera setting, such as the shutter speed, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Plan the production of a 30-second promo (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: audio and video production, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Spice Lane Grill</b> in Gouyave, Grenada, wants a <b>30-second</b> promotional video for Instagram Reels, a <b>60-second</b> version for YouTube and a version for a <b>muted</b> screen inside the restaurant. Filming takes place outdoors at sunset (good light lasts about 20 minutes) on a windy evening, plus ONE interview take with the busy chef in a noisy kitchen lit by cool overhead fluorescent tubes, with a warm tungsten lamp over the grill. The owner has a smartphone, a tablet, a tripod, a gimbal, a lavalier microphone, one LED panel and a reflector. The edit will be done in OpenShot and Audacity, using a licensed music track.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Plan</b> the capture for both locations: devices, stabilisation, exposure triangle settings, white balance and frame rate, justifying each, and explain how you will make the most of the short golden hour. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> the sound for both locations (microphone, placement, levels, monitoring, backup and sync), and <b>evaluate</b> the risk of having only ONE interview take. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Design</b> a lighting setup for the kitchen interview that solves the mixed colour temperatures and the overhead fluorescents, using only the equipment available. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Outline</b> the post-production: edit workflow, cutting principles, colour correction and grading, the Audacity mix stages, and export settings for all THREE outputs. <i>(7 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> your file management and licensing plan. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":800,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Outdoors: ... on a ... at ... fps, shutter ..., aperture ..., ISO ..., white balance ... because ... Golden hour: ... Interview: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Outdoors: ... Indoors: ... Levels: ... Monitoring and backup: ... The single take is risky because ... so I will ...<br><b>(c)</b> The key ... The fill ... The back light ... The colour problem is solved by ...<br><b>(d)</b> Ingest ... rough cut ... fine cut ... cutting principles ... colour ... Audacity: Clean, Balance, Layer, Master ... Exports: Reels ..., YouTube ..., restaurant screen ...<br><b>(e)</b> Folders and names ... backup ... licence ...</p>","checklist":["I gave specific settings (fps, shutter, aperture, ISO, white balance) and justified each.","I planned how to use the 20-minute golden hour.","My sound plan covers both locations, with levels, monitoring, backup and sync.","I evaluated the single-take risk and planned how to reduce it.","My lighting plan solves both the overhead fluorescents and the mixed colour temperatures.","My post-production covers the edit workflow, cutting, colour, the four mix stages and three different exports.","I explained file management and the music licence."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Capture plan","marks":6,"look":["gimbal","tripod","fps|frame rate","shutter|1/50|1/60|180","aperture|f/|iso","white balance|kelvin|\\d{4} ?k","golden hour|sunset|shot list|20 minutes|order"],"min":5,"strong":"Specific, justified settings for each location and a realistic plan for the short golden hour.","weak":"Give device, support and settings for each location with reasons, and say how a shot list helps you capture everything in 20 minutes."},{"criterion":"Sound plan and single-take risk","marks":6,"look":["lavalier|lapel|clip","windscreen|dead cat|foam|wind","fan|extractor|room tone|switch off","-12|-6|level|headroom","headphone|monitor","backup|second device|clap|sync","one take|single take|risk|second camera|tablet"],"min":5,"strong":"Thorough sound plan with a clear evaluation of the single-take risk and how to reduce it.","weak":"Plan mic, placement, levels and monitoring for both places, then explain why one take is risky and what backups you will run."},{"criterion":"Lighting design","marks":4,"look":["key|led","fill|reflector","back ?light|rim|separat","fluorescent|tungsten|mixed|colou?r temperature|white balance|switch off|gel"],"min":3,"strong":"A practical three-point setup that solves both lighting problems with the kit available.","weak":"Place key, fill and back light, then explain how you will deal with the overhead tubes and the warm grill lamp, for example by switching one off and setting white balance to the LED."},{"criterion":"Post-production and exports","marks":7,"look":["rough cut|fine cut|ingest","cut on action|j-cut|l-cut|motivat|pacing","correct","grade|grading","clean|balance|layer|master|limit","9:16|1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1920","16:9|1920 ?(by|x|×) ?1080","caption|subtitle|muted|text"],"min":6,"strong":"A complete post-production plan with three correctly specified exports.","weak":"Cover the edit workflow, two cutting principles, correction then grading, the four mix stages, and settings for Reels, YouTube and the muted screen (with captions)."},{"criterion":"File management and licensing","marks":2,"look":["folder|name|naming|3-2-1|backup|cloud","licen|credit|permission"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear plan for files, backups and the music licence.","weak":"Describe the folder structure, naming and 3-2-1 backup, and say how the music licence will be kept and credited."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Outdoors:</i> the smartphone on the <b>gimbal</b> for smooth moving shots past the band and tables, recording 1080p at <b>30 fps</b> with a <b>1/60</b> shutter (the 180-degree rule) so motion looks natural, plus some 60 fps shots of dancers for slow motion. I would lock focus and exposure on the musicians, keep ISO at its lowest workable value to avoid grain as the light fades, and set <b>white balance</b> manually (about 5600 K, or a \"daylight\" preset) so the warm sunset is recorded as warm rather than corrected away. Because golden hour lasts only about 20 minutes, I would work from a <b>shot list</b> in priority order: the hero wide shot of the restaurant at sunset first, then medium shots, then close-ups of food, which can still be lit by the LED panel after the sun has gone. I would film in 4K 16:9 where possible so 9:16 crops stay sharp. <i>Interview:</i> the smartphone on the <b>tripod</b>, because the shot must stay perfectly still, at 1080p 30 fps, 1/60, with white balance set to the LED panel.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Outdoors:</i> the <b>lavalier</b> on the presenter's shirt, 15 to 20 cm below the chin, under a foam or furry windscreen, with the speaker's back to the wind. <i>Indoors:</i> ask the staff to switch off the extractor fan for the take and pause pan noise, and record 10 seconds of room tone for gentle noise reduction. In both places I would set levels so speech peaks between <b>-12 and -6 dB</b>, <b>monitor on headphones</b> and clap once at the start to sync. <i>Single-take risk:</i> with only one interview take, any fault (a dropped lavalier, a clipped peak or a loud pan) cannot be fixed by reshooting, so the whole interview could be lost. To reduce the risk I would test levels with a practice question, record a backup on the tablet's microphone placed close to the chef, film a second angle on the tablet for cutaways, and ask short, focused questions so the chef can give complete answers within one take.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The overhead fluorescent tubes create shadows under the eyes and a cool colour, while the grill lamp is warm, so the two clash. I would <b>switch off</b> the overhead tubes above the chef if safe, or move him out from directly under them. The <b>LED panel</b> becomes the key light at about 45 degrees to the camera and slightly above eye level; the <b>reflector</b> on the opposite side is the fill, bouncing the key into the shadows; and the warm grill lamp behind him works as a <b>back light</b>, giving a warm rim that separates him from the dark kitchen. Setting the camera's white balance to the LED (about 5600 K) keeps his face neutral while the rim stays warm, which suits the mood.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Edit:</i> ingest the footage into labelled folders, build a rough cut in storyboard order, then a fine cut with the Razor tool, cutting on action (as the chef flips food) and using a J-cut so the music is heard before we see the band. Pacing is fast for the band and slower for the sunset. I would <b>correct</b> white balance and exposure so all clips match, then apply one warm <b>grade</b>. <i>Audacity:</i> <b>Clean</b> (gentle noise reduction using the room tone), <b>Balance</b> (normalise the voice to about -1 dB), <b>Layer</b> (licensed music ducked under the voice, with a fade out) and <b>Master</b> (a limiter below 0 dB), saving a WAV master. <i>Exports:</i> Reels, 30 s, H.264 MP4 at 1080 × 1920 (9:16), 30 fps, AAC 48 kHz, with text clear of the bottom edge; YouTube, 60 s, H.264 MP4 at 1920 × 1080 (16:9), about 8 Mbps; restaurant screen, 1920 × 1080 H.264 MP4 with <b>burned-in captions</b> and on-screen text carrying the message, because the screen is muted. I would watch every export on the device it is meant for.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> A project folder with Footage, Audio, Graphics, Exports and Project Files, with files named SpiceLane_Scene02_Take01_2026-11-07.mp4. The footage is backed up on shoot day using the 3-2-1 rule (laptop, external drive and cloud). The music licence certificate is saved in the project folder and the track is credited in the YouTube description.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 6 + 4 + 7 + 2). Reward specific, justified settings rather than lists of equipment. The strongest answers solve the problems hidden in the stimulus: the short golden hour (a prioritised shot list), the single interview take (backups and a second angle), the mixed lighting (switch off or move away from the tubes and set white balance to the key) and the muted screen (captions). Cap answers that give one generic export for all three outputs.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U6","title":"2D and 3D animation concepts","objectives":["Explain the 2D animation fundamentals of keyframing, timing, spacing and interpolation (tweening), and use them in a 2D bouncing ball exercise with squash and stretch and ease in and ease out.","Describe and apply all twelve principles of animation (Thomas and Johnston), including straight-ahead action versus pose-to-pose.","Use tweening tools in Adobe Animate and free 2D software such as Pencil2D, OpenToonz and Synfig Studio, and export animation in suitable formats.","Explain 3D modelling fundamentals: vertices, edges, faces, polygons, primitives and meshes, and apply a simple material or texture to a 3D shape.","Animate a 3D bouncing ball in Blender using keyframes, the Timeline, Dope Sheet and Graph Editor, and describe the four key poses of a basic walk cycle.","Choose appropriate render and export settings for animation in a Paper 02 practical or the SBA product's animation or 3D component.","Compare 2D and 3D animation by space, process and look (modelling, layout, rendering), weigh the strengths and challenges of each, and identify traditional, legacy (Flash, Toon Boom Studio, Creatoon, Pencil) and current free tools."],"videos":[{"title":"The 12 principles of animation","q":"12 principles of animation explained examples"},{"title":"Bouncing ball in 2D","q":"bouncing ball animation tutorial timing spacing squash and stretch"},{"title":"Bouncing ball in Blender","q":"blender bouncing ball tutorial graph editor squash and stretch beginner"},{"title":"Basic walk cycle","q":"walk cycle animation contact down passing up explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What animation is: frames, keyframes, timing and spacing","html":"<p><b>Animation</b> is <b>the art of manipulating pictures so they appear as moving images</b>, and <b>computer animation</b> is <b>the art of creating moving pictures through computer technology</b>. Small changes between images create the <b>illusion of movement</b> when the sequence plays. Behind the movement sits a lot of work: designing, drawing, creating layouts and preparing photographic sequences all help bring an idea to life, whether it is a 1960s cartoon or the visual effects in a film such as <i>Avengers: Endgame</i>.</p>\n<p>Each still image is a <b>frame</b>. Film and most animation run at <b>24 frames per second (fps)</b>; web and social video often use 25 or 30 fps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Keyframe</b>: a frame where the animator sets an important position or value, such as the start, the end or the extreme of a movement. In classical animation the senior artist drew the <b>key drawings</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Inbetweens</b> (tweens): the frames between keyframes that make the motion smooth. In software, the computer can generate them; this is <b>interpolation</b> or <b>tweening</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Timing</b>: <b>the number of drawings or frames assigned to an action</b>. At a fixed playback rate, more frames make the action slower; fewer make it faster. Timing also shows weight and mood: a bowling ball falls with heavy, quick timing; a balloon drifts slowly.</li>\n<li><b>Spacing</b>: <b>how far</b> the object moves from one frame to the next. Drawings close together look slow; drawings far apart look fast. Changing spacing inside the same timing creates acceleration and deceleration.</li>\n<li><b>Holds</b>: frames where the object does not move, giving the viewer time to read a pose.</li>\n<li><b>On ones / on twos</b>: a new drawing every frame (smooth, more work) or every second frame (12 drawings per second, the classic hand-drawn look).</li>\n</ul>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 80\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Spacing chart: circles close together at the left (slow) and far apart at the right (fast)\">\n<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"32\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"118\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"234\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">key 1: slow (close spacing)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">key 2: fast</text>\n</svg>\n<p>Malik, the video and animation lead at the regional agency Island Pulse Media, explains it to interns like this: \"Timing is the length of the song; spacing is where you put the beats.\" A 2-second logo reveal for an electricity safety message is 48 frames at 24 fps. Whether it feels calm or punchy depends on the spacing inside those 48 frames.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often tests timing versus spacing. Timing = number of frames (duration). Spacing = distance between positions (speed changes). Never use the two words as if they mean the same thing.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Animation is a series of still pictures, called frames, shown quickly so things seem to move. Keyframes are the important poses; inbetweens fill the gaps between them. Timing is how many frames an action takes. Spacing is how far the object moves from one frame to the next. A two-second logo for a Belize radio station is 48 frames at 24 fps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Timing and spacing work together but answer different questions. Timing decides <b>how long</b> an action lasts; spacing decides <b>how it moves</b> within that time. Two 24-frame moves can feel completely different: even spacing looks mechanical, while drawings bunched near the start and end and spread in the middle look natural. Holds matter too. A pose held for too few frames cannot be read, while one held too long feels frozen, so animators often let the pose drift very slightly (a \"moving hold\"). Animating on twos halves the drawing work and gives a classic look, but very fast actions may need ones to stay smooth. In exam answers, never use timing and spacing as if they mean the same thing.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the animation term","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is animating a short Carnival J'ouvert promo. Match each term to what he is doing.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Keyframe","r":"Setting the masquerader's starting pose and the highest point of the jump"},{"l":"Inbetweens","r":"The frames that fill the gap between those two poses"},{"l":"Timing","r":"Deciding the jump will last 18 frames"},{"l":"Spacing","r":"Placing drawings close together at the top of the jump and far apart near the ground"},{"l":"Hold","r":"Keeping the final pose still for 12 frames so viewers can read it"},{"l":"Frame rate","r":"Setting the project to 24 frames per second"},{"l":"Animating on twos","r":"Showing each new drawing for two frames to save drawing time"}],"win":"Very good. You can separate keyframes, inbetweens, timing, spacing and holds, which are the building blocks of every animation question.","tutor":[{"h":"How long or how far?","p":"<p>Number of frames = timing. Distance between positions = spacing.</p>"},{"h":"Who makes the frame?","p":"<p>The important poses the animator sets are keys. The filler frames are inbetweens.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the animation term (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Keyframe","r":"An important pose set by the animator"},{"l":"Inbetweens","r":"Frames that fill the gap between key poses"},{"l":"Timing","r":"How many frames an action takes"},{"l":"Spacing","r":"How far the object moves each frame"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the animation term (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: nine terms, including breakdowns, moving holds and animating on ones. Match each term to what Malik is doing on the J'ouvert promo.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Keyframe","r":"Setting the extreme pose at the very top of the masquerader's leap"},{"l":"Breakdown","r":"The drawing between two keys that decides the head dips in an arc as it turns"},{"l":"Inbetweens","r":"The many drawings that smooth out the move once the keys and breakdowns are done"},{"l":"Timing","r":"Changing the jump from 18 frames to 12 so it feels lighter and quicker"},{"l":"Spacing","r":"Bunching drawings near the top of the leap so the dancer seems to hang in the air"},{"l":"Hold","r":"Keeping the final pose for 12 frames so viewers can read it"},{"l":"Moving hold","r":"Letting a paused pose drift very slightly so the character does not look frozen"},{"l":"Animating on ones","r":"A new drawing on every single frame for a very fast spin"},{"l":"Animating on twos","r":"Each drawing shown for two frames, giving 12 drawings per second"}],"win":"Excellent. You can separate keys, breakdowns and inbetweens, timing from spacing, and the different kinds of hold."}},{"type":"learn","title":"2D and 3D animation compared","html":"<p>The learning goal: understand how still images become animation, then <b>compare the two approaches</b> by their <b>space, process and look</b>.</p>\n<p><b>2D animation lives in width and height.</b> Characters and objects exist only in two-dimensional space. It is the traditional style, with roots in the 1800s: optical toys such as the zoetrope spun drawings into motion, and hand-drawn films followed in the early 1900s. Each drawing differs slightly from the last, and frames play at <b>24 frames per second</b> to create the illusion of motion. Classics include <i>Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs</i> (1937), <i>Bambi</i> (1942) and <i>The Little Mermaid</i> (1989). Digital 2D figures are created or edited as <b>bitmap graphics</b> (built from pixels) or <b>vector graphics</b> (built from mathematically defined paths).</p>\n<p><b>3D animation moves objects through digital space.</b> Digital models have <b>width, height and depth</b>, and can be moved and rotated like real objects; their position, pose and movement change over time. A 3D figure usually starts as a <b>polygon mesh</b>: a surface built from connected polygons. You see 3D in TV, films, games, virtual reality and presentation graphics. The handout's famous example is James Cameron's <i>Avatar</i>, released in <b>2009</b>.</p>\n<p><b>From scene to finished image: the three main stages of 3D</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Modelling</b>: generate the 3D objects; build the shapes that become characters, props and environments.</li>\n<li><b>Layout</b>: position objects in the scene; decide where the action takes place and what the camera sees. Animators also pose and move the models here.</li>\n<li><b>Rendering</b>: produce the finished output, turning the digital scene into images with lighting, surfaces and shadows.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This list puts layout first. In most studio pipelines the models are built first and then placed in the layout, although a rough layout with simple blocks is sometimes made early to plan the camera. Either way, the stages work together.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>2D animation</th><th>3D animation</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Space</b></td><td>Width and height</td><td>Width, height and depth</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Process</b></td><td>Flat drawings, often created frame by frame (or tweened)</td><td>Digital models, layout, modelling and rendering</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Look</b></td><td>An expressive, hand-drawn feel</td><td>A look with realistic depth</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Caribbean example</b></td><td>A hand-drawn Anansi folk tale for a Jamaican children's programme</td><td>A 3D fly-through of a planned cruise terminal in St. John's, Antigua</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A \"distinguish\" or \"compare\" answer must use the same criteria on both sides. Use these three: <b>space, process and look</b>, then give one named example of each.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>2D animation is flat: characters have only width and height, like a drawing. 3D animation adds depth, so models can be turned and seen from any angle. 3D work goes through modelling (building), layout (placing and posing) and rendering (making the final images). A hand-drawn Anansi story is 2D; a fly-through of a new hotel in Nevis is 3D.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing between 2D and 3D is a decision about <b>purpose, look and resources</b>, not about which is \"better\". Once a 3D model is built it can be reused from any angle, which saves time across a series, but modelling, texturing and rendering demand skill and computing power up front. 2D gives freedom to exaggerate and a hand-made warmth, but each new angle may need new drawings. Many modern productions blend the two: 3D backgrounds with 2D characters, or 3D rendered with \"toon\" shading to look hand-drawn. Remember too that 2D figures can be bitmap (pixels, which blur when enlarged) or vector (paths, which stay sharp at any size), which affects how they can be reused in print and on screen.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"2D or 3D animation?","intro":"<p>Using the space, process and look criteria, sort each feature or project into 2D or 3D animation.</p>","main":"Is this 2D or 3D animation?","bins":["2D animation","3D animation"],"items":[{"t":"The figure starts as a polygon mesh","bin":1,"fb":"3D figures are usually built from a polygon mesh of connected polygons."},{"t":"Figures drawn as bitmap or vector graphics in Krita or Synfig","bin":0,"fb":"Bitmap and vector drawings are the two ways to make 2D figures."},{"t":"A virtual reality tour of Fort George in St. George's that visitors can walk around","bin":1,"fb":"VR needs models with depth so the viewer can move through the space."},{"t":"Classic films such as Bambi and The Little Mermaid","bin":0,"fb":"These Disney classics were hand-drawn 2D animation."},{"t":"The stages of modelling, layout and rendering","bin":1,"fb":"These are the three main stages of 3D animation."},{"t":"Models have width, height and depth and can be rotated like real objects","bin":1,"fb":"Depth and the ability to rotate a model to any angle are what make it 3D."},{"t":"Each drawing differs slightly from the last, played at 24 fps","bin":0,"fb":"Sequences of slightly different flat drawings are the traditional 2D process."},{"t":"Objects exist only in width and height","bin":0,"fb":"Two dimensions, width and height, is the definition of 2D space."}],"win":"Well sorted. 2D lives in width and height with drawn or vector figures; 3D adds depth and works through modelling, layout and rendering.","tutor":[{"h":"Count the dimensions","p":"<p>Is there depth? Can you walk around it or rotate it?</p>"},{"h":"Drawn or built?","p":"<p>Drawings and paths point one way; meshes and renders point the other.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"2D or 3D? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Width and height only","bin":0,"fb":"That is 2D space."},{"t":"Width, height and depth","bin":1,"fb":"Depth makes it 3D."},{"t":"Built from a polygon mesh","bin":1,"fb":"Meshes are 3D."},{"t":"Hand-drawn frames like Snow White","bin":0,"fb":"Classic hand-drawn films are 2D."}]},
 "up": {"title":"2D or 3D animation? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin for features that belong to BOTH approaches. Sort each feature or project.</p>","main":"Is this 2D, 3D or both?","bins":["2D animation","3D animation","Both 2D and 3D"],"items":[{"t":"A character redrawn for every new viewing angle","bin":0,"fb":"Flat drawings must be redrawn to show a new angle: a 2D challenge."},{"t":"A polygon mesh that can be rotated to any angle","bin":1,"fb":"A mesh with depth is 3D."},{"t":"Blender, which has Grease Pencil drawing as well as meshes","bin":2,"fb":"Blender supports 3D, and 2D through Grease Pencil."},{"t":"Keyframes and interpolation between them","bin":2,"fb":"Both 2D tweening and 3D animation use keys and interpolation."},{"t":"The stages of modelling, layout and rendering","bin":1,"fb":"These are the three main stages of 3D."},{"t":"Vector figures that stay sharp at any size","bin":0,"fb":"Vector paths are one of the two ways to make 2D figures."},{"t":"Avatar (2009)","bin":1,"fb":"The handout's example of 3D animation."},{"t":"A playback rate of 24 frames per second","bin":2,"fb":"Frame rate applies to every kind of animation."},{"t":"The 12 principles of animation","bin":2,"fb":"The principles apply to both 2D and 3D."},{"t":"The Little Mermaid (1989)","bin":0,"fb":"A hand-drawn 2D classic."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated what is unique to each approach from what they share: keys, interpolation, frame rate and the twelve principles."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The 12 principles of animation (1 to 6)","html":"<p>They are <b>the principles behind convincing motion</b>, or the \"12 Laws of Animation\": they guide clear, consistent and expressive animation, and you use them in <b>both 2D and 3D</b>. They were outlined by Disney animators <b>Ollie Johnston and Frank Thomas</b>, who worked on <i>Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs</i> and <i>Pinocchio</i>, and published them in their 1981 book <i>The Illusion of Life: Disney Animation</i>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Squash and stretch</b> gives objects weight. It makes drawings feel flexible, from a bouncing ball to the muscles of a face. The object flattens (squashes) on impact and elongates (stretches) when moving fast, while keeping the <b>same volume</b>. Example: a rubber ball squashing on the Castries waterfront; a character's cheeks puffing as she blows a conch shell.</li>\n<li><b>Anticipation</b> prepares the audience: it makes the next action readable and believable. \"Bend before the jump.\" Examples: a dancer crouches before leaving the floor; a golfer swings the club back before striking. A Caribbean one: a Barbados fast bowler winds his arm back before bowling.</li>\n<li><b>Staging</b> directs attention to what matters. As in theatre and film, arrange the scene so its most important idea is immediately clear, using pose, camera angle, lighting and composition. Example: a hurricane shutter closing in silhouette against the window so the action reads instantly.</li>\n<li><b>Straight ahead action and pose to pose</b>: straight ahead means drawing the scene frame by frame, from beginning to end; pose to pose means drawing the key frames first, then filling in the intervals. (More on a later screen.)</li>\n<li><b>Follow through and overlapping action</b>: parts of a character do not all stop at once. In <b>follow through</b>, loose parts keep moving after the main body stops. In <b>overlapping action</b>, different parts move at different times as they catch up. Example: a Carnival costume's feathers keep swaying after the dancer stops.</li>\n<li><b>Slow in and slow out</b> softens motion: place <b>more drawings near the starting and ending poses, with fewer in the middle</b>, so the action eases into movement and settles naturally. Example: a ferry easing out of Basseterre and easing into Charlestown harbour.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"passage\">Squash and stretch keeps volume constant. If a ball gets shorter, it must get wider; if it stretches longer, it gets thinner. A ball that just shrinks looks wrong.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"explain\" a principle, give three things: what it is, what effect it has on the viewer, and a concrete example. \"Anticipation prepares the audience for the next action, making it readable; for example, a dancer crouches before leaving the floor.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Here are six of the twelve principles: squash and stretch (objects flatten and stretch but keep their volume), anticipation (a small move before the main one), staging (making the main idea clear), straight ahead or pose to pose (two ways of working), follow through (loose parts keep moving) and slow in and slow out (easing). A cricketer in Guyana winding up to bowl shows anticipation.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The principles exist to make motion <b>readable</b> and <b>believable</b>, so strong answers explain the effect as well as the definition. Anticipation tells the viewer what is about to happen, so a fast action is not missed; the faster the action, the more anticipation it usually needs. Squash and stretch shows the material: a lot for a rubber ball, almost none for a cricket ball. Follow through and overlapping action stop a character looking like a stiff block, because hair, clothing and limbs lag behind. A common error is to treat easing as optional decoration; without slow in and slow out, almost every movement looks mechanical. Machines are the exception, which shows that the principles are applied with judgement.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"The 12 principles of animation (7 to 12)","html":"<ol start=\"7\">\n<li><b>Arcs</b> make motion flow naturally. Most organic actions follow an arc or a slightly circular path: a head turning, an arm swinging, a thrown ball. <b>Mechanical movement can be an exception</b>: a piston or a sliding door moves in a straight line. Example: a frisbee curving across Pigeon Point beach.</li>\n<li><b>Secondary action</b> supports the main action. Small supporting actions enrich the movement and add dimension to the character. They should <b>reinforce the action and keep the audience focused on the main idea</b>. Example: a vendor at the Castries Market calls out to tourists (main action) while wiping her hands on her apron (secondary action).</li>\n<li><b>Timing</b> controls how fast an action feels. It is the number of drawings or frames assigned to an action; at a fixed playback rate, more frames make it slower and fewer make it faster. A quick 3-frame blink feels alert; a slow 10-frame blink feels sleepy.</li>\n<li><b>Exaggeration</b> makes the feeling unmistakable. Push an expression or action <b>beyond literal reality</b> so it reads clearly; <b>perfect imitation can feel static and dull</b> in a cartoon. Example: a character's eyes growing huge at a plate of johnny cakes and saltfish.</li>\n<li><b>Solid drawing</b> makes a flat image feel dimensional. The fundamentals of academic drawing, <b>form, weight, balance and perspective</b>, also apply to animation. In 3D this becomes good modelling and posing, avoiding \"twinning\" (both arms doing the identical thing).</li>\n<li><b>Appeal</b> gives a character presence. Appeal in a cartoon character is like <b>charisma in an actor</b>: a clear design and an expressive personality make the character engaging to watch. Villains can have appeal too.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Group</th><th>Principles</th><th>Mainly about...</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Physics and weight</td><td>Squash and stretch, slow in and slow out, arcs, follow through and overlapping action</td><td>Making movement obey believable physical rules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Performance</td><td>Anticipation, timing, exaggeration, secondary action</td><td>Making action readable and expressive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Design and planning</td><td>Staging, solid drawing, appeal, straight ahead and pose to pose</td><td>How the shot is planned, drawn and presented</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A key resource is Richard Williams' <b><i>The Animator's Survival Kit</i></b> (Faber and Faber), and the Animation Nuggets and Bloop Animation guides online are also useful. Try this challenge: <b>replay one short action and ask which principle makes it work.</b></p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the twelve as a list you can write in one minute, in this order: Squash and stretch, Anticipation, Staging, Straight ahead and pose to pose, Follow through and overlapping action, Slow in and slow out, Arcs, Secondary action, Timing, Exaggeration, Solid drawing, Appeal.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The other six principles are arcs (natural movement follows curves), secondary action (small actions that support the main one), timing (how many frames an action takes), exaggeration (pushing an action so it reads clearly), solid drawing (form, weight and balance) and appeal (a character people enjoy watching). A Jamaican vendor waving while she calls to customers shows secondary action.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The twelve principles overlap, and examiners reward answers that show how they combine. A bouncing ball uses timing, spacing, arcs, squash and stretch and easing at once. Exaggeration is often misunderstood as \"making things silly\"; it means pushing a pose or movement just far enough that the feeling is unmistakable, which even realistic films do. Secondary action must support the main action, not compete with it, which links it to staging. Appeal applies to villains as well as heroes: a well-designed villain is fascinating to watch. Grouping the principles helps you choose which to discuss: a question about weight calls for the physics group, while one about personality calls for performance and appeal.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Name the principle","intro":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, is reviewing animations for a Bahamas tourism campaign. Which principle is each animator using?</p>","items":[{"text":"A cartoon pelican pulls its head back before diving into Nassau harbour.","options":["follow through","anticipation","appeal"],"answer":1,"fb":"The small backward move prepares the viewer for the dive. That is anticipation."},{"text":"After the steel pan player stops, the long ribbons on his sleeves keep swinging, then settle.","options":["staging","secondary action","follow through and overlapping action"],"answer":2,"fb":"Loose parts continuing to move after the body stops is follow through."},{"text":"A ferry starts slowly, speeds up in the middle of the crossing, then slows before docking.","options":["squash and stretch","exaggeration","slow in and slow out"],"answer":2,"fb":"Gradual acceleration and deceleration is slow in and slow out (easing)."},{"text":"A swinging arm is checked frame by frame to make sure the hand follows a curved path.","options":["solid drawing","timing","arcs"],"answer":2,"fb":"Natural movements follow curves. Checking the path of the hand is checking arcs."},{"text":"A character's jaw drops to the floor when she sees the price of a cruise ticket.","options":["staging","exaggeration","anticipation"],"answer":1,"fb":"Pushing an expression beyond reality for clarity and humour is exaggeration."},{"text":"The camera is placed side-on with a plain sky behind the character so the jump is easy to read.","options":["appeal","arcs","staging"],"answer":2,"fb":"Choosing angle and background so one idea reads clearly is staging."},{"text":"While a fisherman hauls in his net (main action), he whistles and taps his foot.","options":["secondary action","timing","overlapping action"],"answer":0,"fb":"Small supporting actions that add personality are secondary action, as long as they do not steal focus."}],"win":"Excellent. You identified principles from their effect on screen, which is how examiners describe them in Paper 01.","tutor":[{"h":"Before or after?","p":"<p>A move before the action is anticipation. Parts still moving after the action are follow through.</p>"},{"h":"Main or extra?","p":"<p>An extra, smaller action that supports the main one is secondary action.</p>"},{"h":"Clarity","p":"<p>If the question is about camera, layout and making one idea clear, think staging.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name the principle (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A ball flattens when it hits the ground.","options":["squash and stretch","appeal"],"answer":0,"fb":"Flattening on impact is squash."},{"text":"A character crouches before jumping.","options":["anticipation","arcs"],"answer":0,"fb":"The preparation move is anticipation."},{"text":"A car speeds up gradually from a stop.","options":["slow out (ease out)","staging"],"answer":0,"fb":"Gradual acceleration is easing."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name the principle (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: four options each, and several principles look alike. Choose the principle that BEST explains each moment Zara is reviewing.</p>","items":[{"text":"A pelican lands on a post, and its wing feathers keep rippling for a moment after its body has stopped.","options":["squash and stretch","follow through and overlapping action","secondary action","appeal"],"answer":1,"fb":"Loose parts continuing after the main body stops is follow through."},{"text":"While the hero runs to the boat (the main action), she grabs her hat as it lifts in the wind, without breaking stride.","options":["exaggeration","staging","anticipation","secondary action"],"answer":3,"fb":"A small supporting action that adds character without stealing focus is secondary action."},{"text":"Before a very fast punch, the boxer pulls his fist far back; for a slower push he barely draws back at all.","options":["slow out","arcs","anticipation","timing"],"answer":2,"fb":"Anticipation prepares the viewer, and faster actions need more of it."},{"text":"A heavy anchor falls in 6 frames; a feather drifts down over 60 frames.","options":["staging","timing","solid drawing","spacing"],"answer":1,"fb":"The number of frames an action takes is timing, and it shows weight."},{"text":"In a 3D shot, both of a character's arms rise at exactly the same moment in mirror-image poses, which looks stiff.","options":["exaggeration","a solid drawing problem called twinning","an appeal success","good staging"],"answer":1,"fb":"Twinning is a solid drawing fault; vary the arms' timing and pose."},{"text":"A sliding door closes in a perfectly straight line. Which statement is TRUE?","options":["It needs squash and stretch","It shows appeal","Mechanical objects may move in straight lines; arcs mainly apply to organic motion","It breaks the arcs principle, so it is wrong"],"answer":2,"fb":"The arcs principle has an exception for mechanical movement."},{"text":"The thief is shown in silhouette against a bright window, so the theft reads instantly in a crowded scene.","options":["staging","follow through","slow in","secondary action"],"answer":0,"fb":"Arranging light, pose and composition so one idea reads clearly is staging."},{"text":"A character's eyes swell to twice their size for three frames when she sees a plate of conkies.","options":["slow out","exaggeration","arcs","solid drawing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Pushing an expression beyond reality so the feeling is unmistakable is exaggeration."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated principles that are easy to confuse: follow through from secondary action, timing from spacing, and the arcs rule from its exception."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Straight-ahead action vs pose-to-pose","html":"<p>The fourth principle describes two working methods. Put simply, <b>straight ahead action</b> means drawing the scene frame by frame, from beginning to end; <b>pose to pose</b> means drawing the key frames first, then filling in the intervals. Professional animators usually combine them.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Straight-ahead action</th><th>Pose-to-pose</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Method</td><td>Draw or pose frame 1, then 2, then 3, in order to the end</td><td>Plan and set the key poses first, then add breakdowns, then inbetweens</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Result</td><td>Spontaneous, fluid, sometimes surprising</td><td>Controlled, clear, well-timed and well-staged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risk</td><td>Proportions and timing can drift; hard to hit an exact length</td><td>Can look stiff or mechanical if the inbetweens are too even</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Fire, water, smoke, splashes, hair, chaotic action</td><td>Acting, dialogue, walks, logo animation, anything with a fixed duration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Software link</td><td>Frame-by-frame drawing in Pencil2D; stop motion</td><td>Keyframes plus tweening in Adobe Animate, Synfig or Blender</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Breakdowns</b> are the drawings between keys that decide how the move happens (for example, whether a head turn dips down in an arc). In Williams' terms, the order of work in pose-to-pose is: <b>keys</b>, then <b>extremes</b>, then <b>breakdowns</b>, then <b>inbetweens</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Combining the two:</b> Malik at the Island Pulse Media animates a dancer for a Mashramani promo in Guyana pose-to-pose, so her moves land exactly on the soca beat. He then animates the flowing headdress feathers straight ahead on top, so they feel loose and alive. In 3D, animators often \"block\" the shot pose-to-pose with stepped (constant) keys, then switch to smooth interpolation and polish.</p>\n<p><b>Stop motion</b> (clay or puppets photographed one frame at a time) is the classic straight-ahead method: you cannot go back and insert a frame once the puppet has moved. Many Caribbean students start with stop motion on a phone using free apps, which is a good way to feel timing.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Distinguish\" answers need the same feature on both sides: \"Straight-ahead animation is drawn in sequence from the first frame to the last, whereas pose-to-pose begins with the key poses and fills in the frames between them.\" Then add when you would use each.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Straight-ahead animation means drawing frame 1, then 2, then 3, all the way to the end. It feels lively but can drift off course. Pose-to-pose means drawing the key poses first, then filling in the frames between them. It is controlled and easy to time. Animators often mix them: pose-to-pose for a Carnival dancer in Trinidad, straight ahead for her flowing feathers.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The choice depends on what must be controlled. If an action must land on a beat, a lip-sync or a fixed duration, pose-to-pose wins, because key poses can be placed on exact frames before any inbetweening. If the motion is chaotic and organic, such as fire, smoke or splashing water, straight ahead captures surprises that planned keys would flatten. Breakdowns bridge the two: they decide <b>how</b> a move travels between keys, such as whether a head dips in an arc while turning. In 3D, \"blocking\" with stepped keys is pose-to-pose, while physics simulations of cloth or hair behave more like straight ahead. A Grade One answer names the method, gives a reason linked to the scene and mentions combining them.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing a working method","q":"Grenada Spice Studios must deliver a 10-second animated advert in which a mascot nutmeg waves, points to a logo and smiles, timed exactly to a voice-over. Which approach is MOST suitable, and why?","options":[{"t":"Straight-ahead, because the computer generates all the inbetweens.","fb":"Software tweening between planned keys is a pose-to-pose workflow, not straight-ahead."},{"t":"Straight-ahead, because it always produces the smoothest motion.","fb":"Straight-ahead can be fluid, but it is hard to control length and synchronisation. It suits fire, water and splashes more than timed acting."},{"t":"Pose-to-pose, because key poses can be planned to match the voice-over and fit the fixed 10-second length.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Pose-to-pose gives control over timing and staging, which matters when an action must hit exact beats and a set duration."},{"t":"Neither: a 10-second advert does not need any planning of poses.","fb":"Every professional animation is planned. A fixed duration and voice-over make planning essential."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Look for constraints","p":"<p>\"Timed exactly\" and \"10 seconds\" are strong clues about control.</p>"},{"h":"Which method plans first?","p":"<p>One method starts with key poses; the other starts at frame 1 and keeps going.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a working method (Foundation)","q":"Which method sets the key poses FIRST and fills in the frames afterwards?","options":[{"t":"Stop motion","fb":"Stop motion is usually a straight-ahead technique."},{"t":"Pose-to-pose","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Keys first, inbetweens later."},{"t":"Straight-ahead","fb":"Straight-ahead works frame by frame in order."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing a working method (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A studio in Port of Spain must animate a 20-second scene: a moko jumbie dances on stilts to a fixed soca beat while his long, ragged costume flaps in the wind. Which working plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"Straight ahead for everything, so the whole scene feels spontaneous.","fb":"The costume would benefit, but the dance would drift off the beat and miss the fixed length."},{"t":"Pose-to-pose for everything, with evenly spaced inbetweens.","fb":"The dance would hit the beats, but evenly spaced costume motion would look stiff and mechanical."},{"t":"Pose-to-pose for the dancer, with key poses placed on the beats, then the costume animated straight ahead (or simulated) on top so it flows loosely.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The main action must hit the beat, so it is planned; the loose costume is secondary motion that benefits from a freer method."},{"t":"Animate the costume first, then fit the dancer's poses around it.","fb":"Secondary motion follows the main action. Animating it first reverses the logic and wastes work."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Strengths of each style and the animator's toolkit","html":"<p><b>Pros and cons: each style has strengths.</b> The rule: <b>choose for the idea, the desired look and the resources available.</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>2D: expressive and classic</th><th>3D: realistic and flexible</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Strength</b></td><td>Stylised drawings give artists freedom to exaggerate shapes and emotion</td><td>Models can be reused, rotated and viewed from different angles</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Challenge</b></td><td>Frame-by-frame work takes time, and a new angle may need new drawings</td><td>Building models and producing finished renders demand technical skills and computing power</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Caribbean example</b></td><td>A quick, hand-drawn Emancipation Day story for Instagram</td><td>A rotating 3D model of a new Rodney Bay hotel, reused in a website and a VR tour</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Tools of the trade.</b> <b>The drawing principles remain; the toolkit has expanded</b>. The <b>traditional toolkit</b> is paper, a pen and a pencil. The <b>digital toolkit</b> is dedicated animation applications and complete software suites.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Status today</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Synfig Studio</b></td><td>Free, open-source 2D: quick motion graphics through to ambitious animation</td><td>Still free and developed</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Toon Boom Studio</b></td><td>All-in-one app for learning 2D fundamentals: special effects, lip-syncing tools, tween generation</td><td>Replaced by <b>Toon Boom Harmony</b>, an industry-standard paid suite</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Flash</b></td><td>Short 2D web animation, games, motion graphics and interactive media</td><td>Renamed <b>Adobe Animate</b> in 2016; browsers stopped playing Flash content at the end of 2020</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Storyboarding software</b></td><td>Turning an idea into visual panels before animating</td><td>Toon Boom Storyboard Pro professionally; paper or Krita for students</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Creatoon</b></td><td>Free cut-out animation: assembling and animating separate parts</td><td>No longer developed; Synfig and OpenToonz do cut-out work now</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pencil</b></td><td>Stripped-back flipbook-style, frame-by-frame practice</td><td>Continued as the free <b>Pencil2D</b></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Free software to start animating</b>: <b>Blender</b> for 3D, and for 2D with <b>Grease Pencil</b>; <b>Krita</b> and <b>OpenToonz</b> for drawing and 2D workflows; <b>Synfig Studio</b> and <b>Pencil2D</b> for more 2D options. Start small: choose one tool and animate a bouncing ball, focusing on <b>timing before detail</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Storyboard the idea before animating</b>: (1) <b>plan the story</b>: decide what happens and what the audience needs to see; (2) <b>lay out shots</b>: arrange the important moments as visual panels; (3) <b>guide animation</b>: use the sequence as a shared production reference. Large productions use storyboard artists; smaller teams like Island Pulse Media sketch their own.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you name software in an exam, say whether it is 2D, 3D or both, and whether it is free. \"Blender is free and supports 3D, and 2D through Grease Pencil\" is a complete answer.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>2D is expressive and classic but can be slow to draw. 3D is realistic and models can be reused, but it needs technical skill and powerful computers. Many tools are free: Blender (3D, plus 2D with Grease Pencil), Krita, OpenToonz, Synfig Studio and Pencil2D. A student in Antigua can start animating at no cost by learning one free tool well.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Tools change; principles do not. Flash became Adobe Animate, Toon Boom Studio gave way to Harmony and Creatoon stopped being developed, so name current tools and say whether they are free or paid, 2D or 3D. When recommending software, match it to the job and the user: Pencil2D is ideal for learning frame-by-frame timing, Synfig for cut-out characters with bones, OpenToonz for a full 2D pipeline and Blender for 3D or mixed work. Cost is not the only factor. Learning time, file compatibility with collaborators and the power of the school's computers all matter. A Grade One recommendation weighs two options against the brief and justifies one, rather than listing every program you know.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the animation tool","intro":"<p>Keisha is writing the comparison report and must list software that supports 2D, 3D or both. Match each tool to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Blender","r":"Free 3D suite that also draws 2D animation with Grease Pencil"},{"l":"Krita","r":"Free painting program with frame-by-frame 2D animation tools"},{"l":"Synfig Studio","r":"Free, open-source vector 2D with motion graphics and bones"},{"l":"Pencil2D","r":"Simple flipbook-style tool for practising frame-by-frame fundamentals"},{"l":"OpenToonz","r":"Free professional 2D production software with an Xsheet"},{"l":"Adobe Animate","r":"The paid successor to Adobe Flash, still using .fla project files"},{"l":"Toon Boom Harmony","r":"Industry-standard paid 2D suite that replaced Toon Boom Studio"},{"l":"Paper, pen and pencil","r":"The traditional animator's toolkit"}],"win":"Well matched. You can name free and paid tools for 2D and 3D animation, exactly what her comparison report asks for.","tutor":[{"h":"Only one does full 3D","p":"<p>Which free tool builds models with depth, and can also draw in 2D?</p>"},{"h":"Old names, new names","p":"<p>Flash and Toon Boom Studio both have successors.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the animation tool (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Blender","r":"Free 3D software (also 2D with Grease Pencil)"},{"l":"Pencil2D","r":"Free frame-by-frame 2D practice"},{"l":"Synfig Studio","r":"Free vector 2D animation"},{"l":"Adobe Animate","r":"The paid successor to Flash"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the animation tool (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: instead of descriptions, each card is a job. Match each tool to the job it suits BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Pencil2D","r":"A beginner practising frame-by-frame timing with onion skin on a school laptop"},{"l":"Synfig Studio","r":"A cut-out character with bones whose limbs rotate at the joints"},{"l":"OpenToonz","r":"A full 2D studio pipeline using an Xsheet, at no software cost"},{"l":"Blender","r":"A 3D fly-through that also needs some hand-drawn 2D effects in the same file"},{"l":"Krita","r":"A digital painter adding hand-drawn animated frames to her illustrations"},{"l":"Toon Boom Harmony","r":"A paid, industry-standard 2D suite used by professional studios"},{"l":"Adobe Animate","r":"Updating an old .fla project and publishing it as HTML5 Canvas"},{"l":"Toon Boom Storyboard Pro","r":"Professional software for turning a script into shot panels"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each tool to a realistic job, which is what a \"recommend software\" question asks for."}},{"type":"learn","title":"2D bouncing ball: step by step","html":"<p>The <b>bouncing ball</b> is the first exercise in every animation course (and in <i>The Animator's Survival Kit</i>) because it teaches timing, spacing, arcs, squash and stretch and ease in one shot. Set up a document at <b>24 fps</b>, 1920 by 1080.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:460px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bouncing ball arcs: ball positions close together at the top of each arc and far apart near the ground, with a squashed ball at each contact\">\n<line x1=\"5\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"335\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<path d=\"M20 20 Q 60 20 100 128 Q 140 50 180 128 Q 215 80 250 128 Q 275 100 300 128\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"20\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"24\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"40\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"84\" cy=\"80\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"62\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"96\" r=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">contact = squash; tops = slow; near ground = fast</text>\n</svg>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Draw the path</b>: sketch the arcs. Each bounce is <b>lower and shorter</b> than the last because the ball loses energy.</li>\n<li><b>Set the keys</b>: key 1 at the top (frame 1), key 2 at the contact (frame 13), key 3 at the next top (frame 23 or so), and so on. Bounces get quicker as they get lower.</li>\n<li><b>Draw a timing chart</b>: a small scale on the key drawing showing where each inbetween will sit. Ticks are <b>close together near the top</b> (ease in and out, the ball hangs) and <b>far apart near the ground</b> (it is falling fast).</li>\n<li><b>Inbetween</b> following the chart, keeping each drawing on the arc.</li>\n<li><b>Add squash and stretch</b>: stretch the ball along its path in the one or two frames before and after contact; squash it flat on the contact frame. Keep the volume the same and the ball round again at the top.</li>\n<li><b>Show weight</b>: a rubber ball squashes a lot and bounces high; a bowling ball barely squashes, has quick timing and hardly bounces; a beach ball floats with slow timing.</li>\n<li><b>Check by flipping</b> or playing back with <b>onion skinning</b> (seeing ghosts of the previous and next frames).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A sample timing chart for one fall (top to contact) on twos: frames 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 with spacing growing from tiny to large: <code>|| | |  |    |      |</code>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 practical you may be asked to sketch keyframes. Number every key, draw the timing chart, label \"squash\" and \"stretch\", and write the fps and total frames. Labels earn marks even when your drawing is rough.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The bouncing ball teaches the basics. Draw the path, set keys at the top of each bounce and at each contact, and make a timing chart with drawings close together at the top and far apart near the ground. Squash the ball when it hits and stretch it as it falls. Each bounce gets lower, like a ball dropped on a netball court in St. Vincent.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The exercise is about <b>weight</b>, and weight comes from timing and spacing more than from the drawing. A heavy ball falls quickly, barely squashes and hardly bounces; a light beach ball has slow timing and drifts. Gravity explains the timing chart: the ball slows as it rises and speeds up as it falls, so drawings bunch at the top and spread near the ground. Stretch should follow the direction of travel, not simply point up and down. Bounces should get quicker as well as lower, because each smaller arc takes less time. A common error is squashing the ball on frames where it is not touching the ground, which makes it look rubbery in mid-air. Check every change with onion skinning.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Order the 2D bouncing ball workflow","intro":"<p>Keisha, the Island Pulse Media marketing lead, is learning to animate a bouncing guinep for an Instagram story. Put her steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create a 24 fps document at the right size for the platform."},{"t":"Sketch the path of the bounces, each one lower and shorter."},{"t":"Draw the key positions: top of each arc and each contact with the ground."},{"t":"Draw a timing chart with close spacing at the tops and wide spacing near the ground."},{"t":"Add the inbetweens on the arc following the timing chart."},{"t":"Add stretch before and after contact and squash on the contact frame, keeping volume."},{"t":"Play back with onion skinning, fix bumps, then export."}],"win":"Well done. You followed the professional order: plan the path, set keys, chart the timing, inbetween, then add squash and stretch and polish.","tutor":[{"h":"Plan before drawing","p":"<p>Set up the document and the path before you draw any ball.</p>"},{"h":"Keys before inbetweens","p":"<p>You cannot place inbetweens until you know where the keys and timing chart are.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the bouncing ball (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Sketch the bounce path."},{"t":"Draw the key positions."},{"t":"Add inbetweens using a timing chart."},{"t":"Add squash and stretch, then play back."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Order the 2D bouncing ball workflow (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: nine steps, separating the first key from the rest and ending with a safe export. Put Keisha's full workflow for the bouncing guinep in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create a 24 fps document at 1920 × 1080."},{"t":"Sketch the path: arcs that get lower and shorter."},{"t":"Draw the first two keys: the top of the first arc and the first contact."},{"t":"Add keys for the remaining tops and contacts, with each bounce quicker than the last."},{"t":"Draw a timing chart on each key: close ticks near the top, wide ticks near the ground."},{"t":"Draw the inbetweens on the arc, following the chart."},{"t":"Add stretch just before and after each contact and squash on the contact frame, keeping the volume."},{"t":"Play back with onion skinning and fix any bumps in the arc."},{"t":"Export an MP4 and keep the editable source file."}],"win":"Correct. Path, keys, timing charts, inbetweens, squash and stretch, playback checks, then export with the source kept."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Tweening in Adobe Animate (formerly Flash) and free 2D tools","html":"<p><b>Interpolation</b> (tweening) is when software calculates the inbetween values (position, scale, rotation, colour, shape) between two keyframes. The animator controls the result with <b>easing</b>.</p>\n<p><b>The Flash interface, now Adobe Animate.</b> Adobe renamed Flash <b>Adobe Animate</b> in 2016. The Flash interface is still there:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>.fla</b>: the editable animation project. Open it to continue editing.</li>\n<li><b>SWF</b> (\"Small Web Format\", originally ShockWave Flash): the published Flash file for multimedia and vector graphics. Browsers stopped supporting Flash Player at the end of 2020, so today you publish <b>HTML5 Canvas</b>, <b>MP4</b> or <b>animated GIF</b> instead.</li>\n<li><b>Tools panel</b>: tools to select, draw and change items on the <b>stage</b>; some also act on the timeline.</li>\n<li><b>Properties panel</b>: select an object on the stage, then modify its characteristics (size, colour, position, ease).</li>\n<li><b>Timeline</b>: represents the frames of the animation. Animate by hand and/or with code (<b>ActionScript</b> in Flash; JavaScript for HTML5 Canvas in Animate). At the same frame rate, <b>more frames mean a longer animation</b>; fewer frames mean a shorter one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Tweening in Animate</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Timeline shortcuts: <b>F6</b> insert keyframe, <b>F7</b> blank keyframe, <b>F5</b> insert frame (extends a hold).</li>\n<li><b>Classic Tween</b>: two keyframes with a symbol (convert artwork with F8), right-click between them, <b>Create Classic Tween</b>; set ease in the Properties panel.</li>\n<li><b>Motion Tween</b>: change a symbol's properties at any frame and Animate creates an editable <b>motion path</b>; fine-tune in the <b>Motion Editor</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Shape Tween</b>: morphs one vector shape into another (a circle into a star).</li>\n<li><b>Onion Skin</b> shows neighbouring frames. Export with <b>File &gt; Export &gt; Export Video/Media</b> (MP4 H.264) or <b>Export Animated GIF</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Free and open-source tools</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Style</th><th>Tweening</th><th>Export</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pencil2D</b></td><td>Frame-by-frame drawing with onion skin</td><td>Mostly hand-drawn inbetweens; camera can be interpolated</td><td>MP4, GIF, image sequence</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>OpenToonz</b></td><td>Professional 2D production</td><td>Xsheet and Function Editor</td><td>Image sequence, MP4</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Synfig Studio</b></td><td>Vector cut-out and motion graphics with bones</td><td>Automatic tweening between <b>waypoints</b></td><td>PNG sequence, GIF, video</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Frame-by-frame vs tweened</b>: frame-by-frame gives the richest, most organic motion but takes the most time. Tweening is fast and consistent, ideal for logos, cut-out characters and infographics, but looks \"floaty\" or mechanical on <b>linear</b> interpolation without easing.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Name files clearly for Paper 02 and the SBA, for example <code>Surname_Task2_BounceBall_24fps.mp4</code>. Always keep the editable source (.fla, .pclx, .sifz, .tnz) as well as the export.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Tweening means the software works out the frames between two keyframes. Adobe Animate (once called Flash) offers Classic, Motion and Shape tweens. Easing makes tweened motion speed up and slow down naturally. Flash's SWF files no longer play in browsers, so export HTML5 Canvas, MP4 or GIF instead. A logo sliding in for a festival in Grenada is a perfect tween.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Tweening is fast and consistent, but the software only knows what you tell it: two keys and a straight path. Without easing, a tween moves at constant speed and looks robotic; without a curved motion path, it travels in a straight line instead of an arc. That is why skilled animators add easing, breakdown keys or motion paths. Shape tweens can produce strange in-between shapes when the start and end shapes are very different, so animators add shape hints or extra keys. The end of Flash Player in 2020 is a lesson in format risk: work published only as SWF became unplayable, while creators who kept their .fla source files could republish it as HTML5 or MP4.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Flash then, Animate now","intro":"<p>Jerome found an old Flash project for a Grenada spice festival and wants to update it. Zara checks his notes against today's software.</p>","q":"Select every CORRECT statement.","lines":["The .fla file is the editable project, so he can open it in Adobe Animate and keep working.","He should publish a SWF file, because every modern browser still plays Flash.","The Properties panel changes the characteristics of the object selected on the stage.","On the Timeline, at the same frame rate, more frames make the animation longer.","The Tools panel is used to select, draw and change items on the stage.","He should export HTML5 Canvas or MP4 so the animation plays on today's phones and browsers.","Animation in Flash could only be done by hand, never with code.","Adding frames at the same frame rate makes the animation shorter."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,5],"win":"Correct. You know the .fla, Tools, Properties and Timeline, and why HTML5 Canvas and MP4 replace SWF today.","miss":"Look again. Flash Player support ended in 2020, Flash also used ActionScript code, and more frames at the same rate means a longer animation.","tutor":[{"h":"Editable or published?","p":"<p>One file type is the project; the other was the published output.</p>"},{"h":"Frames and length","p":"<p>If the frame rate stays the same, what happens to the duration when you add frames?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Flash then, Animate now (Foundation)","q":"Which TWO statements are correct?","lines":["A .fla file is the editable project.","Every browser still plays SWF files.","The Timeline represents the frames of the animation.","The Properties panel deletes the stage."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. The .fla is the project and the Timeline holds the frames.","miss":"Flash Player ended in 2020, and the Properties panel edits a selected object."},
 "up": {"title":"Flash then, Animate now (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: Zara checks Jerome's detailed notes on tweening and the timeline. Some are subtly wrong.</p>","q":"Select every CORRECT statement.","lines":["A Classic Tween needs the artwork converted to a symbol (F8) first.","F6 inserts a keyframe, and F5 inserts a frame that extends a hold.","A Shape Tween morphs one vector shape into another, such as a circle into a star.","SWF is the editable project file that he should keep for future changes.","HTML5 Canvas animations made in Animate can be controlled with JavaScript.","Browsers stopped supporting Flash Player at the end of 2020.","A Motion Tween can only move in a straight line and its path cannot be edited.","Onion Skin shows the neighbouring frames to help line up drawings.","Tweens always look natural without any easing.","Doubling the frame rate while keeping the same number of frames doubles the length of the animation."],"correct":[0,1,2,4,5,7],"win":"Correct. You know the shortcuts, the three tween types, HTML5 Canvas with JavaScript and why the .fla, not the SWF, is the file to keep.","miss":"Look again. The .fla is the editable project, Motion Tweens create editable paths, tweens need easing to feel natural, and a higher frame rate with the same frames makes the animation SHORTER."}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened?","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has a list of jobs. Decide whether each is best done by hand, frame by frame, or with software tweening.</p>","main":"Which technique suits this job best?","bins":["Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)","Tweened (software interpolates)"],"items":[{"t":"A circle morphing into a sun shape in a vector tool.","bin":1,"fb":"A shape tween in Animate (or Synfig) interpolates one vector shape into another."},{"t":"An infographic bar chart growing to show tourist arrivals at the airport.","bin":1,"fb":"Bars growing from one value to another are easily tweened with easing."},{"t":"A cut-out puppet of a mocko jumbie whose limbs rotate at joints in Synfig.","bin":1,"fb":"Cut-out animation with bones relies on interpolation between waypoints."},{"t":"A hand-drawn character crying, with tears and quivering lips.","bin":0,"fb":"Expressive acting with changing drawings is classic frame-by-frame work."},{"t":"A telecom company's logo sliding in and scaling up smoothly for a 5-second bumper.","bin":1,"fb":"Simple changes in position and scale between two keys are perfect for a motion or classic tween."},{"t":"Flames on a coal pot flickering under a pot of soup.","bin":0,"fb":"Fire changes shape unpredictably, so it is usually drawn straight ahead, frame by frame."},{"t":"A crashing wave at Bathsheba with spray and foam changing shape every frame.","bin":0,"fb":"Chaotic, organic shapes need straight-ahead frame-by-frame drawing."},{"t":"A rough pencil sketch animation made in Pencil2D with onion skin.","bin":0,"fb":"Pencil2D is built mainly for drawing each frame by hand."}],"win":"Good judgement. Tweening suits clean, predictable changes; frame-by-frame suits organic, expressive or chaotic motion.","tutor":[{"h":"Predictable change?","p":"<p>If the change is just position, scale, rotation or a simple morph, software can calculate it.</p>"},{"h":"Changing drawings","p":"<p>If the shape itself changes in a complex way every frame, an artist usually draws it.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A logo sliding across the screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Simple movement: tween it."},{"t":"A splashing wave.","bin":0,"fb":"Chaotic shapes: draw by hand."},{"t":"A bar chart growing taller.","bin":1,"fb":"Simple scale change: tween it."},{"t":"Flickering flames.","bin":0,"fb":"Organic movement: draw by hand."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Frame-by-frame or tweened? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder jobs from across the region. Decide which technique suits each job BEST, thinking about how predictable the change is.</p>","main":"Which technique suits this job best?","bins":["Frame-by-frame (drawn by hand)","Tweened (software interpolates)"],"items":[{"t":"A cut-out paper puppet of Papa Bois with rotating joints","bin":1,"fb":"Cut-out animation with bones relies on interpolation."},{"t":"Smoke curling from the Sulphur Springs vents in St. Lucia","bin":0,"fb":"Smoke changes shape unpredictably: draw it straight ahead."},{"t":"A splash as a child cannonballs into a river pool in Dominica","bin":0,"fb":"Chaotic water needs hand-drawn frames."},{"t":"A counter ticking from 0 to 10,000 visitors in an infographic","bin":1,"fb":"A predictable change in value is tweened."},{"t":"A character's hair whipping in hurricane-force wind","bin":0,"fb":"Wild, organic movement is drawn frame by frame."},{"t":"A map where each Caribbean island lights up in turn","bin":1,"fb":"Colour and opacity changes between keys are easily tweened."},{"t":"A lower-third name banner sliding in with easing for a news show","bin":1,"fb":"A simple position change with easing is an ideal tween."},{"t":"A steering-wheel icon rotating in an app tutorial","bin":1,"fb":"A clean rotation between two values is a tween."},{"t":"A grandmother's face slowly crumpling into tears in close-up","bin":0,"fb":"Subtle acting with changing drawings is frame-by-frame."},{"t":"A flag rippling with complex folds, drawn for a hand-made look","bin":0,"fb":"A deliberate hand-made style with changing folds is frame-by-frame work."}],"win":"Excellent. Predictable changes in position, scale, rotation, colour or value suit tweening; chaotic, organic or expressive motion suits frame-by-frame."}},{"type":"learn","title":"3D modelling fundamentals","html":"<p>3D animation means to <b>digitally model and manipulate a figure, usually starting with a polygon mesh</b>: a surface built from connected polygons. Modelling is the first of the three stages of 3D (modelling, layout, rendering). Objects are built in a virtual space with three axes: <b>X</b> (left to right), <b>Y</b> (front to back) and <b>Z</b> (up and down in Blender).</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:260px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A wireframe cube with a vertex, an edge and a face labelled\">\n<path d=\"M40 50 L110 50 L110 120 L40 120 Z M40 50 L80 20 L150 20 L110 50 M150 20 L150 90 L110 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M40 120 L80 90 L150 90 M80 90 L80 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">vertex</text>\n<text x=\"88\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">edge</text>\n<text x=\"56\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">face</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Vertex</b> (plural vertices): a single point in 3D space with X, Y, Z coordinates.</li>\n<li><b>Edge</b>: a straight line joining two vertices.</li>\n<li><b>Face</b>: a flat surface enclosed by three or more edges. Faces are what get shaded and rendered.</li>\n<li><b>Polygon</b>: a face described by its number of sides. <b>Triangles</b> are what game engines finally use; <b>quads</b> (4 sides) are preferred for modelling because they deform and subdivide cleanly; <b>n-gons</b> (5 or more sides) can cause shading errors.</li>\n<li><b>Mesh</b> (polygon mesh): the whole collection of vertices, edges and faces that make up an object. <b>Polygon count</b> affects performance: a mobile game needs low-poly models; a film render can use high-poly.</li>\n<li><b>Primitives</b>: ready-made starting shapes: cube, UV sphere, ico sphere, cylinder, cone, plane, torus. A lighthouse might start as a cylinder and a cone.</li>\n<li><b>Normals</b>: the direction a face points. Flipped normals make faces look black or invisible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>In Blender:</b> add a primitive with <b>Shift+A &gt; Mesh</b>. Press <b>Tab</b> for Edit Mode, then <b>1, 2, 3</b> for vertex, edge and face select. <b>G</b> moves, <b>R</b> rotates, <b>S</b> scales, <b>E</b> extrudes a face, <b>Ctrl+R</b> adds an edge loop. A <b>Subdivision Surface</b> modifier smooths a low-poly shape.</p>\n<p><b>Applying a simple material and texture</b>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Select the object and open <b>Material Properties</b> (the red sphere icon). Click <b>New</b>.</li>\n<li>Change <b>Base Color</b> and <b>Roughness</b> (low = shiny, high = matte). Press <b>Z</b> and choose Material Preview.</li>\n<li>For an image texture (a photo of Anguilla stone or Belize mahogany), go to the <b>Shading</b> workspace, add <b>Shift+A &gt; Texture &gt; Image Texture</b>, open the image and connect its Color output to <b>Base Color</b>.</li>\n<li>If it looks stretched, unwrap: in Edit Mode select all (A), press <b>U &gt; Smart UV Project</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A <b>material</b> describes how a surface reacts to light (colour, shine, transparency); a <b>texture</b> is an image or pattern mapped onto it. Use the two words correctly.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>3D models are built from a mesh: vertices (points), edges (lines between points) and faces (flat surfaces). You start from simple shapes called primitives, such as a cube or a cylinder, then shape them. A material sets how a surface reacts to light; a texture is an image wrapped onto it. A model of a lighthouse in Barbados might start as a cylinder and a cone.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Good modelling balances <b>detail and performance</b>. Every extra face costs processing power, so a model for a mobile AR app must be low-poly, while a film render can afford millions of faces. Clean topology matters: quads deform and subdivide smoothly, which is vital for characters that bend, while n-gons can shade badly. Modifiers such as Subdivision Surface keep a simple, editable base mesh and add smoothness only when needed, a non-destructive approach like layer masks in GIMP. Textures need UV unwrapping, which flattens the 3D surface into 2D like peeling an orange; a poor unwrap stretches the image. Normals are an invisible trap: a flipped normal makes a face render black or disappear.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"From scene to finished image","intro":"<p>Malik is making a 3D animation of a sea turtle swimming past the reef at the Tobago Cays for a marine park campaign. Put his production steps in order, using the 3D stages.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Storyboard the idea: plan the story and lay out the key shots as panels."},{"t":"Modelling: build the turtle, the coral and the seabed as polygon meshes."},{"t":"Add materials and textures to the turtle shell and the coral."},{"t":"Layout: position the models in the scene and decide what the camera sees."},{"t":"Animate: key the turtle's poses and movement over time, using arcs and slow in and slow out."},{"t":"Rendering: turn the scene into finished images with lighting, surfaces and shadows."},{"t":"Export the frames as an MP4 for the campaign."}],"win":"Correct. Plan, model, surface, lay out, animate, render, export: the stages working together as one pipeline.","tutor":[{"h":"Build before you place","p":"<p>You need a model before you can position it in the scene.</p>"},{"h":"Render near the end","p":"<p>Rendering produces the finished images, so it comes after the animation is complete.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"From scene to finished image (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main 3D stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Storyboard the shots."},{"t":"Model the objects."},{"t":"Lay out and animate the scene."},{"t":"Render the finished images."}]},
 "up": {"title":"From scene to finished image (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: ten steps, including UV unwrapping, blocking, polishing and a test render. Put Malik's full sea turtle pipeline in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Write a short brief and storyboard the key shots."},{"t":"Model the turtle, coral and seabed as polygon meshes."},{"t":"UV-unwrap the turtle shell so its texture will not stretch."},{"t":"Add materials and image textures to the shell and the coral."},{"t":"Lay out the models in the scene and set the camera."},{"t":"Block the turtle's key poses with stepped (constant) keys."},{"t":"Switch to Bezier and polish the arcs and easing in the Graph Editor."},{"t":"Render a 50% test in Eevee to check timing and lighting."},{"t":"Render the final frames as a PNG sequence."},{"t":"Join the frames into an H.264 MP4 for the campaign."}],"win":"Correct. Plan, model, unwrap, surface, lay out, block, polish, test, render and export: a professional 3D pipeline."}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the 3D term","intro":"<p>Jerome, the Island Pulse Media mobile developer, is building a low-poly model of the old Court House in St. John's, Antigua, for an AR tour app. Match each term.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Vertex","r":"A single point with X, Y and Z coordinates"},{"l":"Edge","r":"A line joining two points"},{"l":"Face","r":"A flat surface enclosed by edges that is shaded when rendered"},{"l":"Mesh","r":"The whole collection of points, lines and surfaces forming the courthouse"},{"l":"Primitive","r":"The cube he starts from before shaping the walls"},{"l":"Quad","r":"A four-sided polygon that deforms and subdivides cleanly"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A photo of painted wood mapped onto the shutters"},{"l":"Low-poly","r":"Keeping the face count small so the app runs on mid-range phones"}],"win":"Great work. You can describe a 3D model from its smallest part (vertex) to the whole mesh and its surface.","tutor":[{"h":"Point, line, surface","p":"<p>Think 0D, 1D, 2D: a point, a line, then a flat area.</p>"},{"h":"Starting shape","p":"<p>Cube, sphere and cylinder are the ready-made starting shapes.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the 3D term (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Vertex","r":"A single point in 3D space"},{"l":"Edge","r":"A line joining two points"},{"l":"Face","r":"A flat surface enclosed by edges"},{"l":"Mesh","r":"All the points, lines and surfaces of an object"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the 3D term (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: modelling terms beyond the basics. Match each term as Jerome refines the courthouse model for the AR tour.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Normal","r":"The direction a face points; if it is flipped, the face may render black"},{"l":"N-gon","r":"A face with five or more sides that can cause shading errors"},{"l":"Edge loop","r":"A ring of connected edges added with Ctrl+R to add detail"},{"l":"Extrude","r":"Pulling a face outwards with E to create new geometry"},{"l":"Subdivision Surface modifier","r":"Smoothing a low-poly model without changing its base mesh"},{"l":"UV unwrap","r":"Flattening the 3D surface into 2D so a texture maps without stretching"},{"l":"Material","r":"Settings for how a surface reacts to light, such as colour and roughness"},{"l":"Texture","r":"An image of Belize mahogany grain mapped onto the courtroom benches"},{"l":"Low-poly","r":"Keeping the face count small so the app runs on mid-range phones"}],"win":"Excellent. You can explain how a mesh is built, refined, surfaced and kept efficient."}},{"type":"learn","title":"3D bouncing ball in Blender: step by step","html":"<p>This is the 3D version of the classic exercise. Use Blender (free, open source). Shortcuts below are for the 3D Viewport with the mouse over it.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Set up the scene</b>: delete the default cube (X). Add a floor: <b>Shift+A &gt; Mesh &gt; Plane</b>, scale it up (S, 10). Add the ball: <b>Shift+A &gt; Mesh &gt; UV Sphere</b>; right-click &gt; <b>Shade Smooth</b>. Give it a material (for example, red like a beach ball).</li>\n<li><b>Move the pivot to the base of the ball</b> so it squashes against the floor, not around its middle: put the 3D cursor at the bottom (Shift+S &gt; Cursor to Selected on the bottom vertex in Edit Mode), back in Object Mode use <b>Object &gt; Set Origin &gt; Origin to 3D Cursor</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Set timing</b>: in <b>Output Properties</b> choose 24 fps; on the <b>Timeline</b> set Start 1 and End 48.</li>\n<li><b>Key the positions</b>: at frame 1 move the ball up (G, Z) and press <b>I</b> to insert a keyframe (recent versions key location, rotation and scale; right-click a field and choose Insert Keyframe to key only one property). Frame 12: ball on the floor, press I. Frame 22: lower top, I. Frame 30: contact, I. Continue with smaller, quicker bounces. Add forward movement on Y or X so it travels.</li>\n<li><b>Scrub</b> the Timeline. The motion will look floaty, because Blender's default <b>Bezier</b> interpolation eases in and out at <i>every</i> key, including the contacts.</li>\n<li><b>Fix it in the Graph Editor</b>: select the Z Location curve, select the contact keys and press <b>V &gt; Vector</b> so the curve makes a sharp \"V\" at the floor (fast at impact); leave the tops as smooth rounded peaks (slow hang time).</li>\n<li><b>Add squash and stretch</b> with Scale: on the contact frame, scale Z down (about 0.7) and X and Y up (about 1.2) to keep volume; key it. One or two frames before and after, stretch on Z (about 1.15) and thin X and Y; key it. At the tops, return to scale 1.</li>\n<li><b>Retime</b> in the <b>Dope Sheet</b>: it shows every key as a diamond per frame. Drag (G) or scale (S) groups of keys to make bounces faster or slower; Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V copy keys.</li>\n<li>Play with <b>Spacebar</b>, check arcs with <b>Motion Paths</b> (Object Properties), then render.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media uses this same rig, with a coconut model, for a playful TikTok intro for a Montego Bay beach bar.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Examiners reward correct vocabulary for each panel: the <b>Timeline</b> sets frames and plays; the <b>Dope Sheet</b> edits keyframe timing; the <b>Graph Editor</b> edits interpolation and spacing through curves.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>In Blender, add a floor and a sphere, move the ball's origin to its base, set 24 fps and press I to key the ball at each top and contact. Then fix the motion in the Graph Editor so the contacts are sharp, and scale the ball to squash and stretch. The Dope Sheet changes the timing. A coconut bouncing on a beach in Tobago could use the same set-up.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The 3D ball shows why animators must understand interpolation. Blender's default Bezier curves ease into every key, which is right at the top of a bounce but wrong at the contact, where the ball should change direction instantly; hence Vector handles at the floor. Moving the origin to the base matters because scaling happens around the origin, so a centred origin makes the ball shrink into mid-air instead of squashing against the floor. Keying only the properties you need keeps the curves clean and easy to edit. Motion Paths reveal problems the eye misses during playback, such as a wobble in the arc. The same panels, Timeline, Dope Sheet and Graph Editor, are used in every 3D animation, so learn their jobs precisely.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the correct Blender technique","intro":"<p>An intern at the Island Pulse Media wrote these notes after a Blender bouncing ball session. Some are correct and some are wrong.</p>","q":"Select ALL the correct statements.","lines":["Pressing I in the 3D Viewport inserts a keyframe on the current frame.","The Dope Sheet is mainly used to change timing by moving keyframes left or right.","Bezier interpolation at the contact frames makes the ball hit the floor with a sharp, fast impact.","Setting the contact keys to Vector handles in the Graph Editor gives a sharp change of direction.","For squash, scale Z down and X and Y up so the volume stays roughly the same.","The origin should be at the centre of the ball so it squashes against the floor.","Each bounce should be the same height so the motion looks consistent.","Shift+A opens the Add menu, where you choose Mesh then UV Sphere."],"correct":[0,1,3,4,7],"win":"Correct. You know which panel does what, how to sharpen contacts in the Graph Editor, and how to keep volume when squashing.","miss":"Check again: Bezier eases at every key (making contacts floaty), the origin should be at the base of the ball, and bounces lose height as energy is lost.","tutor":[{"h":"Contacts","p":"<p>At the floor the ball should change direction instantly. Which handle type makes a sharp corner?</p>"},{"h":"Physics","p":"<p>A real ball loses energy. What happens to each bounce?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the correct Blender technique (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO correct statements.","lines":["Pressing I inserts a keyframe.","Every bounce should be the same height.","Squash means scaling Z down and X and Y up.","The Dope Sheet is used to add materials."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. I inserts keys, and squash keeps volume by widening as it flattens.","miss":"Bounces get lower over time, and materials are set in Material Properties."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the correct Blender technique (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder notes from a second Blender session. Several sound plausible but are wrong.</p>","q":"Select ALL the correct statements.","lines":["Moving the origin to the base of the ball makes it squash against the floor rather than around its middle.","Vector handles on the contact keys give the Z curve a sharp \"V\" at the floor.","Auto Clamped handles stop the curve overshooting between keys.","In the Graph Editor, the vertical axis shows time in frames.","Render Image (F12) renders the whole animation.","Shift+E > Make Cyclic repeats an F-curve, so a loop continues without extra keys.","For squash, scale Z to about 0.7 and X and Y to about 1.2.","Keying location, rotation and scale on every frame is the best way to get smooth motion.","Motion Paths show the arc the ball travels, so wobbles can be spotted.","Bounces should get higher as the ball travels, to keep it exciting."],"correct":[0,1,2,5,6,8],"win":"Correct. Origin placement, Vector and Auto Clamped handles, cyclic curves, volume-keeping squash and Motion Paths are all sound technique.","miss":"Check again: time runs along the horizontal axis, F12 renders one still, keying every frame defeats interpolation, and a real ball loses height with every bounce."}},{"type":"learn","title":"3D interpolation and the Graph Editor","html":"<p>In 3D, almost every inbetween is calculated by the software. The animator controls it through <b>interpolation modes</b> and <b>animation curves</b> (in Blender, <b>F-curves</b>). In the Graph Editor, the horizontal axis is <b>time (frames)</b> and the vertical axis is the <b>value</b> (for example, height on Z).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Interpolation (Blender: select keys, press T)</th><th>What happens</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Constant</b> (stepped)</td><td>Value jumps from key to key with no inbetweens</td><td>\"Blocking\" a shot pose-to-pose; flickering lights; a clock hand ticking</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Linear</b></td><td>Even change, constant speed; curve is a straight line</td><td>Machines, conveyor belts, a rotating wind turbine, a steady camera pan</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bezier</b> (default)</td><td>Smooth curve that eases in and out of each key</td><td>Natural motion: characters, a boat rocking in the Carenage</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Reading curves</b>: a <b>steep</b> section means fast change; a <b>flat</b> section means slow change or a hold. A curve that flattens as it approaches a key is <b>easing in</b>; one that starts flat leaving a key is <b>easing out</b>. For the bouncing ball, the Z curve should look like a row of upside-down \"U\" shapes with sharp points at the floor.</p>\n<p><b>Handle types</b> (press <b>V</b>): <b>Auto Clamped</b> (smooth, no overshoot), <b>Aligned</b>, <b>Free</b> (move each side separately) and <b>Vector</b> (sharp corner). Blender also offers <b>Easing</b> presets (Ctrl+E) and dynamic effects such as Bounce and Elastic.</p>\n<p><b>Repeating motion</b>: for a looping walk, a spinning fan or waving palm fronds, use <b>Channel &gt; Extrapolation Mode &gt; Make Cyclic</b> (Shift+E) or add a <b>Cycles</b> modifier to the F-curve, so the action repeats forever without extra keys.</p>\n<p><b>Useful shortcuts</b>: <b>Home</b> frames all curves; <b>Ctrl+Tab</b> switches between Graph Editor and Dope Sheet; <b>N</b> opens the side panel to type exact key values.</p>\n<p>2D tools use the same idea. The Motion Editor in Adobe Animate and the waypoint options in Synfig (TCB, ease, linear, constant) are graph editors by another name.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If a Paper 02 question shows a graph and asks where the object moves fastest, look for the <b>steepest</b> part of the curve. Where the curve is flat, the object is slow or still.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>In 3D, the software works out the inbetweens, and you choose how: constant (jumps from key to key), linear (steady speed) or Bezier (smooth easing). The Graph Editor shows this as curves. A steep curve means fast movement; a flat curve means slow movement or none. A wind turbine on a hill in Aruba spins with linear interpolation.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Reading an F-curve is reading speed. The slope at any point is the rate of change, so the steepest part is the fastest movement and a flat section is a hold. For a bouncing ball, the Z curve should form arches with sharp points at the floor; rounded bottoms mean a floaty contact. Bezier handles can overshoot, making an object dip below the floor or pass its target, which is why Auto Clamped handles exist. Cyclic extrapolation is efficient for loops, but the first and last keys must match exactly or the loop will jump. Constant interpolation is not only for blocking: it is the right choice for anything that should snap, such as a clock hand or a flickering neon sign.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"A basic walk cycle","html":"<p>A <b>walk cycle</b> is a set of poses that loops seamlessly, so a character can walk for as long as needed. Richard Williams calls walking \"a process of falling and catching yourself\": the body is always on the edge of losing balance. At 24 fps, a normal walk takes about <b>12 frames per step</b>, so <b>24 frames per cycle</b> (two steps). A brisk walk is faster (8 to 10 frames per step); a tired walk is slower (16 or more).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Pose</th><th>Frame (24-frame cycle)</th><th>What the body does</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Contact</b></td><td>1</td><td>Both feet on the ground, legs at their widest; front heel just touching, back toe pushing. Arms swing opposite to the legs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Down</b> (recoil)</td><td>4</td><td>The front leg bends as it takes the weight; the body is at its <b>lowest</b> point.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Passing</b></td><td>7</td><td>The supporting leg is straight under the body; the back leg passes it with the foot lifted. Body roughly at middle height.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Up</b> (high point)</td><td>10</td><td>The supporting foot pushes off on its toe; the body is at its <b>highest</b> point, ready to fall forward into the next contact.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Contact</b> (opposite leg)</td><td>13</td><td>Same as frame 1 but with the other leg in front. Frames 13 to 24 mirror 1 to 12; frame 25 equals frame 1.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The hips move up and down twice per cycle (once per step) and the arms follow through, swinging opposite to the legs (overlapping action). Adding small head bobs and a tilt of the shoulders (counter to the hips) gives life.</p>\n<p><b>In Blender:</b> use a simple rigged character (Blender's built-in Rigify add-on can generate a human rig). Animate pose-to-pose: key the four poses with <b>Constant</b> interpolation to check them, then switch to Bezier. Animate \"on the spot\" for a loop, make the curves cyclic (Shift+E), and move the whole character forward separately if the shot needs travel. Use <b>Pose Mode</b> (Ctrl+Tab on the armature), and <b>Copy Pose / Paste Flipped Pose</b> to create the opposite-leg poses quickly.</p>\n<p>Malik's walk test for the Island Pulse Media's mascot, a cheeky green iguana walking down a busy main street, uses an 11-frame step so it feels jaunty rather than heavy.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Remember the order <b>Contact, Down, Passing, Up</b>. Students often swap down and up: the body is lowest just after contact (taking the weight) and highest just before the next contact (pushing off).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A walk cycle is a set of poses that loops, so a character can walk for as long as needed. The four key poses are contact (both feet down), down (the body at its lowest), passing (one leg passes the other) and up (the body at its highest). At 24 fps a normal step takes about 12 frames. A tired farmer in Jamaica walking home would take more frames per step.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A walk is \"falling and catching yourself\", so the body's height tells the story of weight. The lowest point comes just after contact as the leg absorbs the weight, and the highest just before the next contact as the foot pushes off. Personality lives in the variations: a jaunty walk has a quicker step and a bigger bounce; a sad walk drags, with a lower head and less arm swing; a heavy character sinks deeper on the down pose. Opposition matters: arms swing opposite to the legs and the shoulders tilt against the hips, or the walk looks robotic. When animating on the spot for a loop, the planted foot must slide back at a steady rate, or the character will seem to skate.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire","intro":"<p>Answer these questions from the Island Pulse Media animation team.</p>","items":[{"text":"A wind turbine on a hill in Curaçao must spin at a perfectly steady speed. Which interpolation?","options":["Linear","Bezier","Constant"],"answer":0,"fb":"Linear gives a constant rate of change, so the speed never eases in or out."},{"text":"On an F-curve, the steepest section shows where the object is...","options":["at its keyframe","holding still","moving fastest"],"answer":2,"fb":"A steep slope means a large change in value per frame, which is high speed."},{"text":"Malik wants to check his key poses with no inbetweens first. He sets interpolation to...","options":["Elastic","Bezier","Constant (stepped)"],"answer":2,"fb":"Stepped keys show only the poses, which is how animators block pose-to-pose."},{"text":"In a walk cycle, the pose where the body is at its LOWEST point is the...","options":["down pose","up pose","passing pose"],"answer":0,"fb":"Just after contact the front leg bends to take the weight: the down pose."},{"text":"In a walk cycle, both feet are on the ground and the legs are at their widest in the...","options":["up pose","contact pose","passing pose"],"answer":1,"fb":"The contact pose has the heel striking and the back toe on the ground."},{"text":"To make a 24-frame walk loop forever without adding more keys, he uses...","options":["Shade Smooth","Smart UV Project","Make Cyclic (a Cycles modifier)"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cyclic extrapolation repeats the curve indefinitely."}],"win":"Excellent. You can read animation curves and name the walk cycle poses, two skills that move you from beginner to Grade One.","tutor":[{"h":"Steady, smooth or stepped","p":"<p>Linear = steady. Bezier = eased. Constant = jumps.</p>"},{"h":"Up and down","p":"<p>Taking the weight = low. Pushing off = high.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Graph Editor and walk quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Steady, constant speed uses which interpolation?","options":["Linear","Bezier"],"answer":0,"fb":"Linear has no easing."},{"text":"Lowest body position in a walk is the...","options":["up pose","down pose"],"answer":1,"fb":"Down: taking the weight."},{"text":"A flat part of a curve means the object is...","options":["very fast","slow or still"],"answer":1,"fb":"Flat = little change."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Graph Editor and walk cycle quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: diagnose problems from curves and walks, and do one calculation.</p>","items":[{"text":"A ball's Z curve has rounded bottoms where it meets the floor. What will viewers see?","options":["a soft, floaty contact, as if the ball slows down before touching the floor","a perfect bounce","the ball holding still at the top","a sharp, fast impact"],"answer":0,"fb":"Rounded bottoms mean easing into the contact. Use Vector handles for a sharp change of direction."},{"text":"A Bezier curve dips below zero between two keys, so a foot sinks into the floor. What is the fix?","options":["Add more frames","Switch to Cycles","Use Auto Clamped handles or adjust the handles to stop the overshoot","Delete the floor"],"answer":2,"fb":"The curve is overshooting. Clamped handles keep it between the key values."},{"text":"A cyclic walk jumps once every loop. What is the MOST likely cause?","options":["The frame rate is 24 fps","The character has too many polygons","The first and last poses do not match exactly","Bezier interpolation was used"],"answer":2,"fb":"For a seamless loop, the last pose must lead exactly into the first."},{"text":"At 24 fps, a brisk walk uses 9 frames per step. How many frames is one full cycle?","options":["24","36","18","9"],"answer":2,"fb":"A cycle is two steps: 2 × 9 = 18 frames."},{"text":"In a walk cycle, the body is at its HIGHEST in the...","options":["contact pose","passing pose","up pose","down pose"],"answer":2,"fb":"The supporting foot pushes off and the body rises just before the next contact."},{"text":"A clock's second hand must snap from one position to the next. Which interpolation?","options":["Bezier","Constant","Elastic","Linear"],"answer":1,"fb":"Constant (stepped) jumps between values with no inbetweens."},{"text":"A character animated on the spot seems to skate. What is wrong?","options":["The head bobs","The planted foot is not sliding back at a steady rate that matches the step length","The arms swing opposite to the legs","The walk has a down pose"],"answer":1,"fb":"On the spot, the ground \"moves\" under the planted foot, so its slide must be steady."},{"text":"Which changes BEST turn a normal walk into a sad walk?","options":["linear interpolation everywhere","removing the passing pose","fewer frames per step and a bigger bounce","more frames per step, a lower head and less arm swing"],"answer":3,"fb":"Slower timing, a dropped head and reduced swing all read as low energy."}],"win":"Excellent. You can diagnose curves, fix loops and change a walk's personality, which is Grade One animation thinking."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Render and export settings","html":"<p><b>Rendering</b> is the process of turning a scene or timeline into finished images or video. For a Paper 02 practical or the SBA product's <b>animation or 3D component</b>, the right settings save time and marks.</p>\n<p><b>Blender</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Render Properties &gt; Render Engine</b>: <b>Eevee</b> is real-time and fast, good for school computers and deadlines; <b>Cycles</b> is a path tracer with realistic light and shadows but is much slower. Workbench is a quick preview engine.</li>\n<li><b>Output Properties</b>: Resolution <b>1920 by 1080</b> (16:9, YouTube) or <b>1080 by 1920</b> (9:16, Reels, TikTok, Stories); set % to 50 for quick tests. <b>Frame rate</b> 24 or 30 fps. <b>Frame Start and End</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Output path</b>: choose a named folder, for example <code>//renders/bounce_</code>.</li>\n<li><b>File format</b>: a <b>PNG image sequence</b> is safest (if Blender crashes at frame 200 you keep frames 1 to 199) and can be joined later in OpenShot or Blender's Video Sequencer. For a direct video choose <b>FFmpeg Video</b>, container <b>MPEG-4</b>, codec <b>H.264</b>, quality Medium or High.</li>\n<li><b>Render &gt; Render Animation</b> (Ctrl+F12). <b>Render Image</b> (F12) renders one still, useful for a poster or an SBA screenshot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2D exports</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Note</th></tr>\n<tr><td>MP4 (H.264)</td><td>Social media, YouTube, the SBA 30-second ad</td><td>Small file, plays everywhere; no transparency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Animated GIF</td><td>Short loops, stickers, website banners</td><td>Only 256 colours; large files for long clips; no sound</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PNG sequence</td><td>Compositing, safe master copy</td><td>Lossless and supports transparency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HTML5 Canvas / SVG</td><td>Interactive web animation</td><td>Scales and stays sharp; needs a browser</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media renders a 10-second test at 50% resolution in Eevee before committing. A full Cycles render of the final shot runs overnight on the office machine, while the PNG frames go into a shared Google Drive folder that Zara reviews in the morning.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02 there is no internet, so you cannot download assets or plugins. Practise with the default tools, state your export settings in your answer (\"MP4, H.264, 1920 by 1080, 24 fps, because...\") and name files clearly with your candidate number.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Rendering turns your scene into finished images. In Blender, Eevee is fast and Cycles is realistic but slow. Set the resolution, frame rate and frame range, and render to a PNG image sequence so a crash does not lose everything. Then join the frames into an MP4. A school in Dominica with older computers would choose Eevee to meet a deadline.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Rendering decisions depend on the <b>deadline, the hardware and the output</b>. Test renders at 50% resolution catch mistakes cheaply, because render time grows with the number of pixels: halving both dimensions leaves a quarter of the pixels. A PNG sequence takes an extra step to assemble, but it is lossless, a crash only costs the frame being rendered, and its alpha channel allows compositing over live footage. Cycles is worth its long render times when realistic light matters, such as glass or reflections; for stylised motion graphics, Eevee often looks just as good. Always state settings with a reason: \"1080 × 1920, 30 fps, MP4 H.264, because it will be posted as an Instagram story.\"</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation","intro":"<p>You are an intern at the Island Pulse Media. Zara needs a 15-second animated sting for an Emancipation Day community event post, due at 9 a.m. tomorrow. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The sting has a 3D conch shell that bounces onto the screen and reveals the event title. How do you begin?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Plan key poses and timing on paper or in a quick storyboard, then block them in Blender with stepped keys.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Pose-to-pose planning lets you hit the exact 15-second duration."},{"t":"Start animating straight ahead from frame 1 and see where it ends up.","to":"bad1","fb":"Straight-ahead makes it hard to control length and staging for a timed brand piece."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your blocked keys look good. When you switch to Bezier, the shell floats and drifts into the floor between bounces. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Open the Graph Editor, set the contact keys to Vector handles and add squash on the contact frames.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Sharp contacts and squash make the impact read clearly."},{"t":"Switch everything to Linear so it moves evenly.","to":"bad2","fb":"Linear removes all easing, so the shell would move like a machine with no hang time."},{"t":"Add more and more keyframes on every frame until it looks right.","to":"bad2","fb":"Keying every frame defeats interpolation and makes changes very slow. The curves are the real problem."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>It is 10 p.m. A full Cycles render would take about 8 hours on the office computer. How do you render?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Render straight to an animated GIF in Cycles at full quality.","to":"bad3","fb":"GIF is limited to 256 colours and gives a large, poor-quality file, and Cycles may miss the deadline."},{"t":"Render in Eevee at 1080 by 1920 (it will be posted as a vertical story), 30 fps, as a PNG sequence, then convert to MP4 H.264.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. Eevee is fast, the PNG sequence protects you from crashes, and MP4 suits Instagram."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The render is done. How do you hand it over?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Name it IPM_EmancipationSting_v1_1080x1920.mp4, upload it with the .blend file to the shared Drive folder and message Zara with the settings used.","to":"good","fb":"Professional. Clear naming, source files kept and a short handover note."},{"t":"Email only the MP4 called final.mp4 and delete the project files to save space.","to":"bad4","fb":"Without the source file no one can fix a typo in the title, and \"final.mp4\" is impossible to track."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Delivered on time.</b> Zara posts the sting at 9 a.m. A Grade One answer would explain why it worked: pose-to-pose planning fixed the timing, the Graph Editor and squash gave weight, Eevee and a PNG sequence met the deadline safely, and clear naming supported teamwork.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Out of time.</b> Your straight-ahead animation runs to 26 seconds and the reveal is badly staged. Try again and plan key poses first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Still floaty, and now messy.</b> You spend hours fighting the motion. Try again and fix the interpolation in the Graph Editor.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Missed deadline and banding.</b> The render is unfinished at 9 a.m. and the GIF colours look blotchy. Try again with a faster engine and a better format.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>No way to edit.</b> Zara spots a spelling mistake in the title and nobody can fix it. Try again and keep the source file with a clear name.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Plan for the deadline","p":"<p>A fixed duration needs key poses first.</p>"},{"h":"Fix the curve, not the frames","p":"<p>Floaty contacts are an interpolation problem.</p>"},{"h":"Speed and safety","p":"<p>Think about engine speed, crash-proof formats and the platform's shape.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Deadline animation (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a 15-second sting is due tomorrow morning.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A full Cycles render takes 8 hours. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Render in Eevee as a PNG sequence, then convert to MP4.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Fast and safe."},{"t":"Render a full-quality animated GIF in Cycles.","to":"bad","fb":"Slow and the wrong format."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You chose a fast engine and a safe, suitable format.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> It misses the deadline and looks blotchy. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Deadline for the Emancipation Day animation (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief: the 15-second sting must run at <b>30 fps</b>, the title must appear exactly on a drum hit at <b>4 seconds</b>, the client loves glossy reflections and it is due at 9 a.m. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How do you begin the conch shell animation?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Animate straight ahead and trim to 15 seconds later.","to":"bad1","fb":"You cannot reliably hit an exact frame or length working straight ahead."},{"t":"Block the poses with Bezier keys from the start, so it looks smooth early.","to":"ok1","fb":"It works eventually, but smooth curves hide pose problems and slow your decisions."},{"t":"Plan the key poses on paper, note that the drum hit falls at about frame 120, then block the poses in Blender with stepped keys.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. 4 seconds × 30 fps = 120 frames, so the reveal is planned on the exact frame."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>After switching to Bezier, the shell drifts into the floor between bounces and lands softly. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"In the Graph Editor, clamp the overshoot, set the contact keys to Vector handles and key a small squash on each contact.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. The curve problems are fixed where they live, and squash adds weight."},{"t":"Key every frame by hand until it looks right.","to":"bad2","fb":"Keying every frame defeats interpolation and makes changes painfully slow."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>It is 10 p.m. A Cycles render would take about 8 hours; Eevee takes about 20 minutes. The client loves glossy reflections. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Render straight to MP4 in Cycles to save the conversion step.","to":"bad3","fb":"A crash near the end would lose the whole file, and the deadline is at risk."},{"t":"Start the full Cycles render now and hope it finishes in time.","to":"ok2","fb":"It finishes at 6 a.m., leaving no time to fix anything."},{"t":"Render a 50% Eevee test with its reflection settings turned on, check it, then render the final in Eevee as a PNG sequence at 1080 × 1920.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. You test cheaply, keep the look the client wants and protect yourself with a frame sequence."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Frames 300 to 320 show a flickering texture on the shell. You have 30 minutes left. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Fix the texture, re-render only frames 300 to 320 into the PNG sequence and rebuild the MP4.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. This is exactly why you rendered a sequence."},{"t":"Re-render the whole animation from frame 1.","to":"ok3","fb":"Safe, but it wastes most of your remaining time."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The fixed sting is ready. How do you hand it over?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Email only an MP4 called final.mp4.","to":"bad4","fb":"Without the source file and a clear name, no one can make changes or track versions."},{"t":"Name it IPM_EmancipationSting_v1_1080x1920_30fps.mp4, upload it with the .blend file and its textures packed, and message Zara the settings used.","to":"good","fb":"Professional. Anyone can find it, play it and fix it."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One delivery.</b> The title lands on the drum hit, the shell has weight and gloss, a late fix took minutes and Zara has everything she needs. You planned in frames, fixed the curves, tested before committing and rendered in a way that let you recover.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow progress.</b> The sting is finished, but late at night after hours of chasing pose problems hidden by smooth curves. A partial success: block with stepped keys first.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>No margin.</b> The render arrives at 6 a.m. with a flicker you have no time to fix. A partial success: test first and use a faster engine.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Just in time.</b> The full re-render finishes minutes before 9 a.m. A partial success: re-render only the frames you need.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Off the beat.</b> The animation runs long and the title misses the drum hit. Try again and plan your keys in frames.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Tangled curves.</b> Hundreds of keys make every change slow and the motion still jitters. Try again and fix the interpolation.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Lost render.</b> Blender crashes at 95% and the MP4 is unreadable. Try again and render a frame sequence.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>No way to edit.</b> Zara spots a spelling mistake in the title and nobody can fix it. Try again and keep the source file with a clear name.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Explain how to animate a bouncing ball","genre":"Structured response: explaining an animation process (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The St. Lucia Youth Sports Council wants a short animated logo in which a cricket ball bounces three times across the screen before landing beside its name. A junior animator has made a version where the ball moves at the same speed throughout, stays perfectly round and bounces to the same height each time. The Council says it \"looks fake\".</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for the junior animator that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>explains</b> the difference between timing and spacing, and how keyframes and interpolation are used to create the bounce;</li><li><b>explains</b> how squash and stretch and slow in and slow out (ease) would improve the animation;</li><li><b>outlines</b> the steps to produce the bounce in a named 2D or 3D software package, including suitable export settings.</li></ol>","min":200,"max":400,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Timing is ... whereas spacing is ... The animator sets keyframes at ... and the software interpolates ...<br><b>(b)</b> Squash and stretch means ... On the contact frame ... Slow in and slow out means ... so at the top of each bounce ...<br><b>(c)</b> In [software], first ... Then ... In the Graph Editor / Properties panel ... Finally, export as ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I distinguished timing (number of frames) from spacing (distance between positions).","I named keyframes at the top of each arc and at each contact.","I explained squash and stretch, including keeping volume.","I explained easing at the tops and fast spacing near the ground.","I named real software tools or panels and gave export settings.","I said each bounce should get lower and shorter.","I used other terms where they help, such as arcs, exaggeration, or modelling and rendering for 3D."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Knowledge of animation principles","marks":5,"look":["timing","spacing","keyframe|key frame|key pose","squash","stretch","ease|slow in|slow out","interpolat|tween|inbetween","arc|anticipat|exaggerat|solid drawing|staging","model|render|layout|polygon|mesh|vector|bitmap"],"min":4,"strong":"You explained the key principles accurately. Next step: link each one to the effect on the viewer, such as weight or realism.","weak":"Define timing (number of frames) and spacing (distance per frame), explain keyframes and tweening, and include squash and stretch and easing."},{"criterion":"Application of technique and software","marks":5,"look":["blender|animate|synfig|pencil2d|opentoonz","graph editor|dope sheet|timeline|motion editor|classic tween|motion tween","vector|bezier|linear|curve","scale|volume","mp4|h\\.?264|png|gif","fps|frames per second|24|30"],"min":3,"strong":"Your steps are practical and specific. Next step: justify each export choice for the platform.","weak":"Name the software and the exact panel or tool, for example \"set the contact keys to Vector in Blender's Graph Editor\", and give export settings (MP4, H.264, 24 fps)."},{"criterion":"Response to the scenario","marks":3,"look":["lower|smaller|shorter|energy","same speed|constant speed|real|fake|natural|believ","cricket|council|logo|st\\.? lucia"],"min":2,"strong":"You fixed each problem the Council noticed. Well targeted.","weak":"Address the three faults in the stimulus: constant speed, no squash and stretch, and identical bounce heights."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Timing is the number of frames an action takes, which controls how long it lasts and how heavy it feels, whereas spacing is the distance the ball moves from one frame to the next, which controls acceleration and deceleration. The animator sets keyframes at the important positions: the top of each arc and each contact with the ground. The software then interpolates (tweens) the inbetween frames. At the moment the ball moves at the same speed because the spacing is even, as if the interpolation were linear.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Squash and stretch would show that the cricket ball has weight. On each contact frame the ball should squash slightly, getting flatter and wider while keeping the same volume; a cricket ball is hard, so the squash should be small. On the frames just before and after contact it should stretch a little along its path. Slow in and slow out means the ball should ease into and out of the top of each arc, with more drawings near the top and fewer in the middle of the fall, so it seems to hang, and spacing wide near the ground where it is falling fastest. The ball should also travel along smooth arcs, because organic motion follows a curved path. Each bounce should also be lower and shorter than the last because the ball loses energy, which will stop it looking fake.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I would use Blender, because it is free and the 3D model can be reused and rotated for other Council graphics. In the modelling stage I would add a UV sphere (a polygon mesh) with a red leather material, set the origin to the base of the ball and set 24 fps on the Timeline. I would press I to insert location keys at each top and contact, moving the ball forward each time, with bounces getting smaller. In the Graph Editor I would set the contact keys to Vector handles for sharp impacts and keep Bezier at the tops for easing. I would key Scale for a small squash on contact and stretch either side, then adjust timing in the Dope Sheet. Finally, in the rendering stage, I would render in Eevee at 1920 by 1080 as a PNG sequence for safety and convert it to MP4 (H.264), because MP4 is small and plays on the Council's website and social media. I would name it SLYSC_LogoBounce_v1.mp4.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In Paper 02 \"explain\" and \"outline\" questions, marks go to correct terms used accurately, a clear sequence of steps and specific tool names. Number your steps, name panels exactly (Timeline, Dope Sheet, Graph Editor) and always justify the export format for the platform.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Explain how to animate a bouncing ball (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: animating a bouncing ball, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> what timing means and what spacing means. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> where the keyframes should go in a bouncing ball animation. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> what should happen to the ball's shape when it hits the ground, and just before it hits. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why each bounce should be lower than the one before. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE piece of software you could use and ONE format to export the animation in. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Timing means ... Spacing means ...<br><b>(b)</b> The keyframes should go at ... and at ...<br><b>(c)</b> When the ball hits the ground it should ... Just before it hits, it should ...<br><b>(d)</b> Each bounce should be lower because ...<br><b>(e)</b> I would use ... and export as ...</p>","checklist":["I explained timing and spacing differently.","I put keyframes at the tops and the contacts.","I described squash on contact and stretch before it.","I said the ball loses energy.","I named software and an export format."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Timing and spacing","marks":2,"look":["timing.*(frame|long|how many)|(frame|long|how many).*timing","spacing.*(distance|far|apart)|(distance|far|apart).*spacing"],"min":1,"strong":"You explained both terms correctly.","weak":"Timing is how many frames an action takes; spacing is how far the ball moves between frames."},{"criterion":"Keyframe positions","marks":2,"look":["top|highest|peak","contact|ground|floor|hit"],"min":2,"strong":"Keys at the tops and the contacts.","weak":"Put keyframes at the top of each bounce and where the ball touches the ground."},{"criterion":"Squash and stretch","marks":2,"look":["squash|flat","stretch|long"],"min":2,"strong":"You described squash and stretch.","weak":"The ball squashes (gets flatter) when it hits and stretches (gets longer) just before."},{"criterion":"Lower bounces","marks":2,"look":["energy|lose|slow|gravity|real","lower|smaller|shorter"],"min":1,"strong":"You linked lower bounces to lost energy.","weak":"Each bounce is lower because the ball loses energy every time it hits the ground, so it looks real."},{"criterion":"Software and format","marks":2,"look":["blender|animate|synfig|pencil2d|opentoonz|krita","mp4|gif|png"],"min":2,"strong":"Real software and a suitable format.","weak":"Name a program such as Blender or Pencil2D and a format such as MP4."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Timing means how many frames the action takes, so it controls how long it lasts. Spacing means how far the ball moves from one frame to the next.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The keyframes should go at the top of each bounce and at each point where the ball touches the ground.</p><p><b>(c)</b> When the ball hits the ground it should squash, becoming flatter and wider. Just before it hits, it should stretch, becoming a little longer in the direction it is moving.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Each bounce should be lower because the ball loses energy every time it hits the ground, so equal bounces look fake.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I would use Blender and export the animation as an MP4.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part. In (a) award 1 mark per correct definition; students who use timing and spacing as the same idea score 0 for that part. A cricket ball should squash only a little; accept any answer that describes squash and stretch correctly. Move students to the Core task when they can add easing (slow in and slow out) to their explanation.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Explain how to animate a bouncing ball (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: animating a bouncing ball, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The St. Lucia Youth Sports Council wants an animated logo in which a <b>cricket ball and a tennis ball</b> bounce across the screen before landing beside its name. It needs a <b>6-second looping</b> version for its website, a version for <b>Instagram</b> and a <b>3D</b> version for a big screen at the national stadium. A junior animator's draft has both balls moving at the same speed throughout, staying perfectly round and bouncing to the same height each time. The Graph Editor shows straight, linear curves, and the balls' origins are at their centres. The Council says it \"looks fake\".</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for the junior animator that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>explains</b> timing, spacing, keyframes and interpolation (including linear, Bezier and Vector handles), and <b>diagnoses</b> why the draft looks fake <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>compares</b> how the cricket ball and the tennis ball should bounce, using squash and stretch, timing, spacing, arcs and bounce height <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>outlines</b> a detailed workflow for the 3D version in Blender, including the origin, the Graph Editor, the Dope Sheet and a seamless loop for the website <i>(7 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>recommends</b> render and export settings for the website loop, Instagram and the stadium screen, with justification <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> whether 2D or 3D is the better choice for this client <i>(2 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":450,"max":800,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Timing is ... whereas spacing is ... Keys go at ... Linear interpolation ... Bezier ... Vector handles ... The draft looks fake because ...<br><b>(b)</b> The cricket ball ... whereas the tennis ball ... Both should follow arcs that ...<br><b>(c)</b> First ... origin ... keys ... Graph Editor ... Dope Sheet ... loop ...<br><b>(d)</b> Website: ... Instagram: ... Stadium: ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> On balance, ...</p>","checklist":["I explained timing, spacing, keyframes and three kinds of interpolation, and diagnosed all the faults in the draft.","I compared the two balls on squash and stretch, timing, spacing, arcs and bounce height.","My Blender workflow covers origin, keys, Graph Editor, Dope Sheet and a seamless loop.","I gave justified settings for three different outputs.","I evaluated 2D against 3D for this client.","I used the stimulus throughout: two balls, the loop, Instagram and the stadium screen."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Principles and diagnosis","marks":6,"look":["timing","spacing","keyframe|key frame|key pose","linear","bezier|ease|slow in|slow out","vector","same speed|constant speed|origin|same height|perfectly round"],"min":5,"strong":"Accurate explanations and a diagnosis that covers every fault in the draft.","weak":"Define timing and spacing, explain linear, Bezier and Vector interpolation, then link each fault (even speed, no squash, equal heights, centred origin) to a cause."},{"criterion":"Comparing the two balls","marks":6,"look":["cricket","tennis","squash","timing|frames|quick|slow","arc","height|lower|higher|energy","whereas|however|while|compared"],"min":5,"strong":"A detailed comparison showing how material and weight change every principle.","weak":"Compare the two balls on each principle: the hard cricket ball squashes little, falls quickly and bounces lower; the tennis ball squashes more and bounces higher."},{"criterion":"Blender workflow","marks":7,"look":["origin|pivot","insert|keyframe| i ","graph editor","vector|handle","dope sheet","scale|squash","cyclic|loop|shift\\+e|first and last"],"min":5,"strong":"A precise, ordered workflow using the right panels, including a seamless loop.","weak":"Give the steps in order: set the origin at the base, key the tops and contacts, fix contacts with Vector handles, key scale for squash, retime in the Dope Sheet and make the loop seamless."},{"criterion":"Render and export settings","marks":4,"look":["eevee|cycles|png sequence","mp4|h\\.?264","1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1920|1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1080|9:16|1:1|4:5","1920 ?(by|x|×) ?1080|16:9|stadium","because|so that"],"min":4,"strong":"Three outputs with correct, justified settings.","weak":"Give engine, format, resolution and frame rate for each output and a reason linked to where it will be shown."},{"criterion":"2D or 3D evaluation","marks":2,"look":["2d","3d","because|on balance|therefore|however"],"min":3,"strong":"A balanced, justified judgement.","weak":"Give one advantage of each approach for this client and make a justified choice."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Timing</b> is the number of frames an action takes, which sets its duration and suggests weight, whereas <b>spacing</b> is the distance the ball moves between frames, which creates acceleration and deceleration. The animator places <b>keyframes</b> at the important positions, the top of each arc and each contact, and the software <b>interpolates</b> the frames between them. <b>Linear</b> interpolation changes values at a constant rate, which is why both balls move at the same speed throughout. <b>Bezier</b> interpolation eases in and out of each key, which suits the tops of the arcs where a ball slows and seems to hang. <b>Vector</b> handles create a sharp corner in the curve, which is needed at each contact because the ball changes direction instantly. The draft looks fake for four reasons: linear curves give even spacing with no easing; there is no squash and stretch, so the balls seem weightless; equal bounce heights ignore lost energy; and with the origins at the centres, any squash would shrink the balls into mid-air instead of flattening them against the ground.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The <b>cricket ball</b> is hard and heavy, so it should squash only very slightly on contact and barely stretch, fall with quick timing and lose height fast, making perhaps two or three short, low bounces. The <b>tennis ball</b> is softer and lighter, so it should squash more noticeably, stretch along its path just before and after contact, rise higher and hang longer at the top of each arc, and its bounces should die away more gradually. Both balls should travel along smooth <b>arcs</b> that get lower and shorter; spacing should be close at the tops and wide near the ground for both, but the cricket ball's arcs should be flatter and quicker. Showing this difference is what makes viewers believe the two balls are made of different materials.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> In Blender: delete the default cube, add a plane for the ground and two UV spheres with red-leather and yellow-felt materials. In Edit Mode, snap the 3D cursor to each ball's bottom vertex and use <b>Set Origin > Origin to 3D Cursor</b>, so scaling squashes against the ground. Set 24 fps and a 144-frame range (6 seconds). Press <b>I</b> to key location at each top and contact, moving the balls forward each time and making each arc lower and shorter. In the <b>Graph Editor</b>, select the contact keys on the Z curves and press V > <b>Vector</b>; keep Bezier at the tops and use Auto Clamped handles to stop the balls dipping below the ground. Key <b>scale</b> on the contact frames (for the tennis ball about Z 0.7, X and Y 1.2; much less for the cricket ball) and stretch on the frames either side. Use the <b>Dope Sheet</b> to retime groups of keys so the cricket ball's bounces are quicker. For the website loop, make frame 144 match frame 1 exactly (both balls back in their starting positions behind the logo, or the camera moving with them), then apply <b>Make Cyclic</b> (Shift+E) to the curves so the loop is seamless. Check the arcs with Motion Paths before rendering.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Render a 50% <b>Eevee</b> test, then the final frames as a <b>PNG sequence</b> so a crash does not lose the work. <i>Website loop:</i> a muted H.264 MP4 at 1280 × 720, about 2 Mbps, set to loop, because it loads quickly on mobile data and is far smaller than a GIF. <i>Instagram:</i> an H.264 MP4 at 1080 × 1080 (or 1080 × 1920 for Reels), 30 fps, AAC audio if music is added, because it fills the phone screen. <i>Stadium screen:</i> a 1920 × 1080 (16:9) H.264 MP4 at a high bitrate, because it is shown large and must stay sharp; I would confirm the screen's exact resolution with the stadium first.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> 3D is the better choice here, because one set of models can be reused for all three outputs and the stadium wants a 3D version, and the balls' materials read convincingly with real lighting. However, a 2D version would be quicker to produce and fully adequate for the website and Instagram, so if time were short, a 2D logo for social media with the 3D version reserved for the stadium would be a sensible compromise.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 6 + 7 + 4 + 2). Top responses diagnose every fault in the stimulus, including the centred origin, and show that material changes every principle, not just squash. Check the maths: 6 seconds at 24 fps is 144 frames. In (c), reward precise panel names and an explanation of how the loop is made seamless; in (d), reward settings that differ sensibly for each screen.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U7","title":"Interactive digital media for collaboration","objectives":["Identify and describe examples of interactive digital media tools used for collaboration, including Google Workspace, Microsoft Teams, Slack, Trello, Miro, Padlet and Figma, and justify a tool for a given task.","Explain what electronic communities are (online groups, forums, wikis, Discord servers and creator communities) and how Caribbean creators use them.","Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using interactive digital media for collaboration, including real-time feedback, global reach, version control, connectivity, the digital divide, privacy and over-reliance on technology.","Distinguish between self-directed and collaborative digital activities and manage roles and responsibilities in a team.","Apply netiquette and professional conduct in digital collaborative spaces, and keep evidence of collaboration for the SBA.","Name tools for each job (keep the conversation going, make task progress visible, create the work together), explain the term electronic community, and weigh its advantages and disadvantages with examples."],"videos":[{"title":"Online collaboration tools compared","q":"best online collaboration tools for students google workspace trello miro explained"},{"title":"Using Trello for a group project","q":"trello tutorial for beginners student group project kanban"},{"title":"Netiquette explained","q":"netiquette rules explained for students online communication"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is digital collaboration?","html":"<p>Here is the challenge: <b>one project, different places</b>. How would your group finish a project if you could not meet? You would need a way to <b>talk</b>, <b>organise tasks</b> and <b>create something together</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Collaboration</b> means working towards a shared goal. People combine their efforts, such as classmates producing one presentation. A <b>collaboration tool</b> helps people do that work. One online platform can bring together team discussion, file sharing, online storage for documentation, task assignments and real-time project collaboration.</p>\n<p>At their heart, collaboration tools <b>align people</b>: everyone can see what to do and where the work belongs. <b>Digital collaboration</b> uses interactive digital media (shared documents, chat, video calls, boards and design files) so that people can create, comment and decide together, whether they are in the same room or on different islands.</p>\n<p>Collaboration can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Synchronous</b>: everyone works at the same time, in real time. Examples: a Microsoft Teams video meeting, three students editing one Google Doc live, a Figma design session.</li>\n<li><b>Asynchronous</b>: people contribute at different times. Examples: comments on a Padlet wall, a Trello card updated overnight, a Slack thread answered the next morning, a forum reply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most digital collaboration tools fall into a few families:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Family</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Communication</td><td>Talking, messaging, meetings</td><td>Microsoft Teams, Slack, Google Meet, Zoom, WhatsApp groups, Discord</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Co-authoring and file sharing</td><td>Creating and storing shared files</td><td>Google Workspace (Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive), Microsoft 365 (Word, OneDrive, SharePoint), Dropbox</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Project management</td><td>Tracking tasks, deadlines and who does what</td><td>Trello, Asana, Microsoft Planner, Notion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visual collaboration</td><td>Brainstorming, mood boards, mind maps</td><td>Miro, Padlet, FigJam, Canva whiteboards</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Design and media</td><td>Working on the same design or video</td><td>Figma, Canva (team), WeVideo</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Knowledge sharing</td><td>Building shared reference content</td><td>Wikis, Google Sites, blogs, Notion</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>At the Island Pulse Media, a typical week uses several of these: Zara (creative director) runs a Monday Teams call, Keisha (marketing) gathers ideas on a Miro board, Maya (web developer) and Jerome (mobile developer) track tasks on Trello, and Malik (video and animation) shares rough cuts through Google Drive for timed comments.</p>\n<p>The SBA criteria list examples such as Skype, Google Drive and Google Docs, Dropbox, wikis, blogs and Google Hangouts. Skype and Hangouts have since been retired, and Microsoft Teams and Google Meet have replaced them, so name current tools in your answers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question says \"identify a tool\", one word is enough. When it says \"describe\" or \"explain\", say <b>what the tool does</b> and <b>how it helps this particular team</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Digital collaboration means working towards a shared goal using online tools: shared documents, chat, video calls, task boards and design files. It can be synchronous, with everyone working at the same time, like a live video call, or asynchronous, with people adding work at different times, like comments on a shared file. A youth group in Montserrat and one in Anguilla could plan an event together this way.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The best teams mix synchronous and asynchronous work on purpose. Live sessions are fast for decisions, brainstorming and settling disagreements, but they need everyone to be free and connected at the same moment, which is hard across time zones, school timetables and unreliable power. Asynchronous work respects different schedules and leaves a written record, but it is slower and messages can be misread. A common weakness is using too many tools with overlapping jobs, so information ends up scattered; agree one tool per family. Tools also change: Skype and Google Hangouts have been retired, so examiners expect current examples. When you explain a tool, always link what it does to how it helps that particular team.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Synchronous or asynchronous?","intro":"<p>A group of Coralstone College students is planning their DME2 SBA. Sort each activity.</p>","main":"Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?","bins":["Synchronous","Asynchronous"],"items":[{"t":"A live brainstorm on a Miro board during class with a timer running.","bin":0,"fb":"Everyone contributes together, in real time: synchronous."},{"t":"A reply to a question on a Discord forum channel two days later.","bin":1,"fb":"Forum threads are classic asynchronous communication."},{"t":"Moving a Trello card to \"Done\" overnight for others to see in the morning.","bin":1,"fb":"The update waits for others, so it is asynchronous."},{"t":"Three members editing the same Figma frame while talking on a call.","bin":0,"fb":"Live co-editing in real time is synchronous."},{"t":"Leaving comments on a Google Doc for a group member to read later.","bin":1,"fb":"The reply can come hours later, so it is asynchronous."},{"t":"Posting mood-board images on a shared Padlet wall over the weekend.","bin":1,"fb":"Contributions build up at different times: asynchronous."},{"t":"A WhatsApp video call to review the 30-second ad draft together.","bin":0,"fb":"A live call is synchronous."},{"t":"A live Microsoft Teams call to agree on the product idea.","bin":0,"fb":"Everyone is present at the same time, so it is synchronous."}],"win":"Well sorted. Synchronous tools give instant discussion; asynchronous tools let people contribute when it suits them across time zones and schedules.","tutor":[{"h":"Same time?","p":"<p>Ask: must everyone be online at once for this to work?</p>"},{"h":"Same tool, two ways","p":"<p>Many tools (Docs, Figma, Miro) can be used either way. Look at how it is used in the example.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A live video call.","bin":0,"fb":"Same time: synchronous."},{"t":"A comment read the next day.","bin":1,"fb":"Different times: asynchronous."},{"t":"Editing a document together live.","bin":0,"fb":"Same time: synchronous."},{"t":"A forum reply posted later.","bin":1,"fb":"Different times: asynchronous."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Synchronous or asynchronous? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: some of these use the same tool in different ways, so decide by WHEN people take part, not by the tool's name.</p>","main":"Is this collaboration synchronous (same time) or asynchronous (different times)?","bins":["Synchronous","Asynchronous"],"items":[{"t":"A designer in Jamaica leaves pinned comments in a Figma file overnight for the team in Trinidad.","bin":1,"fb":"Figma can be live, but comments read the next morning are asynchronous."},{"t":"A Google Form survey that respondents complete during the week.","bin":1,"fb":"People respond whenever they choose: asynchronous."},{"t":"Students vote on Miro sticky notes during a live brainstorm.","bin":0,"fb":"Everyone takes part at the same moment: synchronous."},{"t":"Two developers pair-program on a shared screen during a call.","bin":0,"fb":"Working together live is synchronous."},{"t":"A live-streamed critique session where viewers type questions as it happens.","bin":0,"fb":"Questions and answers happen in real time: synchronous."},{"t":"An email thread about the budget, answered over three days.","bin":1,"fb":"Replies at different times: asynchronous."},{"t":"A teacher leaves voice-note feedback in a WhatsApp group for students to hear after school.","bin":1,"fb":"A voice note is heard later, so it is asynchronous, even though it is spoken."},{"t":"A Discord voice channel where the team talks while each person animates.","bin":0,"fb":"Live conversation in real time is synchronous."},{"t":"A recorded Teams meeting is watched the next day by a member who was absent.","bin":1,"fb":"The meeting was live, but watching the recording later is asynchronous."}],"win":"Excellent. The same tool can be used either way; what matters is whether people take part at the same time."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Three jobs collaboration tools do","html":"<p>Collaboration tools can be grouped by the <b>job</b> they do for a team. Learn these three headings: they give you a ready-made structure for any \"state examples\" or \"describe\" question.</p>\n<p><b>1. Keep the conversation going.</b> People cannot collaborate without communicating. Choose <b>messaging</b> for quick updates or a <b>video meeting</b> when you need to talk things through.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples in the notes</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Messaging</td><td>Flowdock, Slack</td><td>Keep team conversations together. For example, ask which image the group should use.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Video meetings</td><td>GoToMeeting, WebEx</td><td>Discuss ideas through an online call. For example, rehearse your presentation together.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>2. Make task progress visible.</b> <b>Project management tools</b> help a team track objectives, responsibilities and progress. For example, a presentation task can move from \"to do\" to \"done\". Examples in the notes: Asana, DaPulse, ProofHub, Redbooth, Trello, Wimi and Milanote. The cycle has three steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Assign</b>: agree who owns each task.</li>\n<li><b>Update</b>: show what is in progress.</li>\n<li><b>Review</b>: check the shared goal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>3. Create the work together.</b> Teams share responsibility and create together in <b>one shared space</b>, rather than pass separate copies. Examples in the notes: <b>Codingteam</b> (code together, such as on a group website), <b>Igloo</b> (share team knowledge and project guidance), <b>Google Docs</b> (edit a shared class report) and <b>Quip</b> (edit documents and discuss changes).</p>\n<p><b>Keeping the list up to date.</b> Tools come and go, so check a tool is current before you name it in an exam. Flowdock was discontinued by its owner at the end of 2022, so name Slack, Microsoft Teams or Discord for messaging. DaPulse renamed itself <b>monday.com</b> in 2017. GoToMeeting is now branded GoTo Meeting and WebEx is now styled Webex. Salesforce has announced that Quip is being retired and is moving its users to Slack canvases, so Google Docs or Word in Microsoft 365 are safer examples. For coding together, GitHub is the most widely used platform today.</p>\n<p>For the Jolly Harbour marina project, Zara at the Island Pulse Media uses Slack for quick messages and Microsoft Teams for client calls (job 1), a Trello board where Maya assigns, updates and reviews every website task (job 2), and Google Docs and Figma so the team creates in one shared space (job 3).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"state examples of digital media tools used for collaboration\", name the <b>job</b> and a <b>current</b> tool for it: \"Messaging, such as Slack; project management, such as Trello; co-editing, such as Google Docs.\" That shows the examiner you understand what each tool is for.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Collaboration tools do three jobs. They keep the conversation going (messaging such as Slack, and video meetings such as Webex). They make task progress visible (project boards such as Trello, where tasks move from \"to do\" to \"done\"). They let people create the work together in one shared space (such as Google Docs). A school drama club in Barbados might use all three.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Classifying tools by their job is more useful than memorising brand names, because names change while jobs do not. Flowdock closed, DaPulse became monday.com and Quip is being retired, but teams still need messaging, task tracking and shared creation. The project management cycle of <b>assign, update, review</b> explains why task boards work: a named owner removes confusion, visible status reveals problems early and review keeps the shared goal in view. A common exam weakness is naming a tool without its job, or choosing a tool whose job does not fit the need, such as managing deadlines in a chat app. Strong answers match each need to a job, then to a current tool, and explain the fit.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which job does this tool do?","intro":"<p>Use the three headings. A DME2 group at Hummingbird Ridge High School is preparing a class presentation on Emancipation Day. Sort each use of a tool.</p>","main":"Which job is the tool doing here?","bins":["Keep the conversation going","Make task progress visible","Create the work together"],"items":[{"t":"A Slack message asking which photo the group should use on the title slide.","bin":0,"fb":"Quick messaging keeps the team conversation together: job 1."},{"t":"Four students editing the same Google Docs script at the same time.","bin":2,"fb":"One shared space instead of separate copies: job 3."},{"t":"A Trello card called \"Record voice-over\" moved from To do to Done.","bin":1,"fb":"Moving a task from \"to do\" to \"done\" makes progress visible: job 2."},{"t":"A monday.com board (once called DaPulse) showing which tasks are in progress.","bin":1,"fb":"Showing what is in progress is the Update step of project management: job 2."},{"t":"A Webex call to rehearse the presentation the night before.","bin":0,"fb":"Talking things through in an online call is a video meeting: job 1."},{"t":"An Igloo space that holds the project guidance and team knowledge.","bin":2,"fb":"Sharing team knowledge and guidance in one place supports creating together: job 3."},{"t":"Two students coding the group website together in one GitHub repository.","bin":2,"fb":"Coding together in one shared space is creating the work together: job 3."},{"t":"An Asana project where each slide has one named owner and a due date.","bin":1,"fb":"Assigning owners is the first step of Assign, Update, Review: job 2."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can classify collaboration tools by their three jobs: keep the conversation going, make task progress visible and create the work together.","tutor":[{"h":"Talk, track or make?","p":"<p>Ask: is the tool being used to talk, to track who does what, or to build the actual product?</p>"},{"h":"Assign, Update, Review","p":"<p>Any card, board or owner and due date points to project management.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which job does this tool do? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A quick Slack message to the group.","bin":0,"fb":"Messaging: keep the conversation going."},{"t":"A Trello card moved to Done.","bin":1,"fb":"Project management: make task progress visible."},{"t":"Editing one Google Doc together.","bin":2,"fb":"One shared space: create the work together."},{"t":"A video call to rehearse.","bin":0,"fb":"Video meeting: keep the conversation going."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which job does this tool do? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: one tool can do more than one job. Sort each USE by the job it is doing here, not by the tool's name.</p>","main":"Which job is the tool doing here?","bins":["Keep the conversation going","Make task progress visible","Create the work together"],"items":[{"t":"A Webex call where the client approves the final cut.","bin":0,"fb":"A video meeting is a conversation tool."},{"t":"An Asana timeline showing that the edit is behind schedule.","bin":1,"fb":"Reviewing progress against the plan is project management."},{"t":"A Planner tab inside Microsoft Teams showing each task's owner and status.","bin":1,"fb":"Same app as the chat, but this use makes task progress visible."},{"t":"A Google Slides deck the whole group builds at once.","bin":2,"fb":"Co-editing one file is creating the work together."},{"t":"A Microsoft Teams channel where the group asks quick questions about the script.","bin":0,"fb":"Messaging in a channel keeps the conversation going."},{"t":"A Trello checklist ticked off as each scene is filmed.","bin":1,"fb":"Updating a checklist shows progress: the Update step."},{"t":"A GitHub repository where two members merge their website code.","bin":2,"fb":"Coding in one shared space is creating the work together."},{"t":"Two members drawing the storyboard on the same Miro board.","bin":2,"fb":"One shared space for making the work: creating together."},{"t":"A Slack huddle (a short audio call) to settle which logo to use.","bin":0,"fb":"Talking it through live keeps the conversation going."}],"win":"Excellent. You classified each use by its job, which shows you understand what the tool is FOR, not just its name."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Collaboration tools and their uses","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Typical use in a digital media team</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Workspace</b></td><td>Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms, Drive, Meet; real-time co-editing, comments, suggestions and <b>version history</b></td><td>Writing the SBA proposal together; a shared budget in Sheets; a survey in Forms; storing assets in Drive</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Microsoft Teams</b></td><td>Chat, channels, video meetings, file sharing via SharePoint/OneDrive, assignments in education versions</td><td>Class or team channels, recorded meetings, co-editing Word and PowerPoint files</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Slack</b></td><td>Channel-based messaging for workplaces, threads, direct messages, app integrations</td><td>Keeping project talk organised by topic (#design, #client-feedback) instead of one long chat</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Trello</b></td><td><b>Kanban</b> boards with lists and cards; assign members, due dates, checklists, labels</td><td>A production schedule: To Do, Doing, Review, Done</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Miro</b></td><td>Infinite online whiteboard with sticky notes, mind maps, templates, voting</td><td>Brainstorming charts, user journey maps, storyboard sketches</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Padlet</b></td><td>A simple shared wall or board for posts, images, links and video</td><td>Collecting mood-board images, peer feedback, class Q&amp;A</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Figma</b> (and FigJam)</td><td>Browser-based interface design; several people edit one file, leave pinned comments, share prototypes by link</td><td>Wireframes and high-fidelity app or website mock-ups; client review of prototypes</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Choosing a tool</b>: consider the <b>task</b> (talk, write, plan, design), the <b>audience</b> (students, clients, the public), <b>cost</b> (free tiers have limits), <b>device and data</b> requirements, <b>privacy</b> and whether the team already uses it. A school that uses Google Classroom will find Google Workspace easy; a company using Microsoft 365 will favour Teams.</p>\n<p><b>Example</b>: the Tobago Food Week team, working from both Trinidad and Tobago, uses a Trello board for tasks, a Figma file for the event app and a shared Drive folder. French-side and Dutch-side members can work on the same files without travelling across the border each day.</p>\n<p>You should be able to <b>justify</b> your choice of communication and collaboration tools based on audience, purpose and message, and keep a shared digital space such as a blog, wiki or portfolio with drafts, peer feedback and reflection.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A strong \"justify\" answer links a <b>feature</b> to a <b>benefit</b> for the <b>context</b>: \"Trello's cards and due dates let the group see who is responsible for each task, so the production schedule is met before the SBA deadline.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Each collaboration tool has a main purpose. Google Workspace and Microsoft 365 let people write and store files together. Teams and Slack organise chat and meetings. Trello tracks tasks on cards. Miro and Padlet are shared boards for ideas. Figma lets people design screens together. Choose by task, users, cost, devices and what the team already uses, like a school in Trinidad already on Google Classroom.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Justify a tool\" questions are really about <b>fit</b>: a feature only matters if it solves this team's problem. Trello's due dates help a group facing a deadline; Figma's live co-editing helps designers in different places; Padlet's simplicity helps members with low digital skills. Constraints often decide the choice: free tiers limit storage or members, some tools work poorly on phones and data costs matter for prepaid users. Privacy matters too, especially for minors, so school-approved accounts are safer than personal ones. Integration is a strong argument: a school already using Google Classroom gains little by adding separate Microsoft tools. A Grade One justification names the feature, the benefit, the context and one limitation.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the tool to the job","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is setting up a new project for a Jolly Harbour marina. Match each need to the best tool.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Track tasks on cards across To Do, Doing and Done","r":"Trello"},{"l":"Co-design the marina app screens in the browser","r":"Figma"},{"l":"Run a sticky-note brainstorm and vote on ideas","r":"Miro"},{"l":"Write the proposal together with version history","r":"Google Docs (Google Workspace)"},{"l":"Organise team chat into topic channels with threads","r":"Slack"},{"l":"Collect mood-board photos from the client on a simple wall","r":"Padlet"},{"l":"Hold a recorded video meeting with the client in a team channel","r":"Microsoft Teams"}],"win":"Very good. You matched each tool to its main purpose, which is the first step to justifying a tool choice.","tutor":[{"h":"Plan, talk, make","p":"<p>Sort the needs first: planning tasks, communicating, or creating content.</p>"},{"h":"Boards","p":"<p>Trello boards hold task cards; Miro boards hold free-form sticky notes; Padlet is a simple wall.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the tool to the job (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Task cards and due dates","r":"Trello"},{"l":"Designing app screens together","r":"Figma"},{"l":"Writing a document together","r":"Google Docs"},{"l":"Video meetings and team channels","r":"Microsoft Teams"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the tool to the job (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: each need includes a constraint. Match it to the tool that fits BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A team across time zones needs topic channels and threads for quick questions","r":"Slack"},{"l":"Beginners need a visual task board with checklists and due dates they can learn in minutes","r":"Trello"},{"l":"Designers in Kingston and Bridgetown must edit one app screen and pin comments for the client","r":"Figma"},{"l":"A teacher wants students to post mood-board images and links on one simple wall","r":"Padlet"},{"l":"A school already on Microsoft 365 needs recorded class meetings and co-edited Word files","r":"Microsoft Teams"},{"l":"A group needs a live sticky-note brainstorm with voting and templates","r":"Miro"},{"l":"A client wants to co-write a proposal in the browser with suggestions and version history","r":"Google Docs (Google Workspace)"},{"l":"Programmers must merge code and track every change to the group website","r":"GitHub"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each need, including its constraint, to the tool that fits, which is the core of a \"justify\" answer."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Electronic communities","html":"<p>In short: <b>shared interests become relationships</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">A <b>virtual community</b>, or <b>electronic community</b>, is a group of people who connect through the Internet or other collaborative networks. Members share interests, ideas and feelings through public discussions, interactions and information exchanges. Over time, those exchanges can build personal relationships.</p>\n<p>Example: a <b>photography community</b> creates value when members share advice and discuss each other's pictures. Picture a Jamaican photography group where members post sunset shots from Negril and get tips on exposure from photographers in Barbados and Curaçao. Common forms of electronic community:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Online groups</b>: Facebook Groups, WhatsApp communities, LinkedIn groups. Example: a Dominica small-business group sharing advice after Hurricane Maria, or a parents' group for a Form 6 class at Mahoe Grove High School.</li>\n<li><b>Forums</b> (discussion boards): posts organised into topics and threads, often searchable for years. Examples: the official Blender community forums, Reddit communities, Stack Overflow for code questions.</li>\n<li><b>Wikis</b>: websites anyone with permission can edit collaboratively, with page history so changes can be reversed. Wikipedia is the best-known; teams also build private wikis in Google Sites, Notion or Confluence. Try building a class wiki or blog on a school, national or regional topic.</li>\n<li><b>Discord servers</b>: communities with text and voice channels, roles and moderation bots. Popular with gamers, animators, musicians and coders, and many creative software communities run their own servers.</li>\n<li><b>Collaborative platforms</b>: GitHub (code), Behance and ArtStation (portfolios with feedback), YouTube and TikTok comment communities, open-source projects such as Blender itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean creator communities</b>: animation, film and game developers across the region connect online and at events. Examples include the <b>Animae Caribe</b> animation and digital media festival in Trinidad and Tobago, the <b>Caribbean Tales</b> film network, and the <b>Kingston Creative</b> arts movement in Jamaica, alongside many Instagram and Discord groups for Caribbean illustrators, soca and dancehall producers and indie game developers. Check each one's current activity before you cite it.</p>\n<p><b>Why join?</b> Feedback on work (a \"critique\" channel), learning from experts, finding collaborators and clients, showcasing Caribbean culture to a global audience, and staying up to date with trends and software.</p>\n<p><b>Community features to know</b>: moderators, community rules (codes of conduct), roles and permissions, threads, pinned posts, reputation or upvotes, and reporting tools.</p>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media posts his walk-cycle tests in an animation Discord's critique channel and gets notes from animators in Canada, Nigeria and Barbados within an hour.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A <b>collaboration tool</b> is software; an <b>electronic community</b> is the people and their shared space. Discord is a tool, but a Discord server for Caribbean animators is a community.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An electronic community is a group of people who connect online around a shared interest and build relationships over time. Examples include Facebook and WhatsApp groups, forums, wikis, Discord servers and portfolio sites such as Behance. Members share advice, feedback and ideas. A young photographer in St. Kitts can get tips from members in Curaçao and Barbados.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A community is defined by its <b>people and relationships</b>, not its software, which is why one tool can host very different communities. What makes a community valuable is its culture and structure: clear rules, active moderators, roles and searchable history. Forums and wikis build lasting knowledge that newcomers can search years later, while chat-based spaces such as Discord or WhatsApp are lively but lose information in endless scrolling. Communities also carry responsibilities: check their current activity before citing them, follow their codes of conduct and protect personal information. For Caribbean creators, communities offer what small local markets cannot: feedback, collaborators and clients from across the region and the diaspora.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which electronic community?","intro":"<p>Students at Breadfruit Valley Secondary in St. Vincent want to get help and share their work online. Choose the best type of community for each need.</p>","items":[{"text":"A searchable answer to a Blender error message that someone solved three years ago.","options":["a Padlet wall","a live video call","a forum"],"answer":2,"fb":"Forums keep threads organised and searchable for years."},{"text":"A class guide to the history of Kingstown that every student can edit, with a record of each change.","options":["a WhatsApp group","a wiki","a Trello board"],"answer":1,"fb":"Wikis are built for collaborative editing with page history."},{"text":"Live voice chat plus a #critique channel where animators post work for feedback.","options":["an email newsletter","a printed magazine","a Discord server"],"answer":2,"fb":"Discord combines text channels, voice rooms and roles for creative communities."},{"text":"Local entrepreneurs sharing news, offers and advice in a Vincentian business group.","options":["a wiki","an online group (e.g. Facebook or WhatsApp)","a Kanban board"],"answer":1,"fb":"Social online groups suit news and discussion among local members."},{"text":"A portfolio site where illustrators get comments from professionals worldwide.","options":["Microsoft Planner","Behance or ArtStation","Google Sheets"],"answer":1,"fb":"Portfolio communities are built for showcasing work and receiving feedback."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each need to the community format that serves it best: forums for searchable help, wikis for co-written knowledge, Discord for live creative communities.","tutor":[{"h":"Permanent or live?","p":"<p>Forums and wikis keep knowledge for later. Discord and groups are more conversational.</p>"},{"h":"Who edits?","p":"<p>If many people edit the same page, think wiki.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which electronic community? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A page many people can edit, with history.","options":["wiki","forum"],"answer":0,"fb":"Shared editing: wiki."},{"text":"Searchable question-and-answer threads.","options":["video call","forum"],"answer":1,"fb":"Threads that last: forum."},{"text":"Text and voice channels for a creative community.","options":["Discord server","printed newsletter"],"answer":0,"fb":"Discord has text and voice channels."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which electronic community? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: four options, and some questions ask what makes a community work.</p>","items":[{"text":"A student wants a solution to a Blender error that future classes can still find. Which is BEST?","options":["a Padlet wall","a forum thread with a clear title","a WhatsApp voice note","a live Discord voice chat"],"answer":1,"fb":"Forum threads are organised and searchable for years."},{"text":"What makes a Caribbean animators' Discord server a COMMUNITY rather than just a tool?","options":["its moderation bots","that it is free","the people, their shared interest and the relationships they build","its text and voice channels"],"answer":2,"fb":"Discord is the tool; the community is the people and their shared space."},{"text":"A class is co-writing a guide to Kingstown history and wants any vandalism to be reversible. Which feature matters MOST?","options":["emoji reactions","page history in a wiki","voice channels","a like button"],"answer":1,"fb":"Page history lets editors restore earlier versions."},{"text":"An illustrator in St. Vincent wants professional feedback AND possible clients. Which community fits BEST?","options":["a private family WhatsApp group","Microsoft Planner","a shared spreadsheet","Behance or ArtStation"],"answer":3,"fb":"Portfolio communities are built for showcasing work to professionals and clients."},{"text":"A forum has helpful members, but newcomers' questions get buried under arguments. What would help MOST?","options":["making every post anonymous","removing all rules","turning off search","moderators with clear rules, pinned posts and organised threads"],"answer":3,"fb":"Moderation and structure keep a community useful for everyone."},{"text":"Before you cite a Caribbean creator community in an exam answer, what should you check?","options":["that it uses a dark theme","nothing at all","that it runs on Discord","that it is still active"],"answer":3,"fb":"Communities change and close; cite ones that are still active."}],"win":"Excellent. You can choose communities by purpose and explain what makes them work: people, structure and moderation."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Advantages of digital collaboration","html":"<p>You must be able to discuss the <b>advantages and disadvantages</b> of interactive digital media for collaboration. Start with the advantages.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Real-time feedback</b>: comments, suggestions and live co-editing mean problems are spotted and fixed quickly. Zara can leave a comment on Malik's video at the exact second of a mistake instead of waiting for a weekly meeting.</li>\n<li><b>Global reach</b>: teams and communities can include people anywhere. A Barbados agency can work for a client in Curaçao, hire a sound designer in Jamaica and share a campaign with the diaspora in the Netherlands and New York. This is vital for small island states with small local markets.</li>\n<li><b>Version control and version history</b>: tools keep a record of every change, who made it and when, so you can restore an earlier version. Google Docs' version history, Figma's version history and Git on GitHub all do this. It protects against lost work and settles arguments about who did what.</li>\n<li><b>Access anywhere, any time</b>: cloud files are available on laptops, tablets and phones. Work continues if a student is home sick or the team is spread between Port of Spain and Scarborough.</li>\n<li><b>Reduced cost and travel</b>: fewer printed copies, no need to fly between islands for every meeting; many tools have free education tiers.</li>\n<li><b>Transparency and accountability</b>: task boards and edit histories show each person's contribution, which helps fair grading and teamwork.</li>\n<li><b>Diverse ideas and skills</b>: working with people from different backgrounds and islands produces richer creative work.</li>\n<li><b>Single source of truth</b>: one shared file, not five copies called final_v2_REAL.docx attached to emails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: after Hurricane Irma in 2017, many Sint Maarten organisations relied on cloud tools, messaging groups and shared documents to coordinate relief and keep working while offices were damaged. (Use this as a general example; do not invent statistics about it.)</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"discuss\" question, do not just list. Make each point a mini-paragraph: <b>point</b>, <b>explanation</b>, <b>example</b>, and where possible a <b>limitation</b>. \"Version history protects work because... For example... However, it only helps if everyone edits the shared file rather than downloading copies.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Digital collaboration has many advantages. People get feedback quickly, teams can include members from anywhere, and version history saves every change so work can be restored. Files can be opened on any device, travel and printing costs fall, and task boards show who did what. A company in Barbados can work with a client in Curaçao without anyone taking a flight.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each advantage comes with a condition, and Grade One answers state it. Version history protects work only if everyone edits the shared file instead of downloading private copies. Transparency helps fairness only if tasks are assigned to named people. Global reach is powerful for small island states with small local markets, but only if members can actually connect. Real-time feedback speeds improvement, yet a constant stream of comments can become a distraction. Strong \"discuss\" answers therefore turn each advantage into a mini-paragraph: the point, why it helps, a specific Caribbean example and a limitation. Avoid figures you cannot source; general, accurate examples, such as cloud tools helping organisations keep working after Hurricane Irma, are safer.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Disadvantages and how to reduce them","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Disadvantage</th><th>What it means</th><th>Ways to reduce it</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Connectivity issues</b></td><td>Slow or unreliable Internet, power cuts and outages (for example during storms) interrupt live calls and uploads</td><td>Use offline modes (Google Docs offline), schedule asynchronous work, record meetings, keep local backups, use low-data options (audio only)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Digital divide</b></td><td>Unequal access to devices, broadband, data and digital skills between people, households, islands and income groups</td><td>Choose free, browser-based, mobile-friendly tools; share school devices and Wi-Fi; train members; check the latest ITU figures on Caribbean Internet access</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Privacy and security</b></td><td>Data stored on third-party servers, accidental public sharing links, hacked accounts, minors' information exposed</td><td>Use \"restricted\" sharing, two-factor authentication, strong passwords, school-approved accounts; do not post personal data in public channels</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Over-reliance on technology</b></td><td>Work stops when a platform fails or changes its pricing; loss of face-to-face skills; screen fatigue</td><td>Keep backups and exports, have a plan B, hold some face-to-face sessions, set \"no-meeting\" focus time</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Miscommunication</b></td><td>Text loses tone and body language; messages are misread as rude</td><td>Clear, polite messages; short video calls for sensitive issues</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Information overload and distraction</b></td><td>Too many channels and notifications</td><td>Agree which tool is for what; mute non-essential channels</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Unequal contribution</b></td><td>\"Free riders\" can hide in a group</td><td>Assign roles; use task boards and version history to make contributions visible</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Time zones and cost</b></td><td>Global teams struggle to meet live; premium plans cost money in US dollars</td><td>Asynchronous workflows; free education tiers</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media once lost an afternoon when a storm knocked out power in Arima during a client review. Since then the team records every review and keeps an offline copy of key files.</p>\n<p>The <b>digital divide</b> is especially important in Caribbean answers. Access, cost of data and device quality vary between and within territories, so a tool that needs a powerful laptop and fast fibre may exclude some group members or users. Use well-established sources (ITU, World Bank, GSMA) for any figures, with the year.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For an \"evaluate\" question, weigh the advantages against the disadvantages and reach a <b>justified judgement</b>: \"Overall, digital tools benefit the group because..., provided that...\" A conclusion with no judgement loses marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Digital collaboration also has problems. Weak Internet or power cuts can stop work. Some people cannot afford devices or data: this is the digital divide. Shared links can expose private information. Teams can rely too much on one platform, misread messages that have no tone, drown in notifications and let some members do less. A storm in Dominica could stop a whole team's uploads.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>For each disadvantage, examiners want a <b>practical way to reduce it</b>, and the best fixes match the cause. Connectivity problems call for offline modes, asynchronous work and recorded meetings; the digital divide calls for free, browser-based, low-data tools and shared school devices; privacy risks call for restricted sharing, two-factor authentication and school accounts. Over-reliance is subtle: a team that keeps all its work on one free platform is exposed if the service fails or changes its pricing, so regular exports matter. In Caribbean answers the digital divide is especially relevant, because access and data costs vary within and between territories. Quote figures only from reliable sources such as the ITU, with the year.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Advantage or disadvantage?","intro":"<p>Dominica Eco-Trails group is building a hiking app with members in Roseau, Trinidad and Montserrat. Sort each statement.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of digital collaboration?","bins":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"The group restores yesterday's version of the proposal after someone deletes a section by mistake.","bin":0,"fb":"Version control protects work from accidental loss."},{"t":"Trello shows exactly who completed each task, so marks can be shared fairly.","bin":0,"fb":"Transparency and accountability are advantages of task-tracking tools."},{"t":"Work stops for two days when the only shared file is on a platform that goes down.","bin":1,"fb":"Over-reliance on one technology with no backup is a disadvantage."},{"t":"Hikers from the diaspora in London and Toronto give feedback on the trail descriptions.","bin":0,"fb":"Global reach brings in a wider audience and more perspectives."},{"t":"One member in a rural village cannot join video calls because her data plan is too expensive.","bin":1,"fb":"Unequal access to affordable data is the digital divide."},{"t":"A blunt one-line chat message is read as rude and causes an argument.","bin":1,"fb":"Text-based communication can lose tone and lead to miscommunication."},{"t":"A designer in Trinidad comments on the app mock-up minutes after it is uploaded.","bin":0,"fb":"This is real-time feedback, which speeds up improvement."},{"t":"A sharing link set to \"Anyone with the link\" exposes members' phone numbers.","bin":1,"fb":"Poor sharing settings create privacy risks."}],"win":"Well done. You can identify the key advantages (feedback, reach, version control, transparency) and disadvantages (connectivity, digital divide, privacy, over-reliance, miscommunication).","tutor":[{"h":"Helps or hinders?","p":"<p>Ask whether the statement helps the team work better or creates a barrier.</p>"},{"h":"Name it","p":"<p>Try to put a syllabus term on each: real-time feedback, global reach, version control, digital divide, privacy, over-reliance.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Quick comments on a shared file.","bin":0,"fb":"Real-time feedback: advantage."},{"t":"A member cannot afford data.","bin":1,"fb":"Digital divide: disadvantage."},{"t":"Restoring an earlier version.","bin":0,"fb":"Version control: advantage."},{"t":"A public link exposes personal details.","bin":1,"fb":"Privacy risk: disadvantage."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin. Some statements describe a WAY TO REDUCE a disadvantage. Sort each statement about Dominica Eco-Trails' hiking app team.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage, a disadvantage or a way to reduce a disadvantage?","bins":["Advantage","Disadvantage","Way to reduce a disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"Sharing is changed to \"Restricted\" and two-factor authentication is switched on.","bin":2,"fb":"These settings reduce privacy and security risks."},{"t":"The team exports a backup of every file each Friday.","bin":2,"fb":"Regular exports reduce over-reliance on one platform."},{"t":"Members agree to audio-only calls to save data.","bin":2,"fb":"Low-data options reduce the digital divide."},{"t":"Meetings are recorded so members who lose power can watch later.","bin":2,"fb":"Recording meetings reduces the effect of connectivity problems."},{"t":"A rural member's data plan is too expensive for video calls.","bin":1,"fb":"Unequal access is the digital divide."},{"t":"Trello shows exactly who completed each task.","bin":0,"fb":"Transparency and accountability are advantages."},{"t":"The only copy of the proposal sits on one platform that goes down for two days.","bin":1,"fb":"Over-reliance on one platform is a disadvantage."},{"t":"A sharing link set to \"Anyone with the link\" exposes members' phone numbers.","bin":1,"fb":"Poor sharing settings create a privacy risk."},{"t":"A designer in Trinidad comments on the mock-up minutes after it is uploaded.","bin":0,"fb":"Real-time feedback is an advantage."},{"t":"Hikers in London and Toronto test the trail descriptions.","bin":0,"fb":"Global reach is an advantage."}],"win":"Excellent. You can pair disadvantages with practical ways to reduce them, which is exactly what a \"discuss\" answer needs."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Advantages and disadvantages of electronic communities","html":"<p>The previous screens weighed digital <b>collaboration</b>. You also need to state the advantages and disadvantages of <b>electronic communities</b>, and to use examples to explain both benefits and risks.</p>\n<p><b>Advantages: more people can take part</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Leverage for ordinary people</b>: a small campaign can reach supporters beyond its neighbourhood, such as a Tobago beach clean-up group gaining followers in the diaspora.</li>\n<li><b>A forum for topics of interest</b>: book lovers can discuss the same story.</li>\n<li><b>Convenient participation</b>: reply after school when you have time.</li>\n<li><b>Different people and places</b>: exchange ideas with members in other countries.</li>\n<li><b>Interaction can lead to physical meetings</b>: a local club can arrange a safe, supervised meetup.</li>\n<li><b>A sense of anonymity</b>: a nickname may make asking a question feel easier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Advantages: members help shape the conversation</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Some arenas are moderated</b>: a moderator can guide discussion and remove abuse, such as enforcing a club's rules.</li>\n<li><b>Not a broadcast medium</b>: members can reply and contribute, rather than only receive a message (unlike a radio advert).</li>\n<li><b>Media is not \"fixed\"</b>: a discussion can develop as members add updates or correct an explanation.</li>\n<li><b>No built-in opinion restraints</b>: people can offer different views, such as disagreeing respectfully about a film.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Disadvantages: open participation also creates risks</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Discrimination is different, but not absent</b>: people may still face exclusion or prejudiced comments.</li>\n<li><b>No built-in opinion restraints</b>: unrestricted opinions can become insults or harmful claims.</li>\n<li><b>A few can dominate discussion</b>: frequent posters may crowd out quieter members.</li>\n<li><b>Takes time</b>: following replies can interrupt homework.</li>\n<li><b>Losing touch with reality</b>: online activity may displace offline relationships and responsibilities.</li>\n<li><b>A sense of anonymity</b>: hidden identities can encourage deception or hurtful behaviour.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice that two points appear on <b>both</b> sides. <b>Anonymity</b> helps a shy student ask a question, but it also lets someone hide while being cruel. <b>No opinion restraints</b> allows honest debate, but it also allows insults. Examiners reward students who spot this.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use the double-edged points to \"discuss\": \"A sense of anonymity can be an advantage because..., for example... However, the same anonymity can encourage deception because...\" That is balance in one paragraph.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Electronic communities let more people take part: they can join from anywhere, reply when convenient and use a nickname to ask shy questions. Moderators can guide discussion. But there are risks: prejudice, insults, a few loud members taking over, wasted time and people hiding behind fake names to be cruel. A gaming group in Grenada needs good moderators to stay friendly.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key exam skill here is recognising <b>double-edged</b> features. Anonymity helps a shy student ask a basic question but also shields a bully; the absence of opinion restraints allows honest debate but also insults and false claims. Whether a feature helps or harms often depends on moderation and community rules, which is why well-run communities publish codes of conduct and give moderators tools to remove abuse. Domination by a few frequent posters is a quieter problem: newcomers stop contributing, so the community loses fresh ideas. A Grade One answer explains the mechanism, gives an example and suggests a safeguard, such as rotating discussion leaders or a welcome thread for new members.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk?","intro":"<p>Island Shutterbugs is an online photography community for young people across the Caribbean. Sort each situation into the correct list.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?","bins":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"A fake account uses a stolen name to send hurtful messages to a member.","bin":1,"fb":"Anonymity can also encourage deception and hurtful behaviour."},{"t":"A student checks replies all evening and does not finish his homework.","bin":1,"fb":"Electronic communities take time, and following replies can interrupt homework."},{"t":"A shy member uses a nickname and finally asks how to shoot in manual mode.","bin":0,"fb":"A sense of anonymity can make asking a question feel easier."},{"t":"A member in Grenada gives a Bridgetown student detailed tips on her Broad Street night photo.","bin":0,"fb":"Different people and places: members exchange ideas across countries."},{"t":"A member receives prejudiced comments about where she comes from.","bin":1,"fb":"Discrimination is different online, but it is not absent."},{"t":"A moderator removes an abusive comment and reminds everyone of the group rules.","bin":0,"fb":"Some arenas are moderated, so discussion can be guided and abuse removed."},{"t":"Three frequent posters reply to everything, and newer members stop posting.","bin":1,"fb":"A few can dominate discussion and crowd out quieter members."},{"t":"The group arranges a supervised photo walk at Fort Charlotte in St. Vincent with parents present.","bin":0,"fb":"Interaction can lead to safe physical meetings."}],"win":"Excellent. You can explain the advantages and disadvantages of electronic communities with examples, and you saw that anonymity cuts both ways.","tutor":[{"h":"Who gains, who loses?","p":"<p>Does the situation help members take part, or does it push someone out or harm them?</p>"},{"h":"Same feature, two sides","p":"<p>Anonymity appears twice. Look at how it is being used.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Members in other countries give feedback.","bin":0,"fb":"Different people and places: advantage."},{"t":"A moderator removes abuse.","bin":0,"fb":"Moderated arenas: advantage."},{"t":"A few people dominate every discussion.","bin":1,"fb":"A few can dominate: disadvantage."},{"t":"A hidden identity is used to bully.","bin":1,"fb":"Anonymity misused: disadvantage."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Electronic communities: benefit or risk? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: several of these show the SAME feature (anonymity, no opinion restraints) working both ways. Sort each situation in Island Shutterbugs.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of an electronic community?","bins":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"With no limits on opinions, a thread fills with insults about a member's island.","bin":1,"fb":"The same freedom can turn into insults and prejudice."},{"t":"Hiding behind a fake name, someone posts cruel comments about a member's photo.","bin":1,"fb":"The same anonymity encourages hurtful behaviour: a disadvantage."},{"t":"A thread improves as members correct a wrong explanation about exposure.","bin":0,"fb":"Media is not \"fixed\": discussion develops and corrects itself."},{"t":"Using a nickname, a shy member finally asks a \"silly\" question about ISO.","bin":0,"fb":"Anonymity can make asking easier: an advantage."},{"t":"A member spends so long online that she drops out of her local photo club.","bin":1,"fb":"Losing touch with reality: online activity displaces offline life."},{"t":"Members answer the weekly challenge whenever they have time.","bin":0,"fb":"Convenient participation is an advantage."},{"t":"A few senior members answer every question before newcomers can try.","bin":1,"fb":"A few can dominate discussion and crowd out others."},{"t":"Members debate film versus digital photography freely and respectfully.","bin":0,"fb":"No opinion restraints allows honest debate: an advantage."},{"t":"A member's coral-bleaching photo project gains supporters in the diaspora.","bin":0,"fb":"Leverage for ordinary people: reach beyond the neighbourhood."}],"win":"Excellent. You spotted the double-edged features, which is what turns a list into a balanced discussion."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Self-directed vs collaborative activities, roles and responsibilities","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Self-directed digital activities</th><th>Collaborative digital activities</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who</td><td>An individual learner sets goals, chooses resources and manages time</td><td>A group shares goals, divides work and builds one outcome together</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Following a Blender tutorial on YouTube, a free online course, practising in Duolingo, keeping a personal learning blog, researching on library databases</td><td>Co-writing the SBA proposal, a group Miro brainstorm, a class wiki, a shared Figma prototype, a hackathon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strengths</td><td>Own pace, personal interests, builds independence and self-discipline</td><td>Shared skills, peer feedback, bigger projects, real-world teamwork practice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Challenges</td><td>Needs motivation; easy to get distracted; no one to check your work</td><td>Coordination, conflict, unequal effort, scheduling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Helpful tools</td><td>Calendars, to-do apps, Google Keep, learning platforms, bookmarks</td><td>Google Workspace, Teams, Trello, Miro, Padlet, Figma</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Most real projects mix the two. Each member learns a skill on their own (self-directed), then brings it back to the group (collaborative).</p>\n<p><b>Managing roles and responsibilities</b>. The Unit 2 SBA is done in <b>groups of 2 to 3</b>, so clear roles matter. Common roles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Project manager / team lead</b>: schedule, Trello board, meeting notes, deadlines.</li>\n<li><b>Designer</b>: visual identity, wireframes in Figma, storyboards.</li>\n<li><b>Developer / producer</b>: building the website, app or animation component.</li>\n<li><b>Researcher / writer</b>: survey design, data analysis, report writing, proofreading (the report is limited to 1,500 words).</li>\n<li><b>Media lead</b>: the 30-second audio-visual short ad.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a small group each person may hold two roles. Good practice: write a short <b>team charter</b> (goals, roles, how decisions are made, how you will communicate, what happens if someone misses a deadline), use a <b>RACI</b> idea (who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed) for each task, and review progress weekly.</p>\n<p>At Island Pulse Media, Zara is accountable for every project, but each task card on Trello has one named person responsible, so nothing is \"everyone's job\" and therefore no one's.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Every SBA member is assessed on the whole project. Make sure each person's role and contribution is visible in your process documents.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Self-directed activities are things you do alone, at your own pace, like following a Blender tutorial. Collaborative activities are done in a group towards one outcome, like co-writing a proposal. Most projects mix both. Give each group member a clear role, such as project manager, designer or media lead, and agree a team charter. A film club in Guyana works best when every task has one named owner.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Roles work because they remove ambiguity: if a task belongs to \"everyone\", it often belongs to no one. The RACI idea separates the person who does a task (Responsible) from the person who answers for it (Accountable), plus those Consulted and those Informed, which prevents both gaps and duplication. A team charter agreed at the start makes conflict easier to handle, because the group has already decided how decisions are made and what happens when a deadline is missed. Self-directed learning feeds collaboration: a member who teaches herself Figma at home brings a new skill back to the team. The risk in small groups is role overload, so review workloads weekly rather than at the end.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Set up a collaborative SBA workspace","intro":"<p>Three Calabash Point Secondary students in Grenada are starting their DME2 SBA: a website and short ad for a Gouyave fish-fry stall. Put their set-up steps in a sensible order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Meet and agree the project goal, team roles and a short team charter."},{"t":"Choose the tools: one for chat, one for files, one for tasks, and agree what each is for."},{"t":"Create a shared Google Drive folder with clear sub-folders and naming rules, shared only with the group and teacher."},{"t":"Build a Trello board with tasks, owners and due dates from the production schedule."},{"t":"Brainstorm ideas on a Miro board and record the decisions."},{"t":"Work on tasks, leaving comments and using version history as drafts develop."},{"t":"Hold weekly check-ins, export screenshots and minutes as evidence of collaboration."}],"win":"Great. You set up goals and roles first, then tools, structure and tasks, then worked and recorded evidence: a professional collaborative workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"People before tools","p":"<p>Agree goals and roles before picking software.</p>"},{"h":"Evidence comes last but often","p":"<p>Recording evidence happens throughout, but it builds up after the work starts.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Set up a workspace (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the goal and roles."},{"t":"Choose tools and set up a shared folder."},{"t":"Create a task board with owners and dates."},{"t":"Work, review weekly and save evidence."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Set up a collaborative SBA workspace (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: nine steps, including backups and an evidence routine. Put the group's full set-up and working cycle in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Meet and agree the project goal, team roles and a short team charter."},{"t":"Agree the tools: one for chat, one for files and one for tasks, and what each is for."},{"t":"Create a shared Drive folder with sub-folders, naming rules and restricted sharing."},{"t":"Turn the production schedule into a Trello board with owners and due dates."},{"t":"Brainstorm on a Miro board and record the decisions in meeting minutes."},{"t":"Work on tasks, using comments and Suggesting mode on shared drafts."},{"t":"Hold a weekly check-in and update the task board."},{"t":"After each check-in, save dated screenshots of the board and version history."},{"t":"Export a full backup of the shared folder before submission."}],"win":"Correct. Agree first, then set up tools, structure and tasks, then work in a weekly cycle that records evidence and ends with a backup."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Netiquette and professional conduct","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Netiquette</b> (network etiquette) is the set of conventions for polite, respectful and responsible behaviour online. <b>Professional conduct</b> applies workplace standards of reliability, respect and integrity to digital spaces.</p>\n<p><b>Netiquette in collaborative spaces</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Be respectful</b>: critique the work, not the person. \"The logo text is hard to read on mobile\" rather than \"This is bad.\"</li>\n<li><b>Write clearly</b>: proper sentences, no ALL CAPS (it reads as shouting), short messages with a clear subject or thread.</li>\n<li><b>Use the right channel</b>: project talk in the project channel; urgent issues by call; nothing personal in work channels.</li>\n<li><b>Respond in reasonable time</b> and acknowledge messages (\"Got it, will send by Thursday\").</li>\n<li><b>Respect time</b>: avoid messaging group members late at night; mute notifications is fine, but agree working hours.</li>\n<li><b>Video calls</b>: join on time, mute when not speaking, appropriate background and dress, camera on when agreed.</li>\n<li><b>Do not edit or delete others' work</b> without asking; use <b>Suggesting</b> mode or comments.</li>\n<li><b>Respect privacy</b>: do not screenshot and share private conversations; ask before posting someone's photo.</li>\n<li><b>Credit sources and collaborators</b>; respect copyright and licences (you met these rights terms in Unit 1).</li>\n<li><b>No cyberbullying, spam or flaming</b>; report problems to a moderator or teacher.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Professional conduct</b> also includes meeting deadlines, admitting mistakes early, keeping client information confidential, using professional usernames and email addresses (not partyking99@...), and keeping files organised so others can find them.</p>\n<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media manages a client's Instagram. When a follower leaves an angry comment about a late delivery, she replies politely, moves the conversation to direct messages and logs it in the team's Slack #client-feedback channel. She never argues in public.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 likes netiquette scenarios. The correct answer is usually the one that is polite, private for sensitive issues, honest and constructive.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Netiquette means being polite and responsible online. Criticise the work, not the person. Write clearly and avoid ALL CAPS, which reads as shouting. Use the right channel, reply in good time and do not change other people's work without asking. Respect privacy and credit sources. A Jamaican company facing an angry customer online should reply politely and move to a private message.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Netiquette is really about <b>trust</b>. Teams that cannot see each other rely on written messages, which lose tone, so a blunt sentence can damage a working relationship in a way it would not face to face. Constructive feedback follows a pattern: what works, what to improve and a specific suggestion. Professional conduct extends to how you handle mistakes: admitting a missed deadline early, with a new date, protects the team far more than silence. Public and private spaces need different behaviour; complaints and personal issues belong in private messages, while decisions belong where the whole team can see them. In scenario questions, the best answer is usually polite, private for sensitive issues, honest and solution-focused.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the good netiquette","intro":"<p>Here are messages from a group chat for a DME2 SBA team building an app for the Belize Reef Watch project.</p>","q":"Select ALL the messages that show good netiquette and professional conduct.","lines":["\"Thanks for the wireframes, Ana. The menu is clear. Could we make the buttons bigger for older users?\"","\"WHO DELETED MY SECTION?????\"","\"I added my ideas as comments in Suggesting mode so you can accept or reject them.\"","\"Sorry, I will miss Thursday's deadline for the survey. I can finish it by Saturday morning, is that OK?\"","\"lol ur logo is trash, just let me do it\"","\"Here is a screenshot of what Jordan said about the teacher in our private chat.\"","\"Meeting notes and decisions from today are in the Drive folder: Minutes_Week3.\"","\"I changed the whole report to my version so it sounds better.\""],"correct":[0,2,3,6],"win":"Correct. Good netiquette is respectful, constructive, honest about problems and transparent about decisions.","miss":"Look again: shouting in capitals, insults, sharing private messages and overwriting others' work all break netiquette.","tutor":[{"h":"Person or work?","p":"<p>Good feedback is about the work and suggests an improvement.</p>"},{"h":"Honest and early","p":"<p>Admitting a delay early and offering a new date is professional.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the good netiquette (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO polite, professional messages.","lines":["\"Thanks for this. Could we make the text bigger?\"","\"THIS IS ALL WRONG!!!\"","\"I left my suggestions as comments for you to review.\"","\"I rewrote your part without asking.\""],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Polite, constructive and respectful of others' work.","miss":"Capitals read as shouting, and changing others' work without asking is disrespectful."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the good netiquette (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: these messages and actions from another team are less obvious. Some look efficient but break trust or privacy.</p>","q":"Select ALL the messages and actions that show good netiquette and professional conduct.","lines":["\"I think the intro drags a little. Could we cut the first shot to 2 seconds? Happy to try it if you agree.\"","\"As agreed on our call: Ana owns the logo and I'll finish the storyboard by Friday. Shout if that's wrong.\"","Posting a teammate's unfinished draft on Instagram to \"get feedback\", without asking her.","Messaging the whole group at 1 a.m. with \"URGENT\" about a deadline three weeks away.","\"My mistake: I overwrote the budget sheet. I've restored yesterday's version from the history.\"","Moving a heated disagreement about the colour scheme out of the group chat and into a short call.","Using the project chat to share jokes about another class.","Renaming shared files \"CHANGE THIS NOW\" so others notice them.","Crediting the royalty-free track and the photographer in the shared credits document.","Downloading the shared document, rewriting it offline and uploading it as \"final\" over everyone's version."],"correct":[0,1,4,5,8],"win":"Correct. Constructive suggestions, written confirmation of decisions, owning a mistake, moving conflict to a call and crediting sources all build trust.","miss":"Look again: sharing someone's draft without consent, late-night false urgency, off-topic gossip, shouting file names and overwriting the team's work all break netiquette."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Evidence of collaboration for the SBA","html":"<p>Collaboration is not only good practice; it earns marks. In the Unit 2 SBA mark scheme, <b>Progress and Process</b> (12 marks) includes <b>Use of Collaboration Tools</b>: students communicate using collaboration tools (examples given include Skype, Google Docs, Dropbox, wikis, blogs and Google Hangouts) <b>and use digital tools to visually share ideas</b>. Unit 2 students must also keep a <b>portfolio of the roadmap</b> and upload digital outputs periodically to a school-managed facility such as Google Drive or Dropbox, so your collaboration trail is part of the evidence.</p>\n<p><b>What counts as good evidence</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Screenshots of the <b>Trello board</b> at different dates, showing tasks moving and owners named.</li>\n<li><b>Version history</b> screenshots from Google Docs or Figma showing each member's edits over time.</li>\n<li><b>Comment threads</b> on drafts showing feedback given and acted on.</li>\n<li>A <b>Miro or Padlet brainstorm</b> exported as an image or PDF (this also supports your brainstorming chart).</li>\n<li><b>Meeting minutes</b>: date, attendees, decisions, actions, owners, deadlines.</li>\n<li>Chat excerpts (Teams, Slack, WhatsApp) showing planning, with personal details removed.</li>\n<li>A <b>shared folder structure</b> screenshot with consistent file naming.</li>\n<li>A short <b>reflection</b> from each member on their role and what they learned.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Presenting it</b>: put a labelled selection in your appendices with captions (\"Figure 4: Trello board, 12 March, showing the storyboard task completed by Daniel\"). Choose quality over quantity; screenshots do not count towards the 1,500-word limit, but captions should be short. Remove phone numbers, email addresses and anything private.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Weak evidence</th><th>Strong evidence</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\"We used WhatsApp to talk.\"</td><td>A dated chat excerpt showing the group agreeing the target audience after reviewing survey results</td></tr>\n<tr><td>One screenshot of an empty Trello board</td><td>Three dated screenshots showing tasks moving to Done, each with an owner</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A final document only</td><td>Version history plus comments showing how the draft improved</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Start collecting evidence in week one. It is almost impossible to recreate an honest collaboration trail the night before submission, and moderators can tell.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Good teams keep evidence of how they worked together. Useful evidence includes dated screenshots of task boards, version history showing each person's edits, comment threads on drafts, brainstorm boards and meeting minutes. Label each item with a short caption and remove private details such as phone numbers. A film group in St. Lucia should start collecting evidence from week one.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Evidence is strong when it is <b>dated, specific and shows change over time</b>. One screenshot of a task board proves the board existed; three dated screenshots showing tasks moving to \"Done\" with named owners prove the team used it. Version history and comment threads are especially convincing, because they show feedback being given and acted on, which is the heart of collaboration. Honesty matters: a trail assembled the night before submission is easy to spot, and it misrepresents how the work was done. Choose quality over quantity, write short captions that say what each item shows and protect privacy by removing contact details. Professional agencies use the same habits to show clients how a project progressed.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Rescue the group project","intro":"<p>You are the project manager for a 3-person DME2 SBA group at Sea-Almond Secondary School in Trinidad. Your product is a website and 30-second ad for the Grande Riviere Turtle Watch volunteers. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Week 2: files are scattered across three phones and email attachments called \"final\", \"final2\" and \"finalREAL\". What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask everyone to keep emailing attachments but add the date to the name.","to":"bad1","fb":"Attachments still create many copies and no shared version history."},{"t":"Set up one shared Google Drive folder with sub-folders and a naming rule, shared only with the group and teacher.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. One source of truth with version history prevents lost work."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Week 4: one member, Devon, has not completed his storyboard task and is not replying in the group chat. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Send Devon a polite private message asking if he is okay, and offer to agree a new deadline or adjust tasks; inform the teacher if it continues.","to":"n3","fb":"Professional. It is respectful, private and solution-focused."},{"t":"Post in the group chat: \"DEVON IS LAZY. Do your work!!!\"","to":"bad2","fb":"Public shouting and insults break netiquette and damage teamwork."},{"t":"Quietly do his task yourself and say nothing.","to":"bad3","fb":"This hides the problem, overloads you and leaves no honest record of who did what."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Devon explains his home Internet was cut off after a power problem. He can use the school library Wi-Fi only at lunch. How do you adapt?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Insist that all work happens in live evening video calls only.","to":"bad4","fb":"This excludes a member with limited access, which is the digital divide in action."},{"t":"Switch his tasks to asynchronous work: he downloads files for offline editing at lunch, uploads them the next day, and you record meetings for him.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. You reduced the effect of connectivity issues and the digital divide."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Two weeks before submission, how do you show evidence of collaboration?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Write one sentence: \"We all worked together well.\"","to":"bad5","fb":"A claim with no evidence earns little credit."},{"t":"Include dated Trello screenshots, version history, comment threads and meeting minutes, with captions and private details removed.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. This is strong, honest evidence of collaboration."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Project delivered.</b> The site and ad are finished on time and the appendix clearly shows each member's role. A Grade One answer would explain why: a single shared workspace, respectful conduct, adapting to the digital divide and documented evidence of collaboration.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost work.</b> Two members edit different copies and a whole section is lost. Try again with one shared workspace.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Team breakdown.</b> Devon leaves the chat and the group stops talking. Try again with respectful, private communication.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Burnout and confusion.</b> You fall behind on your own tasks and there is no record of Devon's situation. Try again and address the problem openly.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded member.</b> Devon cannot attend and his contribution disappears. Try again and use asynchronous tools.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>No evidence.</b> The moderator cannot see any collaboration. Try again and include dated, captioned evidence.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"One shared space","p":"<p>Many copies cause chaos; one shared folder gives version history.</p>"},{"h":"Private and polite","p":"<p>Handle personal problems privately and respectfully.</p>"},{"h":"Show, do not tell","p":"<p>Moderators reward visible evidence, not claims.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Rescue the group project (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a group member is missing deadlines.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A team member has not finished his task. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Send a polite private message and agree a new plan.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Respectful and solution-focused."},{"t":"Insult him in the group chat.","to":"bad","fb":"That breaks netiquette."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> He explains the problem and the team adapts.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The team falls apart. Try again with respect.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Rescue the group project (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher rescue for the Turtle Watch website and ad. More problems, harder trade-offs, and some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Week 2: files are scattered across phones and email attachments, and the group chat has 400 unread messages mixing jokes with decisions. What do you set up?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask everyone to email files with dates in the names.","to":"bad1","fb":"Dated attachments still create many copies and no shared history."},{"t":"A shared folder only; everything else stays in the chat.","to":"ok1","fb":"Files are safer, but decisions and tasks are still buried in chat."},{"t":"One shared Drive folder with naming rules and restricted sharing, a Trello board for tasks, and a rule that decisions go in meeting minutes, not the chat.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Each job now has one home, so nothing important gets lost."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Week 4: Devon has not finished his storyboard and is not replying. Another member wants to remove him from the group. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post in the group chat: \"Devon, storyboard due Friday please.\"","to":"ok2","fb":"Polite, but public, and it does not find out why he has gone quiet."},{"t":"Agree to remove him and share out his work without telling the teacher.","to":"bad2","fb":"This hides the problem and leaves no honest record of what happened."},{"t":"Message Devon privately, ask if he is okay and offer to adjust his tasks or deadline; tell the teacher if there is no reply in two days.","to":"n3","fb":"Professional. Respectful, private, solution-focused and with a clear escalation."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Devon explains that his home Internet was cut off. He can only use the school Wi-Fi at lunch, and the group usually meets by evening video call. How do you adapt?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Move his work to asynchronous: offline editing, uploads at lunch, recorded meetings and decisions summarised in the minutes.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. You reduced the effect of the digital divide without lowering expectations."},{"t":"Keep the evening calls; he can catch up at the weekend.","to":"bad3","fb":"This excludes him and his contribution will disappear."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The task board shows that Shanice has quietly been doing far more than her share. How do you handle the workload?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Leave it; the project is nearly done.","to":"ok3","fb":"The work gets finished, but the record of contributions is unfair and Shanice is overloaded."},{"t":"Review the board together, rebalance the remaining tasks and record each person's contribution honestly.","to":"n5","fb":"Fair and transparent. The board is doing its job."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Two weeks before submission, how do you show how the group collaborated?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Dated Trello screenshots, version history, comment threads and minutes, each captioned, with personal details removed, plus a short reflection from each member.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Honest, specific evidence of how the team worked."},{"t":"Paste every chat message, unedited, including phone numbers.","to":"bad4","fb":"Quantity is not quality, and it exposes private information."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One project management.</b> The site and ad are finished on time, every member contributed fairly, Devon stayed included and the evidence shows exactly how the team worked. You matched each problem with the right tool, the right tone and an honest record.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Half organised.</b> Files are safe, but two members act on different decisions buried in the chat. A partial success: give decisions and tasks their own home.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Still silent.</b> Devon feels singled out and stays quiet, so the problem continues. A partial success: talk privately and find out what is wrong.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Unfair record.</b> The project is done, but one member carried it and the record does not show that. A partial success: rebalance and record contributions honestly.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost work.</b> Two members edit different copies and a section disappears. Try again with one shared workspace.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Broken trust.</b> The teacher finds out later and the group has no record of what happened. Try again and handle it openly.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded member.</b> Devon cannot attend and his contribution vanishes. Try again with asynchronous work.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy breach.</b> Phone numbers appear in the submitted work and the useful evidence is buried. Try again with selected, captioned evidence.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Evaluating a collaboration tool","q":"Antigua Youth Film Collective has members on three islands, several of whom use only mid-range phones with prepaid data. They need to share storyboards and feedback over a month. Which choice is BEST justified?","options":[{"t":"A desktop-only professional editing suite, because it has the most features.","fb":"More features do not help if members cannot run the software on their phones."},{"t":"A Padlet board, because it is browser-based, works on phones, lets members post images and comments at any time, and needs little data compared with live video calls.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It matches the task (visual feedback), the devices, the data constraint and the asynchronous schedule across islands."},{"t":"Emailing large image attachments to everyone, because email is universal.","fb":"Attachments create many copies, use data and give no single place for organised feedback."},{"t":"Daily two-hour live video calls, because face-to-face discussion is always best.","fb":"Long live calls use a lot of data and exclude members with limited access: the digital divide."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Read the constraints","p":"<p>Phones, prepaid data, three islands and a month: which option fits all four?</p>"},{"h":"Justify","p":"<p>The best answer links features to the users' real situation.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Evaluating a collaboration tool (Foundation)","q":"Members use phones and have limited data. They need to share images and comments at different times. Which is best?","options":[{"t":"Daily long video calls","fb":"This uses a lot of data and needs everyone at once."},{"t":"Desktop-only software","fb":"Members cannot run it on phones."},{"t":"A phone-friendly shared board such as Padlet","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Low data, works on phones, asynchronous."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Evaluating a collaboration tool (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Antigua Youth Film Collective has members on three islands, many with mid-range phones and prepaid data. Over a month they must (1) give feedback on storyboards, (2) agree the final cut in one decision meeting and (3) track who is doing what. Which combination is BEST justified?","options":[{"t":"One WhatsApp group for everything: feedback, decisions and task tracking.","fb":"Low data, but images, decisions and tasks get buried in one scrolling chat with no clear ownership."},{"t":"Daily long video calls for everything, because face-to-face discussion is always best.","fb":"Long calls use a lot of data and need everyone free at once, which excludes some members."},{"t":"Padlet for asynchronous storyboard feedback, one short scheduled call (audio first, video if data allows) for the final decision, and a Trello board with owners and due dates, all browser-based and phone-friendly.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Each tool matches one job, the schedule respects different times, and data use stays low."},{"t":"A desktop project-management suite plus a professional editing suite for every member.","fb":"Members on phones cannot run desktop software, and paid suites add cost."}]}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Discuss digital collaboration for a Caribbean team","genre":"Structured response: discussing advantages and disadvantages (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Caribbean Heritage Animation Project brings together young animators from Barbados, Jamaica and Guyana to create a short film about Emancipation. They will never meet in person. The coordinator, Ms. Baptiste, is planning which digital tools to use.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for Ms. Baptiste that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>identifies</b> THREE collaboration tools and <b>explains</b> how each would help the team;</li><li><b>discusses</b> TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of digital collaboration for this team;</li><li><b>recommends</b> THREE netiquette or professional conduct rules for the team, with reasons.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> First, [tool] would ... because ... Second, ... Third, ...<br><b>(b)</b> One advantage is ... For example, ... Another advantage is ... However, a disadvantage is ... because ... This could be reduced by ...<br><b>(c)</b> The team should ... because ... They should also ... Finally, ...</p>","checklist":["I named three real, current collaboration tools and linked each to a job (keep the conversation going, make task progress visible, create the work together).","I explained two advantages with examples linked to animators on different islands.","I explained two disadvantages and suggested how to reduce them.","I used syllabus terms: real-time feedback, global reach, version control, digital divide, privacy.","I gave three netiquette rules with reasons.","I wrote in paragraphs, not just lists."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Tools and their uses","marks":4,"look":["google|drive|docs|workspace","teams|slack|discord|zoom|meet|webex","trello|asana|planner|monday","miro|padlet|figma|figjam","share|comment|edit|task|storyboard","messag|conversation|video meeting","project management|task progress|assign|shared space"],"min":3,"strong":"You chose suitable tools and explained what each does for the team. Next step: justify each choice against the team's situation.","weak":"Name real tools (e.g. Google Drive, Trello, Miro, Microsoft Teams) and explain the job each does: messaging or video meetings to keep the conversation going, project management to make task progress visible, or a shared space to create the work together."},{"criterion":"Discussion of advantages and disadvantages","marks":6,"look":["real.?time|instant|quick feedback|feedback","global|reach|distance|different islands|travel","version|history|backup","connect|internet|outage|power","digital divide|access|afford|data cost|device","privacy|secur|password|over.?relian|depend"],"min":4,"strong":"Your discussion is balanced and uses syllabus terms. Next step: add a short justified judgement at the end.","weak":"Give two advantages (such as real-time feedback, global reach or version control) and two disadvantages (such as connectivity, the digital divide, privacy or over-reliance), each explained with an example."},{"criterion":"Netiquette and professional conduct","marks":3,"look":["respect|polite|courte","deadline|on time|reliab|respond","privacy|permission|credit|copyright","caps|shout|tone|constructive|channel"],"min":2,"strong":"Your rules are practical and justified.","weak":"Give three clear rules, such as respectful constructive feedback, meeting deadlines and protecting privacy, and explain why each matters."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The team needs tools for three jobs: keeping the conversation going, making task progress visible and creating the work together. First, to create the work together, the team should use <b>Google Drive</b> (Google Workspace) as a shared folder for scripts, storyboards and animation files, because everyone can access the latest version from any island and version history protects against lost work. Second, to make task progress visible, a <b>Trello</b> board would track the production schedule, with a card for each scene showing who is responsible and the due date, so Ms. Baptiste can see progress at a glance. Third, to keep the conversation going, <b>Microsoft Teams</b> (or a Discord server) would provide channels for each department and short video meetings for key decisions, and a <b>Miro</b> board could be added for early storyboarding.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One advantage is <b>real-time feedback</b>. A director in Kingston can comment on a Barbadian animator's walk cycle within minutes, so mistakes are corrected before more work is built on them. Another advantage is <b>global reach</b>: the project can include talent from three countries without the cost of flights, which also brings different cultural perspectives on Emancipation to the film. However, a disadvantage is <b>connectivity</b>. A power cut or slow Internet in rural Guyana could stop a member uploading large files; this could be reduced by working asynchronously and uploading overnight. A second disadvantage is the <b>digital divide</b>: some members may lack powerful computers or affordable data, so the team should choose free, browser-based tools and keep file sizes small. Overall, digital collaboration makes this project possible, provided these barriers are planned for.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The team should give <b>respectful, constructive feedback</b> that focuses on the work, not the person, because text can easily sound harsh and damage trust. They should <b>respond and meet deadlines</b>, or warn the group early if they cannot, because one late scene delays the whole film. Finally, they should <b>protect privacy and credit work</b>: never share private messages or personal details, and credit every animator and music source, because the team is handling young people's information and creative work.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Discuss\" needs both sides and a conclusion. Use one paragraph per point: point, explanation, Caribbean example, and a way to reduce the problem for each disadvantage. Name real tools and use the exact syllabus terms: real-time feedback, global reach, version control, connectivity, digital divide, privacy, over-reliance.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Discuss digital collaboration for a Caribbean team (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: digital collaboration, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> ONE tool the animators could use to talk to each other and ONE tool to share files. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE advantage of digital collaboration for this team, with an example. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE disadvantage and ONE way to reduce it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Is a live video call synchronous or asynchronous? <b>Explain</b> your answer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> TWO netiquette rules the team should follow. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> To talk, they could use ... To share files, they could use ...<br><b>(b)</b> One advantage is ... For example, ...<br><b>(c)</b> One disadvantage is ... This could be reduced by ...<br><b>(d)</b> A live video call is ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> First, the team should ... Second, ...</p>","checklist":["I named a tool for talking and a tool for sharing files.","I gave one advantage with an example.","I gave one disadvantage and a way to reduce it.","I said a live call is synchronous and explained why.","I gave two netiquette rules."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two tools","marks":2,"look":["teams|slack|discord|zoom|meet|whatsapp|webex","drive|dropbox|onedrive|docs|sharepoint"],"min":2,"strong":"A suitable tool for each job.","weak":"Name a tool for talking (such as Microsoft Teams) and one for sharing files (such as Google Drive)."},{"criterion":"Advantage with example","marks":2,"look":["feedback|reach|distance|island|version|travel|cost|any time|anywhere","example|for instance|such as|jamaica|guyana|barbados"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear advantage with a team example.","weak":"For example: real-time feedback, because an animator in Jamaica can comment on work from Barbados straight away."},{"criterion":"Disadvantage and reduction","marks":2,"look":["internet|power|connect|data|divide|privacy|afford|device","reduce|record|offline|backup|restrict|free|password|audio"],"min":2,"strong":"A disadvantage with a practical fix.","weak":"For example: power cuts stop uploads; reduce this by recording meetings and working offline."},{"criterion":"Synchronous explained","marks":2,"look":["synchronous","same time|real time|live|together|at once"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct term and reason.","weak":"A live call is synchronous, because everyone takes part at the same time."},{"criterion":"Netiquette rules","marks":2,"look":["respect|polite|kind|constructive","deadline|on time|caps|privacy|credit|ask|reply"],"min":2,"strong":"Two clear rules.","weak":"For example: give polite, constructive feedback; and do not share other people's private messages."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> To talk, they could use Microsoft Teams. To share files, they could use Google Drive.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One advantage is real-time feedback. For example, an animator in Jamaica can comment on a scene from Barbados as soon as it is uploaded.</p><p><b>(c)</b> One disadvantage is that power cuts in rural Guyana could stop a member uploading work. This could be reduced by recording meetings and letting members work offline, then upload later.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A live video call is synchronous, because everyone is taking part at the same time.</p><p><b>(e)</b> First, the team should give polite, constructive feedback about the work, not the person. Second, they should not share anyone's private messages or details without permission.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the point and 1 for the example, reason or fix. Accept any current, real tools in (a). Encourage students to use the exact terms (synchronous, digital divide, netiquette). When secure, ask them to add a second advantage and a second disadvantage in paragraphs, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Discuss digital collaboration for a Caribbean team (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: digital collaboration, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Caribbean Heritage Animation Project brings together six young animators, aged 15 to 19, to make a short film about Emancipation in 10 weeks. Two live in Barbados (reliable fibre), two in Jamaica (good 4G) and two in rural Guyana (frequent power cuts, prepaid data, mid-range phones). They will never meet in person. The coordinator, Ms. Baptiste, has no budget for paid tools.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a report for Ms. Baptiste that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>designs</b> a toolkit covering all three jobs of collaboration tools (keep the conversation going, make task progress visible, create the work together), <b>justifying</b> each choice against the team's constraints <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE advantages and THREE disadvantages of digital collaboration for this team, giving a way to reduce each disadvantage, and reaches a justified judgement <i>(9 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>plans</b> roles and responsibilities for the six members, using a team charter or the RACI idea <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>writes</b> a four-rule code of conduct, including a rule that protects members under 18, with a reason for each rule <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>explains</b> how the team will keep honest evidence of its collaboration <i>(2 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":450,"max":800,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Conversation: ... because ... Task progress: ... because ... Creating together: ... because ... Constraints: free, phone-friendly, low data ...<br><b>(b)</b> Advantage 1 ... Advantage 2 ... Advantage 3 ... Disadvantage 1 ... reduced by ... Disadvantage 2 ... Disadvantage 3 ... Overall ...<br><b>(c)</b> Roles: ... Responsible ... Accountable ... Charter: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Rule 1 ... because ... Rule 2 ... Rule 3 ... Rule 4 ...<br><b>(e)</b> Evidence: ...</p>","checklist":["My toolkit covers all three jobs, and every tool is free, phone-friendly and justified against the constraints.","I evaluated three advantages and three disadvantages, each disadvantage with a fix, and ended with a judgement.","I gave roles for six members using RACI or a charter.","My four rules each have a reason, and one protects members under 18.","I explained how evidence of collaboration will be kept honestly.","I referred to the islands, power cuts, prepaid data and the 10-week deadline."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Justified toolkit","marks":6,"look":["teams|slack|discord|whatsapp|meet","trello|asana|planner|monday","drive|docs|figma|miro|padlet|workspace","free|no cost|budget","phone|mobile|data|offline|browser","because|so that|which means"],"min":5,"strong":"All three jobs covered, each tool justified against cost, devices, data and power.","weak":"Choose a tool for each job and justify it with the constraints: free, works on phones, low data and usable offline."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of advantages and disadvantages","marks":9,"look":["feedback|real.?time","reach|distance|island|travel","version|history|transparen|accountab","power|connect|internet|outage","digital divide|data|afford|device","privacy|secur|minor|under 18|over.?relian","reduce|record|offline|backup|restrict|two.?factor","overall|on balance|judgement|therefore"],"min":6,"strong":"Three advantages and three disadvantages evaluated in context, each disadvantage reduced, with a clear judgement.","weak":"Write a mini-paragraph for each point with a Caribbean example, give a fix for every disadvantage and end with \"Overall ... provided that ...\"."},{"criterion":"Roles and responsibilities","marks":4,"look":["role|project manager|lead|director|editor|animator|storyboard","responsible|accountable|consulted|informed|raci","charter|decision|missed deadline|communicat","six|6|each member|every member"],"min":3,"strong":"Clear roles for six members with RACI or a charter that prevents gaps.","weak":"Give each of the six members a role, show who is responsible and who is accountable, and include what happens if a deadline is missed."},{"criterion":"Code of conduct","marks":4,"look":["respect|constructive|polite","deadline|reply|on time","privacy|personal|minor|under 18|parent|school account","credit|copyright|permission","because|so that"],"min":4,"strong":"Four practical rules, each justified, including protection for minors.","weak":"Write four rules with a reason each, and include one about protecting the personal information of members under 18."},{"criterion":"Evidence of collaboration","marks":2,"look":["screenshot|version history|minutes|comment","dated|date|caption|honest|remove"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear, honest evidence routine.","weak":"Say what evidence will be kept (dated screenshots, version history, minutes) and how it will be captioned and protected."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Keep the conversation going:</i> a free <b>Microsoft Teams</b> or <b>Discord</b> server with one channel per department (story, design, animation, sound) and short scheduled calls for decisions. Both run on phones, calls can be audio-only to save data, and meetings can be recorded for members who lose power. <i>Make task progress visible:</i> a free <b>Trello</b> board with a card for each scene, an owner and a due date, so Ms. Baptiste can see at a glance whether the 10-week schedule is on track; it works in a phone browser and needs little data. <i>Create the work together:</i> <b>Google Drive</b> and Docs for the script and storyboards, with offline mode for the Guyana members, and a free <b>Miro</b> board for early visual brainstorming. Large animation files are uploaded overnight or at school, not during calls. Every tool is free, works on mid-range phones and has an asynchronous option, which matters more for this team than advanced features.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Real-time feedback:</i> a director in Kingston can comment on a Bridgetown animator's scene within minutes, so problems are fixed before more work is built on them; however, constant comments can distract, so feedback is batched into a daily review. <i>Global reach:</i> the project draws on talent and perspectives from three countries without any travel cost, which enriches a film about a shared regional history. <i>Version history and transparency:</i> every edit is recorded, so lost work can be restored and each member's contribution is visible, provided everyone edits shared files rather than private copies. <i>Connectivity:</i> power cuts in rural Guyana could stop live work; this is reduced by asynchronous tasks, recorded meetings and offline editing. <i>Digital divide:</i> prepaid data and mid-range phones put two members at a disadvantage; this is reduced by low-data tools, audio-first calls, compressed preview files and access to school Wi-Fi. <i>Privacy:</i> members are minors, and a careless public link could expose their details; this is reduced by restricted sharing, two-factor authentication and school or parent-approved accounts. Overall, digital collaboration is the only way this project can happen, and it will succeed provided the team plans around power and data from the first week rather than treating them as surprises.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Ms. Baptiste is <b>Accountable</b> for the whole project. Roles: a project manager (Barbados) runs the Trello board and minutes; a director (Jamaica) leads story and approves scenes; a storyboard and design lead (Guyana) works mainly offline; two animators (Barbados and Jamaica) handle the heavier animation files on fibre and 4G; and a sound and edit lead (Guyana) records voice-overs offline and edits low-resolution proxies. Each Trello card has one <b>Responsible</b> owner, the director is <b>Consulted</b> on creative changes and everyone is <b>Informed</b> through the weekly summary. A short team charter agreed in week one states how decisions are made (majority vote, director breaks ties) and that a member who will miss a deadline must say so 48 hours ahead.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> 1. <b>Give constructive feedback on the work, not the person</b>, because text loses tone and trust is hard to rebuild between people who never meet. 2. <b>Reply within 24 hours and warn the team early about any delay</b>, because one late scene holds up the whole film. 3. <b>Never share personal details, photos or private messages, and use only school or parent-approved accounts</b>, because members are under 18 and their safety comes first. 4. <b>Credit every contributor and use only licensed music and images</b>, because the film will be shown publicly.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> After each weekly check-in, the project manager saves dated screenshots of the Trello board and version history and adds the minutes to a shared folder. Each item gets a one-line caption, personal details are removed, and each member writes a short reflection at the end. Collecting from week one keeps the record honest and complete.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 9 + 4 + 4 + 2). Grade One reports are driven by the constraints in the stimulus: free tools, phones, prepaid data, power cuts and minors. Penalise toolkits with paid or desktop-only software, and \"discuss\" sections without a judgement. In (b), each disadvantage must have a fix that matches its cause. In (d), reward rules that are specific and justified, not generic slogans.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U8","title":"Industry practices for promotional campaigns","objectives":["Apply current digital media industry practices to plan and create a promotional campaign for a digital media product or service.","Compare social networking campaigns on Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn and X, and choose platforms that fit a target audience.","Explain content marketing (blogs, vlogs, podcasts, newsletters), text messaging and WhatsApp, guerrilla, viral, cross-platform, email and influencer marketing, with Caribbean examples.","Use email marketing tools such as Mailchimp, Constant Contact and GetResponse to plan an email campaign.","Plan a campaign step by step: objective, audience, message, channels, content calendar and budget.","Measure campaign effectiveness using analytics, KPIs, reach, impressions, engagement rate, click-through rate and conversions.","Explain and distinguish six channels (viral, social networking, guerrilla, email, influencer and cross-platform marketing), including the eight types of email campaign, six email strategies and the principles of a unified cross-platform strategy."],"videos":[{"title":"Digital marketing campaign planning","q":"how to plan a digital marketing campaign step by step for beginners"},{"title":"Guerrilla and viral marketing explained","q":"guerrilla marketing vs viral marketing explained examples"},{"title":"Mailchimp tutorial for beginners","q":"mailchimp tutorial for beginners create email campaign"},{"title":"Reach, impressions, engagement rate and CTR","q":"reach vs impressions engagement rate CTR explained social media metrics"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Promotional campaigns and social networking","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>promotional campaign</b> is a planned series of coordinated messages, delivered over a set period through chosen channels, to achieve a specific goal such as awareness, sign-ups or sales.</p>\n<p>You must be able to apply these industry practices: <b>social networking, the Internet, blogs, text messaging, guerrilla campaigns, viral marketing, cross-platform marketing, email marketing and influencer marketing</b>. You also need to plan a campaign step by step and <b>measure its effectiveness</b>. When you use digital communication tools (blogs, email, social media, livestreams), justify each choice by audience, purpose and message.</p>\n<p>There are <b>six ways brands promote today</b>: viral marketing, social networking, guerrilla marketing, email marketing, influencer marketing and cross-platform marketing. Your goal is to <b>explain and distinguish</b> between them. Start with social networking, where <b>relationships continue between campaigns</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Social networking</b> sites are Internet-based social media sites that connect friends, family, colleagues, customers and clients. They can serve social purposes, business purposes, or both.</p>\n<p>Marketers use social networking to <b>build relationships</b>: a following gives brands access to new, recent and old customers. The notes give three steps: <b>1. Be recognised</b> (a consistent presence increases brand recognition and encourages loyalty); <b>2. Be accessible</b> (customers react to posts, images, videos and comments); <b>3. Support conversion</b> (interaction brings followers to the website, where they can become customers). In short: <i>the relationship is the route to the sale</i>.</p>\n<p>Example: Meltwater's review of 2023 social media campaigns named Burger King's <b>\"You Rule\"</b> as the top campaign. Throughout the year its social posts carried one theme across different occasions (\"It's Crowning Time\" in a homecoming post, \"y'all out here ruling spooky season.\" for Halloween), and the repeated theme kept a recognisable brand voice. <b>One theme, many posts.</b></p>\n<p>The notes list Facebook, Instagram, Pinterest, Messenger, Twitter, Reddit, Snapchat, WhatsApp, Google and Tumblr. Updates: Twitter is now <b>X</b> (2023), and Google's social network, Google+, closed to the public in 2019. Add TikTok and LinkedIn to any current list.</p>\n<p>At the regional agency <b>Island Pulse Media</b>, Keisha (marketing and social media) always starts with one question: <i>where does this audience already spend its time?</i></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Platform</th><th>Typical audience</th><th>Content that works</th><th>Campaign use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Facebook</b></td><td>Broad, strong with adults 30+, families, community groups</td><td>Events, albums, longer posts, Facebook Groups, Marketplace</td><td>Community events, local businesses, paid ads targeted by location and age</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Instagram</b></td><td>Young adults, tourists, lifestyle and food audiences</td><td>High-quality photos, Reels (9:16), Stories, carousels</td><td>Visual brands: restaurants in Rodney Bay, beach bars in Negril, fashion</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TikTok</b></td><td>Teens and young adults</td><td>Short vertical video, trends, sounds, duets</td><td>Reach young audiences quickly; trend-based and behind-the-scenes content</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>LinkedIn</b></td><td>Professionals, employers, B2B (business to business)</td><td>Articles, case studies, company pages, job posts</td><td>Recruiting, professional services, conferences, partnerships</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>X (formerly Twitter)</b></td><td>News followers, journalists, sports and politics audiences</td><td>Short text updates, threads, live commentary</td><td>Real-time updates: hurricane advisories, cricket scores, breaking news</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <b>social networking campaign</b> mixes <b>organic</b> content (free posts that reach followers) with <b>paid</b> content (boosted posts and ads targeted by location, age and interests). Good campaigns use a consistent <b>hashtag</b> (for example, a #SXMCarnivalVibes), a clear <b>call to action</b> (CTA) such as \"Book now\" or \"Link in bio\", and are scheduled for times when the audience is online.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, never write \"post it on social media\". Name the <b>platform</b>, the <b>format</b> (Reel, Story, carousel, thread) and the <b>reason</b> linked to the audience: \"TikTok, because the target audience of 15 to 19 year olds spends most of its social time watching short vertical video.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A promotional campaign is a planned set of messages, sent through chosen channels over a set time, to reach a goal such as sales or sign-ups. Social networking builds relationships with customers: be recognised, be easy to reach and guide followers towards buying. Choose the platform your audience already uses, such as Instagram for visitors to a beach bar in Barbados.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Platform choice is an audience decision, and each platform rewards a different <b>format</b>. A strong answer names the platform, the format and the audience reason together: \"Instagram Reels, because visitors planning a trip browse short, visual content.\" The mix of organic and paid content matters too. Organic posts build the relationship cheaply but reach only some followers, while paid ads can target by location, age and interest but stop working when the money stops. Consistency turns many posts into one campaign: a single theme, hashtag and tone of voice make every post recognisable, as Burger King's year-long \"You Rule\" theme showed. Remember that \"social media\" on its own is too vague to earn marks.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the platform to the campaign","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media has seven briefs on her desk. Match each brief to the platform that fits it best.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"LinkedIn","r":"Recruit experienced accountants for a firm in Port of Spain"},{"l":"TikTok","r":"Get secondary school students excited about a youth coding camp"},{"l":"Instagram","r":"Show off the plating of dishes at a Holetown restaurant to visitors"},{"l":"X (formerly Twitter)","r":"Post live, minute-by-minute updates during a storm watch"},{"l":"Facebook Events","r":"Invite parents and grandparents to a church fair in Linden"},{"l":"Instagram Stories","r":"Share a 24-hour flash discount with followers who check in daily"},{"l":"Facebook Groups","r":"Build a community of St. Lucian home gardeners swapping tips"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose each platform by its audience and content format, which is the justification examiners reward.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is the audience?","p":"<p>Professionals, teens, visitors, older adults: each group gathers on different platforms.</p>"},{"h":"What is the format?","p":"<p>Live text updates, short vertical video, polished photos and community discussion each suit a different platform.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the platform (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"LinkedIn","r":"Recruiting professional staff"},{"l":"TikTok","r":"Short trend videos for teens"},{"l":"Instagram","r":"Polished food photos for visitors"},{"l":"X (formerly Twitter)","r":"Live news updates"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the platform to the campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: eight briefs from across the region, including platform features rather than whole platforms. Match each to the BEST fit.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"LinkedIn","r":"Attract partner businesses to sponsor a technology conference in Port of Spain"},{"l":"TikTok","r":"Reach 15 to 19 year olds with a trend-based challenge for a youth soca contest"},{"l":"Instagram Reels","r":"Show tourists a 20-second visual tour of a boutique hotel in Rodney Bay"},{"l":"X (formerly Twitter)","r":"Give journalists real-time updates during an election count"},{"l":"Facebook Events","r":"Invite older residents to a church harvest, with a venue map and reminders"},{"l":"WhatsApp Channels","r":"Send one-way daily specials to customers who chose to follow a bakery"},{"l":"Facebook Groups","r":"Host an ongoing discussion among parents about a school's new uniforms"},{"l":"YouTube","r":"Publish long, searchable recipe tutorials that keep earning views for years"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched audience, format and purpose, including the features inside platforms, which is the justification examiners reward."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Content marketing, text messaging and WhatsApp","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Content marketing</b> attracts and keeps an audience by giving them useful, interesting or entertaining content, instead of only advertising at them. Trust comes first; sales follow.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Blogs</b>: regularly updated written posts on a website (WordPress, Blogger, Wix). They build search visibility (SEO) because every post is a new page Google can find. Example: a Bonaire dive shop writes \"Five reef-safe sunscreen tips for Bonaire\".</li>\n<li><b>Vlogs</b>: video blogs, usually on YouTube, TikTok or Instagram. They show personality and process. Example: a sorrel drink maker films the sorrel being picked and steeped.</li>\n<li><b>Podcasts</b>: episodic audio distributed by RSS to Spotify and Apple Podcasts. Good for long-form talk: interviews with Caribbean entrepreneurs, Carnival history, study tips. Listeners often hear them while driving or working.</li>\n<li><b>Email newsletters</b>: a regular email to subscribers who opted in, with news, tips and offers. The business owns the list, so it is not at the mercy of a social media algorithm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Text messaging (SMS) and WhatsApp</b> matter enormously in the Caribbean, where WhatsApp is often the main way people communicate.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SMS</b>: short (160-character) messages that reach any phone, even without data. Useful for appointment reminders and urgent alerts.</li>\n<li><b>WhatsApp Business</b>: a free app with a business profile, catalogue, quick replies and labels. <b>Broadcast lists</b> send one message to many contacts who have saved your number. <b>WhatsApp Status</b> works like Stories. <b>WhatsApp Channels</b> let followers receive one-way updates.</li>\n<li>Rules: only message people who opted in, identify your business, keep messages short and give a way to stop receiving them. Spamming people's personal WhatsApp damages a brand quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Island Pulse Media's client, Johnny Cake Corner in Dangriga, posts the day's specials to WhatsApp Status each morning at 7 a.m., sends a broadcast on Fridays about the fish fry night, and links a short blog recipe for saltfish johnny cakes from its Instagram bio. Each channel has a job.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Content marketing is about <b>value to the audience</b>; direct messaging (SMS, WhatsApp, email) is about <b>reaching people personally</b>. If a question asks you to \"explain\" content marketing, say what value the content gives and how that leads to the business goal.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Content marketing attracts customers by giving them something useful or entertaining, not just adverts. Blogs are written posts that help a business appear in search results. Vlogs are video diaries. Podcasts are audio episodes. Newsletters are regular emails to people who signed up. SMS and WhatsApp reach people directly. A bakery in Antigua could share a recipe blog and send WhatsApp specials.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Content marketing works over a long time: it builds <b>trust</b> first, and sales follow because customers already value the business. That is why it needs a schedule and patience; one blog post rarely changes anything. Owned channels matter strategically. A business owns its email list and website, but it only rents its audience on social platforms, where an algorithm change can cut reach overnight. In the Caribbean, WhatsApp is often the most effective direct channel, yet it is also the easiest to abuse: unwanted broadcasts feel like an intrusion into a personal space and can get a number reported or blocked. A Grade One answer gives each channel a job and explains how its value leads to the business goal.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter?","intro":"<p>The Grenada Spice Trail tour company is planning its content marketing. Sort each idea into the content type it describes.</p>","main":"Which content marketing format is this?","bins":["Blog","Vlog","Podcast","Email newsletter"],"items":[{"t":"A 30-minute audio interview with a spice farmer, released every two weeks on Spotify.","bin":2,"fb":"Episodic audio distributed to listening apps is a podcast."},{"t":"A YouTube video of the guide walking through a cocoa estate, talking to camera.","bin":1,"fb":"A video diary or walkthrough with a presenter is a vlog."},{"t":"A subscriber-only \"Spice of the Month\" email with a recipe and a booking link.","bin":3,"fb":"It goes to a list the company owns, by email: a newsletter."},{"t":"A TikTok series called \"A day in the life of a tour guide\", filmed on a phone.","bin":1,"fb":"Short personal video diaries are vlogs, even on TikTok."},{"t":"A written post titled \"Ten things to pack for a rainforest hike\", with photos and links.","bin":0,"fb":"It is written, published on the web and updated regularly: a blog post."},{"t":"A monthly email to past guests with new tour dates and a discount code.","bin":3,"fb":"A regular email to opted-in subscribers is a newsletter."},{"t":"A 1,000-word written guide, \"How nutmeg is processed in Gouyave\", published on the company website.","bin":0,"fb":"Written posts on a website are blog content, and they help the site rank in search results."},{"t":"An audio series where listeners hear the history of Grenada's spice trade while driving.","bin":2,"fb":"Audio that people listen to while doing other things is the strength of a podcast."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can now tell the four content marketing formats apart: written web posts, video diaries, episodic audio and subscriber emails.","tutor":[{"h":"Look at the medium","p":"<p>Is it text on a web page, video, audio only, or something arriving in an inbox?</p>"},{"h":"TikTok is still video","p":"<p>A personal video diary is a vlog whatever platform it is on.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Content formats (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A written guide on the company website.","bin":0,"fb":"Written web post: blog."},{"t":"A video tour with the guide talking to camera.","bin":1,"fb":"Video diary: vlog."},{"t":"An audio interview released every two weeks.","bin":2,"fb":"Episodic audio: podcast."},{"t":"A monthly email to subscribers.","bin":3,"fb":"Email to a list: newsletter."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Blog, vlog, podcast or newsletter? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: six bins, adding the two direct channels, SMS and WhatsApp Business. Sort each idea for Grenada Spice Trail tours.</p>","main":"Which format or channel is this?","bins":["Blog","Vlog","Podcast","Email newsletter","SMS","WhatsApp Business"],"items":[{"t":"A broadcast to customers who saved the business number, sharing a last-minute discount.","bin":5,"fb":"Broadcast lists are a WhatsApp Business tool for opted-in contacts."},{"t":"A catalogue of tours with prices and quick replies inside a messaging app.","bin":5,"fb":"Catalogues and quick replies are WhatsApp Business features."},{"t":"A 900-word guide with photos on the company website, updated monthly.","bin":0,"fb":"A regularly updated written web post is a blog."},{"t":"A guide filming herself on the Grand Etang trail, talking to camera.","bin":1,"fb":"A presenter-led video diary is a vlog."},{"t":"A fortnightly audio interview with a nutmeg farmer on Spotify.","bin":2,"fb":"Episodic audio on a listening app is a podcast."},{"t":"A monthly email with tour dates, sent to past guests who opted in.","bin":3,"fb":"A regular email to a list the company owns is a newsletter."},{"t":"A daily Status update with photos of today's tour group.","bin":5,"fb":"WhatsApp Status works like Stories."},{"t":"A post titled \"How to choose reef-safe sunscreen\", written to rank in Google.","bin":0,"fb":"Written web content built for search: a blog."},{"t":"A 160-character reminder about tomorrow's tour that reaches guests with no mobile data.","bin":4,"fb":"SMS reaches any phone, even without data."},{"t":"A text alert to booked guests that the tour is cancelled because of a tropical storm warning.","bin":4,"fb":"Short, urgent, data-free alerts are a classic SMS use."}],"win":"Excellent. You can tell the four content formats apart and give each direct channel its job."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Guerrilla marketing and viral marketing","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Guerrilla marketing</b> surprises people in public. A company uses surprise or unconventional interactions to promote a product or service. It relies on personal interaction, a smaller budget and small groups of promoters working in a particular location, rather than widespread media campaigns.</p>\n<p>The term borrows from guerrilla warfare: small forces using surprise instead of big budgets. The spelling \"guerilla\" is also used; \"guerrilla\" is the more common spelling. Both are accepted, so pick one and use it consistently within an answer.</p>\n<p>Common guerrilla tactics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ambient or street art</b>: chalk art, stickers or installations in public spaces (with permission). Example: footprints painted along the Castries waterfront leading to a new juice bar.</li>\n<li><b>Flash mobs and pop-ups</b>: a surprise dance at a mall, or a pop-up photo booth during Carnival fete nights.</li>\n<li><b>Stunts</b>: an oversized object that people stop to photograph, such as a giant \"phone screen\" frame on Grand Anse beach inviting selfies with a hashtag.</li>\n<li><b>QR code trails</b>: posters around a campus, each unlocking a clue to a game or a discount.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Guerrilla campaigns are often <b>designed to be photographed and shared</b>, so the offline stunt becomes online content. Risks: getting permission from the property owner or the government, avoiding anything that looks like vandalism, and making sure the stunt cannot be misunderstood (a fake \"emergency\" would be dangerous and illegal).</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Viral marketing</b> is a message people choose to pass on. People pass information about a product or service through word of mouth, online sharing and email. Social media provides an ideal ecosystem. \"Viral\" means spreading quickly and widely.</p>\n<p>The notes show three steps: <b>1. Create the message</b> (the enterprise is deliberate: give people a reason to share); <b>2. Pass it along</b> (friends, family and others distribute the message organically); <b>3. Reach expands</b> (the goal is exponential growth as each person brings others in). Spread you did not pay for is called <b>organic reach</b>.</p>\n<p>A classic example is the <b>ALS Ice Bucket Challenge</b> (2014). The challenge existed before The ALS Association used it to raise awareness and generate donations. Massive sharing of videos on social media created a worldwide sensation and greatly increased awareness of ALS.</p>\n<p>What makes content shareable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Emotion</b>: humour, pride, surprise, nostalgia. Caribbean national pride is a strong driver, such as a video celebrating a local athlete or a classic soca tune.</li>\n<li><b>Relatability</b>: \"Every Caribbean mother when...\" style content people tag friends in.</li>\n<li><b>Participation</b>: a challenge, a dance or a template people can copy and remix (user-generated content).</li>\n<li><b>Ease of sharing</b>: short, vertical, captioned (many people watch with the sound off) and timed to a trend or season such as Carnival, Emancipation Day or the start of the school year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nobody can guarantee a post will \"go viral\". Professionals design for <b>shareability</b> and then measure shares, saves and reach.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Examiners like you to <b>distinguish</b> these two. Guerrilla marketing is defined by its <b>method</b> (unconventional, low cost, surprising, often physical); viral marketing is defined by how the message <b>spreads</b> (people sharing it). A guerrilla stunt can become viral, but they are not the same thing.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Guerrilla marketing surprises people in public using a small budget, like chalk footprints leading to a new shop. Viral marketing is a message that spreads because people choose to share it, like a dance challenge. A guerrilla stunt can go viral if people photograph and share it. Both need permission and care. A pop-up photo booth at Crop Over in Barbados could do both.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The two terms are defined by different things: guerrilla by its <b>method</b> (unconventional, low cost, surprising, usually physical) and viral by its <b>spread</b> (people passing it on). That distinction is what examiners test. Nobody can guarantee virality, so professionals design for shareability (emotion, relatability, participation and ease of sharing) and then measure shares and reach. Guerrilla work carries specific risks: a stunt without permission can look like vandalism or break the law, and anything that could be mistaken for a real emergency is dangerous. Cultural sensitivity matters too; a stunt that plays with national symbols or religious events can backfire. The best guerrilla ideas build in a hashtag or QR code, so the offline surprise produces measurable online results.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Name that practice","intro":"<p>Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are reviewing campaigns from around the region. Identify the industry practice in each.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Trinidad snack brand leaves giant foam \"doubles\" on park benches in Port of Spain with a QR code on each one.","options":["influencer marketing","guerrilla marketing","email marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Unexpected physical objects in public spaces, cheap to produce and surprising: classic guerrilla marketing."},{"text":"A Jamaican dancehall step challenge spreads because thousands of people film themselves copying it and tag friends.","options":["viral marketing","cross-platform marketing","content curation"],"answer":0,"fb":"The spread comes from people sharing and copying it, which is viral marketing with user-generated content."},{"text":"A Barbados festival uses the same logo, colours, hashtag and slogan on its website, Instagram, radio ads and posters.","options":["guerrilla marketing","cross-platform marketing","SMS marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Consistent messaging across several channels is cross-platform marketing."},{"text":"A gym in Bridgetown sends members a free monthly email with workouts and a renewal reminder.","options":["guerrilla marketing","email marketing","viral marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Regular emails to opted-in members are email marketing, combined with content marketing."},{"text":"A Guyanese food creator with 40,000 engaged local followers is paid to review a new restaurant, and labels it #ad.","options":["cross-platform marketing","influencer marketing","organic reach"],"answer":1,"fb":"Partnering with a trusted creator who has a relevant audience is influencer marketing, and the #ad label is the honest disclosure."},{"text":"A dentist in Mandeville texts patients a reminder the day before each appointment.","options":["vlogging","text messaging (SMS)","viral marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Short direct messages to a phone are SMS marketing, which works even without mobile data."}],"win":"Excellent. You identified each practice by its defining feature: method, spread, consistency, channel or partner.","tutor":[{"h":"Method or spread?","p":"<p>Surprising and physical points to one practice; people sharing it points to another.</p>"},{"h":"Who delivers the message?","p":"<p>If a third-party creator delivers it to their own followers, think about which practice that is.</p>"},{"h":"Same look everywhere","p":"<p>When the question stresses consistency across channels, it is describing one specific practice.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name that practice (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A surprise low-cost stunt in a public place is...","options":["email marketing","guerrilla marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Unconventional, low cost and surprising: guerrilla."},{"text":"Content that spreads because people share it is...","options":["viral marketing","SMS marketing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Spread by sharing: viral."},{"text":"The same message and branding on many channels is...","options":["influencer marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Consistency across channels: cross-platform."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name that practice (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: some campaigns combine practices, and some questions ask what a campaign is NOT. Choose the MOST accurate answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A sunscreen brand turns a parking space on Frederick Street into a tiny beach lounge for one Saturday, and photos spread online with #ShadeTT. Which statement is MOST accurate?","options":["It is only email marketing","It is guerrilla marketing designed to become viral","It is cross-platform marketing because it is outdoors","It is influencer marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"The method is guerrilla; the hashtag is there so the spread becomes viral."},{"text":"A Jamaican snack brand uses the same hashtag, colours and slogan on TikTok, radio and bus-stop posters, with a different cut for each. This is...","options":["SMS marketing","cross-platform marketing","viral marketing","guerrilla marketing"],"answer":1,"fb":"One consistent message adapted across several channels is cross-platform marketing."},{"text":"A gym in Bridgetown emails members a free monthly workout with a renewal link. Which practices are combined?","options":["guerrilla and viral marketing","cross-platform and guerrilla marketing","influencer and SMS marketing","email marketing and content marketing"],"answer":3,"fb":"Useful content (the workout) is delivered by email to an opted-in list."},{"text":"A creator posts a paid review with no #ad label. What is the main problem?","options":["It should have been an email","It is undisclosed influencer marketing, which misleads followers and may break the law","It is too short","Nothing, because disclosure is optional"],"answer":1,"fb":"Disclosure is ethical and, in many countries, a legal requirement."},{"text":"Which example is viral but NOT guerrilla?","options":["A surprise flash mob at a mall","Giant foam doubles left on park benches","A home-made dance challenge video that thousands copy and share online","A crossing near the cruise pier painted as piano keys"],"answer":2,"fb":"It spreads by sharing, but there is no physical, surprising public stunt."},{"text":"Which example is guerrilla but may NOT become viral?","options":["A celebrity's sponsored post","A trending TikTok challenge","A national television advert","A small chalk trail outside a campus that few people photograph"],"answer":3,"fb":"The method is guerrilla, but spread depends on people choosing to share it."},{"text":"A dentist in Mandeville sends reminders by SMS rather than WhatsApp. What is the BEST justification?","options":["SMS needs an app to be installed","SMS is a social platform","SMS can carry videos","SMS reaches any phone, even without mobile data"],"answer":3,"fb":"SMS works on every phone and needs no data, which suits urgent reminders."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated method from spread, spotted combined practices and justified a channel by its strength."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Guerilla marketing trades budget for surprise","html":"<p>Here is <b>why</b> guerilla marketing works and <b>where</b> it happens. It trades budget for surprise.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Free extra reach</b>: in-your-face promotions can spread through viral marketing and word of mouth, reaching a broader audience for free. This is where guerilla and viral marketing meet.</li>\n<li><b>Emotional connection matters</b>: \"edgy\" products and younger consumers are common targets.</li>\n<li><b>Public places</b>: streets, concerts and parks put the brand in public. Choose the right <b>time and place</b> to avoid legal issues.</li>\n<li><b>Three settings</b>: campaigns can be <b>indoor</b>, <b>outdoor</b> or <b>experiential</b> (people take part). An <b>event ambush</b> uses a crowd that has already gathered for something else, such as a concert, to invite public interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Guerilla formats from the notes</b> (illustrative recreations of stunt formats, not evidence of specific campaigns):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Format</th><th>The idea</th><th>A Caribbean version</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Crosswalk ad</td><td>Road markings become part of the design</td><td>A zebra crossing near the Bridgetown cruise terminal painted to look like piano keys for a music festival (only with government permission)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bowling lane takeover</td><td>An everyday surface carries a surprise message</td><td>A bowling alley lane wrapped to look like a runway for an airline promotion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Branded delivery truck</td><td>A moving vehicle becomes a billboard</td><td>A van wrapped as a giant sorrel bottle, touring Frederick Street</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parking space installation</td><td>A parking bay is turned into a scene</td><td>A parking space turned into a tiny beach lounge for a sunscreen brand</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Escalator wrap</td><td>Steps and rails carry the brand</td><td>Mall escalator steps printed to look like a stack of johnny cakes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Street lamp turned into a cup</td><td>Street furniture becomes a product</td><td>A lamp post on the waterfront dressed as a giant iced coffee cup</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each format surprises people <b>in a particular location</b>, invites them to take a photo, and lets the photos carry the message online. That is the guerilla method feeding viral spread.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If a Paper 02 question asks you to design a guerilla stunt, state the <b>place</b>, the <b>time</b> (for example a Carnival fete night), the <b>surprise</b>, the <b>permission</b> you would obtain, and how photos will be shared with a hashtag or QR code.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Guerilla marketing swaps a big budget for a surprise in a public place. Common formats turn everyday things into adverts: crossings, escalators, parking spaces, street lamps and delivery vans. They can be indoor, outdoor or experiential, where people take part. The aim is for people to stop, take a photo and share it, like a van in Kingston wrapped to look like a giant patty.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Guerilla marketing is efficient when it reaches the <b>right people in the right place at the right moment</b>. An event ambush works because the crowd is already gathered and in a good mood; the same stunt on an empty Tuesday wastes effort. The format should connect with the product, so the surprise reinforces the message: a parking space turned into a beach lounge suits sunscreen, not insurance. Because guerilla budgets are small, the photos and shares that follow provide most of the reach, which is why designers plan the \"photo moment\" and the hashtag as carefully as the stunt itself. Permission from property owners or the government must be in place, and the set-up must be safe for the public.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the guerilla tactics","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media has a list of ideas for launching an energy drink aimed at young people in Trinidad and Tobago.</p>","q":"Select ALL the ideas that are guerilla marketing.","lines":["Wrap the escalator at a shopping centre so the steps look like cans of the drink.","Buy a full-page advert in a printed newspaper for six weeks.","Turn a parking space on Frederick Street into a mini chill-out lounge for one Saturday, with permission.","Send a welcome email to everyone who subscribes on the website.","Set up a surprise sampling team at a concert in Chaguanas to invite the crowd to take part (an event ambush).","Pay for a 30-second television advert across the region.","Dress a street lamp on the Brian Lara Promenade as a giant drink can with a QR code.","Post the same weekly graphic on Facebook, Instagram and X."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Correct. Guerilla tactics use surprise, a small budget and a particular public place: an escalator wrap, a parking space installation, an event ambush and a street lamp turned into a product.","miss":"Look again. Newspaper and television adverts are widespread media campaigns, the email is email marketing, and posting the same graphic on several platforms is social or cross-platform marketing.","tutor":[{"h":"Surprise in public","p":"<p>Is the idea unconventional, low budget and happening in a particular physical place?</p>"},{"h":"Not widespread media","p":"<p>Guerilla marketing contrasts with widespread media campaigns.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the guerilla tactics (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO guerilla ideas.","lines":["An escalator wrapped to look like drink cans.","A television advert.","A street lamp dressed as a giant cup.","A monthly newsletter."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Both surprise people in a public place on a small budget.","miss":"Guerilla marketing happens in public, by surprise. Television and newsletters are not guerilla."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the guerilla tactics (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: Keisha's new list for the energy drink includes influencer, viral and cross-platform ideas that might be mistaken for guerilla marketing.</p>","q":"Select ALL the ideas that are guerilla marketing.","lines":["Wrap a delivery van to look like a giant can and drive it along Frederick Street at lunchtime.","Pay a TikTok creator to post a review of the drink.","Turn a bus shelter into a \"cool zone\" with a fan and free samples for one afternoon, with permission.","Sponsor a radio phone-in competition.","Turn up at a beach volleyball tournament with a sampling team and a photo frame (an event ambush).","Launch a dance challenge that spreads only on TikTok.","Paint footprints on the promenade leading to a hidden pop-up stand, with permission.","Send a WhatsApp broadcast to opted-in customers.","Run the same graphic on Facebook, Instagram and a billboard."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Correct. A branded van, an experiential bus shelter, an event ambush and a footprint trail all use surprise in a public place on a small budget.","miss":"Look again. A paid creator is influencer marketing, an online-only challenge is viral marketing, the broadcast is direct messaging, the radio sponsorship is traditional media and the same graphic everywhere is cross-platform."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cross-platform and influencer marketing","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Cross-platform marketing</b> promotes a product, service or brand across multiple platforms to reach a wider, more diverse audience. Reach across channels, keep the message cohesive, adapt the execution.</p>\n<p>The notes give three steps: <b>find the audience</b> (meet people where they \"live\" online), <b>use each strength</b> (choose channels that facilitate conversation effectively) and <b>keep one message</b> (a cohesive, consistent campaign adapted to each platform). <i>Different executions, one recognisable brand.</i></p>\n<p>Consistency means the same <b>logo, colours, typefaces, tone of voice, slogan, hashtag and offer</b> everywhere. Adapting means changing the <b>shape and length</b>: a 9:16 Reel for TikTok and Instagram, a 16:9 version for YouTube, a 1:1 square for the Facebook feed, a 30-second radio spot, a printed poster with a QR code that leads to the website.</p>\n<p>Why it works: people see the message several times in different places, which builds recognition. It also reaches people who are not on every platform: an older listener hears the radio spot, a teenager sees the TikTok. The risk is inconsistency: if the Instagram post says the event starts at 7 p.m. and the poster says 8 p.m., trust drops. A shared <b>brand guide</b> and a single <b>content calendar</b> prevent this.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Influencer marketing</b> is a collaboration built around an influential voice. A brand collaborates with an online influencer to market a product or service, or simply to improve brand recognition. The effect can be less tangible than traditional advertising. Influence typically comes from <b>expertise, popularity or reputation</b>, and any person, group, brand or place could potentially be an influencer.</p>\n<p>The notes call influencers <b>borrowed credibility</b>. This approach is distinctive because it <b>appeals to the influencer's needs</b> rather than directly to the customer's. That creates a <b>partnership trade-off</b>: <b>earn the fit</b> (the collaboration must make sense for the influential voice carrying the brand) and <b>accept less control</b> (legal trouble or a fall from public favour can harm a product's chance of success). The major drawback is <b>lower controllability</b> than traditional marketing.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Followers (rough guide)</th><th>Strength</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nano</td><td>1,000 to 10,000</td><td>Very local, high trust, low cost; ideal for small island businesses</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Micro</td><td>10,000 to 100,000</td><td>Strong niche engagement (food, fitness, beauty, gaming)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Macro and mega</td><td>100,000 to millions</td><td>Huge reach, high cost, lower engagement rate</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>How to identify the right influencer:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Relevance</b>: does their audience match yours (location, age, interests)?</li>\n<li><b>Engagement rate</b>, not follower count: real comments and shares matter more than a big number, which may include fake or inactive followers.</li>\n<li><b>Values and brand fit</b>: check past posts for anything that clashes with your brand.</li>\n<li><b>Authenticity</b>: would they genuinely use the product?</li>\n<li><b>Agreement</b>: a written brief covering deliverables, dates, payment, usage rights and disclosure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Disclosure</b> is an ethical and, in many countries, a legal requirement. Paid posts should be clearly labelled (#ad, \"Paid partnership\" tag). Hiding a payment misleads followers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A nano-influencer in Castries with 3,000 engaged local followers is often a better choice for a local café than a celebrity with 2 million followers abroad. Always justify the choice with <b>audience fit and engagement</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Cross-platform marketing spreads one message across many channels, from TikTok to radio and posters, while keeping the logo, colours and offer the same everywhere. Influencer marketing pays a trusted creator to promote a product to their followers. Pick influencers whose audience matches yours and who have real engagement, and label paid posts #ad. A small café in St. Kitts should choose a local creator, not a foreign celebrity.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Both practices trade control for reach. Cross-platform campaigns multiply exposure, but every extra channel is another place for errors, such as a wrong date on one poster, so a shared brand guide and one content calendar are essential. Influencer marketing borrows credibility, but the brand cannot control what the influencer says or does later, so a scandal can damage the product too. That is why agreements cover deliverables, dates, usage rights and disclosure. Engagement rate matters more than follower count, because followers can be bought and many are inactive. Disclosure is ethical and, in many countries, a legal requirement; hiding a payment misleads followers and usually backfires when it is discovered, harming both the influencer and the brand.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing the right influencer","q":"Rodney Bay Smoothie Shack wants to increase visits from St. Lucia residents aged 18 to 30 and has a small budget. Which influencer partnership is the BEST choice?","options":[{"t":"An account with 80,000 followers that bought most of them and gets about 10 likes per post.","fb":"Bought followers do not visit shops. The tiny engagement shows the audience is not real or not interested."},{"t":"A popular gaming streamer in Europe with 50,000 followers who does not drink smoothies.","fb":"Wrong location, weak brand fit and no authenticity. Followers would not trust the recommendation."},{"t":"A US celebrity with 3 million followers, mostly in North America, who charges a very high fee per post.","fb":"Huge reach, but almost none of it is local, and the cost is far beyond a small budget. Reach without relevance wastes money."},{"t":"A St. Lucian fitness creator with 6,000 followers, mostly local young adults, who gets around 400 genuine comments and shares per post.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The audience matches by location and age, engagement is high, and a nano-influencer fits a small budget."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Three tests","p":"<p>Check audience match, engagement and budget fit, in that order.</p>"},{"h":"Follower count is a trap","p":"<p>A big number is not the same as people who will actually walk into a shop in Rodney Bay.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing the right influencer (Foundation)","q":"A small local shop wants more young local customers. Which influencer is best?","options":[{"t":"An account with many fake followers.","fb":"Fake followers never become customers."},{"t":"A foreign celebrity with millions of followers.","fb":"Expensive, and the followers are not local."},{"t":"A local creator with a small but very engaged young audience.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Audience match and engagement matter most."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing the right influencer (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A chocolate shop in Grenada has XCD 400 for ONE influencer post to reach young residents. Compare the options using audience, engagement rate, cost and brand fit. Which is BEST?","options":[{"t":"A Miami lifestyle influencer with 250,000 followers (5% in the Caribbean), about 2,500 engagements per post, a fee of XCD 4,000.","fb":"Ten times the budget, few local followers and an engagement rate of only about 1%."},{"t":"A Grenadian food creator with 8,000 followers (80% in Grenada), about 600 engagements per post, a fee of XCD 350, who already buys the shop's chocolate.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Local audience, an engagement rate of about 7.5%, within budget and authentic brand fit."},{"t":"A local gamer with 12,000 followers (90% in Grenada), about 900 engagements per post, a fee of XCD 300, who has never mentioned food.","fb":"Strong numbers, but weak brand fit: followers would not trust a sudden chocolate recommendation."},{"t":"A Grenadian account with 60,000 followers, about 60 engagements per post, a fee of XCD 400.","fb":"An engagement rate of about 0.1% suggests bought or inactive followers."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"A unified cross-platform strategy","html":"<p><b>A cross-platform mix spans digital and print</b>. A typical channel mix: email blasts, newsletters, Facebook, Twitter (now X), Instagram, TikTok, influencer marketing, Google ads and print mailers.</p>\n<p><b>Four principles keep a unified strategy on track</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Understand your target audience</b>: identify who they are and which platforms they use most, so you can allocate resources effectively.</li>\n<li><b>Maintain consistent branding</b>: keep logos, colours, tone of voice and overall branding elements recognisable.</li>\n<li><b>Adapt content for each platform</b>: tailor formats to user behaviour. What works on Instagram may not work on LinkedIn.</li>\n<li><b>Monitor and engage</b>: use social media to listen and respond. Each platform has different strengths and demographics.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Four more tactics for unified campaigns</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Search engine optimisation (SEO)</b>: use relevant keywords, quality content and a mobile-friendly site.</li>\n<li><b>Email marketing</b>: reach your audience directly with compelling content.</li>\n<li><b>Influencer marketing</b>: collaborate across platforms to tap diverse audiences and credibility.</li>\n<li><b>Cross-platform advertising</b>: Google Ads and Facebook Ads (now run through Meta's ads tools, covering Facebook and Instagram) allow targeting of specific demographics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Finally, <b>analytics and monitoring, plus customer feedback and engagement, close the loop</b>: you check what worked and feed it into the next round.</p>\n<p><b>Example.</b> For an Emancipation Day concert in Georgetown, Zara at the Island Pulse Media sets one look (colours, logo, slogan). Malik cuts a vertical TikTok, a square Facebook post and a radio spot from the same footage; Maya optimises the ticket page for mobile search; Keisha sends an email blast to opted-in fans and runs Meta ads targeted at 18 to 35 year olds on the island; printed mailers go to older residents. Keisha monitors comments daily and replies.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to \"explain how to keep a cross-platform campaign unified\", use the four principles as your paragraph headings: audience, consistent branding, adapt content, monitor and engage.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A unified campaign uses many channels but feels like one campaign. Follow four principles: know your audience, keep the branding the same, adapt the content to each platform, and watch and reply to comments. Use tactics such as search engine optimisation, email, influencers and targeted ads. A concert in Georgetown might use TikTok for teenagers and printed mailers for older residents.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The four principles and four tactics form a <b>loop</b>, not a list. Audience research decides the channels; consistent branding makes each channel reinforce the others; adapting content respects how each platform is used; and monitoring feeds back into the next round of decisions. Tactics only work inside that loop: SEO helps people who are already searching, email reaches people who have opted in and targeted ads find new people who match the audience profile. A common weakness is treating every channel the same, such as posting a landscape video with tiny text on TikTok. Another is collecting comments without acting on them. In an \"explain\" answer, use the principles as headings and give one concrete Caribbean example for each.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Principle or tactic?","intro":"<p>Sort each statement for Emancipation Day concert campaign.</p>","main":"Is this one of the four principles of a unified strategy, or one of the four tactics?","bins":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign"],"items":[{"t":"Add keywords such as \"Georgetown concert tickets\" and make the page mobile-friendly.","bin":1,"fb":"This is search engine optimisation, one of the four tactics."},{"t":"Partner with a local creator who posts on both TikTok and Instagram.","bin":1,"fb":"Influencer marketing across platforms taps diverse audiences and credibility."},{"t":"Make a vertical video for TikTok and a square version for the Facebook feed.","bin":0,"fb":"Adapting content for each platform tailors formats to user behaviour."},{"t":"Find out that 18 to 25 year olds on the island mostly use TikTok and Instagram.","bin":0,"fb":"Understanding your target audience, so resources go to the right platforms."},{"t":"Send opted-in fans a compelling email with the line-up and a ticket button.","bin":1,"fb":"Email marketing reaches the audience directly."},{"t":"Use the same logo, colours and tone of voice on every post and poster.","bin":0,"fb":"Maintaining consistent branding keeps the brand recognisable."},{"t":"Run Facebook and Instagram ads aimed at 18 to 35 year olds in Guyana.","bin":1,"fb":"Cross-platform advertising lets you target specific demographics."},{"t":"Reply to questions in the comments every evening and note common complaints.","bin":0,"fb":"Monitoring and engaging means listening and responding."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can separate the four principles (audience, consistent branding, adapt content, monitor and engage) from the four tactics (SEO, email, influencer, cross-platform advertising).","tutor":[{"h":"Rule or action?","p":"<p>Principles guide every decision. Tactics are specific channels or techniques you use.</p>"},{"h":"Count to four","p":"<p>Each bin has exactly four items.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Principle or tactic? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Keep the same logo and colours everywhere.","bin":0,"fb":"Consistent branding: a principle."},{"t":"Learn which platforms the audience uses.","bin":0,"fb":"Understand your audience: a principle."},{"t":"Use keywords and a mobile-friendly site.","bin":1,"fb":"SEO: a tactic."},{"t":"Run targeted Google Ads.","bin":1,"fb":"Cross-platform advertising: a tactic."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Principle or tactic? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin for MISTAKES that break a unified strategy. Sort each statement about the Emancipation Day concert campaign.</p>","main":"Is this a principle, a tactic or a mistake?","bins":["Principle of a unified strategy","Tactic for a unified campaign","Mistake that breaks a unified strategy"],"items":[{"t":"Questions about parking are ignored for the whole campaign.","bin":2,"fb":"Not monitoring or engaging: a mistake."},{"t":"\"Georgetown concert tickets\" keywords are added to a mobile-friendly ticket page.","bin":1,"fb":"Search engine optimisation: a tactic."},{"t":"A 9:16 Reel, a 1:1 feed post and a 30-second radio spot are all cut from one shoot.","bin":0,"fb":"Adapting content for each platform: a principle."},{"t":"A brand guide fixes the logo, colours and tone of voice for every channel.","bin":0,"fb":"Maintaining consistent branding: a principle."},{"t":"A survey shows that 18 to 25 year olds on the island mostly use TikTok and Instagram.","bin":0,"fb":"Understanding the audience: a principle."},{"t":"Comments are answered every evening and common questions are logged.","bin":0,"fb":"Monitoring and engaging: a principle."},{"t":"The Instagram post says the concert starts at 7 p.m., but the poster says 8 p.m.","bin":2,"fb":"Inconsistent details break trust: a mistake."},{"t":"Opted-in fans receive an email with the line-up and a ticket button.","bin":1,"fb":"Email marketing: a tactic."},{"t":"One landscape video with tiny text is posted unchanged on every platform.","bin":2,"fb":"Failing to adapt content: a mistake."},{"t":"Meta ads target 18 to 35 year olds on the island.","bin":1,"fb":"Cross-platform advertising: a tactic."}],"win":"Excellent. You can apply the principles and tactics, and spot the mistakes that break a unified campaign."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Email marketing with Mailchimp, Constant Contact and GetResponse","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Email marketing</b> is direct mail, done electronically. Email promotes products or services while developing relationships with potential customers or clients, instead of reaching them through the postal service. Promotion and relationship-building share the same inbox.</p>\n<p>In practice, messages go to a list of subscribers who have given permission (opted in).</p>\n<p>Try using <b>Constant Contact, Mailchimp or GetResponse</b> with a collaboration tool such as Google Drive to plan and run an email campaign. All three are <b>email service providers</b> (ESPs). They offer drag-and-drop templates, contact lists, sign-up forms, scheduling, automation and reports. Each has a free or trial option; check the current plan limits on the provider's website, as these change.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Known for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mailchimp</b></td><td>Beginner-friendly editor, free starter plan, audience tags and segments, landing pages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Constant Contact</b></td><td>Event and small-business focus, strong support, event registration tools</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GetResponse</b></td><td>Automation workflows, webinars and landing pages in one platform</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Step by step in Mailchimp</b> (the others follow a similar flow):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Audience</b>: create an audience and import contacts (CSV from Google Sheets) <i>only</i> if they gave permission. Add a <b>sign-up form</b> to the website or a QR code on a poster.</li>\n<li><b>Segment and tag</b>: group contacts, for example \"students\", \"parents\", \"past buyers\", so each group gets relevant messages.</li>\n<li><b>Create campaign</b>: choose Email, then Regular. Write a clear <b>subject line</b> (under about 50 characters) and <b>preview text</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Design</b>: pick a template; add logo, brand colours, one main image with <b>alt text</b>, short copy and one strong <b>call-to-action button</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Test</b>: send a test to yourself, check on phone and desktop, check every link. Try an <b>A/B test</b> of two subject lines on a small part of the list.</li>\n<li><b>Schedule</b>: send when the audience reads email (for example, Tuesday 7 p.m. for parents).</li>\n<li><b>Report</b>: check opens, clicks, unsubscribes and bounces, then improve the next email.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Rules and ethics</b>: get consent, include the sender's name and address, and always provide an <b>unsubscribe</b> link. Laws such as the EU's GDPR and the US CAN-SPAM Act set these standards, and Caribbean data protection laws are growing. <b>Automation</b> can send a welcome email the moment someone subscribes, or a reminder the day before an event.</p>\n<p>Maya at Island Pulse Media built a Mailchimp sign-up form for the Bridgetown Book Fair. Visitors scanned a QR code at the Independence Square stall, received an automatic welcome email with the programme, and a reminder on the morning of each author talk.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 answer, two marks often hide in <b>permission</b> and <b>measurement</b>: say how subscribers opt in, and name the metrics (open rate, click-through rate, unsubscribes) you would check.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Email marketing sends messages to people who agreed to receive them. Tools such as Mailchimp, Constant Contact and GetResponse help you build a list, design emails, schedule them and read reports. Always get permission, include your business details and an unsubscribe link, and test on a phone. A school fair in Grenada could collect sign-ups with a QR code.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Email's strength is <b>ownership</b>: the list belongs to the business, so messages are not filtered by a social media algorithm. Its weakness is attention, because inboxes are crowded, so the subject line, preview text and a single clear call to action carry most of the work. Segmentation makes email relevant; parents and students rarely need the same message. Automation is efficient but must be checked: a welcome email with a broken link is a new subscriber's first impression. Consent is not just politeness. Laws such as GDPR and CAN-SPAM set rules, and a bought list damages deliverability because many addresses bounce or report spam. Measure opens and clicks, but judge success by the action the campaign was designed to produce.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build an email campaign","intro":"<p>Keisha is training a new intern to run the Bridgetown Book Fair email campaign in Mailchimp. Put the steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Collect subscribers with a sign-up form and QR code, so everyone on the list opted in."},{"t":"Segment the audience with tags such as \"students\", \"teachers\" and \"parents\"."},{"t":"Create a Regular email campaign and write the subject line and preview text."},{"t":"Design the email from a template: logo, one image with alt text, short copy and a call-to-action button."},{"t":"Send a test email and check it on a phone and a desktop, clicking every link."},{"t":"Schedule the send for the time the segment usually reads email."},{"t":"Review the report: open rate, click-through rate, unsubscribes and bounces."}],"win":"Correct order. Permission, segmentation, creation, design, testing, scheduling and reporting is the professional email workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Who comes first?","p":"<p>You cannot email anyone until you have a list of people who agreed to hear from you.</p>"},{"h":"Test before you send","p":"<p>Once an email is sent it cannot be recalled, so checking happens before scheduling.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build an email campaign (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Collect subscribers who opted in."},{"t":"Design the email from a template."},{"t":"Send a test and check the links."},{"t":"Send and review the report."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build an email campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: eight steps, including an A/B test before scheduling. Put the Bridgetown Book Fair email workflow for the parents segment in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Add a sign-up form and QR code so every subscriber has opted in."},{"t":"Tag subscribers into segments: students, teachers and parents."},{"t":"Create a Regular campaign for the parents segment, with a subject line under about 50 characters."},{"t":"Design the email: logo, one image with alt text, short copy and one call-to-action button."},{"t":"Send a test to yourself and check it on a phone and a desktop, clicking every link."},{"t":"A/B test two subject lines on a small part of the segment."},{"t":"Schedule the winning version for Tuesday at 7 p.m., when parents read email."},{"t":"Review opens, clicks, unsubscribes and bounces, and note changes for the next email."}],"win":"Correct. Permission, segmentation, creation, design, testing, an A/B test, scheduling the winner and reviewing results."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Eight types of email campaign and six strategies","html":"<p>There are <b>eight types of email campaign</b>. Learn what each one is for.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Welcome email</b></td><td>Greets a new subscriber and sets expectations</td><td>\"Welcome to Sol Studio! Here is what to expect each month.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Newsletter</b></td><td>Regular news and useful content</td><td>A monthly round-up of events in St. George's</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Promotional email</b></td><td>Pushes an offer or sale</td><td>\"20% off all beach towels this weekend\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Special occasion email</b></td><td>Marks a birthday, holiday or anniversary</td><td>A Carnival greeting or a birthday discount</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Announcement email</b></td><td>Shares big news</td><td>\"Our new Montego Bay branch opens on 1 June\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lead nurturing email</b></td><td>A series that builds interest in someone not ready to buy</td><td>Three emails over two weeks about a dive course</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Transactional email</b></td><td>Triggered by an action: receipt, booking or password reset</td><td>\"Your ferry ticket to Anguilla is confirmed\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Abandoned cart email</b></td><td>Reminds a shopper who left items in an online cart</td><td>\"You left a rum cake gift box in your cart\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Six strategies for better email</b> (in short: permission, relevance, rhythm)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Build your own list</b>: grow an audience that wants to hear from you (never buy a list).</li>\n<li><b>Follow the law</b>: meet applicable consent and unsubscribe requirements.</li>\n<li><b>Mix up your messages</b>: balance promotional messages with useful content.</li>\n<li><b>Respect your subscribers</b>: honour preferences and make leaving straightforward.</li>\n<li><b>Follow a schedule</b>: create a dependable sending rhythm.</li>\n<li><b>Optimise for mobile</b>: make every message easy to read on a phone, where most Caribbean users read email.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mailchimp, Constant Contact and GetResponse can automate several types: a welcome email when someone subscribes, a transactional receipt after a purchase, and an abandoned cart reminder for an online shop.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often describes an email and asks which type it is. Look for the <b>trigger</b>: a new sign-up (welcome), a purchase or booking (transactional), items left behind (abandoned cart), or a date (special occasion).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are eight types of email campaign: welcome, newsletter, promotional, special occasion, announcement, lead nurturing, transactional and abandoned cart. Each has its own trigger: a sign-up, a date, a sale, news, a purchase or items left in a cart. Good strategy means building your own list, following the law, mixing messages, respecting subscribers, sending regularly and designing for phones, since most Caribbean readers use phones.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The types differ by <b>trigger and purpose</b>, and automation depends on knowing which is which. Welcome, transactional and abandoned-cart emails are triggered by a person's action, so they arrive at the moment of highest interest and usually perform best. Newsletters and promotional emails follow a schedule, so they compete for attention. Lead nurturing is a sequence, not a single email, designed for people who are interested but not ready to buy. A common mistake is overloading subscribers with promotions, which raises unsubscribe rates; the \"mix up your messages\" strategy exists to prevent this. Transactional emails are a quiet opportunity too: a ferry booking confirmation can include a helpful packing tip without becoming a sales pitch.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the email campaign type","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is setting up emails for the Punda Market online shop. Match each email to its type.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Welcome email","r":"Sent the moment someone subscribes, introducing the shop"},{"l":"Newsletter","r":"A monthly round-up of new stalls, recipes and market news"},{"l":"Promotional email","r":"\"Half price on all spices this Saturday only\""},{"l":"Special occasion email","r":"A birthday greeting with a small discount code"},{"l":"Announcement email","r":"\"We now deliver to Otrobanda and Westpunt\""},{"l":"Lead nurturing email","r":"A three-part series building interest in a cooking class"},{"l":"Transactional email","r":"An order receipt with the delivery time"},{"l":"Abandoned cart email","r":"\"You left two jars of hot sauce in your basket\""}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify all eight types of email campaign by their purpose and trigger.","tutor":[{"h":"What triggered it?","p":"<p>A sign-up, a purchase, items left behind or a date each trigger a different type.</p>"},{"h":"Selling or nurturing?","p":"<p>A one-off offer is promotional; a series that slowly builds interest is lead nurturing.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the email campaign type (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Welcome email","r":"Sent when someone first subscribes"},{"l":"Transactional email","r":"An order receipt"},{"l":"Abandoned cart email","r":"A reminder about items left in a basket"},{"l":"Promotional email","r":"A weekend sale offer"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the email campaign type (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each card describes the TRIGGER or timing rather than the content. Match each email to its type.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Welcome email","r":"Sent the moment a visitor scans the QR code at the stall and subscribes"},{"l":"Newsletter","r":"Arrives on the first Monday of every month with recipes and market news"},{"l":"Promotional email","r":"Pushes a 48-hour offer on hot sauce bundles"},{"l":"Special occasion email","r":"Arrives on a customer's birthday with a small gift code"},{"l":"Announcement email","r":"Tells everyone that a new stall from Bonaire has joined the market"},{"l":"Lead nurturing email","r":"Email two of three about a cooking class, for people who clicked but did not book"},{"l":"Transactional email","r":"Confirms a payment and gives the delivery time"},{"l":"Abandoned cart email","r":"Sent when a shopper leaves two jars in the basket and closes the page"}],"win":"Excellent. You identified every type from its trigger, which is how automation is set up in a real email tool."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Planning a campaign step by step","html":"<p>The course outline asks you to <b>plan and create</b> a promotional campaign. Island Pulse Media plans every campaign in six steps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Objective</b>: what must change? Make it <b>SMART</b> (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound). Weak: \"Get more people to know about us.\" Strong: \"Sell 300 tickets to the youth film night by 15 March.\"</li>\n<li><b>Target audience</b>: who exactly? Age, location, interests, devices, habits. A <b>persona</b> helps: \"Aaliyah, 17, Frangipani Hill Academy student, on TikTok and WhatsApp daily, saves money for events.\"</li>\n<li><b>Key message</b>: one clear idea, plus a <b>call to action</b>. \"Local stories, big screen. Get your ticket for BZD 15.\"</li>\n<li><b>Channels</b>: choose the platforms and practices that reach this audience (social, WhatsApp, email, influencer, guerrilla, radio, print) and say why.</li>\n<li><b>Content calendar</b>: what goes out, where, when and who is responsible.</li>\n<li><b>Budget</b>: costs for ads, printing, influencer fees, tools, prizes, plus a small contingency.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then <b>launch</b>, <b>monitor</b> and <b>evaluate</b> against the objective.</p>\n<p><b>Sample content calendar</b> (two weeks before Belize City Youth Film Night):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Date</th><th>Channel</th><th>Content</th><th>Owner</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Mar</td><td>Instagram and TikTok</td><td>15-second teaser Reel (9:16) with hashtag #SXMFilmNight</td><td>Malik</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 Mar</td><td>Email (Mailchimp)</td><td>Announcement to students and teachers segment, \"Get tickets\" button</td><td>Keisha</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Mar</td><td>School campuses</td><td>Guerrilla: chalk film-reel trail to a QR code poster (with permission)</td><td>Zara</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8 Mar</td><td>TikTok</td><td>Nano-influencer behind-the-scenes video, labelled #ad</td><td>Keisha</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12 Mar</td><td>WhatsApp Status and broadcast</td><td>\"3 days left\" countdown graphic</td><td>Keisha</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14 Mar</td><td>Facebook Events</td><td>Reminder for parents with venue map</td><td>Maya</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Sample budget</b> (BZD; BZD 2 = USD 1): boosted posts BZD 180, poster printing BZD 120, influencer fee BZD 150, chalk and props BZD 40, contingency (10%) BZD 49. Total BZD 539.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question says \"plan a promotional campaign\", use the six headings. Examiners can then see every element, and you will not forget the objective or the budget, which are the two most often left out.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Plan every campaign in six steps: set a SMART objective, describe the target audience, write one key message with a call to action, choose the channels, make a content calendar and set a budget. Then launch, watch the results and compare them with the objective. A youth football tournament in St. Kitts could aim to sell 400 tickets by its opening day.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The six steps depend on each other, so a weak early step undermines everything after it. A vague objective makes the budget impossible to justify and the results impossible to judge. The key message should be short enough to survive every channel: if it cannot fit on a WhatsApp Status, it is probably too complicated. The content calendar turns strategy into accountable action, because every item has a date, a channel and an owner. Budgets need a contingency, often around 10%, because reprints, extra boosts or a rain date are common. Finally, plan the evaluation before launch: decide which KPI will prove the objective was met and how it will be tracked, such as UTM links or unique codes.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the SMART objectives","intro":"<p>A group's campaign plan for Belize Reef Clean-Up lists several \"objectives\". Only some are SMART.</p>","q":"Select every objective that is SMART (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound).","lines":["Recruit 120 volunteers aged 15 to 25 through Instagram and WhatsApp by 30 April.","Make the clean-up really popular.","Grow the Instagram account from 800 to 1,200 followers during the six-week campaign.","Get everyone in Belize to care about the reef.","Achieve a 25% open rate on the three volunteer emails sent in April.","Post a lot of content on social media.","Collect 200 pledge sign-ups on the campaign landing page before Earth Day (22 April).","Be the best environmental campaign ever."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Correct. Each SMART objective names a number, an audience or channel, and a deadline, so success can be measured.","miss":"Check each line for a number and a deadline. \"Popular\", \"everyone\", \"a lot\" and \"the best\" cannot be measured or achieved.","tutor":[{"h":"The number test","p":"<p>Can you tell, with a figure, whether it was achieved?</p>"},{"h":"The calendar test","p":"<p>Is there a date or a time period?</p>"},{"h":"The reality test","p":"<p>Could a small team actually achieve it?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the SMART objectives (Foundation)","q":"Select the SMART objectives.","lines":["Recruit 120 volunteers by 30 April.","Make the clean-up popular.","Collect 200 sign-ups before 22 April.","Post a lot online."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Both have a number and a deadline.","miss":"Look for a number and a date."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the SMART objectives (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: some of these have numbers but still fail a SMART test, because they are unrealistic or have no deadline. Belize Reef Clean-Up is planning its campaign.</p>","q":"Select every objective that is fully SMART (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound).","lines":["Increase volunteer sign-ups from 40 to 120 among 15 to 25 year olds by 30 April.","Gain 1 million Instagram followers in two weeks.","Reach 5,000 people on TikTok.","Achieve a 25% open rate on the three volunteer emails sent in April.","Collect 200 pledges on the campaign landing page before Earth Day (22 April).","Improve our social media presence by the end of the campaign.","Sell 300 T-shirts through the online shop and school stalls by 15 April to fund the clean-up.","Raise awareness of the reef among everyone in Belize this year.","Increase website visits."],"correct":[0,3,4,6],"win":"Correct. Each SMART objective has a number, a realistic target, a link to the campaign and a deadline.","miss":"Check every line against all five letters. One million followers in two weeks is not achievable, \"reach 5,000\" and \"increase website visits\" have no deadline, and \"presence\" or \"awareness among everyone\" cannot be measured."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Measuring campaign effectiveness","html":"<p>A campaign is only successful if you can <b>prove</b> it met its objective. Professionals use <b>analytics</b>: the data collected by platforms such as Meta Business Suite (Facebook and Instagram Insights), TikTok Analytics, YouTube Studio, LinkedIn page analytics, Mailchimp reports and Google Analytics for websites.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">A <b>KPI</b> (key performance indicator) is a measurable value chosen in advance to show whether a campaign is achieving its objective.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th><th>How it is calculated</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reach</b></td><td>Number of <b>unique people</b> who saw the content</td><td>Counted by the platform</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Impressions</b></td><td>Total number of <b>times</b> the content was displayed (one person can see it several times)</td><td>Counted by the platform; always equal to or greater than reach</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Engagement</b></td><td>Interactions: likes, comments, shares, saves, clicks</td><td>Sum of interactions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Engagement rate</b></td><td>How actively the audience responded</td><td>(engagements ÷ reach) × 100 (some tools divide by impressions or followers; state which)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Click-through rate (CTR)</b></td><td>Share of viewers who clicked the link</td><td>(clicks ÷ impressions) × 100; for email, clicks ÷ emails delivered</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Conversions</b></td><td>The goal action completed: a ticket bought, a form signed, an app installed</td><td>Count; <b>conversion rate</b> = (conversions ÷ clicks) × 100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open rate</b> (email)</td><td>Share of delivered emails that were opened</td><td>(opens ÷ delivered) × 100</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> The Belize City Youth Film Night Reel had a reach of 8,000 people, 12,000 impressions, 640 engagements and 360 link clicks, which led to 90 ticket sales.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Engagement rate = 640 ÷ 8,000 × 100 = <b>8%</b></li>\n<li>CTR = 360 ÷ 12,000 × 100 = <b>3%</b></li>\n<li>Conversion rate = 90 ÷ 360 × 100 = <b>25%</b></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other useful tools: <b>UTM links</b> (tagged URLs that show which post brought each visitor in Google Analytics), unique <b>discount codes</b> per influencer, and <b>QR code scan counts</b> for posters. These connect offline guerrilla and print activity to online results.</p>\n<p>Beware <b>vanity metrics</b>: likes and follower counts look good but may not move the objective. If the goal was 300 tickets, conversions are the KPI that matters.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Show your working in calculation questions: formula, substitution, answer with % sign. Then <b>interpret</b> it: \"A CTR of 3% means the post attracted clicks, but the next step is to improve the ticket page because...\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Measure whether a campaign worked using analytics. Reach is how many different people saw it; impressions count every time it was shown. Engagement rate is engagements divided by reach, times 100. Click-through rate is clicks divided by impressions, times 100. Conversions are the goal actions, such as tickets bought. A concert campaign in Jamaica should judge success by tickets sold, not likes.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Metrics form a <b>funnel</b>: people see the content (reach and impressions), react (engagement), click (CTR) and complete the goal (conversions). Where the funnel narrows tells you what to fix. High reach with a low CTR suggests a weak call to action; a high CTR with few conversions points to the landing page, such as slow loading or a confusing form. Always state the denominator, because engagement rate can be calculated by reach, impressions or followers and the results differ. Attribution is the hardest part: UTM links, unique discount codes and QR scan counts connect each sale to its source. Vanity metrics such as likes feel encouraging but can hide a campaign that is failing its real objective.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Metrics workout","intro":"<p>Keisha is preparing a report for the Antigua Sailing Week Fan Zone campaign. Use the formulas to answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A post reached 5,000 people and received 400 engagements. What is the engagement rate (by reach)?","options":["8%","4%","80%","12.5%"],"answer":0,"fb":"400 ÷ 5,000 × 100 = 8%. Engagement rate compares interactions with the people reached."},{"text":"An ad had 20,000 impressions and 300 clicks. What is the CTR?","options":["6.7%","0.15%","1.5%","15%"],"answer":2,"fb":"300 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = 1.5%. CTR divides clicks by impressions."},{"text":"300 people clicked through and 45 bought tickets. What is the conversion rate?","options":["15%","6.7%","30%","45%"],"answer":0,"fb":"45 ÷ 300 × 100 = 15%. Conversion rate divides goal actions by clicks."},{"text":"One person saw the same Story three times. How does this affect reach and impressions?","options":["Reach 1, impressions 3","Reach 1, impressions 1","Reach 3, impressions 3","Reach 3, impressions 1"],"answer":0,"fb":"Reach counts unique people (1); impressions count every display (3)."},{"text":"The objective is \"sell 500 fan zone passes by 20 April\". Which is the most important KPI?","options":["passes sold (conversions)","impressions","number of likes","follower count"],"answer":0,"fb":"The KPI must match the objective. Likes and followers are vanity metrics here; sales are the conversion that matters."},{"text":"Keisha wants to know which of three influencers produced the most sales. The best tracking method is...","options":["give each influencer a unique discount code or UTM link","count each influencer's followers","ask customers to guess","compare the influencers' profile photos"],"answer":0,"fb":"Unique codes or tagged links tie each sale to the source that produced it."}],"win":"Strong analysis. You calculated engagement rate, CTR and conversion rate, separated reach from impressions, and chose a KPI that matches the objective.","tutor":[{"h":"Which number goes on the bottom?","p":"<p>Engagement rate: reach. CTR: impressions. Conversion rate: clicks.</p>"},{"h":"Unique or total?","p":"<p>One metric counts people, the other counts views.</p>"},{"h":"Back to the objective","p":"<p>A KPI is chosen to measure the objective, not whatever looks impressive.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Metrics workout (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Reach 1,000, engagements 100. Engagement rate?","options":["1%","10%"],"answer":1,"fb":"100 ÷ 1,000 × 100 = 10%."},{"text":"Impressions 10,000, clicks 200. CTR?","options":["20%","2%"],"answer":1,"fb":"200 ÷ 10,000 × 100 = 2%."},{"text":"Unique people who saw a post are counted by...","options":["reach","impressions"],"answer":0,"fb":"Reach counts unique people."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Metrics workout (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder calculations and interpretation for the Antigua Sailing Week Fan Zone report. Show your working on paper.</p>","items":[{"text":"Reach 12,000; engagements 900. What is the engagement rate by reach?","options":["0.75%","13.3%","7.5%","75%"],"answer":2,"fb":"900 ÷ 12,000 × 100 = 7.5%."},{"text":"Impressions 40,000; clicks 600. What is the CTR?","options":["15%","6.7%","1.5%","0.15%"],"answer":2,"fb":"600 ÷ 40,000 × 100 = 1.5%."},{"text":"600 clicks led to 48 pass sales. What is the conversion rate?","options":["12.5%","0.8%","48%","8%"],"answer":3,"fb":"48 ÷ 600 × 100 = 8%."},{"text":"An email was delivered to 2,000 subscribers; 500 opened it and 80 clicked. What is the email CTR (clicks ÷ delivered)?","options":["16%","25%","8%","4%"],"answer":3,"fb":"80 ÷ 2,000 × 100 = 4%. (Clicks ÷ opens would be 16%, a different measure.)"},{"text":"A post has a reach of 10,000 and 25,000 impressions. On average, how many times did each person see it?","options":["10","25","2.5","0.4"],"answer":2,"fb":"25,000 ÷ 10,000 = 2.5 views per person."},{"text":"Reel A had 20,000 impressions at a 2% CTR. Reel B had 8,000 impressions at a 6% CTR. Which produced more clicks?","options":["They are equal","Reel A, with 400 clicks","Reel B, with 480 clicks","It cannot be calculated"],"answer":2,"fb":"A: 20,000 × 0.02 = 400. B: 8,000 × 0.06 = 480."},{"text":"A campaign has a 6% CTR but only 2 sales from 600 clicks. What should Keisha investigate FIRST?","options":["The hashtag","The colour of the Reel","The follower count","The ticket page, because people click but do not buy"],"answer":3,"fb":"The funnel narrows after the click, so the problem is on the page people land on."},{"text":"The same post shows a 5% engagement rate in one report and 2% in another. What is the most likely reason?","options":["The engagements changed","Different denominators: impressions are larger than reach, so the rate is smaller","Reach is always larger than impressions","One report is simply wrong"],"answer":1,"fb":"Always state whether you divided by reach, impressions or followers."}],"win":"Excellent. You calculated accurately, compared channels and read the funnel to find where a campaign was failing."}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Running the Carnival app campaign","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has built an Antigua Carnival companion app with the parade route, J'ouvert times and Carnival City show line-ups. Keisha must promote it with a small budget. Make the decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The client says, \"Just tell everybody about the app.\" What does Keisha do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Start posting immediately on every platform she can think of.","to":"bad1","fb":"Without an objective or audience, there is no way to choose channels or judge success."},{"t":"Agree a SMART objective: 3,000 downloads from residents and visitors in the four weeks before the Carnival Tuesday parade.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. A measurable objective with a deadline tells the team what success looks like."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The main audience is 16 to 35 year old Carnival lovers on the island, plus visitors arriving through V. C. Bird International Airport. Which channel mix does she choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A LinkedIn article and a printed brochure only.","to":"bad2","fb":"LinkedIn and brochures do not reach young Carnival fans, and nothing links them to a download."},{"t":"TikTok and Instagram Reels with a branded hashtag, WhatsApp Status graphics, a nano-influencer partnership, and QR code posters at Carnival City.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong cross-platform mix. It reaches young locals where they are and links offline posters to downloads."},{"t":"Buy a list of 10,000 email addresses and send them all a message.","to":"bad3","fb":"Bought lists break consent rules and damage the brand. Email must go to people who opted in."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Two weeks in, the Reels have huge reach and lots of likes, but downloads are only 600. What does Keisha do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Celebrate the likes and report that the campaign is a success.","to":"bad4","fb":"Likes are a vanity metric here. The objective is downloads, and it is far behind."},{"t":"Check analytics: CTR is low, so she adds a clear \"Download free\" call to action, a link sticker and a UTM link, and tests a new caption.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. She looked past vanity metrics to the conversion problem and changed one thing at a time."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>An influencer offers to post about the app without labelling it as paid, \"so it looks natural\". How does Keisha respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Insist on clear disclosure (#ad or the paid partnership label) in the written agreement.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. Disclosure is honest, protects the brand and is required in many jurisdictions."},{"t":"Accept, because unlabelled posts get more engagement.","to":"bad5","fb":"Hiding a paid relationship misleads followers and can lead to complaints and loss of trust."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign success.</b> Downloads pass 3,000 before the Carnival Tuesday parade. A Grade One answer would explain why: a SMART objective, a channel mix matched to the audience, measurement focused on conversions, and ethical influencer practice.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Scattered effort.</b> Posts go everywhere, nobody knows who they are for, and the client cannot tell whether the money was well spent. Try again and start with a SMART objective.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong audience.</b> Professionals on LinkedIn are not the target, and brochures cannot be tapped to download an app. Try again and match channels to the audience.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Spam complaints.</b> Many emails bounce, recipients report spam and the sending account is suspended. Try again and use permission-based channels.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Objective missed.</b> The campaign ends with 1,100 downloads and a disappointed client. Try again and measure what the objective actually asks for.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Trust damaged.</b> Followers discover the post was paid and accuse the app of being fake. Try again and choose honest disclosure.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Plan before posting","p":"<p>Objective and audience come before channels.</p>"},{"h":"Measure the goal","p":"<p>Which metric shows downloads, not just attention?</p>"},{"h":"Ethics","p":"<p>Consent for email and disclosure for influencers are not optional.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Keisha is promoting a Carnival app to young locals.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which plan should she choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post anything, anywhere, and hope for the best.","to":"bad","fb":"No objective and no audience focus."},{"t":"A download target with a deadline, TikTok and WhatsApp content, and QR posters at Carnival City.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Measurable, matched to the audience and linked to downloads."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You planned with an objective, audience and channels.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Without a plan, results cannot be measured. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Running the Carnival app campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher version: Keisha has <b>XCD 1,500</b> and four weeks before the Carnival Tuesday parade, and the client wants a report proving what worked. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The client says, \"Just tell everybody about the app.\" What does Keisha do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree a target of \"lots of downloads\".","to":"ok1","fb":"There is a goal, but no number, deadline or tracking."},{"t":"Agree a SMART objective (3,000 downloads in four weeks, at least half from residents), the audience, and how downloads will be tracked by channel.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. The objective, the audience and the tracking plan are fixed before any money is spent."},{"t":"Start posting on every platform she can think of.","to":"bad1","fb":"Without an objective or audience, there is no way to choose channels or judge success."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How should she split the XCD 1,500?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Spend the whole budget on one macro-influencer based abroad.","to":"ok2","fb":"Big reach, but mostly not local, and nothing is left for other channels."},{"t":"Buy a list of 10,000 email addresses and message them all.","to":"bad2","fb":"Bought lists break consent rules and damage the brand."},{"t":"TikTok and Reels boosts with a branded hashtag (XCD 500), two local nano-influencers with unique codes (XCD 500), QR posters at Carnival City and at airport arrivals (XCD 350), free WhatsApp Status, and a contingency (XCD 150).","to":"n3","fb":"A balanced, trackable mix that reaches locals and visitors and keeps a contingency."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Two weeks in: the Reels have a reach of 60,000 but a CTR of only 0.5%. There are 600 downloads, and 90% of them came through the influencers' codes. What does Keisha do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Move boost money from the weak Reels to the influencers who are converting, add a \"Download free\" link sticker to the Reels, and keep tracking with UTM links.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. She follows the data and fixes the weak part of the funnel."},{"t":"Stop all paid activity to save money.","to":"ok3","fb":"It saves money, but it also cuts the channel that is working."},{"t":"Report success to the client because the reach is huge.","to":"bad3","fb":"Reach is a vanity metric here. The objective is downloads."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>One influencer offers to post without labelling it as paid, \"so it looks natural\". How does Keisha respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Insist on clear disclosure (#ad or the paid partnership label) in the written agreement.","to":"n5","fb":"Correct. Honest disclosure protects followers, the influencer and the brand."},{"t":"Accept, because unlabelled posts get more engagement.","to":"bad4","fb":"Hiding a payment misleads followers and can break the law."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>After the parade, the client asks for a report. What does Keisha present?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Screenshots of the most-liked posts.","to":"ok4","fb":"Attractive, but it does not show whether the objective was met."},{"t":"Results against the objective: downloads by channel, conversion rates and cost per download, what worked and what did not, and recommendations for next year.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. The report proves value and guides the next campaign."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One campaign.</b> Downloads pass 3,000, more than half from residents, and the report shows which channel produced each download and at what cost. Keisha set a SMART objective, built a trackable mix, followed the data and kept the campaign honest.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Hard to judge.</b> The campaign runs, but nobody can say whether it succeeded or what to repeat. A partial success: set a number, a deadline and a way to track it.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong reach.</b> The post is seen widely abroad, but few residents download the app. A partial success: match the influencer to the audience and spread the budget.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Stalled.</b> Downloads flatten at 900 once the influencers stop. A partial success: cut what is failing, not what is working.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Pretty but unproven.</b> The client likes the posts but cannot see the value for money. A partial success: report against the objective.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Scattered effort.</b> Posts go everywhere and nobody can tell whether the money was well spent. Try again and start with a SMART objective.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Spam complaints.</b> Emails bounce, recipients report spam and the sending account is suspended. Try again with permission-based channels.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Objective missed.</b> The campaign ends with 1,100 downloads and a disappointed client. Try again and measure what the objective asks for.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Trust damaged.</b> Followers discover the post was paid and accuse the app of being fake. Try again with honest disclosure.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan a promotional campaign","genre":"Structured response: planning a digital promotional campaign","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Guavaberry Gardens café in Marigot has launched a mobile ordering website. The owner wants more young residents (aged 16 to 30) in Sint Maarten and Saint-Martin to use it. Her marketing budget for one month is XCG 900 (about USD 500).</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a campaign plan for the café that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>states ONE SMART objective and describes the target audience;</li><li>selects and <b>justifies</b> THREE industry practices or channels (for example a social platform, influencer, guerrilla, email or WhatsApp marketing);</li><li>outlines a short content calendar and how the budget will be used;</li><li>explains TWO metrics or KPIs you would use to measure effectiveness, with a formula for one of them.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Objective: By [date], the café will ... The target audience is ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, ... because ... Second, ... because ... Third, ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Week 1: ... Week 2: ... The budget of XCG 900 will cover ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would measure ... calculated as ... This shows ... I would also track ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My objective has a number and a deadline.","I described the audience by age, location and habits.","I named three practices or channels and justified each with \"because\", using the correct terms (viral, social networking, guerilla, email, influencer, cross-platform).","I gave a simple calendar and showed how the money is spent.","I named two metrics and gave a formula for one.","I linked everything back to Guavaberry Gardens."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Campaign planning (objective, audience, calendar, budget)","marks":5,"look":["by [0-9]|by the end|within|weeks?|month","[0-9]+ (order|customer|download|visit|sign)","aged|16|young|student|resident","week 1|week one|calendar|schedul|monday|friday","budget|xcg|usd|\\$"],"min":3,"strong":"Your plan covers the key planning elements clearly. Next step: make sure every calendar item names a channel and a date.","weak":"Use the planning headings: a SMART objective with a number and date, a described audience, a short calendar (what, where, when) and a budget breakdown."},{"criterion":"Selection and justification of practices","marks":5,"look":["tiktok|instagram|facebook|reel|social network","influencer|creator|credib","guerr?illa|qr|pop.?up|chalk|surprise","whatsapp|email|mailchimp|newsletter|sms","viral|share|word of mouth","cross.?platform|consistent|cohesive|brand","because|so that|this means|which"],"min":3,"strong":"You chose suitable practices and justified them by audience. Strong answers also explain how each one leads to an online order.","weak":"Name three specific practices (for example TikTok Reels, a nano-influencer, QR code posters) and justify each with \"because\" linked to young local users."},{"criterion":"Measuring effectiveness","marks":4,"look":["reach|impression","engagement rate|engagement","ctr|click.?through","conversion|orders? placed|sales","divid|÷|/|x 100|× 100|per cent|%"],"min":2,"strong":"You named relevant metrics and showed how to calculate one. Make sure you say which metric is the main KPI and why.","weak":"Name two metrics such as CTR and conversions, and give a formula, e.g. CTR = clicks ÷ impressions × 100."}],"model":"<p><b>(a) Objective and audience.</b> By 31 May, Guavaberry Gardens will receive 250 orders through its mobile ordering website from customers aged 16 to 30. The target audience is students and young workers on both sides of the island who use TikTok, Instagram and WhatsApp daily, eat out with friends after school or work, and prefer ordering on their phones.</p>\n<p><b>(b) Practices.</b> First, <b>TikTok and Instagram Reels</b>, because this audience spends most of its social time watching short vertical video. Reels will show drinks being made, with a \"Order on your phone\" call to action and a link sticker. Second, <b>influencer marketing</b> with two local nano-influencers (a food creator and a student athlete) whose followers are young residents, because their recommendations are trusted and each will use a unique discount code, labelled #ad. Third, a <b>guerrilla</b> element: a pop-up sampling table at the Marigot waterfront on Saturday with QR code cups that open the ordering page, because it surprises people and turns a physical moment into an online order.</p>\n<p><b>(c) Calendar and budget.</b> Week 1: teaser Reel and WhatsApp Status countdown. Week 2: influencer videos go live. Week 3: Saturday pop-up and Reels of the event. Week 4: \"last week\" offer by WhatsApp broadcast to opted-in customers. Budget: boosted Reels XCG 300, influencer fees XCG 300, QR cups and samples XCG 210, contingency (10%) XCG 90. Total XCG 900.</p>\n<p><b>(d) Measurement.</b> The main KPI is <b>conversions</b>: orders placed through the site, tracked with UTM links and discount codes so we know which channel produced each order. I would also track <b>click-through rate</b>, calculated as clicks ÷ impressions × 100. If a Reel has 10,000 impressions and 250 clicks, the CTR is 2.5%. A low CTR would show the call to action needs improving, while a high CTR but few orders would point to a problem on the ordering page.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Use the six planning headings as signposts and justify every channel with \"because\" plus an audience fact. In the measurement part, the examiner wants to see that the KPI matches the objective, so if the objective is orders, the KPI is conversions, not likes.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Plan a promotional campaign (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: planning a promotional campaign, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b> a SMART objective for the café, with a number and a date. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> the target audience: give their age and ONE habit. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE social platform and <b>explain</b> why it suits this audience. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE other practice (influencer, guerrilla, WhatsApp or email marketing) and <b>describe</b> how it would work for the café. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE thing you would measure to see if the campaign worked, and say why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> By ..., Guavaberry Gardens will get ... orders on its website.<br><b>(b)</b> The audience is people aged ... who ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would use ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would also use ... For example, ...<br><b>(e)</b> I would measure ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My objective has a number and a date.","I gave the audience's age and one habit.","I named a platform and said why it suits the audience.","I described one more practice for the café.","I named one thing to measure and said why."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"SMART objective","marks":2,"look":["[0-9]+","by|before|within|end of|week|month|may|june|july"],"min":2,"strong":"A number and a deadline.","weak":"Include a number and a date, for example \"250 website orders by 31 May\"."},{"criterion":"Target audience","marks":2,"look":["16|30|young|student|teen|aged","phone|tiktok|instagram|whatsapp|friends|school|work|order"],"min":2,"strong":"Age and a habit.","weak":"Give the age range (16 to 30) and one habit, such as ordering on their phones."},{"criterion":"Social platform","marks":2,"look":["tiktok|instagram|facebook|snapchat|reel","because|young|video|use|spend"],"min":2,"strong":"A suitable platform with a reason.","weak":"Choose TikTok or Instagram and say that young people spend their time watching short videos there."},{"criterion":"Second practice","marks":2,"look":["influencer|creator|guerr?illa|pop.?up|qr|whatsapp|email|newsletter","would|post|send|set up|give|share"],"min":2,"strong":"A second practice described for the café.","weak":"For example: a local food creator posts a video about the café and labels it #ad."},{"criterion":"Measurement","marks":2,"look":["order|sale|conversion|click|ctr|reach|engagement","because|so|shows"],"min":2,"strong":"A sensible measure with a reason.","weak":"Measure the number of website orders, because that is the objective."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> By 31 May, Guavaberry Gardens will get 250 orders on its mobile ordering website.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The audience is people aged 16 to 30 who live on both sides of the island and order food on their phones.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I would use TikTok, because young people spend a lot of time watching short videos there, so a Reel of drinks being made would catch their attention.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I would also use a local influencer. For example, a young food creator from Marigot posts a video about the café, labelled #ad, with a discount code.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I would measure the number of orders on the website, because the objective is about orders, not likes.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the choice and 1 for the reason or detail. The most common error is an objective with no number or no date. Encourage \"because\" in every part. Students ready to move on should add a second channel and a simple budget, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Plan a promotional campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Extended response: planning a promotional campaign, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Guavaberry Gardens café in Marigot has launched a mobile ordering website. The owner wants more young residents (aged 16 to 30) on both sides of the island, Sint Maarten and Saint-Martin, to use it. Her marketing budget for one month is <b>XCG 900</b> (about USD 500). Her customers speak English and French, and some speak Spanish. A competitor launched a similar ordering app last month. The owner also wants to know, at the end, which channel worked best.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a full campaign plan that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>sets ONE SMART objective plus ONE supporting KPI, and describes the audience as a persona <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>selects</b> and <b>justifies</b> FOUR industry practices as one unified cross-platform strategy, including ONE guerrilla element, and explains how consistency is kept across languages and platforms <i>(7 marks)</i>;</li><li>sets out a four-week content calendar with owners, and a budget that adds up to XCG 900 with a contingency <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>explains</b> how effectiveness will be measured: the funnel metrics, formulas with a worked example, how each order will be traced to its channel, and what you would change if the CTR is high but orders are low <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>explains</b> the ethical and legal responsibilities in your plan <i>(3 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":450,"max":800,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Objective: by ..., ... Supporting KPI: ... Persona: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Practice 1 ... because ... Practice 2 ... Practice 3 ... Guerrilla element ... Consistency: ...<br><b>(c)</b> Week 1 ... Week 2 ... Week 3 ... Week 4 ... Budget: ... + ... + ... + contingency ... = XCG 900<br><b>(d)</b> Funnel ... CTR = ... e.g. ... Conversion rate = ... Tracking ... If CTR is high but orders are low ...<br><b>(e)</b> Consent ... disclosure ... permission ...</p>","checklist":["My objective is fully SMART and my supporting KPI fits it.","My persona includes age, location, language, habits and devices.","I justified four practices as one unified strategy, including a guerrilla element, and explained bilingual consistency.","My calendar has dates, channels and owners, and my budget adds up to exactly XCG 900 with a contingency.","I gave formulas with a worked example, a tracking method per channel and a funnel diagnosis.","I covered consent, disclosure and permission.","I used the competitor and the two sides of the island in my reasoning."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Objective, KPI and persona","marks":4,"look":["[0-9]+ (order|customer)","by [0-9]|by the end|31|30 ","kpi|ctr|click|conversion|repeat","aged|1[6-9]|2[0-9]|persona|french|english|spanish"],"min":3,"strong":"A SMART objective, a fitting KPI and a detailed, bilingual persona.","weak":"Give a number and a date for orders, add one KPI that supports it, and describe a persona with age, location, languages and habits."},{"criterion":"Unified strategy of four practices","marks":7,"look":["tiktok|instagram|reel","influencer|creator","guerr?illa|pop.?up|qr|chalk|surprise","whatsapp|email|sms|newsletter","cross.?platform|consistent|same logo|brand guide|hashtag","french|english|bilingual|language","because|so that","competitor|rival|other app|stand out"],"min":6,"strong":"Four well-justified practices that work as one campaign, with a clear bilingual consistency plan and a response to the competitor.","weak":"Choose four practices, justify each with an audience fact, explain how logo, colours, hashtag and offer stay the same in English and French, and say how the campaign stands out from the competitor."},{"criterion":"Calendar and budget","marks":5,"look":["week 1|week one","week 4|week four|final week","owner|responsible|keisha|malik|zara|maya|manager","contingency","900"],"min":4,"strong":"A dated, owned calendar and an accurate budget that totals XCG 900.","weak":"Lay out four weeks with channels and owners, then itemise the budget with a contingency so it totals exactly XCG 900."},{"criterion":"Measurement and diagnosis","marks":6,"look":["reach|impression","ctr|click.?through","conversion","÷|/|divid|× 100|x 100","utm|code|qr scan","landing page|ordering page|checkout|form|load"],"min":5,"strong":"A complete funnel with formulas, a worked example, attribution for every channel and a clear diagnosis.","weak":"Explain the funnel, give CTR and conversion formulas with numbers, say how each order is traced (UTM links, codes, QR scans) and what a high CTR with low orders tells you."},{"criterion":"Ethics and law","marks":3,"look":["consent|opt.?in|opted","disclos|#ad|paid partnership","permission|permit|approval"],"min":3,"strong":"All three responsibilities explained and built into the plan.","weak":"Cover consent for WhatsApp and email, disclosure for influencers and permission for the guerrilla activity."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Objective:</i> by 30 June, Guavaberry Gardens will receive 300 orders through its mobile ordering website from customers aged 16 to 30, at least 100 of them from the Dutch side. <i>Supporting KPI:</i> a click-through rate of at least 2% on paid Reels, because enough people must reach the ordering page for the order target to be possible. <i>Persona:</i> \"Léa, 19, a student in Marigot who works weekend shifts in Philipsburg; speaks French at home and English at work; uses TikTok, Instagram and WhatsApp daily on a mid-range Android phone with prepaid data; meets friends after class and likes to order ahead to skip queues.\"</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>1. TikTok and Instagram Reels</i> (the core channel), because Léa's group spends most of its social time on short vertical video. Reels show drinks being made and end on one call to action, \"Order ahead in 30 seconds\", with a link sticker. <i>2. Two local nano-influencers</i>, one French-speaking from Marigot and one English-speaking from Philipsburg, because trusted local voices reach both sides and their unique discount codes prove what they produce. <i>3. WhatsApp Business</i> for opted-in customers, with Status updates of the day's specials and a weekly broadcast, because WhatsApp is the main way young people on the island communicate. <i>4. Guerrilla pop-up:</i> a Saturday sampling table on the Marigot waterfront with QR-code cups that open the ordering page, because a surprise in a busy place gives people a reason to film and share it and links the offline moment to an online order. <i>Consistency:</i> one brand guide fixes the logo, colours, the hashtag #GuavaGo and the offer; every asset is made in English and French versions from the same template, so the campaign looks identical in both languages. <i>Against the competitor:</i> the campaign stresses what is local and personal, the café's own recipes and bilingual service, rather than competing on discounts alone.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <i>Week 1:</i> teaser Reels in both languages and WhatsApp Status countdown (owner: social media lead). <i>Week 2:</i> influencer videos go live with codes and #ad labels (owner: marketing lead). <i>Week 3:</i> Saturday pop-up, then Reels of the event (owners: manager and video lead). <i>Week 4:</i> \"last week\" offer by WhatsApp broadcast, plus boosting the best-performing Reel (owner: social media lead). <i>Budget:</i> Reel boosts XCG 250; influencer fees XCG 300 (XCG 150 each); pop-up cups, samples and QR stickers XCG 180; bilingual design and printing of QR flyers XCG 80; contingency (10%) XCG 90. Total XCG 900.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The funnel runs from reach and impressions to engagement, clicks and orders. <i>CTR</i> = clicks ÷ impressions × 100: a Reel with 15,000 impressions and 300 clicks has a CTR of 2%, meeting the KPI. <i>Conversion rate</i> = orders ÷ clicks × 100: if those 300 clicks produce 45 orders, the conversion rate is 15%. <i>Attribution:</i> each Reel link carries a UTM tag, each influencer has a unique code and each pop-up cup has its own QR code, so every order is traced to its channel and the owner learns which channel worked best. <i>Diagnosis:</i> if the CTR is high but orders are low, the Reels are working and the problem lies on the ordering page, so I would test it on a budget Android phone for loading speed, shorten the form and check that the French version is complete.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> WhatsApp and email messages go only to people who opted in, with a clear way to stop receiving them. Both influencer agreements require #ad or the paid partnership label, because hiding payment misleads followers and breaks advertising rules in many places. The pop-up needs permission from the Marigot authorities for use of the waterfront, and food samples must meet hygiene rules.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 4 + 7 + 5 + 6 + 3). Check that the budget totals exactly XCG 900 and that the KPI matches the objective. Grade One plans work as a single campaign: one brand guide across languages, a tracking method for every channel, and a measurement section that can diagnose a weak point in the funnel. Reward plans that use the stimulus details: both sides of the island, three languages and the competitor.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U9","title":"Trends and developments in visual and interactive design","objectives":["Discuss the six factors that influence trends and developments in visual and interactive design: commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment, with examples.","Describe current interface trends: dark mode, neumorphism, glassmorphism, micro-interactions, immersive UI and motion design, and evaluate when each is appropriate.","Explain how AI-assisted design tools such as Adobe Firefly, Midjourney and DALL-E are changing design work, and discuss the ethical and copyright issues they raise.","Explain uses of augmented reality (AR) and virtual reality (VR) in visual design, with Caribbean applications.","Use research tools such as Google Trends, Behance, Awwwards, Dribbble and Pinterest Trends to discover and track design trends."],"videos":[{"title":"UI design trends explained","q":"UI design trends explained glassmorphism neumorphism dark mode"},{"title":"Micro-interactions in UX design","q":"micro interactions UX design examples explained"},{"title":"AI image generators and copyright","q":"AI generated images copyright ethics explained for designers"},{"title":"How to use Google Trends","q":"how to use Google Trends tutorial for beginners"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Commoditisation, pervasiveness and affordability","html":"<p>The field of visual and interactive design is dynamic, and new technologies and approaches continue to shape its landscape. Factors which influence trends and developments include: <b>commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Commoditisation</b>: in the realm of visual interactive design, commoditisation refers to the process by which design elements or services become <b>standardised, widely available, and perceived as interchangeable or common</b>.</p>\n<p>Canva offers thousands of ready-made flyers, WordPress and Wix sell themes for a small fee, and free UI kits are shared on Figma Community. A basic event poster for a Port of Spain fete is no longer rare or expensive. <b>Drawback</b> (from the handout): as more designers and tools enter the market, certain design features or patterns may become <b>ubiquitous, losing their uniqueness and value</b>. At Island Pulse Media, Zara tells clients, \"Anyone can make a Canva flyer. We design a brand that only you can own.\" So professionals compete on originality, strategy and brand storytelling.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Pervasiveness of technology</b>: the widespread and all-encompassing presence of technology in various aspects of human life and society. In recent decades technology has become an integral part of our daily existence, impacting how we communicate, work, learn and entertain ourselves.</p>\n<p>There are <b>five ways</b> this shapes design:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Used in every step</b>: technology is involved from planning to final product. Common tools include Canva, Figma and Adobe Creative Suite.</li>\n<li><b>Designs for all devices</b>: work must function on phones, tablets, computers and more (think of the kiosks and flight screens at an airport). <b>Responsive design</b> is now a basic expectation.</li>\n<li><b>Supports accessibility and inclusion</b>: tools help make designs usable for people with disabilities, with features such as screen readers, contrast checkers and scalable text.</li>\n<li><b>Enables constant improvement</b>: designers can test, track and update based on user behaviour, and feedback can be gathered instantly through analytics.</li>\n<li><b>Always connected</b>: cloud-based tools allow real-time collaboration, so teams can design and update projects from anywhere (Maya in Kingston and a client in Curaçao can edit the same Figma file).</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Affordability</b>: creating designs that are not only aesthetically pleasing and functional but also <b>cost-effective</b>. It involves finding ways to deliver high-quality design solutions <b>without exceeding budget constraints</b>.</p>\n<p>Free and low-cost tools (GIMP, Inkscape, Blender, the free tiers of Figma and Canva) and good phone cameras help a small business in Grenada or a craft shop on Bay Street in Nassau get professional work within a small budget. <b>Effect on trends:</b> more creators, more experimentation, and more phone-shot vertical video.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" means more than define. For each factor give the <b>definition</b>, a <b>specific example</b> and its <b>effect on design trends</b>. For commoditisation, always add the <b>drawback</b>: patterns become ubiquitous and lose their uniqueness and value.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Six factors shape design trends: commoditisation, pervasiveness, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment. Commoditisation means designs become standard and cheap, like ready-made Canva flyers. Pervasiveness means technology is everywhere in daily life, so designs must work on every device. Affordability means good design within a small budget, helped by free tools such as GIMP and Blender, used even by a small shop in Nevis.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>These factors explain <b>why</b> trends happen, so strong answers show cause and effect. Commoditisation lowers the price of basic design, which pushes professionals to compete on strategy, originality and brand storytelling, but it also makes popular patterns so common that they lose their value. Pervasiveness raises expectations: once people use polished apps every day, a clumsy local website looks untrustworthy. Affordability widens who can create, which is why phone-shot vertical video and indie games have boomed across the Caribbean. The factors also interact: cheap phones (affordability) put screens everywhere (pervasiveness), which spreads templates (commoditisation). In a \"discuss\" answer, define each factor, give a specific example and explain its effect on trends, including at least one drawback.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Pervasiveness of technology: the five points","intro":"<p>There are five ways the pervasiveness of technology affects design. Match each point to the Caribbean example that shows it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Used in every step","r":"Keisha plans in Canva, Zara designs in Figma and Malik finishes the video in Adobe Premiere Pro"},{"l":"Designs for all devices","r":"The Nevis ferry site must work on a phone at the dock, a tablet and a hotel laptop"},{"l":"Supports accessibility and inclusion","r":"A contrast checker and scalable text are added to a Barbados government form"},{"l":"Enables constant improvement","r":"Analytics show users leave the checkout page, so the team updates it the same week"},{"l":"Always connected","r":"Designers in Bridgetown and Kingston edit the same cloud file in real time"}],"win":"Well done. You can explain all five points on the pervasiveness of technology, each with an example.","tutor":[{"h":"Planning to final product","p":"<p>Which point is about technology being involved at every stage of the job?</p>"},{"h":"Data and feedback","p":"<p>Analytics let designers test, track and update. Which point is that?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Pervasiveness (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Designs for all devices","r":"Works on phones, tablets and computers"},{"l":"Always connected","r":"Cloud tools for real-time teamwork"},{"l":"Enables constant improvement","r":"Update the design using analytics"},{"l":"Supports accessibility and inclusion","r":"Screen readers and scalable text"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Pervasiveness of technology: the five points (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: the examples are subtler and none of them repeats the wording of the notes. Match each point to the example that shows it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Used in every step","r":"An idea is sketched on a tablet, refined in Figma and delivered by cloud link, with no paper at any stage"},{"l":"Designs for all devices","r":"A ticket page is rebuilt to work on a watch notification, a phone, a laptop and a stadium kiosk"},{"l":"Supports accessibility and inclusion","r":"A screen reader announces every button in a Belize bank app because each has a text label"},{"l":"Enables constant improvement","r":"An A/B test shows the green button gets more taps, so it goes live for everyone the next day"},{"l":"Always connected","r":"A designer in Anguilla updates a shared component and the change appears at once in her colleague's file in Aruba"}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised all five points from examples that do not repeat the notes, which shows real understanding."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Accessibility and adoption","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Accessibility</b>: in interactive design, accessibility refers to creating digital interfaces and experiences that can be easily and effectively used by <b>individuals with disabilities</b>. It is essential to ensure that everyone, regardless of their abilities, can access and interact with digital content and applications.</p>\n<p><b>Why accessibility matters</b> (three reasons):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It helps <b>more people</b> use your website, app or product.</li>\n<li>It is <b>inclusive and fair</b> design.</li>\n<li>It is often <b>required by law</b> (for example, WCAG standards: the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Some considerations for accessibility</b> from the handout:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Consideration</th><th>What it means</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Inclusive design</b></td><td>A growing focus on designs accessible to a diverse range of users, including those with disabilities: colour contrast, text readability and navigational structures.</td><td>An electricity company's outage page with high-contrast text and large, clearly labelled menu buttons.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cross-platform compatibility</b></td><td>Making sure interactive designs are accessible across different platforms: desktops, tablets and mobile devices.</td><td>A Jamaican bank app that works equally well on a budget Android phone and a desktop browser.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>User-centric design</b></td><td>User research and testing have become widespread; designers prioritise understanding user needs and preferences to create more effective and enjoyable experiences.</td><td>Island Pulse Media tests a menu with older customers at a Speightstown restaurant before launch.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Design thinking</b></td><td>Design thinking methods are now applied not only in traditional design but in many business processes to foster innovation.</td><td>A Trinidad credit union uses empathise, define, ideate, prototype and test to redesign loan forms.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A strong answer can also mention <b>access to technology</b> itself: as fibre and 4G/5G coverage spread across the region (check the latest ITU figures for your territory), designers create mobile-first, data-light experiences.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Adoption</b>: the process and rate at which people and organisations take up a new technology or style until it becomes normal.</p>\n<p>Everett Rogers' <b>diffusion of innovations</b> model describes innovators, early adopters, early majority, late majority and laggards. A design idea becomes a trend when it moves from early adopters (designers on Dribbble) to the majority, often because a <b>major platform adopts it</b>. Dark mode became mainstream after Apple and Google built it into iOS 13 and Android 10 in 2019. Low adoption has the opposite effect: confusing styles, such as some early neumorphic buttons, fade.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the three \"why it matters\" reasons and the four considerations by name. A Paper 02 answer that says \"inclusive design, for example colour contrast and text readability\" uses the exact terms and shows precise knowledge.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Accessibility means designing so that everyone, including people with disabilities, can use a product: good contrast, readable text, captions and clear menus. It matters because more people can use it, it is fair and it is often required by law (WCAG). Adoption is how quickly people take up a new idea until it is normal. Dark mode spread quickly once phones built it in, in Barbados as everywhere else.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Accessibility and adoption often work together. A style that excludes users struggles to be widely adopted: neumorphism looked fashionable, but its low-contrast buttons were hard to see, so it faded. Rogers' diffusion model explains the spread: a design idea moves from innovators and early adopters to the majority, and the tipping point is often a major platform building it in, which is why designers watch Apple, Google and Microsoft closely. Accessibility also includes <b>access to technology</b>: where many people use budget phones on prepaid data, mobile-first, data-light design is an accessibility decision. A Grade One answer uses the exact considerations (inclusive design, cross-platform compatibility, user-centric design, design thinking) and links them to the users in the scenario.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which accessibility consideration?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing the Lagoon Kayak Tours app. Choose the accessibility consideration that each change shows.</p>","items":[{"text":"Jerome raises the colour contrast of the grey text and enlarges the font for readability.","options":["Design thinking","Inclusive design","Cross-platform compatibility"],"answer":1,"fb":"Colour contrast, text readability and navigational structure are the inclusive design points in the handout."},{"text":"The booking screen is checked on a tablet, a laptop and two different phones.","options":["Inclusive design","User-centric design","Cross-platform compatibility"],"answer":2,"fb":"Making the design accessible across desktops, tablets and mobile devices is cross-platform compatibility."},{"text":"Before redesigning, the team interviews tour guides and visitors about what frustrates them.","options":["Cross-platform compatibility","User-centric design","Commoditisation"],"answer":1,"fb":"User research and testing to understand needs and preferences is user-centric design."},{"text":"The tour company uses empathise, define, ideate, prototype and test to improve its whole booking process, not just the app.","options":["Design thinking","Inclusive design","Affordability"],"answer":0,"fb":"The handout notes that design thinking is now applied to business processes to foster innovation."},{"text":"The owner asks why accessibility matters. Which reason is correct?","options":["It is often required by law, for example WCAG standards","It makes the app cheaper to host","It removes the need for testing"],"answer":0,"fb":"The three reasons are: more people can use it, it is inclusive and fair, and it is often required by law (WCAG)."}],"win":"Excellent. You can name and apply the four accessibility considerations and the reasons accessibility matters, using the correct terms.","tutor":[{"h":"Devices or people?","p":"<p>If the change is about working on different devices, think one consideration. If it is about research with people, think another.</p>"},{"h":"Beyond the screen","p":"<p>Which consideration is used for whole business processes as well as design?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Accessibility consideration (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Better colour contrast and readable text is...","options":["inclusive design","design thinking"],"answer":0,"fb":"Contrast and readability are inclusive design."},{"text":"Testing on phones, tablets and desktops is...","options":["user-centric design","cross-platform compatibility"],"answer":1,"fb":"Different platforms: cross-platform compatibility."},{"text":"Interviewing users about their needs is...","options":["commoditisation","user-centric design"],"answer":1,"fb":"Understanding user needs is user-centric design."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which accessibility consideration? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: four options each, and two questions link accessibility to other factors. Jerome is reviewing apps for several clients.</p>","items":[{"text":"The team adds a skip-to-content link, full keyboard navigation and labelled form fields.","options":["Design thinking","Affordability","Commoditisation","Inclusive design"],"answer":3,"fb":"Making navigation and forms usable for people with disabilities is inclusive design."},{"text":"The booking site is rebuilt so it works in an older browser on a school computer as well as on new phones.","options":["Adoption","Cross-platform compatibility","User-centric design","Empowerment"],"answer":1,"fb":"Working across different devices and platforms is cross-platform compatibility."},{"text":"The team watches five older visitors try to book a tour and notes where they hesitate.","options":["Commoditisation","User-centric design","Inclusive design","Pervasiveness"],"answer":1,"fb":"User research and testing to understand real needs is user-centric design."},{"text":"A bank in Trinidad uses empathise, define, ideate, prototype and test to redesign how customers open accounts, both in branches and online.","options":["Cross-platform compatibility","Inclusive design","Affordability","Design thinking"],"answer":3,"fb":"Applying the design thinking process to a whole business process is design thinking."},{"text":"A tour company says accessibility is optional because \"few disabled tourists visit\". Which is the STRONGEST reply?","options":["They are right; it is optional","It is needed only on government sites","It helps more people use the app, it is fair, and it is often required by law (WCAG)","It makes the app load faster"],"answer":2,"fb":"All three reasons apply, and many visitors (older people, people in bright sun) benefit too."},{"text":"A design style becomes common only after Apple and Google build it into their systems. Which factor does this show?","options":["Adoption","Inclusive design","Affordability","Commoditisation"],"answer":0,"fb":"Uptake reaching the majority after major platforms adopt an idea is adoption."},{"text":"Making an app data-light so visitors on roaming plans can use it extends accessibility to...","options":["motion design","neumorphism","commoditisation","access to technology"],"answer":3,"fb":"A strong answer adds access to technology itself, such as data cost and coverage, to the definition."}],"win":"Excellent. You can apply all four considerations, defend accessibility with its three reasons and link it to adoption and access to technology."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Designing for empowerment","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Empowerment</b>: in visual and interactive design, empowerment involves creating interfaces and experiences that <b>empower users</b>, enabling them to <b>accomplish their goals, make informed decisions, and have a positive impact on their lives</b>.</p>\n<p>There are <b>eight key considerations</b> for incorporating empowerment into visual and interactive design. Here they are with an example from the Jamaica Small Business Hub app, which helps market vendors in Kingston and Montego Bay register and sell online.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Consideration</th><th>In practice</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. User-centric design</b></td><td>The app is built around what vendors actually need, found through interviews at the market.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Intuitive navigation</b></td><td>Users find Sell, Orders and Help without instructions; menus are where they expect them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Clarity and simplicity</b></td><td>One task per screen, plain words, no clutter.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Empowering visuals</b></td><td>Photos and icons show real local vendors and help users understand at a glance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Feedback and progress indicators</b></td><td>\"Step 2 of 4\" bars, loading spinners and \"Saved\" messages tell users what is happening.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Continuous improvement</b></td><td>The team reviews feedback and analytics and releases updates regularly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. User control and autonomy</b></td><td>Users can undo, edit, cancel, choose settings (such as dark mode or language) and delete their data.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>8. Inclusive language</b></td><td>Respectful, plain wording that does not exclude anyone; options in English, Dutch, French or Spanish.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Effect on trends:</b> empowerment explains why progress bars, undo buttons, onboarding tips, personalisation settings and plain-language microcopy are now normal. It also extends to <b>creators</b>: no-code tools (Wix, Glide, Thunkable) and social platforms let a soca artist in Trinidad or a craft maker in Dominica design, publish and sell without an agency, so many trends now start with individuals on TikTok rather than big studios.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A memory hook for the eight: <b>\"Users Navigate Clearly, Envisioning Feedback, Change, Control, Inclusion\"</b>. In an exam, name a consideration, then show it in the scenario: \"Feedback and progress indicators: a 'Step 2 of 4' bar tells the vendor how much is left, so they feel in control.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Empowerment means designing so users can reach their goals, make good decisions and feel in control. The eight considerations include clear navigation, simple screens, progress bars, undo buttons, settings users can choose and plain, inclusive language. A vendor in Castries using a selling app should always know which step she is on and be able to fix her mistakes.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Empowerment is about <b>confidence and control</b>, and it explains many interface conventions that now feel normal: progress bars, undo, onboarding tips, language choices and plain-language microcopy. Each consideration answers a user worry. Progress indicators answer \"how much is left?\", undo answers \"what if I make a mistake?\" and inclusive language answers \"is this meant for me?\". Empowerment applies to creators too: no-code tools and social platforms let individuals publish and sell without an agency, which is why many trends now start with independent creators. A common weakness is listing the eight considerations without showing them in a scenario. Name one, show it in the product and explain how it makes the user more capable.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Which features empower users?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing a draft of the Coronation Market Pay app in Kingston against the eight empowerment considerations.</p>","q":"Select every feature that EMPOWERS users.","lines":["A progress bar shows \"Step 2 of 3\" during sign-up.","Users can undo a payment request within one minute.","Menu labels use plain words such as \"Pay\", \"Sell\" and \"Help\".","The home screen shows twelve buttons, three banners and a pop-up advert at once.","Error messages say only \"Error 4031\".","Users can choose English, Dutch, French or Spanish.","The settings page is hidden three menus deep with no label.","A \"Saved\" message appears when a product listing is stored."],"correct":[0,1,2,5,7],"win":"Correct. Progress indicators, undo (user control), clear labels (clarity and intuitive navigation), language choice (inclusive language) and \"Saved\" feedback all empower users.","miss":"Check again. Clutter breaks clarity and simplicity, a bare error code gives no useful feedback, and a hidden settings page breaks intuitive navigation and user control.","tutor":[{"h":"Can the user act confidently?","p":"<p>An empowering feature helps the user know what is happening, decide, or change their mind.</p>"},{"h":"Use the eight","p":"<p>Test each line against clarity, navigation, feedback, control and inclusive language.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Empowering features (Foundation)","q":"Select the features that empower users.","lines":["A progress bar shows how many steps are left.","An undo button lets users fix a mistake.","Error messages show only a code number.","The screen is full of adverts and pop-ups."],"correct":[0,1],"win":"Yes. Progress indicators and user control empower users.","miss":"Bare error codes and clutter leave users confused, not empowered."},
 "up": {"title":"Which features empower users? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: Keisha reviews the next version of Coronation Market Pay. Some features look helpful but quietly take control away from users.</p>","q":"Select every feature that EMPOWERS users.","lines":["A form saves progress automatically, so users can come back later without losing data.","An error message says \"Something went wrong.\" with no next step.","Users can delete their account and data from Settings in two taps.","Onboarding forces users through ten screens before they can see the app.","Icons show real local vendors and are paired with text labels.","Users are signed up for promotional texts automatically, with no way to opt out.","Before money is sent, a summary screen shows the amount, the recipient and the fee.","Buttons are in Dutch only, although most users speak English.","A \"Step 3 of 4\" indicator appears, with an Edit link on each completed step.","The menu changes order every week, based on an algorithm."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,8],"win":"Correct. Saved progress, control over personal data, empowering visuals with labels, an informed-decision summary and progress with edit links all put users in charge.","miss":"Look again. A vague error, forced onboarding, automatic opt-ins, a single language that excludes most users and a constantly shifting menu all reduce users' control or understanding."}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which factor is driving this?","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media keeps a notebook of changes she sees in design across the Caribbean. Sort each note by the factor it best illustrates.</p>","main":"Which factor is the main driver?","bins":["Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology","Affordability","Accessibility"],"items":[{"t":"Cheaper mid-range phones with good cameras lead to more vertical, phone-shot ads.","bin":2,"fb":"Lower device cost (affordability) changed the style of video content."},{"t":"A Belize student makes a professional animation with Blender, which costs nothing.","bin":2,"fb":"Free software lowers cost, so more people can create."},{"t":"Smartwatches, smart TVs and car dashboards all need interfaces readable at a glance.","bin":1,"fb":"Screens everywhere in daily life illustrate pervasiveness."},{"t":"A government website adds captions, alt text and a high-contrast mode for visually impaired users.","bin":3,"fb":"Designing for people with disabilities is accessibility."},{"t":"Free public Wi-Fi in a town square lets more residents use an online heritage guide.","bin":3,"fb":"Improving access to technology widens who can use digital content, which a strong answer adds to the accessibility definition."},{"t":"The same airline booking flow must work on a phone, a tablet, a laptop and an airport kiosk.","bin":1,"fb":"Technology on every kind of screen drives responsive, multi-device design."},{"t":"Hundreds of near-identical restaurant menus in Barbados use the same free template.","bin":0,"fb":"Standardised, interchangeable design products are a sign of commoditisation."},{"t":"Basic website themes now sell for a few dollars and buyers choose mainly on price.","bin":0,"fb":"When buyers see little difference and choose on price, the product has been commoditised."}],"win":"Well sorted. You separated standardisation, everywhere-technology, lower cost and access for all, which is the precision \"discuss\" questions need.","tutor":[{"h":"Standard or cheap?","p":"<p>If buyers see the products as interchangeable, think one factor. If the point is that more people can now afford to create, think another.</p>"},{"h":"Two meanings","p":"<p>Accessibility covers both access to technology and design for people with disabilities.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which factor? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Many menus use the same cheap template.","bin":0,"fb":"Standard and interchangeable: commoditisation."},{"t":"Apps must work on phones, TVs and kiosks.","bin":1,"fb":"Screens everywhere: pervasiveness."},{"t":"Free software lets students make animations.","bin":2,"fb":"Lower cost: affordability."},{"t":"A website adds captions and alt text.","bin":3,"fb":"Design for all abilities: accessibility."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which factor is driving this? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: all six factors are now in play. Sort each of Zara's notes by the factor that MAINLY drives it.</p>","main":"Which factor is the main driver?","bins":["Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology","Affordability","Accessibility","Adoption","Empowerment"],"items":[{"t":"Swipe-to-delete became expected once major email apps used it.","bin":4,"fb":"A behaviour becoming normal through widespread uptake: adoption."},{"t":"No-code tools let a craft maker in Dominica build and run her own online shop.","bin":5,"fb":"Empowerment extends to creators who can now publish and sell without an agency."},{"t":"Hundreds of salons use the same free booking-page theme.","bin":0,"fb":"Standardised, interchangeable design: commoditisation."},{"t":"A farmers' app gives clear steps, undo and a language choice, so growers can sell without help.","bin":5,"fb":"Helping users reach their goals with confidence: empowerment."},{"t":"A data-light version of a news app is built for readers on prepaid plans.","bin":3,"fb":"Widening access to the technology itself is part of accessibility."},{"t":"Logos sell for a few dollars on marketplaces, so agencies now sell brand strategy instead.","bin":0,"fb":"When basic design becomes a cheap commodity, professionals compete on something else."},{"t":"A cruise line's booking flow must work on phones, laptops, kiosks and smartwatches.","bin":1,"fb":"Screens in every part of life: pervasiveness."},{"t":"A student in Belize animates a short film using free software.","bin":2,"fb":"Lower cost lets more people create: affordability."},{"t":"Dark mode spread to most apps after phone operating systems built it in.","bin":4,"fb":"Major platforms tipped the idea into the majority: adoption."},{"t":"A ministry website adds captions, alt text and a high-contrast mode.","bin":3,"fb":"Design for people with disabilities: accessibility."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated all six factors, including two that are easy to confuse: affordability (cost) and accessibility (who can use it)."}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the six factors","intro":"<p>Match each factor to the Caribbean example that best illustrates it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Commoditisation","r":"Logo templates are so common that a Kingston agency competes on original brand strategy instead"},{"l":"Pervasiveness of technology","r":"Flight information, payments and hotel check-in at the airport all happen on screens"},{"l":"Affordability","r":"A Guyanese teen edits music videos on a low-cost phone with a free app"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"A St. Lucia tourism site meets WCAG contrast rules and captions every video"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"Dark mode becomes expected in every app once major phone systems include it"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"A Dominica bill-pay app shows clear steps, progress bars and an undo button so customers pay confidently on their own"}],"win":"Excellent. You can link each of the six factors to a concrete example, which is the core of a Paper 02 \"discuss\" answer.","tutor":[{"h":"Uptake over time","p":"<p>When something becomes normal because many people take it up, which factor is that?</p>"},{"h":"Goals and decisions","p":"<p>Which factor is about interfaces that help users accomplish their goals and make informed decisions?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the factors (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Affordability","r":"Free editing apps on cheap phones"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Captions and high contrast for all users"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"Dark mode becomes expected everywhere"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"Clear steps and undo help users reach their goal"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the six factors (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each card describes the EFFECT of a factor on a trend. Match each factor to its effect.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Commoditisation","r":"So many sites use the same template that a distinctive hand-drawn style suddenly stands out"},{"l":"Pervasiveness of technology","r":"Designs must now be readable at a glance on car dashboards and smartwatches"},{"l":"Affordability","r":"Cheap phones with good cameras made vertical, phone-shot adverts the norm"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Neumorphism faded partly because its low-contrast buttons were hard to see"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"A gesture feels natural only after millions of people have learned it in popular apps"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"Progress bars and undo buttons became standard because they help users feel in control"}],"win":"Excellent. You linked each factor to the way it changes trends, which is the \"effect\" step in a discuss answer."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Current trends: dark mode, neumorphism and glassmorphism","html":"<p>The course outline names six current interface trends. The first three are about <b>surface and colour</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Dark mode</b> uses light text and elements on a dark background. Benefits: less glare at night, can save battery on OLED screens, a sleek look for media and gaming apps, and user choice. Good practice: use dark grey (not pure black) backgrounds, slightly reduce saturation of bright colours, keep contrast high enough for WCAG, and let users switch with a toggle or follow the system setting. Weakness: long passages of light-on-dark text can be tiring for some readers.</p>\n<p><b>Neumorphism</b> (\"new skeuomorphism\", popular from about 2019 to 2020) makes elements look softly extruded from or pressed into the background, using one background colour with a light shadow on one side and a dark shadow on the other. It looks soft and tactile, but it has <b>low contrast</b>: buttons can be hard to see and their state (pressed or not) unclear. That accessibility problem limited its <b>adoption</b>, a good example of a factor shaping a trend.</p>\n<p><b>Glassmorphism</b> imitates frosted glass: semi-transparent panels with a background blur, a thin light border and colourful shapes behind. It gives depth and layering. Apple's macOS Big Sur and Microsoft's Fluent Design popularised it, and Apple's \"Liquid Glass\" look (announced in 2025) pushed glass effects further. Risks: text over a busy background can lose contrast, and heavy blur effects can slow older phones.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 110\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three button styles: flat dark mode, neumorphic with two shadows, glassmorphic with blur\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\"/>\n<rect x=\"130\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"14\"/>\n<path d=\"M134 74 Q180 82 226 74\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n<path d=\"M134 16 Q180 8 226 16\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 4\"/>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"12\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"275\" cy=\"35\" r=\"12\"/><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"58\" r=\"9\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"92\">Dark mode</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"49\">Play</text>\n<text x=\"180\" y=\"92\">Neumorphism</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"49\">Play</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"92\">Glassmorphism</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"49\">Play</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Trend</th><th>Best for</th><th>Main risk</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dark mode</td><td>Streaming, music, night reading, dashboards</td><td>Poor contrast if greys are too close</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neumorphism</td><td>Small decorative controls, smart-home panels</td><td>Low contrast, unclear button states</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glassmorphism</td><td>Overlays, cards, music players over artwork</td><td>Text legibility over busy backgrounds; performance</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media designed the Antigua Carnival app with a dark mode for J'ouvert morning (people use it before sunrise) and glass panels over parade photos, but kept all text on solid backgrounds so it stays readable in sunlight on the parade route in St. John's.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"evaluate\" a trend, give a benefit, a limitation and a judgement: \"Glassmorphism adds depth, but blurred panels reduce contrast, so it suits overlays more than forms.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Three surface trends: dark mode uses light text on a dark background, which can save battery on some screens and reduce glare at night. Neumorphism makes buttons look softly raised using gentle shadows, but they can be hard to see. Glassmorphism looks like frosted glass, with blurred, see-through panels. A music app in Trinidad might use dark mode with glass panels over album art.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each trend carries a specific accessibility risk, and evaluating it means weighing the look against usability. Dark mode works best with dark grey rather than pure black and slightly desaturated colours, because pure white text on black can seem to glow and smear for some readers. Neumorphism's weakness is built in: it relies on subtle shadows of one colour, so contrast is low and pressed states are unclear, which is why it never became mainstream. Glassmorphism adds depth, but text over a busy, blurred background can lose contrast, and blur effects cost processing power on older phones. A sensible approach, as in Maya's Carnival app, is to use a trend where it helps and keep important text on solid, high-contrast backgrounds.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Spot the trend","intro":"<p>Jerome is reviewing app designs for Caribbean Beats music app. Identify the trend or evaluate it.</p>","items":[{"text":"A now-playing panel is semi-transparent with the album art blurred behind it and a thin white border.","options":["dark mode","neumorphism","glassmorphism"],"answer":2,"fb":"Translucency, background blur and a light border are the signs of glassmorphism."},{"text":"Buttons look softly raised from a pale grey background, with a light shadow top-left and a dark shadow bottom-right.","options":["glassmorphism","flat design","neumorphism"],"answer":2,"fb":"Two soft shadows on a same-colour background create the extruded look of neumorphism."},{"text":"Users can switch to light text on a dark grey background for late-night listening.","options":["immersive UI","dark mode","motion design"],"answer":1,"fb":"Light content on a dark background, chosen by the user, is dark mode."},{"text":"Testers with low vision cannot tell whether a neumorphic play button is pressed. What is the main problem?","options":["low contrast and unclear state","too many colours","file size"],"answer":0,"fb":"Neumorphism relies on subtle shadows, so contrast is low and state changes are hard to see."},{"text":"Which is the BEST practice for a dark mode palette?","options":["pure black with fully saturated neon text","dark grey background with softened colours and good contrast","white text on mid-grey"],"answer":1,"fb":"Dark grey reduces harsh contrast and smearing, while softened colours stay readable and pass contrast checks."}],"win":"Great eye. You identified dark mode, neumorphism and glassmorphism by their visual features and judged their accessibility risks.","tutor":[{"h":"Blur or shadow?","p":"<p>Frosted blur behind a panel is one trend; two soft shadows on the same colour is another.</p>"},{"h":"Think about users","p":"<p>Which trend gives the weakest contrast between a control and its background?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the trend (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Frosted, see-through panels with blur are...","options":["neumorphism","glassmorphism"],"answer":1,"fb":"Blur and translucency: glassmorphism."},{"text":"Soft raised buttons with two shadows are...","options":["neumorphism","dark mode"],"answer":0,"fb":"Extruded look: neumorphism."},{"text":"Light text on a dark background is...","options":["motion design","dark mode"],"answer":1,"fb":"That is dark mode."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the trend (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: these questions ask you to evaluate the trends, not just name them.</p>","items":[{"text":"A banking app is adding dark mode. Which palette is BEST?","options":["A white background with grey text","A #121212 background, off-white text and a slightly desaturated brand blue","Mid-grey text on dark grey","Pure black with fully saturated neon blue text"],"answer":1,"fb":"Dark grey, off-white and softened colours keep contrast high without glare or smearing."},{"text":"Why did neumorphism never become mainstream?","options":["It was too colourful","Its low contrast made buttons and their states hard to see","It was banned","It needed VR headsets"],"answer":1,"fb":"An accessibility weakness limited its adoption."},{"text":"A glass-effect login panel sits over a busy Carnival photo and the text is hard to read. What is the BEST fix?","options":["Add more photos behind it","Make the text thinner","Increase the blur and add a darker tint behind the text, or put the form on a solid panel","Remove the border"],"answer":2,"fb":"More blur and a tint, or a solid panel, restore contrast for the text."},{"text":"On an older phone, a glass-effect music player stutters. Why?","options":["Dark mode uses too much battery","Background blur is demanding to render","Neumorphism needs more memory","The album art is too small"],"answer":1,"fb":"Real-time blur costs processing power, so heavy glass effects can slow older devices."},{"text":"Dark mode saves battery mainly on which kind of screen?","options":["Projectors only","Printers","OLED screens, where black pixels are switched off","All LCD screens equally"],"answer":2,"fb":"On OLED screens, dark pixels use little or no power."},{"text":"Which judgement about glassmorphism is BEST balanced?","options":["It is always inaccessible","It should be used everywhere","It adds depth to overlays, but busy backgrounds reduce contrast, so forms belong on solid backgrounds","It has no effect on performance"],"answer":2,"fb":"A benefit, a limitation and a judgement: that is evaluation."}],"win":"Excellent. You evaluated each trend by its benefit, its accessibility and performance risks, and how to use it well."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Micro-interactions, immersive UI and motion design","html":"<p>The other three trends are about <b>movement, feedback and experience</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Micro-interactions</b> are small, single-purpose moments of feedback in an interface: a heart that pops when you like a post, a toggle that slides, a button that shakes when a password is wrong, a pull-to-refresh spinner.</p>\n<p>Dan Saffer's model describes four parts: <b>trigger</b> (the user taps or the system detects something), <b>rules</b> (what happens), <b>feedback</b> (what the user sees, hears or feels, such as a vibration) and <b>loops and modes</b> (what happens if it repeats). Micro-interactions confirm actions, prevent errors and add personality. Example: the Paramaribo bus app shows a tiny bus moving along a line while it looks for the next bus to Lelydorp.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Immersive UI</b> surrounds the user in the experience, so the interface almost disappears: full-screen video, edge-to-edge photos, 3D scenes, scroll-driven storytelling, spatial interfaces in AR and VR headsets.</p>\n<p>Examples: a full-screen vertical story feed; a tourism site where scrolling takes you from Grand Anse Beach up to the Grand Etang rainforest; product pages with 360-degree views. Risks: heavy files on slow connections, motion sickness, and content that screen-reader users cannot follow unless designed carefully.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Motion design</b> is the purposeful use of animation in interfaces and graphics: transitions between screens, animated logos, kinetic typography, animated illustrations and Lottie files.</p>\n<p>Good motion has a <b>purpose</b>: it shows where something came from, draws attention to a change, or explains a process. Keep UI animations short (roughly 200 to 500 milliseconds), use easing (slow in, slow out) so movement feels natural, and respect the <b>reduced-motion</b> setting on phones for users who feel unwell with movement. Tools: Adobe After Effects, Figma Smart Animate, Rive, LottieFiles, and CSS transitions.</p>\n<pre><code>/* A simple CSS micro-interaction with reduced-motion support */\n.like { transition: transform 0.2s ease-out; }\n.like:active { transform: scale(1.25); }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .like { transition: none; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Malik at Island Pulse Media animated the Carnival app's splash screen as a 1.5-second burst of feathers in the festival colours, but switched it to a simple fade when reduced motion is turned on.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Link trends back to the factors. Motion design spread because devices became <b>affordable</b> and powerful enough to animate smoothly, and reduced-motion settings show <b>accessibility</b> shaping how the trend is used.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Micro-interactions are tiny moments of feedback, like a heart popping when you tap \"like\". Immersive UI surrounds you in the experience with full-screen video, 3D scenes or scrolling stories. Motion design uses purposeful animation, such as smooth screen transitions. Keep animations short and let users turn motion down. A bus app in Guyana might animate a small bus while it searches for times.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>All three trends are justified only when movement has a <b>purpose</b>: confirming an action, showing where something came from or explaining a process. Saffer's model makes micro-interactions designable: a trigger, rules, feedback, and loops and modes. Timing matters: roughly 200 to 500 milliseconds feels responsive, while longer animations make an app feel slow. Easing makes movement natural, linking interface design to the animation principle of slow in and slow out. Immersive UI is powerful for storytelling and tourism, but it can exclude users on slow connections or with motion sensitivity, and screen readers may not follow it. Respecting the reduced-motion setting shows accessibility shaping a trend, a strong link back to the six factors.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Which trend fits the brief?","q":"Dominica Waitukubuli Trail app needs to confirm instantly when a hiker taps \"Save trail for offline use\", so that users know the download has started without opening a new screen. Which design trend is the BEST fit?","options":[{"t":"Neumorphism: make the save button look softly raised.","fb":"A visual style does not provide feedback, and neumorphic buttons often make the pressed state harder to see."},{"t":"A micro-interaction: the save icon fills with a small progress animation and the phone gives a short vibration.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A micro-interaction gives small, immediate feedback for a single action, exactly what the brief describes."},{"t":"Dark mode: switch the whole app to a dark theme.","fb":"Dark mode changes the colour scheme; it does not tell the user that their action worked."},{"t":"Immersive UI: a full-screen 3D fly-through of the whole trail.","fb":"Impressive, but it does not confirm the save, and it would use a lot of data on a trail with weak signal."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Read the need","p":"<p>The key words are \"confirm instantly\" and \"without opening a new screen\".</p>"},{"h":"Size matters","p":"<p>The trend you want is small and tied to one action.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which trend fits? (Foundation)","q":"A tiny animation confirms that a button tap worked. This is...","options":[{"t":"glassmorphism","fb":"Glassmorphism is a visual surface style."},{"t":"a micro-interaction","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Small, single-purpose feedback."},{"t":"dark mode","fb":"Dark mode is a colour scheme."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which trend fits the brief? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The Waitukubuli Trail app in Dominica must (1) confirm offline downloads instantly, (2) run on budget phones with weak signal and (3) be comfortable for hikers who are sensitive to motion. Which design decision BEST meets all three needs?","options":[{"t":"An immersive 3D fly-through of the trail every time the app opens.","fb":"Heavy on data and processing, it does not confirm downloads and could cause discomfort."},{"t":"Glass-effect panels over a full-screen trail video.","fb":"Blur and video strain budget phones, and the style does not confirm the action."},{"t":"A short micro-interaction (the save icon fills with progress and the phone vibrates) that switches to a simple static progress bar when reduced motion is turned on.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It gives instant, lightweight feedback and respects the reduced-motion setting."},{"t":"A five-second animated mascot that dances after every tap.","fb":"Far too long for feedback, and constant motion is a problem for sensitive users."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"AI-assisted design: tools, ethics and copyright","html":"<p><b>Generative AI</b> creates new images, text, audio or video from a text <b>prompt</b>. In design it is now built into everyday tools.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Note</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Firefly</b></td><td>Text-to-image, Generative Fill and Expand in Photoshop, text effects, vector recolouring in Illustrator</td><td>Adobe says it is trained on licensed content (such as Adobe Stock) and public-domain material, and markets it as safe for commercial use</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Midjourney</b></td><td>Highly stylised, artistic images from prompts, through its website and Discord</td><td>Paid subscription; strong for concept art and mood boards</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>DALL-E</b> (OpenAI)</td><td>Text-to-image and editing, available through ChatGPT</td><td>Good at following detailed prompts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Also</td><td>Canva Magic Studio, Figma AI features, Microsoft Designer</td><td>AI is spreading into commoditised template tools</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>How designers use AI well</b>: rapid <b>ideation</b> and mood boards, background removal and extension, mock-ups for client approval, variations of an idea, and saving time on repetitive tasks. The designer still sets the brief, selects, edits, checks and takes responsibility.</p>\n<p><b>Ethical issues</b> you must be able to discuss:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Training data and consent</b>: many models were trained on images collected from the web without artists' permission. Artists and image libraries have taken legal action in several countries.</li>\n<li><b>Copyright ownership</b>: in the United States, the Copyright Office has said that material generated purely by AI, without enough human creative control, cannot be registered for copyright. Rules differ by country and are still developing, so check the tool's terms of use.</li>\n<li><b>Style imitation</b>: prompting \"in the style of\" a living artist can harm their income and reputation.</li>\n<li><b>Bias and stereotypes</b>: models may show Caribbean people, food or places inaccurately or as clichés. Designers must check and correct this.</li>\n<li><b>Deepfakes and misinformation</b>: realistic fake images of real people or events, for example a fake hurricane-damage photo, can mislead the public.</li>\n<li><b>Jobs and transparency</b>: AI changes illustration and photography work; honest practice is to <b>disclose</b> AI use to clients and audiences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Island Pulse Media's policy: Zara uses Firefly for mood boards and background extension, never generates images of real people, labels AI-assisted work, and keeps all final artwork human-made for the Speightstown Heritage Festival brand.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For your SBA, academic integrity rules still apply. If you use AI for any asset, say which tool, what you prompted and what you changed, and make sure the main creative work is your own.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Generative AI tools create images, text or sound from a typed prompt, and many design apps now include them. Designers use them for quick ideas, mood boards and background edits, but the designer still decides, edits and takes responsibility. There are serious issues: copyright, copying living artists' styles, bias and fake images. An agency in Barbados should label AI-assisted work honestly.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Evaluating AI in design means separating <b>use</b> from <b>authorship</b>. Using a generator for early mood boards is very different from submitting generated artwork as your own. The legal position is still developing: in the United States, material generated without enough human creative control cannot be registered for copyright, and training on artists' work without consent is being challenged in court in several countries. Bias is a particular Caribbean risk, because models trained mostly on foreign images often show the region as clichés, so every output must be checked against real local references. Deepfakes add a public-safety issue; a fake hurricane-damage photo could cause panic. Honest practice is to disclose AI use, avoid imitating living artists and keep the main creative work human.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The AI artwork dilemma","intro":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. The Jacmel Food Festival in Haiti wants a poster and social pack in three days. Make the right calls.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The client loves a Midjourney image a colleague made by typing \"in the style of\" a well-known living Caribbean painter. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use it; the client likes it and nobody will notice.","to":"bad1","fb":"The artist and their fans are likely to notice, and the festival could face public criticism."},{"t":"Explain the ethical and legal risk of imitating a living artist, and propose original concepts instead.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Copying a living artist's style can harm their livelihood and the client's reputation."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You decide to use AI for early ideas only. Which tool and workflow do you choose for concept images?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Adobe Firefly for mood-board ideas, then design the final poster yourself in Illustrator and Photoshop.","to":"n3","fb":"Sensible. AI speeds up ideation while the final work stays human-made and under your control."},{"t":"Generate the final poster entirely with AI and submit it as your own hand-made work.","to":"bad2","fb":"Presenting AI output as fully your own is dishonest, and purely AI material may not be protected by copyright."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>An AI mood-board image shows festival-goers in clichéd, inaccurate \"tropical\" costume that does not reflect Haitian culture. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Reject it, and use your own photographs from local events, with permission, as the visual reference.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Checking AI output for bias and using authentic local imagery respects the community."},{"t":"Keep it; tourists expect that look.","to":"bad3","fb":"Stereotyped imagery misrepresents the culture and can offend the local audience."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The client asks how AI was used. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Disclose honestly: AI helped with early mood boards; all final artwork and photography is original.","to":"good","fb":"Transparency builds trust and protects both the agency and the client."},{"t":"Say no AI was used at all.","to":"bad4","fb":"A false statement could damage the relationship if the truth comes out later."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Ethical and on time.</b> The festival gets an original poster that reflects local culture. A Grade One answer would explain the issues you handled: style imitation, copyright ownership, bias and transparency.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Public backlash.</b> The painter posts a side-by-side comparison and the festival withdraws the poster. Try again and respect artists' rights.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Integrity problem.</b> The client discovers the work was fully AI-generated and questions what they paid for. Try again and keep AI as an assistant, not the author.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Misrepresentation.</b> Local residents complain that the imagery is a stereotype. Try again and check AI output for bias.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Trust lost.</b> The client later finds the mood boards in the project folder. Try again and disclose AI use.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Living artists","p":"<p>Whose income and reputation could be affected by copying a style?</p>"},{"h":"Assistant or author?","p":"<p>Where in the process is AI safest to use?</p>"},{"h":"Honesty","p":"<p>What should a client be told about the tools used?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The AI artwork dilemma (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you are making a festival poster.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How should you use AI on this job?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy a living artist's style with AI and hide it.","to":"bad","fb":"That harms the artist and misleads the client."},{"t":"Use it for early ideas, make the final design yourself, and tell the client.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Ethical, original and transparent."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You used AI ethically.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about artists' rights and honesty. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"The AI artwork dilemma (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher version of the Jacmel Food Festival job: more pressure from the client, a request for a \"photo\" that never existed and a three-day deadline. Make the right calls.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The client loves an AI image a colleague made by prompting \"in the style of\" a well-known living Caribbean painter. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the painter for permission, but offer no payment or credit.","to":"ok1","fb":"Asking is better than copying, but expecting free use of her style undervalues her work."},{"t":"Explain the risks (harm to the artist's income, possible legal and reputational damage) and offer two original directions, one developed with a paid local illustrator.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. You protect the artist and the client, and you still give the client choices."},{"t":"Use it, but change the colours slightly so it is \"different enough\".","to":"bad1","fb":"Small colour changes do not stop it being an imitation of her style."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The team agrees AI may help with early ideas only. Which workflow do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use a generator whose terms say it is trained on licensed images for rough mood boards, design the final poster yourself and keep a record of prompts and edits.","to":"n3","fb":"Sensible. AI speeds up ideas while the final work stays original and documented."},{"t":"Skip AI completely and sketch the concepts by hand.","to":"n3","fb":"Also ethical. It is slower, but every idea is original."},{"t":"Generate the final poster with AI and present it as hand-made.","to":"bad2","fb":"Presenting AI output as your own is dishonest, and the client may not own copyright in it."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>An AI mood board shows festival-goers in clichéd \"tropical\" costume that does not reflect Haitian culture. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep it, because tourists expect that look.","to":"bad3","fb":"Stereotyped imagery misrepresents the culture and can offend local people."},{"t":"Reject it, use your own photos from local events (with permission) and check the visuals with the festival's cultural committee.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Checking for bias and using authentic local references respects the community."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Last year's crowd photos were lost. The client asks you to generate a realistic \"photo\" of a packed festival crowd and present it as a real picture from last year. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Refuse to present a generated scene as a real photo; offer a clearly labelled illustration, or real photos from attendees used with permission.","to":"n5","fb":"Correct. Passing off a fake image as real is misinformation, even in an advert."},{"t":"Generate it and caption it \"Last year's festival\".","to":"bad4","fb":"That is a fake image presented as fact, which misleads the public."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>At the final meeting, the client asks exactly how AI was used. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Disclose honestly: AI helped with early mood boards only, all final artwork and photography is original, and the prompts are recorded in the project file.","to":"good","fb":"Transparency builds trust and protects both the agency and the client."},{"t":"Say no AI was used at all.","to":"bad5","fb":"A false statement damages trust if the truth comes out later."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Ethical, original and on time.</b> The festival gets an authentic poster and an honest record of how it was made. You handled style imitation, authorship, bias, misinformation and disclosure, the five issues a Grade One answer would discuss.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Polite refusal.</b> The painter declines to let her style be used for free, and you lose a day. A partial success: commission artists properly or create original work.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Public backlash.</b> The painter posts a side-by-side comparison and the festival withdraws the poster. Try again and respect artists' rights.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Integrity problem.</b> The client discovers the work was fully generated and questions what they paid for. Try again and keep AI as an assistant, not the author.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Misrepresentation.</b> Residents complain that the imagery is a stereotype. Try again and check AI output for bias.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Misinformation.</b> Attendees point out that the \"photo\" never happened, and the festival's credibility suffers. Try again and never present generated scenes as real.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Trust lost.</b> The client later finds the mood boards in the project folder. Try again and disclose AI use.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"AR and VR in visual design","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Augmented reality (AR)</b> adds digital content (images, 3D models, text, sound) on top of the real world, usually through a phone camera or smart glasses. <b>Virtual reality (VR)</b> replaces the real world with a fully computer-generated environment, viewed through a headset. <b>Mixed reality (MR)</b> lets digital objects interact with the real space around you.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>AR</th><th>VR</th></tr>\n<tr><td>World</td><td>Real world plus digital layers</td><td>Fully virtual</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Smartphone, tablet, AR glasses</td><td>Headset (for example Meta Quest, Apple Vision Pro), sometimes a PC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost to user</td><td>Low: the phone they already own</td><td>Higher: a headset</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Instagram and Snapchat filters, IKEA Place furniture preview, Google Maps Live View</td><td>Virtual tours, training simulations, VR games</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>How AR and VR change visual design</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Designers work in <b>3D space</b>, not flat screens: depth, scale, lighting and where the user is looking all matter.</li>\n<li><b>Spatial UI</b>: menus float in space or attach to real surfaces; text must be large and high-contrast to read at a distance.</li>\n<li><b>Comfort and safety</b>: avoid fast camera movement (motion sickness), keep users aware of real-world hazards.</li>\n<li>Tools: Meta Spark was closed in 2025, so creators now use tools such as Snapchat Lens Studio, Effect House for TikTok, Adobe Aero, Unity and Blender for 3D assets. Check which tools are current.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean applications</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tourism</b>: a VR preview of a Grace Bay hotel room or a dive in the Bonaire National Marine Park, shown at a travel fair in New York.</li>\n<li><b>Heritage</b>: an AR app that shows what Brimstone Hill Fortress looked like centuries ago when you point your phone at the ruins.</li>\n<li><b>Disaster preparedness</b>: a VR simulation that shows residents how storm surge could affect their street, building on lessons from Hurricane Irma in 2017.</li>\n<li><b>Retail and marketing</b>: an AR filter that puts Carnival feathers on your selfie, or lets you \"try on\" jewellery from a Bay Street store.</li>\n<li><b>Education</b>: AR anatomy models or 3D molecules in a CAPE biology class.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Link to the factors: AR spread faster than VR because of <b>affordability</b> and <b>pervasiveness</b> (almost everyone has a phone camera), while VR <b>adoption</b> is slower because headsets are costly and can be uncomfortable.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Distinguish between AR and VR\" needs both sides on the same feature: \"AR overlays digital content on the real world, whereas VR replaces the real world with a virtual one.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Augmented reality (AR) adds digital things on top of the real world through a phone camera, like a filter that puts Carnival feathers on your selfie. Virtual reality (VR) replaces the real world with a computer-made one, viewed through a headset, like a virtual dive on a reef. AR is cheaper because people already own phones; VR needs a headset.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The design challenges differ. AR content must sit believably in real, changing surroundings, so lighting, scale and placement matter, and bright Caribbean sunlight can wash out a phone screen. VR controls the whole environment, which allows total immersion, but designers must avoid fast camera movement that causes motion sickness and keep users aware of real-world hazards. The six factors explain adoption: AR spread quickly because of affordability and pervasiveness, since almost everyone carries a phone camera, while VR is slower because headsets are costly and can be uncomfortable. Platforms also change; Meta Spark closed in 2025, so check that a tool is current before recommending it. \"Distinguish\" answers must compare AR and VR on the same feature.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"AR or VR?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media has received eight project ideas. Sort each one.</p>","main":"Is this augmented reality or virtual reality?","bins":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)"],"items":[{"t":"An Instagram filter that adds Carnival feathers to your selfie.","bin":0,"fb":"Face filters add graphics on top of the live camera view: AR."},{"t":"A 360-degree headset tour of a Barbados hotel suite before booking.","bin":1,"fb":"The viewer is immersed in a recorded or virtual space, not their real surroundings: VR."},{"t":"See how a new sofa would look in your living room through the phone camera.","bin":0,"fb":"A virtual object is placed in the real room: AR."},{"t":"A virtual concert hall where fans with headsets watch a soca artist perform as avatars.","bin":1,"fb":"Users are inside a fully virtual environment: VR."},{"t":"Wear a headset and explore a fully virtual coral reef without leaving the classroom.","bin":1,"fb":"The real world is replaced by a virtual one: VR."},{"t":"A fully simulated cockpit for training pilots at a flight school in Trinidad.","bin":1,"fb":"An immersive simulated environment is VR."},{"t":"Arrows appear on the live street view to guide you to the Basseterre ferry.","bin":0,"fb":"Directions overlaid on the real street are AR."},{"t":"Point a phone at Brimstone Hill Fortress and see the original walls rebuilt on screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Digital content is layered over the real ruins, so it is AR."}],"win":"Well sorted. AR adds to the real world; VR replaces it.","tutor":[{"h":"Can you still see the room?","p":"<p>If the real world is still visible with extras added, that is one technology. If it disappears, that is the other.</p>"},{"h":"Hardware hint","p":"<p>A phone camera usually means one type; a headset that blocks your view usually means the other.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"AR or VR? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A selfie filter adds feathers to your face.","bin":0,"fb":"Added to the real view: AR."},{"t":"A headset takes you to a virtual reef.","bin":1,"fb":"Fully virtual: VR."},{"t":"Arrows appear on the live street view.","bin":0,"fb":"Overlay on reality: AR."},{"t":"A fully simulated cockpit for training.","bin":1,"fb":"Simulated world: VR."}]},
 "up": {"title":"AR or VR? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin for <b>mixed reality (MR)</b>, where digital objects interact with the real space around you. Sort each of Jerome's project ideas.</p>","main":"Is this AR, VR or mixed reality?","bins":["Augmented reality (AR)","Virtual reality (VR)","Mixed reality (MR)"],"items":[{"t":"A virtual ball bounces off your real table and rolls under your real chair.","bin":2,"fb":"The digital object reacts to real surfaces: MR."},{"t":"A headset takes students on a fully virtual coral reef dive.","bin":1,"fb":"The real world is replaced: VR."},{"t":"A fully simulated cockpit for training pilots.","bin":1,"fb":"A completely virtual environment: VR."},{"t":"Fans with headsets watch a soca artist perform as avatars in a virtual concert hall.","bin":1,"fb":"Users are inside a virtual world: VR."},{"t":"Arrows appear on the live street view to guide you to the Basseterre ferry.","bin":0,"fb":"Directions overlaid on reality: AR."},{"t":"Point a phone at the Brimstone Hill ruins and see the original walls rebuilt on screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Digital content layered over the real view: AR."},{"t":"In a safety course, a virtual fire spreads across the real kitchen counter and reacts to where you aim.","bin":2,"fb":"The virtual fire responds to the real room and your actions: MR."},{"t":"A selfie filter adds Carnival feathers to your face.","bin":0,"fb":"Graphics on top of the live camera: AR."},{"t":"A 3D hotel model stands on the meeting-room table and hides behind a real laptop as you walk around it.","bin":2,"fb":"Digital content interacting with real objects (occlusion) is MR."}],"win":"Excellent. AR adds to the real world, VR replaces it, and MR lets digital objects interact with it."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Research tools for tracking trends","html":"<p>You need to know <b>research tools for discovering and tracking trends</b>. Professionals check several sources, because one platform shows only part of the picture.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it shows</th><th>How to use it</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Trends</b> (trends.google.com)</td><td>How search interest in a term changes over time and by region (a relative score from 0 to 100, not a raw count)</td><td>Compare terms, such as \"glassmorphism\" vs \"neumorphism\"; set the region to a country and the time range to 5 years; check \"related queries\" for rising topics</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Behance</b> (Adobe)</td><td>Portfolios and full case studies by professional designers</td><td>Search a style or industry; study the process, not just the final image; follow Caribbean creatives</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dribbble</b></td><td>Short \"shots\" of UI, icons and illustration; an early signal of interface styles</td><td>Search \"dark mode dashboard\" or \"travel app\"; note repeated patterns</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Awwwards</b></td><td>Award-winning websites judged on design, usability, creativity and content</td><td>Browse \"Site of the Day\" for cutting-edge web, motion and immersive UI</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pinterest Trends</b> and Pinterest Predicts</td><td>What people are searching and saving on Pinterest; an annual forecast report</td><td>Check rising colours, aesthetics and seasonal themes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Also</td><td>Adobe and Pantone colour reports, Figma Community, industry blogs, design conferences</td><td>Use to confirm what you see elsewhere</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>A simple trend-research method</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Define the question: \"Is glassmorphism still growing for music apps?\"</li>\n<li>Collect evidence from at least three tools (Google Trends data, Dribbble and Behance examples, Awwwards winners).</li>\n<li>Record findings with dates, screenshots and links in a shared board (Milanote, Pinterest board, Google Slides).</li>\n<li>Identify the <b>factor</b> driving the trend (adoption by Apple? affordability of devices?).</li>\n<li>Judge whether it suits <b>your audience</b> (for example, data-light users in rural Jamaica).</li>\n<li>Apply it in a mock-up and test with real users.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media keeps a monthly \"trend watch\" slide for the team: one Google Trends chart, three Dribbble or Behance examples and one Awwwards winner, each with a note on whether it suits Caribbean clients.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A common Paper 01 trap: Google Trends shows <b>relative interest</b>, not the number of searches or users. And a trend on Dribbble is not automatically right for your audience; always justify it with user needs.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Designers track trends with several tools. Google Trends shows how interest in a search term changes over time and place. Behance shows full design case studies, Dribbble shows short design shots, Awwwards shows award-winning websites and Pinterest Trends shows what people save. Use at least three sources, record dates and links, and test ideas with real users, as a CAPE class in Antigua might.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each tool shows only part of the picture, and each has a bias. Google Trends gives <b>relative</b> interest from 0 to 100, not search counts, and data for small territories can be too thin to show at all. Dribbble shows what designers find exciting, which may run ahead of what users want; Awwwards rewards cutting-edge sites that may be too heavy for budget phones. That is why triangulating across several sources matters, and why the final test is always the audience: a trend is worth adopting only if it serves the users' needs and devices. Good research is also documented. Dates, screenshots and links let you show the evidence behind a design decision, and studying a designer's work is never the same as copying it.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Good trend research?","intro":"<p>A CAPE group in Antigua is researching design trends for a school app. Here are statements from their notes.</p>","q":"Select every statement that shows GOOD trend research practice.","lines":["We compared \"dark mode\" and \"glassmorphism\" in Google Trends over five years with the region set to the Caribbean countries available.","Google Trends shows that exactly 50 people searched for neumorphism last week.","We collected examples from Dribbble, Behance and Awwwards and recorded the date and link for each.","We saw one cool design on Dribbble, so it must be the biggest trend in the world.","We noted that dark mode grew after Apple and Google built it into their phone systems, an example of adoption.","We checked whether a heavy immersive design would load on the low-cost phones most students use.","We copied a Behance designer's layout exactly and called it our own.","We tested our trend-based mock-up with ten students and recorded their feedback."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,7],"win":"Correct. Good trend research compares several sources, records evidence, links trends to factors, checks audience fit and tests with users.","miss":"Look again. Google Trends shows relative interest (0 to 100), not exact counts; one example is not a trend; and copying a portfolio is plagiarism.","tutor":[{"h":"More than one source","p":"<p>Trusted conclusions use evidence from several tools.</p>"},{"h":"Know what the tool measures","p":"<p>What does a Google Trends score of 50 actually mean?</p>"},{"h":"Integrity","p":"<p>Inspiration is fine; copying is not.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Good trend research? (Foundation)","q":"Select the good practices.","lines":["Compare examples from several tools.","Copy a designer's layout exactly.","Test the design with real users.","Treat one example as a global trend."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Use several sources and test with users.","miss":"Copying is plagiarism, and one example is not a trend."},
 "up": {"title":"Good trend research? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: the CAPE group in Antigua has gone further. Some of these notes misread the tools or skip the audience test.</p>","q":"Select every statement that shows GOOD trend research practice.","lines":["We set Google Trends to \"Past 5 years\", compared two terms and noted that the scores are relative.","Google Trends showed no data for St. Kitts, so we concluded nobody there is interested in the trend.","We cross-checked a style seen on Dribbble against Awwwards winners and a Pinterest Predicts report.","A site won on Awwwards, so we copied its heavy 3D intro for our low-data audience.","We recorded each example with its date, link and a screenshot on a shared board.","We linked the rise of glass effects to adoption by major operating systems.","We used one designer's Behance case study as proof of a global trend.","We tested two mock-ups, one trend-based and one plain, with students on their own phones.","We downloaded a Behance designer's files and submitted them as our design."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,7],"win":"Correct. Relative scores understood, sources triangulated, evidence recorded, factors identified and the audience tested on real devices.","miss":"Look again. Missing data for a small territory is not proof of no interest, award-winning sites may not suit your audience, one case study is not a trend, and submitting someone else's files is plagiarism."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Discuss the factors behind a design trend","genre":"Extended response: discussing factors that influence design trends","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Island Connect Nassau app, used by visitors arriving at the Nassau cruise port and Lynden Pindling International Airport (LPIA), is being redesigned. The design team at the Island Pulse Media wants to add dark mode, micro-interactions and an AR feature that labels landmarks on Bay Street through the phone camera.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Discuss</b> how THREE of the following factors influence trends in visual and interactive design: commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption, empowerment. Use the Island Connect Nassau redesign as an example.</p><p>(b) Name TWO research tools the team could use to track whether these trends are growing, and explain how each would be used.</p>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The first factor is ..., which means ... For example, ... This influences design trends because ...<br>The second factor is ... However, ...<br>The third factor is ... As a result, ...<br><b>(b)</b> The team could use ... to ... They could also use ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I defined each of my three factors, using the correct terms (for example inclusive design, cross-platform compatibility, feedback and progress indicators).","I gave a specific example for each, linked to the tourism app.","I explained the effect of each factor on design trends.","I included at least one limitation or \"however\" point.","I named two research tools and said how to use each one."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Knowledge of the factors","marks":5,"look":["commodit","pervasiv|everywhere","afford|cost|cheap|free|budget","accessib|wcag|contrast|caption|disabilit","adopt|uptake|mainstream","empower","inclusive design|cross.?platform|user.?centr|design thinking","progress indicator|intuitive navigation|user control|clarity|inclusive language","ubiquit|uniqueness|interchangeable|standardi"],"min":3,"strong":"You identified and defined three factors accurately. Next step: make each definition distinct so the examiner can see you understand the difference.","weak":"Choose three factors and define each using the correct terms, e.g. \"Pervasiveness of technology is the widespread and all-encompassing presence of technology in human life and society.\" Name a consideration, such as inclusive design or feedback and progress indicators."},{"criterion":"Discussion and application","marks":6,"look":["dark mode","micro.?interaction|animation|feedback","ar\\b|augmented|camera","tourist|visitor|bay street|port|lpia|airport","because|as a result|therefore|this means","however|but|although|limitation|risk"],"min":3,"strong":"You linked each factor to the tourism app and explained its effect. A \"however\" point shows balanced discussion.","weak":"Show the effect: say how each factor leads to dark mode, micro-interactions or AR, and include at least one limitation or counter-point."},{"criterion":"Research tools","marks":4,"look":["google trends","behance|dribbble|awwwards|pinterest","compar|search interest|over time|region","example|portfolio|case stud|winner|shot"],"min":2,"strong":"You named suitable tools and explained how to use them.","weak":"Name two tools, e.g. Google Trends (compare search interest over time and by region) and Dribbble (collect current UI examples)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The first factor is <b>pervasiveness of technology</b>, which means digital technology is present everywhere in daily life. Cruise and air visitors use their phones from the moment they arrive at the Nassau cruise port or LPIA, in bright sunshine on Bay Street and late at night on Cable Beach. This influences trends because designers must make interfaces that work in every situation, so dark mode has become standard for night use and micro-interactions give quick confirmation when users are walking and only glance at the screen.</p>\n<p>The second factor is <b>adoption</b>, the rate at which people take up a new technology until it becomes normal. Dark mode became expected after Apple and Google built it into iOS 13 and Android 10 in 2019, so a tourism app without it now feels outdated. However, AR adoption is still uneven: many visitors have never used an AR landmark feature, so the team should add a short onboarding animation and keep a normal map view as an option.</p>\n<p>The third factor is <b>accessibility</b>: creating digital interfaces and experiences that can be easily and effectively used by individuals with disabilities. Through <b>inclusive design</b>, dark mode must still meet WCAG contrast rules and keep text readable, and through <b>cross-platform compatibility</b> the AR feature must work on mid-range phones and tablets as well as new devices, since visitors may also be on roaming data. Micro-interactions can act as <b>feedback and progress indicators</b>, which also empowers users, but they should respect the reduced-motion setting for users who feel unwell with animation. As a result, the trend is shaped not only by what looks modern but by what everyone can use.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The team could use <b>Google Trends</b> to compare search interest in terms such as \"dark mode app\" and \"AR tour\" over five years and by region, remembering that the scores are relative, not raw counts. They could also use <b>Dribbble</b> and <b>Awwwards</b> to collect current examples of travel apps and award-winning sites, noting repeated patterns, so they can judge whether these trends are growing and which suit their visitors.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For \"discuss\", each factor needs definition, example and effect, plus some balance (a \"however\"). Choose factors you can clearly separate; pervasiveness, adoption and accessibility work well together. In part (b), say what the tool shows, not just its name.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Discuss the factors behind a design trend (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: factors behind design trends, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Choose</b> ONE factor (commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption or empowerment) and <b>explain</b> what it means. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> an example of that factor from the Island Connect Nassau redesign. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> a SECOND factor, explain it and give an example from the app. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE problem that dark mode, micro-interactions or the AR feature could cause for some users. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE research tool and <b>state</b> what it shows. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> ... means ...<br><b>(b)</b> For example, in the tourism app ...<br><b>(c)</b> A second factor is ..., which means ... For example, ...<br><b>(d)</b> One problem is that ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> The team could use ..., which shows ...</p>","checklist":["I explained one factor correctly.","I gave an example from the tourism app.","I explained a second factor with an example.","I named a problem with one of the trends.","I named a research tool and said what it shows."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"First factor explained","marks":2,"look":["commodit|pervasiv|afford|accessib|adopt|empower","means|is when|refers"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear explanation of the factor.","weak":"Name one factor and say what it means, for example \"Accessibility means everyone, including people with disabilities, can use the app.\""},{"criterion":"Example from the app","marks":2,"look":["tourist|visitor|app|bay street|port|lpia|airport|dark mode|ar\\b|camera","example|such as|for instance"],"min":2,"strong":"A fitting example from the scenario.","weak":"Link the factor to the tourism app, for example dark mode for visitors using phones at night."},{"criterion":"Second factor with example","marks":2,"look":["commodit|pervasiv|afford|accessib|adopt|empower","example|such as|tourist|visitor|app"],"min":2,"strong":"A second factor, explained and applied.","weak":"Choose a different factor, say what it means and give an app example."},{"criterion":"Problem with a trend","marks":2,"look":["contrast|read|motion|dizzy|data|slow|battery|old phone|budget phone","because|so|for"],"min":2,"strong":"A real problem with a reason.","weak":"For example: the AR feature may run slowly on budget phones, so some visitors cannot use it."},{"criterion":"Research tool","marks":2,"look":["google trends|behance|dribbble|awwwards|pinterest","show|search|interest|example|design"],"min":2,"strong":"A real tool and what it shows.","weak":"Name Google Trends and say it shows how interest in a search term changes over time."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Accessibility means designing an app so that everyone, including people with disabilities, can use it easily.</p><p><b>(b)</b> For example, in the tourism app the dark mode must still have strong contrast, so older visitors can read the text.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A second factor is adoption, which means how quickly people start using a new idea until it is normal. For example, visitors now expect dark mode because their phones already have it.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One problem is that the AR feature may be slow on budget phones because it uses the camera and a lot of processing power.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The team could use Google Trends, which shows how interest in a search term, such as \"AR tour\", changes over time.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the point and 1 for the example or reason. Students often define two factors in the same words; check that (a) and (c) are clearly different. Move students on to the Core task once they can add an effect on trends (\"this means designers now...\") to each example.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Discuss the factors behind a design trend (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: factors behind design trends, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Island Connect Nassau app, used by visitors arriving at the Nassau cruise port and Lynden Pindling International Airport (LPIA), is being redesigned. The design team at the Island Pulse Media wants to add dark mode, micro-interactions and an AR feature that labels landmarks on Bay Street through the phone camera. Analytics show that 40% of users are on budget Android phones, many on expensive roaming data; 15% are over 60; and most use the app outdoors in bright sun. A rival island's tourism app recently received poor reviews because its AR feature was slow and drained batteries.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Discuss</b> how FOUR of the six factors (commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption, empowerment) influence trends in visual and interactive design. For each, give a definition, an example from the redesign and its effect on trends, and show how at least two factors <b>interact</b>. <i>(10 marks)</i></p><p>(b) <b>Evaluate</b> each of the three proposed trends (dark mode, micro-interactions, AR) against the analytics, and <b>recommend</b> how each should be implemented. <i>(9 marks)</i></p><p>(c) <b>Design</b> a trend-research plan using THREE tools, explaining how you would allow for each tool's limitations. <i>(4 marks)</i></p><p>(d) <b>Make</b> a justified overall recommendation to the client. <i>(2 marks)</i></p>","min":500,"max":850,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Factor 1: ... means ... In the redesign ... As a result, trends ... Factor 2 ... Factor 3 ... Factor 4 ... These factors interact because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Dark mode: ... however ... so I recommend ... Micro-interactions: ... AR: ... the rival app shows ... so ...<br><b>(c)</b> Tool 1 ... limitation ... allowed for by ... Tool 2 ... Tool 3 ...<br><b>(d)</b> Overall, I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I discussed four distinct factors, each with a definition, an example and an effect.","I explained how at least two factors interact.","I evaluated all three trends against the analytics (budget phones, roaming data, over-60s, bright sun).","I gave a practical implementation for each trend.","I used three research tools and explained each one's limitation.","I ended with a justified recommendation that uses the rival app's experience."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Discussion of four factors","marks":10,"look":["commodit","pervasiv","afford","accessib|wcag|inclusive design","adopt","empower","means|refers to|is the","as a result|this means|therefore|so designers","interact|combin|together|leads to|because of"],"min":6,"strong":"Four factors clearly defined, applied and linked to trends, with a convincing explanation of how they interact.","weak":"For each of four factors give a definition, an example from the app and its effect on trends, then explain how two of them work together."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of the three trends","marks":9,"look":["dark mode","micro.?interaction","ar\\b|augmented","budget|android|old phone|low.?cost","roaming|data","over 60|older|60","sun|outdoor|glare|bright","however|but|although|risk","recommend|should|implement|toggle|option"],"min":7,"strong":"Each trend weighed against the real audience data, with specific, practical implementation advice.","weak":"For each trend give a benefit, a risk shown by the analytics and a practical way to implement it safely."},{"criterion":"Research plan with limitations","marks":4,"look":["google trends","behance|dribbble|awwwards|pinterest","relative|0 to 100|not.*count|small territor|thin","test|users|audience|prototype"],"min":3,"strong":"Three tools, each with its limitation and a way to allow for it, ending in a user test.","weak":"Name three tools, say what each cannot tell you and how you would make up for it, for example by testing with real visitors."},{"criterion":"Justified recommendation","marks":2,"look":["recommend|overall|on balance","because|rival|reviews|therefore"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear recommendation justified by the evidence.","weak":"Say which trends to launch now, which to delay, and why."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Pervasiveness of technology</i> is the widespread and all-encompassing presence of technology in human life. Visitors use their phones from the moment they land at LPIA, outdoors, at night and on the move, so designers must create interfaces that work in every condition. As a result, dark mode and quick, glanceable feedback have become standard trends. <i>Adoption</i> is the rate at which people take up a new idea until it becomes normal. Dark mode became expected after Apple and Google built it into iOS 13 and Android 10 in 2019, so a tourism app without it now feels dated; AR adoption, however, is still uneven, so many visitors will need a first-time explanation. <i>Affordability</i> means delivering good design within cost limits, for users as well as designers. Because 40% of users have budget phones on roaming data, trends that need powerful hardware or heavy downloads are effectively unaffordable for them, which pushes the redesign towards data-light, efficient features. <i>Accessibility</i> means interfaces that everyone can use, including people with disabilities. Through inclusive design, the app's text must meet WCAG contrast guidelines for the 15% of users over 60, and through cross-platform compatibility the app must work on budget Android phones as well as new iPhones. <i>Interaction:</i> affordability and accessibility combine here, because access to technology itself (data cost and device power) decides who can use a trend; and pervasiveness drives adoption, because the more screens people use daily, the faster they learn and expect new conventions.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Dark mode</i> suits night-time users and is already widely adopted, but most use is outdoors in bright sun, where dark themes can be harder to read. I recommend dark mode as an option that follows the phone's system setting, with a dark grey background, off-white text and WCAG-compliant contrast, while the default remains a high-contrast light theme for daytime. <i>Micro-interactions</i> are lightweight and empower users with instant feedback, such as a pulsing pin when a landmark is saved; they add little data or processing load. The risk is motion sensitivity among some users, so animations should last about 200 to 500 milliseconds and switch off when reduced motion is turned on. <i>AR</i> is the most exciting trend, but the analytics and the rival app's reviews show the danger: on budget phones it can be slow and drain batteries, and on roaming data any downloaded 3D content is costly. I recommend a light AR label mode (text labels only, no 3D models), offered as an optional feature with a short tutorial, with an ordinary map view as the default and a warning about battery use.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <i>Google Trends:</i> compare \"AR tour\" and \"dark mode app\" over five years. Its scores are relative (0 to 100) and data for small territories such as the Turks and Caicos Islands may be missing, so I would compare the visitors' home countries instead. <i>Dribbble and Behance:</i> collect current travel-app examples and note repeated patterns. They show what designers like, not what users can run, so I would note each example's likely performance on budget phones. <i>App store reviews of rival tourism apps:</i> read reviews that mention AR, battery and data. Reviews over-represent unhappy users, so I would treat them as warning signs rather than proof. Finally, I would test two prototypes with ten real visitors at the cruise pier, on their own phones.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Overall, I recommend launching dark mode as an option and accessible micro-interactions now, because they are cheap, widely adopted and help every user, and introducing AR later as an optional, data-light feature, because the rival app shows that a heavy AR launch can damage a tourism app's reputation with exactly the visitors this app is meant to welcome.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 10 + 9 + 4 + 2). The best answers let the stimulus data drive the evaluation: budget phones, roaming data, older users and bright sun. Reward explanations of how factors interact, not just four separate definitions. In (c), credit students who show they understand what each tool cannot tell them. Penalise recommendations that ignore the rival app's experience.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U10","title":"Considerations for a project proposal","objectives":["Outline the basic considerations for developing a project proposal: writing, funding, budget and pitching.","Write the main sections of a proposal: executive summary, problem statement, SMART objectives, methodology and timeline, with target audience and market analysis.","Identify and evaluate types of funding (debt and equity) and sources of funding: government grants, crowdfunding, sponsorship, angel investors, loans, venture capital, bootstrapping and regional bodies.","Prepare an itemised budget covering production, equipment, software, personnel, hosting and contingency, in XCG or USD.","Pitch a project convincingly to stakeholders using a visual pitch deck in Canva, Google Slides or PowerPoint.","Link proposal skills to the Unit 2 SBA: the 14-mark Proposal section and the 30-second short ad."],"videos":[{"title":"How to write a project proposal","q":"how to write a project proposal executive summary problem statement objectives"},{"title":"Writing SMART objectives","q":"SMART objectives explained with examples"},{"title":"How to make a pitch deck","q":"how to make a pitch deck in Canva for beginners"},{"title":"Crowdfunding vs grants vs angel investors","q":"crowdfunding vs grants vs angel investors explained for creative projects"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What a project proposal is and why it matters","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>project proposal</b> is a formal document that describes a planned project, the problem it solves, how it will be done, what it will cost and why it deserves support, so that decision-makers (a client, funder, school or investor) can approve it.</p>\n<p>You must be able to <b>outline basic considerations</b> for a project proposal under four headings: <b>(a) proposal writing, (b) sources of funding, (c) budget and (d) project pitching</b>. This unit expands these into proposal sections, target audience and market analysis, specific funding sources, itemised budgets and visual pitch decks. Teams also develop <b>production schedules, budgets and equipment lists</b> and address legal, ethical and safety considerations during pre-production.</p>\n<p>Why proposals matter in digital media:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Approval and funding</b>: no one pays for an idea they cannot see clearly.</li>\n<li><b>Shared understanding</b>: the client and team agree on scope, deadlines and deliverables before work starts, which prevents \"scope creep\" (extra work nobody agreed to).</li>\n<li><b>Planning</b>: writing the proposal forces you to think through risks, resources and costs.</li>\n<li><b>Accountability</b>: the objectives become the yardstick for judging success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the Island Pulse Media, Zara will not start a project without a signed proposal. When the Curaçao Youth Heritage Foundation asked for \"an app about our history\", her proposal turned that vague wish into a clear plan: an AR walking tour of six sites in Willemstad, built in 16 weeks, for XCG 38,000, aimed at students aged 12 to 18.</p>\n<p><b>Link to your SBA.</b> Your Unit 2 SBA asks your group (2 to 3 students) to design a product or service, preferably for a local social or entrepreneurial need, and to include a <b>proposal</b>. The <b>Proposal section is worth 14 of the 45 marks</b>. Everything in this unit feeds directly into those marks, and into the 30-second short ad in the Product section.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Outline\" is a lower-level command word: give the main points briefly, in a logical order, with a short explanation for each. Do not write a full essay on one point and leave out the others.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A project proposal is a formal document that explains a planned project: the problem, the solution, how it will be done, what it will cost and why it deserves support. Clients, funders and schools use it to decide whether to say yes. A youth group in St. Vincent asking for money to make a heritage video would need a clear proposal first.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A proposal is a <b>persuasive</b> document as well as a plan. It must convince a decision-maker who has many other requests, so clarity and evidence matter more than length. It also protects the creator: once scope, deliverables and deadlines are agreed in writing, \"scope creep\" (extra work nobody agreed to pay for) can be refused politely. Notice how Zara turned a vague wish, \"an app about our history\", into specifics: six sites, 16 weeks, a budget and a defined audience. That transformation is the core skill. \"Outline\" questions reward breadth, so cover each consideration (proposal writing, funding, budget and pitching) briefly and in a logical order, rather than writing at length about only one.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Proposal writing: the core sections","html":"<p>Most professional proposals use these sections, in this order.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Title page</b>: project name, client or funder, team names, date.</li>\n<li><b>Executive summary</b>: a half-page overview of the whole proposal (problem, solution, audience, cost, timeline, expected impact). It is written <b>last</b> but placed <b>first</b>, because busy decision-makers may read only this.</li>\n<li><b>Background and problem statement</b>: the context and the specific problem, supported by evidence (survey results, statistics, interviews). A good problem statement says <b>who</b> is affected, <b>what</b> the problem is, <b>where</b>, and <b>why it matters</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Proposed solution (concept)</b>: what you will create and how it works, with draft designs, sketches or mock-ups.</li>\n<li><b>Objectives</b>: what the project will achieve, written as <b>SMART</b> objectives (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound).</li>\n<li><b>Target audience and market analysis</b>: who the users are and what already exists.</li>\n<li><b>Methodology</b>: how the work will be done: the model or process (for example the creative design process, or a waterfall or agile approach), the tools (Figma, Blender, Premiere Pro, WordPress, MIT App Inventor) and the techniques.</li>\n<li><b>Timeline</b>: phases, tasks, durations, milestones and deliverables, often as a <b>Gantt chart</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Budget</b>: itemised costs (covered later in this unit).</li>\n<li><b>Evaluation</b>: how success will be measured (KPIs, user testing, feedback).</li>\n<li><b>Conclusion</b> and appendices (data, survey instrument, CVs).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Weak vs strong problem statement</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Weak</th><th>Strong</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Young people don't know about history.\"</td><td>\"In a survey of 120 Curaçao secondary students in September, 78% could not name three historic sites in Willemstad. School trips are rare because of cost and transport, and existing information is mostly in printed booklets that students do not read.\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Weak vs strong objective</b>: \"Make a cool app\" is weak. \"Launch an Android AR walking-tour app with six heritage sites by 30 April, and reach 1,000 downloads from students by the end of June\" is SMART.</p>\n<p>Write in the <b>third person</b> or a professional \"we\", use headings, short paragraphs and tables, and support claims with data you collected or cite from reliable sources (ITU, World Bank, the Central Bureau of Statistics Curaçao (CBS)).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A frequent exam question asks you to \"state THREE components of a project proposal and outline the purpose of each\". Name the component, then say what it does for the reader: \"The executive summary gives decision-makers a quick overview so they can decide whether to read further.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A professional proposal has standard sections: title page, executive summary, background and problem statement, proposed solution, SMART objectives, audience and market analysis, methodology, timeline, budget, evaluation and appendices. The executive summary is written last but placed first, because busy readers may read only that. A Jamaican film proposal needs evidence, not just opinions, in its problem statement.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each section answers a question the funder will ask, so the order follows the reader's thinking: what is this, why is it needed, what will you do, for whom, how, when, at what cost and how will we know it worked? Gaps are easy to spot: a proposal with no evaluation section suggests the team has not thought about success. The problem statement is the foundation, and it is strongest when it uses your own data, such as survey results, rather than general claims. Objectives connect the beginning to the end, because the evaluation measures exactly those objectives. Write professionally, with headings, short paragraphs, tables and cited sources. A common weakness is an executive summary that introduces the project but leaves out the cost or the timeline.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the proposal section","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is checking a draft proposal for the Grenada Spice Island Recipes app. Match each extract to its section.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Executive summary","r":"A half-page overview of the problem, solution, cost of USD 9,500 and 12-week timeline"},{"l":"Problem statement","r":"65% of surveyed young Grenadians said they rarely cook traditional dishes because recipes are not written down"},{"l":"Objectives","r":"Publish 40 video recipes and reach 2,000 app users by 31 August"},{"l":"Methodology","r":"We will follow the creative design process, filming in 4K and building the app in Thunkable"},{"l":"Timeline","r":"A Gantt chart: research weeks 1 to 2, filming weeks 3 to 6, build weeks 7 to 10, testing weeks 11 to 12"},{"l":"Target audience","r":"Grenadians aged 16 to 35 at home and in the diaspora who use smartphones daily"},{"l":"Budget","r":"An itemised table of equipment, software, personnel, hosting and a 10% contingency"}],"win":"Well matched. You can identify each proposal section by its purpose, which is what \"state and outline\" questions test.","tutor":[{"h":"Overview or detail?","p":"<p>Only one section summarises everything else in brief.</p>"},{"h":"Why, what, how, when","p":"<p>Why = problem. What will be achieved = objectives. How = methodology. When = timeline.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the proposal section (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Executive summary","r":"A short overview of the whole proposal"},{"l":"Problem statement","r":"Evidence of the issue the project solves"},{"l":"Methodology","r":"The process and tools that will be used"},{"l":"Timeline","r":"Phases and deadlines shown as a Gantt chart"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the proposal section (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: eight extracts from the Grenada recipes proposal, including the background, the evaluation and the appendices. Match each extract to its section.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Executive summary","r":"In brief: a USD 9,500, 12-week project creating 40 recipe videos for young Grenadians at home and abroad"},{"l":"Background","r":"Grenada's spice-cooking heritage is passed on by word of mouth, and many of the elders who hold the recipes are now in their 80s"},{"l":"Problem statement","r":"65% of 200 surveyed Grenadians aged 16 to 35 rarely cook traditional dishes because they have no written recipes"},{"l":"Objectives","r":"Publish 40 videos by 31 August and reach 2,000 active app users by 31 December"},{"l":"Methodology","r":"We will follow the creative design process, filming in 4K and building the app in Thunkable because it suits a small team"},{"l":"Budget","r":"Equipment XCG 2,400; software XCG 600; personnel XCG 9,000; hosting XCG 300; contingency 10%"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Success will be judged by downloads, average watch time and a user survey after three months"},{"l":"Appendices","r":"The full questionnaire, the raw survey data and the team's CVs"}],"win":"Excellent. You told the background from the problem statement and the objectives from the evaluation, which shows you understand each section's purpose."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Target audience and market analysis","html":"<p>A funder's first question is: <i>who is this for, and will they use it?</i> Two parts of the proposal answer this.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Target audience analysis</b> identifies and describes the specific group of people the product is designed for.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Demographics</b>: age, gender, location, language, income, education, occupation.</li>\n<li><b>Psychographics</b>: interests, values, lifestyles, attitudes.</li>\n<li><b>Technographics</b>: devices, operating systems, data plans, platforms used, digital skills.</li>\n<li><b>Needs and pain points</b>: what frustrates them now.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Summarise this as a <b>persona</b>: \"Devon, 16, Coralstone College student, Android phone on a prepaid data plan, uses TikTok and WhatsApp daily, interested in football and music, finds history lessons boring but loves challenges and badges.\"</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Market analysis</b> studies the wider environment: the size of the market, existing competitors, gaps and opportunities.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Market size</b>: how many potential users? Use reliable figures (for example CBS Curaçao for population, ITU for Internet use) and say where they came from.</li>\n<li><b>Competitor analysis</b>: what similar products already exist (local, regional, international)? What do they do well or badly?</li>\n<li><b>Gap</b>: what is missing that your product provides?</li>\n<li><b>SWOT analysis</b>: Strengths, Weaknesses (internal), Opportunities, Threats (external).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th colspan=\"2\">SWOT: Willemstad AR Heritage Walk</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Strengths</b>: unique local content; team skilled in Blender and Unity</td><td><b>Weaknesses</b>: small team; Android only at launch</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Opportunities</b>: cruise visitors at the Willemstad cruise terminal; school curriculum links</td><td><b>Threats</b>: hurricane season disruption; free global apps competing for attention</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Collecting the data</b>: the SBA mark scheme rewards a <b>data capture instrument</b> (a questionnaire, interview guide or observation checklist) with data collected, plus <b>data analysis</b>. Use Google Forms or Microsoft Forms, ask 8 to 12 clear questions (mostly closed, one or two open), aim for at least 30 responses, then present results in charts and say what they show.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Never write \"the target audience is everyone\". Examiners reward a <b>narrow, described</b> audience with evidence. Even a community product has a primary audience (who it is mainly for) and a secondary audience.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Funders want to know who the product is for and whether they will use it. Describe the audience with demographics (age, location), psychographics (interests, values), technographics (devices, data) and pain points, then sum it up as a persona. Market analysis looks at competitors, the gap you fill and a SWOT. A bus app for St. Kitts should study existing bus apps first.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Market analysis is about proving <b>demand</b> and <b>difference</b>. Demand comes from evidence: survey data from real users, or reliable figures such as population and Internet-use statistics with their sources. Difference comes from competitor analysis, which reveals the gap your product fills. SWOT is often done badly: strengths and weaknesses are <b>internal</b> (your team and your product), while opportunities and threats are <b>external</b> (the market, the weather, competitors). \"Hurricane season\" is a threat, not a weakness. A good survey uses mostly closed questions so results can be charted, plus one or two open questions for insight, with enough responses to be meaningful. Never describe the audience as \"everyone\"; even a community product has a primary audience.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Audience and market check","intro":"<p>Jerome is reviewing a CAPE group's proposal for a St. Kitts bus-times app. Answer each question.</p>","items":[{"text":"\"Most of our users use Android phones on prepaid data plans.\" This is an example of...","options":["demographics","psychographics","technographics"],"answer":2,"fb":"Devices and data plans describe technology use, which is technographics."},{"text":"\"Our users value saving time and dislike waiting in the sun without information.\" This is...","options":["psychographics and pain points","market size","a competitor analysis"],"answer":0,"fb":"Values, attitudes and frustrations are psychographics and pain points."},{"text":"The group lists two existing bus apps from other islands and notes what each lacks. This is...","options":["competitor analysis","a SWOT strength","a problem statement"],"answer":0,"fb":"Studying existing products and their weaknesses is competitor analysis, which reveals the gap."},{"text":"\"Heavy rain and hurricanes could stop buses and make the app's data wrong.\" In SWOT this is a...","options":["weakness","strength","threat"],"answer":2,"fb":"It is external and harmful, so it is a threat. Weaknesses are internal to the team or product."},{"text":"Which is the BEST data capture instrument for this proposal?","options":["copying statistics from an unrelated country","asking two friends what they think","a Google Forms survey of bus users at the Basseterre bus terminal, with 10 clear questions"],"answer":2,"fb":"A designed survey of real users gives relevant evidence and earns the SBA research marks."}],"win":"Strong analysis. You can separate demographics, psychographics and technographics, use SWOT correctly and choose a sound data capture instrument.","tutor":[{"h":"Three \"graphics\"","p":"<p>Who they are, what they value, what technology they use.</p>"},{"h":"Inside or outside?","p":"<p>In SWOT, strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats come from outside.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Audience and market check (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Devices and data plans are...","options":["demographics","technographics"],"answer":1,"fb":"Technology use: technographics."},{"text":"A hurricane risk in SWOT is a...","options":["strength","threat"],"answer":1,"fb":"External and harmful: threat."},{"text":"Studying existing similar apps is...","options":["an executive summary","competitor analysis"],"answer":1,"fb":"That is competitor analysis."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Audience and market check (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: four options each, and questions on SWOT, evidence and survey design for the St. Kitts bus-times app.</p>","items":[{"text":"\"Most users are 18 to 24 and live in Basseterre.\" This is...","options":["psychographics","a threat","technographics","demographics"],"answer":3,"fb":"Age and location are measurable facts: demographics."},{"text":"\"Users want to feel safe while waiting for buses after dark.\" This is...","options":["psychographics and a pain point","technographics","market size","a strength"],"answer":0,"fb":"A value and a worry: psychographics and a pain point."},{"text":"\"Our team has no iOS developer.\" In SWOT this is a...","options":["weakness","threat","opportunity","strength"],"answer":0,"fb":"It is internal to the team and limits the product: a weakness."},{"text":"\"A new cruise berth will bring more visitors next year.\" In SWOT this is an...","options":["strength","weakness","threat","opportunity"],"answer":3,"fb":"External and helpful: an opportunity."},{"text":"The group says its market is \"about 50,000 people\". What makes this figure credible?","options":["Giving its source, such as national statistics, and the year","Leaving the source out","Using a bigger number","Rounding it up"],"answer":0,"fb":"A cited, dated source lets the funder check the figure."},{"text":"Which survey design is BEST for the proposal?","options":["Ten mostly closed questions plus one open question, answered by at least 30 real bus users","One yes/no question","50 open questions sent to anyone online","Two open questions sent to five friends"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mostly closed questions can be charted, the open question adds insight, and real users give relevant evidence."},{"text":"Competitor analysis shows that two island bus apps lack real-time updates. What should the proposal do with this?","options":["Copy one of the apps","Ignore it","State the gap and make real-time updates the product's unique selling point","Remove the market analysis"],"answer":2,"fb":"The gap becomes the reason the product deserves funding."}],"win":"Excellent. You can classify audience data, apply SWOT correctly, make figures credible and design a survey that produces useful evidence."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Methodology and timeline","html":"<p class=\"passage\">The <b>methodology</b> explains <b>how</b> the project will be carried out: the process model, the phases, the tools and the techniques.</p>\n<p>In Unit 1 and Module 1 of Unit 2 you studied the creative process and implementation models. Name the model you will follow and show each phase. For example, the creative design process: <b>acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation, selection, implementation, evaluation</b>. Then state the <b>tools</b> for each phase and justify them: \"Figma for wireframes, because the three of us can edit the same file at once\"; \"Blender for 3D models, because it is free and exports to Unity\".</p>\n<p>The SBA Progress and Process section gives marks for tools <b>fully described</b> and for a detailed methods outline with <b>all phases</b> of a selected model, so the methodology in your proposal is the foundation for those marks too.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">The <b>timeline</b> (production schedule) shows <b>when</b> each task happens, how long it takes, who does it, and what is delivered at each <b>milestone</b>.</p>\n<p>A <b>Gantt chart</b> shows tasks as horizontal bars across weeks. Make one in Google Sheets, Excel, Canva or a free tool such as TeamGantt. A <b>milestone</b> is a key checkpoint with a deliverable (for example \"wireframes approved\").</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Weeks</th><th>Owner</th><th>Deliverable (milestone)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Research and survey</td><td>1 to 2</td><td>Whole team</td><td>Survey results and analysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concept and brainstorming chart</td><td>3</td><td>Zara</td><td>Approved concept and mood board</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storyboard and wireframes</td><td>4 to 5</td><td>Maya</td><td>Clickable Figma prototype</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asset creation (3D, audio, video)</td><td>6 to 9</td><td>Malik</td><td>Final assets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Build and integrate</td><td>8 to 12</td><td>Jerome</td><td>Working beta app</td></tr>\n<tr><td>User testing and fixes</td><td>13 to 14</td><td>Whole team</td><td>Test report, final version</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Launch and 30-second ad</td><td>15 to 16</td><td>Keisha</td><td>Published app and promotional ad</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Notice the <b>overlap</b> between weeks 8 and 9: some tasks run in parallel, which a Gantt chart shows well. Build in a little <b>buffer time</b>, especially around exams, Carnival and the hurricane season (June to November), when power cuts and school closures can delay work, as Dominica learned after Hurricane Maria in 2017.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The SBA production schedule criterion looks for <b>requirements, duration and deliverables</b>. Make sure your timeline table has a column for each, not just task names and dates.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The methodology explains how the project will be done: the process model, its phases, the tools and why you chose them. The timeline shows when each task happens, who does it and what is delivered at each milestone, often as a Gantt chart. Add buffer time for exams, Carnival and hurricane season, as any team in Antigua or Sint Maarten knows.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A convincing methodology <b>justifies</b> choices rather than listing them: \"Blender, because it is free and exports to Unity\" shows reasoning, while \"we will use Blender\" does not. Link each tool to a phase of the chosen model, so the reader can see the whole process. The timeline should be realistic about dependencies: the app cannot be finished before its assets exist, though some tasks can overlap, which a Gantt chart shows clearly. Each milestone needs a deliverable someone could check, such as \"clickable Figma prototype approved\", not just \"design done\". Buffer time is not padding. In the Caribbean, power cuts, storms and school closures are predictable risks, and planning for them shows professional judgement.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the proposal in order","intro":"<p>A CAPE group in Barbados has written its proposal sections on separate pages. Put them in the order a reader would expect to find them.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Title page"},{"t":"Executive summary"},{"t":"Background and problem statement"},{"t":"Proposed solution and SMART objectives"},{"t":"Target audience and market analysis"},{"t":"Methodology and timeline"},{"t":"Itemised budget"},{"t":"Evaluation plan and conclusion"}],"win":"Correct. A proposal moves from overview, to the problem, to what you will do, for whom, how, at what cost, and how success will be judged.","tutor":[{"h":"Written last, read first","p":"<p>One section summarises everything, so it sits right after the title page.</p>"},{"h":"Why before how","p":"<p>Readers need to understand the problem before they care about the method or the cost.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Put the proposal in order (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four main sections in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Executive summary"},{"t":"Problem statement"},{"t":"Methodology and timeline"},{"t":"Budget"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Put the proposal in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: all eleven sections, with the solution, objectives, methodology and timeline as separate pages. Put them in the order a reader expects.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Title page"},{"t":"Executive summary"},{"t":"Background and problem statement"},{"t":"Proposed solution (concept)"},{"t":"SMART objectives"},{"t":"Target audience and market analysis"},{"t":"Methodology"},{"t":"Timeline (Gantt chart)"},{"t":"Itemised budget"},{"t":"Evaluation plan"},{"t":"Conclusion and appendices"}],"win":"Correct. Overview, problem, solution, objectives, audience, process, schedule, cost, evaluation and supporting evidence: the order follows the funder's questions."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Sources of funding: the big picture","html":"<p>The objective for this part of the unit is to <b>evaluate project funding opportunities available for digital content creators</b>. Digital media projects need money for equipment, software, people and promotion, and every funder wants something different in return. You need to know the main <b>sources of funding</b>. Four key ones are government grants, crowdfunding, sponsorships and angel investors, and this unit adds more.</p>\n<p>Start with the two <b>types of funding</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Debt</b>: you borrow money and pay it back, usually with interest (for example, bank and credit union loans).</li>\n<li><b>Equity</b>: you sell part of the ownership of your business in return for money (angel investors, venture capital, equity crowdfunding).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some sources are <b>neither</b>: grants, sponsorships, donations and your own savings do not have to be repaid and do not cost you shares, although each comes with conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Source</th><th>Overview</th><th>Benefit</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Grants</b> (government, foundations, arts councils)</td><td>Non-repayable money for projects that meet public or cultural goals</td><td>No debt and no loss of ownership; adds credibility</td><td>Competitive; detailed proposals, timelines and reporting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Crowdfunding</b></td><td>Many supporters give small amounts online (Kickstarter, Indiegogo, GoFundMe)</td><td>Builds a loyal audience early and proves demand</td><td>Needs heavy promotion and updates; campaigns can miss the goal; platform fees</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sponsorships and partnerships</b></td><td>A business gives money or resources (in-kind) for brand exposure in your content</td><td>Credibility and repeat collaborations</td><td>Creative choices may have to follow sponsor guidelines</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Angel investors</b></td><td>Individuals who back early projects with their own money</td><td>Mentoring and industry contacts as well as money</td><td>Usually want equity and some influence over decisions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Loans</b> (debt financing)</td><td>Money from banks, credit unions or other lenders, repaid with interest</td><td>You keep full ownership and creative control</td><td>Repayments are due even if income is delayed</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Venture capital</b></td><td>Firms investing larger sums in businesses with high growth potential</td><td>Faster growth, expertise, networks and follow-on funding</td><td>A large equity stake and board or decision-making power</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bootstrapping</b> (self-funding)</td><td>Your own savings, income or small pre-sales</td><td>Complete ownership and creative freedom</td><td>Limited budget; slower growth</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Caribbean bodies to research</b> (always check what is open now, as programmes change): the <b>Caribbean Development Bank</b> has run a Cultural and Creative Industries Innovation Fund; <b>UNESCO's</b> International Fund for Cultural Diversity accepts proposals from developing countries; the <b>IDB Lab</b> supports innovation in Inter-American Development Bank member countries such as Jamaica and Barbados; national arts councils and culture ministries across the CARICOM region offer grants and awards; and in many territories local banks, credit unions and microlenders (such as <b>Qredits</b> in the Dutch Caribbean) lend to small entrepreneurs.</p>\n<p><b>Link to your SBA budget.</b> Your budget total is the amount you must fund. A strong proposal also shows where the money will come from, usually a <b>mixed funding</b> plan, so the project does not depend on one source. For example, the Storm Ready Bahamas video series could seek telecom sponsorship for data-free viewing, apply for a regional resilience grant and run a small GoFundMe for equipment.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To <b>distinguish</b> sponsorship from angel investment: a sponsor buys <b>visibility</b>, whereas an angel investor normally buys <b>ownership (equity)</b>. Grants are given, loans are repaid with interest, and equity is rewarded through a share of the business and its profits.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Projects need money for equipment, software, people and promotion. Debt means borrowing and paying it back with interest, like a bank loan. Equity means selling part of your business, as with an angel investor. Grants, sponsorships and your own savings are neither. Each source has a benefit and a drawback. A film group in Barbados might combine a grant, a sponsor and crowdfunding.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Every funder wants something in return, and the strongest answers name it. A lender wants repayment with interest; an equity investor wants ownership and influence; a sponsor wants visibility and may set content rules; a grant body wants public or cultural outcomes and detailed reporting. Choosing funding is therefore about <b>what the project can afford to give up</b>: money later, control, creative freedom or time spent on applications. A mixed funding plan reduces risk, because the project does not collapse if one source falls through. For small Caribbean creators, grants, sponsorship and rewards crowdfunding are usually more realistic than venture capital. Always check that a regional programme is currently open before naming it.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Benefit or disadvantage?","intro":"<p>This is the \"Funding a Digital Media Project\" activity. Each card is a statement about one of seven funding categories: crowdfunding, grants, angel investors, loans, venture capital, sponsorships and partnerships, and bootstrapping. Is it a benefit or a disadvantage for the creator?</p>","main":"Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?","bins":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"Sponsorships and partnerships: creative choices may need to follow sponsor guidelines.","bin":1,"fb":"A sponsor protects its brand, which can limit what you say or show: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Grants: they are competitive and demand detailed proposals, timelines and reporting.","bin":1,"fb":"Many applicants, long forms and strict reports take time and may still end in rejection: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Bootstrapping: it preserves complete ownership and creative freedom.","bin":0,"fb":"Using your own money means nobody else owns or directs the project: a benefit."},{"t":"Loans: you keep full ownership and creative control of the project.","bin":0,"fb":"A lender wants repayment, not shares or a say in your content: a benefit."},{"t":"Angel investors: they often add mentoring and industry connections along with the money.","bin":0,"fb":"Advice and contacts from an experienced investor can be worth as much as the cash: a benefit."},{"t":"Crowdfunding: it needs heavy promotion and frequent updates, and campaigns can fail to reach the goal.","bin":1,"fb":"A campaign is a lot of marketing work with no guarantee of success: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Crowdfunding: it can build a loyal audience early and prove demand before launch.","bin":0,"fb":"Backers are your first fans, and their pledges are evidence that people want the product: a benefit."},{"t":"Loans: repayments add financial pressure and are due even if income is delayed.","bin":1,"fb":"The bank still expects its monthly payment if the series launches late: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Grants: they provide money without giving up ownership or taking on debt.","bin":0,"fb":"Non-repayable money that costs you no shares is a major benefit."},{"t":"Venture capital: it typically requires significant equity and board or decision-making power.","bin":1,"fb":"Giving away a large share and control of direction is the main drawback of VC."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can weigh the benefit and the disadvantage of each funding source, which is exactly what \"evaluate\" questions ask for.","tutor":[{"h":"Whose side?","p":"<p>Read each card from the creator's point of view. Does it help them, or cost them time, money or control?</p>"},{"h":"Control and ownership","p":"<p>Statements about giving away shares or following someone else's rules usually describe a drawback.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Grants do not have to be repaid.","bin":0,"fb":"No repayment: a benefit."},{"t":"Loan repayments are due even if sales are slow.","bin":1,"fb":"Financial pressure: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Bootstrapping keeps complete creative freedom.","bin":0,"fb":"Full control: a benefit."},{"t":"Venture capital firms want a large share of the company.","bin":1,"fb":"Loss of ownership: a disadvantage."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Benefit or disadvantage? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: these statements come from real situations, and several show a hidden cost of a source that sounds attractive. Is each a benefit or a disadvantage for the creator?</p>","main":"Is this a benefit or a disadvantage?","bins":["Benefit","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"Grants: winning one adds credibility when applying to other funders.","bin":0,"fb":"A funder's approval signals quality: a benefit."},{"t":"Grants: the money may be restricted to named costs, such as equipment only.","bin":1,"fb":"Restrictions reduce flexibility: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Sponsorship: the sponsor asks the team to cut a scene that criticises mobile data prices.","bin":1,"fb":"Loss of creative freedom: a disadvantage."},{"t":"All-or-nothing crowdfunding: missing the target by USD 100 means receiving nothing.","bin":1,"fb":"A near miss still collects nothing: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Crowdfunding: platform fees and the cost of making and posting rewards reduce the money actually received.","bin":1,"fb":"Hidden costs shrink the real total: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Venture capital: follow-on funding is easier to arrange for later growth.","bin":0,"fb":"VC firms help arrange later funding rounds: a benefit."},{"t":"Loans: interest means the project ends up costing more than its budget.","bin":1,"fb":"Interest is an extra cost: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Angel investor: her contacts put the series in front of a regional broadcaster.","bin":0,"fb":"Industry connections are a key benefit of angels."},{"t":"Sponsorship: a telecom sponsor provides free data for viewers (in-kind support).","bin":0,"fb":"Useful support beyond cash: a benefit."},{"t":"Crowdfunding: backers' pledges provide evidence of demand to show a future funder.","bin":0,"fb":"Proof of demand strengthens later applications: a benefit."},{"t":"Bootstrapping: no applications or pitches are needed, so work can start immediately.","bin":0,"fb":"Speed and independence: a benefit."},{"t":"Angel investor: she wants a say in which stories are told.","bin":1,"fb":"Influence over decisions comes with equity: a disadvantage."}],"win":"Excellent. You spotted the hidden costs and the less obvious benefits, which is what turns a list into an evaluation."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Debt, equity and FFF","html":"<p>Funding is grouped into <b>debt</b> (lending institutions) and <b>equity</b> (crowdfunding, angels, venture capitalists and other investors). It starts with the people closest to you.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Friends, family and fools (FFF)</b> are the first source of money for many creators: people who are close to you, or who believe in your idea so strongly that they will risk their own money on it. (\"Fools\" is a light-hearted word for backers who invest on trust rather than evidence.)</p>\n<p>FFF money is often the easiest to get, but it mixes personal relationships with business. Many new ventures fail, so ask yourself whether you are ready to lose a friend's or a relative's savings. Protect the relationship by putting the deal in writing: is it a gift, a loan (repaid by when?) or a share of the business?</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Debt financing</b> means borrowing money that must be paid back, usually with <b>interest</b>, on an agreed schedule. It is the type most used by small business owners and new entrants.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Where</b>: commercial banks, credit unions (owned by their members), microcredit lenders and other loan providers.</li>\n<li><b>Secured loan</b>: backed by <b>collateral</b> (an asset such as equipment, a vehicle or property) that the lender can take if you do not repay.</li>\n<li><b>Unsecured loan</b>: no collateral, so it usually carries a higher interest rate and needs a good credit record.</li>\n<li><b>Guarantor</b>: a person who signs to repay the loan if you cannot. Young creators often need a parent or guardian as guarantor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Equity financing</b> means raising money by selling part of the <b>ownership</b> (shares) of your business. There is no monthly repayment; investors are rewarded through profits or by selling their shares later.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Must it be repaid?</td><td>Yes, with interest, on a schedule</td><td>No fixed repayment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ownership and control</td><td>You keep 100%</td><td>You share ownership and some decisions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main risk to you</td><td>Repayments due even if sales are slow</td><td>Loss of control; profits are shared</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Projects with steady, predictable income</td><td>High-growth ideas that need larger sums</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Bootstrapping</b> (self-funding) sits outside both: you use your own savings, income or pre-sales. Salt Pond Animation Studio in Sandy Ground, Anguilla, began this way, paying for its first animated short with income from wedding videos, before taking a credit union loan for two new workstations.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to <b>evaluate</b> a funding source, give a benefit and a disadvantage, then reach a judgement for the specific project: \"A loan suits the studio because its client work brings in steady monthly income to cover the repayments.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Friends, family and \"fools\" (FFF) are often the first people to fund a creator, but mixing money and relationships is risky, so put the deal in writing. Debt is borrowed money repaid with interest; a secured loan uses an asset as collateral. Equity means selling shares of your business. A young animator in Grenada may need a parent as guarantor for a loan.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The choice between debt and equity depends on the project's <b>cash flow</b> and <b>growth potential</b>. Debt suits projects with steady, predictable income, because repayments start on schedule whether or not sales arrive; a studio with regular client work can carry a loan, while a first web series with no income cannot. Equity suits high-growth ideas that need larger sums, but the founder permanently shares ownership and profits. FFF money can be either, which is exactly why it needs a written agreement: is it a gift, a loan with a repayment date or a share of the business? Unsecured loans cost more because the lender takes more risk. A Grade One evaluation states a benefit, a drawback and a judgement for the specific project.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Debt or equity?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is explaining funding offers to a new client, the Salt Pond Animation Studio in Sandy Ground, Anguilla. Sort each offer.</p>","main":"Is this debt or equity?","bins":["Debt","Equity"],"items":[{"t":"Fans invest through an equity crowdfunding platform and each receive a small share of the company.","bin":1,"fb":"Equity crowdfunding gives backers shares, so it is equity."},{"t":"A short-term bridge loan pays the voice actors until a grant arrives, then is paid back.","bin":0,"fb":"Bridge funding is temporary borrowing that is repaid: debt."},{"t":"A credit union lends to the studio, secured against its camera and lighting equipment.","bin":0,"fb":"A secured loan uses assets as collateral, but it is still borrowing: debt."},{"t":"The owner's uncle lends XCD 5,000 under a written agreement to be repaid within a year.","bin":0,"fb":"FFF money can be either type. This one must be repaid, so it is debt."},{"t":"The owner's aunt gives XCD 5,000 in exchange for a 5% share of the studio.","bin":1,"fb":"FFF money in exchange for ownership is equity."},{"t":"A venture capital firm invests a large sum, takes 30% ownership and a seat on the board.","bin":1,"fb":"VC is equity funding: a big stake plus influence over direction."},{"t":"An angel investor puts in USD 30,000 in return for 10% of the studio's shares.","bin":1,"fb":"Money in exchange for a share of ownership is equity."},{"t":"300 people each lend a small amount through an online platform and are repaid with interest.","bin":0,"fb":"This is debt crowdfunding (peer-to-peer lending): many small loans, still debt."},{"t":"A local bank lends XCD 20,000 for animation computers, repaid monthly over three years at an agreed interest rate.","bin":0,"fb":"Borrowed money repaid with interest is debt. The bank owns no part of the studio."}],"win":"Well sorted. Debt is borrowed and repaid with interest; equity is money in exchange for a share of ownership. Friends and family money can be either, so always read the deal.","tutor":[{"h":"Who owns what afterwards?","p":"<p>If the funder ends up owning part of the studio, which type is it?</p>"},{"h":"Is there a repayment schedule?","p":"<p>Money that must come back, with interest, by a date belongs in one bin only.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Debt or equity? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A bank loan repaid monthly with interest.","bin":0,"fb":"Borrowed and repaid: debt."},{"t":"An angel investor takes 10% of the shares.","bin":1,"fb":"A share of ownership: equity."},{"t":"An uncle lends money to be repaid in a year.","bin":0,"fb":"A loan, even from family: debt."},{"t":"A venture capital firm takes 30% ownership.","bin":1,"fb":"Ownership stake: equity."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Debt or equity? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: a third bin for money that is NEITHER debt nor equity. Sort each offer to Salt Pond Animation Studio.</p>","main":"Is this debt, equity or neither?","bins":["Debt","Equity","Neither debt nor equity"],"items":[{"t":"Peer-to-peer lending: many small online loans, repaid with interest.","bin":0,"fb":"Debt crowdfunding is still borrowing: debt."},{"t":"The owner's aunt invests XCD 5,000 for a 5% stake.","bin":1,"fb":"FFF money for ownership is equity."},{"t":"The owner's grandmother lends XCD 3,000 under a written agreement, repayable by December.","bin":0,"fb":"FFF money that must be repaid is debt."},{"t":"A bank loan for new workstations, repaid monthly with interest.","bin":0,"fb":"Borrowed and repaid with interest: debt."},{"t":"A credit union loan secured against the studio's camera equipment.","bin":0,"fb":"A secured loan is debt backed by collateral."},{"t":"A culture ministry grant that does not have to be repaid.","bin":2,"fb":"Grants are neither borrowed nor paid for with shares."},{"t":"Income from the owner's wedding videos pays for the first short film.","bin":2,"fb":"Bootstrapping uses your own money: neither."},{"t":"Equity crowdfunding backers each receive a small share of the company.","bin":1,"fb":"Shares for backers: equity."},{"t":"Rewards crowdfunding: backers receive stickers and a credit in the film.","bin":2,"fb":"Backers get rewards, not shares or repayment."},{"t":"An angel invests USD 30,000 for 10% of the shares.","bin":1,"fb":"Money for ownership: equity."},{"t":"A sponsor pays for its logo to appear at the end of each episode.","bin":2,"fb":"The sponsor buys visibility, not ownership or repayment."}],"win":"Excellent. Debt is repaid, equity buys ownership, and grants, sponsorship, rewards crowdfunding and bootstrapping are neither."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Angels, venture capital and equity financing","html":"<p class=\"passage\">An <b>angel investor</b> (also called a private investor, seed investor or angel funder) is a high-net-worth individual who provides financial backing for small start-ups or entrepreneurs, typically in exchange for <b>ownership equity</b> in the company.</p>\n<p>Angels are sometimes found within an entrepreneur's own circle of family, friends and contacts. Their money may be a one-time investment to get the business off the ground, or an ongoing injection to carry it through the difficult early stages. Many angels also give advice, contacts and credibility.</p>\n<p><b>Update:</b> Angel funding is sometimes described as paid back \"at the agreed upon interest rate\". In practice, angel investors <b>normally take equity</b> (a share of the company) rather than repayment with interest: they earn their return if the business grows and its shares become more valuable. Some angels use a <b>convertible note</b>, a loan that later turns into shares instead of being repaid in cash, but equity is the typical arrangement. Money that is simply repaid with interest is a loan (debt). Either way, the terms belong in a signed agreement.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Equity financing</b> raises money by selling <b>shares</b> in proportion to the amount each investor puts in.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To issue shares, the business must be <b>legally registered</b> as a company. In most territories, businesses register with a companies registry or a Chamber of Commerce.</li>\n<li>Investors usually expect a formal <b>pitch</b> and business proposal; they then study the project and ask questions about its <b>feasibility</b> before deciding.</li>\n<li>Shareholders have a strong say in the company's operations, and the company must give them regular reports (for example quarterly and yearly).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Update:</b> IPO stands for <b>Initial Public Offering</b>: the first time a company sells its shares to the general public on a stock exchange (in the region, for example, the Jamaica Stock Exchange or the Trinidad and Tobago Stock Exchange). An IPO is costly and heavily regulated, so it is used by <b>large, established companies</b> with a track record. It is not a typical first step for a small creator or a student start-up, which would look to FFF, angels or equity crowdfunding first.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Venture capital (VC)</b> is equity funding from firms that invest large sums in young companies with high growth potential, in return for a significant ownership stake.</p>\n<p>VC suits new companies with a limited operating history but a big, scalable idea, such as an app aimed at the whole Caribbean and its diaspora. VC investors usually want a board seat, help decide the company's direction and arrange later rounds of funding. A small studio making local content would rarely attract VC; an angel, a loan or a grant is a more realistic fit.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Order equity sources by stage and size: FFF and angels (early, smaller sums), venture capital (growth, larger sums), IPO (large, established company). An exam answer that suggests an IPO for a student project loses credibility.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An angel investor is a wealthy person who invests their own money in an early business, usually for shares, and often gives advice too. Venture capital firms invest larger sums in fast-growing companies and want a big share and a board seat. An IPO is when a large company first sells its shares to the public. A student start-up in Trinidad would not begin with an IPO.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Equity sources follow a <b>stage and size</b> ladder: friends, family and angels at the start with smaller sums, venture capital for growth with larger sums, and an IPO only for large, established companies with a track record, such as those listed on the Jamaica or Trinidad and Tobago stock exchanges. Each step up brings more money and more obligations: regular reports to shareholders, a board that influences direction and pressure to grow quickly. That pressure can clash with a creative mission, such as making authentic local content that may never be hugely profitable. Remember the correction in the notes: angels normally take equity, not interest, and money repaid with interest is a loan. Selling shares also requires the business to be registered as a company.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which funding source?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is advising clients on how to fund their projects. Sort each description.</p>","main":"Which source of funding is described?","bins":["Government grant","Crowdfunding","Sponsorship","Angel investor"],"items":[{"t":"420 people each pledge between USD 5 and USD 100 on Kickstarter to fund a Caribbean folklore animation.","bin":1,"fb":"Many small online pledges from the public is crowdfunding."},{"t":"A ministry awards XCD 15,000 for a youth culture documentary; it does not need to be repaid but receipts must be submitted.","bin":0,"fb":"Public money for a project that meets public goals, with reporting rules: a government grant."},{"t":"A beverage company pays for the stage screens at a festival in return for its logo on every video.","bin":2,"fb":"Money in return for visibility is sponsorship."},{"t":"The applicant must complete a formal application form and meet a fixed deadline set by a public department.","bin":0,"fb":"Formal applications to a public department are the typical route to a government grant."},{"t":"A retired businesswoman invests USD 50,000 in a student's app start-up for 15% of the company.","bin":3,"fb":"Personal money for a share of ownership (equity) is angel investment."},{"t":"A mobile network provides free data for viewers of a learning app, and its name appears on the splash screen.","bin":2,"fb":"In-kind support in exchange for brand visibility is sponsorship."},{"t":"Supporters receive rewards such as stickers, a credit in the film or an early download, depending on how much they pledge.","bin":1,"fb":"Reward tiers are a typical feature of crowdfunding campaigns."},{"t":"The investor also mentors the team and joins its board of directors.","bin":3,"fb":"Angel investors often bring advice and contacts, and may want a say in decisions."}],"win":"Well sorted. Grants are given, crowdfunding comes from many small backers, sponsors buy visibility and angel investors buy equity.","tutor":[{"h":"What does the funder get?","p":"<p>Nothing but results? Visibility? A reward? Ownership? Each answer points to a different source.</p>"},{"h":"How many funders?","p":"<p>Hundreds of small amounts suggests one particular source.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which funding source? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Public money that need not be repaid.","bin":0,"fb":"Government grant."},{"t":"Many people pledge small amounts online.","bin":1,"fb":"Crowdfunding."},{"t":"A company pays to show its logo.","bin":2,"fb":"Sponsorship."},{"t":"An individual invests for a share of the company.","bin":3,"fb":"Angel investor."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which funding source? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: six sources now, including loans and bootstrapping, and each has two less obvious examples. Sort each description.</p>","main":"Which source of funding is described?","bins":["Government or cultural grant","Crowdfunding","Sponsorship","Angel investor","Loan","Bootstrapping"],"items":[{"t":"Income from wedding videos pays for the studio's first short film.","bin":5,"fb":"Using your own income is bootstrapping."},{"t":"A regional fund's application asks for objectives, a timeline and a budget by a fixed deadline.","bin":0,"fb":"Formal applications with deadlines are typical of grants."},{"t":"A culture ministry awards XCD 15,000 and asks for receipts for every cost.","bin":0,"fb":"Non-repayable public money with reporting: a grant."},{"t":"Pre-sales of an e-book on the author's own website pay for its illustrations.","bin":5,"fb":"Small pre-sales of your own product count as bootstrapping."},{"t":"A retired businesswoman invests USD 50,000 for 15% of the company and mentors the team.","bin":3,"fb":"Personal money for equity, plus advice: an angel investor."},{"t":"420 people pledge between USD 5 and USD 100 on Kickstarter.","bin":1,"fb":"Many small online pledges: crowdfunding."},{"t":"Supporters pay a small monthly amount on Patreon for behind-the-scenes content.","bin":1,"fb":"Ongoing memberships from many fans are a form of crowdfunding."},{"t":"A mobile network provides free data for viewers, with its name on the splash screen.","bin":2,"fb":"In-kind support for brand exposure: sponsorship."},{"t":"An individual backs the start-up with her own money and joins its board.","bin":3,"fb":"Private equity backing with influence: an angel."},{"t":"A credit union lends XCD 8,000, repaid over two years.","bin":4,"fb":"Borrowed money repaid with interest: a loan."},{"t":"A microlender gives a small business loan to a young entrepreneur.","bin":4,"fb":"Microcredit is a form of loan."},{"t":"A beverage company pays for the festival screens in return for its logo on every video.","bin":2,"fb":"Money for visibility: sponsorship."}],"win":"Excellent. You identified six funding sources from what each one gives and expects in return."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Crowdfunding models and other funding terms","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Crowdfunding</b> is a way to raise money by collecting small, individual contributions from a large pool of backers (the \"crowd\"), usually through an online platform and social media.</p>\n<p>There are three main models:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Model</th><th>What backers get</th><th>Best for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Equity crowdfunding</b></td><td>Shares in the company, or a right to part of its revenue or profits</td><td>Early-stage companies that want to grow</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Debt crowdfunding</b> (peer-to-peer lending)</td><td>Repayment with interest; each lender spreads risk by lending small amounts across many loans</td><td>Businesses with income to make repayments</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Donation or rewards-based</b></td><td>Nothing (donation), or a reward such as a credit, merchandise or the finished product (rewards)</td><td>Community causes, creative projects, first products</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Donation crowdfunding suits non-profit causes such as a youth sports team or hurricane relief; rewards crowdfunding is widely used for films, games, comics and gadgets.</p>\n<p><b>Update:</b> The notes say that if the target is not met, the funds are returned. That is true on <b>all-or-nothing</b> platforms such as <b>Kickstarter</b>: backers are only charged if the target is reached by the deadline, so a creator who misses it collects nothing. It is not always true, though. Some platforms, such as <b>Indiegogo</b>, offer <b>flexible funding</b>, where you keep what you raise even if you miss the goal (and still owe backers their rewards), and donation sites such as <b>GoFundMe</b> normally let you keep donations. Always read the platform's rules.</p>\n<p><b>Update:</b> The \"6 Best Crowdfunding Platforms of 2022\" list in the slides is now dated: for example, SeedInvest was taken over by StartEngine in 2023. Current well-known examples are <b>Kickstarter</b> and <b>Indiegogo</b> (rewards), <b>GoFundMe</b> (donations), <b>Patreon</b> (ongoing memberships for creators) and <b>StartEngine</b> (equity). Rankings change, and some platforms limit which countries creators can launch from, so check current terms and fees before relying on one.</p>\n<p><b>Other funding terms</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Restricted funding</b>: may only be used for the specific purpose it was given for, and the funder can ask for reports. A grant for \"equipment only\" is restricted.</li>\n<li><b>Unrestricted funding</b>: can be used in any way the organisation or person chooses, often for running costs or to cover a shortfall to finish production.</li>\n<li><b>Bridge funding</b> (temporary funding): covers a short-term need while you wait for promised money, and is paid back when that money arrives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Earned revenue</b> is different from funding: digital publishers now earn through <b>subscriptions</b> and <b>advertising</b>. Readers and viewers will pay only for content they value and cannot find free elsewhere.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In your SBA budget, label any restricted money. A funder can reject a report that shows an \"equipment only\" grant spent on boosted posts.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Crowdfunding collects small amounts from many people online. In equity crowdfunding, backers get shares; in debt crowdfunding, they are repaid with interest; in donation or rewards crowdfunding, they get nothing or a reward such as a credit or a sticker. Some platforms are all-or-nothing. Restricted funding can only be spent on its stated purpose, as a film group in Dominica should remember.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Crowdfunding is really a <b>marketing campaign</b> that raises money as a result. Success depends on an existing audience, a strong video, realistic reward tiers and regular updates, so creators who build a community first do far better. Platform rules change the risk: on all-or-nothing platforms, missing the target means collecting nothing, so the target must be realistic; flexible funding lets you keep what you raise, but you still owe every reward, which can be costly if few people pledge. Fees and reward costs belong in the budget, or a \"successful\" campaign can lose money. Restricted and unrestricted funding matter in the budget too: label restricted money clearly, because a funder can reclaim money spent on the wrong things.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the funding term","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is preparing a glossary for a funding workshop for young creators in Kingstown. Match each term to its definition.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Debt financing","r":"Borrowed money repaid with interest on an agreed schedule"},{"l":"Equity financing","r":"Money raised by selling shares of ownership in the business"},{"l":"FFF","r":"Early money from friends, family and supporters who back you on trust"},{"l":"Angel investor","r":"A wealthy individual who invests personal money in an early-stage business, usually for equity"},{"l":"Venture capital","r":"A firm investing large sums in high-growth companies for a big stake and board influence"},{"l":"Restricted funding","r":"Money that may only be spent on the purpose the funder specified"},{"l":"Bridge funding","r":"Temporary money to cover a short gap until expected funding arrives"},{"l":"Bootstrapping","r":"Funding a project from your own savings, income or pre-sales"}],"win":"Well matched. You can define the key funding terms precisely, which earns the \"define\" and \"distinguish\" marks.","tutor":[{"h":"Repay or share?","p":"<p>Two terms are the main types of funding: one is about repayment, the other about ownership.</p>"},{"h":"Size and stage","p":"<p>One investor is a single person at the early stage; the other is a firm putting in much larger sums.</p>"},{"h":"Rules and timing","p":"<p>One term limits how money is spent; another is about filling a gap in time.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the funding term (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Debt financing","r":"Borrowed money repaid with interest"},{"l":"Equity financing","r":"Money in exchange for shares of ownership"},{"l":"Restricted funding","r":"Money that can only be spent on one stated purpose"},{"l":"Bridge funding","r":"Short-term money until promised funds arrive"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the funding term (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: eight more precise terms for Malik's funding workshop glossary. Match each term to its definition.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Secured loan","r":"A loan backed by collateral that the lender can take if it is not repaid"},{"l":"Unsecured loan","r":"A loan with no collateral, usually at a higher interest rate"},{"l":"Guarantor","r":"A person who signs to repay a loan if the borrower cannot"},{"l":"Convertible note","r":"A loan that later turns into shares instead of being repaid in cash"},{"l":"IPO","r":"The first sale of a company's shares to the public on a stock exchange"},{"l":"All-or-nothing crowdfunding","r":"Backers are charged only if the target is reached by the deadline"},{"l":"Flexible funding","r":"The creator keeps whatever is raised, even if the goal is missed, but still owes the rewards"},{"l":"Unrestricted funding","r":"Money that can be spent however the organisation chooses, such as on running costs"}],"win":"Excellent. You can define the finer funding terms precisely, which helps when you evaluate an offer."}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales","intro":"<p>Zara and Malik at the Island Pulse Media want to make a six-episode animated web series, Salt Pond Tales, retelling Caribbean folk stories for children. The budget is XCD 45,000. You are advising them on funding.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Zara asks for your first funding step. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Plan an IPO on a regional stock exchange to raise the whole amount.","to":"bad1","fb":"An Initial Public Offering is for large, established companies, not a first project."},{"t":"Apply to a CARICOM-region arts council or cultural grant programme with the full proposal, budget and timeline, and ask a local business to sponsor the launch.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. A non-repayable grant plus a sponsor starts a mixed funding plan without debt or loss of ownership."},{"t":"Borrow the full XCD 45,000 as an unsecured, high-interest loan, even though no income is expected until the series is finished.","to":"bad2","fb":"Repayments would start long before the series earns anything."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The grant panel awards XCD 25,000, restricted to animation and voice talent. Bay Street Bakehouse sponsors XCD 5,000 for its logo at the end of each episode. XCD 15,000 is still needed. Keisha suggests crowdfunding. Which campaign do you plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A rewards-based Kickstarter campaign with a realistic XCD 15,000 target, a 60-second trailer, reward tiers (name in the credits, sticker pack, early access) and weekly updates.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. A realistic target, a strong video and clear rewards give an all-or-nothing campaign the best chance."},{"t":"A Kickstarter campaign with an XCD 60,000 target \"to be safe\", promoted with one post.","to":"bad3","fb":"On an all-or-nothing platform, missing a target that high means collecting nothing."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The campaign succeeds, but the grant payment is delayed by two months and the voice actors must be paid now. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Arrange short-term bridge funding, such as a small credit union loan, repay it when the grant arrives and add the interest to the budget.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Bridge funding is designed for exactly this kind of short gap."},{"t":"Accept an angel investor's offer of XCD 10,000 for 40% of the rights to the series.","to":"bad4","fb":"That gives away a large, permanent share to solve a two-month problem."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The grant arrives. Malik suggests using some of it to pay the office rent and boost social media posts. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No: the grant is restricted to animation and voice talent. Pay rent and promotion from unrestricted money, such as the sponsor fee, and keep receipts for the grant report.","to":"good","fb":"Exactly. Restricted funding must be spent only on its stated purpose, and reports must prove it."},{"t":"Yes: the funder will probably not check.","to":"bad5","fb":"Misusing restricted money breaks the agreement and damages your reputation with funders."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Fully funded.</b> Salt Pond Tales is made on budget with a grant, a sponsor, a successful crowdfunding campaign and a short bridge loan, and Island Pulse Media keeps full ownership. A Grade One answer would explain why: mixed sources, a realistic target, the right tool for a short gap and respect for restricted funding.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong stage.</b> IPO means Initial Public Offering, used by large, established companies with years of records. A first web series needs grants, sponsors, crowdfunding or small loans. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Debt trap.</b> Monthly repayments with high interest begin months before the series earns any money, putting the whole agency under pressure. Debt suits projects with steady income. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign failed.</b> Kickstarter is all-or-nothing: the target was missed, so backers were not charged and the project received nothing. Set a realistic target and promote it properly. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Too much equity.</b> The angel now owns 40% of the series and wants a say in the stories. A temporary gap needed temporary bridge funding, not permanent equity. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Grant clawed back.</b> The final report shows restricted money spent on rent and ads, and the funder asks for it back and refuses future applications. Try again and respect restricted funding.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Match source to stage","p":"<p>Which sources suit a first project with no income yet?</p>"},{"h":"All-or-nothing","p":"<p>What happens on Kickstarter if you miss your target?</p>"},{"h":"Read the conditions","p":"<p>Is the money temporary, restricted, or in exchange for ownership?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Island Pulse Media needs XCD 45,000 for an animated web series.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which funding plan is best for a first project?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A cultural grant, a local sponsor and a realistic crowdfunding campaign.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Mixed sources with no heavy debt."},{"t":"An IPO on a stock exchange.","to":"bad","fb":"IPOs are for large, established companies."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The grant is restricted to animation costs. Can you spend it on office rent?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No, rent must come from unrestricted money.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. Restricted money has one purpose."},{"t":"Yes, money is money.","to":"bad","fb":"Restricted funding can only be used as agreed."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You chose suitable sources and respected the grant conditions.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Match the source to the project stage and follow funding conditions. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Funding Salt Pond Tales (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief: XCD 45,000 for six episodes. Island Pulse Media earns about XCD 3,000 a month from client work. Every decision has a trade-off, and some end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What is your first funding recommendation?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A mixed plan: apply for a cultural grant now, approach a local sponsor, and run a crowdfunding campaign later to prove demand.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Several sources spread the risk and none of them costs ownership."},{"t":"Plan an IPO on a regional stock exchange.","to":"bad1","fb":"An IPO is for large, established companies, not a first web series."},{"t":"Fund it entirely from client income over the next 15 months.","to":"ok1","fb":"Full control and no debt, but the series takes well over a year and stalls if client work dips."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The grant panel awards XCD 25,000, restricted to animation and voice talent. A soft-drinks company offers XCD 5,000, but wants its mascot to appear in every episode. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Negotiate in writing: the logo in the end credits and a \"presented by\" card, but no mascot inside the stories.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. The sponsor gets visibility and the stories keep their integrity."},{"t":"Refuse all sponsorship.","to":"ok3","fb":"You keep full control, but now need XCD 20,000 from other sources."},{"t":"Accept the mascot in every episode.","to":"ok2","fb":"The money arrives, but the folk tales now carry a soft-drink advert."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>XCD 15,000 is still needed. Which crowdfunding set-up do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Flexible funding, with a printed book posted to every backer who pledges XCD 10.","to":"bad2","fb":"Each reward costs more than the pledge, so every backer loses the project money."},{"t":"An all-or-nothing rewards campaign with an XCD 15,000 target, rewards costed into the budget (making and posting them), 30 days, a trailer and weekly updates.","to":"n4","fb":"Realistic and fully costed, with the promotion it needs."},{"t":"An all-or-nothing campaign with an XCD 60,000 target \"to be safe\".","to":"bad3","fb":"An unrealistic target on an all-or-nothing platform risks collecting nothing."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The grant payment is two months late and the voice actors must be paid XCD 6,000 now. A credit union offers a bridge loan at 1% interest per month. An angel offers XCD 10,000 for 40% of the series rights. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Take the bridge loan, add the interest (about XCD 120 for two months) to the budget and repay it when the grant arrives.","to":"n5","fb":"Correct. A short gap needs short-term money, and the cost is small and budgeted."},{"t":"Accept the angel's offer.","to":"bad4","fb":"A permanent 40% share to solve a two-month gap is far too costly."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The grant arrives. Malik suggests using some of it for office rent and boosted posts. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Yes, because the funder will probably not check.","to":"bad5","fb":"Misusing restricted funding breaks the agreement and damages your reputation."},{"t":"No: the grant is restricted to animation and voice talent. Pay rent and promotion from unrestricted money, such as the sponsor fee, and keep every receipt for the grant report.","to":"good","fb":"Exactly. Restricted money must be spent only on its stated purpose."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Fully funded, fully owned.</b> Salt Pond Tales is made on budget from a grant, a negotiated sponsorship, a costed crowdfunding campaign and a cheap bridge loan, and Island Pulse Media keeps all the rights. You matched each source to its job and respected every condition.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow but independent.</b> The series is finished, but more than a year later and only after cutting two episodes when client work slowed. A partial success: combine self-funding with outside sources.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Compromised content.</b> The series is funded, but critics say the stories feel like adverts. A partial success: negotiate what a sponsor can and cannot control.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Under-funded.</b> Without the sponsor, the crowdfunding target becomes too high and two episodes are dropped. A partial success: negotiate rather than refuse.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong stage.</b> No exchange would list a first web series. Try again with grants, sponsors, crowdfunding or a small loan.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Losing money.</b> Hundreds of XCD 10 pledges each need a book that costs more than XCD 10 to print and post. Try again and cost every reward.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign failed.</b> The target is missed and the project receives nothing. Try again with a realistic target.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Too much equity.</b> The angel now owns 40% of the series and wants changes to the stories. Try again and use temporary money for a temporary gap.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Grant clawed back.</b> The report shows restricted money spent on rent and ads, and the funder demands it back. Try again and respect restricted funding.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Budget planning: itemised costs","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>budget</b> is an itemised estimate of all the costs of a project, showing the quantity, unit cost and total for each item, so a funder can see exactly where the money will go.</p>\n<p>The SBA budget criterion (3 marks) asks for a budget that is <b>realistic and includes all critical elements</b>, for example design and building, content creation, maintenance, equipment, resource persons, transportation and <b>website hosting</b>. You lose marks for each missing element or unrealistic figure. Group costs into categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Production</b>: content creation, filming days, voice-over, printing, transport to locations.</li>\n<li><b>Equipment</b>: cameras, microphones, lighting, tripods, drawing tablets (buy, rent or borrow).</li>\n<li><b>Software</b>: subscriptions and licences (Adobe Creative Cloud, Canva Pro), or free options (GIMP, Blender, DaVinci Resolve).</li>\n<li><b>Personnel</b>: designers, developers, actors, voice talent, translators, resource persons.</li>\n<li><b>Hosting and maintenance</b>: domain name, web hosting, app store developer fees, updates.</li>\n<li><b>Marketing</b>: boosted posts, promotional ad production, launch event.</li>\n<li><b>Contingency</b>: usually 5% to 10% of the subtotal for unexpected costs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Sample budget</b> for Willemstad AR Heritage Walk (XCG; the Caribbean guilder is pegged at about XCG 1.79 = USD 1). Prices are illustrative estimates; get real quotes for your own budget.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Item</th><th>Qty</th><th>Unit cost (XCG)</th><th>Total (XCG)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Production</td><td>Historian consultant (resource person)</td><td>10 hours</td><td>90</td><td>900</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Production</td><td>Transport to six sites</td><td>6 trips</td><td>40</td><td>240</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Camera and lens rental</td><td>5 days</td><td>110</td><td>550</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Lavalier microphone (purchase)</td><td>2</td><td>90</td><td>180</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Software</td><td>Design software subscription</td><td>4 months</td><td>110</td><td>440</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Software</td><td>Blender and Unity (free tier)</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personnel</td><td>Voice-over artist</td><td>6 scripts</td><td>75</td><td>450</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Domain and web hosting</td><td>12 months</td><td>25</td><td>300</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Google Play developer fee (one-time, USD 25)</td><td>1</td><td>45</td><td>45</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marketing</td><td>Boosted posts and QR posters</td><td>1</td><td>400</td><td>400</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"4\"><b>Subtotal</b></td><td><b>3,505</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"4\"><b>Contingency (10%)</b></td><td><b>351</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"4\"><b>Grand total</b></td><td><b>3,856</b> (about USD 2,154)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Show items you will get free or in-kind (donated equipment, free software, volunteer time) with a value of 0 or note them: this proves you considered them. Check every calculation: <b>quantity × unit cost = total</b>.</p>\n<p><b>From budget to funding plan.</b> Once you know the grand total, show how it will be funded. Add a short <b>funding plan</b> under the budget: source, type, amount and status (confirmed, applied for, planned). Mark any <b>restricted</b> money, count loan interest and platform fees as costs, and make sure the plan adds up to the grand total.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Source</th><th>Type</th><th>Amount (XCG)</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Curaçao Youth Heritage Foundation grant (restricted to content)</td><td>Grant</td><td>2,000</td><td>Applied for</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Telecom sponsorship</td><td>Sponsorship</td><td>1,000</td><td>Confirmed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rewards crowdfunding</td><td>Crowdfunding</td><td>500</td><td>Planned</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Team savings</td><td>Bootstrapping</td><td>356</td><td>Confirmed</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"2\"><b>Total</b></td><td><b>3,856</b></td><td></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> State the <b>currency</b> once in the heading, keep units consistent and round sensibly. An examiner should be able to recalculate any line and get your answer. Missing hosting and contingency are the two most common SBA budget faults.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A budget lists every cost of a project, with the quantity, unit cost and total for each item. Group costs into production, equipment, software, personnel, hosting and maintenance, and marketing, then add a contingency of 5% to 10%. Check every sum and show free or donated items too. A team budgeting for a music video in Tobago should get real price quotes.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A budget is a test of <b>realism</b>. Funders check whether quantities make sense, whether unit costs match local prices and whether anything critical is missing, such as hosting, software renewals or transport. Every line should be recalculable: quantity × unit cost = total. Contingency protects against the unexpected, but it is calculated on the subtotal, not guessed. The currency must be consistent; mixing XCG and USD without a conversion note causes errors. Showing in-kind and free items, valued at zero or noted, proves you considered them rather than forgot them. The budget then becomes the target for the funding plan, which must add up to the same grand total and must count loan interest and platform fees as costs.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Audit the budget","intro":"<p>A CAPE group in Jamaica is proposing a 30-video cooking channel. Maya at the Island Pulse Media is checking their budget lines.</p>","q":"Select every budget line that has a PROBLEM (missing detail, unrealistic figure, or a calculation error).","lines":["Camera rental: 4 days × USD 60 = USD 240","Food ingredients: lots of food = USD 100","Editing software: DaVinci Resolve free version = USD 0","Microphone: 2 × USD 45 = USD 120","Presenter fee: 30 videos × USD 0.50 = USD 15","Web hosting for the recipe site: 12 months × USD 8 = USD 96","Contingency (10% of subtotal) = USD 70","Miscellaneous stuff = USD 500"],"correct":[1,3,4,6,7],"win":"Good auditing. You spotted a vague quantity, a calculation error (2 × 45 = 90, not 120), an unrealistic fee, a contingency that is not 10% of the other lines, and an unexplained \"miscellaneous\" line.","miss":"Check each line: does it give a quantity and unit cost, does the arithmetic work, and is the figure realistic? Vague words like \"lots\" and \"stuff\" are not acceptable.","tutor":[{"h":"Do the maths","p":"<p>Multiply quantity by unit cost for every line.</p>"},{"h":"Realistic?","p":"<p>Would anyone present 30 videos for that fee?</p>"},{"h":"Specific?","p":"<p>An examiner must be able to see exactly what is being bought.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Audit the budget (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines with a problem.","lines":["Camera rental: 4 days × USD 60 = USD 240","Microphone: 2 × USD 45 = USD 120","Hosting: 12 months × USD 8 = USD 96","Miscellaneous stuff = USD 500"],"correct":[1,3],"win":"Yes. 2 × 45 = 90, and \"stuff\" is too vague.","miss":"Check the arithmetic and look for vague items."},
 "up": {"title":"Audit the budget (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: Maya audits a second budget for a Jamaican cooking channel. Some errors are in the arithmetic, some in the currency, some in realism and some in missing detail.</p>","q":"Select every budget line that has a PROBLEM.","lines":["Camera rental: 5 days × USD 55 = USD 275","Voice actors: 3 actors × 4 episodes × USD 40 = USD 360","Web hosting: USD 8 per month","Editing software: DaVinci Resolve (free version) = USD 0","Drawing tablet: 1 × USD 180 = USD 180","Transport: 6 trips × BBD 40 = USD 240","Presenter fee: 30 videos × USD 0.50 = USD 15","Domain name: 1 year × USD 15 = USD 15","Contingency: 25% of the subtotal","Marketing: \"some boosted posts\" = USD 100"],"correct":[1,2,5,6,8,9],"win":"Good auditing. 3 × 4 × 40 = 480, not 360; hosting has no quantity or total; the transport line mixes BBD and USD; USD 0.50 per video is unrealistic; 25% is far above the usual 5% to 10%; and \"some boosted posts\" has no quantity or unit cost.","miss":"Check each line four ways: is the arithmetic right, is there a quantity and a total, is the currency consistent, and is the figure realistic?"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Project pitching and visual pitch decks","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>pitch</b> is a short, persuasive presentation of a project to stakeholders (clients, funders, investors, judges, community leaders) to win their support.</p>\n<p><b>A strong pitch structure</b> (about 5 to 10 minutes, or 60 seconds for an \"elevator pitch\"):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Hook</b>: a striking fact, question or story. \"Seventy-eight per cent of students we surveyed could not name three historic sites in their own capital.\"</li>\n<li><b>Problem</b>: who is affected and why it matters.</li>\n<li><b>Solution</b>: your product, shown visually (mock-up, prototype demo, short video).</li>\n<li><b>Audience and market</b>: who will use it and the gap it fills.</li>\n<li><b>How it works and why you</b>: methodology, timeline and the team's skills.</li>\n<li><b>Budget and the ask</b>: what you need (\"XCG 3,856 and access to the six sites\").</li>\n<li><b>Impact</b>: what success looks like, with measurable objectives.</li>\n<li><b>Call to action and questions</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Visual pitch decks</b> support the talk; they do not replace it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Canva</b>: ready-made pitch deck templates, brand kits, easy charts, present or download as PDF or PPTX.</li>\n<li><b>Google Slides</b>: free, cloud-based, real-time collaboration for group work, comments and version history.</li>\n<li><b>PowerPoint</b>: industry standard, Designer suggestions, rehearse with Presenter Coach, works offline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Deck design rules</b> (apply your Module 1 design principles): about 10 to 12 slides; one idea per slide; large readable type (at least about 24 pt on screen); few words, strong images; consistent colours and fonts from your brand; charts for survey data; your logo on the title and closing slides. Use high-contrast colours so the deck is readable on a projector in a bright classroom.</p>\n<p><b>Delivery</b>: rehearse and time it; make eye contact; know your numbers; prepare for questions (\"What if you don't get full funding?\"); and have a backup (PDF on a USB drive) in case the Wi-Fi fails.</p>\n<p><b>SBA link: the 30-second short ad.</b> The Unit 2 Product section includes a <b>short ad</b>: an audio-visual presentation of <b>no more than 30 seconds</b> that sells the product or service (5 marks: 2 for description of the product, 3 for innovation and creativity in the use of digital tools, formatting, text, cropping, images and overall presentation). The mark scheme also lists a 30-second audio-visual pitch under Proposal/Research. Think of it as a pitch compressed into 30 seconds: hook (0 to 5 s), problem and solution (5 to 20 s), call to action with logo (20 to 30 s). Export as MP4 (H.264), 1920 × 1080 or 1080 × 1920, and check the duration before you submit.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Script your 30-second ad at about 70 to 80 spoken words. Any more and you will rush or run over time, and going over 30 seconds breaks the requirement.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A pitch is a short, persuasive talk that wins support for a project. Start with a hook, explain the problem and the solution, show who will use it, how it works, what it costs, exactly what you are asking for and the impact. Use a simple deck: one idea per slide, large text and strong images. Rehearse and prepare for questions, as a team pitching in Kingston would.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A pitch persuades through <b>story and evidence</b> together. The hook makes the panel care, often through one person's experience; data then proves the problem is real and widespread. The \"ask\" must be specific, an amount and what it will buy, because vague requests are easy to refuse. Slides support the speaker, so a deck crowded with text competes with your voice for attention. Anticipating questions is a strength, not a defensive move: \"What if you get only half the funding?\" is best answered with a phased plan. Know what each funder wants; an angel investor and a sponsor will judge the same pitch differently, so adjust the emphasis. Always carry an offline backup of the deck.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing the right deck slide","q":"A group is pitching the Tobago Reef Watch app to a panel of local sponsors. They have 10 minutes. Which slide design is MOST effective for presenting their survey results?","options":[{"t":"Five different charts on one slide, each in different colours and fonts, to show how much work was done.","fb":"Clutter and inconsistent design weaken the message. Choose the one chart that proves the point."},{"t":"A slide with the full questionnaire and every response pasted in 12-point text.","fb":"Too much text: the panel cannot read it from a distance and the key finding is lost. Put full data in an appendix."},{"t":"One slide with a bold headline (\"82% of divers want live reef updates\"), one clear bar chart in brand colours, and the source of the data in small text.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. One idea per slide, a visual chart, a headline that states the finding and a cited source make the evidence quick to grasp and credible."},{"t":"A slide with a dramatic stock photo of the sea and no data at all.","fb":"Attractive, but it gives no evidence. Sponsors need proof that users want the app."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Distance test","p":"<p>Could someone at the back of the room read it in five seconds?</p>"},{"h":"One idea","p":"<p>What single finding should the panel remember?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing the right slide (Foundation)","q":"What is the best way to show survey results in a pitch deck?","options":[{"t":"Every response pasted in small text.","fb":"Too much to read."},{"t":"A photo with no data.","fb":"No evidence."},{"t":"One clear chart with a headline stating the key finding.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Simple, visual and memorable."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing the right deck slide (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The Tobago Reef Watch team has 10 minutes with sponsors who care about tourism. Which overall deck plan BEST supports the pitch?","options":[{"t":"25 slides showing every survey question and answer, to prove how thorough the team was.","fb":"Thoroughness belongs in the appendix. The panel cannot absorb 25 slides in 10 minutes."},{"t":"Five slides of stunning reef photos with no data, then the total cost.","fb":"Attractive, but there is no evidence of the problem or the audience, and no clear ask."},{"t":"Start with the budget so sponsors know the cost at once, then the team's biographies, then the problem.","fb":"Leading with cost before the panel cares about the problem makes the ask harder to accept."},{"t":"About 10 slides, one idea each: a hook (a diver's 15-second clip), the problem with one chart, a solution demo, audience and gap, a budget summary with the exact ask, impact metrics and a call to action.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It follows the persuasive structure, uses evidence and ends with a specific ask."}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media","intro":"<p>Your CAPE group is pitching an app, Market Day KY, that lists local vendors at the George Town farmers' market, to a panel that includes a possible sponsor and an angel investor. Make the decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>You have 8 minutes. How do you open?</p>","choices":[{"t":"With a hook: a 15-second phone video of a vendor saying sales dropped because tourists cannot find her stall, plus one survey statistic.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. A human story and evidence grab attention and establish the problem."},{"t":"By reading your full proposal document aloud from the first page.","to":"bad1","fb":"Reading a document loses the audience. A pitch must be selective and engaging."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The sponsor asks, \"Why should we believe vendors and visitors will use this?\" How do you answer?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Everyone will love it, trust us.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"Claims without evidence do not persuade funders."},{"t":"Show your market analysis: survey of 60 visitors and 25 vendors, competitor gaps, and the persona slide.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. Evidence from your research answers the question convincingly."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The angel investor asks about money. Which budget slide do you show?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A single line: \"Total cost: about KYD 10,000, give or take.\"","to":"bad3","fb":"A vague figure suggests poor planning; investors want to see what the money buys."},{"t":"A clear itemised summary by category (production, equipment, software, personnel, hosting, marketing) with a 10% contingency, total in KYD and USD, and your exact ask.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Clear categories, contingency and a specific ask show you have planned realistically."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The investor offers money in return for 40% of the app. You only wanted sponsorship. What do you say?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Accept on the spot to secure the money.","to":"bad4","fb":"Giving away a large share without thinking can cost you control of your own project."},{"t":"Thank them, explain the difference between equity and sponsorship, and ask for time to consider and compare options.","to":"good","fb":"Wise. Understanding what each funding source costs you is part of a professional pitch."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Successful pitch.</b> The sponsor agrees to fund the launch campaign and the investor leaves the offer open. A Grade One answer would explain why: a strong hook, evidence-based market analysis, a realistic itemised budget, a clear ask and an understanding of funding sources.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost the room.</b> The panel stops listening within two minutes. Try again and open with a hook.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unconvinced panel.</b> Without data the sponsor declines. Try again and use your research evidence.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Credibility gap.</b> The investor doubts your planning and asks you to return with a proper budget. Try again with itemised costs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Control lost.</b> You now own much less of your own idea, and the investor wants changes you disagree with. Try again and weigh the offer first.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Hook and evidence","p":"<p>Open with a story or statistic, then prove the need.</p>"},{"h":"Show the numbers","p":"<p>Funders want categories, contingency and a clear ask.</p>"},{"h":"Know the source","p":"<p>What does an angel investor want in return that a sponsor does not?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Pitch day (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you are pitching a market app to sponsors.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which pitch plan is best?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A hook, survey evidence, an itemised budget and a clear ask.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. That is a convincing structure."},{"t":"Read the whole proposal aloud and give a rough total.","to":"bad","fb":"Too long and too vague."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your pitch was clear and evidence-based.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Pitches must be short, visual and specific. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Pitch day at Island Pulse Media (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher panel for Market Day KY: a telecom sponsor with conditions and an angel investor with a hard offer. Some choices end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>You have 8 minutes. How do you open?</p>","choices":[{"t":"By reading the proposal aloud from page one.","to":"bad1","fb":"Reading a document loses the room within minutes."},{"t":"With slides introducing each team member's skills.","to":"ok1","fb":"Credibility matters, but the panel does not yet know why the problem matters."},{"t":"With a hook: a 15-second phone video of a vendor explaining that tourists cannot find her stall, plus one survey statistic.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. A real person and real evidence establish the problem at once."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The sponsor asks: \"Why would vendors actually use this?\" How do you answer?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Everyone will love it, trust us.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"Claims without evidence do not persuade funders."},{"t":"Show your survey of 25 vendors and 60 visitors, the competitor gaps, and the three vendors already signed up for the pilot.","to":"n3","fb":"Evidence plus early commitment is very persuasive."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The angel investor asks about money. What do you show?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"About KYD 10,000, give or take.\"","to":"bad3","fb":"A vague figure suggests poor planning."},{"t":"An itemised summary by category with a 10% contingency, the total in KYD and USD, an exact ask of KYD 6,000 for the pilot, and a phased plan if only half arrives.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Clear, realistic and ready for the \"what if\" question."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The sponsor says: \"We will fund it if only OUR network's customers can see vendor discounts.\" How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Thank them, explain that excluding other networks' users would hurt the vendors and the audience, and offer prominent sponsor branding plus a data-free offer for their customers instead.","to":"n5","fb":"Professional. You protect the product's purpose and still give the sponsor value."},{"t":"Accept the condition.","to":"ok2","fb":"The money arrives, but most visitors are shut out of the discounts the vendors wanted."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The angel offers KYD 6,000 for 40% of the app. You came for sponsorship. What do you say?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Thank them, explain the difference between equity and sponsorship, and ask for time to compare options and take advice before deciding.","to":"good","fb":"Wise. You understand what each offer costs you."},{"t":"Accept on the spot.","to":"bad4","fb":"Giving away 40% without thought can cost you control of your own project."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One pitch.</b> The sponsor funds the launch on fair terms and the investor leaves the offer open. A strong hook, real evidence, an itemised ask with a fallback, a principled negotiation and an understanding of equity won the room.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow start.</b> The panel warms up only halfway through, and you run out of time before the ask. A partial success: open with the problem.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Narrow reach.</b> The app launches, but vendors complain that most customers cannot see their offers. A partial success: protect the product's purpose in negotiations.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost the room.</b> The panel stops listening within two minutes. Try again and open with a hook.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unconvinced panel.</b> Without data the sponsor declines. Try again and use your evidence.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Credibility gap.</b> The investor asks you to return with a proper budget. Try again with itemised costs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Control lost.</b> You now own much less of your own idea, and the investor wants changes you disagree with. Try again and weigh the offer first.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Your SBA proposal: the 14 marks","html":"<p>The Unit 2 SBA is marked out of 45 (weighted to 90): <b>Proposal 14</b>, <b>Progress and Process 12</b>, <b>Product 19</b> (including the 30-second short ad, 5 marks). The report should be no more than <b>1,500 words</b>, excluding diagrams, graphs, tables and bibliography. Here is how the Proposal marks break down in the mark scheme, with what to include.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Marks</th><th>What earns the marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Overview: brief introduction (background)</td><td>1</td><td>Comprehensive context: the community, the issue, why now</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overview: description of service or product</td><td>3</td><td>Rationale and purpose (2), target audience identified (1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concept: how the idea works</td><td>1</td><td>Clearly articulated, with draft designs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concept: significance</td><td>2</td><td>At least two justifications for why the concept matters</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Budget</td><td>3</td><td>Realistic, including all critical elements (design and building, content creation, maintenance, equipment, resource persons, transport, hosting)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brainstorming chart</td><td>1</td><td>Problem identification, possible solutions, most viable solution and intended outcomes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Research: data capture instrument with data collected</td><td>1</td><td>A designed questionnaire or interview guide, with responses</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Research: data analysis</td><td>2</td><td>Charts and interpretation that justify why the project was selected</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"2\"><b>Total</b></td><td><b>14</b></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Checklist before you submit your proposal</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Background explains the local context (for example, a community need in your territory) in one or two paragraphs.</li>\n<li>Rationale says why the product is needed, using your own data.</li>\n<li>Target audience is specific and described.</li>\n<li>Concept includes sketches, wireframes or mock-ups and explains how it works.</li>\n<li>Two or more clear reasons for its significance (social, economic, cultural, educational).</li>\n<li>Itemised budget with every critical element, realistic figures, currency stated and a contingency.</li>\n<li>Brainstorming chart with all four parts.</li>\n<li>Survey instrument attached, results charted and analysed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep the methodology and production schedule strong as well: they score in Progress and Process (tools fully described 2, methods outline 3, production schedule with requirements, duration and deliverables 3).</p>\n<p>The project is to design a product or service, implemented with digital tools, with a proposal, incremental progress and process. Check the final mark scheme your teacher gives you, and keep every draft as evidence of progress.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use the mark-scheme criteria as your <b>headings</b>. The moderator can then find each criterion quickly, and you can see at a glance if anything is missing.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The Unit 2 SBA is out of 45: Proposal 14, Progress and Process 12 and Product 19. The 14 Proposal marks come from eight parts: the background, the description of your product, how the idea works, why it matters, the budget, a brainstorming chart, your survey and your data analysis. Use these eight parts as the headings of your proposal so nothing is missed.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Look at where the marks cluster. The description (3), the budget (3), significance (2) and data analysis (2) carry 10 of the 14 marks, so a proposal that only describes the idea cannot score well. Each heavy criterion needs <b>evidence</b>: your own survey figures for the rationale, itemised costs for every critical element, and charts with a written interpretation. Significance needs at least two separate justifications, for example social and economic. The brainstorming chart earns its mark only with all four parts: the problem, possible solutions, the most viable solution and the intended outcomes. Finally, plan the 30-second short ad early: it sits in the Product section (5 marks), but its message should come straight from the concept and audience you set out here.</p>"},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Outline the considerations for a project proposal","genre":"Structured response: outlining proposal considerations","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Your group plans a digital media project, Hurricane Ready SXM: a set of short animated videos and a mobile web page that teach families in Sint Maarten how to prepare for hurricane season in English, Spanish and Dutch. You must present a proposal to a local sponsor and a regional funding body.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Outline FOUR sections you would include in the written proposal and state the purpose of each. Include a SMART objective.</p><p>(b) Identify TWO suitable sources of funding, state whether each is debt, equity or neither, and give one advantage and one disadvantage of each.</p><p>(c) List FIVE itemised budget lines (with quantity, unit cost and total, in XCG or USD), including hosting and contingency.</p><p>(d) Give TWO tips for pitching the project using a visual pitch deck.</p>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The executive summary ... The problem statement ... An objective: By ..., we will ... The methodology ...<br><b>(b)</b> One source is ... An advantage is ... However, ... A second source is ...<br><b>(c)</b> 1. ... × ... = ... 2. ... 5. Contingency (10%) ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ...</p>","checklist":["I named four proposal sections and gave the purpose of each.","My objective has a number and a deadline.","I named two funding sources, said whether each is debt, equity or neither, and gave an advantage and a disadvantage for each.","Each budget line has quantity × unit cost = total, with a currency.","I included hosting and contingency in the budget.","I gave two practical pitch deck tips."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Proposal writing","marks":5,"look":["executive summary|overview","problem statement|problem","objective","methodolog|timeline|gantt|schedule","by [0-9a-z ]*(20|june|july|may|august|septem|octob|novem|week|month)|within [0-9]+"],"min":3,"strong":"You outlined proposal sections clearly with their purposes and a measurable objective.","weak":"Name four sections (e.g. executive summary, problem statement, objectives, methodology) and say what each does for the reader. Make the objective SMART with a number and a date."},{"criterion":"Funding and budget","marks":6,"look":["grant","crowdfund|kickstarter|gofundme|indiegogo","sponsor","angel|investor|equity|debt|loan|neither|regional|development bank|unesco|arts council","host|domain","contingenc","xcg|usd|\\$","[0-9]+ ?(x|×) ?[0-9]+"],"min":4,"strong":"You chose suitable funding sources and gave a realistic itemised budget with hosting and contingency.","weak":"Give each funding source an advantage and a disadvantage, and write budget lines as quantity × unit cost = total, including hosting and a 5 to 10% contingency."},{"criterion":"Pitching","marks":3,"look":["canva|google slides|powerpoint","hook|story|statistic","one idea|few words|large|readab|chart|visual|image","rehears|practi|time|question"],"min":2,"strong":"Your pitch tips are practical and specific.","weak":"Give concrete pitch tips, e.g. open with a hook, keep one idea per slide with large text, and rehearse to time."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The <b>executive summary</b> gives the sponsor a half-page overview of the problem, solution, cost and timeline, so they can decide quickly whether to support it. The <b>problem statement</b> uses evidence to show the need: in our survey of 80 households, 46% said they had no written hurricane plan, and many Spanish- and Dutch-speaking families said most guidance is only in English. The <b>objectives</b> state what we will achieve, for example: \"By 1 June, we will publish six 60-second animated videos in three languages and reach 5,000 views across WhatsApp and Facebook by 31 July.\" The <b>methodology and timeline</b> explain how we will work (scripts, storyboards, animation in Adobe Animate, web page in WordPress) and when, using a Gantt chart with milestones.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One source is <b>sponsorship</b> from a local business such as a hardware store. An advantage is that it may provide in-kind support, such as storm supplies for a giveaway. However, the sponsor may want a say in the content or heavy branding. A second source is a <b>regional funding body</b>, such as a disaster-resilience or creative industries fund. An advantage is that grants do not need to be repaid. A disadvantage is that applications are competitive and need detailed reporting, and the money may be restricted to named costs. Neither source is debt or equity: the grant is non-repayable and the sponsor buys visibility, so we keep full ownership and have no loan repayments.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> (XCG) 1. Voice-over artists: 3 languages × 6 videos × 40 = 720. 2. Animation software subscription: 3 months × 110 = 330. 3. Microphone purchase: 1 × 180 = 180. 4. Domain and web hosting: 12 months × 25 = 300. 5. Contingency (10% of the subtotal of 1,530) = 153. Total: 1,683 (about USD 940).</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, build the deck in <b>Canva</b> with one idea per slide, large text and one clear chart of our survey results, so the panel can read it from a distance. Second, open with a <b>hook</b>, such as a short clip of a Hurricane Irma survivor, then <b>rehearse</b> to finish within the time and prepare answers to likely questions about cost and impact.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Outline\" questions reward breadth: cover every part briefly but precisely. In budgets, show the calculation on every line and include hosting and contingency, which are the items most often forgotten. Keep the currency consistent throughout.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Outline the considerations for a project proposal (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: project proposal considerations, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO sections of a project proposal and <b>state</b> what each is for. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> ONE SMART objective for Hurricane Ready SXM, with a number and a date. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE funding source and <b>state</b> whether it is debt, equity or neither. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> TWO budget lines in the form quantity × unit cost = total. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE tip for making a good pitch deck. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The ... is for ... The ... is for ...<br><b>(b)</b> By ..., we will ...<br><b>(c)</b> One source is ... It is ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> 1. ... × ... = ... 2. ... × ... = ...<br><b>(e)</b> One tip is ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I named two proposal sections and their purposes.","My objective has a number and a date.","I named a funding source and said whether it is debt, equity or neither.","Both budget lines show quantity × unit cost = total.","I gave one pitch deck tip with a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two proposal sections","marks":2,"look":["executive summary|problem|objective|methodolog|timeline|budget|evaluation|audience","for|shows|explains|tells|gives"],"min":2,"strong":"Two sections with their purposes.","weak":"For example: the executive summary gives a short overview; the budget shows what the project will cost."},{"criterion":"SMART objective","marks":2,"look":["[0-9]+","by|before|within"],"min":2,"strong":"A number and a deadline.","weak":"For example: \"By 1 June, we will publish six videos in three languages.\""},{"criterion":"Funding source","marks":2,"look":["grant|sponsor|crowdfund|loan|angel|savings|bootstrap","debt|equity|neither"],"min":2,"strong":"A source correctly classified.","weak":"For example: a grant, which is neither debt nor equity because it does not have to be repaid."},{"criterion":"Budget lines","marks":2,"look":["[0-9]+ ?(x|×) ?[0-9]+","xcg|usd|\\$"],"min":2,"strong":"Two correct budget lines with a currency.","weak":"Write each line as quantity × unit cost = total, for example \"12 months × XCG 25 = XCG 300\" for hosting."},{"criterion":"Pitch deck tip","marks":2,"look":["one idea|large|few words|chart|image|hook|rehears|contrast|readab","because|so "],"min":2,"strong":"A practical tip with a reason.","weak":"For example: one idea per slide with large text, so the panel can read it from a distance."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The executive summary is for giving the sponsor a short overview of the whole project. The budget is for showing exactly what the project will cost.</p><p><b>(b)</b> By 1 June, we will publish six 60-second animated videos in English, Spanish and Dutch.</p><p><b>(c)</b> One source is a regional grant. It is neither debt nor equity, because it does not have to be repaid and we do not give away any ownership.</p><p><b>(d)</b> 1. Web hosting: 12 months × XCG 25 = XCG 300. 2. Voice-over: 3 languages × 6 videos × XCG 40 = XCG 720.</p><p><b>(e)</b> One tip is to put one idea on each slide with large text, because the panel must be able to read it quickly from a distance.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part. In (d), check every multiplication; a line with the wrong total earns only 1 mark. Students who finish confidently should add a contingency line and a second funding source, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Outline the considerations for a project proposal (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Extended response: project proposal considerations, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Your group plans <b>Hurricane Ready SXM</b>: six short animated videos and a mobile web page that teach families in Sint Maarten how to prepare for hurricane season in English, Spanish and Dutch. The videos must launch before hurricane season begins on 1 June. You will present a proposal and a 10-minute pitch to a local hardware store (a possible sponsor) and a regional disaster-resilience fund, whose grants are restricted to content production. The group has XCG 500 in savings. Your survey of 80 households found that 46% had no written hurricane plan and many Spanish- and Dutch-speaking families said most guidance is only in English.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Outline</b> the structure of the written proposal and the purpose of each section, write a strong problem statement using the survey evidence, and give TWO SMART objectives. <i>(6 marks)</i></p><p>(b) <b>Evaluate</b> THREE funding sources for this project, classifying each as debt, equity or neither, and <b>design</b> a mixed funding plan that adds up to your grand total, marking any restricted money. <i>(7 marks)</i></p><p>(c) <b>Produce</b> an itemised budget of at least EIGHT lines in categories, with a subtotal, a 10% contingency and a grand total, showing quantity × unit cost = total on every line. <i>(6 marks)</i></p><p>(d) <b>Plan</b> the 10-minute pitch: its structure, three deck design decisions, and ONE likely question with your answer. <i>(4 marks)</i></p><p>(e) <b>Identify</b> ONE risk to the timeline and explain how your schedule handles it. <i>(2 marks)</i></p>","min":500,"max":900,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Sections: title page ... executive summary ... problem statement ... solution ... objectives ... audience and market ... methodology ... timeline ... budget ... evaluation ... appendices. Problem statement: ... Objectives: 1. By ... 2. By ...<br><b>(b)</b> Source 1 ... (neither/debt/equity) ... benefit ... drawback ... Source 2 ... Source 3 ... Funding plan: ... + ... + ... = ...<br><b>(c)</b> Category | Item | Qty | Unit cost | Total ... Subtotal ... Contingency ... Grand total ...<br><b>(d)</b> Structure ... Deck decisions ... Question: ... Answer: ...<br><b>(e)</b> Risk ... Schedule ...</p>","checklist":["I outlined every section with its purpose and wrote a problem statement that uses the survey figures.","Both objectives are fully SMART.","I evaluated three funding sources, classified each and designed a funding plan that equals my grand total, with restricted money marked.","My budget has at least eight lines, correct arithmetic, a subtotal, a 10% contingency and a grand total in one currency.","My pitch plan has a structure, three deck decisions and a prepared answer.","I identified a timeline risk and showed how the schedule handles it."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Proposal structure, problem statement and objectives","marks":6,"look":["executive summary","problem","methodolog","timeline|gantt","evaluation","46%|80 households","by [0-9]|by 1 june|by 31|before 1 june","spanish|dutch|three languages"],"min":6,"strong":"Every section outlined with its purpose, an evidence-based problem statement and two fully SMART objectives.","weak":"List the sections with a purpose each, use the survey figures in the problem statement and give two objectives with numbers and dates."},{"criterion":"Funding evaluation and plan","marks":7,"look":["grant|resilience fund","sponsor","crowdfund|savings|bootstrap|loan","debt|equity|neither","restricted","benefit|advantage|however|drawback|disadvantage","total|=|adds up"],"min":6,"strong":"Three sources evaluated and classified, and a funding plan that equals the grand total with restricted money marked.","weak":"Give a benefit and a drawback for each source, say whether it is debt, equity or neither, and make the funding plan add up to the budget total."},{"criterion":"Itemised budget","marks":6,"look":["[0-9]+ ?(x|×) ?[0-9]+","host|domain","contingency","subtotal","grand total|total","xcg|usd"],"min":6,"strong":"A realistic, correctly calculated budget with every critical element.","weak":"Use at least eight lines with quantity × unit cost = total, include hosting, show a subtotal, add 10% contingency and give the grand total."},{"criterion":"Pitch plan","marks":4,"look":["hook","ask|xcg|need","slide|deck|chart|large|one idea|contrast","question|what if|answer|phase"],"min":3,"strong":"A persuasive structure, sensible deck decisions and a well-prepared answer.","weak":"Give the pitch structure, three specific deck decisions and one likely question with a confident answer."},{"criterion":"Timeline risk","marks":2,"look":["risk|delay|late|power|storm|exam|translation|grant","buffer|milestone|earlier|phase|week"],"min":2,"strong":"A real risk handled by a specific scheduling decision.","weak":"Name one realistic risk and say exactly how the schedule allows for it."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Structure:</i> a <b>title page</b>; an <b>executive summary</b> giving the sponsor and fund a half-page overview of the problem, solution, cost and timeline; the <b>background and problem statement</b> proving the need; the <b>proposed solution</b> with storyboards and a web page mock-up; <b>SMART objectives</b>; the <b>target audience and market analysis</b> (families, especially Spanish- and Dutch-speaking households, and existing guidance in English only); the <b>methodology</b> (the creative design process, scripts reviewed by a disaster-preparedness adviser, animation in a 2D tool, a mobile web page); the <b>timeline</b> as a Gantt chart with milestones; the <b>itemised budget</b> and funding plan; the <b>evaluation plan</b>; and <b>appendices</b> with the questionnaire and results. <i>Problem statement:</i> \"Families in Sint Maarten, particularly Spanish- and Dutch-speaking households, need clear, short hurricane-preparation guidance in their own language, because in our survey of 80 households, 46% had no written hurricane plan and many said most guidance is only in English.\" <i>Objectives:</i> (1) By 25 May, publish six 60-second animated videos in English, Spanish and Dutch on a mobile web page. (2) By 31 July, reach 5,000 views and have 200 households complete the page's \"my plan\" checklist.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Regional disaster-resilience grant (neither debt nor equity):</i> it does not have to be repaid and gives credibility, but it is competitive, needs detailed reporting and is restricted to content production. <i>Hardware store sponsorship (neither):</i> the store gains visibility with families preparing for storms and may add in-kind prizes such as storm kits, but it may want branding or influence over content, so the agreement will limit it to end cards and the web page. <i>Rewards crowdfunding (neither):</i> it shows community demand and involves residents early, but it needs constant promotion, and platform fees and reward costs reduce the money received. A loan (debt) is unsuitable, because the project earns no income to repay it. <i>Funding plan (XCG):</i> resilience grant 6,000 (restricted to the animator, voice-over and scripts); hardware store sponsorship 2,000 (unrestricted); rewards crowdfunding 1,268 net (target 1,400, allowing for fees and reward costs); group savings 500. Total 9,768, equal to the grand total.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> (XCG; about XCG 1.79 = USD 1) <i>Personnel:</i> freelance animator, 6 videos × 900 = 5,400; voice-over artists, 3 languages × 6 videos × 40 = 720; script translation, 3 languages × 6 scripts × 30 = 540; preparedness adviser (resource person), 6 hours × 90 = 540. <i>Software:</i> animation subscription, 4 months × 110 = 440. <i>Equipment:</i> microphone, 1 × 180 = 180. <i>Hosting:</i> domain and web hosting, 12 months × 25 = 300. <i>Marketing:</i> boosted posts and QR posters, 1 × 600 = 600. <i>Production:</i> transport for community testing, 4 trips × 40 = 160. Subtotal 8,880. Contingency (10%) 888. Grand total 9,768 (about USD 5,460).</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Structure:</i> a hook (a 15-second clip of a Philipsburg resident describing Hurricane Irma, then the 46% statistic), the problem, a demo of one finished video, audience and gap, the method and timeline, the budget summary with the exact ask (XCG 2,000 from the sponsor), impact metrics and a call to action. <i>Deck decisions:</i> one idea per slide with at least 24 pt text; the survey results as one clear bar chart in the brand colours; high-contrast colours so the deck reads on a bright projector, with a PDF backup on a USB drive. <i>Likely question:</i> \"What if the grant is refused?\" <i>Answer:</i> \"We have a phased plan: with the sponsor and crowdfunding alone, we would launch three videos, in all three languages, before 1 June, and apply to the fund's next round for the rest.\"</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <i>Risk:</i> translation and voice recording in three languages may take longer than planned. The schedule finishes the English scripts in week 2 so translation can start early, records all three languages in the same week, and places one week of buffer before the 25 May launch milestone, so a delay still leaves the videos live before hurricane season begins.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 7 + 6 + 4 + 2). Check the arithmetic on every budget line and that the funding plan equals the grand total exactly; a plan that does not add up should lose marks in both (b) and (c). Reward evaluation of funding sources against this project (no income, so no loan) rather than general descriptions, and a pitch answer that offers a phased fallback.</p>"}}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 1 Test","pass":70,"items":[{"q":"King Street Fabrics asks Island Pulse Media for a promotional video. Which pre-production activity should Zara's team complete FIRST?","options":["Drawing the storyboard for each scene","Booking the camera, tripod and lighting kit","Choosing transitions and text overlays in WeVideo","Defining the problem, purpose and goal of the video"],"answer":3,"why":"Problem definition comes first because every later decision (audience, message, shots, budget) depends on knowing what the product must achieve. The storyboard is tempting because it is an early planning tool, but you cannot plan scenes until you know the purpose and target audience."},{"q":"Which planning tool shows each production task as a bar against a time scale, with milestones such as 'storyboard approved'?","options":["A site map","A mood board","A storyboard","A Gantt chart"],"answer":3,"why":"A Gantt chart plots tasks against time, showing durations, overlaps and milestones, so a team can manage a production schedule. The storyboard is tempting because it is also a sequence, but it shows the order of shots in the product, not the timing of the work."},{"q":"A document that fixes the logo placement, fonts, hex colour codes and spacing rules so every designer on a Carnival campaign produces consistent work is a","options":["mood board","storyboard","rough sketch","style guide"],"answer":3,"why":"A style guide sets fixed rules that keep every item in a project consistent. A mood board is tempting because it also deals with colours and fonts, but it is a loose collection of inspiration that sets a feeling; it does not give exact rules."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the elements of visual design?","options":["Texture","Proximity","Value","Shape"],"answer":1,"why":"The elements are line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour. Proximity is a principle of composition (how elements are arranged), not a basic element. Value is tempting because it sounds abstract, but it is the lightness or darkness of a colour and is a core element."},{"q":"The logo of the Frigate Bay Grill uses orange and blue, which sit directly opposite each other on the colour wheel. This colour scheme is","options":["analogous","triadic","complementary","split-complementary"],"answer":2,"why":"Complementary colours are directly opposite each other on the wheel and create strong contrast. Split-complementary is tempting because it is based on the complement, but it uses a base colour with the two colours either side of its complement, so it has three colours, not two."},{"q":"A photographer frames the Castries courthouse so that its cupola sits where two lines of a three-by-three grid cross. This applies","options":["repetition","the golden ratio","symmetrical balance","the rule of thirds"],"answer":3,"why":"The rule of thirds divides the frame into a three-by-three grid and places the subject on a line or intersection. The golden ratio is tempting because it is also a composition rule, but it uses a proportion of about 1 to 1.618 and a spiral, not equal thirds."},{"q":"On the ANU Ferry booking app, the 'Pay' button turns green and a message reads 'Booking confirmed' as soon as it is tapped. Which principle of interactive design is shown?","options":["Consistency","Predictability","Feedback","Learnability"],"answer":2,"why":"Feedback means the system responds visibly to every user action, confirming that it worked. Predictability is tempting, but it is about users being able to expect what will happen BEFORE they act, not the response afterwards."},{"q":"Malik is designing a villain for a Caribbean animated short and wants colour alone to signal danger and menace. Which palette is MOST suitable?","options":["Black, deep red and dark purple shades","Bright yellow and sky blue","Pale analogous greens","Pastel pink and baby blue tints"],"answer":0,"why":"Dark shades of red and purple with black are widely read as threatening, powerful and dangerous, which suits a villain. Bright yellow and sky blue is tempting because it is high contrast, but those colours suggest cheerfulness and energy, which fit a hero or a comic character better."},{"q":"Which statement BEST describes an indigenous digital product?","options":["A foreign film dubbed into a Caribbean language","Any game that is sold in a Caribbean store","Any app that can be downloaded in the Caribbean","Digital content created by local creators that reflects their own culture, identity and stories"],"answer":3,"why":"Indigenous products are made by people from the culture and express that culture from the inside. A dubbed foreign film is tempting because it is adapted for local audiences, but it is still imported content: the stories and creative decisions come from elsewhere."},{"q":"A Trinidadian studio produces an animated series featuring folklore characters such as Papa Bois and the soucouyant. What is the MAIN cultural motivation for this product?","options":["To copy the style of imported cartoons exactly","To avoid paying any production costs","To avoid copyright law because folklore is old","To preserve and share Caribbean heritage with local and global audiences"],"answer":3,"why":"Using local folklore keeps stories alive, builds cultural pride and introduces Caribbean heritage to the world. Avoiding copyright is tempting because folklore is traditional, but the studio's own scripts, characters and animation are still protected, and avoiding the law is not a cultural motivation."},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of POLICY support for local digital creators?","options":["An imported game renaming characters with Caribbean names","A viewer streaming a Hollywood film","A creator buying followers on Instagram","A government film fund offering grants to Caribbean filmmakers"],"answer":3,"why":"A government fund is a policy decision that directly finances local creative work. The renamed imported game is tempting because it looks local, but it is surface localisation of foreign content and does not support local creators."},{"q":"Wikipedia is BEST classified as which type of digital media platform?","options":["Infrastructure platform","Marketplace","Application store","Knowledge platform"],"answer":3,"why":"A knowledge platform exists to collect and share information, which is exactly Wikipedia's purpose. Infrastructure platform is tempting because Wikipedia is large and widely used, but infrastructure platforms provide underlying services (such as cloud hosting) for other products to be built on."},{"q":"Maya needs the Island Pulse Media logo on a website with a transparent background, sharp edges and no compression artefacts. Which format is BEST?","options":["MP3","GIF","PNG","JPEG"],"answer":2,"why":"PNG is lossless and supports full transparency, so edges stay crisp. GIF is tempting because it also supports transparency, but it is limited to 256 colours and only on-or-off transparency, so smooth edges and gradients look rough."},{"q":"Maya places a YouTube video of the Dominica Carnival parade inside a web page so that it plays there, without copying the video file to her own server. This is called","options":["compressing","importing","exporting","embedding"],"answer":3,"why":"Embedding displays content hosted elsewhere inside your page using code such as an iframe. Importing is tempting, but importing brings a copy of the file into your own software or project."},{"q":"Malik needs a full-quality master of a voice-over to edit in Audacity before the final export. Which format should he record and save in?","options":["OGG","WAV","MP3","MP4"],"answer":1,"why":"WAV is uncompressed, so no quality is lost while editing; you export to MP3 or OGG only at the end. OGG is tempting because it is an open format, but like MP3 it is lossy, and repeated saves in a lossy format degrade the audio."},{"q":"In three-point lighting, which light is placed behind the subject to separate it from the background?","options":["Fill light","Practical light","Back light","Key light"],"answer":2,"why":"The back light (rim or hair light) shines from behind to outline the subject. The fill light is tempting because it is also a supporting light, but it sits on the opposite side of the key light to soften shadows on the face."},{"q":"Keisha is recording an interview on a windy beach at Montego Bay. Which action BEST reduces unwanted noise?","options":["Use the camera's built-in microphone from five metres away","Turn the input gain up as high as possible","Use a lavalier microphone with a furry windscreen and shield it from the wind","Add loud background music to cover the wind"],"answer":2,"why":"A close lavalier with a windscreen captures the voice strongly while blocking wind. Turning the gain up is tempting because it makes the voice louder, but it also amplifies the wind noise and can cause clipping."},{"q":"Which export setting is MOST suitable for a 30-second reel to be posted on Instagram?","options":["MP4 (H.264), 1080 x 1920, 9:16 vertical","GIF, 4:3, 256 colours","WAV, 48 kHz","PDF, A4"],"answer":0,"why":"Instagram Reels expect vertical 9:16 video, and MP4 with H.264 gives good quality at a small file size. GIF is tempting because it can animate, but it has no sound, poor colour and a very large file size for video-length content."},{"q":"When a bouncing ball flattens as it hits the ground and lengthens as it rises, the animator is applying","options":["squash and stretch","staging","anticipation","follow-through"],"answer":0,"why":"Squash and stretch deforms the object to show weight, impact and speed while keeping its volume. Anticipation is tempting because it also involves a change of shape, but it is a preparatory movement before an action, such as crouching before a jump."},{"q":"In Adobe Animate, Malik sets the ball's position on frame 1 and frame 24, and the software creates all the frames in between. This process is called","options":["interpolation (tweening)","rigging","rendering","onion skinning"],"answer":0,"why":"Interpolation, or tweening, is the automatic generation of in-between frames between keyframes. Onion skinning is tempting because it is also used when animating, but it only shows faint previous and next frames as a guide; it does not create frames."},{"q":"In Blender, the flat surface enclosed by three or more edges of a mesh is called a","options":["face","vertex","keyframe","modifier"],"answer":0,"why":"A face is the flat surface bounded by edges; faces together form the visible surface of the model. A vertex is tempting because it is also part of a mesh, but it is a single point where edges meet."},{"q":"In a basic walk cycle, which key pose shows the legs furthest apart with both feet touching the ground?","options":["Contact","Down","Passing","Up"],"answer":0,"why":"The contact pose has the front heel and back toe both on the ground with the legs at full stride. The passing pose is tempting because it is also a key pose, but it shows one leg passing the other under the body, with the legs close together."},{"q":"Why is version history in Google Docs an ADVANTAGE for a group writing an SBA proposal?","options":["It closes the digital divide between group members","The group can see who changed what and restore an earlier version if needed","It removes the need for an internet connection","It stops group members from editing the same document"],"answer":1,"why":"Version control records every change and lets the team roll back mistakes, which protects shared work. Working without internet is tempting, but Google Docs offline mode is limited and needs syncing; version history itself has nothing to do with connectivity."},{"q":"Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of digital collaboration for a design team with members in Dominica and Guyana?","options":["Unreliable connectivity during storms and power cuts","Real-time feedback on shared files","Automatic saving to the cloud","Access to a wider pool of talent"],"answer":0,"why":"Connectivity problems and the digital divide can stop members from joining calls or uploading work. Access to a wider pool of talent is tempting because it involves distance, but it is an advantage (global reach), not a disadvantage."},{"q":"Spice Lane food court paints giant footprints along the St. George’s boardwalk leading from the cruise pier to its stalls. This is an example of","options":["email marketing","influencer marketing","guerrilla marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"answer":2,"why":"Guerrilla marketing uses unconventional, low-cost, surprising tactics in public spaces. Cross-platform marketing is tempting because the idea could be shared online too, but cross-platform means keeping one consistent message across several channels, not one physical stunt."},{"q":"Which KPI BEST shows whether a Mailchimp email campaign persuaded readers to visit a tour booking page?","options":["Open rate","Click-through rate","Total number of subscribers","Number of emails sent"],"answer":1,"why":"Click-through rate measures the percentage of readers who clicked the link to the booking page, which is the action the campaign wanted. Open rate is tempting, but it only shows that people opened the email, not that they acted on it."},{"q":"Smartphones, smart TVs and connected screens are now found in almost every home, school and workplace in Barbados, so designers must plan for them. Which driving factor does this BEST illustrate?","options":["Empowerment","Affordability","Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology"],"answer":3,"why":"Pervasiveness means technology is present everywhere in daily life. Commoditisation is tempting because cheap devices help spread technology, but commoditisation describes technology becoming standardised and cheap, not how widespread it is."},{"q":"A user interface uses frosted, semi-transparent panels floating over a blurred, colourful background. This trend is known as","options":["neumorphism","flat design","skeuomorphism","glassmorphism"],"answer":3,"why":"Glassmorphism imitates frosted glass with transparency and background blur. Neumorphism is tempting because it is also a recent soft UI trend, but it uses solid, same-colour surfaces with soft shadows so elements look pushed out of or into the background."},{"q":"A St. Lucian youth group raises money for an animated film by collecting small donations from hundreds of supporters on an online platform. This source of funding is","options":["crowdfunding","a government grant","an angel investor","sponsorship"],"answer":0,"why":"Crowdfunding raises many small contributions from the public online. Sponsorship is tempting because supporters give money, but sponsorship comes from one business or organisation in return for publicity, not from many individuals."},{"q":"Which cost belongs under 'personnel' in an itemised budget for a 30-second ad?","options":["Website hosting for one year","A new tripod","A monthly Adobe Creative Cloud subscription","The fee for a freelance voice actor"],"answer":3,"why":"Personnel costs are payments to people for their time and skills. The Adobe subscription is tempting because it is a recurring cost, but it belongs under software, and the tripod belongs under equipment."}]},"paper2":{"type":"write","title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: visual design, colour, composition, animation and promotional campaign","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Reef Guardians SLU</b> is a youth conservation group in Gros Islet, St. Lucia. It has asked Island Pulse Media to promote a beach clean-up and turtle-nesting awareness day at Grande Anse beach. The audience is students aged 13 to 19 and residents; the day falls during the busy Carnival season. Zara reviews the group's first draft materials:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a poster with red text on a bright green background, six different fonts, and a small turtle photo placed in the exact centre, surrounded by logos with almost no empty space;</li>\n<li>a 10-second animated turtle for social media that moves across the screen at exactly the same speed throughout, with no keyframes except the first and last, and a bouncing beach ball that never changes shape;</li>\n<li>a plan to post the poster once on Facebook, with no other channels and no way of measuring success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The group has a small budget and a deadline of three weeks.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Identify <b>TWO</b> weaknesses in the draft poster. For each, name the design element or principle involved and recommend an improvement. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a colour harmony scheme for the campaign. Justify your choice for the audience and explain how tints or shades could be used. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how <b>keyframing</b>, <b>timing and spacing</b>, and <b>squash and stretch</b> could improve the turtle and beach ball animation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Outline a cross-platform promotional campaign for the clean-up day. Describe <b>TWO</b> strategies and state <b>ONE</b> KPI that would measure the success of each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> One weakness is ... This breaks the principle of ... I recommend ... A second weakness is ... I recommend ...<br><b>(b)</b> I recommend a ... scheme using ... because the audience ... Tints/shades could be used to ...<br><b>(c)</b> Keyframing would ... Timing and spacing would ... Squash and stretch would ...<br><b>(d)</b> Strategy 1: ... measured by (KPI) ... Strategy 2: ... measured by (KPI) ... The message stays consistent because ...</p>","checklist":["I named two poster weaknesses using correct terms (contrast, colour, hierarchy, negative space, rule of thirds, point of interest) and gave a fix for each.","I recommended a named harmony scheme with specific colours, justified it for the audience and explained tints or shades.","I explained keyframing, timing and spacing, and squash and stretch separately, each applied to the turtle or the ball.","I described two different campaign strategies on more than one platform and matched a measurable KPI to each.","I labelled my answers (a) to (d) and tied every point back to Reef Guardians KIN."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Poster critique and improvements","marks":4,"look":["contrast|legib|readab|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind","font|typeface|hierarch|consisten","negative space|white space|clutter|crowd|breathe","rule of thirds|point of interest|focal|centre|center|prominen"],"min":2,"strong":"You identified two weaknesses with precise design terms and gave practical fixes.","weak":"Name the element or principle and fix it, e.g. \"Red text on bright green has poor contrast and is hard for colour-blind viewers to read; use dark navy text on a pale sand background.\""},{"criterion":"Colour harmony recommendation","marks":3,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|tetradic","blue|teal|turquoise|aqua|sand|green|coral|orange","tint|shade|tone|lighter|darker","audience|student|youth|calm|ocean|nature|energ"],"min":2,"strong":"Your scheme is named, specific and justified for the audience, with a clear use of tints or shades.","weak":"Name a scheme and colours, e.g. an analogous scheme of teal, turquoise and sea green with a coral accent, and explain that tints are used for backgrounds and darker shades for headings."},{"criterion":"Animation principles applied","marks":4,"look":["keyframe|key pose|key frame","timing|spacing|eas(e|ing)|slow in|slow out|speed","squash|stretch","turtle|ball|flipper|bounce|weight"],"min":3,"strong":"You applied all three principles accurately to the turtle and the ball.","weak":"Explain each principle in context: add keyframes for flipper strokes; use closer spacing at the start and end so the turtle eases in and out; squash the ball on impact and stretch it as it falls and rises."},{"criterion":"Cross-platform campaign and KPIs","marks":4,"look":["instagram|tiktok|whatsapp|facebook|email|mailchimp|influencer|school|radio|poster","guerrilla|viral|cross.?platform|consistent|hashtag|content marketing","kpi|reach|engagement|click|share|view|sign.?up|registration|attend|rate"],"min":2,"strong":"Your two strategies suit the audience and each has a measurable KPI.","weak":"Describe two strategies on different channels, e.g. a TikTok challenge with a #ReefGuardiansSXM hashtag (KPI: number of shares) and a Google Form sign-up linked from WhatsApp groups (KPI: registrations)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> One weakness is <b>poor colour contrast</b>. Red text on a bright green background is hard to read, vibrates visually and is almost invisible to viewers with red-green colour blindness, so the key message is lost. I recommend dark navy text on a pale sand-coloured background, which gives strong contrast and suits a beach theme. A second weakness is <b>clutter and a weak point of interest</b>. Six fonts destroy consistency and visual hierarchy, and the small turtle in the exact centre, crowded by logos, has no <b>negative space</b> around it, so the eye has nowhere to land. I recommend two fonts only (one bold display font for the headline and one clean sans-serif for details), enlarging the turtle and placing it on a <b>rule of thirds</b> intersection, and grouping the logos in a strip at the bottom with white space around the headline.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> I recommend an <b>analogous scheme</b> of teal, turquoise and sea green with a small <b>complementary accent</b> of coral orange for the date and call to action. The analogous blues and greens link directly to the ocean and nature, feel calm and trustworthy for a conservation message, and the coral accent draws students' eyes to the action. Light <b>tints</b> of turquoise can form the background so the text stands out, while darker <b>shades</b> of teal can be used for headlines and the logo strip to create hierarchy.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Keyframing</b>: the turtle has only a start and end keyframe, so it slides like a cut-out. Extra keyframes for each flipper stroke and a slight rise and fall of the shell would make the swim believable. <b>Timing and spacing</b>: the constant speed looks mechanical. Placing drawings closer together at the start and end (ease in and ease out) and further apart in the middle would make the turtle accelerate and slow naturally; for the ball, wide spacing near the ground and close spacing at the top of each arc shows gravity. <b>Squash and stretch</b>: the beach ball should squash (flatten) on impact with the sand and stretch (lengthen) as it falls and rebounds, while keeping the same volume, so it feels light and bouncy rather than rigid.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The campaign will use one hashtag, <b>#ReefGuardiansSXM</b>, and the same turtle mascot and colours on every channel for <b>cross-platform consistency</b>. <b>Strategy 1: a TikTok and Instagram Reels challenge</b> in which students post a 15-second clip picking up litter with the hashtag, encouraged by a local student influencer. KPI: number of shares and hashtag uses before the event. <b>Strategy 2: a low-cost guerrilla stunt</b>: chalk turtle tracks leading from the Gros Islet bus stop to a QR code that opens a Google Form sign-up, also shared in school WhatsApp groups. KPI: number of registrations through the QR code, compared with attendance on the day.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In design questions, examiners reward the <b>correct term plus a specific fix</b>. Do not write \"the poster is ugly\"; write \"poor contrast between red text and green background reduces legibility\". For animation, treat each principle separately and apply it to the object in the scenario. For campaigns, always pair a strategy with a <b>measurable KPI</b> (shares, click-through rate, registrations), because \"it will be popular\" earns no marks.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Paper 02 structured response, Foundation: visual design, colour, animation and promotion (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> ONE problem with the poster's colours and ONE way to fix it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE problem with the poster's layout (fonts, clutter or the position of the turtle) and ONE way to fix it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> a colour scheme for the campaign and TWO colours you would use. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> ONE change that would make the turtle move more naturally, and what squash and stretch would do for the beach ball. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE more platform for the campaign and ONE thing you would count to see if it worked. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":100,"max":280,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> One problem with the colours is ... To fix it, I would ...<br><b>(b)</b> One problem with the layout is ... To fix it, I would ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would use a ... scheme with ... and ...<br><b>(d)</b> To make the turtle move more naturally, I would ... Squash and stretch would make the ball ...<br><b>(e)</b> I would also use ... and I would count ...</p>","checklist":["I named a colour problem (such as poor contrast) and a fix.","I named a layout problem (fonts, clutter or the centred turtle) and a fix.","I named a colour scheme and two colours.","I described one animation fix for the turtle and what squash and stretch does for the ball.","I named another platform and something to count."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Colour problem and fix","marks":2,"look":["contrast|read|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind|clash","navy|dark|white|light|sand|change|use"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear colour problem with a sensible fix.","weak":"Red text on bright green is hard to read; use dark navy text on a pale background instead."},{"criterion":"Layout problem and fix","marks":2,"look":["font|clutter|crowd|logo|centre|center|space|small","two fonts|fewer|space|third|bigger|move|group|remove"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear layout problem with a sensible fix.","weak":"Six fonts look messy; use two fonts only. Or: the turtle is small and crowded; make it bigger and give it space."},{"criterion":"Colour scheme","marks":2,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|monochrom","blue|teal|turquoise|green|sand|coral|orange"],"min":2,"strong":"A named scheme with two suitable colours.","weak":"For example: an analogous scheme of turquoise and sea green."},{"criterion":"Animation fixes","marks":2,"look":["keyframe|ease|slow in|slow out|speed up|slow down|spacing|timing|flipper","squash|stretch|flatten|bouncy|weight"],"min":2,"strong":"One turtle fix and a correct use of squash and stretch.","weak":"Add keyframes for each flipper stroke, and squash the ball when it hits the sand so it looks bouncy."},{"criterion":"Platform and measure","marks":2,"look":["tiktok|instagram|whatsapp|email|school|radio|youtube","count|share|view|like|sign.?up|registration|people|attend"],"min":2,"strong":"A suitable platform and a measurable count.","weak":"For example: post on TikTok and count how many people sign up for the clean-up."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> One problem with the colours is that red text on a bright green background is hard to read, especially for colour-blind viewers. To fix it, I would use dark navy text on a pale sand-coloured background.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One problem with the layout is that the small turtle is crowded by logos in the centre. To fix it, I would make the turtle bigger, move it to one side and put the logos in a strip at the bottom.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I would use an analogous scheme with turquoise and sea green, because they remind people of the sea.</p><p><b>(d)</b> To make the turtle move more naturally, I would add keyframes for each flipper stroke so it speeds up and slows down. Squash and stretch would make the ball flatten when it hits the sand and stretch as it bounces, so it looks light and bouncy.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I would also use TikTok, and I would count how many students sign up for the clean-up day.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the problem or choice and 1 for the fix, detail or reason. Insist on correct terms (contrast, analogous, keyframe, squash and stretch). Students who manage every part without the sentence starters are ready for the Core question; ask them to add a KPI with a number next time.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Paper 02 extended response, Grade One extension: visual design, colour, composition, animation and promotional campaign (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Reef Guardians SLU</b> is a youth conservation group in Gros Islet, St. Lucia. It has asked Island Pulse Media to promote a beach clean-up and turtle-nesting awareness day at Grande Anse beach. The audience is students aged 13 to 19 across the island (some speak mainly Kwéyòl at home) and local residents. The day falls during the busy Carnival season, so the campaign competes with many other events. Zara reviews the group's first draft materials:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a poster with red text on a bright green background, six different fonts and a small turtle photo placed in the exact centre, surrounded by logos with almost no empty space;</li>\n<li>a 10-second animated turtle for social media that moves across the screen at exactly the same speed throughout, with keyframes only on the first and last frames, and a bouncing beach ball that never changes shape and bounces to the same height every time;</li>\n<li>a plan to post the poster once on Facebook, with no other channels and no way of measuring success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The group has a budget of XCD 1,000 and a deadline of three weeks. Members want to film students on the beach, near the nesting area, for the social media videos.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Critique</b> the draft poster using at least FOUR different elements or principles of design, and <b>propose</b> a redesign that specifies the layout grid, the visual hierarchy, the typefaces and an accessibility standard. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Design</b> a colour palette for the campaign: name the harmony rule and the wheel positions, give hex codes, apply the 60-30-10 guide, explain your use of tints and shades, and justify the palette with colour psychology and accessibility. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> the improved animation at a stated frame rate: keyframes and breakdowns, timing and spacing (with frame numbers), squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through. Give suitable export settings for TikTok and Instagram Reels. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> a cross-platform campaign with a SMART objective and THREE strategies, giving a KPI with a formula and a target for each, and explain how consistency is kept across platforms and languages. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> ONE ethical or legal issue raised by the campaign and explain how the group should handle it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":500,"max":900,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Weakness 1 (contrast) ... Weakness 2 (typography/consistency) ... Weakness 3 (point of interest/rule of thirds) ... Weakness 4 (white space/proximity) ... Redesign: grid ..., hierarchy ..., typefaces ..., accessibility standard ...<br><b>(b)</b> Harmony rule ... wheel positions ... #...... (60%), #...... (30%), #...... (10%) ... tints ... shades ... psychology ... contrast ratio ...<br><b>(c)</b> At ... fps ... turtle keys at frames ... breakdowns ... easing ... flippers follow through ... ball keys at frames ... squash on ... stretch on ... Export: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Objective: by ..., ... Strategy 1 ... KPI = ... ÷ ... × 100, target ... Strategy 2 ... Strategy 3 ... Consistency ...<br><b>(e)</b> Issue ... handled by ...</p>","checklist":["I critiqued the poster with at least four different design terms, each with evidence, and specified a full redesign.","My palette names the harmony rule, its wheel positions, valid hex codes, a 60-30-10 split, tints and shades, and a contrast check.","My animation plan states the frame rate, gives frame numbers for keys and contacts, and covers keyframes, breakdowns, timing, spacing, squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through.","I gave export settings suitable for vertical social video.","My campaign has a SMART objective, three strategies on different channels and a KPI with a formula and target for each.","I explained consistency across platforms and in English and Kwéyòl.","I evaluated a real ethical or legal issue from the scenario and how to handle it."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Poster critique and redesign","marks":6,"look":["contrast|legib|readab|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind","font|typeface|consisten|repetition","hierarch","negative space|white space|clutter|proximity","rule of thirds|power point|point of interest|focal|golden","wcag|4\\.5|contrast ratio"],"min":5,"strong":"Four or more precise weaknesses and a fully specified redesign with an accessibility standard.","weak":"Use four different terms (contrast, consistency, hierarchy, negative space, point of interest), give evidence for each, then specify the grid, hierarchy, two typefaces and a contrast standard such as WCAG 4.5:1."},{"criterion":"Colour palette design","marks":5,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|tetradic","wheel|opposite|adjacent|next to","#[0-9a-f]{6}","60","tint|shade","calm|trust|nature|ocean|energ|urgen|psycholog"],"min":5,"strong":"A precise palette with hex codes and proportions, justified by psychology and checked for contrast.","weak":"Name the rule and where the colours sit on the wheel, give hex codes with a 60-30-10 split, say where tints and shades go and why the colours suit the audience."},{"criterion":"Animation plan and export","marks":6,"look":["fps|frames per second","frame [0-9]+|frames? [0-9]+","keyframe|key pose","breakdown|inbetween","ease|slow in|slow out|spacing","squash","stretch","arc|follow.?through|overlap","1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1920|9:16"],"min":7,"strong":"A precise, frame-numbered plan that applies every principle, with correct export settings.","weak":"State the frame rate, give frame numbers for keys and contacts, and apply keyframes, breakdowns, easing, squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through; export H.264 MP4 at 1080 × 1920."},{"criterion":"Campaign plan and KPIs","marks":6,"look":["by [0-9]|by the|three weeks|event day","tiktok|instagram|reel","whatsapp|email|school|form","guerr?illa|chalk|qr|pop.?up|influencer","÷|/|divid|× 100|x 100","target|%","hashtag|consisten|same logo|mascot","kwéyòl|kweyol|creole|bilingual|language"],"min":6,"strong":"A SMART objective with three well-chosen strategies, each with a formula-based KPI and target, and clear bilingual consistency.","weak":"Set a SMART objective, give three strategies on different channels, attach a KPI with a formula and a target to each, and explain how the hashtag, mascot and colours stay the same in English and Kwéyòl."},{"criterion":"Ethical or legal issue","marks":2,"look":["permission|permit|consent|release|parent|licen|nest|wildlife|disturb","because|so that|should|must"],"min":2,"strong":"A real issue from the scenario, evaluated with a practical way to handle it.","weak":"Choose an issue such as filming minors, using music, chalk on public paths or filming near turtle nests, and explain what the group must do about it."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Contrast:</i> red text on bright green has similar value, so it vibrates on screen and is almost unreadable for viewers with red-green colour deficiency; the key message is lost. <i>Consistency and hierarchy:</i> six fonts break repetition and give no clear order, so nothing tells the viewer what to read first. <i>Point of interest:</i> the turtle is small and dead centre, competing with logos of similar size, so the poster fails the two-second test. <i>Negative space and proximity:</i> there is almost no empty space, and the logos are mixed in with the information, so related details are not grouped. <i>Redesign:</i> a rule-of-thirds grid on an A3 portrait poster, with a large turtle on the lower-right power point, swimming towards the headline to give lead room; hierarchy of headline (\"Guard the Nest\"), then date and place in one block, then the call to action with a QR code, and logos grouped in a strip at the bottom; two typefaces only, a bold rounded display face for the headline and a clean sans-serif for details; all text to meet the WCAG 4.5:1 contrast guideline, and no information carried by colour alone.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> An <b>analogous</b> base of blue-green and green, which sit next to each other on the RYB wheel, with a <b>complementary</b> accent of coral, a red-orange that sits opposite blue-green. <i>60%:</i> a pale turquoise tint #CFF1EF for backgrounds. <i>30%:</i> a deep teal shade #0B4F52 for headlines and the logo strip, and sea green #2E8B57 for shapes. <i>10%:</i> coral #FF6F59 for the date badge and the QR frame. The teal and green link to the sea and nature and feel calm and trustworthy, which suits a conservation message, while the warm coral accent creates urgency and draws students' eyes to the action. Tints keep large areas light so text stands out; shades give weight to the most important text. Teal text on the pale tint measures about 7.7:1, well above 4.5:1. Coral is used only as an accent shape, never as a background for small text, because white text on coral would fail the contrast check.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> At <b>24 fps</b>, the 10-second clip is 240 frames. <i>Turtle:</i> set key poses at the extremes of each flipper stroke (frames 1, 25, 49 and so on), with breakdowns halfway (frames 13, 37...) that decide how the flippers sweep along curved <b>arcs</b>. Ease in over frames 1 to 12 and ease out over frames 229 to 240, with closer spacing at the start and end and wider spacing mid-swim, so the turtle accelerates and glides. Add a gentle rise and fall of the shell, and let the flipper tips <b>follow through</b> a few frames behind the main stroke. <i>Ball:</i> keys at the top (frame 1), first contact (frame 12), lower top (frame 21), second contact (frame 28) and a final small bounce (contact frame 33), each bounce lower and quicker because the ball loses energy; close spacing near the tops and wide spacing near the sand. On contact frames the ball <b>squashes</b> (flatter and wider, same volume); on the frames just before and after it <b>stretches</b> along its path. <i>Export:</i> H.264 MP4, 1080 × 1920 (9:16), 24 fps, AAC audio at 48 kHz, with captions burned in and text kept clear of the bottom of the screen.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>SMART objective:</i> 150 students aged 13 to 19 register for the clean-up by the day before the event, three weeks from launch. <i>Strategy 1, TikTok and Reels challenge:</i> two student nano-influencers (one English-speaking, one Kwéyòl-speaking) post 15-second \"litter pick\" clips with #ReefGuardiansSXM. KPI: engagement rate = engagements ÷ reach × 100, target 6%. <i>Strategy 2, school WhatsApp groups:</i> a short message with a sign-up link carrying a UTM tag, shared by student councils. KPI: conversion rate = registrations ÷ link clicks × 100, target 20%. <i>Strategy 3, guerrilla chalk turtle tracks</i> (with permission) leading from the Gros Islet bus stop to a QR poster. KPI: registrations from that QR code ÷ QR scans × 100, target 25%. <i>Consistency:</i> one brand sheet fixes the turtle mascot, the palette, the typefaces and the hashtag, and every asset is made from the same template in English and Kwéyòl. <i>Budget:</i> boosts XCD 400, influencer thank-you fees XCD 300, chalk and QR posters XCD 200, contingency XCD 100.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> Filming near the nesting area raises a <b>wildlife and safety</b> issue as well as a consent issue: crews, lights and crowds can disturb nests, and the students filmed are minors. The group should film only in areas approved by the turtle-monitoring volunteers, never at night near nests, and collect signed release forms from parents or guardians for every student who appears. This protects the turtles the campaign exists to save and protects the group legally.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 5 + 6 + 6 + 2). Check every hex code and contrast claim, and that the frame numbers in (c) are consistent with the stated frame rate (10 seconds at 24 fps is 240 frames). Grade One responses connect the parts: the palette from (b) appears in the redesign in (a), the animation in (c) serves the campaign in (d), and the ethics in (e) come from the scenario rather than a generic list.</p>"}}});
